/* Machine translations of Kant, Schriften 1747–1756 (AA I), in the manner of Kemp Smith: see translation-briefs.js. Not the translators' own work. */
window.KANT_TR = window.KANT_TR || {};
KANT_TR['aa01/kemp'] = {"lebendige-kraefte-0001":["Thoughts\non the True Estimation\nof\nLiving Forces,\nand\nan Assessment of the Proofs\nwhich\nHerr von Leibniz and other Mechanicians\nhave employed\nin this Controversy,\ntogether with some Preliminary Considerations\nwhich\nconcern the Force of Bodies\nin General,\nby\nImmanuel Kant.\nKönigsberg,\nprinted by Martin Eberhard Dorn. 1746.",["- \"Lebendige Kräfte\" rendered \"living forces\" (the *vis viva* of the dispute); \"Streitsache\" as \"Controversy\"; \"Mechaniker\" as \"Mechanicians\".","- \"Leibnitz\" is given in modern spelling \"Leibniz\" in the English."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0002":["To the\nHighly Well-born, Highly Learned and Highly Experienced Herr,\nHerr\nJohann Christoph Bohl,\nDoctor of Medicine and Second Ordinary Professor at the Academy\nof Königsberg,\nand also\nRoyal Physician in Ordinary,\nmy especially Highly Honoured Patron.\nHighly Well-born Sir,\nHighly Learned and Highly Experienced Herr Doctor,\nEspecially Highly Honoured Patron!",["- The German gives \"Bohlius\", the Latinised form of the name; I retain it as \"Bohlius\" below in spirit but render the form here as \"Bohl\" only if you prefer; strictly the text reads *Bohlius*, and \"Johann Christoph Bohlius\" should stand. Please read the name as **Johann Christoph Bohlius**.","- The honorific formulae (Hochedelgebohrner etc.) are rendered in the conventional English equivalents of the period."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0003":["To whom can I better turn than to Your Highly Well-born Self, in order to draw some advantage from so poor a thing as the present work? From the special mark of kindness which you have shown me I venture to hope that this liberty will be received by you also as a proof of my gratitude. The character of this little work contains nothing on which I could build any confidence in this regard; for the honour of adorning a treatise with your name is not the sort of thing by which a gift could be made to Your Highly Well-born Self. A multitude of imperfect thoughts, which are perhaps incorrect in themselves, or at any rate lose all value through the lowliness of their author, and which finally convince me sufficiently that they are not worthy of being dedicated to you: that is all I have in my power to offer to Your Highly Well-born Self. Notwithstanding this, by means of the perfect concept which I have formed of your kindness, I make myself the hope that it will render me the service which I most highly prize, namely, of making known to Your Highly Well-born Self my gratitude [*Erkenntlichkeit*] towards you. I shall in future have more than one occasion to remember the obligation by which I am bound to you; but the present one will be among the best on which I publicly avow that I remain, with everlasting esteem,",["- Restated the dedication's long periods only lightly; the formal courtesy is kept, as nothing is gained by smoothing it further.","- \"Erkenntlichkeit ... zu erkennen zu geben\" plays on *erkennen*; the English \"gratitude ... making known\" loses the wordplay.","- \"Begriff\" rendered \"concept\"; \"die Niedrigkeit ihres Verfassers\" kept as \"the lowliness of their author\".","- \"mit eine von den besten\" read as \"mit eine[r] von den besten\" (\"among the best\"); \"im͂erwährender\" read \"immerwährender\" (\"everlasting\")."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0004":["Highly Well-born Sir,\nHighly Learned and Highly Experienced Herr Doctor,\nEspecially Highly Honoured Patron,\nYour Highly Well-born Self's\nKönigsberg, 22 April 1747.\nmost obliged servant\nImmanuel Kant.",["- The date 1747 differs from the title-page date 1746; reproduced as printed.","- \"Bohlius\" in the preceding passage: the name should read Bohlius, as in the German."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0005":["Preface.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0006":["Nothing is more to be guarded against than that we follow, after the manner of cattle, the flock that goes before us, proceeding not where we ought to go, but where people go.",["- Latin motto from Seneca, kept in the Latin in the original; the English is supplied beside it. \"antece dentium\" in the source is an OCR/print division for *antecedentium*, silently corrected. The Latin should stand in italics: *Nihil magis praestandum est, quam ne pecorum ritu sequamur antecedentium gregem, pergentes, non qua eundum est, sed qua itur.*"]],"lebendige-kraefte-0007":["Seneca, *De vita beata*, Chap. I.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0008":["I.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0009":["I believe I have reason to form so good an opinion of the judgment of the world, to which I commit these pages, as to expect that the liberty I take in contradicting great men will not be construed as a crime. There was a time when much was to be feared from such an undertaking; but I imagine that this time is now past, and that the human understanding [*Verstand*] has happily shaken off the fetters which ignorance and admiration once laid upon it. Nowadays one may boldly venture to count the authority of the Newtons and Leibnizes as nothing, if it should oppose itself to the discovery of truth, and to obey no persuasion other than the pull of the understanding.",["- \"Zuge des Verstandes\" rendered \"pull of the understanding\"; \"Zug\" could also be \"impulse\" or \"drift\".","- \"die Newtons und Leibnitze\" rendered \"the Newtons and Leibnizes\", keeping the plural as a type."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0010":["II.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0011":["If I undertake to reject the opinions of Herr von Leibniz, Wolff, Hermann, Bernoulli, Bilfinger and others, and to give preference to my own, I should not wish to have worse judges than these same men; for I know that their verdict, even if it rejected my opinions, would yet not condemn the intention behind them. One can pay these men no finer praise than that one may blame, without fear, in their presence all opinions, not excepting their own. A moderation of this kind was, though on another occasion, very much to the credit of a great man of antiquity. Timoleon, notwithstanding the services he had rendered to the freedom of Syracuse, was once summoned before a court. The judges were indignant at the presumption of his accusers. But Timoleon viewed this event quite differently. Such a proceeding could not displease a man who found his whole pleasure in seeing his country in the most perfect freedom. He protected those who made use of their freedom even against himself. All antiquity has accompanied this conduct with praise.",["- \"Herrmanns\" and \"Bülfingers\" rendered \"Hermann\" and \"Bilfinger\" (Jakob Hermann, Georg Bernhard Bilfinger), the evident persons meant.","- \"Vermessenheit\" rendered \"presumption\"; the sentence on the judges is kept as it stands."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0012":["After such great efforts as the greatest men have devoted to the freedom of the human understanding, should one really have reason to fear that the outcome of those efforts would displease them?",["- \"ihnen\" resolved as the great men, not the efforts; \"Erfolg\" rendered \"outcome\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0013":["III.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0014":["I shall make use of this moderation and fairness to my advantage. But I shall find it only where the mark of merit and of superior learning shows itself. Besides these, there remains a great crowd over whom prejudice and the authority of great persons still exercise a cruel dominion. These gentlemen, who would gladly be regarded as arbiters in learning, seem very skilful in judging a book without having read it. To expose it to censure one need only show them its title. If the author is unknown, without reputation and merit, the book is not worth wasting time on; still less if he undertakes great things: to censure famous men, to improve the sciences, and to recommend his own thoughts to the world. If it came down to numbers before the judgment-seat of the sciences, my cause would be a very desperate one. But this danger does not trouble me. These are those who, as the saying goes, dwell only at the foot of Parnassus, who possess no property and have no vote in the election.",["- The sentence beginning \"Wenn der Verfasser unbekannt...\" is irregular in construction; I have divided it and supplied \"still less\" for \"noch mehr aber\", and a colon for the list of the author's undertakings.","- \"Charakter\" rendered \"reputation\" (standing, rank), a judgment."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0015":["IV.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0016":["Prejudice is made precisely for men; it favours comfort and self-love, two qualities which one does not lay aside without laying aside humanity. He who is possessed by prejudice exalts certain men, whom it would be in vain to belittle or to bring down to his own level, above all others to an unscalable height. This preeminence covers all the rest with the appearance of a perfect equality, and prevents him from perceiving the difference which still obtains among these others, and which would otherwise expose him to the vexatious observation of how many times he is still surpassed by those who are yet within the bounds of mediocrity.",["- The first relative sentence is broken (\"Derjenige der von Vorurtheilen eingenommen, erhebet ...\"): I supply \"is possessed\" (\"[ist] eingenommen\"); the German lacks the verb.","- \"zu sich herunterzulassen\" rendered \"bring down to his own level\"; \"diesen\" in \"unter diesen\" resolved as the other men, not the exalted ones."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0017":["So long, then, as the vanity of human minds [*Gemüther*] remains powerful, so long will prejudice maintain itself; that is, it will never cease.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0018":["V.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0019":["In the course of this treatise I shall have no scruple in freely rejecting the proposition of even the most famous man, if it presents itself to my understanding as false. This freedom will bring upon me very hateful consequences. The world is much inclined to believe that one who thinks he has in this or that case a more correct knowledge [*Erkenntniß*] than, say, a great scholar, also in his own imagination sets himself above him. I venture to say that this appearance is very deceptive, and that here it really deceives.",["- \"Erkenntniß\" rendered \"knowledge\"; \"Einbildung\" rendered \"imagination\" though \"conceit\" is also possible in the sense.","- \"Schein ... betrüglich ... betrüge\" kept as \"appearance ... deceptive ... deceives\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0020":["In the perfection of the human understanding [*Verstand*] there is no such proportion and similarity as there is, say, in the structure of the human body. In the body it is indeed possible to infer the size of the whole from the size of one limb or another; but with the capacity of the understanding the case is quite different. Science [*Wissenschaft*] is an irregular body, without symmetry or uniformity. A scholar of dwarfish stature often surpasses, in this or that part of knowledge, another who, with the whole compass of his science, nevertheless towers far above him. Man's vanity, to all appearance, does not extend so far that it should fail to notice this difference, or should regard insight into one truth or another as the same thing as the wide compass of an eminent knowledge. At the least, I know that I should be wronged if this reproach were made against me.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0021":["VI.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0022":["The world is not so unreasonable as to think that a scholar of rank is no longer subject to the danger of error. But that a humble and unknown writer should have avoided errors from which all the acuteness of a great man could not save him: that is the difficulty, and it is not so easily digested. There is much presumption in these words: \"The truth which the greatest masters of human knowledge have sought in vain has first presented itself to my understanding.\" I do not venture to justify this thought, but I should not willingly renounce it either.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0023":["VII.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0024":["I am under the impression that it is sometimes not useless to place a certain noble confidence in one's own powers. Assurance of this kind animates all our endeavours and gives them a certain impetus, which is very favourable to the investigation of truth. When a man is in a position to persuade himself that he may still trust his reflection with something, and that it is possible to catch a Herr von Leibnitz in errors, he employs every means to make his conjecture true. And when he has gone astray a thousand times in such an undertaking, the gain which has accrued to knowledge of the truths will nevertheless be far more considerable than if he had kept always to the highway.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0025":["On this I take my stand. I have already marked out the course which I mean to hold. I shall set out upon my way, and nothing shall hinder me from pursuing it.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0026":["VIII.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0027":["There is yet a new objection which will be made against me, and which, as it seems, I must forestall. I shall at times be heard in the tone of a man who is very well assured of the correctness of his propositions, and who does not fear that he will be contradicted or that his inferences can deceive him. I am not so vain as actually to imagine this; nor have I reason to remove so carefully from my propositions the appearance of error. For after so many false steps to which the human understanding has been subject in all ages, it is no longer a disgrace to have erred. A quite different purpose lies behind my procedure.\n\nThe reader of these pages is doubtless already prepared, before he turns to my treatise, by the doctrines now current concerning living forces. He knows what was thought before Leibnitz announced to the world his estimation of forces, and this man's thought must also be known to him already. He has unfailingly let himself be won over by the inferences of one of the two parties, and in all likelihood this is the Leibnitzian party, for the whole of Germany has now declared itself for it. In this frame of mind he reads these pages. The defences of living forces, in the guise of geometrical proofs, have taken possession of his whole soul. He therefore regards my thoughts merely as doubts, and, if I am very fortunate, as plausible doubts, whose resolution he leaves to time, and which, he thinks, cannot after all stand in the way of truth. I, on the other hand, must employ all my art to hold the reader's attention a little longer. I must present myself to him in the full light of conviction which my proofs afford me, in order to draw his attention to the grounds which inspire me with this confidence.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0028":["If I were to put forward my thoughts only under the name of doubts, the world, which is in any case inclined to regard them as nothing better, would very easily pass them by. For an opinion which men once believe to have been proved will long maintain itself in favour, however plausible the doubts by which it is assailed, and however difficult it may be to resolve them.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0029":["A writer commonly draws his reader imperceptibly into the very state of mind in which he himself was when composing his work. I should therefore, if it were possible, rather impart to him the state of conviction than that of doubt; for the former would be more advantageous to me, and perhaps to the truth also, than the latter. These are the small artifices which I must not now despise, if I am to restore even somewhat the balance of the scales, in which the authority of great men gives so mighty a turn.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0030":["IX.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0031":["The last difficulty which I wish to remove is the one that will be raised against me on the score of discourtesy. It seems that I might have treated the men whom I have undertaken to refute with more reverence than I actually have. I ought to have pronounced my judgment upon their propositions in a much milder tone. I ought not to have called them errors, falsehoods, or even delusions. The harshness of these expressions appears derogatory to the great names against whom they are directed. In the age of distinctions, which was also the age of rough manners, the answer would have been that propositions must be judged apart from all the personal merits of their authors. But the politeness of this century lays quite another law upon me. I should not be excusable if the manner of my expression offended the high regard which the merit of great men demands of me. But I am assured that it does not.\n\nWhen we meet with manifest errors alongside the greatest discoveries, this is not so much a fault of the man as of humanity; and one would do humanity far too much honour in the person of the scholars, if one wished to exempt them wholly from it. A great man who is erecting an edifice of propositions cannot turn his attention equally strongly to every possible side. He is particularly engrossed in a certain consideration, and it is no wonder if errors then escape him from some other side, errors which he would unfailingly have avoided had he, apart from this occupation, but directed his attention to that side.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0032":["I will simply confess the truth without circumlocution. I shall not be disinclined to hold for actual errors and falsehoods those propositions which appear to me in that shape; and why should I put myself under the constraint of concealing this thought so anxiously in my writing, in order to seem what I do not think, but what the world would gladly have me think?",["- Notes below apply to the whole set; 0020: German \"Wissenschaft\" is rendered \"science\" and \"Erkenntniß\" \"knowledge\" in keeping with the fixed terms.","- 0027, 0031: long passages divided into two paragraphs, with some sentences kept as in the German; no content added.","- 0027: \"Kräftenschätzung\" rendered \"estimation of forces\"; \"Lehrsätze\" as \"doctrines\".","- \"Leibnitz\" retained in Kant's spelling; \"Herr von Leibnitz\" kept as written.","- 0031: \"Zeit der Unterscheidungen\" rendered literally as \"age of distinctions\"; its sense (scholastic distinctions) is uncertain.","- 0031: \"ohnfehlbar\" rendered \"unfailingly\" (also 0027); \"in dieser Verfassung\" rendered \"in this frame of mind\", and in 0024 \"in der Verfassung stehet\" as \"in a position\".","- 0029: \"Ausschlag\" rendered \"turn\" (of the scales), i.e. the decisive tipping of the balance."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0033":["And, to speak generally, I should get on badly with the ceremony of giving to all the judgments I pronounce upon great men a certain flourish of courtesy, of skilfully softening my expressions, and of letting the mark of deference be seen everywhere. This effort would often bring me, on account of the choice of words, into a vexatious narrowness, and would subject me to the necessity of straying from the footpath of philosophical reflection altogether. I shall therefore make use of the occasion of this Preface to make a public declaration of the reverence and high esteem which I shall always cherish towards the great masters of our knowledge, whom I shall now have the honour to call [*heissen*] my opponents, and which the freedom of my poor judgments cannot in the least diminish.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0034":["X.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0035":["After the various prejudices which I have now endeavoured to remove, there still remains in the end a certain legitimate prejudice, to which I owe in particular whatever may yet be found convincing in my work. When many great men of proven acuteness and power of judgment are led, partly by different ways and partly by the same way, to maintain one and the same proposition, it is far more probable that their proofs are correct than that the understanding of some poor writer should have observed the rigour of those proofs more exactly. The latter therefore has great reason to make the object of his reflection especially clear and plain to himself, and so to analyse and set it out that, should he perhaps commit a fallacy, it would at once strike his eyes. For it is presupposed that, even where the reflection is intricate, the man who surpasses the other in acuteness will be the sooner to discover the truth. He must therefore make his inquiry as simple and easy as possible, so that, in proportion to his power of judgment, he may presume as much light and correctness in his reflection as the other, in proportion to his own, may presume in a far more intricate inquiry.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0036":["This observation I have made a law for myself in carrying out my undertaking, as will soon be perceived.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0037":["XI.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0038":["Before we bring this Preface to a close, let us briefly acquaint ourselves with the present state of the controversy concerning living forces.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0039":["Herr von Leibniz, to all appearance, did not first perceive living forces in the cases in which he first presented them to the world. The beginning of an opinion is commonly much simpler, especially of an opinion that carries with it something so bold and remarkable as the estimation by the square. There are certain very common experiences by which we perceive that an actual motion, for example a blow or impact, always carries more force with it than a dead pressure, however strong the latter may be. This observation was perhaps the seed of a thought which could not remain unfruitful in the hands of Herr von Leibniz, and which afterwards grew into one of the most famous doctrinal systems.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0040":["XII.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0041":["To speak generally, the doctrine of living forces seems, so to say, made precisely for the purpose that the understanding should at some time or other, whatever the age might have been, be seduced by it. The overcome obstacles of gravity, the displaced matters, the compressed springs, the moved masses, the velocities arising in compound motion: all agree in a remarkable manner to produce the semblance of the estimation by the square. There is a time in which multiplicity of proofs counts for what in another time would be accomplished by their rigour and distinctness. This time is now present among the defenders of living forces. If in one or another of their proofs they feel little conviction, the semblance of truth which shows itself in its favour from so many more sides confirms their assent and does not let it waver.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0042":["XIII.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0043":["It is harder to say on which side the presumption of victory has hitherto shown itself most strongly in the controversy over living forces. The two Bernoullis, Herr von Leibniz and Hermann, who stood at the head of the philosophers of their nation, could not be outweighed by the authority of the remaining scholars of Europe. These men, who had all the weapons of geometry in their power, were alone able to hold up an opinion which perhaps would not have dared to show itself had it been in the hands of a less famous defender.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0044":["Both the party of Descartes and that of Herr von Leibniz have felt for their opinion all the conviction of which one is commonly capable in human knowledge. On both sides men have sighed over nothing but the prejudice of their opponents, and each party has believed that its opinion could not possibly be called in question if its opponents would only take the trouble to regard it in a proper equilibrium of the inclinations of the mind [*Gemüthsneigungen*].",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0045":["Nevertheless a certain remarkable difference shows itself between the manner in which the party of living forces seeks to maintain itself and that in which the Cartesian estimation defends itself. The latter appeals only to simple cases, in which the decision between truth and error is easy and certain. The former, on the contrary, makes its proofs as intricate and obscure as possible, and saves itself, so to say, by the help of night from a combat in which in a proper light of distinctness it would perhaps always come off the worse.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0046":["The Leibnizians have also almost all experiments [*Erfahrungen*] on their side; this is perhaps the only thing in which they have the advantage over the Cartesians. Messrs. Poleni, 's Gravesande and van Musschenbroek have rendered them this service, the consequences of which would perhaps be excellent, had it been used more correctly.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0047":["I shall give in this Preface no account of what I intend to accomplish in the present treatise on the matter of living forces. This book has no other hope of being read than that which it builds upon its brevity; it will therefore be easy for the reader to acquaint himself with its contents.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0048":["If I might trust anything to my own conceit, I should say that my opinions could render some not inconvenient assistance towards settling one of the greatest schisms now prevailing among the geometers of Europe. But this persuasion is vain: nowhere does a man's judgment count for less than in his own cause. I am not so enamoured of my own as to lend an ear, for its benefit, to a prejudice of self-love. However that may be, I venture to predict with confidence that this dispute will either be settled shortly or will never cease.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0049":["First Chapter,\nOn the Force of Bodies in General.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0050":["§ 1.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0051":["Every body has an essential force.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0052":["Because I believe that it can contribute something to my purpose of making the doctrine of living forces at last certain and decisive, if I first establish some metaphysical concepts of the force of bodies in general, I shall begin with this.",["- 0033: The sentence beginning \"diese Bemühung\" is divided from the preceding one; *heissen* kept in brackets because \"to call my opponents\" renders it. \"Erkenntniß\" is rendered \"knowledge\".","- 0033: \"über den Fußsteig ... von allen auszuschweifen\" is obscure; rendered \"straying from the footpath ... altogether\", reading \"von allen\" as \"entirely\". Doubtful.","- 0035: \"Vorwurf seiner Betrachtung\" rendered \"object of his reflection\"; the long final sentences are kept close but the \"denn es wird vorausgesetzt\" clause is left as a presupposition, which is the sense Kant requires.","- 0035: \"denenselben\" refers to the proofs (\"Beweise\"); \"Schärfe\" = rigour.","- 0039, 0041, 0043 etc.: \"Schätzung nach dem Quadrat\" = \"estimation by the square\" (of the velocity); \"Leibnitz\" modernised to Leibniz.","- 0041: \"Verstand\" = understanding; \"Schein\" = semblance.","- 0044: \"Gemüthsneigungen\" rendered \"inclinations of the mind\" (Gemüt = mind).","- 0046: \"Erfahrungen\" here means experimental results; rendered \"experiments\", with the German given.","- 0047: \"Inbegrif\" rendered \"contents\" (literally the sum or sum-total)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0053":["It is said that a body which is in motion has a force. For overcoming obstacles, bending springs, displacing masses: this everyone entitles acting [*würken*]. If one looks no further than the senses teach, one holds this force to be something communicated to the body wholly from outside, and of which it has nothing when it is at rest. The whole host of philosophers before Leibniz was of this opinion, Aristotle alone excepted. It is believed that the obscure entelechy of that man is the secret of the actions of bodies. The Schoolmen, one and all, who followed Aristotle, did not grasp this riddle, and perhaps it was not made for anyone to grasp. Leibniz, to whom human reason owes so much, was the first to teach that an essential force dwells in body, which belongs to it even prior to extension. *Est aliquid praeter extensionem imo extensione prius*: these are his words.",["- *Würken*/*Würkung* rendered \"act\"/\"action\" (older spelling of *wirken*); \"force\" for *Kraft*.","- The first sentences are restated slightly: \"Denn …; dieses nennet alle Welt würken\" is made a single explanatory sentence with a colon.","- *Weltweisen* = \"philosophers\"; *Schullehrer* = \"Schoolmen\". *Vor* in \"vor etwas\", \"vor die Würkungen\" is old usage for *für* (\"to be\"/\"of\")."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0054":["§ 2.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0055":["Leibniz called this force of bodies, in general, the active force [*würkende Kraft*].",["- Heading-like sentence; *überhaupt* taken as \"in general\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0056":["The discoverer gave this force the general name of the active force. One ought merely to have followed him step by step in the systems of metaphysics; but men have sought to determine this force somewhat more closely. The body, it is said, has a moving force, since one sees it do nothing else than produce motions. When it presses, it strives toward motion; but the force is in exercise only when the motion is actual. I maintain, however, that if one ascribes to body an essential moving force (*vim motricem*), so as to have an answer ready to the question about the cause of motion, one is practising, in a certain measure, the device of the Schoolmen, who, in investigating the grounds of heat or of cold, took refuge in a *vis calorifica* or *frigifaciens*.",["- *Auf dem Fusse nachfolgen* rendered \"follow step by step\".","- Text reads *frigifaciente* (ablative form, presumably OCR or original); I give the nominative *frigifaciens* and flag it as possibly a normalisation.","- The long final sentence is kept whole, as its structure is clear in English."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0057":["§ 3.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0058":["The essential force ought properly to be called *vim activam*.",["- Heading-like sentence; Latin accusative kept as in the source."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0059":["It is not correct to speak as if motion were a species of actions, and to ascribe to body, on that account, a force of the same name. A body which meets with infinitely little resistance, and which therefore scarcely acts at all, has the most motion. Motion is only the outward phenomenon of the state of the body in which it does not indeed act, but yet is endeavouring to act. When, however, it suddenly loses its motion through an object, that is, at the moment in which it is brought to rest, it acts. One ought therefore not to name the force of a substance after that which is no action at all; and still less ought one to say, of bodies which act in a state of rest (for example, of a ball which by its weight presses upon the table on which it lies), that they have an endeavour to move. For since they would not act if they moved, one would have to say: while a body acts, it has an endeavour to pass into the state in which it does not act. One ought therefore to entitle the force of a body far rather a *vis activa* in general than a *vis motrix*.",["- *Gegenstand* rendered \"object\" in the sense of the obstacle encountered, not as a technical epistemological term.","- *Bemühung* = \"endeavour\"; *Ruhestand* = \"state of rest\".","- \"Man wird … nennen sollen\" rendered \"ought … to entitle\", following the method's *heißen/nennen* = \"entitle\".","- The sentence beginning \"Man sollte daher\" is syntactically irregular in German (the second clause depends loosely on \"sollte\"); I have smoothed it by repeating \"ought\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0060":["§ 4.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0061":["How motion can be explained from active force in general.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0062":["But nothing is easier than to derive the origin of what we call motion from the general concepts of active force. The substance A, whose force is determined to act outside itself (that is, to alter the inner state of other substances), either finds at the very first moment of its endeavour an object that endures its whole force, or it finds none. If the former happened to all substances, we should know no motion at all, and so should not name the force of bodies after it. But if the substance A cannot at the moment of its endeavour apply its whole force, it will apply only a part of it. With the remaining part, however, it cannot remain inactive. It must rather act with its whole force, for otherwise it would cease to be entitled a force if it were not applied entire. Since, then, the consequences of this exercise are not to be met with in the co-existent state of the world, they must be found in the second dimension of the world, namely in the successive series of things. The body will therefore apply its force not all at once, but little by little. In the following moments, however, it cannot act upon the very same substances upon which it acted at the start, for these endure only the first part of its force and are not capable of receiving the rest; A therefore acts successively always upon other substances. But the substance C, upon which it acts in the second moment, must have to A a quite different relation of place and situation from that of B, upon which it acted at the outset; otherwise there would be no ground why A should not at the beginning have acted at once upon the substance C as well as upon B. In the same way the substances upon which it acts in the following moments each have a different situation relative to the first place of the body A. That is to say, A alters its place in acting successively.",["- Several long sentences are divided or recast; \"Daher weil …\" is rendered \"Since, then, …\".","- *Abmessung* = \"dimension\"; *coexistirend* rendered \"co-existent\".","- *Lage* = \"situation\"; *Ort* = \"place\". *Erdulden* = \"endure\".","- *Heissen* rendered \"to be entitled\".","- \"Es\" in \"dessen … Kraft\": the body/substance A and \"he\" (*er*) are used interchangeably in the German; I use \"it\" and sometimes \"the body\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0063":["§ 5.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0064":["What difficulties flow from this into the doctrine of the action of body upon the soul, if one ascribes to bodies no other force than the *vis motrix*.",["- \"Diesen\" taken to refer to bodies (*Körper*), the nearest plural antecedent."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0065":["Because we are not distinctly aware of what a body does when it acts in a state of rest, we always think back to the motion that would follow if the resistance were removed. It would be enough to use this motion in order to have an external characteristic of what goes on in the body and what we cannot see. But commonly motion is regarded as that which the force does when it breaks fully loose, and as the sole consequence of it. Since it is so easy to find one's way back from this small digression to the right concepts, one should not suppose that such an error would be of consequence. Yet it is in fact, though not in mechanics and natural science. For it is precisely from this that it becomes so hard in metaphysics to represent how matter is in a position to produce representations in the human soul in a truly effective manner (that is, through physical influence). What else does matter do, it is said, than cause motions? Hence all its force will come to this, that at most it displaces the soul from its place. But how is it possible that the force which alone brings forth motions should generate representations and ideas? These are such different kinds of things that it is inconceivable how one could be the source of the other.",["- *Vorstellungen* = \"representations\"; *Begriffe* = \"concepts\"; *Ideen* = \"ideas\".","- \"Es wäre genug sich derselben dazu zu bedienen\" rendered freely but in sense; \"derselben\" refers to the motion.","- *Physischer Einfluß* = \"physical influence\" (*influxus physicus*).","- *Würksame Art* rendered \"effective manner\" (*würksam* = effective, truly acting)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0066":["§ 6.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0067":["The difficulty which arises from this when the question is of the action of the soul upon the body; and how it can be removed by the designation of an *vis activa* in general.",["- Source reads *vis actiuae* (genitive/OCR for *activae*); rendered as the nominative-style phrase \"*vis activa*\" for English, with the sense of the designation \"active force\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0068":["A like difficulty shows itself when the question is whether the soul is also in a position to set matter in motion. Both difficulties vanish, however, and physical influence receives no small measure of light, if the force of matter is reckoned not by motion but by effects upon other substances, which we need not determine more closely. For the question whether the soul can cause motions, that is, whether it has a moving force, is transformed into this: whether its essential force can be determined to an effect outwards, that is, whether it is capable of acting outside itself upon other beings and of producing changes in them. This question can be answered in a quite decisive manner: the soul must be able to act outwards for this reason, that it is in a place. For if we analyse the concept of what we entitle place, we find that it indicates the effects of substances upon one another. Nothing, therefore, has prevented a certain acute writer from making the triumph of physical influence over pre-established harmony complete, other than this slight confusion of concepts, from which one readily extricates oneself as soon as one directs one's attention to it.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0069":["If the force of bodies in general is called merely an acting force [*würkende Kraft*], it is easy to grasp how matter can determine the soul to certain representations.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0070":["It is equally easy to grasp the manner of the paradoxical proposition, namely how it is possible that matter, which is nevertheless imagined to be capable of causing nothing but motions, should impress certain representations and images upon the soul. For matter that has been set in motion acts upon everything that is connected with it in space, and hence also upon the soul; that is, it alters the soul's inner state in so far as this relates to what is outer. Now the whole inner state of the soul is nothing other than the sum of all its representations and concepts, and in so far as this inner state relates to the outer, it is entitled the *status repraesentatiuus vniuersi*. Hence matter, by means of the force which it has in motion, alters the state of the soul through which the soul represents the world to itself. In this way one grasps how matter can impress representations upon the soul.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0071":["§ 7.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0072":["Things can really exist and yet be present nowhere in the world.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0073":["It is hard not to digress in a subject of such wide compass; but I must turn back to what I wished to remark about the force of bodies. All connection and relation among substances existing outside one another arises from the reciprocal effects which their forces exert upon one another. Let us see, then, what truths can be derived from this concept of force. Either a substance stands in connection and relation with others outside it, or it does not. Since every self-subsistent being contains within itself the complete source of all its determinations, it is not necessary to its existence that it stand in connection with other things. Hence substances can exist and yet have no external relation to others, nor stand in any actual connection with them. Now since without external connections, positions and relations there is no place, it is quite possible that a thing should really exist and yet be present nowhere in the whole world. This paradoxical proposition, although it is a consequence, and indeed a very easy consequence, of the best-known truths, has, so far as I know, been remarked by no one. But other propositions flow from the same source which are no less wonderful, and which take hold of the understanding, so to speak, against its will.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0074":["§ 8.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0075":["It is true in the strictly metaphysical sense that more than one world can exist.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0076":["Nothing can be said to be a part of a whole if it stands in no connection whatever with the other parts; otherwise there would be no difference between an actual union and an imagined one. But the world is an actually composite being. A substance, therefore, that is connected with nothing in the whole world will not belong to the world at all, save perhaps in thought; that is, it will be no part of it. If many such beings exist which have no connection with anything in the world but stand in relation to one another, there arises from them an altogether peculiar whole; they make up an altogether separate world. It is therefore incorrect to teach, as is constantly done in the lecture-halls of philosophy, that in the metaphysical sense no more than a single world can exist. It is really possible that God has created many millions of worlds, even taken in the strictest metaphysical meaning; it remains, therefore, undecided whether they actually exist or not. The error committed here has undoubtedly arisen from not paying exact attention to the definition of the world. For the definition reckons to the world only what stands in an actual connection with the other things,* whereas the theorem forgets this limitation and speaks of all existing things in general.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0077":["* *Mundus est rerum omnium contingentium simultanearum & successiuarum inter se connexarum series.*",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0078":["§ 9.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0079":["If substances had no force to act outside themselves, there would be no extension and no space.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0080":["It is easy to prove that there would be neither space nor extension if substances had no force to act outside themselves. For without this force there is no connection, without connection no order, and without order, finally, no space. But it is somewhat harder to see how the plurality of the dimensions of space follows from the law according to which this force of substances acts outside them.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0081":["The ground of the threefold dimension of space is still unknown.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0082":["Since I perceive a circular inference in the proof which Herr von Leibniz somewhere in the *Theodicy* takes from the number of lines that can be drawn at right angles to one another from a point, I have thought of proving the threefold dimension of extension from what is observed in the powers of numbers. The first three powers are quite simple and cannot be reduced to any other; but the fourth, as the square of the square [*Quadratoquadrat*], is nothing but a repetition of the second power. However well this property of numbers seemed to me to serve in explaining the threefold dimension of space, it did not hold up in application. For the fourth power is, in everything that we can represent to ourselves of space by the imagination, an impossibility [*Unding*]. In geometry one can multiply neither a square by itself nor the cube by its root. Hence the necessity of the threefold dimension rests not so much on this, that if one posited more dimensions one would do nothing but repeat the earlier ones (as is the case with the powers of numbers), but rather on a certain other necessity which I am not yet in a position to explain.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0083":["§ 10.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0084":["It is probable that the threefold dimension of space arises from the law according to which the forces of substances act upon one another.",["- 0068: \"nach draussen\" rendered \"outwards\"; \"Würkungen\" as \"effects\"/\"acting\" (old spelling of *Wirkung*). \"physischer Einfluß\" = \"physical influence\" (*influxus physicus*), \"vorherbestimmte Harmonie\" = \"pre-established harmony\". The acute writer is unnamed in the text.","- 0068: \"nicht auf die Rechnung der Bewegung … setzet\" rendered \"reckoned not by motion but by effects\"; the final sentence is kept as one, with \"other than\" supplied to smooth \"nichts mehr … als\".","- 0070: \"heißt er\" = \"is entitled\"; *status repraesentatiuus vniuersi* left in Latin, italicised, with the original spelling. \"Zustand\" = \"state\".","- 0073: \"Verstand\" in the final clause = \"understanding\". \"vor Wahrheiten\" read as \"was für Wahrheiten\" (\"what truths\"). \"verhanden\" rendered \"present\".","- 0076: The parenthesis is turned into a separate sentence, and the long first sentence is divided into three. \"Verstande\" in \"metaphysischen Verstande\" rendered \"sense\". The footnote mark is kept after the clause it follows.","- 0077: footnote is a Latin definition (Wolffian); left untranslated as technical.","- 0082: \"Zirkelschluß\" = \"circular inference\"; \"Unding\" = \"impossibility\", literally a non-thing. \"Herr von Leibnitz\" modernised to \"Leibniz\". The passage's \"Theodicee\" is rendered *Theodicy*.","- 0082: \"hielte sie … nicht Stich\" rendered \"did not hold up\"; \"daß wenn man mehrere setzte\" supplied \"dimensions\" for sense."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0085":["Everything that occurs among the properties of a thing must be capable of being derived from that which contains in itself the complete ground of the thing itself. The properties of extension, and therefore its threefold dimensionality also, will accordingly be grounded upon the properties of the force which substances possess in respect of the things with which they are combined. The force with which a substance acts in union with others cannot be thought without a certain law, which manifests itself in the manner of its action. Now the kind of law according to which substances act upon one another must also determine the kind of union and composition of many of them. Hence the law according to which a whole collection of substances (that is, a space) is measured, or the dimension of extension, will proceed from the laws according to which substances, by virtue of their essential forces, seek to unite with one another.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0086":["The threefold dimensionality seems therefore to arise from the fact that substances in the existing world act upon one another in such a way that the strength of the action stands in inverse proportion to the square of the distances.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0087":["In accordance with this, I hold the following. First, the substances in the existing world, of which we are a part, have essential forces of such a kind that, in union with one another, they spread their actions abroad according to the inverse double ratio of the distances. Second, the whole which arises from this, by virtue of this law, has the property of threefold dimension. Third, this law is arbitrary, and God could have chosen another for it, for example the inverse triple ratio. Fourth and finally, from another law there would also have flowed an extension having other properties and dimensions. A science of all these possible kinds of space would undoubtedly be the highest geometry that a finite understanding could undertake. The impossibility we find in ourselves of representing a space of more than three dimensions seems to me to arise from the fact that our soul likewise receives impressions from without according to the law of the inverse double ratio of the distances, and that its very nature is made not merely to suffer in this way but also to act outside itself in this manner.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0088":["§ 11.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0089":["The condition under which it is probable that there are many worlds.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0090":["If it is possible that there are extensions of other dimensions, it is also very probable that God has actually placed them somewhere; for all his works have the greatness and manifoldness that they can in any way contain. Spaces of this kind could not possibly stand in connection with those which are of wholly different essences. Such spaces would therefore not belong to our world at all, but would have to constitute worlds of their own. In what precedes I have shown that several worlds, taken in the metaphysical sense, could exist together. Here, however, is at the same time the condition which, as it seems to me, is the only one on account of which it would also be probable that many worlds actually exist. For if only the one kind of space, that which admits of a threefold dimension, is possible, then the other worlds which I posit outside the one in which we exist could be connected with ours as regards space, since they are spaces of one and the same kind. It would then be a question why God separated the one world from the other, when by connecting them he would have imparted a greater perfection to his work; for the more connection, the more harmony and agreement there is in the world, whereas gaps and divisions violate the laws of order and perfection. It is therefore not probable that many worlds exist (although it is possible in itself), unless the many kinds of space of which I have just spoken are possible.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0091":["These thoughts may form the sketch of a consideration which I reserve for myself. I cannot deny, however, that I communicate them just as they occur to me, without having secured their certainty by a longer investigation. I am therefore ready to reject them again as soon as a more mature judgment shall disclose their weakness to me.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0092":["§ 12.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0093":["Some teachers of metaphysics maintain that body, by virtue of its force, strives towards motion in all directions.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0094":["The newest philosophy (*Weltweisheit*) fixes certain concepts of the essential force of bodies which cannot altogether be approved. This force is entitled a perpetual striving towards motion. Besides the fault which this concept carries with it, as I showed at the outset, there is another of which I will now speak. If force is a perpetual endeavour to act, it would be an obvious contradiction to say that this exertion of force is, in respect of external things, wholly indeterminate. For by its definition it is indeed endeavouring to act outside itself upon other things; and according to the doctrines adopted by the newest teachers of metaphysics, it actually acts upon them. Those therefore seem to speak most correctly who say that it is directed towards all directions rather than that it is wholly indeterminate as regards direction. The celebrated Herr Hamberger accordingly maintains that the substantial force of the monads strives equally towards motion in all directions, and so, like a balance, maintains itself at rest through the equality of the counter-pressures.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0095":["§ 13.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0096":["First objection to this opinion.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0097":["According to this system, motion arises when the equilibrium of two opposed tendencies is removed, and the body moves in the direction of the greater tendency, with the excess of force which this has obtained over the smaller opposed one. This explanation still satisfies the imagination (*Einbildungskraft*) in the case where the moving body always advances together with the body moved; for this case is similar to that in which someone supports with his hand one of two scale-pans of equal weight, and thereby causes the motion of the other. But a body to which its motion has been communicated by an impact continues that motion to infinity, although the impelling force ceases to act upon it. According to the doctrinal system cited, however, it would not be able to continue its motion; as soon as the impelling body ceased to act upon it, it would suddenly fall into rest. For since the tendencies of the body's force, directed towards all directions, are inseparable from its substance, the equilibrium of these inclinations will be restored at that very moment at which the external force, which had been opposed to the one tendency, ceases to act.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0098":["Second objection to the same opinion.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0099":["§ 14.",["- 0085: \"Abmessung\" rendered \"dimension\"/\"dimensionality\" for the threefold *dreyfache Abmessung*; \"Weiten\" = \"distances\"; the first sentence is split in two to bring out the argument.","- 0087: the enumeration (erstens … viertens) is set out as separate sentences; the first \"erstens\" is lacking in the German (\"Diesem zu folge … daß\"), and \"First\" is supplied.","- 0087: \"doppelte umgekehrte Verhältniß\" = \"inverse double ratio\", i.e. the inverse-square law; \"Seele\" = \"soul\".","- 0090: \"im metaphysischen Verstande\" rendered \"in the metaphysical sense\" (*Verstand* here = sense, not faculty); \"Räume … von ganz andern Wesen\" = \"of wholly different essences\"; \"Es ist also nicht wahrscheinlich…\" is kept as one sentence.","- 0094: \"Weltweisheit\" given in German; \"heißen\" rendered \"entitled\" in \"man nennet\"; \"Gegendrücke\" = \"counter-pressures\".","- 0097: \"Waagschalen\" = \"scale-pans\"; \"ins unendliche\" = \"to infinity\".","- 0098/0099: headings only; 0099 breaks off with the section number."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0100":["This, however, is not the only difficulty. Since a thing must be thoroughly determined, the endeavour towards motion which substances exert in all directions must have a certain degree of intensity. For it cannot be infinite; and yet a finite endeavour to act, without a certain magnitude of exertion, is impossible. Since, then, the degree of intensity is finite and determinate, let us suppose that a body A, of equal mass, strikes against it with a force three times greater than all the endeavour towards motion which the latter has in the essential force of its substance. By its *vis inertiae* it will then be able to take from the impinging body only the third part of its velocity. But it will itself also acquire no greater velocity than that which is equal to a third of the velocity of the moving body. After the impact, therefore, A, the impinging body, is to continue moving with two degrees of velocity, but B with only one degree, in the same direction. Now B stands in A's way, and does not take on as much velocity as it would need in order not to obstruct the motion of A; yet, in spite of this, it is not able to arrest A's motion. A will therefore actually move in the direction A C * with velocity 2, and B, which is in A's way, will move in this same direction with velocity 1; and the motions of both will nevertheless proceed unhindered. This is impossible, unless one were willing to suppose that B is penetrated by A, which is a metaphysical absurdity. **",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0101":["* Fig. I.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0102":["** This is grasped still more clearly if we consider that, after the impact, body A will be at C when B has not yet passed the point D, which divides the line A C in half. A must therefore have penetrated B, for otherwise it could not have gained an advance over it.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0103":["§ 15.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0104":["Twofold division of motion.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0105":["It is time for me to end this metaphysical preparation. I cannot, however, refrain from adding one more remark, which I hold to be indispensable for understanding what follows. The concepts of dead pressure and of its measure, which occur in mechanics, I presuppose in my readers. In general, I shall not in these pages present a complete treatise on everything that belongs to the doctrine of living and dead forces; I shall merely sketch some slight thoughts which seem to me new and which serve my chief aim, that of amending the Leibnizian measure of forces. I therefore divide all motions into two principal kinds. The one has the property that it maintains itself in the body to which it has been communicated, and continues to infinity if no obstacle opposes it. The other is a perpetual effect of a constantly impelling force. It does not even require a resistance to annihilate it, but depends solely on the external force, and vanishes as soon as this ceases to sustain it. Examples of the first kind are bullets that are shot and all bodies that are thrown; of the second kind is the motion of a ball gently pushed forward by the hand, and likewise all bodies that are carried, or drawn with moderate velocity.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0106":["§ 16.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0107":["Motion of the second kind is not distinct from dead pressure.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0108":["It is easily grasped, without entering upon any deep metaphysical consideration, that the force which manifests itself in motion of the first kind has, in comparison with force of the second kind, something infinite about it. For the latter in part annihilates itself, and ceases of itself suddenly as soon as the impelling force is withdrawn. It may therefore be regarded as if it vanished at every moment, but was also generated anew as often. The former, on the other hand, is an inner source of a force that is in itself imperishable, and which performs its effect throughout continuing time. It stands to the other, therefore, as a moment to time, or as the point to the line. Motion of the latter kind is accordingly not distinct from dead pressure, as Baron Wolff has already remarked in his Cosmology.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0109":["§ 17.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0110":["Motion of the first kind presupposes a force that is proportional to the square of the velocity.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0111":["Since I wish to speak properly of the motion which maintains itself of its own accord in an empty space to all eternity, I shall consider its nature briefly, according to the concepts of metaphysics. If a body runs in free motion through an infinitely subtle space, its force can be measured by the sum of all the effects it produces throughout eternity. For if this aggregate were not equal to its whole force, one would have to take a longer time than infinite time in order to find a sum equal to the whole intensity of the force; which is absurd. Now compare two bodies A and B, of which A has a velocity as 2, and B a velocity as 1. From the beginning of its motion, A presses throughout eternity upon the infinitely small masses of the space which it traverses with twice the velocity of B; but in this infinite time it also covers a space twice as great as B. The whole magnitude of the effect which A produces is therefore proportional to the product of the force with which it meets the small parts of space and the multitude of these parts; and the force of B is constituted in the same way. Now the effects of both upon the small *moleculae* of space are proportional to their velocities, and the multitude of these parts is likewise as the velocities. Consequently the magnitude of the whole effect of one body is to the whole effect of the other as the square of their velocities, and so their forces also are in this ratio. *",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0112":["* Since in this treatise I properly intend to set certain objections against the opinion of Herr von Leibniz, it seems that I contradict myself in offering in this section a proof in support of his opinion. But in the last chapter I shall show that Herr von Leibniz's opinion, if only it is restricted in a certain way, does actually hold good.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0113":["§ 18.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0114":["Second ground of this.",["- 0100: Restated the long sentence beginning \"Daher weil… so setze man\" as several shorter sentences; \"ihn\" taken to refer to body B (the body at rest), as the later \"B steht A im Wege\" requires.","- 0100: \"Gewalt\" rendered \"force\", \"Kraft\" also \"force\", \"Bemühung/Bestrebung\" \"endeavour\"; \"vim inertiae\" kept in Latin. The sentence \"weil er diesem ungeachtet…\" is irregular in German; smoothed into \"yet, in spite of this…\".","- 0102: \"jener… diesen\" resolved as A and B respectively; \"Vorsprung\" as an advance over B.","- 0105: \"Dieandere\" in the OCR silently corrected to \"Die andere\". \"Verstande\" rendered \"understanding\" in the sense of comprehension.","- 0108: \"Cosmologie\" is Wolff's *Cosmologia generalis*; \"Baron Wolf\" normalised to Wolff.","- 0111: \"Aggregat\" kept as \"aggregate\"; \"Moleculas\" kept in italic Latin as *moleculae*. \"will mit wenigem\" lacks a subject (\"ich\"), supplied in the English."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0115":["For a better understanding of this property of living forces, we may recall what was said in § 16. Dead pressures can have nothing more than simple velocity as their measure. Their force does not rest upon the bodies in which they are exercised but is brought about by an external power; and so the resistance which overcomes it requires no particular exertion in regard to the strength with which this force seeks to maintain itself in the body. (For the force is in no way rooted in the acting substance, nor does it strive to maintain itself therein.) The resistance has only to destroy the single velocity which the body needs in order to change its place.\n\nWith living force the case is quite otherwise. The state in which a substance finds itself, when it proceeds with a certain velocity in free motion, is grounded wholly upon its inner determinations; and the substance is therefore at the same time striving to maintain itself in this state. The external resistance, then, besides the force which it needs in order to balance the velocity of this body, must also have a particular power to break the endeavour with which the inner force of the body is exerted to maintain this state of motion in itself. The whole strength of the resistance which is to bring to rest bodies that are in free motion must accordingly stand in a compound ratio, made up of the proportion of the velocity and of the force with which the body strives to maintain in itself this state of endeavour. That is to say, since the two ratios are equal to one another, the force which the resistance requires is as the square of the velocity of the impinging bodies.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0116":["§ 19.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0117":["I dare not promise myself to attain anything decisive and incontestable in a consideration that is purely metaphysical. I therefore turn to the following chapter, which, through the application of mathematics, may perhaps be able to make a stronger claim upon conviction.\n\nOur metaphysics, like many other sciences, stands in fact only on the threshold of a truly thorough knowledge; God knows when it will be seen to cross it. It is not difficult to perceive its weakness in much that it undertakes. One very often finds prejudice as the greatest strength of its proofs. Nothing is more to blame for this than the prevailing inclination of those who seek to extend human knowledge. They would gladly have a great philosophy, but it were to be wished that it might also be a thorough one.\n\nFor a philosopher it is almost the only recompense for his labour if, after laborious inquiry, he can at last rest content in the possession of a truly thorough science. It is therefore a great deal to ask of him that he should only seldom trust his own approval; that in his own discoveries he should not conceal the imperfections which he is unable to remedy; and that he should never be so vain as to set the pleasure which the fancy of a thorough science affords above the true utility of knowledge. The understanding is very prone to assent, and it is indeed very hard to hold it back for long. Yet one ought at last to impose this constraint upon oneself, in order to sacrifice to well-grounded knowledge everything that has about it something extensive [*weitläuftig*] and alluring.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0118":["Second Chapter.\nExamination of the Doctrines of the Leibnizian Party concerning Living Forces.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0119":["§ 20.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0120":["I find in the treatise which Herr Bülfinger presented to the Petersburg Academy a reflection which I have at all times employed as a rule in the investigation of truths. When men of sound understanding, among whom suspicion of ulterior motives is to be found either on neither side or on both, maintain opinions wholly opposed to one another, it accords with the logic of probabilities to direct one's attention chiefly to a certain middle proposition which, in a certain measure, allows both parties to be in the right.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0121":["§ 21.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0122":["I do not know whether I have otherwise been fortunate in this manner of thinking, but in the controversy over living forces I hope to be so. Never has the world been divided more equally in certain opinions than in those which concern the measure of the forces of bodies in motion. The parties are, to all appearance, equally strong and equally fair. Ulterior motives may indeed mingle in; but of which party could one say that it was wholly free from them? I therefore choose the safest way, in that I adopt an opinion in which both great parties find their account.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0123":["§ 22.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0124":["Leibniz's and Descartes's Estimation of Forces.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0125":["Before Leibniz the world had paid homage to the one proposition of Descartes, which assigned to bodies in general, even to those in actual motion, mere velocity as the measure of their force. No one allowed it to occur to him that it was possible to set a doubt against it. But Leibniz, by proclaiming a new law, suddenly roused human reason to revolt; and this law has since become one of those which have offered scholars the greatest contest of understanding. Descartes had estimated the forces of bodies in motion simply by their velocities; Herr von Leibniz, however, set the square of their velocity as their measure. He did not, as one might suppose, put forward this rule only under certain conditions which would still leave some room for the former. No: he denied Descartes's law absolutely and without limitation, and straightway put his own in its place.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0126":["§ 23.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0127":["First Fault in the Leibnizian Measure of Forces.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0128":["There are properly two points which I find to object to in Herr von Leibniz's rule. That with which I shall now deal draws after it no consequences of importance in the matter of living forces. Yet one cannot refrain from remarking it, so that in the case of so great a proposition nothing may be neglected which can free it from all the minor reproaches that might be made against it:\n\n---\n\n\n- 0115: \"Drucke\" rendered \"pressures\"; the long parenthesis kept; the final sentence divided into two for clarity. \"§ 16\" as in the source.\n- 0115: \"in Absicht auf die Stärke\" rendered \"in regard to the strength\"; \"Bemühung/Bestrebung\" as \"exertion/endeavour\" (effort toward self-maintenance).\n- 0117: Divided into several paragraphs and sentences. \"weitläuftiger reizendes\" in the original is evidently corrupt (OCR or print); I read \"etwas Weitläuftiges, Reizendes\" and flag it as doubtful.\n- 0117: \"Erkenntniß\" rendered \"knowledge\"; \"Weltweisheit\" as \"philosophy\"; \"Vergeltung vor\" read as \"recompense for\".\n- 0117: \"dem wahren Nutzen ... hindan zu setzen\" is syntactically irregular (dative for accusative); smoothed as \"set ... above\".\n- 0120: The relative clause \"bey denen ... Vermuthung fremder Absichten\" smoothed with \"among whom\".\n- 0125: \"Empörung\" rendered \"revolt\"; \"Wettstreit des Verstandes\" as \"contest of understanding\"; \"Cartes\" given as Descartes.\n- 0128: Passage ends with a colon, so what follows in the next passage is not translated; \"Stücke\" as \"points\".",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0129":["The Leibnizian measure of forces has always been put forward in this formula: If a body is in actual motion, its force is as the square of its velocity. According to this proposition, therefore, the mark of this measure of force is nothing but actual motion. A body may, however, move actually, and yet its force be no greater than that which it would exert with this initial velocity merely by pressure. I have already proved this in the previous chapter, and I repeat it here. A ball which I push quite gently along a smooth surface at once ceases to move any further when I withdraw my hand. In such a motion, then, the force of the body vanishes at every instant, but is as often restored by a fresh pressure. At the very instant, therefore, in which the body encounters the object, its force is not still its own from the previous motion; no, that motion is already wholly annulled. It possesses only the force which the impelling power imparts to it at the very instant in which it touches the object. We may thus regard it as if it had not moved at all, and as if it exerted its pressure against the resistance merely in a state of rest. Such a body is accordingly not different from one that exerts a dead pressure, and its force is therefore not as the square of its velocity, but as the velocity simply. This, then, is the first limitation which I place upon the Leibnizian law. Leibniz ought not to have given actual motion alone as the mark of living force; it was also necessary to add free motion. For if the motion is not free, the body never has a living force. With this determination, the Leibnizian law, where it is otherwise correct, will have to appear in this formula: A body which is in actual and free motion has a force which is as the square, etc., etc.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0130":["§ 24.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0131":["What an actual motion is.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0132":["I now make the second remark, which will disclose to us the sources of the notorious dispute, and which perhaps also offers the only means of settling it.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0133":["The defenders of the new estimation of living forces still agree with the Cartesians in this, that bodies, when their motion is only at its beginning, possess a force which is as their mere velocity. But as soon as the motion can be called actual, the body, in their opinion, has the square of the velocity as its measure.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0134":["Let us now inquire what an actual motion properly is. For this word was the cause of the defection from Descartes; but perhaps it may also become a cause of reunion.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0135":["A motion is called actual when it is not merely at the point of its beginning, but when, in the course of its duration, a time has elapsed. This elapsed time, which lies between the beginning of the motion and the instant at which the body acts, is what properly makes it possible to call the motion actual.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0136":["But it should be well noted that this time * is not something of fixed and measured magnitude; it is wholly undetermined, and may be determined at pleasure. That is to say, we may assume it as small as we like, if we are to use it to indicate an actual motion. For it is not this or that magnitude of time which properly makes the motion actual; no, it is time in general, be it as small or as great as it may.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0137":["* In the formula of the Leibnizian measure of forces.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0138":["§ 25.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0139":["Second principal defect of the Leibnizian measure of forces.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0140":["Accordingly, the time expended in the motion is the true and sole characteristic of living force; and it alone is that through which living force obtains a measure peculiar to it, as against dead force.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0141":["Let us now represent, by the line A B, the time which elapses from the beginning of the motion until the body encounters an object upon which it acts, the beginning being at A. ** At B, then, the body has a living force; but at the starting-point A it has none, for there it would merely press, with an effort towards motion, against an obstacle standing in its way. Let us, however, go on to argue as follows. First,",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0142":["** Fig. II.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0143":["First, the time A B is such a determination of the body that is at B, whereby a living force is posited in it; and the starting-point A (that is, if I place the body at it) is a determination which is a ground of dead force. Second,",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0144":["Second, if in thought I make this determination, expressed by the line A B, smaller, I place the body nearer to the starting-point; and it is easily understood that if I continued this, the body would at last be found at A itself. Consequently, by being shortened, the determination A B is set ever nearer to the determination in A; for if it did not approach the latter at all, the body could never, by the shortening of the time, even if I carried it on infinitely, attain the point A, which is absurd. The determination of the body at C therefore comes nearer to the conditions of dead force than at B, at D nearer still than at C, and so on, until at A itself it has all the conditions of dead force, and the conditions of living force have wholly vanished. But if, third,",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0145":["Third, if certain determinations which are the cause of a property of a body are gradually changed into other determinations which are the ground of an opposite property, then the property which was a consequence of the former conditions must change along with them, and gradually be transformed into that property which is a consequence of the latter. * Now when I shorten in thought the time A B (which is a condition of a living force at B), this condition of living force is necessarily set nearer to the condition of dead force than it was at B. The body at C must therefore actually have a force which comes nearer to the dead force than that at B, and nearer still if I place it at D. A body, then, which under the condition of elapsed time possesses a living force, does not possess it in any time whatever, however short one may like; no, the time must be determined and definite, for if it were shorter, the body would no longer have this living force. Leibniz's law of the estimation of forces cannot therefore hold; for it ascribes a living force without distinction to bodies that have moved for any length of time at all (which is to say, that are actually moving), whether this time be as short or as long as one pleases. **",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0146":["* According to the rule *posita ratione ponitur rationatum* [when the ground is posited, the grounded is posited].",["- The ids are returned in the required format; the \"Vors\"/\"1ste\", \"2te\", \"3tens\" breaks (0141, 0143, 0144, 0145) are split mid-word in the OCR, so I completed \"Vors[t]\" as \"First\"/\"Second\" and repeated the ordinal at the start of the next passage; the German itself reads \"Vors\" at the end of 0141 and 0143 and 0144 (\"Wenn aber\") leads to \"3tens\".","- 0141: \"Vors\" is a cut-off word (Vors[te], \"first\"); I rendered it as \"First,\" and did not complete the sentence further. Passage 0143 and 0144 end in the same way.","- 0129: \"Kraft … wie das Quadrat\" rendered \"as the square\", i.e. proportional to; \"Bestimmung\" = determination, as in the stated terms.","- 0129: \"Formul\" rendered \"formula\"; \"Bewegungbegriffen\" read as \"Bewegung begriffen\" (OCR slip).","- 0129: \"diese ist schon alle vernichtet\" taken as \"schon ganz vernichtet\" (alle = ganz); rendered \"wholly annulled\".","- 0129: \"Er\" in \"Er hätte … sollen\" is Leibniz; named in the English for clarity.","- 0136: the footnote asterisk after \"Zeit\" is kept in place; the footnote (0137) refers to the time in the Leibnizian formula.","- 0145: the \"Nach der Regel\" Latin tag is kept in Latin in italics, with a bracketed English gloss that I supplied.","- 0143–0144: \"Bestimmung\" and \"Bedingung\" kept distinct (determination / condition); \"Gegenstand\" = object in 0129 and 0141."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0147":["** The substance of this proof is as follows. The time which lies between the beginning of the motion and the moment at which the body strikes can be thought as short as we please, without its being intelligible that the condition of living force should thereby be lost (§ 24). But this shortening is a ground from which it can be understood that, if it were carried on, the body would at last be at the point of departure, where the living force actually vanishes and the condition of dead force takes its place. The diminution of this time is therefore not a ground that withdraws anything from the condition of living force, and yet it is at the same time a ground for doing so. This is self-contradictory.",["- Restated as several sentences, as the method allows; the original runs on with semicolons and colons. \"**\" at the opening is kept as it stands in the text (probably a footnote or editorial sign).","- *lebendige Kraft* = \"living force\", *todte Kraft* = \"dead force\" (fixed throughout; not \"vis viva\" since Kant writes German).","- \"Anfangspunct\" = \"point of departure\" (literally \"initial point\"); \"Augenblick\" = \"moment\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0148":["§ 26.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0149":["Proof of the same from the law of continuity.",["- *Gesetz der Continuität* = \"law of continuity\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0150":["What I have just proved is an exact consequence of the law of continuity, whose wide-ranging utility has perhaps not yet been sufficiently appreciated. Herr von Leibniz, its discoverer, made it a touchstone, and the laws of Descartes failed to stand the test. I hold it to be the greatest proof of its excellence that it almost alone provides a means of properly uncovering the most celebrated law of the whole of mechanics and of showing it in its true form.",["- \"Leibnitz\" modernised to \"Leibniz\". The German \"ihn\" (masculine) refers back to \"Gesetz\" and is rendered \"it\".","- \"das berufenste Gesetze der ganzen Mechanick\" is read as a singular, \"the most celebrated law\"; the plural form \"Gesetze\" is grammatically possible, so the reading is a judgment.","- \"Cartes\" = Descartes."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0151":["We need only direct our attention to the manner in which Herr von Leibniz made use of this principle against Descartes, and we shall readily perceive how it must be applied here. He proves that the rule which holds when a body strikes against one that is in motion must also remain valid when it runs against one that is at rest, since rest is not different from a very small motion. What holds when unequal bodies run against one another must also hold when the bodies are equal, since a very small inequality may be confused with equality.",["- \"Grundsatz\" = \"principle\". \"Gegen einen stößt\" is rendered \"strikes against one\", with \"body\" understood; the supplied noun is not bracketed since it is plainly implied."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0152":["In this way I too argue: what holds in general when a body has been in motion for some time must also hold when the motion is only at its beginning, since a very small duration of motion is not different from the mere beginning of it, or the two may fitly be confused. From this I infer that if the body in general has a living force when it has been in motion for some time (be that as short as one likes), it must also have it when it has only begun to move. For it is all one whether it is just beginning, or has already continued to move for some exceedingly small time. And so I conclude that, since this absurdity follows from the Leibnizian law of the estimation of forces, namely that the force would be living at the very point of departure of the motion, the law cannot be accepted.",["- \"Kräften-Schätzung\" = \"estimation of forces\". \"Anfangspuncte der Bewegung\" = \"point of departure of the motion\".","- The final clause, \"so könne man ihm nicht beypflichten\", is in indirect speech after \"schließe ich\"; rendered as a plain conclusion for clarity."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0153":["It is easy to see how strongly the understanding (*Verstand*) resists this law when it is set before it in the proper light of distinctness. It is impossible to persuade oneself that a body which at the point A has a dead force should have a living force infinitely many times greater than the dead force, if it has merely moved away from this point by an imperceptibly small line. This leap of thought is too abrupt; there is no path that leads us from the one determination (*Bestimmung*) to the other.",["- \"wie die todte\" = \"than the dead force\" (older \"wie\" for \"als\").","- \"Sprung der Gedanken\" = \"leap of thought\", an allusion to the *lex continui*; \"Bestimmung\" = determination."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0154":["§ 27.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0155":["The time that has elapsed in the motion, and therefore also the actuality of the motion, is not the true condition under which a living force belongs to the body.",["- \"Würklichkeit\" = \"actuality\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0156":["Let us attend well to what follows from this. The elapsed time, if it is presented as undetermined, cannot be a condition of living force; this I have proved above. But even if it is presented as determined and limited to a certain magnitude, it still cannot furnish the proper condition of living force; and this I now prove in the following way.",["- \"undeterminirt / determinirt\" = \"undetermined / determined\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0157":["Suppose it could be proved that a body having this velocity will have a living force after one minute, and that this minute is the condition under which this force belongs to it. Then, if the magnitude of this time were doubled, everything in the body would be doubled which, taken singly, had previously already established a living force in it. But the magnitude of the first minute added a new dimension to the force of the body (*per hypothesin*). Therefore the magnitude of two minutes, since it contains within itself doubled the conditions which the first contained, will add one dimension more to the force of the body. The body, then, which continues its motion freely, will indeed have at the point of departure only a force of one dimension, and after the lapse of one minute a force of two dimensions; but in the second minute its force has three dimensions, in the third four, in the fourth five, and so on. That is to say, in uniform motion its force will have as its measure now the velocity simply, now the square of it, now the cube, now the biquadrate, and so forth. These are such extravagances as no one will undertake to defend.",["- The sentence beginning \"so würde, wenn…\" is restated in several sentences. \"Abmessungen\" and \"Dimension\" are both rendered \"dimension(s)\". *per hypothesin* kept in Latin, italicised.","- \"Quadratoquadrat\" = \"biquadrate\" (fourth power). \"in ihn … setzte\" is read as \"established in it\".","- The sentence \"Es setzte aber … hinzu\" is rendered \"added\", past tense, as in the German's hypothetical sense."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0158":["We need not doubt the correctness of these inferences. For if it is demanded that a time of determinate magnitude, elapsing from the beginning of a body's motion up to a certain point, should wholly contain within itself the conditions of living force, it cannot be denied that in a time twice as great there would also be twice as much of these conditions, since time has no other determinations than its magnitude. And if, therefore, a single time is the sufficient ground for bringing a new dimension into the force of a body, a double time will establish two such dimensions (according to the rule: *rationata sunt in proportione rationum suarum*). We may add this further point. Time could be a condition of living force only because the body, in the course of it, recedes from the condition of dead force, which consists in the initial moment; and this time must have a determinate magnitude because in less time the body would not have receded sufficiently from the determinations of dead force as the magnitude of a living force requires. Since, however, in a greater time it recedes ever further from the initial moment, that is, from the condition of dead force, the force of the body, the longer it moves, would have to acquire more and more dimensions without end, even with its uniform velocity; which is absurd.",["- Latin rule kept in italics: \"things grounded are in proportion to their grounds.\"","- \"Anfangsaugenblick\" = \"initial moment\". \"als es die Größe … erfordert\" is smoothed slightly in English."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0159":["Thus, in the first place, the absence [sic: *Abwesenheit*] of the actuality of motion is not the true and proper condition which ascribes to the force of a body the estimation by simple velocity.",["- Doubtful reading: \"Abwesenheit der Würklichkeit der Bewegung\" (\"absence of the actuality of motion\") seems at odds with the argument, which concerns the *presence* of actuality (cf. § 27 heading); possibly a misprint for \"Würklichkeit\" alone or \"Anwesenheit\". Translated as it stands and flagged with [sic].","- \"schlechte Geschwindigkeit\" = \"simple velocity\" (*schlecht* in the old sense \"plain, simple\")."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0160":["Secondly, neither the actuality of motion in general, together with the general and indeterminate consideration of the elapsed time connected with it, nor the determinate and fixed magnitude of the time, is a sufficient ground of living force and of its estimation by the square.",["- \"undgesetzte\" is an OCR or print slip; read as \"und gesetzte\" (\"and fixed/posited\")."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0161":["§ 28.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0162":["Mathematics cannot prove living forces.",["- \"Die Mathematik kan die lebendigen Kräfte nicht erweisen\": \"erweisen\" = \"prove\", \"demonstrate\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0163":["From this consideration we wish to draw two consequences of importance.",["- Passage breaks off here; the two consequences follow in the next passage."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0164":["The first is this: that mathematics can never offer any proofs in favour of living forces, and that a force estimated in this way, even if it otherwise holds good, is at least outside the domain of mathematical consideration. Everyone knows that, when in this science we seek to estimate the force of a body moved with a certain velocity, we are not bound to any determinate moment of the time that has elapsed in the motion; in respect of this limitation, everything is indeterminate and indifferent. The estimation of the force of moving bodies which mathematics supplies is therefore of such a kind that it extends to all motions whatever, however short the time that has elapsed in them may be, and that it sets us no limits in this respect. But an estimation of this kind applies also to the motion of bodies which is at its beginning (§§ 25, 26), which is therefore dead, and which has simple velocity for its measure. And since living forces cannot be comprised together with dead forces under one and the same estimation, it is easily seen that the former must be wholly excluded from mathematical consideration.",["- The opening \"Die erste\" refers back to the first of two consequences; the second is announced in 0168.","- \"Schätzung\" rendered \"estimation\"; \"schlechte Geschwindigkeit\" as \"simple velocity\" (i.e. velocity alone, without the square).","- The last clause reads \"seyn\" for \"sey\" (subjunctive of indirect statement); I have rendered it \"must be\", which the sense requires.","- Restated slightly: \"und daß sie uns hierinn gar keine Grenzen setzet\" is kept as a coordinate clause."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0165":["Besides, mathematics considers in the motion of a body nothing but the velocity, the mass, and perhaps also the time, if one wished to include it. Velocity is never a ground of living force; for if the body, according to the opinion of the Leibnizians, possessed a living force, it could not have it at every moment of its motion. There would be a time after the beginning of the motion in which it did not yet have it, although all the velocity was already present in it (§§ 25, 26). Mass is still less a ground of it. Finally, we have proved the same of time also. Hence the motion of any body, taken by itself, contains nothing which in a mathematical consideration would indicate a living force inhering in it. Now all inferences that are made as to what a body in motion does must be derived from the *notions* contained in the consideration of velocity, mass and time. If, then, they are correctly drawn out, they will yield no consequences that establish living forces. And if it seems that they render this service, we should not trust the appearance; for the consequences would then contain more than the principles comprehend, that is, the *rationatum* would be greater than its *ratio*.",["- Divided the long penultimate sentence (\"Weil nun alle Schlüsse...\") into two for clarity.","- \"Notionen\" kept as *notions*, Kant's own Latinate word, rather than \"concepts\" (Begriffe).","- \"Wenn es scheinet\" rendered \"if it seems\", and \"Schein\" as \"appearance\" in the sense of semblance, not *Erscheinung*."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0166":["After such manifold and great efforts as the geometers of these two centuries have made to settle the dispute between Descartes and Herr von Leibniz by the doctrines of mathematics, it seems very strange that I should begin by denying to this science the power to decide it. For some time, indeed, there has been dispute whether this science is favourable to Descartes's laws or whether it defends the party of Herr von Leibniz. But in this division everyone is agreed on one point: that, in order properly to resolve the question of the estimation of forces, we must leave it to the pronouncement of mathematics. It is surprising enough that such great reasoners should have strayed on to such by-paths without perceiving, or even thinking to ask, whether this is the road that can lead them to possession of the truth they have been tracking. Here, however, I think I find grounds which compel me to dismiss all this wonder to the winds; and whither else should I turn, once mathematics has pronounced?",["- \"Zwiespalt\" rendered \"division\" (of opinion); \"Schlußkünstler\" as \"reasoners\".","- The final question is obscure: \"nach ihrem Ausspruche\" I take to refer to mathematics' verdict, which Kant claims favours Descartes (cf. 0167–0168). The reading is doubtful.","- \"Kräftenschätzung\" is rendered \"estimation of forces\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0167":["By its very nature mathematics already confirms the laws of Descartes.",["- Evidently a marginal heading or summary line for the following section."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0168":["The second consequence which I draw from the foregoing considerations is this: that the grounds of mathematics, so far from being favourable to living forces, will always rather confirm the laws of Descartes. This must already be clear from the propositions of this section, and I can add the following. Mathematical magnitudes, lines, surfaces and so forth, have precisely the same properties when they are ever so small as when they are of whatever size one pleases. Hence from the smallest mathematical magnitudes, from the smallest parallelogram, from the fall of a body through the smallest line, the same properties and consequences must be derivable as from the largest of these kinds. Now if a line which indicates a motion as it is immediately after the beginning has precisely the same determinations and properties, and also the same consequences, as a line which indicates a motion long after the beginning, then the force which is obtained in a mathematical consideration of the motion of a body will never have other properties than the force which is present in the body in the smallest time, that is, in an infinitely small time, from the initial moment. Since this is a dead force, and so has for its measure simple velocity, all and every motion mathematically considered will yield no estimation other than that according to mere velocity alone.",["- \"§phi\" is an OCR/typographical form of \"§\"; rendered \"section\".","- \"wer weiß was vor eine Größe\" rendered \"of whatever size one pleases\"; the idiom is colloquial.","- Split the first long sentence after \"§phi\" and the middle one into separate sentences; \"Bestimmungen\" = determinations."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0169":["§ 29.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0170":["We know, therefore, even before we enter upon a closer investigation of the matter, that the followers of Leibniz, because they wish to defend themselves with weapons far removed from the nature of their cause, will succumb in the notorious dispute against Descartes. After this general consideration we shall examine in particular the proofs which Leibniz's party has chiefly employed in this dispute.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0171":["Herr von Leibniz was first led to his opinion by what is observed in the fall of bodies through their gravity. But it was a wrongly applied principle of Descartes that led him into an error which has perhaps since become the most plausible that has ever crept into human reason. He laid down, namely, the following proposition: the same force is needed to raise a body of four pounds one foot high as to raise a body of one pound four feet.",["- \"Schuh\" rendered \"foot\" (the unit of length); \"scheinbarste\" as \"most plausible\" (most specious in appearance)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0172":["§ 30.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0173":["The proposition which first led Herr von Leibniz to living forces.",["- Heading; no finite verb in the German."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0174":["Since he appeals to the assent of all the mechanicians of his time, it seems to me that he inferred this proposition from a rule of Descartes which the latter used to explain the nature of the lever. Descartes assumed that the weights suspended from a lever traverse the infinitely small spaces which can be described at their distances from the fulcrum. Now two bodies are in equilibrium when these spaces are inversely as the weights of the bodies; and so, Leibniz concluded, no more force is needed to raise a body of one pound to a height of four than to raise another whose mass is four to a single height. It is easily seen that this conclusion follows from Descartes's fundamental rule only if the times of the motion are equal. For in the steelyard the times in which the weights would traverse their infinitely small spaces are equal to one another. Herr von Leibniz overlooked this condition, and inferred also to motion in times which are not equal to one another.",["- \"Ruhepunct\" rendered \"fulcrum\"; \"Schnellwaage\" as \"steelyard\".","- \"Gewichter\" (plural of Gewicht) rendered \"weights\"; \"schloß auch auf\" as \"inferred also to\".","- \"Höhe vier\" kept as \"a height of four\" (units not stated)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0175":["§ 31.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0176":["Herr Hermann's proof that the forces are as the heights which they can attain by means of them.",["- Heading. \"durch dieselben\" is ambiguous (it should refer to the forces); rendered \"by means of them\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0177":["The defenders of this man seem to have noticed the objection which might be raised against them on the score of time. They have accordingly tried so to arrange their proofs as if the difference of time were to be regarded as of no account whatever in the force which bodies acquire through falling.",["- \"Vor nichts anzusehen\" rendered \"of no account\"; \"Kraft\" as \"force\" (the reading of the physical term in this early work).","- \"Dieses Mannes\" refers to Herr Herrmann (named in the next passage); the antecedent lies outside this passage."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0178":["Let AB* be the infinite spring, which represents the gravity that pursues the body during its fall from A to B. Then, says Herr Herrmann, gravity will impart to the body an equal pressure at each point of the space. These pressures he represents by the lines AC, DE, BF, and so on, which together make up the rectangle AF. In his opinion, therefore, when the body has reached the point B it has a force equal to the sum of all these pressures, that is, to the rectangle AF. Hence the force at D is to the force at B as the rectangle AE is to the rectangle AF, that is, as the space traversed AD is to the space AB, and consequently as the squares of the velocities at D and B.",["- Long second-to-last sentence kept intact; \"u. s w.\" rendered \"and so on\".","- The asterisk after AB is Kant's footnote marker (Fig. III), kept as in the source.","- \"Druck/Drucke\" rendered \"pressure(s)\"; \"Rektangulum\" as \"rectangle\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0179":["* Fig. III.",["- Footnote to the preceding passage; kept as a footnote."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0180":["Such is Herr Herrmann's inference, in that he maintains that the effect which gravity produces in a body falling freely is determined by the space which the body traverses in falling.",["- \"richte sich nach\" rendered \"is determined by\" (literally, \"is regulated by\")."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0181":["The Cartesians, on the other hand, maintain that the effect of gravity is proportional, not to the spaces traversed while the motion is retarded, but to the times in which the body either falls or rises again. I shall now give a proof which will place the opinion of the Cartesians beyond doubt, and from which it will at the same time be seen wherein Herr Herrmann's apparent proof is at fault.",["- \"aufgehaltener Bewegung\" rendered \"retarded\" [literally, \"checked\" or \"held back\"]; the sense is the motion of a body rising against gravity.","- \"Scheinbare\" rendered \"apparent\" in the sense of \"seeming\", not \"visible\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0182":["§ 32.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0183":["Proof which refutes Herr Herrmann's case.",["- \"Fall\" here means \"case\" (argument), not a physical fall; the pun with the preceding discussion of falling is probably intended."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0184":["Just as much force is needed to keep a single one of five equally tensed springs* A, B, C, D, E compressed for one second as to compress all five successively within that same time. For divide the second, as the time during which the body M holds the spring A compressed, into five equal parts. Instead of M pressing upon the spring A through all five parts of the second, let us suppose that it presses the spring A only in the first part of the second, and that in the second part of the second another spring B, which has the same degree of tension, is substituted for the spring A. There will then be no difference to be found in the force which M requires for pressing, as a result of this exchange. For the springs B and A are in all respects perfectly alike, and it is therefore all one whether in the second fifth of the second the same spring A or the spring B is pressed. Equally, it is all one whether M in the third part of the second tenses the third spring C, or whether in this part of the time it still pressed upon the preceding spring B; for one spring can be put in the place of another, since they are in no way different. The body M therefore expends as much force in holding the single spring A compressed for a whole second as it needs to tense five such springs successively within that same time. The same can be said however much we may increase the number of springs, even to infinity, provided only that the time of pressure is equal. It is therefore not the number of springs compressed by which the force of the body that tenses them all is measured; the time of pressing is the proper measure.",["- \"Secunde\" rendered \"second\" (unit of time); \"Secundtheile\" as \"fifth of the second\".","- \"Zugedrückt\" rendered \"compressed\"; \"gespannt/spannen\" as \"tensed/tense\".","- Kant's \"weil sie nichts unterschieden seyn\" (a slip for \"sind\") rendered plainly; \"an der andern Stelle\" read as \"in the place of another\".","- The asterisk after \"springs\" is the footnote marker (Fig. IV).","- \"Wornach ... abgemessen wird\" rendered \"by which ... is measured\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0185":["* Fig. IV.",["- Footnote to the preceding passage."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0186":["Now let us accept the comparison which Herr Herrmann draws between the effect of the springs and the pressure of gravity. We shall then find that the time during which the force of the body can resist gravity, and not the space traversed, is that by which the whole effect of the body must be estimated.",["- \"Würkung\" rendered \"effect\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0187":["This, then, is the first trial, which, as I believe, confirms what I said above, namely that the opinion of Descartes surpasses the law of Herr von Leibnitz in mathematical proofs.",["- \"Versuch\" rendered \"trial\" [attempt]; \"übertreffe\" as \"surpasses\" in the sense of outweighing."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0188":["§ 33.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0189":["The error of the Cartesians in maintaining the same thing.",["- The heading is elliptical; \"eben derselben Sache\" is taken to refer to the thesis that force is estimated by time."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0190":["In the dispute of the Cartesians against the defenders of living forces, which the Marquise du Châtelet has conducted with great eloquence, I find that the former also made use of the difference of time in order to deprive the Leibnitzians' inferences from the fall of bodies of their force. But from what she cites out of Herr von Mairan's writing against the new estimation of forces, I see that the true advantage which he could have drawn from the difference of time was unknown to him. This advantage, which I believe I pointed out in the preceding section, is certainly so simple and clear that one must wonder how it was possible not to perceive it in so bright a light of understanding.",["- Kant writes \"Chastelet\"; I give the usual modern spelling \"Châtelet\" [Marquise du Châtelet]. \"Frau Marquisin\" rendered \"Marquise\".","- Last long sentence divided into two; \"welcher\" is taken to refer to \"Vortheil\".","- \"Lebendige Kräfte\" rendered \"living forces\" (*vis viva*); \"Verstand\" as \"understanding\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0191":["It is certainly strange how far these men have gone astray while following a true law of nature, namely that the force which gravity takes from a body is proportional to the time and not to the space. Having gone so far wrong as to grant to the Leibnitzians that a body with double velocity can produce fourfold effect, and having, I say, so ruined their case, they are compelled to save themselves by a rather poor evasion, namely that the body does indeed produce a fourfold effect, but only in double the time. They therefore insist with extraordinary earnestness that the forces of two bodies must be estimated by the effects which they produce in equal times, and that no regard whatever is to be paid to what they may accomplish in unequal times. This evasion has been met with infinite clearness, and I cannot comprehend how it was possible still to resist the constraint of truth.",["- \"Daß ... Gesetze\" taken as an appositive to \"ein wahres Gesetze der Natur\"; \"Gesetze\" is an old-form dative.","- \"sich sich\" in the German is a printer's duplication and is rendered once.","- \"Ausflucht\" rendered \"evasion\"; \"Zwange der Wahrheit\" as \"constraint of truth\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0192":["But we also see from this that it is really only the fallacies of the Cartesians which make Leibniz's party triumph, and that they do not lose the dispute through any weakness of their cause. They would always retain the upper hand if they would take up the right weapons which the nature of the matter itself offers them.",["- \"Fehlschlüsse\" rendered \"fallacies\"; \"möchten\" taken as \"were to\", conditional."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0193":["§ 34.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0194":["A doubt of Herr Lichtscheid is removed.",["- \"Lichtscheid\" is Kant's spelling of the name (Ferdinand Lichtscheid); kept as printed."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0195":["I have proved that the effects which gravity exerts, and the resistance which it offers in the body's ascent, are proportional to the time which bodies spend in the motion. But I recall a case which is perhaps plausible enough to make this proposition doubtful to some. Herr Lichtscheid remarks in the *Acta Eruditorum* that if a pendulum* is let fall from D in such a way that the thread catches on the obstacle E, so that, in rising again from B to C, it describes a smaller circle, it nevertheless regains, by virtue of the velocity acquired in B, the height CF, which is equal to the height DG from which it fell. Now the time which the pendulum spends in its fall through the arc DB is longer than the time in which it rises again to C. Hence gravity has acted on the pendulum longer in the former case than in the latter. One would now think that, if what I proved earlier is true, namely that gravity produces a greater effect in greater times, the body must have received in B a greater velocity than gravity is able to take from it again in the motion from B to C. By means of this velocity it would therefore have to be capable of swinging up still higher than the point C; and this, according to the proofs of Herr Lichtscheid, is false.",["- The sentence beginning \"Man sollte nun denken\" is kept as one but with the \"daß\" clause set off by commas; \"dorten ... hier\" rendered \"in the former case ... in the latter\".","- The asterisk after \"pendulum\" is Kant's footnote marker (see next passage, Fig. V); \"gnug\" = \"genug\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0196":["* Fig. V.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0197":["But if we consider only that the thread AB opposes the body more strongly as it moves from D to B, and hinders its fall more by its own gravity, than the thread EB or EC does in the fall from C to B, it is easy to see that the element of force which accumulates and gathers in the body at every moment of its descent from D to B is smaller than the elementary force which gravity, on the contrary, brings into the body at each moment when it sinks from C to B. For since it makes no difference whether a body fastened to a thread is compelled by the check A to run through the arc DB or CB, or whether it rolls freely down a surface curved in just the same way, BD CB, we may represent the fall of which we speak as actually taking place on two such concave surfaces joined to one another. Now the surface DB is inclined more steeply to the horizontal line than the other, CB. Hence on the former the body is indeed exposed longer to the impulses of gravity than on the latter; but in return the surface hinders a greater part of the gravity, which strives to incorporate itself in the body, than the other, CB, does.",["- The long first sentence is kept whole, but \"so lässet sich auch leicht begreifen\" is rendered \"it is easy to see\".","- \"Zurückhalt A\" rendered \"check A\"; the letter A is as in the text (possibly an OCR/edition confusion with E, which appears elsewhere; left as printed).","- \"BD CB\" kept as printed; the reading is doubtful.","- \"holen\" read as \"hohlen\" (concave)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0198":["I might have been spared the solution of this objection, since the followers of Herr von Leibniz seem to have perceived its weakness themselves, for I find nowhere that they have made use of it. But Herr von Leibniz, who had been chosen by Herr Lichtscheid as judge of his treatise, gave it his laudable approval, and it is his authority which could lend it some weight.",["- \"Leibnitz\" is rendered \"Leibniz\"; \"Lichtscheiden\" is the dative form of Lichtscheid."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0199":["§ 35.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0200":["Before I leave the subject of the fall of bodies through their gravity, I will give the defenders of the living forces one more case to resolve, which, it seems to me, should suffice to show that the consideration of time cannot possibly be excluded from the estimation of the force which gravity brings into a body, as Herr von Leibniz and his defenders have hitherto sought to persuade us.",["- *lebendige Kräfte* rendered \"living forces\" (cf. *vis viva*)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0201":["§ 36.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0202":["A new case which shows that in the estimation of the force arising through gravity, time must necessarily be taken into account.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0203":["The case is as follows. In the manner customary with Cartesians and Leibnizians, I represent the pressures of gravity, which are communicated to a body from the height* aB to the horizontal line bc, by the infinite number of sheet-metal springs AB, CD, EF, GH. Further, I place a body m on the inclined plane ac, and let another, l, fall freely from a to b. How will the Leibnizians now estimate the force of the body m, which is driven down the inclined plane ac by the pressure of the springs, at the end of this oblique fall in c? They can do no other than give as the measure the product of the number of springs which drive the body from a to c into the force which each spring impresses upon it in the direction ac; for their system requires this, as we saw from the case of Herr Hermann, § 31. In the same way they will be obliged to estimate the force found in the other body l, which falls freely from a to b, by the product of the number of springs by which it was driven onward into the intensity with which each of them has pushed it. But the number of springs is the same on both sides, both through the inclined plane ac and through the height ab. Hence only the strength of the force which each spring brings into its body in the two cases remains as the true measure of the forces acquired by the bodies l and m in b and c. This strength, with which each of the sheet-metal springs presses the body m in the direction of the inclined plane ac, is related to the intensity of the pressure of these same springs on the body l in the direction of its motion ab, as ab to ac, as the first elements of mechanics teach us. Therefore the force which the body l has at the end of the perpendicular fall in b will likewise be related to the force which m has at the end of the oblique fall in c as ac to ab; which is absurd, for both bodies have equal velocities in b and c, and therefore also equal forces.",["- Kant's \"durch das Factum\" is rendered \"by the product\" (Latin *factum* = product); \"zu schätzen genöthiget\" lacks \"sind\"; I supplied \"they will be obliged\".","- The ratio \"ab to ac\" then \"ac to ab\" is as in the text; the inversion is Kant's, not corrected.","- Footnote marker * refers to Fig. VI."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0204":["* Fig. VI.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0205":["The Cartesians escape this objection by bringing in time. For although each spring brings less force into the body m on the inclined plane ac (because a part is consumed by the resistance of the plane), these springs in return act upon the body m much longer than upon the body l, which is exposed to their pressure for a much shorter time.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0206":["§ 37.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0207":["Having proved that the consideration of bodies falling through gravity is in no way advantageous to the living forces, it is time to consider another kind of proofs, on which the defenders of the living forces have at all times greatly prided themselves. These are the proofs which the doctrine of the motion of elastic bodies seems to offer them.",["- \"sich zu gute thun auf\" rendered \"prided themselves on\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0208":["§ 38.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0209":["In the schism to which Herr von Leibniz's estimation of forces in the world has given rise, there have arisen among the geometers as many delusions and wrong turnings as one would scarcely expect among such great masters of inference. The accounts of all the incidents of this notorious dispute which will be preserved for us will one day occupy a very useful place in the history of the human understanding. No consideration is more triumphant over the conceit of those who so greatly exalt the correctness of our inferences of reason than such seductions, which the most acute masters of geometry were unable to escape in an inquiry that ought, more than any other, to have afforded them distinctness and conviction.",["- *Kräftenschätzung* rendered \"estimation of forces\"; *Schlußkünstlern* as \"masters of inference\"; *Vernunftschlüsse* as \"inferences of reason\".","- Third sentence is long and tangled; kept as one sentence, but \"which ought... to have afforded\" resolves *die ihnen vor andern ... hätte gewähren sollen* with *Untersuchung* as antecedent.","- *Trennung* taken as \"schism\" (the division among the geometers). *aufbehalten wird* (sic) read as \"will be preserved\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0210":["It would have been impossible to stray into such wrong turnings if the Leibnizians had been willing to take the trouble of directing their attention to the construction of those proofs themselves, which they now regard as invincible demonstrations in favour of living forces.",["- *lebendige Kräfte* rendered \"living forces\" (*vis viva*).","- *Beweisthümer* rendered \"demonstrations\" to distinguish from *Beweise* (\"proofs\")."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0211":["§ 39.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0212":["The sum of all the proofs drawn from the motion of elastic bodies.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0213":["Almost all the proofs, at least the most plausible of those which have been borrowed in favour of living forces from the motion of elastic bodies by impact, have arisen in the following way. The force which is present in the bodies after the impact has been compared with the force before the collision. The former has been found greater than the latter when both are estimated by the product of the mass into the velocity; a perfect equality appeared only when, instead of the simple velocity, the square of it was put. From this the Leibnizians have concluded that an elastic body would never be capable of imparting to the body which it strikes as much motion as actually occurs, if its force were merely as its velocity; for by this measure the cause would always be smaller than the effect produced.",["- Divided the second German sentence at the semicolon-like *allein*; the rest is kept close to the German.","- *schlechte Geschwindigkeit* / *schlechthin* rendered \"simple\" / \"merely\" (the unsquared velocity). *Stoß* = \"impact\", *Anstoß* = \"collision\".","- *Würkung* = \"effect\"; *Ursach* = \"cause\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0214":["§ 40.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0215":["The Leibnizians refute their own inferences through their own mechanical systems of doctrine.",["- *Lehrgebäude* rendered \"systems of doctrine\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0216":["This inference is completely refuted by the propositions of the very persons who have made use of it. I will not cite the mechanical discoveries of Wren, Wallis, Huygens and others. Herr Regierungsrat and Freiherr von Wolff shall be my authority. Let the reader consult his *Mechanics*, which is in everyone's hands; he will find in it proofs which leave no further doubt that elastic bodies, in complete conformity with the law of the equality of effects and cause, impart all their motions to other bodies, without our needing to posit in them any other force than mere velocity. I may add that one need not know living forces at all, not even by name, without this being in the least a hindrance to recognising that from the force of a perfectly elastic body, in its running against other bodies of like kind, there will flow just those motions which each person derives from it. Is it not strange, after a geometrical proof in which the force estimated according to mere velocity has been found sufficient to derive from it a certain quantity of motion in other bodies (I say, after such a proof), still to let the thought enter one's mind that this force is not great enough for the purpose? Does this not mean retracting everything that has once been proved in all strictness, and that merely on account of a slight appearance to the contrary? I beg those who read these pages merely to hold the *Mechanics* I have cited alongside this; they can feel nothing but the greatest conviction that they have no need whatever of a concept of the estimation according to the square, in order to find, in all strictness, those consequences and motions which are wont to be ascribed to perfectly elastic bodies. Let us then not allow ourselves to be led from this footpath by any seductions. For what is found true in a geometrical proof will remain true to all eternity.",["- Kant writes \"Wolf\"; I give \"Wolff\" for the philosopher Christian Wolff. *Regierungs-Rath* kept as the German title.","- *federharten Körper* (\"spring-hard\") rendered \"perfectly elastic bodies\"; *Anlaufe* as \"running against\".","- The \"Mechanik\" is Wolff's; italicised as a title. \"Anscheinung\" rendered \"appearance\" in the ordinary sense (not Kant's later *Erscheinung*).","- Fifth-from-last question: parenthesis \"(I say...)\" kept from Kant's *ich sage*; sentence is long but intelligible."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0217":["§ 41.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0218":["The case of Herr Hermann concerning the impact of three elastic bodies.",["- *Herrmann* = Jakob Hermann; spelled \"Hermann\" in English."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0219":["Let us show in a particular case what we have proved in general. In the treatise which he composed in defence of living forces, Herr Hermann has a body * A, whose mass is 1 and whose velocity is 2, strike, upon a perfectly smooth surface, a sphere B, which is at rest and whose mass is 3; and afterwards, as A rebounds from the sphere B and returns with one degree of velocity, [strike] a sphere C, whose mass is 1. The sphere A will impart to the sphere B one degree of velocity, and to the body C likewise one, and then it will find itself at rest. Herr Hermann concludes from this that, if forces were only as the velocities, A before the impact would have a force as 2, but after the impact there would be in the bodies B and C together a fourfold force, which seems to him absurd.",["- Supplied \"[strike]\" to carry the construction of *lässet ... stoßen* across the long interposed clause.","- The asterisk refers to the footnote marker \"Fig. VII.\"; kept in place.","- Restated Kant's sequence of events lightly; the figures are as in the German (the arithmetic is Kant's report of Hermann; not corrected)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0220":["* Fig. VII.",["- Footnote: reference to the figure (Fig. 7)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0221":["We shall inquire how the body A, with a force as 2, can impart to the bodies B and C a fourfold force without a miracle, that is, without our needing to summon the living forces to our aid. Let the elastic force of the body ∗∗ A, which becomes active through the impact, be represented by the spring AD, and the elasticity of the sphere B by the spring DB.\n\nWe know from the first principles of mechanics that, by means of the springs, the body A goes on imparting new pressures and forces to the sphere B until B and A move on with equal velocities. This occurs when the velocity of these bodies is to the velocity of the sphere A before the impact as the mass A is to the sum of the two masses A and B together. In the present case, that is, it occurs when they move on with a velocity of ½ in the direction BE. No one denies that here the effect is still found proportional to the force estimated according to velocity.\n\nBut let us also inquire what happens to the springs AD and DB while the body A acts upon the sphere B by means of them. The spring AD must exert at the point D just as much force against the spring DB as the latter is to impress upon the body B, and the sphere B resists the action upon it just as strongly. It is therefore clear that the spring DB, through the exertion of the other spring, will be compressed with just the same degree of force as it imparts to the sphere B. In the same way the sphere A will compress its spring AD with just the same degree as that with which the spring acts at the point D upon the spring DB. For the latter spring presses back against the spring AD just as strongly as this acts upon it, and therefore just as strongly as the sphere A strives to compress its own spring.\n\nNow the force with which the spring DB is tensed is equal to the resistance of the sphere B, and so also to the force which this sphere thereby receives; and the force of compression of the spring AD is again equal to that force. Both are therefore as great as the force which the body B has here received, that is, the force with which it moves with a mass as 3 and a velocity of ½ degree. When, therefore, these two springs fly open, the spring DB gives the sphere B a velocity equal to what it had before the springing open, namely ½. The spring AD gives the body A, since it has three times less mass than B, three times as much velocity, namely 1 + ½ degrees; for if the forces are equal, the velocities are in inverse proportion to the masses, *per hypothesin*.\n\nThe sphere B thus has, from the impact of the body A and afterwards from the springing open of its spring, a total velocity of 1 degree in the direction BE. As for the sphere A, the velocity of ½ which still remained in it after the impact in the direction AE must be subtracted from the velocity which the springing open of the spring imparted to it in the direction AC. It therefore likewise receives one degree of velocity, with which it moves on in the direction AC. ∗ This is precisely the case which Herr Herrmann held it impossible to explain by the Cartesian law.",["- Restated the long opening sentences as several; \"Anlauf\" rendered \"impact\", as with \"Stoß\" (both appear; the German varies).","- \"Würkung\" = \"effect\" or \"action\" as the sense requires; \"lebendige Kräfte\" = \"living forces\".","- \"1 + ½\" kept as in the German; \"Feder\", \"Kugel\" = spring, sphere.","- The German asterisks mark footnotes (the second ∗ refers to the footnote at 0223, the ∗∗ to the figure note at 0222); I print them as ∗ to avoid confusion with markup.","- Supplied \"that is\" and \"It therefore likewise\" for the German \"d. i.\" and the participial construction; the last sentence was divided for clarity."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0222":["∗∗ Fig. VIII.",["- Figure caption (the footnote to ∗∗ in the preceding passage)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0223":["∗ I do not bring the body C into this, for since its velocity and mass do not differ in anything from the mass and velocity of the sphere B, it is introduced by Herr Herrmann, needlessly, in place of the body B.",["- Footnote to ∗ in 0221."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0224":["I conclude from this that the body A, with 2 degrees of velocity and also with 2 degrees of force, can perfectly well produce the effect which Herr Herrmann has sought to deny it; and that the law of the equality of causes and effects is violated if one maintains that it had 4 degrees of force and yet accomplished only as much as it could have accomplished with 2.",["- \"Könne\" and \"verletze\" are subjunctives of indirect statement; rendered as ordinary statements within the conclusion."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0225":["§ 42.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0226":["The ground of the error in Herr Herrmann's inference.",["- \"Schlußrede\" rendered \"inference\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0227":["At the moment in which even inelastic bodies collide, more force is in exercise than there was before the impact.",["- Section heading/summary; \"Ausübung\" = \"exercise\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0228":["We shall now look in Herr Herrmann's inference for the precise point of falsity, which is also to be found almost everywhere that elastic bodies have been used in support of the living forces. The inference has been this: the forces of the bodies after the impact must be equal to the force before it, for the effects are as great as the causes, which have exhausted themselves in producing them. From this I see that they have held the state and magnitude of the force after the impact to be solely and entirely an effect of the force which was present in the impinging body before the collision. This is the false step whose consequences we have seen.\n\nFor the motions which properly, and in a complete manner, derive from the force of the impinging body A amount to no more than this: that A and B, at the time when the spring was compressed, both moved on together with a velocity of ½. The compression of the spring was not so much a particular effect of the force with which A advanced against B as rather a consequence of the force of inertia of both bodies. For B could not acquire the force 1 + ½ without reacting just as strongly against the pressing spring DB; and the spring AD could therefore impart no force to B unless the state of equality of pressure and counter-pressure had at the same time tensed the spring BD. Further, the body A could not press the spring DB by means of its spring AD without this spring being thereby tensed to an equal degree of intensity.\n\nWe need not wonder that in this way two quite new forces come into nature, which were not previously present in A alone. This in fact always happens when even an inelastic body acts upon another; only in that case the consequences of this new force are not preserved, as they are with perfectly elastic bodies, but are lost. For at the moment in which A acts with the force *x* upon B, B not only receives this force in the direction BE, but at the same time acts back upon A with the intensity *x*. There are thus, in the first place, 2*x* present in nature: namely *x* for the pressure of the sphere A against B, and likewise *x* for the counter-pressure of the sphere B; and secondly a further *x*, as the force which passes over from A into B in the direction BE.\n\nThe first two powers are, in the collision of elastic bodies, employed in tensing two springs, which afterwards, when they fly open, communicate their forces to the bodies. Elastic bodies are therefore those machines of nature which are so contrived as to preserve the whole magnitude of force that is present in nature at the moment of collision; for without them a part of the forces which the *conflictus* of the bodies has brought into the world would be lost.",["- Divided the passage into three paragraphs, and broke several long sentences; \"Würkung\" = \"effect\" or \"action\".","- \"Federharte Körper\" = \"perfectly elastic bodies\" (literally \"spring-hard\").","- \"Trägheitskraft\" = \"force of inertia\"; \"Gewalten\" = \"powers\".","- Source reads \"1 + ½\" for B's force; kept as printed, though the sense suggests the velocity-force relation discussed in 0221 (possible slip in the original, not corrected).","- The double negation \"keine Kraft … ohne daß … nicht … gespannet hätte\" is smoothed to a single affirmative condition.","- \"Feder BD\" is given as in the German, although elsewhere the spring is DB.","- \"*conflictus*\" left in Latin as in the German."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0229":["§ 43.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0230":["In resolving the Herrmannian case I have said nothing that could, in the ground of the proof, have been unknown to this philosopher, or that the most eminent champions of the living forces would demand to deny, if they were called upon to declare themselves on the point. Herr Herrmann must necessarily have known how the motions which arise in the impact of elastic bodies can be derived from their bare velocity; for without this it could not possibly have been known to him *a priori* that a ball of simple mass, impinging upon a triple mass with two degrees of velocity, produces four degrees of force. I say that this case could not have been known to him either, apart from the manner of resolution which we have given. For everyone knows that, in a mechanical investigation, the motions which an elastic body produces by impact are found by first seeking separately what the body does without its spring-force, and then adding the effect of the elasticity; both are determined by what the body can do in proportion to its mass and its simple [*schlechten*] velocity.\n\nIn the form of argument entitled an *argumentum ad hominem* nothing stronger can be brought against Herr Herrmann and the Leibnizians in general. For they must either admit that all the proofs, in which they have hitherto been agreed, for giving the ground of the motions which arise in the impact of elastic bodies, have been false; or they must concede that such a body has produced the motions, for the sake of which they believed it to require the square of the velocity, solely by a force proportioned simply to mass and velocity taken together.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0231":["§ 44.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0232":["This resolution was unknown to Madame du Châtelet. [Kant's text: *Chastelet*.]",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0233":["The dispute of the Marquise du Châtelet with Herr von Mairan convinces me that it was not superfluous to have now given a detailed development of the manner in which elastic bodies, by impact, bring into the world a greater quantity of motion than was in it before the impact.\n\nHerr von Mairan says: Elastic force is a true machine of nature, etc., etc.; if one wishes to consider separately all the effects of the impact of elastic bodies, by summing as positive what they give in the two opposite directions, one must by no means ascribe the new force which seems to arise from this in nature and shows itself through the impact to the activity of the impinging body, as though it merely transferred this force to the body struck, but [must ascribe it] to a foreign source of force, etc., etc.; in a word, to a certain physical cause of elasticity, whatever it may be, whose efficacy the impact has merely set free, and has, so to speak, released the spring, etc., etc. I say, when Herr von Mairan says this, Madame du Châtelet answers him that it is useless to examine it until the author of this opinion has taken the trouble to base what he here wishes to assert on some proof. I have taken the honour of undertaking this trouble in place of Herr von Mairan, and this is the justification by which I excuse my prolixity on this subject.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0234":["§ 45.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0235":["Herr Jurin's objection concerning the collision of two inelastic and unequal bodies.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0236":["This objection has further been made to the Leibnizians by Herr Jurin and others: that two inelastic bodies which meet each other with velocities inversely proportional to their masses nevertheless remain at rest after the impact. Here, then, according to the doctrine of the living forces, are two forces, which can be made as unequal as one pleases, and which nevertheless hold each other in equilibrium.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0237":["Herr Bernoulli's refutation of this objection by comparison with the compression of springs.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0238":["I find in the physics of Madame du Châtelet an answer to this objection which, as I see from the citation, has the famous Herr Bernoulli as its author. Herr Bernoulli has not been fortunate in finding a defence for his opinion worthy of his name. He says that inelastic bodies, by the impression of their parts upon one another, produce just the same effect as if they compressed a spring situated between them. Hence he assumes a spring R * which extends at the same time to both sides and drives bodies of unequal mass on either side. He proves that the velocities imparted to the bodies by this spring are in inverse ratio to their masses, and that therefore, if the balls A and B returned with these velocities, they would restore the spring to its first state of compression. Thus far everything is correct, and in complete agreement with the theorems of the Cartesians.\n\nBut let us see how he pursues his conclusion. The parts of the spring, as it springs apart, move partly towards the side of A, partly towards the side of B; the point of division, however, lies at R, which divides the spring in the inverse proportion of the masses A and B. The part RB of the spring R therefore acts upon the body B, whose mass is 3, while the other part RA imparts its force to the ball A, whose mass is 1. But the forces brought into these bodies are related as the number of springs which have applied their pressure to them; consequently the forces of the balls A and B are unequal, although their velocities stand in inverse proportion to their masses. Now if the spring R has extended completely, and the bodies came back against it with just the velocities which it imparted to them on springing loose, one easily sees that each would, by means of the compression of the spring, bring the other to rest. Now their forces are unequal; hence one recognises from this how it is possible for two bodies with unequal forces to bring each other to rest. Of this he makes the application to the collision of inelastic bodies.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0239":["* Fig. IX.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0240":["§ 46.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0241":["Herr Bernoulli's thoughts are refuted.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0242":["In this argument I do not recognise Herr Bernoulli, who was accustomed to construct his proofs with much more perfect rigour. It is indisputably certain that the spring, as it springs apart, must impart just as much force to one of the bodies A and B as to the other. For it brings into the ball A as much force as is the magnitude of the intensity with which it braces itself against the other ball B. If it braced itself against no resistance whatever, it would impart no force at all to the ball A, for then it would spring loose without any effect. Hence this spring can apply no force to A without pressing in, from the other side, just the same degree of power upon the movable ball B. The forces of the balls A and B are therefore equal to one another, and not, as Herr Bernoulli has falsely persuaded himself, as the length AR to RB.",["- 0230: Divided the long final sentences into shorter ones and began a new paragraph at \"Man kan nichts stärkeres\"; the *argumentum ad hominem* is kept in Latin.","- 0230: *schlechten Geschwindigkeit* rendered \"simple velocity\" (bare velocity, not squared); *schlechthin* as \"simply\".","- 0230: In the German \"ein solcher Körper … weswegen sie ihn … nöthig zu haben glaubten\" the antecedent of \"ihn\" is the body; \"the square of the velocity\" is taken as the object of \"nöthig\".","- 0232, 0233: Kant's \"Chastelet\" is rendered in the modern form \"du Châtelet\"; \"Frau\" and \"Frau Marquisin\" given as \"Madame\" and \"Marquise\".","- 0233: Mairan's speech is run on in a single sentence in the German; I kept it as a reported statement, supplying \"[must ascribe it]\" to complete the construction, and in the closing sentences \"I say\" is retained as in the original (\"ich sage wenn\").","- 0233: \"inden\" read as \"in den\" (OCR slip); \"etc. etc.\" kept as in the source.","- 0238: \"Fig. IX.\" is the footnote to \"R *\"; it is kept with its asterisk. The thought \"Nun sind ihre Kräfte ungleich\" is Bernoulli's, reported by Kant.","- 0238/0242: *Vorstellung*-type terms do not occur; *Kraft* is \"force\", *lebendige Kräfte* \"living forces\", *Federkraft* \"spring-force\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0243":["It is easy to see how the error in Herr Bernoulli's inference arose. Its source is the proposition on which the Leibnizian party so strongly insists, namely, that the force of a body is proportional to the number of springs which have acted upon it.* We have already refuted this proposition above, and Herr Bernoulli's case confirms our view.",["- The footnote asterisk is kept after \"it\" as in the German, where it follows the sentence's clause. \"Parthey\" rendered \"party\" (school of followers).","- \"Schluße\" rendered \"inference\"; \"Federn\" as \"springs\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0244":["* The bodies A and B have equal forces, therefore, because the springs RA and RB have acted upon them for an equal length [of time], and because the parts of these springs were all equally strongly tensed.",["- Footnote text. \"[of time]\" is supplied; the German has only \"gleich lange\", which the argument (cf. § 47, \"Zeit der Würkung\") shows to be temporal."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0245":["§ 47.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0246":["Herr Bernoulli's thought confirms our opinion.",["- Heading."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0247":["One cannot observe without pleasure how admirably this explanation, which was meant to serve in defence of the living forces, supplies us with weapons to overthrow them utterly instead. For since it is once certain that the spring R imparts equal forces to the bodies whose masses are 1 and 3 (§ 46), and further that the velocity of the ball whose mass is 1 is threefold, and that of the other simple, as the Leibnizians themselves admit, two consequences follow, both of which flatly contradict the living forces. First, that the force which a body receives through the pressure of the springs is not proportional to the number of springs which have impelled it, but rather to the time of their action. Second, that a body which has a simple mass and a threefold velocity has no more force than another which has three times as much mass but only a simple velocity.",["- Restated the long \"Denn da … so fließen\" sentence with the consequences divided into two short sentences.","- \"lebendigen Kräfte\" rendered \"living forces\" (vires vivae); \"Massen\" rendered \"mass\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0248":["§ 48.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0249":["Defence of the living forces by appeal to the constant conservation of one and the same quantity of force in the world.",["- Heading."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0250":["Thus far we have seen how Leibniz's followers have made use of the collision of elastic bodies in order to defend the living forces. Their application of it, however, was merely mathematical. But they have also believed that they could find, in this department of phoronomy, a metaphysical ground in support of their opinion. Herr von Leibniz is himself its author, and his authority has lent it no small weight.",["- \"Stücke der Phoronomie\" rendered \"department of phoronomy\"; \"derselben\" (the collision) and \"desselben\" (the ground) resolved by sense."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0251":["He willingly accepted Descartes's principle, that the same quantity of force is always conserved in the world, but only of a force whose quantity must be estimated by the square of the velocity. He showed that the old measure of force does not permit this beautiful rule. For if the old measure is adopted, force in nature would [he said] be unceasingly diminished or increased, according as the position of bodies relatively to one another is altered. Leibniz held it unbecoming to the power and wisdom of God that He should be compelled to renew without cessation the motion which He had imparted to His work, as Herr Newton imagined; and this drove him to seek a law by which he might remedy this difficulty.",["- \"vermindere oder vermehre\" is subjunctive (reported speech); the bracketed \"[he said]\" makes this explicit.","- Passage begins mid-section (§ 48 continues from AA 1:58)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0252":["§ 49.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0253":["First solution of this objection.",["- Heading."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0254":["Since we have proved in the foregoing that the living forces, in the manner in which their defenders themselves have employed them, namely in the mathematical sense, can find a place nowhere, the power and wisdom of God are already vindicated here of themselves, through consideration of the complete impossibility of the thing. We can always take cover behind this bulwark, should we perhaps get the worst of it in some other manner of answering this objection. For even if, according to the law of motion which we have maintained, it were necessary that the structure of the world, after a gradual exhaustion of its forces, should finally fall into complete disorder, this blow could still not touch the power and wisdom of God. For it can never be held against these that they have not introduced into the world a law which we know to be absolutely impossible, and which therefore could in no way obtain.",["- \"im mathematischen Verstande\" rendered \"in the mathematical sense\" (Verstand here is not the faculty). \"den kürzern ziehen\" rendered idiomatically \"get the worst of it\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0255":["§ 50.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0256":["Second answer to the said objection.",["- Heading."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0257":["But let us only recover ourselves. We are not yet compelled to resort to so desperate an evasion. This would be to cut the knot; we prefer to untie it.",["- \"Dies würde heissen\" rendered \"This would be to\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0258":["If the Leibnizians hold it indispensably necessary, for the preservation of the world-machine, that the force of bodies be subject to estimation by the square, we can concede this small demand to them. All that I have proved hitherto, and intend to prove up to the close of this chapter, is directed only to convincing them that neither in abstract consideration nor in nature will the force of bodies, when weighed in the way the Leibnizians do it, namely mathematically, yield an estimation by the square. But I have not on that account renounced the living forces altogether. In the third chapter of this treatise I shall show that forces are actually to be found in nature whose measure is the square of their velocity; only with the restriction that they will never be discovered in the way in which people have hitherto set about it, that they will hide themselves for ever from this kind of consideration (the mathematical), and that nothing but some metaphysical inquiry, or perhaps a special kind of experience, can make them known to us. We are therefore not strictly disputing here the thing itself, but the *modus cognoscendi*.",["- Restated \"daß…\" clauses of the third-chapter sentence with semicolons; kept as a single sentence to preserve the series of three \"daß\" restrictions.","- \"Hauptstück\" rendered \"chapter\"; \"Schätzung nach dem Quadrat\" \"estimation by the square\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0259":["Accordingly we are in agreement with the Leibnizians on the main point; perhaps, then, we may come to be so also in the consequences they draw from it.",["- \"derselben\" resolved as the Leibnizians' consequences, not those of the main point alone."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0260":["§ 51.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0261":["The source of the Leibnizian inference to the conservation of one and the same quantity of force.",["- Heading."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0262":["The objection of Herr von Leibniz, however, rests upon a false presupposition, which has for a long time brought much inconvenience into philosophy. It has become a principle in natural science that no motion arises in nature except by means of a matter which is itself in actual motion; and that therefore motion which has been lost in one part of the world can be restored by nothing else than either another actual motion or the immediate hand of God. This proposition has always caused much trouble to those who have given it their assent. They have been compelled to weary their imagination with artificially contrived vortices, and to build one hypothesis upon another. Instead of leading us finally to such a plan of the world-structure as would be simple and comprehensible enough to allow the composite appearances of nature to be derived from it, they confuse us with an infinity of strange motions, which are far more wonderful and incomprehensible than all that for the explanation of which they are to be employed.",["- Divided the final long sentence into two; \"Erscheinungen\" rendered \"appearances\" per the fixed term.","- \"Weltweisheit\" rendered \"philosophy\"; \"Naturlehre\" \"natural science\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0263":["How this difficulty can be remedied.",["- Heading; text breaks off here, the discussion continuing in the next passage."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0264":["Herr Hamberger, so far as I know, was the first to offer a means of remedying this evil. His idea is a fine one, for it is simple and therefore in accordance with nature. He shows (though as yet only in a very imperfect sketch) how a body can receive an actual motion through a matter which is itself only at rest. This guards against innumerable false paths, and often even against the miracles that go with the opposite opinion. It is true that the ground of this idea is metaphysical, and so not to the taste of present-day natural philosophers. But it is at the same time evident that the very first sources of the effects of nature must throughout be a matter for metaphysics.\n\nHerr Hamberger has not succeeded in his purpose of pointing the world to a new way, shorter and more convenient, of leading us to knowledge of nature. This field has remained uncultivated. Men have not yet been able to tear themselves from the old road so as to venture on the new. Is it not strange that men entrust themselves to a boundless sea of digressions and arbitrary fictions of the imagination, and yet disregard the means which are simple and comprehensible, and for that very reason also natural? But this is already the common plague of the human understanding. Men will long be swept along by this stream. They will take pleasure in a mode of consideration that is intricate and artificial, and in which the understanding perceives its own strength. We shall have a physics full of excellent proofs of acuteness and inventiveness, but no plan of nature itself and its effects. In the end, however, that opinion will prevail which depicts nature as it is, that is, simply and without endless detours. The way of nature is only a single way. One must therefore first have tried countless false paths before one can reach the one which is true.",["- Restated the long second-last sentences as shorter ones; paragraph break added at \"Herr Hamberger ist ... nicht gelungen\" for clarity.","- \"Vorwurf\" rendered \"matter [for metaphysics]\" (object of study); \"Erkenntniß\" = knowledge.","- \"diesem Uebel\" refers to the preceding context (not given); left as \"this evil\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0265":["The Leibnizians, more than others, ought to embrace the opinion of Herr Hamberger. For it is they who maintain that a dead pressure, which is preserved in the body to which it has been imparted, unless an insuperable obstacle again annuls it, grows into an actual motion. They cannot therefore deny that a body which clings more to the parts of a surrounding fluid in one direction than in another will then receive an actual motion, provided the fluid is of such a kind that by its resistance it does not again annul the body's force. This must convince them of what I now assert, namely, that a body can receive an actual motion from a matter which is itself at rest.",["- \"Druck\" rendered \"pressure\" (*todter Druck*, \"dead pressure\"); \"ihm\" in \"ihm seine Kraft\" taken as the body, and \"sie\" as the Leibnizians in the second sentence."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0266":["Resolution of the objection raised by Herr von Leibniz.",["- Heading; \"Entscheidung\" rendered \"resolution\" (could be \"decision\")."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0267":["How then shall we evade the blow which Herr von Leibniz seeks to deal to the Cartesian law by considering the wisdom of God? Everything depends on whether a body can receive an actual motion even through the action of a matter which is at rest. On this I base my case. The very first motions in this structure of the world were not produced by the force of a matter in motion; otherwise they would not be the first. Nor were they caused by the immediate power of God, or of any intelligence, so long as it is still possible that they could have arisen through the action of a matter at rest. For God spares himself as many actions as he can without detriment to the world-machine, whereas he makes nature as active and effective as is at all possible. If, then, motion was first brought into the world by the force of a matter in itself dead and unmoved, it will also be maintained by the same, and, where it has suffered loss, be able to restore itself. One would therefore have to have a great fondness for doubting, if one still wished to hesitate in believing that the structure of the world need suffer no diminution, even if in the impact of bodies certain forces which were previously in them were lost.",["- \"Weltgebäude\" rendered \"structure of the world\"; \"Weltmaschine\" \"world-machine\".","- \"Hierauf gründe ich mich\" rendered \"On this I base my case\" (smoothed).","- \"dieselbe/durch dieselbe\" taken to refer to the dead matter; \"wiederherstellen können\" has motion as subject."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0268":["§ 52.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0269":["According to Leibniz's law, the force in the impact of a small elastic body against a larger one is the same before and after the impact.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0270":["I recover from a digression which has taken me somewhat away from the main matter in which I am engaged. I have already remarked that the champions of the living forces are particularly proud of the observation by which they have found that, when the force of bodies is estimated according to the law of Herr von Leibniz, the same magnitude of force is always found in the collision of elastic bodies before and after the impact. This thought, which seems in so wondrous a manner to favour the living forces, shall rather help us to overthrow them. Let us argue as follows. That law according to which, in the collision of a smaller elastic body against a larger, no more force is found after the impact than before it, is false. Now Leibniz's law is of this kind. Therefore, etc., etc.",["- Kant's syllogism stands as given; \"Ergo &c. &c.\" kept as \"Therefore, etc., etc.\"","- The syllogism's major premise as printed seems to contradict §52 (equality); I translate as it stands, without correcting.","- \"sich viel dünken lassen\" rendered \"are proud\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0271":["§ 53.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0272":["The observation cited by the Leibnizians is wholly opposed to the living forces.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0273":["- Restated the long final sentences of the first paragraph as several shorter ones, and divided the passage into paragraphs for clarity; the German is a single block.\n- \"Würkung/Gegenwürkung\" rendered \"effect/counter-effect\" (could also be \"action/reaction\"); \"Kraft\" = force, \"Trägheits-Kraft\" = force of inertia, \"Feder\" = spring.\n- The text speaks of \"Kugel\" (sphere) and \"Körper\" (body) interchangeably; I keep both. The figure (Fig. VIII) is not supplied, so the labels A–E are left as they stand.\n- \"ihm\" in \"nicht genommen werden\" and \"in ihn fortpflanzet\" refers to A; I resolved it so by the argument.\n- \"Schluß\" (\"Schlußrede\") rendered \"syllogism\"; \"Major\" = major premiss.\n- \"davor\" in \"ohne daß davor eben so viel ... abginge\" rendered \"like amount being lost\", i.e. in compensation.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0274":["- Footnote to the asterisk in 0273; the figure itself is not reproduced.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0275":["- \"federharter Körper\" rendered \"body of hard spring\" (a perfectly elastic body); the phrase is awkward in German too.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0276":["§ 54.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0277":["The foregoing becomes still clearer if we take the case in which a larger elastic body strikes a smaller one.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0278":["- Supplied \"[A]\" at the end for the ambiguous \"jener Kugel\", which the argument shows to be A. In the German, \"Federkraft ist gespannet mit demjenigen Grade\" is read as the degree of A's motion toward C.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0279":["* Fig. VIII.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0280":["We see from this that only and solely in the case where a larger body strikes one of smaller mass is the same degree of force preserved in the impact; and that in all other cases, where the elasticity does not find on one side as much force to annihilate as it generates on the other, the force after the impact always becomes greater than before it. This destroys the Leibnizian law, for according to that law the very same quantity of force always remains in nature in every possible case, without any loss or increase.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0281":["§ 55.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0282":["Calculation confirms that in the case where a larger body strikes a smaller one, the same quantity of force remains according to the Cartesian law.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0283":["- \"Conflictus\" is left in Latin, in italics, as Kant's term. \"phoronomisch\" rendered \"phoronomic\"; \"2B : A+B\" kept as printed.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0284":["§ 56.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0285":["The force with which the smaller body rebounds from the larger bears the sign minus.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0286":["- \"Phänomena\" rendered \"phenomena\", as Kant's own word here, not the technical \"Erscheinungen\" (appearances) of the Critical period.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0287":["Now when an elastic body strikes another of greater mass, we know from the laws of motion that the smaller body returns after the impact with a certain degree of force. We have also learned from the last paragraphs that this force, with which the small body rebounds from the larger, is equal to the excess which the exertion of the elasticity, once brought to life, has over the force of the body A, with which, before the spring-forces of both spheres became active, A was moving forward together with the sphere B in the direction AE.\n\nNow (according to what has been proved earlier) so long as the elasticity still met in body A a force directed along AD, which it was able to annul in the same measure as it brought force into the sphere B, I say that for so long there was nothing in the two bodies taken together that did not contain exactly the same quantity of force as had previously been present in A alone, as the cause. Consequently the state of the two bodies was, for so long, to be regarded as a lawful effect of the force which A had before the impact. For the effect is at all times neither greater nor smaller than the cause.\n\nBut we know further that when the spring-force has already annulled all the force that was still left in A in the direction AE, it brings new forces into both bodies A and B; and these are added over and above those which constituted the genuine and complete effect of the sphere A. We shall therefore be able to extract these again from the motion of the two spheres in the following way: we take from body A the force with which it returns after the impact, and subtract just as much from the force which the sphere B has acquired. From this it is easy to see that the force with which a small elastic sphere rebounds from a larger one against which it runs is of a negative kind, and has the sign minus before it.\n\nIf, for example, a sphere A runs with 2 degrees of velocity against a sphere B of three times its mass, it rebounds after the impact with one degree of velocity, and gives the sphere B also one degree. The force with which A returns after the impact cannot now be added to the force of the sphere B, if one wishes to have the whole magnitude of the effect which A has produced. No: it must be taken away from body A, and also subtracted from the force that is in B. The remainder, which is 2, will be the entire and complete effect accomplished by the force of the sphere A. Thus a sphere which has 2 for its mass and 1 for its velocity has the same force as another which has a simple mass and a double velocity.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0288":["§ 57.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0289":["Madame du Châtelet jested about this at the wrong moment.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0290":["The enlightened Marquise du Châtelet, then, was pleased to be facetious, at the wrong moment, against Monsieur de Mairan. To the very observation we have just cited she replies that she did not think he would readily make an experiment, or care to place himself in the path of a body which, marked with the sign minus, rebounded with 500 or 1000 degrees of force. I think so too; and I should be much mistaken if I feared that Monsieur de Mairan would undertake to settle the truth in this way. But the point does not turn on this, that the force marked with the sign minus is not a real force, as the Marquise seems to infer from it. Monsieur de Mairan undoubtedly did not mean to say this by it. It is in fact a real force, and would also exert real effects if one chose to put it to the test.\n\nAll that is indicated by this is that neither this force, nor a part of the force of the sphere B which is equal to it, can be reckoned as belonging to the complete effect of the sphere A. Rather, one must regard it as though it were not present in A at all and were, on the contrary, still to be subtracted from B; and the force remaining after this then first properly presents the complete effect of the force which existed before the collision. But when a magnitude is regarded in this way, it counts in the summation for less than nothing, and requires the negative sign.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0291":["§ 58.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0292":["The Leibnizians flee from the investigation of living forces by means of the impact of inelastic bodies.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0293":["My readers will now suppose that they will find cited, from the doctrine of the motion of inelastic bodies by impact as well, certain proofs which the adherents of the Leibnizian estimation had employed to defend the living forces. But they are mistaken. These gentlemen do not find motions of this kind altogether advantageous to their opinion, and so they seek to exclude them entirely from this investigation. This is a malady from which those who make ventures in the knowledge of truths ordinarily lie sick. They shut their eyes, so to speak, to whatever seems to contradict the proposition they have got into their heads. A small evasion, a frigid and feeble excuse, is able to satisfy them, when it is a matter of removing a difficulty that stands in the way of the opinion for which they are prepossessed.\n\nPhilosophy might have been spared many errors if people had been willing to impose some constraint upon themselves in this respect. When one is engaged in drawing together all the grounds which the understanding offers for confirming an opinion one has set before oneself, one ought, with the same attention and effort, to take pains to found the contrary on every kind of proof that presents itself at all, just as one can always do for a favoured opinion. One ought to despise nothing that seems in the least favourable to the opposite side, and in defending it ought to carry matters to the utmost. In such an equilibrium of the understanding an opinion would often be rejected which would otherwise infallibly have been accepted; and truth, when at last it came forth, would present itself in a so much greater light of conviction.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0294":["§ 59.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0295":["The impact of inelastic bodies is more decisive, with regard to the living forces, than the impact of elastic ones.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0296":["It has already been urged upon the defenders of the living forces more than once that the motions of inelastic bodies by impact are much better fitted to decide whether the living forces have a place or not than is the motion of elastic ones. For in the latter the spring-force always intermingles, and makes the confusions endless, whereas the motion of the former is determined by nothing but action and reaction alone. There is no doubt that the Leibnizians would let themselves be convinced by the clearness of this thought, if only it did not overturn the whole edifice of the living forces.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0297":["§ 60.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0298":["The evasion of the Leibnizians with regard to the objection raised against them from the impact of inelastic bodies.",["- 0287: Several long sentences were divided and paragraphed; \"Nun war … ich sage, so lange …\" was restated, the parenthesis kept, the resumptive \"ich sage\" rendered \"I say that for so long\".","- 0287: \"Federkräfte/Federkraft\" = \"spring-force(s)\"; \"lebendig gemachten Elasticität\" = \"elasticity, once brought to life\" (literal; a pun on *vis viva* is possible but not marked).","- 0287: The final sentence is as printed (mass 2, velocity 1 = mass 1, velocity 2), though by the example's own figures the sums look inconsistent; I did not correct it.","- 0290: \"Frau v. Chastelet\" is given in the modern form \"du Châtelet\"; \"Herr von Mairan\" as \"Monsieur de Mairan\". The sentence \"Sie glaubte, er würde…\" is rendered in indirect speech as \"she did not think he would…\".","- 0290: \"gilt sie in der Summirung weniger, wie nichts\" = \"counts for less than nothing in the summation\"; \"allererst\" = \"first properly\".","- 0293: \"Erkenntniß der Wahrheiten\" rendered \"knowledge of truths\"; \"ordentlicher Weise\" = \"ordinarily\"; \"Unternehmungen machen\" = \"make ventures\".","- 0293: \"Verstand\" = understanding; \"in einem solchen Gleichgewichte des Verstandes\" = \"equilibrium of the understanding\".","- 0296: \"Wirkung und Gegenwirkung\" = \"action and reaction\", as the context requires. 0298 is a section heading only."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0299":["They have therefore been compelled to take refuge in an exception, which is perhaps the worst that has ever been employed. They assert, namely, that in the impact of inelastic bodies a part of the force is always lost, in that it is expended in pressing in the parts of the body. Hence half of the force which an inelastic body has is lost when it strikes another body of equal mass that is at rest, and is consumed in the pressing in of the parts.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0300":["§ 61.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0301":["The origin of this erroneous thought.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0302":["This thought has more than one bad side. We shall consider some of them.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0303":["At the first glance it cannot be hard for us to perceive the source of this error. We know, partly from experience and partly from the grounds of natural science [*Naturlehre*], that a hard body which in impact alters its shape only very little or not at all is always elastic, and that, on the contrary, the parts of inelastic bodies are so put together that they yield and are pressed in upon impact. Nature has commonly combined these properties. In a mathematical consideration, however, we are not obliged to take them together.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0304":["The adherents of the living forces have become confused over this. They imagine that, because in nature an inelastic body commonly has a structure such that its parts yield and are pressed in upon impact, the rules which a purely mathematical consideration of the motion of such bodies provides cannot hold without this property. This is the origin of the difficulty which we saw in § 60, and which is wholly without ground, as we shall now learn.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0305":["§ 62.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0306":["First answer to the exception of the Leibnizians.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0307":["In mathematics, by the elastic force [*Federkraft*] of a body nothing else is understood than that property through which it thrusts back again another body that runs against it, with precisely the same degree of force with which that body had run against it. Hence an inelastic body is one that does not have this property.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0308":["Mathematics does not concern itself with the manner in which this property manifests itself in nature. With it, it is and remains wholly undetermined whether elasticity flows from the alteration of shape and its sudden restoration, or whether a hidden entelechy, a *qualitas occulta*, or God knows what other cause besides, is its source. When elasticity is found described in the mechanical treatises as arising from the pressing in and springing back of the parts of a body, it should be noted that the mathematicians who make use of this explanation meddle in what does not concern them, what contributes nothing to their purpose, and what is properly a subject for natural science [*Naturlehre*].",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0309":["If, then, the consideration of an inelastic body in mathematics presupposes nothing further than that it has in itself no force to rebound a body which strikes it, and if this single determination [*Bestimmung*] is what the whole chapter on the motion of inelastic bodies is built upon, then it is absurd to assert that the rules of these motions are what they are because the pressing in of the parts of the colliding bodies admits of these laws and no others. For in the principles from which these laws have been drawn there is not a trace of the pressing in of the parts. All the concepts on which they have been built are so undetermined with regard to this restriction that, without prejudice to those principles, one can count among inelastic bodies just as well those which do not alter their shape in impact as those which suffer a compression of their parts. Now if in the construction of these laws no attention was paid to this pressing in, in order to arrange the rules of motion in accordance with it, or if not even such concepts as include this pressing in were laid down as a foundation, then it is indeed very strange to put upon it the blame for the laws mentioned being constituted as they actually are.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0310":["§ 63.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0311":["Second answer. Because a body can be called inelastic even though it is perfectly hard.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0312":["We have said that in the consideration which mathematics offers us of the motion of inelastic bodies, these can also be regarded as perfectly hard, as if their parts were not pressed in by the impact. Nature too offers us examples showing that the body whose parts yield more than those of another is not always the more inelastic; rather, a body whose parts are scarcely pressed in at all by impact, in comparison with another, is often nevertheless less elastic than another whose parts yield more easily. For let a wooden ball fall down upon the pavement: it will by far not spring back so high as a stuffed one, which can yet be pressed in very easily, and in comparison with which the former may be called uncommonly hard. From this we see that even in nature a body is not inelastic because its parts are pressed in, but only because they do not restore themselves with the same degree of force with which they were pressed in. We can therefore also posit bodies whose parts yield infinitely little in impact, but which are at the same time so constituted that they do not restore themselves from even this infinitely small compression, or, where they do so, do so by no means with the degree of velocity with which they were pressed in. A wooden ball would do much the same, if one may compare small things with great. Bodies of the kind I am speaking of would be perfectly hard* and yet inelastic. One could therefore not exempt them from the laws of the impact of inelastic bodies, and yet their parts would not be pressed in. How would the exception of the Leibnizian gentlemen stand up here?",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0313":["* For a body which allows itself to be pressed in only infinitely little may without error be called perfectly hard.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0314":["§ 64.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0315":["Third answer: The pressing in of the parts is no ground why, in the impact of inelastic bodies, a part of the force should be lost.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0316":["We can still grant the Leibnizians their presupposition that inelastic bodies always suffer a pressing in of their parts, and it shall do us no harm. A body exerts upon another movable body, whose parts it presses in by the impact, precisely the same effect which it would exert if a spring were situated between the two, which it compressed by its onset. I may freely make use of this thought, because it is not only plain and convincing, but because it has also been employed in just this case by a great patron of the living forces, Herr Bernoulli.\n\n---  notes\n- 0299: \"Kraft\" rendered \"force\" throughout; \"lebendige Kräfte\" as \"living forces\", as the established English usage for the period.\n- 0303: \"Naturlehre\" rendered \"natural science\" with the German given; it is Kant's term for physics as distinct from mathematics.\n- 0309: Divided no sentences, but kept \"determination\" for *Bestimmung* per the term list; \"Eintrag thun\" rendered \"without prejudice to\".\n- 0309: \"jenen\" taken to refer to the principles/laws (the antecedent of \"Eintrag\"); \"diejenige\" (plural sense) rendered \"those which\".\n- 0312: \"Wie etwa eine hölzerne Kugel thun würde\" is elliptical; I read it as \"a wooden ball would do much the same\", and turned the fragment into a full sentence. The asterisk footnote mark is kept in place after \"hard\".\n- 0312: \"Federkraft\" in 0307 is rendered \"elastic force\" with the German shown; \"weichen\" rendered \"yield\".\n- 0316: \"Schutzgotte\" rendered \"patron\" (literally tutelary god); \"Anlauf\" rendered \"onset\".",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0317":["Suppose now that a ball A* is moved against another ball B, and presses the spring R together in its onset. I say that all the small degrees of force which are expended in compressing the spring pass over into the mass of the body B, and accumulate until they have brought into the said body B the whole force with which the spring has been compressed. For the body A loses not a single degree of force, and the spring is not compressed by the least amount except in so far as it braces itself against the body B. But it braces itself against this ball with the very same violence with which it would spring open on this side if the ball suddenly gave way; that is, with the force with which A compresses it from the other side, and which this body expends and consumes in compressing it. Now it is evident that the very same degree of force with which the spring strives to expand against B, and which the force of inertia (*vis inertiae*) of the ball B resists, must pass into that same ball. Thus B receives, to move in the direction BE, the whole force which is consumed in A in compressing the spring R.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0318":["* Fig. IX.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0319":["The application is easily made. For the spring R represents the parts of the inelastic balls A and B which are pressed in by the impact. Hence the body A, in pressing in the parts on both sides in its impact against B, consumes in this indentation nothing of its force that the body B does not receive, and with which it moves after the impact. No part, then, is lost, still less so great a part as the Leibnizians falsely allege.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0320":["§ 65.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0321":["I grow weary of raking out all the inaccuracies and contradictions contained in this difficulty, which the Leibnizians have wished to raise against us regarding the impact of inelastic bodies. The single one that I still wish to adduce might alone suffice to render it useless.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0322":["Fourth Reply. On the proportion of the hardness of inelastic bodies, and on the degree of force of the onset, which, in the Leibnizians' exception, must be determined.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0323":["Even if we granted our opponents everything else, we still cannot forgive the boldness contained in the demand that, in the impact of inelastic bodies, neither more nor less, but exactly so much of the force should be consumed by the indentation of the parts as they themselves find necessary in each case according to their estimate. It is an audacity impossible to swallow, that we should be pressed to believe, without any proof, that a body must lose, in an impact against an equal body, exactly half, and in an impact against a body three times as great, exactly ¾ of its force, and so on, through the indentation of the parts, without anyone being able to give us a reason why precisely so much, and no more or less, should be spent. For suppose that the concept of an inelastic body necessarily requires some loss of force in the indentation: I still do not know from what one would conclude that this absence of elasticity requires that exactly so much, and no less, force must be consumed.\n\nThe Leibnizians surely cannot deny that the less the firmness of the mass of the inelastic bodies in comparison with the force of the body striking, the more strongly will the force be consumed in the indentation of the parts; but the harder both bodies are, the less of it must be lost. For if they were perfectly hard, no loss of force would take place. A certain determinate ratio of the hardness of two equal inelastic bodies is therefore required, if exactly half of the force of the striking body is to be consumed and annihilated in the impact. Without this proportion, more or less would result, according as the colliding bodies were made softer or harder.\n\nNow in the rules of motion of inelastic bodies, against which the Leibnizians seek an exception, the degree of firmness, and still more its proportion to the strength of the onset, is entirely undetermined. Consequently it cannot be understood from these rules at all whether an indentation of the parts takes place, whether a force is thereby consumed, and how much of it will be lost. Least of all do they offer any ground from which it could be understood that, when one ball strikes another of equal weight, exactly half of the force is lost. For this does not happen without a certain, quite exactly determined ratio between the hardness of these bodies and the violence of the impact. Since, then, no such determination is to be found in the principles from which the laws of impact of inelastic bodies are derived, containing any ground of a determinate loss of force, the cause why these rules are constituted thus and not otherwise is not to be sought in the indentation of the parts, which in every case would make just so much force forfeit as the Leibnizians see fit to cancel.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0324":["Application of Our Conclusions.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0325":["Now that the pretext by which the defenders of living forces (*lebendige Kräfte*) seek to evade the blow dealt them by all the laws of the impact of inelastic bodies has been found ineffectual in more than one way, nothing further hinders us from using these laws for the service which they will always render us most admirably, namely to clear the living forces out of the domain of mathematics, into which they have intruded unlawfully.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0326":["§ 66.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0327":["The impact of inelastic bodies wholly cancels the living forces.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0328":["But it is superfluous to set out here at length the manner in which the motion of inelastic bodies cancels the living forces. Every case one takes does this without the slightest exception or difficulty. For example, if an inelastic body A strikes another body B, of the same kind and equal weight, which is at rest, both move after the impact with ½ the degree of velocity that there was before the impact. According to the Leibnizian mode of estimation, there is therefore in each after the impact ¼ of force, and so in all together ½ degree of force, whereas before it a whole degree was present in nature. Half has therefore been lost without having produced an effect equal to it, or even without having encountered a single resistance by which it could in some way have been consumed. This, even by the admission of our opponents, is one of the greatest absurdities that can be committed.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0329":["§ 67.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0330":["General proof that the collision of elastic bodies must always be contrary to the living forces.",["- 0317: \"Anlauf\" rendered \"onset\" (also in 0322, 0323, 0328 for \"Anlaufende\"/\"Anstoß\" contexts: \"striking body\", \"impact\"); \"Trägheits-Kraft\" given with Latin *vis inertiae* added from Kant's usual parallel.","- 0317: \"welche in A verzehret ist\" taken as relative to \"Kraft\"; \"dem ... widerstehet\" restructured as \"which ... resists\".","- 0323: very long sentences divided into several; \"verlustig macht ... aufzuheben\" smoothed. \"Helfte\" = Hälfte. \"gesetzt, daß\" rendered \"suppose that\".","- 0323: last sentence's \"die ... verlustig macht\" is taken as relating to \"Eindrückung der Theile\"; the sense is somewhat loose in the German.","- 0325: \"lebendige Kräfte\" kept with German in parenthesis once; \"Schlage\" rendered \"blow\".","- 0328: \"Schätzungsart\" rendered \"mode of estimation\"; \"¼ Kraft\" kept as \"¼ of force\" (vis viva ∝ mv²); \"wie\" in \"ein so großer Theil\" rendered idiomatically.","- 0318: footnote marker text \"Fig. IX.\" kept as a footnote."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0331":["I do not wish to close this section, in which we have refuted the living forces by way of the collision of bodies, without first adding a general consideration which comprehends all that can ever be said against the living forces in this manner. I shall show in it that, even if we were willing to grant the Leibnizians their estimation of forces, it would still be quite contrary to the nature of the matter to wish to prove that estimation from the collision of bodies. Collision would never offer, nor indeed could it offer, any other measure than simple velocity, even if the estimation according to the square were a wholly true and undoubted matter. It is impossible, I say, that this estimation should be capable of being known from the collision of bodies, however manifestly it may show itself in a thousand other cases.",["- Long second sentence restated as two; \"diese\" (the collision) resolved as the subject of \"darbiethen würde\".","- *Kräften-Schätzung* rendered \"estimation of forces\"; *schlechte Geschwindigkeit* rendered \"simple velocity\" (i.e. velocity alone, unsquared).","- *erkannt werden* rendered \"be known\" (Erkenntnis = knowledge)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0332":["§ 68.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0333":["Elaboration of this proof.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0334":["My proof rests on the following.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0335":["On this point there is agreement: that the motion of bodies through impact can be employed for the purpose of which we speak only if the force which a moving body brings into others by impact is regarded as the effect by which we must measure the quantity of the cause that has exhausted itself in producing it. That is, the magnitude of the cause must be sought in the effects which are its consequence. It is therefore self-evident that we must take particular care to take, in the bodies struck, only that force which is really nothing other than the effect immediately produced by the onset of the other body; otherwise the whole measure we have sought is deceptive and useless. Now it is evident that, immediately after the moment in which the impelling body has exerted its effect in the body struck, all the force then present in the latter is an undoubted effect of the impact. Hence we must necessarily use this force, and no other, in order to make it the measure of the force which the impinging body expended in producing it. Now a body which receives its motion through the impact of another has, immediately after the moment in which the impact brought the force into it, and so long as it has not yet been able to move away from contact with the impelling body by any finite distance, already all the force that the latter could impart to it, but as yet no actual motion, since no time has been allowed for it; it has only a mere endeavour towards motion, and so a force that is dead, and has simple velocity as its measure. Thus the force that was present in the impelling body has exhausted itself in order to awaken in the other a force whose exact estimation can never be anything other than mere velocity, even if one wished by a hypothesis to posit in the impelling body a force whose measure were, I will not say the square, but even the cube, the biquadrate, and who knows what further powers of the velocity.",["- Sentences with *Das ist*, *Daher*, and the long \"Nun hat ein Körper…\" restated and punctuated for clarity; the parenthetical clause \"und wenn er also…\" turned into \"so long as\".","- *Würkung* = effect; *Anlauf*/*anlaufend* = onset/impinging; *todte Kraft* = dead force; *Quadratoquadrat* = biquadrate.","- \"welche… hat\" and \"die Kraft, die er… in Hervorbringung derselben\" — \"derselben\" resolved as the force in the body struck."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0336":["Now it would be an absurdity, one which would entirely overturn the law of the equality of effect and cause, to suppose that a force requiring estimation according to the square were expended in producing another that is estimated by velocity alone. For since the former is infinitely many times greater than the latter, this would be the same as saying that the whole content of a square had been expended in producing a line, and indeed a finite line. It is therefore clear that all the laws, of elastic as of inelastic bodies, will never furnish proof of any estimation other than simple velocity, and that by their very nature they must always be opposed to the living forces, however much one may exhaust one's inventiveness in devising cases which have the appearance of favouring them.",["- \"jene… diese\" resolved as the force estimated by the square and the force estimated by velocity respectively."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0337":["§ 69.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0338":["Since everything in the preceding section depends on taking, as the measure of the force of the impinging body, only that force of the body driven forward which is found in it immediately after the moment of the effect imparted, just as it detaches itself from contact with the impelling body, yet before this motion has actually taken place, I do not doubt that this will be the point against which the gentlemen whom I now have the honour to call my opponents will chiefly rebel. I wish I might be so fortunate as to forestall them with what follows.",["- \"dieses\" and \"diese Bewegung\" kept close to the German; \"jenem\" (the body driven forward) resolved by sense."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0339":["Continued proof that in the impact of bodies nothing is to be considered except the initial velocity of the body struck.",["- Heading; *Anfangs-Geschwindigkeit* = initial velocity."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0340":["Either the force which the body struck has in the moment before it moves away from the impelling body is equal to the force which it has after it has actually moved and withdrawn from it, or it is not equal. If the former, my restriction is not even needed; we may take the force of the body struck at whatever moment of the motion we please, and we shall find it everywhere simply in accordance with the velocity,* since it is equal to that which it had before its motion was actual. If it is not equal, what is meant by this is unfailingly that the force present in the body struck, after it has moved away from the impelling body, is greater than it was in contact. But if so, I admit that this is precisely the reason why I cannot use that force to estimate the force of the impact. For if in the body struck, having already moved away from the impinging body after the impact, there is one degree of force more than was in it while it still touched the latter, this new degree of force is also no effect of the impinging body, for bodies act on one another only so long as they are in contact; the former alone, [that is, the body struck,] is its source. Hence the force present in contact [*jene*] can most fittingly be used to measure the force that was consumed in producing it.",["- Final sentences: \"der erstere\" is ambiguous; I take it as the body struck (the new degree comes from itself), and \"jene\" as the force in contact — both bracketed or flagged as judgment. Doubtful.","- \"Denn so lange…\": footnote follows in next passage."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0341":["* For so long as the motion of the body struck has not yet become actual (that is, so long as it has not yet moved away from the impelling body), so long is its force, even by the admission of the Leibnizians, still dead.",["- Footnote to the preceding passage."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0342":["§ 70.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0343":["We have happily surmounted the difficulties which the collision of bodies might have presented to the old law of Descartes. I flatter myself that I may now boldly say that the party of Herr von Leibniz will gain nothing against it from this side. Let us endeavour to be able to boast the same of the remaining difficulties.",["- \"ihm\" = the Cartesian law; \"dieses auch rühmen\" rendered \"boast the same\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0344":["§ 71.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0345":["On the defence of the living forces through the composition of motion.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0346":["Let us now consider those cases which the defenders of the living forces have borrowed from the compound motions of bodies in order to support their estimation. A bad cause always has this mark about it, that it likes to hide behind obscure and tangled cases. So too the party of the living forces has sought to turn to account the confusion into which one easily falls in considering compound motions. We shall endeavour to strip off the cloak of obscurity which has hitherto favoured the living forces, and favoured them alone. Herr Bülfinger has deserved best of all in this kind of proof, and his thoughts shall therefore be the first that we put to the test.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0347":["We find his treatise in the first volume of the *Commentarii Petropolitani*. The proposition which lies at the foundation of his whole structure is as follows.* A body A receives two motions at the same time: one in the direction AB with the velocity AB, and another, in a direction perpendicular to the former, with the velocity AC. It moves through the diagonal of this rectangular parallelogram in the same time in which it would traverse either of the sides separately. Now the forces directed along the sides of the parallelogram are not opposed to one another, and so neither can take anything away from the other. Hence the force which the body has when it yields to both, that is, when it moves along the diagonal, will be equal to the forces along the sides taken together. But on Descartes's estimation this would not hold. For the diagonal AD is always smaller than the two sides AB and AC taken together. In all other possible estimations as well, moreover, the force which the body has with the velocity AD would never be equal to the sum of the forces with the velocities AB and AC, save in the one case in which they are estimated according to the squares of their velocities. From this Herr Bülfinger concludes that the force of a body in actual motion can be measured by nothing other than the square of its velocity.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0348":["* Fig. X.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0349":["§ 72.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0350":["Herr Bülfinger has not erred altogether in his proof. His inferences are, at bottom, perfectly correct. It is only their application that is faulty, and it bears the mark of a hasty judgment.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0351":["In what sense the Bülfingerian proof is correct.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0352":["If we regard the motion which the body has along* the side AC in the customary way, namely, as one with which the body strives to strike the surface CD perpendicularly, then it is certain that the other lateral motion, in the line AB, is in this respect in no way opposed to it. For that motion runs parallel to the surface CD, and consequently draws the body neither towards it nor away from it. In just the same way the lateral motion AC will not be opposed to the motion along the other side AB, in respect of the effect which the body strives to produce with the latter against the surface BD, since AC likewise runs parallel to this surface. But what follows from this? Nothing further than that the body, when it yields to both lateral motions at once and traverses the diagonal, will exert against the surfaces CD and BD, at one and the same time, the same effects as it would have exerted in separate motion through the sides. In its motion through the diagonal the body therefore has within it, in respect of the two surfaces CD and BD, a force equal to the sum of both forces along the sides. But this equality is to be found in it only under the condition which I have stated.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0353":["* Fig. X.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0354":["§ 73.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0355":["Herr Bülfinger has drawn a conclusion that goes beyond the sense of the point in dispute.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0356":["Herr Bülfinger did not bind himself to this condition, although the nature of his proof should have obliged him to do so. He concluded straight off: Therefore the body in its motion through the diagonal has within it a force equal to the sum of both lateral forces.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0357":["This proposition, put forward without any restriction, ordinarily takes on a meaning far removed from the sense of the conclusion in Bülfinger's proof. For when it is said that a body which possesses such and such a velocity has such and such a force within it, what is understood is the force which it would exert in the straight direction of its motion upon an object which it strikes perpendicularly. When, then, the force of a body is spoken of in so restricted a manner, its magnitude must be sought to be determined in this sense and in no other. Otherwise one believes that the body has in the straight direction of its motion a certain force which it can in fact exert only sideways, given a certain position of the object it strikes. Herr Bülfinger, who overlooked this, has thereby exposed himself to the charge of a *fallacia ignorationis elenchi*. For he abandoned the sense of the point in dispute. He ought to have proved that the body, in its motion through the diagonal, will strike an object set perpendicularly against the direction of this motion with a force equal to the sum of the forces with which it would strike the surfaces lying in its way by the separate lateral motions. Instead he proved that it does indeed exert the aggregate of these forces, but only against the two lateral surfaces CD and BD, and not against the perpendicular surface set directly against its motion.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0358":["§ 74.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0359":["The same proof is faulty in respect of the point about which the dispute is waged.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0360":["Everything therefore depends merely on my proving that a body moved in the diagonal AD does not have within it, in the straight direction AD, the sum of the lateral forces taken together. For this I need nothing further than to regard each of the lateral motions as compound, as mathematicians are accustomed to do.* Let the lateral motion AB, then, be compounded of the motions AF and AH, and the lateral motion AC, on the other hand, of the motions AE and AG. Now the motions AF and AE are directly opposed to one another, and since they are equal, they cancel each other. There remain, then, only the motion with the velocity AH and that with the velocity AG, with which the body continues in the direction of the diagonal. Accordingly the whole force of the two lateral motions is not present in the direction of the diagonal; in this respect only a part of it is to be found. Further, the motions AF and AE in any case run parallel to the surface BH, which the body in its diagonal motion strikes perpendicularly, so that neither of them can reach it. From this as from the foregoing one sees that the body will not strike the object set perpendicularly against its motion through AD with the sum of the forces along the sides AC and AB.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0361":["* Fig. XI.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0362":["§ 75.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0363":["Conclusion from this.",["- 0346: \"Schätzung\" rendered \"estimation\" (the measure of force); \"lebendige Kräfte\" as \"living forces\", kept literal.","- 0347: The OCR spacing \"Commentarii Petropolitani .\" tidied; the title italicised. \"wie\" after the comparative (\"kleiner, wie\") rendered as \"than\". The sentence \"bewegt sich die Diagonallinie ... hindurch\" is made grammatical in English (\"moves through the diagonal\"). \"Cartesens\" = Descartes.","- 0347: Long sentence on forces divided into several; the footnote marker (*) kept after the sentence it stands in.","- 0352: The marker \"nach *\" follows the text; I placed the asterisk after \"along\". The Latin-style figure letters (A–H) are referred to Kant's figures, which are not reproduced.","- 0357: Long final sentence divided; \"Verstande/Sinne\" for the sense of the question rendered \"sense\". *fallacia ignorationis elenchi* kept in Latin and italics.","- 0360: Text has \"die Bewegung ... übrig\" with a singular where plural is needed; I rendered the plural sense. \"Fläche BH\" is as printed (possibly a misprint for another surface); flagged as doubtful.","- 0348, 0353, 0361: Footnote texts are only figure references, given as they stand."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0364":["Everything is now settled. For we now know that a body moving along the diagonal against an obstacle standing perpendicularly in its way does not exert the whole sum of the two lateral forces, which the body possesses with each of its lateral motions against the surfaces set equally perpendicular to them. It follows necessarily that the force in the motion along the diagonal is smaller than the two lateral forces taken together. Consequently the force of a body cannot be estimated according to the square of its velocity; for on that method of estimation the aforesaid equality would necessarily have to be found, and in fact it is not to be found.",["- Restated the long final sentence as two; the colon clause is kept as a \"for\" clause.","- \"Vorwurf\" rendered \"obstacle\" (the object thrown in the body's way); \"Seitenkräfte\" = \"lateral forces\"; \"Geschwindigkeit\" = \"velocity\".","- \"gedachte Gleichheit\" = the equality of the diagonal force with the sum of the lateral forces, as the argument requires."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0365":["§ 76.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0366":["From the Bülfinger case the living forces themselves are refuted.",["- \"Fall\" rendered \"case\"; \"lebendige Kräfte\" = \"living forces\" (*vis viva*)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0367":["We do not wish to rest content with this. Instead of fearing Herr Bülfinger's conclusions, we would rather accept them willingly, in order to prove Descartes's laws by means of them. A good cause always has this mark: that even the weapons of its opponents must serve in its defence; and we have seen more than once that our own cause can boast of this advantage. * By what has now been proved, the lateral motion AB brings into the direction of the diagonal no other velocity than the velocity AH alone, with which the body, in isolated motion, would strike the surface BH perpendicularly. Again, the other lateral motion AC, by itself, brings into the direction of the diagonal only the velocity AG, with which the body would strike the surface CG at a right angle. Out of the forces which these two motions AH and AG carry with them, the whole force of the diagonal is composed; and what is not to be found in those two will therefore not be present in this one either, since otherwise the sum could contain more than the *summandi* taken together. The force with the velocity AD is thus to be equal to the force with the velocity AH plus the force with the velocity AG; and the question is what powers of AH, of AG, and of AD must be taken in order that the sum of the first two shall equal the last. Here it is clear from the simplest principles of arithmetic that, if one wished to estimate the forces by a power of the lines AH, AG and AD greater than the first power, the force of the body with the velocity AD, estimated in this way, would be greater than the sum of the forces with the velocities AH and AG. If, however, one wished to take a smaller function (as Herr Bülfinger expresses it) than the function of the simple velocities, the aggregate of the partial forces would be greater than the whole force arising from them, which has the velocity AD as its mark. On the contrary, they will be found equal if everything together is estimated by the bare velocity. From this it follows that one must either set the forces in proportion to the velocities AH, AG and AD, or admit that the aggregate can be smaller or greater than the *aggregandi* taken together.",["- Divided several long sentences; \"Merkmahl\" rendered \"mark\"; \"schlechten Geschwindigkeiten\" = \"simple velocities\" (schlecht = plain).","- \"Potenzen\" = \"powers\"; \"Function\" kept as Bülfinger's term. *Summandi* and *aggregandi* left in Latin form as in the German.","- The asterisk marks the footnote reference (Fig. XI); \"Wir ... gesehen\" clause kept as in the text.","- \"die Unsrige\" taken as \"our cause\" (Sache), by the argument's sense."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0368":["* Fig. XI.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0369":["§ 77.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0370":["The same refutation in another manner.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0371":["We can demonstrate the same thing in another way. Like Herr Bülfinger, we assume that the lateral forces ** AB and AC are communicated to the body a by the impact of two equal spheres, with the velocities ba = AB and ca = AC, and that these two impulses, occurring simultaneously, give rise to the motion and force along the diagonal. But since it makes no difference, we shall suppose that these spheres set out from C and B and strike the body a at the point D with the velocities CD = ba and BD = ca. It is undeniable that the body a at this place will receive from the said spheres just the same force as it could receive at the point A; for the place makes no difference at all, everything else being equal. The question therefore is: what force will the sphere a at the point D receive, against the perpendicular surface FE, from these two impacts BD and CD made upon it at the same time? I answer: the sphere B, with the motion BD, will impart to the body a, in respect of the effect upon this surface, properly only the velocity BE; and from the onset of the sphere C, with the velocity CD, the same body A will acquire only the velocity CF, with which it can act upon the surface FE at the point D. For the other two motions, Bg and Ch, which a has still received from this twofold impact, run parallel to the surface; consequently they do not meet it, but rather annul one another, since they are opposed and equal. Both lateral forces BD and CD, or, what is the same, AC and AB, have therefore imparted to the body, in respect of the surface which it strikes perpendicularly in the diagonal motion, only such a force as is equal to the sum of the forces with the velocities BE and CF. Consequently, first, they have not imparted their whole forces; and secondly, they have imparted a force of which it is as evident here as in the previous section that it must stand to those from which it is composed as the velocity AD stands to the velocities CF and BE, and not as the squares of these.",["- Divided the last long sentence; \"Kugel a\" and \"Körper A\" are as printed (the German varies between a and A, probably a slip; left unaltered).","- \"Anlauf\" = \"onset\"; \"Antriebe\" = \"impulses\".","- \"** \" marks the footnote reference (Tab. II, Fig. XII). \"Perpendikularfläche\" = \"perpendicular surface\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0372":["** Tab. II. Fig. XII.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0373":["§ 78.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0374":["The direct force in the diagonal is not equal to the sum of the forces along the sides.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0375":["We see from the foregoing consideration that, if one presupposes that the forces exerted along the sides of the parallelogram in the diagonal motion are together equal to the force in the direction of the diagonal, it follows that the forces must be estimated according to the squares of the velocity. But we have at the same time proved that this presupposition is false, and that the effects which a body exerts in oblique motion, until all its force within it is exhausted, are always greater than what it would accomplish by a perpendicular impact.",["- \"Würkungen ... allemal größer\" kept as the German states it (the sense is as the author's argument goes; the comparison is awkward, not corrected)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0376":["This observation has the appearance of a paradoxical proposition. For it follows from it that a body can exert more force, in respect of certain surfaces opposed to it in a particular manner, than it is supposed to have in itself at all. For a body is said to have as much force as it expends by a perpendicular impact against an insuperable obstacle.",["- \"bey sich habe\" rendered \"to have in itself\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0377":["As regards the metaphysical solution of this difficulty, we may remain quite unconcerned; for however the matter may stand, mathematics has once for all pronounced its verdict, and after its judgment one can doubt no longer.",["- \"Schwürigkeit\" = \"difficulty\"; \"dörfen\" = \"need\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0378":["§ 79.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0379":["In the Leibnizian estimation of forces the sum of the forces exerted in an oblique direction is equal to the diagonal force; but in the Cartesian estimation the former is often infinitely many times greater than the latter.",["- \"Kräftenschätzung\" = \"estimation of forces\" (the eighteenth-century spelling of the genitive plural compound)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0380":["From the division of motion it is clear that, if a body runs obliquely against many surfaces one after another, it will lose its motion entirely when the sum of the squares of all the sines of the angles of incidence (*sinuum angulorum incidentiae*) is equal to the square of the whole sine (*sinus totius*), which indicates the initial velocity of its motion. So far all the mechanicians are agreed, the Cartesians not excepted. But from this there follows, for the Leibnitzians in particular, the following. If the estimation by the square is allowed to hold, the body has lost all its motion when the forces exerted in oblique direction are, taken together, equal to the force which inheres in it in straight motion. On the Cartesian estimation, on the other hand, the matter is quite otherwise. The forces which the body exerts through many successive impacts in oblique direction, until all its motion is consumed, are, according to that estimation, together much greater than the single undivided force which it possesses in straight motion. Hence, on that estimation, the body has not yet lost its motion when the sum of all the forces exerted in divided motion is already equal to its whole undivided force.\n\nFor in respect of many oblique surfaces a body can accomplish far more than against a surface which it strikes perpendicularly in a straight direction. It does so in this manner: [on the assumption that the inclination of the impact is at equal angles on all the oblique surfaces,] the magnitude of the force required to consume a body's force by obliquely opposed obstacles stands to that which would cancel it in a straight direction as the *sinus totus* to the sine of the angle of incidence (*sinus anguli incidentiae*). Thus, for example, if the *sinus totus* is to the sine of the angle of incidence as 2 : 1, this force is also twice as great as the other; if as 8 : 1, eight times as great; and if the sine is infinitely small, it is infinitely many times greater than the power of the obstacles that would have sufficed to consume the body's whole motion in a directly opposed direction.\n\nAccordingly, on the Leibnitzian estimation a certain obstacle entirely deprives a body of its force, whereas on the estimation of Descartes the same obstacle, in the same direction, is able to destroy only an infinitely small part of it. That is to say, on the estimation by the square, if the whole power of the summed obstacles which the moving body has overcome is finite, the loss of the body's force is also finite, however oblique the motion in which the body has overcome these obstacles. On the estimation by velocities, on the other hand, the total force of the effects which a body has exerted may be finite, and the loss of the body's force nevertheless infinitely small, provided only that the angle at which it overcomes all these obstacles is infinitely small.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0381":["This difference is astonishing. Some effect of it must show itself somewhere in nature, wherever that may be, and it will be worth the trouble to seek it out. For the consequence will be not only that we can decide whether the force of a body along the diagonal of a rectangular parallelogram is or is not equal to the sum of the forces along the sides, but also whether the estimation of Herr von Leibnitz or that of Descartes is the true one; for the one question is inseparably bound up with the other.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0382":["§ 80.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0383":["The living forces are refuted by a new case.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0384":["The motion of a body in a circular line about a centre towards which it is drawn by its gravity (of which kind are the motions of the planets) is the case we are seeking.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0385":["Let us suppose a body that has received a centrifugal impetus sufficient to make it run round the earth in a circular line. Let us also abstract from all obstacles, other than gravity, which could diminish its motion. It is then certain, first, that the velocity of its motion will be finite, and second, that it will continue undiminished to infinity, with the same degree and in the same line. I take these two lemmas as my foundation, for they are approved by both parties, the Leibnitzian as well as the Cartesian.\n\nI further assume, thirdly, that gravity brings into a freely moving body, in a finite time, a finite force, or equally consumes a finite force in it, when the two forces, that which inheres in the body and that with which gravity presses, act against one another. Now the supposed body, which runs in a circle about the given centre, is unceasingly exposed to the pressure of gravity. Through the sum of all the infinitely small pressures of gravity it therefore undergoes, in a finite time, a finite force with which it is driven towards the centre of its revolution, *per Lemma 3*. Meanwhile the body, by its own proper force, holds the balance against all these pressures made upon it, since it keeps itself always at the same distance from the centre. Hence in any finite time it has also exerted a finite force in respect of the obstacles of gravity which it has overcome.\n\nNow it is clear from what we saw in § 79 that, when a body has overcome in oblique direction a certain number of obstacles which together amount to a finite magnitude of force, it must at the same time (if the Leibnitzian estimation is admitted) suffer a loss of finite magnitude in the force inhering in it. Consequently the supposed body, in any finite time of its circular course, loses a finite force through the restraints of gravity, and therefore loses in a certain determinate time its whole force and velocity; for the velocity which it possesses in its circular course is only finite. *Lemma 1.*",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0386":["Therefore either it cannot run in a circle at all, unless it has an infinite velocity, or one must grant that a body can here accomplish, through the sum of all the oblique effects, infinitely more than the force it possesses in a straight impact, and that the Leibnitzian measure of forces, which does not grant this, is false.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0387":["§ 81.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0388":["Because the thought which we have set out here is very fruitful in consequences, we will remove all the small difficulties around it and make it, as far as possible, clear and plain.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0389":["He knows that a body running in a circle exerts against gravity just such an effect as if it ran against an oblique surface.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0390":["One must first learn to grasp clearly that the force which the moving body applies in circular motion to hold the balance against gravity exerts an oblique effect, and is to be compared with the impact of a body against an oblique surface, just as we actually did in the preceding section.",["Passage 0380: restated the long closing sentences as several shorter ones; \"Schätzung\" rendered \"estimation\", \"Kraft\" as \"force\", and the bracketed words in the \"dergestalt\" clause are my supplied \"on the assumption\" (German has parentheses there).","0380: \"Sinui\" (dative) corrected silently to *sinus anguli incidentiae*; the passage's \"Sie\" refers to the required force (Größe der Kraft).","0380: \"Die Cartesianische Schätzung\" is the estimation by velocities; \"nach dem Quadrat\" the Leibnizian one. The \"Also hat alsdenn…\" sentence is the Cartesian consequence.","0385: \"Lehnsätze\" rendered \"lemmas\"; \"Lemma 3\" and \"Lemma 1\" are Kant's own cross-references and left as in the text. \"Schwerdrückungen\" rendered \"pressures of gravity.\"","0385: \"Kreislauf/Cirkellauf\" rendered \"circular course.\"","0389: \"Er weiß\" is odd (the antecedent is lacking); it may be an OCR or textual slip, perhaps for \"Es ist\"/\"Man weiß\". Translated literally as \"He knows\"; doubtful reading.","0390: \"im vorigen §\" taken as § 80."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0391":["For this purpose let us represent the infinitely small arcs which the body traverses in its circular motion as so many infinitely small straight lines, just as in mathematics the circle is commonly regarded as a polygon of infinitely many sides. * The body which has now traversed the infinitely small line *ab* would, if gravity set no obstacle in its way, continue in the straight direction of this motion, and in the second infinitely small portion of time would be at *d*. But through the resistance of gravity it is compelled to leave this direction and to describe the infinitely small line *bc*. This obstacle of gravity has thus taken from it, *per resolutionem virium* [by resolution of forces], the lateral motion *ac*, which is expressed by the perpendicular *ac*, drawn to the line *bd* extended to *c*. Through the obstacle of gravity at the point *b*, therefore, the body suffers precisely the same resistance as it would have suffered from a surface *cd* against which it had run at the angle *abc*; for the obstacle which this surface opposes to it is expressed, just as here, by the small perpendicular *ac*. We may therefore quite properly compare the force which a body exerts in its circular motion against gravity, which draws it downward, with its impact against oblique surfaces, and estimate it in just the same way as the latter. Q.E.D.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0392":["* Fig. XIII.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0393":["§ 82.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0394":["The body moving in a circle performs, in any finite time, against the obstacles of gravity an effect of a finite force.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0395":["Secondly, the third of the principles assumed in our proof in § 80 seems still to need some confirmation. At least, when one has to do with such opponents, one cannot be careful enough even with regard to the most evident truths. For the dispute over living forces has sufficiently convinced us how much partisanship for certain opinions can be more powerful and more compelling than the bare strength of truth, and how far the freedom of the human understanding extends, to go on doubting, or to suspend its judgment, even in the case of the most evident truths.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0396":["For the proposition that gravity brings into a body moving freely a finite force also in any given finite time, I could appeal to § 32. But that section already has its opponents among the defenders of living forces, and it is better to strike them down with their own weapons. The body assumed, which in its circular motion has traversed the arc *af* in a finite time, receives the pressures of all the springs of gravity to which it is unceasingly exposed throughout the whole finite space *af*. Now even by the admission of the Leibnizians, the springs of the gravity-producing matter [*der schwermachenden Materie*] situated in a certain finite space, which communicate their pressure throughout to a body, produce in it a finite force. Ergo, etc.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0397":["§ 83.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0398":["The Conclusion.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0399":["Accordingly, the force exerted in divided motion, if it is estimated as proportional to the squares of the sides of the rectangular parallelogram, is wholly inconsistent with the best-known laws of the circular motion of bodies, and with the central forces which they exert. The lateral forces in any compound motion are therefore not, as the Leibnizian estimation requires, in the proportion of the squares of their velocities. Hence the conclusion is also general: that the estimation according to the square is entirely mistaken; for every motion can be regarded as compounded, as is known from the first principles of mechanics.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0400":["§ 84.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0401":["How the Cartesian estimation relieves this difficulty.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0402":["It is still necessary to remark how admirably the Cartesian estimation of forces relieves the difficulty under which the Leibnizian, as we have now seen, succumbs.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0403":["It is known from mathematics that the small line *ac*, * which is parallel and equal to the *sinus versus* *bi* of the infinitely small arc *ab*, is an infinitely small quantity of the second degree, and so infinitely many times smaller than the infinitely small line *ab*. Now *ac* is the sine of the angle at which the body everywhere in its circular motion works against the pressure of gravity, and *ab*, as an infinitely small part of the body's own absolute motion, is its *sinus totus*. But from § 79, proved above, it is known that when a body in oblique motion acts against a certain obstacle in such a way that the sine of the angle of incidence is throughout infinitely small in relation to the *sinus totus*, the force lost through the obstacles is, on the Cartesian estimation, infinitely small in comparison with the total power of all the obstacles overcome. Hence the body in its circular course loses through the pressures of gravity no finite force until it has overcome, in the whole sum of all the restraints of gravity, a force that is infinitely great. Now the sum of all the pressures of gravity through a finite time amounts to only a finite force (§ 80, Lemma 3), and consequently to an infinite force only after an infinite time. Therefore the body which runs in a circle about a centre toward which it is drawn by its gravity loses, through the obstacles of gravity, a finite force only in an infinite time, and consequently in any finite time infinitely little. On the Leibnizian estimation, by contrast, the loss under these very circumstances would amount to something finite in every finite time (§ 80). Consequently the Cartesian estimation is, in this case, not subject to the difficulty to which, as we have seen, the Leibnizian is always exposed.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0404":["* Fig. XIII.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0405":["§ 85.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0406":["A further new contradiction to which living forces are here exposed.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0407":["The objection which we have now raised against living forces discloses at the same time a curious kind of contradiction in the estimation of forces according to the square. For everyone is agreed that the force estimated by the rectangle of the velocity multiplied into itself must have infinitely more power than that which is expressed merely by the simple measure of the velocity, and that in relation to the latter it is what the surface is to the line. Here, however, the very opposite appears: namely, that in the case we have seen, where both kinds of force are supposed to act in entirely like circumstances, the Leibnizian force is capable of infinitely less than the Cartesian, and is consumed by infinitely fewer obstacles than the latter. This is a contradiction than which none greater can be conceived.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0408":["§ 86.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0409":["The destruction of the general principle that the magnitude of force in compound motion is equal to that in simple motion overturns at the same time many more cases which the champions of living forces have built upon this same ground.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0410":["Refutation of Bernoulli's case of the tension of four equal springs.",["- 0391: \"Cirkelbewegung\" rendered \"circular motion\"; \"Hinderniß\" as \"obstacle\"; the figure letters are kept as in the text (the original reads \"bc\" for the arc described, and I leave it so).","- 0391: \"per resolutionem virium\" kept in Latin italics with English in square brackets, as supplied gloss.","- 0391: the original's \"gefället\" (gefällt, \"drawn/dropped\") rendered \"drawn\"; \"W. Z. E.\" (was zu erweisen) rendered \"Q.E.D.\"","- 0395: \"bedörfen\" and \"Partheylichkeit\" modernised in sense; the long second sentence is divided in two.","- 0396: \"Federn der Schwere\" rendered \"springs of gravity\" (Kant's elastic-matter image); \"der schwermachenden Materie\" kept as bracketed German. \"§. 32\" is a cross-reference to an earlier section.","- 0399: \"rechtwinklichten Parallelogramms\" is the rectangular parallelogram of the composition of motions; \"Seitenkräfte\" rendered \"lateral forces\".","- 0403: \"Sinus versus\" and \"sinus totus\" kept as Latin technical terms in italics. \"Schwürigkeit\" is \"difficulty\" (Schwierigkeit); I supply \"no\" to \"not\" only by following the German \"nicht eher … als\".","- 0403: \"bi\" and \"ac\" are taken from the text as printed; the letters of the figure are not checked against the plate and the reading may be corrupt."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0411":["The Bernoullian case, which Herr von Wolf cites in his Mechanics, is one of the most considerable among these. He assumes four springs, each of which requires an equal force to be bent. He then lets a body with two degrees of velocity strike perpendicularly against the first spring at an angle of 30 degrees, whose sine is as 1; then, with the remainder of the motion, against the second at an angle whose sine is likewise as 1; and so also against the third, and finally against the fourth. This body now bends each of these springs. With two degrees of velocity it therefore exerts four degrees of force, and consequently it must have had them, since otherwise it could not have exerted them. Hence the force of this body is not as its velocity, 2, but as the square of the velocity.",["- Restated the long second sentence by inserting semicolons; the German \"perpendikular anlaufen\" is placed after the angle clauses, and I kept that order, though the sense of \"perpendicular\" alongside \"30 degrees\" is awkward in the original.","- \"Kraft nöthig haben gespannet zu werden\" rendered \"requires an equal force to be bent\"; *spannen* = bend/tension (of a spring).","- \"dessen Sinus wie 1 ist\" kept literally (\"as 1\"); the sine of 30° is ½, so the text is doubtful or loosely stated."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0412":["I do not demand to maintain that a body with two degrees of velocity could under no circumstances exert four degrees of force. But it can exert them only in an oblique impact. It is enough that we have proved that its force in a direct impact is always only as 2, and that in oblique motion it is always greater than in the perpendicular. Now everyone estimates the force of a body by the power to be found in it in perpendicular impact. Therefore, in that kind of action which is free of ambiguity, and in which all opponents agree that it is the true measure of force, the advantage lies on the side of Descartes against the party of the living forces.",["- \"Ich verlange es nicht zu behaupten\" rendered \"I do not demand to maintain\"; i.e. I do not insist on asserting.","- Restated the last sentence's relative clauses (\"darinn alle Gegner zusammen stimmen, daß sie ... sey\") as \"in which all opponents agree that it is the true measure of force\"; *Gewalt* = power, *Kraft* = force.","- *Parthey der lebendigen Kräfte* = \"party of the living forces\" (*vis viva*)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0413":["§ 87.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0414":["Finally, there is yet another case that rests upon the composition of motion, which might well be called the Achilles of our opponents.",["- \"Achilles\" is the name of a classic (Zenonian) argument, i.e. the opponents' strongest argument."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0415":["Herr von Mairan's objection to the Hermannian case.",["- Marginal heading, rendered as a title."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0416":["It consists in this. A body A, whose mass is 1 and whose velocity is 2, strikes at one time, at an angle of 60 degrees, two bodies B and B, each of which has a mass of 2. Here the impinging body A remains at rest after the impact, and the bodies B and B each move with one degree of velocity, and consequently both taken together with four degrees of force.",["- Divided the German into short sentences after the colon.","- Figures as given in the German; \"4 Graden Kraft\" is the author's computation and I have not altered it."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0417":["Herr von Mairan has very well observed how strange and paradoxical it turns out that a particular case, restricted to certain circumstances only, should prove a new estimation of forces, which, if it were true, would have to show itself without distinction in each and every circumstance. The Leibnizians are always so bold as to demand that when a body exerts four degrees of force, in whatever manner it may be, one can always safely say that it will exert just the same force in perpendicular direction also. But in the present case it is evident that everything depends upon a determinate number of the elements which are to be moved, and upon a determinate position of them with respect to the impinging body. Consequently the matter will stand quite differently if these determinations were altered; and so one is greatly deceived if one reasons thus: the body has exerted this or that force in these circumstances, therefore it must (to speak straightforwardly, without any restriction) also have this or that force, and, if one wishes, could also release it in a perpendicular action.",["- Divided the first long sentence at the semicolon into separate sentences; \"allein in diesem gegenwärtigen Falle\" is rendered \"But in the present case\".","- *Bestimmungen* = determinations; \"mithin\" rendered \"and so\".","- *Kräftenschätzung* = \"estimation of forces\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0418":["I have wished only to express the sense of Herr von Mairan's thought, which he opposed to the Hermannian case in his reply to the objections that Madame du Châtelet had made to him in her *Physics* [*Naturlehre*]. But it seems to me that the whole matter can be disposed of much more easily and convincingly by means of what we have hitherto noted with regard to the composition and division of forces, and that it has for the most part been disposed of already by this. For this reason I believe that the reader of these pages will readily excuse me from further prolixity, by recalling what I have remarked on this point.",["- \"Frau von Chastellet\" in the text is rendered \"Madame du Châtelet\" (the standard form); *Naturlehre* is her *Institutions de physique*, bracketed German supplied.","- Restated the last clause (\"durch Herbeyziehung ... überheben\") more plainly as \"by recalling what I have remarked\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0419":["§ 88.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0420":["Herr von Mairan is the only one among the defenders of Descartes who has made any reflections upon the choice of the grounds on which the Leibnizians wish to build a new estimation of forces; but he has done so only in the single case which we cited in the preceding section. This kind of inquiry seems to be of no great importance if one looks at it superficially, but it is in fact of quite excellent utility, as much as any method in the art of thinking can be.",["- \"§pho\" is an abbreviated form of \"Paragrapho\"; rendered \"section\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0421":["Utility of this method of Herr von Mairan.",["- Marginal heading."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0422":["We must have a method by means of which, in any case whatever, through a general consideration of the principles upon which a certain opinion has been built, and through comparing them with the conclusion drawn from them, we can judge whether the nature of the premisses comprehends everything that is required in respect of the doctrines inferred from them. This is done when we carefully note the determinations that attach to the nature of the conclusion, and pay good heed whether, in constructing the proof, principles have been chosen that are limited to the particular determinations contained in the conclusion. If this is not found to be so, we may safely believe that these inferences, defective in this way, prove nothing, even though we cannot yet discover where the fault actually lies, and even if this were never to become known.\n\nThus, for instance, from a general consideration of the motions of elastic bodies I inferred that the phenomena which show themselves in their collision could not possibly prove a new estimation of forces differing from the Cartesian. For I recalled that all these phenomena are resolved by the mechanicians from the single source of the product of mass into velocity, together with elasticity. Of this a hundred proofs can be shown to the Leibnizians, all having the greatest geometers as their authors, and which one sees them themselves confirm countless times by their own approval. Therefore, I concluded, what has been brought about solely by force estimated according to the simple measure of velocity can furnish proof of no other estimation than that according to velocity. At that time I did not yet know where precisely the fault in the Leibnizians' inferences about the collision of elastic bodies was to be sought. But once I had been convinced in the manner indicated that a fallacy must lie hidden somewhere in them, however concealed it might be, I applied all my attention to seeking it out, and it seems to me that I have found it in more than one place.",["- Divided this very long passage into two paragraphs and several shorter sentences; the \"Also habe ich z. E.\" marks the example and is rendered \"Thus, for instance\".","- *Vordersätze* = premisses; *Schlußsatz*/*Conclusion* = conclusion; *Bestimmungen* = determinations.","- *Phänomena* kept as \"phenomena\"; *Beweisthum abgeben* = \"furnish proof\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0423":["This method is the chief source of this whole treatise.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0424":["In a word, this whole treatise is solely and entirely a creature of this method of thinking. I will confess it candidly. All those proofs in favour of the living forces, whose weakness I now believe I fully comprehend, I at first regarded as so many geometrical demonstrations, in which I suspected not the slightest error, and in which I should perhaps never have found a single one, had not the general consideration of the conditions under which Herr von Leibniz's estimation is established given my reflection an entirely different turn. I saw that the actuality of motion is the condition of this measure of forces, and that it constitutes the proper reason why the force of the moving body should not be estimated in the same way as the force of a body that merely strives towards motion.\n\nBut when I weighed the nature of this condition, I readily grasped the following. The condition can be placed in one and the same genus with the condition of dead force, and differs from it only in magnitude. It therefore cannot possibly have a consequence that is different *toto genere* from the consequence of the conditions of a dead force. Nor does the consequence remain any less infinitely different from it, even when the condition that is the one cause of this consequence is set so near to the other condition that it almost merges with it.\n\nThus I saw, with a certainty that yields nothing to the geometrical, that the actuality of motion cannot be a sufficient ground for concluding that the forces of bodies in this state must be as the square of their velocity. For in a motion of infinitely short duration, or, what is the same thing, in the mere striving towards it, they have nothing but the velocity as their measure. I inferred from this that if mathematics has on its side only the actuality of motion as the ground of the estimation by the square, and nothing else, its inferences must limp badly.\n\nArmed with this well-founded distrust of all the Leibnizian proofs, I attacked the inferences of the defenders of this estimation. My purpose was not only to know, as I now did, that there must be errors in them, but also to know in what these consist. I flatter myself that my undertaking has not wholly failed me.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0425":["§ 89.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0426":["The lack of this method has been one cause why certain manifest errors have remained hidden for so very long.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0427":["Had men at all times applied themselves to this manner of thinking, they could have spared themselves many errors in philosophy; at the least it would have been a means of extricating themselves from them much sooner. I venture even to say that the tyranny of errors over the human understanding, which has at times lasted through whole centuries, has arisen chiefly from the lack of this method, or of others akin to it, and that one should therefore henceforth apply oneself to it before others, in order to guard against that evil in future. We shall prove this.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0428":["What the remedy must be like by which the long duration of errors is prevented.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0429":["Suppose that by means of certain inferences, which conceal somewhere a very plausible error, one believes oneself to have proved a certain opinion. Suppose further that one has no other means of becoming aware of the invalidity of the proof than that the error hidden in it should first be discovered. One would then have to know beforehand what kind of error it is that makes the proof untenable, before one could say that there is one in it. If, I say, one has no other method than this, then I maintain that the error will remain undiscovered for an uncommonly long time, and that the proof will deceive countless times before the deception comes to light.\n\nThe reason is as follows. I presuppose that when the propositions and inferences occurring in a proof are perfectly plausible and have the appearance of the best-known truths, the understanding will give its assent to them and will not engage in any laborious and protracted search for an error in them. For in that case the proof, so far as the conviction it produces in the understanding is concerned, counts for just as much as one that has geometrical rigour and correctness. And the error that lies hidden among the inferences, because it is not perceived, does just as little to diminish assent as if it were not to be met with in the proof at all. Hence the understanding must either never give assent to any proof, or it must do so in a case where it sees nothing resembling an error, that is, where it suspects none, even though one were hidden in it.\n\nIn such a case, then, it will never apply any special effort to the search for an error, since it has no motive for doing so. Consequently the error will come to light only through a happy accident, and so will commonly remain hidden for a very long time before it is discovered; for this happy accident may fail to occur for many years, and often for whole centuries. This is almost the chief origin of those errors which, to the shame of the human understanding, have persisted through many ages, and which a very easy reflection has afterwards laid bare. For the error that lurks somewhere in a proof resembles at first sight a known truth. The proof is therefore regarded as perfectly rigorous; no error is suspected in it; none is therefore sought; and so it is found only by chance.\n\nFrom this it is easy to gather where the secret is to be sought that would prevent this difficulty and would make it easier for us to discover the errors we have committed. We must possess the art of divining and conjecturing from the premisses whether a proof constructed in a certain way will also contain, in respect of the conclusion [*Folgerung*], sufficient and complete principles. In this way we shall be able to infer whether an error must be present in it, even though we see it nowhere; and we shall then be moved to search for it, since we have sufficient reason to suspect it. This will therefore be a bulwark against the dangerous readiness of assent, which, without this motive, would divert all the activity of the understanding from the investigation of an object in which it finds no cause whatever for doubt and distrust. This method has helped us in §§ 25, 40, 62, 65 and 68, and it will continue to render us good service.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0430":["§ 90.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0431":["It would be a reflection of no small utility if one were to set out this method somewhat more distinctly and to show the rules of its application; but this kind of investigation does not fall within the jurisdiction of mathematics, to which this treatise ought properly to belong entirely. We shall, however, still offer a specimen of its usefulness in the refutation of the inferences that are borrowed in favour of the living forces from the composition of motions.",["- 0424: Long sentences divided into shorter ones, and the \"da … sie … kan\" clause (a condition of the same genus as that of dead force) restated as separate statements; \"Folgerung\" rendered \"consequence\" here.","- 0424: \"lebendige Kräfte\" / \"todte Kraft\" kept literally as \"living forces\" / \"dead force\"; \"Schwung\" rendered \"turn\".","- 0424: \"die Bedingung die eine Ursache dieser Folgerung ist\" is awkward; I read \"the one cause\" as referring to the condition of actual motion.","- 0429: The opening protasis (\"Wenn … wenn man, sage ich …\") is broken up into \"Suppose\" sentences to preserve the anacoluthic construction in readable English.","- 0429: \"Folgerung\" rendered \"conclusion [*Folgerung*]\" where it denotes what the proof is to establish.","- 0429: Paragraph divisions in the English are added for readability and are not in the German.","- 0428: This is a heading (the §'s title) as it stands, translated as given."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0432":["Consider the composition of dead pressures, for example of weights which pull upon a knot in oblique directions, when these directions enclose a right angle. Their initial velocities are likewise expressed by lines which are the sides of a rectangular parallelogram, and the pressure resulting from them is represented by the diagonal. Now here also the square of the diagonal is equal to the sum of the squares of the sides. It by no means follows from this, however, that the compound force will be related to one of the simple forces as the square of the lines expressing the initial velocities. On the contrary, everyone agrees that, notwithstanding this, the forces in this case are in simple proportion to the velocities.\n\nNow take also the composition of actual motions, as mathematics represents it, and compare it with this. The lines which constitute the sides and the diagonal of the parallelogram are nothing other than the velocities in these directions, precisely as is the case in the composition of dead pressures. The diagonal has the same relation to the sides as it has there, and the angle is also the same. Thus none of the determinations which enter into the mathematical representation of compounded actual motions differs from those under which, in the same science, the compositions of dead pressures are represented. Since, then, no estimation of forces according to the square of the velocity follows from the latter, none can be inferred from the former either; for the fundamental concepts are precisely the same, and therefore they have the same consequences.\n\nIt will still be objected that a manifest difference is to be found between them, since it is presupposed that the one is a composition of actual motions, the other only a composition of dead pressures. But this presupposition is vain and fruitless. It does not enter into the plan of the fundamental concepts which constitute the theorem; for mathematics does not express the actuality of the motion. The lines which are the object of the inquiry are only representations of the relation of the velocities. The restriction to the actuality of the motion is therefore here only an idle and inert concept, which is merely thought alongside and from which nothing is inferred in the mathematical consideration. From this it follows that nothing favourable to the living forces can be concluded from this kind of inquiry into compounded motions, and that there must be, if anything, some philosophical inferences mingled in, of which, however, there is now no question.\n\nIn this way, with the help of our vaunted method, we have now come to understand that the mathematical proofs for the living forces from the composition of motions must be false and full of errors. We do not yet know what errors they are; but we have a well-grounded presumption, or rather a certain conviction, that they must unfailingly be present in them. We must therefore not grudge the trouble of seeking them out in earnest. I have spared my readers this trouble, for it seems to me that I have found these errors and pointed them out in the immediately preceding paragraphs.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0433":["§ 91.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0434":["Our method is, finally, a sword against all the knots of subtleties and distinctions with which Herr Bülfinger has sought to secure his conclusions, which we have hitherto refuted, against an objection that his opponents can make to him. It is a great advantage to us that we can cut these knots through, since it would otherwise be very laborious to untie them.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0435":["Bülfinger's distinctions, by which he seeks to escape the objection of Herr von Mairan, are disposed of by means of this method.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0436":["Herr Bülfinger noted very well that it would be objected against him that his proofs, if they were correct, must prove precisely the same for the composition of dead pressures also. On this side, however, he has fortified himself with a bulwark of intricate metaphysical distinctions, such as he knows how to make. He observes that the effect of a dead force must be estimated by the product of the intensity into the path which it takes, and that this is expressed by the square of this line. One may therefore concede to the Cartesians that the effects in the composition of dead pressures are equal; but it does not yet follow from this that the forces must on that account also be equal. He adds: *in motibus isochronis solum actiones sunt ut vires; non in nisu mortuo*.\n\nA metaphysical inquiry has a singular effect in a mathematical dispute. The mathematician believes that he has no understanding of these subtleties; and even if he is unable to resolve them, he is far from letting himself be led astray by them. He proceeds along the guiding thread of geometry, and every other path is suspect to him. The geometers have behaved in the same way with regard to Herr Bülfinger's evasions. So far as I know, no one has yet engaged him with these weapons. People have spared themselves this trouble with good forethought; for a metaphysical inquiry, especially one so intricate and compounded, always leaves on every side countless lurking-places, to which one of the opponents can retreat without the other being able to pursue him or draw him out.\n\nWe did very well to attack Herr Bülfinger's conclusions from the outset on that side where, by his own admission, mathematics alone gives the verdict. But by means of our method, as I have already said, we are masters of these distinctions also, however impenetrable the coverings of obscurity behind which they have hidden themselves.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0437":["Our method forestalls Herr Bülfinger's distinctions.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0438":["The question here is chiefly this: whether Herr Bülfinger's distinctions can make valid the mathematical proof which he has drawn for the living forces from the relation of the diagonal to the side lines in the composition of actual motions, or whether, in spite of all this, this mathematical proof can furnish no defence of the new estimation. This is properly the point in dispute. For if Herr Bülfinger's edifice rests only on metaphysical principles, and is not supported by the mathematical concepts of the composition of motions, then the aim of this chapter already excuses us from entering upon an examination of it.\n\nThe relation of the diagonal velocity to the side velocities in the composition of actual motions is, however, proved from one and the same ground from which this relation is likewise derived in the composition of dead pressures. It is therefore true even if no other properties and determinations are to be met with in compounded actual motions than those found in dead pressures, since it can be sufficiently proved without requiring anything other than what must also be presupposed in the case of dead pressures that are compounded. From the relation of the diagonal velocity in actual motions it cannot, then, be concluded that the compounded forces must be of another nature and mode of estimation than dead pressures; for precisely the same relation holds even if the nature of the compounded forces is not at all different from that of dead pressures, since no other grounds are needed to prove it than those which would also be needed here. It is therefore vain for Herr Bülfinger to wish to make use of it in order to conclude from it that forces stand in proportion not to the velocities but to their squares.",["- 0432: Divided the long opening sentences into shorter ones. The first sentence (\"In der Zusammensetzung… wird… vorgestellet\") is irregular in construction; I smoothed it into \"Consider the composition…\" with the sense kept.","- 0432: \"todte Drucke\" = \"dead pressures\"; \"schlechter Proportion\" rendered \"simple proportion\" (i.e. direct, not squared); \"lebendige Kräfte\" = \"living forces\"; \"Schätzung\" = \"estimation\".","- 0432: \"Würklichkeit der Bewegung\" = \"actuality of the motion\"; \"würkliche Bewegungen\" = \"actual motions\". \"Vorwurf\" taken as \"object\" (of inquiry).","- 0432: \"wir haben… begreifen\" lacks the auxiliary in the German (a slip); rendered \"we have come to understand\". \"Schlußreden\" = \"inferences\".","- 0434: \"Schwerdt… Knoten\" is the Gordian-knot image; \"hat verwahren wollen\" rendered \"has sought to secure\".","- 0436: The Latin quotation is kept in italics, with \"vt\" silently read as \"ut\" and \"innisu\" as \"in nisu\" (OCR/spelling); the printed text reads \"non innisu mortuo\". Left untranslated as in the source.","- 0436: \"Mathematickundige\" rendered \"mathematician\"; \"Geometer\" = \"geometers\". \"sind wir… Meister\" supplies \"masters of\".","- 0438: \"Hauptstück\" = \"chapter\"; \"Diagonalgeschwindigkeit\" = \"diagonal velocity\". Divided the two long sentences at the semicolon-like joins for clarity."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0439":["Accordingly, the metaphysical distinctions which this philosopher employed may perhaps offer something from which a continued philosophical consideration could draw certain grounds in favour of the living forces. But they do not suffice to uphold the mathematical proof of which we are speaking. For that proof, by its very nature, leaves undetermined precisely what is required for the rule which it is intended to yield.",["- Restated the final clause (\"weil er schon seiner Natur nach…\") as a separate sentence; \"er\" is the mathematical proof (Beweis), not the philosopher.","- *lebendige Kräfte* = \"living forces\", kept literal as a technical term."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0440":["§ 92.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0441":["A particular composite case of Herr von Leibniz.",["- \"Leibnitz\" is given in the standard modern spelling, Leibniz. *zusammengesetzer* is read as *zusammengesetzter* (original slip)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0442":["After all these different kinds of proofs, whose incorrectness we have shown to the defenders of the living forces, I come at last to the one which has for its author Herr von Leibniz himself, the father of the living forces, and which also bears the mark of his acuteness. He first presented it to the world in the *Acta Eruditorum* * on the occasion of his answering the objections of the Abbé Catelan. Afterwards, too, whenever he wished to throw light upon his estimation of forces, he appealed to it in particular. We shall therefore have to regard it as a chief support of the living forces, and to remove it.",["- The asterisk refers to the footnote in the next passage (*Acta* 1690). \"Actis Eruditorum\" is rendered in the nominative, as the title.","- *Kräftenschätzung* = \"estimation of forces\" (the measure of force)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0443":["* Acta 1690.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0444":["** Let a sphere A, of fourfold mass, fall on the inclined and curved surface, whose height 1 AE is as 1, from 1 A to 2 A, and continue its motion on the horizontal surface EC with the degree of velocity which it has acquired by the fall, and which is as 1. Let it further be supposed that it transfers all the force which it has to a sphere B of simple mass, and after this itself rests at the point 3 A. What velocity must the sphere B, which has 1 for its mass, receive from the sphere A, which has four times more mass and a simple degree of velocity, if its force is thereby to become equal to the force which the body A had? The Cartesians say that its velocity will have to be fourfold. Let the body B, then, run with 4 degrees of velocity on the horizontal surface from 1 B to 2 B, and, having there met the inclined and curved surface 2 B 3 B, let it move up this surface, and so reach upon it, by the velocity inherent in it, the point 3 B, whose perpendicular height 3 BC is as 16. Let us further assume the inclined balance 3 A 3 B, which moves about the point F, and one arm of which, F 3 B, is four times, and a little more, as long as the other beam 3 AF, but which nevertheless hold each other in equilibrium. Now when the body B reaches the point 3 B and there steps on to the arm of the balance, it is clear that, since the beam F 3 B is somewhat greater in relation to the other, 3 AF, than the mass of the body 3 A is in comparison with the mass of the sphere 3 B, the equilibrium will be disturbed, and the body 3 B will sink down from 3 B to 4 B, while at the same time raising the sphere 3 A from 3 A to 4 A. But the height 4 A 3 A is almost the fourth part of the height 3 BC, hence as 4; thus the body B has in this way raised the sphere A to an almost fourfold height. By a simple mechanical contrivance it can now be arranged that the sphere 4 A goes back again from 4 A to 1 A, and with the force acquired by its falling back performs certain mechanical effects; that afterwards it runs down once more from the point 1 A along the inclined surface 1 A 2 A and restores everything to its former state; and that it also transfers to the sphere B, which by an imperceptibly small inclination of the surface 2 B 4 B can be at the point 1 B again, all its force as before, and so accomplishes everything once more. Herr von Leibniz proceeds to conclude: From the Cartesian estimation of forces it follows that a body, if only its force be well used, can go on performing ever more and more effects to infinity, driving machines, bending springs, and overcoming obstacles, without anything being lost from its capacity to continue doing just the same without end; that the effect can therefore be greater than its cause, and that perpetual motion, which all mechanicians hold to be absurd, is possible.",["- The OCR text is kept as it stands; the letter-number labels (1 A, 3 B, etc.) refer to the lost Fig. XIV and are left unaltered. Kant's \"Kugel/Körper\" is rendered \"sphere/body\" accordingly.","- Restated the long sentence \"Wenn nun der Körper B…\" with \"since\" for the German colon construction (\"so ist klar, daß: weil…\"); the comparison of beam-length and mass is kept as in the German, which is awkward.","- \"Schnellwaage\" = balance (steelyard type); \"Kunststück\" = \"contrivance\". \"Vermögen\" = \"capacity\". \"Mechanicker\" = \"mechanicians\".","- \"seinem Vermögen etwas entgehe, eben dieses…zu verüben\" is smoothed: \"anything being lost from its capacity to continue doing just the same\".","- The German twice has \"Waagbalken/Arm\"; I use \"beam\" and \"arm\" as in the source. \"vor eine Geschwindigkeit\" = \"what velocity\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0445":["** Fig. XIV.",["- Figure reference; the figure itself is not part of the text."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0446":["§ 93.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0447":["The point of the fallacy in this proof.",["- *Fehlschluss* = \"fallacy\" (literally a false inference)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0448":["This proof is the only one among all the defences of the living forces whose plausibility could excuse the haste which the Leibnizians have shown in regard to the grounds for protecting their estimation. Herr Bernoulli, Herr Hermann and Wolff have said nothing that came up to it in ingenuity and apparent strength. So great a man as Herr von Leibniz was could not err without even the very thought which led him into error having to be creditable to him. In regard to this proof we wish to say what Hector boasts of himself in Virgil:",["- Passage breaks off at the colon; the Virgil lines follow in the next passages.","- Divided nothing, but \"Herrmann\" is modernised to Hermann, \"Wolf\" to Wolff (the philosopher Christian Wolff).","- \"Schutzgründe\" = \"grounds for protecting\"; \"Emporhaltung\" appears elsewhere."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0449":["– – – – – – – – –",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0450":["*Si Pergama dextra*",["- Virgil, *Aeneid* II.291–2 (Aeneas reports Hector's words). Latin kept in the original."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0451":["*defendi possent, etiam hac defensa fuissent.*",["- Text as in the edition; Latin kept, \"If Troy could be defended by a right hand, it would have been defended by this one.\""]],"lebendige-kraefte-0452":["Virg. *Aeneid.*",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0453":["I shall state my judgment of it briefly. Herr von Leibniz should not have said that the falling back of the sphere A is an effect of the force transferred to the sphere B. By this I mean its falling back after it has been raised by means of the balance to the fourfold height 4 A 3 A, and returns from 3 A to the inclined surface 1 A, having first exerted mechanical forces. However much it may seem to be such an effect, it is not. This exerted mechanical force is, as we shall soon see, indeed the subsequent state in the machine, which was occasioned by means of the force that passed over into B; but it is nevertheless no effect of this force. We must very carefully avoid confusing these two meanings, for here is the real point of the fallacy, on which all the plausibility that appears in the Leibnizian proof is founded. For if all these mechanical consequences are not a true effect of the force which the body A has transferred to the other body B, then all appearance of a paradoxical thought vanishes at once, even if one says that more is contained in the subsequent state of the machine than in the preceding. For the effect is not on that account greater than its cause. Perpetual motion itself is in this case no absurdity, since the motion produced is not the true effect of the force, which has properly only occasioned it, and consequently may always be greater than this force without our offending against the fundamental law of mechanics.",["- Divided the long first sentence into several; \"so sehr dieselbe es auch scheinet zu seyn\" moved to a separate sentence for clarity.","- \"Würkung\" = \"effect\"; \"die Vermengung dieser zweyen Bedeutungen\": the two meanings are (a) a subsequent state occasioned by the force and (b) a true effect of it; I have not supplied them in the text.","- \"veranlasset\" = \"occasioned\", contrasted with \"hervorgebracht\" (produced).","- \"Punkt des Fehlschlusses\" = \"point of the fallacy\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0454":["§ 94.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0455":["The force which A receives through the arrangement of the machine is not a produced effect of the force of the body B.",["- Heading; \"hervorgebrachte Würkung\" = \"produced effect\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0456":["The body B, into which the whole force of the ball A has been transferred, expends it entirely in running up the inclined plane 2 B 3 B. At the point 3 B it has therefore completed the whole magnitude of its effect and has used up all the force imparted to it. When it now arrives at that point upon the beam of the balance, the force with which it raises the body 3 A is no longer the former force. The renewed power of gravity alone produces this effect, and the force which B had received from the ball A has no share in it. Further, when the ball A has thereby been raised as far as 4 A, the preponderant force of the ball 3 B has in this way also exercised its full effect. The force which the body A receives in returning from 4 A to 1 A is again the effect of a new cause. This cause is entirely distinct from the action of the lever and much greater than it; it is the pressure of gravity, which is imparted to the body in free fall. The force, then, with which the body A exercises mechanical effects before it arrives again at the point 1 A is something which has indeed been occasioned by the force of the ball B (that is, has been handed over to certain mechanical causes), but which does not have that force itself as its producing cause.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0457":["§ 95.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0458":["This is confirmed.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0459":["If the Leibnitzians insist that the subsequent state arising in nature shall always contain precisely as much force as the preceding state, I should like to know how they would extricate themselves from the objection which can be made to them from their own proof. Suppose I place the ball B at 3 B upon the steelyard, so that it presses down the beam there and raises the body A from 3 A to 4 A. This is the preceding state of nature. The force which A afterwards acquires, in falling back from 4 A, is the subsequent state, which is occasioned by the former. But this state contains much more force than the other. For the excess of weight of the body 3 B over the body 3 A may be incomparably small in respect of its own weight. The velocity with which 3 A is raised may therefore be exceedingly small compared with the velocity which it acquires by free fall from 4 A to 1 A. In the latter case the undiminished pressures of gravity accumulate; in the former only such pressures as are incomparably small by comparison. The subsequent state of force in nature is therefore indisputably greater than the preceding state which occasioned it.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0460":["§ 96.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0461":["The same proved from the law of continuity.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0462":["Everything here depends chiefly upon our being convinced that the force which B possesses with 4 degrees of velocity is not the producing cause of the effect which here shows itself in the machine. The Leibnitzians must presuppose that it is, if they wish to show an absurdity in the law of Descartes. For if it were so, then a very slight diminution of this cause would make the effect also only very slightly smaller. In the machine, however, the case is quite otherwise. Suppose that the body 1 B has somewhat less than 4 degrees of velocity. It will then climb only as far as the point a on the curved surface 2 B a. There the length 3 AF of one arm of the balance stands in exactly fourfold ratio to the length of the other arm. The weight of the body B therefore does not move the lever, nor displace the body 3 A in the least from its place. So if B has a part of the force less, which may be assumed so small as to come almost wholly out of consideration, 3 A then acquires no force at all. If, on the contrary, this small amount is added, 3 A will not only recover the force which it originally had but will gain far more besides. It is evident that this leap would not occur if the force of the body 3 B were the true producing cause of the state which shows itself in the machine.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0463":["§ 97.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0464":["The whole magnitude of the sufficient ground in the preceding state.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0465":["Consider the arrangement of the lever in this machine and its geometrical determination in respect of the proportion of the bodies. Add to this the excess of the ratio of the height 3 B 4 B to the height 1 AE over the proportion of the mass of the body B to the mass A. (For the height 3 B 4 B is to the height 1 AE as 16 to 1, whereas the mass A is to B only as 4 to 1.) We then have the whole magnitude of those determinations which occasioned the force in A. Add further the pressures of gravity, which are made more effective by means of the advantageous arrangement of the geometrical determinations. We then have the entire sum of all the sufficient grounds, in which the magnitude of the force arising in A will be found again completely. If the force of the body B alone is separated from this, it is no wonder that it is found far too small to furnish the ground of the force which comes into A. All that the body B does in this process is this: at the same time as it overcomes the restraints of gravity, it acquires a certain modality, that is, a certain quantity of height, which is greater than corresponds to the proportion of its velocity, and consequently of its mass.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0466":["The force of the body B is therefore not the true effective cause of the force which is generated in A. In respect of it, then, the great law of mechanics, *effectus quilibet aequipollet viribus causae plenae* [every effect is equivalent to the forces of its full cause], will be without validity. A perpetual motion may in this way be produced all the same, without this fundamental law being in the least violated.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0467":["§ 98.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0468":["The sole difficulty that might still lie in the Leibnitzian argument.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0469":["All, then, that Herr von Leibnitz can set against us with his argument comes to this. Even if the complete impossibility of the matter cannot be demonstrated, it would still turn out very irregular and contrary to nature that one force should awaken another greater than itself, in whatever manner this might happen. Herr von Leibnitz himself inclines to this side: * *Sequeretur etiam causam non posse iterum restitui suoque effectui surrogari; quod quantum abhorreat a more naturae & rationibus rerum facile intelligitur. Et consequens esset: decrescentibus semper effectibus, neque unquam crescentibus, ipsam continue rerum naturam declinare, perfectione imminuta, neque unquam resurgere atque amissa recuperare posse sine miraculo. Quae in Physicis certe abhorrent a sapientia constantiaque conditoris.* He would not have spoken so mildly if he had not seen that the nature of the case imposed this moderation upon him. One may be quite assured that he would have taken the field against his enemy with all the thunder of his geometrical ban and all the might of mathematics, had his penetration not perceived this weakness. But he saw himself obliged to call the wisdom of God to his aid, a sure sign that geometry had offered him no serviceable weapons.",["- 0456: Restated the closing sentence's \"das ist\" clause as a parenthesis; \"Würkung\" = effect, \"Kraft\" = force, used consistently.","- 0459: \"Schnellwaage\" rendered \"steelyard\"; \"Ueberwucht\" = \"excess of weight\"; \"unvergleichbar\" = \"incomparably\".","- 0462: \"Sprung\" rendered \"leap\" (the discontinuity argument); the figure label \"2 Ba\" is OCR-uncertain and rendered \"2 B a\".","- 0465: Divided the opening conditional chain into separate sentences; \"Modalität\" kept as \"modality\" after Kant's Latin-derived term.","- 0466: Bracketed English gloss supplied for the Latin law; the German \"Grundgesetze\" rendered \"fundamental law\".","- 0469: Latin quotation (Leibniz) left in Latin and italicised; I silently corrected the OCR run-together \"restituisuoque\" to \"restitui suoque\". The footnote asterisk is kept at its place."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0470":["* Act. Erud. 1691 p. 442.",["- Footnote marker kept; \"Act. Erud.\" = *Acta Eruditorum*."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0471":["*Nec deus intersit, nisi dignus vindice nodus inciderit* – – – – – – – – – – – – – – – – – – – – – – – – – – – –",["- Latin left as in the source (Horace, *Ars poetica*); OCR \"devs\" silently corrected to \"deus\", and the capital of \"Inciderit\" lowered to follow the line as quoted. Kemp Smith-style practice would give the sense: \"Let no god intervene, unless a knot worthy of such a deliverer should arise.\""]],"lebendige-kraefte-0472":["Horat. *de arte poët.*",["- Horace, *De arte poetica*; abbreviation kept."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0473":["[It] is answered.",["- Marginal heading. \"Wird beantwortet\" has no subject; \"[It]\" is supplied. The heading evidently refers to the objection discussed in the text."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0474":["But even this small defence is not lasting. We are here speaking only of the estimation of forces as it is known through mathematics, and it is no wonder if this does not wholly satisfy the wisdom of God. Mathematics is a science taken from the midst of all modes of knowledge [*Erkenntnisse*]. By itself it is not sufficiently consistent with the rules of what is fitting and seemly, and it must be taken together with the doctrines of metaphysics if it is to be applied completely to nature. The harmony found among truths is like the congruity in a painting. If one takes out a single part, the fitting, the beautiful and the skilful vanish; all the parts must be seen together if these qualities are to be perceived. The Cartesian estimation is contrary to the purposes of nature; hence it is not the true measure of forces in nature. This, however, does not prevent it from being the true and legitimate measure of forces in mathematics. For the mathematical concepts [*Begriffe*] of the properties of bodies and of their forces are still far removed from the concepts found in nature, and it is enough that we have seen that the Cartesian estimation is not opposed to the former. But to determine the true measure of forces in nature we must combine the metaphysical laws with the rules of mathematics. This will fill the gap and will better satisfy the purposes of the wisdom of God.",["- \"Schutzwehre\" rendered \"defence\"; \"Kräftenmaaß\" as \"measure of forces\". \"Schätzung\" as \"estimation\".","- Restated \"die Harmonie… Wenn man…; allein sie müssen…\" in slightly simplified clauses; \"dasselbe\" resolved as \"these qualities\" (the fitting, beautiful, skilful).","- \"jenen\" taken to refer to the mathematical concepts."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0475":["§ 99.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0476":["Mr. Papin's objection.",["- \"Herr\" rendered \"Mr.\"; Denis Papin."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0477":["Mr. Papin, one of the most notorious opponents of living forces, conducted the cause of Descartes against this argument of Herr von Leibniz very unhappily. He left the field of battle to his adversary and ran across country to hold some post that should shelter him. He grants to Herr von Leibniz that if one presupposes that the body A has transferred its whole force to the body B, a perpetual motion follows on the Cartesian estimation; and he very good-naturedly concedes to him that this kind of motion is an absurdity: *Quomodo autem per translationem totius potentiae corporis A in corpus B juxta Cartesium obtineri possit motus perpetuus evidentissime demonstrat, atque ita Cartesianos ad absurdum reductos arbitratur. Ego autem & motum perpetuum absurdum esse fateor, & Cl. Vir. demonstrationem exsupposita translatione esse legitimam;* Having spoiled his case in this way, he seeks his escape in denying the presupposition of his opponent, which is a very accidental part of the argument, and in challenging him to untie this knot for him. The following words make known his opinion: *Sed Hypothesis ipsius possibilitatem translationis nimirum totius potentiae ex corpore A in corpus B pernego, &c* – – – *",["- Latin passages left untranslated, as in the source; the semicolon after \"legitimam\" and the break before \"Nachdem\" are as printed.","- \"Ungereimtheit\" = \"absurdity\"; \"qverfeldeingelaufen\" = \"ran across country\".","- The asterisk refers to the footnote that follows."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0478":["* Act. 1691 pag. 9.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0479":["§ 100.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0480":["Herr von Leibniz disarmed his opponent at a stroke and left him not the slightest escape. He showed him that the actual transfer of the force is no essential part of his proof, and that it suffices to posit in B a force that can be substituted for the force in A. All this may be found proved in the treatise which he contributed to the *Acta* and which we have already cited. I cannot, however, refrain from pointing out a lapse on the part of Herr von Leibniz which in a public disputation would have played victory into his opponent's hands. It consists in this: that in order to establish a subsidiary point in the argument he concedes something which, as he himself remarks, does not properly belong to the main issue; and this, if it is accepted, does indeed confirm the subsidiary condition, but wholly overturns the principal point in the proof.",["- Divided the final sentence's relative clauses slightly; \"was\" in \"was aber, wenn es angenommen wird\" resolved as \"this\".","- \"Acta\" = *Acta Eruditorum*."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0481":["A lapse of Herr von Leibniz.",["- Marginal heading."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0482":["The matter stands thus. Mr. Papin had got it into his head to make no other exception to his opponent's argument than this: that it is impossible for one body to communicate its whole force to another. He tried to make suspect all the devices by which Herr von Leibniz thought to accomplish this. He therefore contested with all zeal that the fourfold body 1 A,* by an impact upon the perfectly rigid lever 1 A C B at the point 1 A, whose distance from the fulcrum C is a fourth part of [i.e. one quarter of] the distance CB, could communicate its whole force to the simple body B; for that is what Herr von Leibniz was aiming at in maintaining his mechanical case, which we have discussed. Mr. Papin did not notice the advantage his cause might have gained, had he accepted this solution and himself drawn from it a conclusion against living forces. He attacked it, therefore, but with such weak grounds that they increased his opponent's courage to persist in his assertion. Leibniz accordingly insisted on the correctness of this device, which he believed he could use in order to transfer into one body the whole force of another by a single impact. He accepted with gratitude the grounds Papin had adduced to show its plausibility, and removed the difficulties by which Papin in turn supposed he could frustrate it. I believe he said the following in all seriousness: *Cum florentiae essem, dedi amico aliam adhuc demonstrationem, pro possibilitate translationis virium totalium &c. corpore majore in minus quiescens, prorsus affinem illis ipsis, quo Cl. Papinus ingeniosissime pro me juvando excogitavit, pro quibus gratias debeo imo & ago sinceritate ejus dignas.* We shall now see that Leibniz gave his cause a very poor turn by stiffly persisting in maintaining this proposition, which he ought rather to have conceded to his opponent; for then he would indeed have lost the subsidiary point (the loss of which could do him no harm), but would have won the main point. Mr. Papin could and should have argued in the following way, in order to catch his opponent in his own admission.",["- \"viertheilig\" rendered \"a fourth part of\"; the bracketed gloss is mine. \"1 A\" is the figure label, kept.","- The sentence \"Herr Papin wurde… gewahr\" is \"würde\" in sense (OCR/orig. \"wurde\"); rendered as conditional-sense \"might have gained\".","- \"Ruhepuncte\" = fulcrum (resting point of the lever). \"Kunststücke\"/\"Kunstgriff\" = devices.","- Restated the long final sentences in shorter ones. Latin quotation left unchanged."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0483":["* Fig. XV.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0484":["Proof that a fourfold body, by an impact upon a lever, can communicate four degrees of velocity to a simple body.",["- Heading; \"1fachen\" = \"simple\" (single) body; \"4 Grade\" kept as \"four degrees\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0485":["If the fourfold body 1 A strikes the lever at 1 A with one degree of velocity, it is evident that by this impact it will transfer its whole force and velocity to another body 2 A, which has the same mass as itself and is likewise at the same distance from the lever's point of rest. Now this velocity, with which 2 A is thrust away, is a continuation of the motion by which the lever, in driving the body forward, traverses the infinitely small space 2 A 2 a. The velocity of this infinitely small motion is therefore equal to the velocity of the body 2 A that is driven forward, and so equal to that with which 1 A strikes the lever. Consequently this sphere 1 A, in its onset, will press the lever down through the infinitely small line 1 A 1 a, and that line will be traversed with just the same velocity as that with which 1 A runs against it.\n\nNow put in place of the body 2 A the sphere B, which has four times less mass than A, at four times the distance from the point of rest C, and let us see what hindrance the body B will then offer to the body A, while the latter endeavours to press the lever down from 1 A to 1 a. It is well known that the *vis inertiae*, that is, the resistance which a body, by means of its force of inertia, puts in the way of the motion of another, is proportional to its mass. Now a mass of one fourth, at four times the distance from the point of rest, is to be reckoned equal in quantity to a simple mass at one fourth of that distance [*sic*: the German reads \"in viertheiliger Entfernung\"]. Hence B at B offers to the impact of the body 1 A upon the lever just so much resistance, and no more, as the body 2 A = 1 A at 2 A would have offered. So in this case also, where the sphere B is on the lever in place of the sphere 2 A, the body 1 A will traverse the infinitely small line 1 A 1 a together with the lever, and with just the same velocity as in the previous case, that is, a velocity as great as that with which it runs against the point 1 A.\n\nBut the body 1 A cannot press the lever down from 1 A to 1 a without at the same time moving the other end at B upward from B to b; and the infinitely small line B b is four times greater than 1 A 1 a. By this impact of the lever, therefore, the body B will receive a velocity which is fourfold that with which A runs against it.",["- \"Vierfacher Körper\" rendered \"fourfold body\" (a body of four times the mass); \"viertheilige Masse\" as \"mass of one fourth\".","- The long second sentence was divided into several, and \"mithin\" resolved as \"consequently\"/\"therefore\".","- The clause \"in viertheiliger Entfernung\" is the German text as printed; I translate it literally and flag it with [*sic*]. The argument seems to require \"a simple mass at fourfold distance\" equalling a fourfold mass at simple distance, or some similar equivalence, so the reading is doubtful (possibly corrupt or Kant's slip).","- Letters and figure labels (1 A, 2 A, B, C, a, b) follow the diagram, which is not given; I leave them as printed.","- \"Hinderniß\" = hindrance; \"Widerstand\" = resistance; \"Stoß\" = impact."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0486":["The same proved in another way.\n---",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0487":["This is made clear in yet another way. All hard bodies we can represent to ourselves as elastic, that is, as yielding to impact but springing back again. We can therefore attribute such a spring-force to the rigid lever 1 ACB also. The body 1 A, then, which strikes the lever with a degree of velocity equal to 1, expends its whole force in bending the spring 1 AC and pressing it in through the space 1 A 1 a.\n\nNow the *momenta* of velocity which this spring, throughout the whole time of this pressure, consumes by its resistance in the body 1 A are equal to the *momenta* with which the spring C 2 A, as the continued arm of the lever, springs back at the same time, in virtue of this tension, through the space 2 A 2 a. Hence, if this rigid line is extended to B, the *momenta* of velocity with which the spring CB springs back, as the lever 1 a CB restores itself to the straight line 1 a C b, are four times greater than the *momenta* with which it recoils at the point 2 A. (For the space b B, which the point B traverses at the same time, is four times greater than 2 A 2 a.)\n\nHowever, because of the fourfold distance of the point B from the point of rest C, the stiffness of the spring CB is nevertheless four times weaker than that of the spring C 2 A. We must therefore, on the other hand, make the resistance at B four times smaller than at 2 A. The *momentum* of velocity which the spring CB imparts to the body B, of one fourth the mass, then remains fourfold, whereas the *momentum* which the spring C 2 A would apply to the fourfold body 2 A is simple.\n\nNow the time in which the spring CB acts is as great as that in which C 2 A would spring back. The velocities which the two bodies, 2 A and B, receive through the action of two springs, C 2 A and CB, acting for equally long, are as the *momenta* of the velocities which these springs impart to their bodies; hence in the body B they are four times greater than in 2 A. But the velocity which 2 A would receive from the thrust of the spring C 2 A is equal to the velocity with which 1 A runs against [the lever at] 1 A. Therefore the velocity which the body B receives by this impact of the body 1 A upon the lever will be four times greater than that with which 1 A made its impact. Q. E. D. [*W. z. E.*, \"was zu erweisen war\"]",["- Divided Kant's long single sentences into paragraphs and shorter sentences; \"mithin\" rendered \"hence\"/\"therefore\" as the argument requires.","- *momentum* (pl. *momenta*) left in Latin as Kant's technical term.","- \"Feder\" rendered \"spring\"; \"Steife\" as \"stiffness\"; \"Ruhepunct\" as \"point of rest\" (the fulcrum).","- \"[the lever at]\" supplied to complete \"in 1 A anläuft\".","- \"W. z. E.\" expanded in brackets.","- Figure labels as printed; diagram not available."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0488":["How Herr Papin could have argued from this against Leibniz.\n---",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0489":["We see, then, from this twofold proof that a fourfold body can impart to a simple body, by a single impact, a fourfold velocity. This is true according to mechanical principles which not even the most zealous defenders of living forces would be in a position to call in question. Herr Papin could have driven his opponent thoroughly into a corner by this, had he properly noted his advantage. He ought to have said to him: \"You have conceded to me that a fourfold body, by means of a lever, can bring all its force into a simple body whose distance from the centre is fourfold. But I can show you that in these circumstances it imparts to the latter four degrees of velocity. Hence a simple body with 4 degrees of velocity has all the force of a fourfold body with 1 degree. But this is precisely the point in dispute, which you demand that I deny.\"",["- \"lebendige Kräfte\" rendered \"living forces\" (*vis viva* controversy).","- Kant's \"den ihr mir zu leugnen verlanget\" is read as \"which you demand that I deny\", i.e. the Leibnizian is made to say Papin must deny it; the sense is slightly obscure and may be a slip for the reverse (that the opponent denies it).","- Quotation marks added around Papin's imagined speech for clarity."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0490":["§ 101.\n---",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0491":["Thus the most formidable blow of all, with which the living forces threatened the Cartesian estimation, has come to nothing. There is now no hope left that, after this, they will find means to maintain themselves upright.\n---",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0492":["– – –\n---",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0493":["*vires in ventum effudit, & ultro Ipse gravis graviterque ad terram pondere vasto Concidit: ut quondam cava concidit aut Erymantho, Aut Ida in magna, radicibus eruta pinus.*\n---\n- Latin left untranslated as in Kant's text (Virgil, *Aeneid* V, on Entellus, who spent his strength upon the wind and himself fell heavily to the ground, like a hollow pine uprooted on Erymanthus or great Ida). Kant's quotation is slightly abridged/reworded from Virgil.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0494":["Virg. *Aen.* Lib. V.\n---",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0495":["§ 102.\n---",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0496":["We have refuted the principal grounds of the Leibnizians.\n---",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0497":["We have so far set out the most considerable and celebrated grounds of the innovation concerning living forces, and have taken care to pay this sect back, by the right of retaliation, all the reproaches and corrections which it has so frequently made to the disciples of Descartes. It would be unfair to demand of us that we should draw in everything that has been written on this matter on the side of Herr von Leibniz, in order to prepare a complete triumph for our party out of it. That would be to spare nothing, from the cedars on Lebanon to the hyssop that grows out of the wall, merely in order to enrich one's work. We could make more than one raid into the territory of our opponents, plunder their goods, and erect for the following of Descartes as many trophies and triumphal arches; but I believe my readers will show no great desire for it. If it has ever been said with reason that a big book is a great evil, it might be said of one which, like this, adduces little else than various defences of one and the same matter, and a very abstract matter at that, and adduces them, finally, for a single purpose only, namely to refute them all.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0498":["We cannot, however, renounce this abuse of prolixity so completely as not to be entitled to adduce one further proof, the omission of which the whole number of the opponents and defenders of our controversy would nevertheless excuse. This proof has a claim to a place in this treatise only on account of the rank of its author; in respect of the authority which it enjoys among the adherents of both parties, it has not the slightest. The Leibnizians did not believe that it could be of any service to their opinion, and it has not been observed that they had recourse to it, however often they were hard pressed.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0499":["§ 103.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0500":["An Argument of Herr Wolf.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0501":["Herr Wolf is the one from whom we have this proof, and he has presented it, adorned with all the pomp of method, in the first volume of the Petersburg *Commentarii*. It may be said that the way in which he carries his proposition through a long series of preceding propositions, which by means of a strict method are very exactly divided and multiplied, is comparable to the stratagem of an army which, in order to dazzle its enemy and conceal its weakness, divides itself into many bodies and extends its wings widely.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0502":["Anyone who reads his treatise in the work of the Academy cited will find that it is very difficult to pick out of it what constitutes the real proof; so much has everything been stretched out and made unintelligible by the analytic bent that displays itself there. Let us make ourselves to some extent acquainted with the nature of his undertaking.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0503":["§ 104.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0504":["The Principal Fundamental Proposition of this Argument.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0505":["Herr Papin had maintained that one cannot say that a body has done anything if it overcomes no obstacles, displaces no masses, bends no springs, and so on. Herr Wolf contradicts him in this, and on the following ground. When a man carries a load through a certain space, everyone agrees that he has done and accomplished something; now a body carries its own mass through a space, by virtue of the force which it possesses in actual motion; by this very fact its force has done and exerted something. At the beginning of his treatise Herr Wolf promises to abandon this ground and to prove his proposition independently of it; but he has not kept his word.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0506":["Having explained what he understands by harmless effects (*effectus innocuos*), namely those in whose production the force is not consumed, he lays down a proposition on which his edifice is erected solely and alone, and which we need only take from him in order to render all the labour of his work fruitless. *Si duo mobilia per spatia inaequalia transferuntur, effectus innocui sunt vt spatia.* This is the proposition we mean (*). Let us see how he has set about proving it. He argues in the following manner. If the effect through the space A is e, then the effect which occurs in an equal, or the very same, space A is also e; consequently in the space 2A it is 2e, in the space 3A it will be 3e, that is, the effects will stand in the proportion of the spaces.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0507":["(*) Herr Wolf has therefore ascribed certain effects to the body in its motion through a space in which nothing resists it, that is, through an empty space; and he afterwards makes use of these effects as a measure of the body's force. Consequently he has not kept his promise.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0508":["His proof rests, then, on this presupposition: if the body passes through the very same space, it has also exerted the very same harmless effect. This is the real point of seduction and of the error which afterwards spreads over his whole work. It is not enough that the space alone be the same, if the effect which an equal body has produced in it is also to be the same; one must at the same time take into consideration the velocity with which the body traverses the space. If this is not likewise equal, then, notwithstanding all the equality of the space, the harmless effect will nevertheless be different. To grasp this we must represent to ourselves, as we did in § 17, the space which the body traverses as not perfectly empty, but as filled with matter, though with matter infinitely thin and therefore offering infinitely little resistance. This is done only so that we may have a true effect and a definite subject of it; for in other respects it remains a harmless effect, just as in the Wolffian argument. If, then, a body traverses a space as great as that traversed by another body equal to it, both have displaced the same quantity of matter, but they have not on that account always exerted the same effect. For if the one has run through its space with twice the velocity, then all the particles of its space have received from it, through its effect, twice as much velocity as the particles of the space which the other body traverses with simple velocity; consequently the former body has exerted the greater effect, although the mass and the space traversed were equal in both.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0509":["§ 105.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0510":["Another Principal Ground of the Wolffian *Schediasma*.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0511":["Thus the fundamental proposition of all Herr Wolf's inferences is evidently false, and conflicts with what can be proved most clearly and certainly from the concepts of acting and of motion. Once one has erred, the consequence is nothing but a chain of errors. From his fundamental proposition Herr Wolf derives another, which furnishes his system with all those great consequences which so unexpectedly surprise the reader and fill him with wonder. It is as follows: Since in uniform motion the spaces are in the compound ratio of the velocities and times, the harmless effects are as the masses, times and velocities taken together. Upon this he builds the theorem: *Actiones, quibus idem effectus producitur, sunt vt celeritates.*",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0512":["Refuted.",["- 0497: \"Neurung\" rendered \"innovation\"; \"Sekte\" as \"sect\"; \"Gründe\" (plural, with the singular adjectives) as \"grounds\". The final sentence, with its repeated \"anziehet\", is kept close to the German; \"adduces\" stands for \"anziehen\".","- 0498: \"Weitläuftigkeit\" = \"prolixity\"; \"auf öfters in die Enge getrieben\" is evidently a slip for \"so sehr sie auch öfters…\" and is translated accordingly.","- 0501: \"Commentarii\" is the Petersburg Academy's *Commentarii*; \"Hindurchführung\" rendered \"carries through\".","- 0506: \"innocuos\"/\"unschädlich\" rendered \"harmless\"; Wolff's Latin proposition is left in Latin. \"Effect\" and \"Würkung\" are both given as \"effect\" (Würkung/Wirkung/Effect).","- 0508: \"§ 17\" is Kant's own cross-reference to the earlier section of this work. \"Verführung\" = \"seduction\".","- 0510: \"Schediasma\" is Wolff's term for a short occasional treatise; left untranslated in italics.","- 0511: \"Raume\" in \"die Raume\" read as \"Räume\" (spaces); \"Verhältniß\" rendered \"ratio\". The long sentence was divided in two."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0513":["In the proof of this proposition there is a fallacy which is, if possible, still more serious than the one we have only just noticed. He had proved that, if two equal bodies produce the same effect in unequal times, their velocities are inversely as the times in which these equal effects are produced. That is to say, the body which completes its effect in half the time has two degrees of velocity, whereas the other, which on the contrary must spend the whole time upon it, possesses only one degree. From this he concludes: Since everyone admits that the action which accomplishes its effect in half the time taken by another is twice as great, the *actiones* in this case will be in the inverse ratio of the times, that is, in the direct ratio of the velocities. He then goes further and considers the case in which two different bodies produce the same effect in equal times. He shows that in this case the velocities will be inversely as the masses, and concludes further as follows: *Quoniam hic eadem est ratio massarum, quae in casu priori erat temporum; ratio vero celeritatum eodem modo se habeat: perinde est, sive massae diversae & tempus idem, sive massae sint eaedem & tempus diversum &c.* This inference is a monstrosity, and not an argument such as ought to be found in a mathematical treatise.\n\nLet us recall why it was said, in the former case, that the *actiones* of two equal bodies which produce an equal effect in unequal times are inversely as the times. It was because the action which produces an effect in a shorter time is, for that very reason, and in the same measure, greater than another which spends more time upon it. The inference therefore holds in that case on this ground: the shortness of the time in which an effect is completed always testifies to a correspondingly greater action. But if, as here in the second case, I put the inequality of the masses in place of the inequality of the times, and on the other hand make the times equal, it is easy to see that the inequality of the masses does not have the consequence which the inequality of the times has. For in the former case the body which completed its effect in the smaller time exerted a greater action precisely because the time was smaller. Here, however, the body which has the smaller mass, and with it produces in equal time just as much effect as the other, does not have a greater activity on account of the smallness of its mass. To say this would be quite absurd; for the smallness of the mass is a true and essential ground upon which the smallness of the activity rather rests. And if a body, notwithstanding this smallness of mass, nevertheless produces in equal time as much effect as another, we can only conclude that what its *actio* lacks on account of a small mass has been made good and made up by a greater velocity, and that its *actio* has thereby been made equal to that of the other.\n\nThus, when the masses are unequal but the times and effects equal, we cannot say that the *actiones* of the bodies are inversely as their masses, although in the case of unequal times and equal masses this proportion held with regard to the times and the *actiones*. It is therefore not the same thing whether the masses are unequal and the times equal, or the times unequal and the masses equal.",["- The opening sentence of the \"Er hatte bewiesen\" passage is kept in sequence; the long sentence beginning \"Allein, wenn ich…\" is divided in two for clarity.","- \"Er\" is Wolff's Latin treatise's author in context (Herr Wolf, as in the following section, § 106); I left it as \"he,\" as in the German.","- \"der geraden von den Geschwindigkeiten\": rendered \"in the direct ratio of the velocities.\"","- \"Würkung/Wirkung\" = effect; \"Action/Actio/Actiones\" kept in Latin italics as technical terms; \"Activität\" = activity.","- The Latin quotation is left untranslated, as in the source; \"ratiomassarum\" in the OCR silently corrected to *ratio massarum*."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0514":["The proof, then, upon which a principal theorem in the Wolffian treatise has been founded is invalid and useless; and the living forces (*lebendige Kräfte*) will accordingly find in it no soil to nourish them.",["- \"kein Land finden, das sie nähren kan\" rendered figuratively as \"no soil to nourish them.\"","- *lebendige Kräfte* given in German in italics as Kant's technical term (*vires vivae*), alongside \"living forces.\""]],"lebendige-kraefte-0515":["There are sometimes in a work certain moderate errors which do not spread very far and do not wholly destroy the validity of the main contention. But in the work of which we are speaking the propositions run down the method as along a rope; hence one or two errors make the whole system untenable and unusable.",["- \"an der Methode als an einem Seile herab\": the image is of propositions strung along a rope, so that one break brings all down. I kept the image and left the sense to the following clause."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0516":["§ 106.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0517":["We have as yet no dynamics.",["- \"Dynamick\" rendered \"dynamics.\""]],"lebendige-kraefte-0518":["Herr Wolff had it in mind in his treatise to supply us with the first foundation of a dynamics. His undertaking has turned out unfortunately. So at present we have no dynamical principles upon which we can rightly build. Our essay, which promises to set forth the true estimation of living forces, is meant to make good this deficiency. The third chapter is to make an attempt at this. But can we really hope to hit the mark, when one of the most experienced in this mode of inquiry has not succeeded in reaching it?",["- \"Herr Wolf\" kept as in the text, with the spelling \"Wolff\" normalised.","- \"Versuchtesten\" taken as \"most experienced\" (practised, tried); the reading is a judgment.","- \"Betrachtung\" rendered \"inquiry\"; the long last sentence is divided at the semicolon."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0519":["§ 107.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0520":["The argument of Herr von Musschenbroek.",["- Spelling of the name normalised to the usual \"Musschenbroek.\""]],"lebendige-kraefte-0521":["Just as I am about to conclude the refutation of the grounds upon which the most celebrated Leibnizians base their estimation of forces, with the preceding case, I receive the *Grundlehren der Naturwissenschaft* of Herr Petrus von Musschenbroek, translated by Professor Gottsched, which appeared at the Easter fair of this year 1747. This great man, the greatest among the investigators of nature of our time, in whose opinions prejudice and sectarian zeal have less share than in the doctrines of any other man, this philosopher of such renown, has subjected Herr von Leibniz's estimation first to his mathematical investigation and then to the experiments which he knows so skilfully how to make, and has found it confirmed in both. This latter path which he has taken does not belong to the present chapter; the former, however, does. The purpose of this treatise requires me to consider the difficulties which the renowned author there raises against the Cartesian estimation, and, if possible, to turn them away from the object whose defence is our business. But will not the narrow limits of these pages, or, to express myself frankly, the astonishing inequality which here shows itself, set insuperable obstacles in my way?",["- \"Hauptstücke\" rendered \"chapter\"; \"Abhandlung\" as \"treatise.\"","- \"Peters\" (for Petrus) rendered \"Petrus.\"","- \"die erstaunliche Ungleichheit\" taken to mean the inequality between the author's stature and the writer's own means; the sense is a judgment, and I added no gloss in the text.","- The long opening sentence is kept whole, as it divides awkwardly."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0522":["Let us see what the grounds were which seemed to him, in his mathematical consideration, to prove Leibniz's laws. * Suppose there is given a certain external cause which moves along with the body it presses, for example a spring BC, fastened to the abutment AS, which pushes a body F forward. If the body is at rest, this spring will impart to it 1 degree of velocity. But as soon as the body already possesses this degree, twice as many springs are required to give it the second degree of velocity. For if the single spring were to extend itself once more by itself alone, the body, which is already actually moving with the very degree of velocity with which the spring expands, would flee from it and would not take up its pressure into itself. The second spring ** DB must therefore be added. It brings it about that the point B, against which the spring BC braces itself, follows the body with the velocity with which the body would otherwise escape, and that in this way the body F is at rest, as at the beginning, with respect to the spring BC, so that when this spring extends, the body receives the degree of velocity as 1. In the same way three springs ED, DB, BC are required to impart to the body F, which already possesses 2 degrees of velocity, merely the third. To impart a single new degree to a body which already has 100 degrees, 101 springs are required, and so on. Hence the number of springs needed to give a body a certain degree of velocity is as the number of degrees into which the whole velocity of the body is divided. That is, the whole force of the springs which impart one degree of velocity to a body is as the whole velocity which the body would then have if it possessed this degree. Now in the triangle ** ABC, whose cathetus AB has been divided into equal parts, the lines DE, FG, HJ, etc. are as the lines AD, AF, AH. Consequently the line DE can be used to indicate the spring which imparts to the body the first degree of velocity AD; the line FG, twice as great, to indicate the double spring which produces the second degree of velocity DF; the line HJ, to indicate the spring three times as great, which excites the third degree of velocity FH; and so on. If we think of these lines DE, FG, etc. as infinitely near one another, they will, by the method of the infinitely small which Cavalieri introduced into geometry, make up the whole area of the triangle ABC. Hence the sum of all the springs which generate the velocity AB in a body is as the surface ABC, that is, as the square of the velocity AB. But these springs represent the forces which together have produced the stated velocity in the body; and as the number of forces acting in a body stands, so stands also the force produced in it. Therefore the force of a body is as the square of the velocity which it possesses.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0523":["* Fig. XVI.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0524":["** Fig. XVII.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0525":["* Fig. XVIII.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0526":["** Fig. XIX.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0527":["§ 108.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0528":["Examination of this Argument.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0529":["I believe that a follower of Descartes would raise the following objection to this proof:",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0530":["If we wish to estimate the force transferred to a body by the sum of certain springs, we must take only those springs which actually bring their power into the body. Those which have not acted in it at all cannot be used to posit in the body a force equal to their own. This proposition is one of the very clearest in mechanics, and no Leibnizian has ever called it in question. Herr von Musschenbroek himself acknowledges it at the end of his proof, for these are his words: As the number of forces acting in a body stands, so stands also the force produced in it. But when a body F, which is already moving with 1 degree of velocity, receives the 2nd degree through the extension of the two springs DB, BC, then of these two springs only BC acts in it, whereas DB brings nothing of its tension-force into it. For the spring DB extends itself with 1 degree of velocity, but the body F is already actually moving with 1 degree; F therefore flees the pressure of this spring, and the spring, in its expansion, cannot reach it so as to transfer the force of its tension into it. It does nothing further than carry the abutment B, against which the spring BC braces itself, after the body F with the very velocity with which F moves, so that the body is at rest with respect to this abutment, and the spring BC brings its whole force, which is as 1, into it. DB is therefore not an acting cause but only an occasional cause [*Gelegenheitsursache*] of the force which in this way is added in F to the earlier one; the single spring BC, however, is its efficient cause [*würkende Ursache*]. Further, if this body already possesses 2 degrees of velocity, then of the three equal springs ED, DB, BC only the single spring BC imparts its force to it, and also the third degree of velocity, and so on to infinity. Thus if DE * is the first spring whose force has entered the body F and has excited in it the first degree of velocity AD, then the spring fG, which is equal to it, has given it the second degree of velocity and transferred its force into it; the spring hI the third degree, and so on. Consequently the sum of the springs DE + fG + hI + kM + lN + rO + bC = BC makes up the whole magnitude of the force which has been applied to the body F from its state of rest, and which has excited in it the velocity AB. But BC is as AB, and BC is the force, AB the velocity. Therefore the force is as the velocity, and not as the square of it.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0531":["* Fig. XIX.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0532":["§ 109.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0533":["A New Case in Confirmation of the Cartesian Measure of Forces.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0534":["We are now past all the difficulties that could stand in our way in maintaining the Cartesian law. But we do not wish to let this suffice. An opinion which is once in possession of authority, and even of prejudice, must be pursued without end and driven out of all its lurking-places. Such an opinion is like the many-headed monster which hatches new heads after every blow.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0535":["*Vulneribus foecunda suis erat ille: nec ullum De centum numero caput est impune recisum, Quin gemino cervix haerede valentior esset.*",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0536":["Ovid, *Metamorphoses*.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0537":["I should regard it as highly honourable if this work were censured for having refuted the Leibnizian estimation of forces superfluously, and with more grounds than was necessary; but I should be ashamed if I had allowed it to lack them.",["- 0522: The long sentences on the springs and the triangle are divided into shorter ones; \"Wiederhalt\" is rendered \"abutment\", and \"Triangel\" as \"triangle\".","- 0522: In \"dieselbe fliehen\" and \"ihm ... nachfolge\", the pronouns are resolved by the argument: the body flees the spring, and the point B follows the body.","- 0522: \"Cavalerius\" is rendered in the modern form \"Cavalieri\". The figure letters (the \"J\" in HJ, and the small letters in 0530) are kept as printed; the OCR may be faulty in \"bC\" and \"rO\".","- 0530: \"Gelegenheitsursache\" is rendered \"occasional cause\" and \"würkende Ursache\" as \"efficient cause\", following Kant's Latin parallels (*causa occasionalis*, *causa efficiens*). In \"Sie ist also keine würkende\" I supplied \"DB\" for the pronoun.","- 0530: \"Spannungskraft\" is rendered \"tension-force\". The Musschenbroek quotation is kept as Kant gives it, without quotation marks.","- 0530: \"zu setzen\" is rendered \"to posit\"; \"Qvadrat\" is rendered \"square\".","- 0534: \"vielköpfigte Ungeheuer\" is taken to be the Hydra; the Latin verse (Ovid, *Met.* 9) is left in Latin, unaltered.","- 0523–0526, 0531: figure references only; the passage 0531 repeats \"Fig. XIX.\" as printed."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0538":["Take an inclined steelyard * ACB, of which one arm CB is four times as long as the other, AC. The weight B, which presses on the end of the longer arm, is a fourth part of the weight A on the other. In the position in which we have placed them, these will be at rest and in perfect equilibrium with one another. Now hang a small weight *e* on the body A in addition. Then the body B will be raised through the arc B b, and A, on the other hand, will sink through the arc A a; but in this motion the body B will acquire four times as much velocity as A. Now remove the weight *e*, and instead hang a weight *d*, four times smaller, on the body b at the end of the steelyard arm Cb. Then b will be pressed down through the arc b B, and a will be raised through the arc a A. But b, which is the same as B, will thereby acquire just as much velocity as in the former case; and a, which is the same as A, will likewise now receive the velocity that was imparted to it in the former case, with this one difference, that the direction of the motions is reversed.\n\nNow the effect which the attached weight *e* exercises consists in the force that the bodies A and B have together; and the effect which the four-times-smaller *d* produces is likewise to be reckoned as that force which b = B and a = A thereby receive together. It is therefore clear that these weights *e* and *d* have exerted equally great effects, and consequently must have expended, and so also have possessed, an equal amount of force. But the velocities with which these weights *e* and *d* act (namely both their initial velocities and the final velocities that they acquire through the accumulation of all these pressures) are inversely as their masses. Hence two bodies whose velocities are in inverse proportion to their masses have equal forces; and this overturns the estimation according to the square.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0539":["* Fig. XX.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0540":["§ 110.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0541":["Leibniz's Knot of Doubt.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0542":["The Cartesians could never have defied the defenders of the new measure of forces with greater confidence than after Mr. Jurin found the case by which one sees, in a simple way and with clarity as bright as the sun, that the doubling of the velocity always posits only the doubling of the force. Herr von Leibniz denied this especially in the *Specimen Dynamicum*, which he incorporated into the *Acta* *. Let us hear him speak as follows: *Cum igitur comparare vellem corpora diversa, aut diversis celeritatibus praedita, equidem facile vidi: si corpus A sit simplum, & B duplum, utriusque autem celeritas aequalis, illius quoque vim esse simplam, hujus duplam, cum praecise quicquid in illo ponitur semel, in hoc ponatur bis. Nam in B est bis corpus ipsi A aequale, & aequivelox nec quicquam ultra. Sed si corpora A & C sint aequalia, celeritas autem in A sit simpla, & in C dupla, videbam non praecise quod in A est duplari in C.* This knot Mr. Jurin has untied by the easiest case in the world.\n\n[In the Latin, \"ponaturbis\" and \"necquicquam\" are rendered as \"ponatur bis\" and \"nec quicquam\", and a full stop is supplied at the end; the German text gives no full stop after the last Latin sentence.]",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0543":["* *Acta* 1695, p. 155.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0544":["Mr. Jurin's Solution.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0545":["He assumed a movable surface, e.g. ** a boat AB, which moves in the direction BC with a velocity of 1 and carries the ball E along with it with equal motion. This ball has therefore, through the motion of the surface, the velocity 1, and also the force 1. He further assumes on this surface a spring R, which is released against the abutment D and imparts to the said ball E, on its own account, one further degree of velocity, and thus also one degree of force. The ball has therefore received in all two degrees of velocity, and with these two degrees of force. Consequently the doubling of the velocity entails nothing more than the doubling of the force, and not, as the Leibnizians falsely persuade themselves, its quadrupling.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0546":["** Fig. XXI.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0547":["This proof is infinitely clear and admits of no evasion. For the motion of the surface can do no more than impart to the body a velocity equal to its own, that is, a simple velocity, and consequently also a simple force. The spring R, however, since it has a motion in common with the surface and the ball at the same time, acts with nothing but its tension-force. This is of just such magnitude that it can impart to a body like ours no more than one degree of velocity, and so also only one degree of force. Hence in everything that enters into the construction of this problem one will find nothing more than the causes of 2 degrees of force, turn where one will; and yet 2 degrees of velocity will actually be present in the body.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0548":["§ 111.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0549":["Madame de Châtelet's Objection to Jurin's Argument.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0550":["The Marquise de Châtelet has contested this argument of Mr. Jurin's, but in a manner whose weakness she would have been acute enough to notice, had not inclination toward an opinion once chosen the power to give the fairest gloss to a bad cause.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0551":["She objected as follows. The boat AB is not an immovable surface; consequently, when the spring R braces itself against the abutment D, it will introduce certain forces into the boat, and one will therefore find again in the mass of the boat the 2 degrees of force which, by the Leibnizian estimation, one misses in the body E.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0552":["§ 112.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0553":["In this evasion there is found the fault of that fallacy which is called *fallacia ignorationis elenchi*. It does not properly attack her opponent's argument at the place where he has put the sinew of his proof, but concerns itself with an accidental side-circumstance which seems favourable to her opinion, but which is not necessarily attached to Jurin's proof. We can easily remove this stumbling-block. Nothing prevents us from representing to ourselves the boat AB as driven by such a force that it is not permitted, by virtue of the exertion of the spring against D, to give way in the least in the direction AF. To this end one need only conceive it as of infinitely great mass. The boat will then yield to the finite force of the spring R only infinitely little, that is, not at all. The body will therefore receive just as much force from this spring as if the spring, stretched against a wholly immovable abutment, were released; that is, it will receive its whole force.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0554":["§ 113.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0555":["Mr. Richter's Objection to Jurin's Argument.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0556":["Mr. Richter, who deserves no mean place in the list of those who have contributed to the upholding of the new measure of forces, has put forward an objection to Jurin's argument that has somewhat more appearance of plausibility. *",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0557":["* *Acta Eruditorum* 1735, p. 511.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0558":["He believes that one and the same force may, in relation to different things, be very unequal. The spring R, he holds, did indeed impart to the ball E a force of 1 with regard to those things which move together with the boat in the same direction and with the same velocity; but with regard to those objects which are actually at rest outside the boat, the spring gave the ball not a simple but a threefold force.\n\n---notes\n- 0538: Division into two paragraphs and restatement of the long sentence on the steelyard's motions as shorter sentences; \"viertheilig\" rendered \"a fourth part of the weight A\" (judged from the argument).\n- 0538: \"Gewichter\" (plural of Gewicht) rendered \"weights\"; \"die Schätzung nach dem Quadrate\" as \"the estimation according to the square\" (of the velocity).\n- 0542: Latin quoted passage is OCR-damaged (\"ponaturbis\", \"necquicquam\"); silently corrected to \"ponatur bis\", \"nec quicquam\". I have not translated the Latin, which stays in italics as Kant's quotation. The final full stop is supplied by me. \"Specimen Dynamicum\" supplied for \"Versuch einer Dynamischen Abhandlung\" is a gloss; the German title is rendered literally in sense only.\n- 0542: Kant writes \"Versuch einer Dynamischen Abhandlung\"; I have replaced it with the Latin title *Specimen Dynamicum* for the reader's identification, which departs from the German wording.\n- 0545: \"Kahn\" rendered \"boat\"; \"Widerhalt\" as \"abutment\"; \"Feder\" \"spring\"; \"loschnellet\" as \"is released\".\n- 0547: \"Spannungs-Kraft\" rendered \"tension-force\"; the sentence \"man mag sich wenden, wohin man wolle\" rendered \"turn where one will\".\n- 0550, 0553: \"sie\" refers to the Marquise; \"ihrer Meynung\" in 0553 is hers, so \"her opponent\" is supplied for \"ihres Gegners\" (i.e. Jurin). \"Trugschluss\" rendered \"fallacy\".\n- 0558: \"Relation\" and \"Ansehung\" both rendered \"relation\"/\"regard\"; \"Gegenstände\" as \"objects\"; the word \"threefold\" follows the text (\"dreyfache\") as printed, though one would expect a different multiple; flag as possible printing/authorial slip.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0559":["I should like to know where the two degrees of force come from which, in Herr Richter's opinion, the body E acquires in relation to the objects at rest. They cannot have arisen in it through an empty abstraction or an idle thought; on the contrary, there must be active causes and forces by which they were supposed to have been produced. But if everything is in absolute rest in relation to external things, and the boat begins to move with one degree of velocity, there arises in the body E thereby one degree of absolute force. From that moment the boat no longer exerts any action upon the body, for it is at rest with respect to it; but the tensile force of the spring begins to release its activity. This has precisely as much as is required for the production of one degree of force; more will be sought in it in vain. No more absolute effect, therefore, has been exerted upon the body than is reckoned at 2 degrees of force. Now if 4 degrees of force were to have arisen in the body in relation to the things at rest, that is, in the absolute sense, and yet no more than 2 degrees of absolute effect had been exerted in it, then 2 degrees must have arisen by chance and without cause, or have crept forth out of nothing.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0560":["To avoid every scruple entirely (if in so clear a matter any scruple has a place), the case of Herr Jurin may be arranged as follows. Suppose that, everything being in absolute rest, the body E first receives from the spring one degree of velocity while the boat is still at rest; then this force acquired by the body E will indisputably be an absolute force. If the boat now also begins to move with one degree, this again is an absolute motion, since it was previously at rest in relation to all things. It therefore communicates to everything belonging to its mass, and consequently to the body E also, a further degree of force. Since the cause that produced it acted in absolute motion, this degree cannot be more than simple. Thus in this way also no more than 2 degrees of force arise in all for the body E.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0561":["Herr Richter seeks to extricate himself by yet another evasion, which he takes from the impact of elastic bodies. But his defence is built upon the common hypothesis of the Leibnizians, that after the impact of elastic bodies exactly the force that existed before the impact must be found again. We have refuted this presupposition; it is therefore unnecessary to engage with Herr Richter on this point in particular.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0562":["§ 113.\nAdditions and Elucidations\nconcerning some parts of this chapter.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0563":["I.\nElucidation of § 25.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0564":["Because the theorem of this section is the chief foundation of our present considerations, we shall present it in a somewhat clearer form.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0565":["A clearer presentation of § 25.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0566":["The mark of an actual motion is a finite duration of it. This duration, however, or the time elapsed since the beginning of the motion, is indeterminate and can therefore be assumed at pleasure. If, then, the line A B * represents the finite time elapsing during the motion, the body has an actual motion at B, likewise at C, the half, also at D, the point of the quarter, and so on in all the still smaller parts of this time, however small one may make them, to infinity; for the indeterminate concept of its magnitude permits this. Hence I can think this time as infinitely small without anything thereby being lacking to the concept of the actuality of the motion. But if the time of this duration is infinitely small, it is to be reckoned as nothing, and the body is only at the initial point, that is, in a mere endeavour toward motion. Consequently, if it is true without further restriction, as Leibniz's law demands, that the force of the body in every actual motion has the square for its measure, then it is so constituted also in a mere endeavour toward motion; and this they themselves must deny.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0567":["* Plate I, Fig. 2.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0568":["Why the undetermined concept of finite time includes within itself the infinitely small.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0569":["At first glance it seems as if Leibniz's law were sufficiently secured, by the restriction to finite elapsed time attached to it, against being extended to a motion whose duration is infinitely small. For finite time is a concept indicating a kind quite different from infinitely small time; so it appears that, given this restriction, what is admitted only under the condition of the finite can by no means be extended to the infinitely small time. This is also correct, if one speaks of finite time in such a way as to presuppose that it must be determinate, and its magnitude determined, if this or that property is to follow from it as a condition. But if a finite time is required, while it is allowed that it may be taken as great or as small as one pleases, then infinitely small time is included in its kind as well. This cannot be unknown to the Leibnizians. For they must know that their forefather built the law of continuity on this ground: namely, that if one assumes A to be greater than B, but in such a way that it is indeterminate how much or how little greater, one can also say, without prejudice to the laws that hold under this condition, that A is equal to B. Or again: if one lets A run against B, and assumes that B also moves, then, if the degree of its motion is indeterminate, one can also assume that B is at rest, without thereby cancelling what is established under that condition; and so in further cases.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0570":["Leibniz's estimation does not hold under the condition of finite velocity either.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0571":["Should one finally say that Leibniz's estimation is indeed not true under the condition of finite time, but nevertheless under the presupposition of finite velocity (although this would obviously be contrary to their doctrine), note that finite velocity can be represented by the line A B * just as well as finite time. It will then likewise be shown that if their law holds at all for finite velocity, it must also hold for an infinitely small one, which they themselves cannot but deny.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0572":["* Plate I, Fig. II.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0573":["II.\nAdditions to §§ 31 to 36.",["- 0559: \"in absolutem Verstande\" rendered \"in the absolute sense\"; Verstand here is \"sense\", not \"understanding\". \"Thätige Ursachen\" = \"active causes\".","- 0559: \"hätten hervorgebracht werden sollen\" is rendered \"were supposed to have been produced\"; \"Grade Kraft\" kept as \"degrees of force\".","- 0560: The long first sentence was divided into several; \"überkomme\" (subjunctive) given as \"receives\" within a supposition. \"Einfach\" taken as \"simple\" (a single degree).","- 0566: \"Bestrebung\" = \"endeavour\" (the *conatus*-like mere striving); \"Quadrat\" is short for the square of velocity, left as \"the square\".","- 0566, 0571: \"Linie A B *\" retains the asterisk of the footnote marker; the figure is not reproduced.","- 0569: \"Anherr\" rendered \"forefather\" (Leibniz); \"Geschlecht\" as \"kind\"; the final \"und so in andern Fällen mehr\" is rendered \"and so in further cases\".","- 0569: Long sentence on the law of continuity restated in two sentences, with \"Or again\" added for the second example."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0574":["Our opponents count it among the clearest concepts one can possibly have, that a body has precisely the force of all the springs which it compresses until its whole motion has been taken from it, no matter what the time may be in which these springs are compressed. Herr Johann Bernoulli says of those who are not satisfied with the number of springs overcome alone, but still go on asking about the time of the compression, that they are as absurd as a man who wishes to measure the quantity of water in a beaker, and, not content with the actual measure he has before him, namely the capacity of the beaker, thinks that he must also know the time in which the beaker was filled. In his confidence and indignation he adds:* *Desine igitur quaerere nodum in scirpo.* The Marquise du Châtelet has a jest just as droll in readiness. But both are mistaken, and, if I may be permitted to say so, to the no less great detriment of their reputation than the confidence which they have let be seen in this error.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0575":["* *Acta Erud.* 1735, p. 210.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0576":["Why time necessarily comes into account in the case of the resistance of gravity.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0577":["Suppose that each of the springs A, B, C, D, E is of such a kind that it resists only a single pressure of the body M, and by this loses all its activity at once, so that it afterwards exerts no further effect upon the body M, however long the body may be exposed to it. In that case I myself admit that the body has exercised the same force, whether it has compressed these springs in a single time or in four times that time; for once it has compressed them, it spends the remaining time idle in their presence. If, on the contrary, the force of the body does not at the same time cancel the activity of the spring whose pressure it overcomes, then new degrees of force pass from the spring into the opposing body at every moment. For the efficacy of this spring, which in the first moment was the cause of a degree of force extinguished in the body, is still so, and just as strongly, in the second moment, then in the third, and so on through all the following moments to infinity. Under these conditions it is not a matter of indifference whether the body that overcomes the pressure of this spring does so in a shorter or a longer time; for in the longer time it has endured more pressures than in the shorter. Now the pressure of gravity is of this kind. Each of its springs acts at every moment with equal activity, and the body that overcomes its pressure in the first moment has not thereby done so for all the following moments. For the second it will need just as much force, and so on. The force, therefore, which a body expends in resisting the pressure of a single part of the matter that produces weight, is not merely as the intensity of the gravitational pressure, but as the rectangle formed from this intensity and the time.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0578":["Yet another proof against the living forces.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0579":["To the proof, itself more than sufficient, of the proposition that not the number of springs but the time is the measure of the effect produced, the following may be added. A body thrown obliquely, whose motion is parabolic, would have to traverse a certain height much more quickly by falling, and also to acquire at the end of it a much greater velocity and force, than vertical fall from the same height could impart to it. For in describing the curved line it passes through a greater space, up to the end of the fall, than if it had fallen vertically. In that greater space, however, it must necessarily endure more springs of gravity than it could meet with in the short straight line, since the matter that exerts the pressure of weight is spread equally on all sides. Hence, by Leibniz's proposition, it would have to acquire more force and velocity in the former than in the latter, which is absurd.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0580":["Thoughts on the Dispute\nbetween\nthe Marquise du Châtelet\nand Herr von Mairan,\nconcerning the Living Forces.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0581":["Herr von Mairan has hit upon the plan of estimating the force of a body by the obstacles not overcome, the springs not compressed, the matters not displaced, or, as Madame du Châtelet expresses it, by what the body does not do. This opponent believed she found something so odd in this thought that she supposed she had only to cite it in order to make it ridiculous. Yet this famous man had attached to his thought a limitation upon which everything really depends, namely, that these springs would nevertheless have been compressed if one assumed, by a hypothesis, that the body had retained its force, or had constantly taken it up again. Even so, his opponent finds something so illicit and unwarranted in this hypothesis that she brings against him a much harsher reproach on that account. I shall show briefly how certain and unerring the thought of this excellent man is, and that, apart from that of Herr Jurin, which we have already cited, nothing more decisive and thorough could readily have been devised in this matter.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0582":["Defence of Herr von Mairan's mode of estimation against Madame du Châtelet.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0583":["If one takes what the force of a body has lost in the course of overcoming certain obstacles, if, I say, one measures this loss, then one knows with the greatest certainty how great the total power of the resistance overcome has been. For the body could not have overcome this resistance or obstacle without expending in the process a degree of force equal to it; and as great as is this force destroyed and consumed in the body, so great also was the obstacle that took it from the body, and so great also the effect that was produced in that way.",["- 0574: OCR \"nodumin scirpo\" corrected to *nodum in scirpo*; \"Marquisin von Chastelet\" given as Marquise du Châtelet. The last clause is restated for clarity; its comparison (\"mit eben so großem Nachtheile … als die Zuversicht\") is kept literal.","- 0577: Long opening sentences were divided; \"Thätigkeit\" = activity, \"Wirksamkeit\" = efficacy. \"Rectangulum\" is rendered \"rectangle\" (the product of intensity and time).","- 0577: \"einfacher, oder vierfacher Zeit\" rendered \"a single time or four times that time\".","- 0579: The subject of \"müste\" is ambiguous in the first sentence; I read it as the obliquely thrown body, as in the following sentence. \"Leibnitzens Satze\" = Leibniz's proposition (that force is proportional to the number of springs overcome).","- 0581: \"hatte\" (for *hätte*) is taken as a subjunctive, \"had retained\". \"Hypothese\" in the sense of a supposition.","- 0581: \"Gegnerinn\" is rendered \"opponent\", given in the feminine by context.","- 0580, 0582: \"Vertheidung\" read as *Vertheidigung* (defence)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0584":["Suppose now a body* which rises vertically from the horizon with five degrees of velocity, and let the space, or the height which it attains, be expressed in the usual way by the area of the triangle A B C. In this triangle the line A B is to express the time elapsed, and B C the velocity with which the body rises to that height. The equal lines AD, DF, FH, and so on, are to express the elements of the whole time AB. Consequently the small triangles of which the surface of the large one is composed, all of which are as large as A D E, express the elements of the whole space, that is, the number of all the springs which the body compresses within the time A B. Accordingly, in the first small portion of time BK, in which it begins to rise, our body compresses the 9 springs which it meets in the space KL BC. If, however, the holding back of these springs had not consumed any force in it, or if this loss had always been made good from elsewhere, it would in addition have compressed the spring LlC, which it cannot now compress, since just so much force as it would need for this has been used up in compressing the others. The spring LlC is therefore the measure of that force which the resistance of the 9 compressed springs has consumed in our body. When it has done this, it continues to rise further with the remainder of its force, which is left to it after the loss indicated, and in the second small portion of time K H it compresses the 7 springs which are met with in the space HI KL. Here again it is clear that if our body had been able to compress these 7 springs and yet had retained its force entire, it would in the same minute have compressed and overcome the spring Ii L in addition. But since it has not done this, it follows that through compressing the 7 remaining springs it has lost the degree, the supplying of which would have put it in a position to overcome Ii L also. This spring therefore indicates the magnitude of the loss which the resistance of the 7 springs has brought upon its force. In just the same way the spring G gi will make known the loss of force through the restraints of gravity in the third small portion of time FH, and so on. The loss, then, which the body rising freely upwards suffers in overcoming the obstacle of gravity is proportional to the sum of the uncompressed springs Llc, IiL, Ggi, Eeg, AaE. So also is the quantity of the obstacle itself which it has overcome, and hence its force. And since the uncompressed springs have the ratio of the times or velocities, the force of the body is likewise proportional to these. Q.E.D. [W. Z. E.]",["- Long sentences divided (e.g. from \"Er würde aber…\", \"Hier ist nun aufs neue klar…\", \"So ist denn also…\").","- Footnote asterisk after \"Körper\" kept; text of the note is the next passage.","- Figure letters (LlC, Ii L, G gi, Llc, Eeg, AaE) kept as printed, with their inconsistencies in spacing and case; possible OCR/print slips not corrected.","- \"Feder\" = spring (the elastic springs of Kant's model); \"Zeittheilchen\" = small portion of time; \"zudrücken\" = compress.","- \"Minute\" kept literally as in the German; here it means a moment.","- \"Kraft … verzehren\" rendered \"consume\".","- \"W. Z. E.\" (was zu erweisen war) rendered Q.E.D., with the German in brackets.","- \"wie die Summe\" read as \"proportional to the sum\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0585":["* Compare Fig. XXII.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0586":["It is further evident from this why Herr von Mairan is entitled to assume by an hypothesis that the body has overcome obstacles and yet retained its force entire, which at first seems to contradict the first fundamental law of motions. For the obstacles do indeed take from it a part of its force equal to themselves. Yet it remains open to us always to make good this deficiency in thought from elsewhere, and so to hold the body undamaged, in order that we may see how much more it would accomplish, its force being in this way undiminished, than if what the obstacle had consumed had remained lost. This will then give the whole measure of that force which the resistance actually takes from the body, since it shows what degree must be added in order that the body may have lost nothing.",["- \"befugt sey\" rendered \"is entitled\"; \"Hypothese\" as \"hypothesis\".","- \"Grundgesetz der Bewegungen\" kept in the plural, \"laws of motions\".","- Last sentence: \"was vor einen Grad\" read as \"was für einen Grad\", \"what degree\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0587":["I cannot refrain from making here a further remark on the manner in which the Marquise attacks the doctrines of her opponent. It seems to me that she could not have chosen a better method of dealing him the most sensitive blow than by busying herself to give his inferences the look of something odd and absurd. A serious presentation draws the reader to proper attention and examination, and leaves the soul open to all the grounds which may press into it from one side or the other. But the strange figure under which she makes her opponent's opinions appear at once takes possession of the reader's weak side, and destroys in him the desire for closer consideration. That power of the soul which governs judgment and reflection is of a sluggish and quiet nature. It is pleased to find the point of its rest, and gladly stands still at whatever releases it from laborious thought. Hence it is easily won over by such representations [Vorstellungen] as at once reduce one of two opinions below probability and declare the trouble of further inquiry unnecessary. Our philosopher could therefore have used her *ridendo dicere verum*, or the idea of telling her opponent the truth with a laugh, with greater fairness and perhaps also with better success, if her opponent had been incapable of serious arguments and one had wished to make him feel how ridiculous he was. The remark which I make here would, against any other person of her sex, bear the appearance of ill-mannered conduct and of a behaviour which is called pedantic. But the superiority of understanding and learning in the person of whom I speak, which sets her above all others of her sex and above a great part of the other as well, deprives her at the same time of what is the proper prerogative of the fairer part of mankind, namely flattery and the praises which have it for their foundation.",["- Several long sentences divided, notably the one beginning \"Diejenige Kraft der Seele…\".","- \"Frau Marquisin\" = Mme du Châtelet; rendered \"the Marquise\". \"Seele\" rendered \"soul\", not \"mind\".","- \"die … heruntersetzet\" refers to \"Vorstellungen\"; the relative clause is smoothed.","- \"Auslachenswürdigkeit\" rendered \"how ridiculous he was\".","- \"Verstand\" rendered \"understanding\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0588":["Herr von Mairan's choice is made excellent by this further fact: that the springs which in his method are the measure of the force expended would not only be equal, but would also have been compressed in equal times. Consequently both the Leibnizians are satisfied, who insist on equality of space when they are to admit that the force is equal, and the Cartesians, who require this in regard to time.",["- \"seyn … würden seyn zugedrücket worden\" is irregular; smoothed to \"would … have been compressed\".","- \"dieses\" in the last clause read as the equality of force, to be judged by time."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0589":["III.\nAdditions to §§ 45, 46, 47.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0590":["It seems to me that I could have said nothing more certain and incontrovertible than this: that a spring cannot possibly drive a body forward unless it braces itself with equal force against a resistance, and presses against it as strongly as it drives the body on the other side by its tensile force. Consequently, since in Herr Bernoulli's case there is no other resistance than the body B, the spring must exert the same force of exertion against B as it can exert against A. For the spring would not drive the body A forward at all if B did not keep the spring in tension by resisting its extension. Hence B, since it is no immovable resistance, likewise receives all the force which the spring imparts to A. Although the whole world thinks in the same way, Herr Johann Bernoulli nevertheless found in the opposite view some bright light, I know not what, on which he based an unshakeable confidence. He says: *Non capio, quid pertinacissimus adversarius, si vel scepticus esset, huic evidentissimae demonstrationi opponere queat*; and shortly afterwards: *Certe, in nostra potestate non est, aliquem eo adigere, vt fateatur, diescere, quando videmus solem horizontem ascendere*. Let us not regard this lapse of human reason, in the person of so great a man, with indifference. Let us learn from it to place a wise distrust even in our greatest conviction, and always to presume that even then we are not yet out of danger of deceiving ourselves; so that the understanding may at least preserve its equilibrium until it has gained time to become acquainted, through sufficient examination, with the circumstances, the proof, and the contrary view.",["- The long first sentence is divided into several; the \"und folglich … müsse\" clause is kept dependent on \"Mich deucht\" and recast as \"Consequently, … the spring must exert\".","- \"derselbe\" (before \"weil er kein unbeweglicher Wiederhalt ist\") is resolved as the body B; \"sie\" as the spring.","- \"Wiederhalt\" rendered \"resistance\" (a fixed support or abutment is meant); \"Gegensatz\" rendered \"the opposite view\".","- Bernoulli's Latin is left in Latin and not translated, as the passage gives it; \"Zufall\" rendered \"lapse\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0591":["In this very treatise of which we are speaking, Herr Bernoulli shows how the same force can be imparted to a body in a shorter time by the pressure of an equal number of springs. So far as our business properly concerns it, I have already given sufficient answer. Here, however, I wish to add an observation which, though it does not touch our present purpose, may nevertheless have its own particular use. He says there that the ball F will always receive the same force from the 4 springs a, b, c, d, whether they are arranged in one line, as in Fig. 23, or in two parts side by side, as in Fig. 24, or in 4 such divisions, as Fig. 25 shows.",["- \"Zertheilungen\" rendered \"divisions\"; \"zusammensetzen\" taken as \"arranged\" (set together).","- The first sentence is a heading-like statement of Bernoulli's claim and is kept as indirect statement."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0592":["Remark on the way in which Herr Bernoulli supposes that the whole force of many springs can be brought into a body.",["- Marginal heading; \"vermeynet\" rendered \"supposes\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0593":["Here the following caution should be observed. His thought is true only in circumstances where the springs a, b, c, d, hanging one behind another, do not impart a greater velocity than that with which one of these springs, taken separately by itself, would spring open. For as soon as this is so, the plan fails, if, following Herr Bernoulli's proposal, one wishes by springs joined side by side to give the body the same velocity as they can impart to it successively in a row. Let the velocity which the row of springs in the 23rd figure imparts to the body, until they have fully extended, be as 10, while the velocity with which one of them, a for example, springs open by itself alone, that is, without driving a body forward, is as 8. Then it is clear that in the method of the 25th figure the 4 springs will be able to impart to the body only 8 degrees of velocity. For as soon as the body has received these degrees, it has just as much velocity as the springs which are to drive it have themselves when they spring open freely; so they will then be unable to bring anything more into it. Yet it is beyond dispute that, if this body F is to press the 4 springs shut again by its impact in the 25th figure, it needs for this just as much as 10 whole degrees of force, as in the 23rd or 24th. But since this 25th figure can be the representation of the elastic force of any body, it follows that it is possible for a perfectly elastic body to strike with a certain velocity against an immovable resistance, and that, nevertheless, the velocity with which it rebounds can be much smaller than that with which it had struck. If, however, one wishes the 4 springs to communicate their whole force to the body they drive, one must add 2⁄10 to the mass F; for then the 4 springs will make up, in quantity of matter, what they cannot supply in velocity.",["- \"Cautele\" rendered \"caution\"; \"Anschlag\" rendered \"proposal\"; \"Grade\" as \"degrees\".","- \"derselben\" in \"Der Gedanke desselben\" refers to Bernoulli; \"es schlägt fehl\" taken as \"the plan fails\".","- \"Anlauf\" rendered \"impact\"; \"angestoßen hatte\" rendered \"had struck\" (the sense is the incoming velocity)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0594":["IV.\nElucidation of § 105.",["- \"§\" kept as in the original numbering."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0595":["Detailed exposition of the errors in the Wolffian proof.",["- \"Wolfischen\" rendered \"Wolffian\" in the usual English form."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0596":["I did not explain myself clearly enough when, on p. 147, I wished to point out the extraordinary error in the argument of Baron Wolff. At first glance it seems as if the conclusion there still came out mathematically enough, namely according to the rule *aequales rationes sibi substitui invicem possunt*; but in fact it has no connection with that rule at all. The preceding case was this: *Tempora, quibus duo mobilia, si sunt aequalia, eosdem effectus patrant, sunt reciproce vt celeritates*. There follows in the second number of the proof: *Massae corporum inaequalium, quae eosdem effectus patrant, sunt reciproce vt celeritates*. From this Herr Wolff now concludes (for so his argument runs when properly resolved) that, because the ratio of the times and the ratio of the masses in both cases are equal to the ratio of the velocities, they will be equal to one another. This may be allowed, provided only that one does not overlook the determinations under which they are equal to one another, namely: that the masses of unequal bodies which produce the same effect are related as the times in which, *NB.*, equal bodies produce the same effect; for that is the limitation which, as can be seen, attaches to the ratios. But Herr Wolff's conclusion is this: therefore the masses of these bodies are related as the times in which these same unequal bodies produce their equal effect; which is an evident falsification of the given proportion.",["- Latin quotations left in Latin as in the source; \"NB.\" kept in italics.","- \"Verhältniß\" rendered \"ratio\"; \"Bestimmungen\" rendered \"determinations\" in the sense of conditions.","- \"die Verhältniß … gleich seyn\" is plural in sense; the sentence is smoothed to \"the ratio … are equal\".","- \"dieselbe\" in \"mit derselben\" taken as the rule."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0597":["If our author had only thought of comparing with one another the two propositions which he wishes to derive from each other, he would have seen as clear as day that they not only do not flow from one another but even directly contradict each other. The first proposition is this: *Actiones, quibus corpora aequalia eosdem effectus patrant, sunt ut celeritates*. From this he wishes to derive the other proposition, which is the result of the second number in the proof, namely: *Actiones, quibus corpora inaequalia eosdem effectus patrant, sunt etiam vt ipsorum celeritates; celeritates autem eorum sunt reciproce vt massae*.",["- \"Satz\" rendered \"proposition\"; Latin left untranslated.","- \"Nemlich der erste Satz\" is rendered \"The first proposition\", dropping the connective \"Nemlich\" for smoothness."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0598":["If now, following the first proposition, we take two equal bodies A and B, so that B has twice the velocity of A, then according to this rule the action with which B produces the same effect as A is twice as great as the action of body A; for B, because of its greater velocity, performs this effect in half the time. According to the second rule, however, I could make B half as large, and the said action would still be just as great as before, provided the velocity remained as before. Now it is evident that if B becomes half as large as it was, and its velocity remains the same, it cannot possibly perform the given effect in the same time as when its mass was twice as great; it will need more time for it. Since, then, the action becomes smaller the greater the time employed for the same effect, the action must necessarily be smaller in this case than when the mass of B, at the same velocity, is twice as great. This contradicts the result of the second number.",["- Restated \"so ist ... weil jener ...\" with \"B\" for \"jener\" and \"A\" for \"diesen\" resolved: \"jener\" = B (the faster body), \"diesen Effect\" = the effect.","- \"zweymal größer/kleiner\" rendered \"twice as great / half as large\"; \"Nummer\" rendered \"number\" (the numbered rule).","- \"Effect\", \"Action\" kept as in the German (effect, action)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0599":["All these contradictions, however, are to be found in the Wolffian proof under consideration, even if one grants it the proposition that it takes as its ground, namely that *actiones* can be unequal whose *effectus* are nevertheless equal. This proposition, which no mortal has ever thought of maintaining, is a contradiction in the best form, as exact as one could possibly devise. For the word \"action\" is a relative word, which indicates the effect or operation in a thing in so far as another thing contains the ground of it. Effect and action are therefore one and the same, and the meaning differs only in that I now refer it to the thing which is its ground, now consider it apart from that thing. To say this would therefore be as much as to say that an action could be unequal to itself. Moreover, it has the name of action only because an effect depends upon it; and if there could be a part in this action upon which no equal effect depended, that part could not bear the name of action either. Even if the times in which the same effects are produced are unequal, the actions expended upon them nevertheless remain equal, and the only consequence is that, with equal times, the effects, and the actions corresponding to them, will be unequal.",["- \"Wirkung oder Effect\" rendered \"effect or operation\"; \"Wolfischen\" rendered \"Wolffian\".","- \"referire\" rendered \"refer\"; \"Effectus\" and \"Actiones\" kept in Latin italics as Latin plural forms.","- Broke the sentence \"Es würde also ...\" into a plainer English form with \"To say this\" supplied as subject."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0600":["To speak briefly: it is at once evident that quite special causes must have occasioned such exceptional faults in this treatise, faults wholly out of keeping with the well-known and highly praised acuteness of the author, which shines forth in everything that is his own. It is not hard to judge that the praiseworthy desire to save the honour of Herr von Leibniz, which was then regarded as the honour of the whole of Germany, produced this effort, and presented the proofs in a much more favourable guise than, apart from this light, they would have appeared to their author. The cause itself was of so desperate a kind that it could not be defended without errors; yet the undertaking was so alluring that it left no room for coolness of examination. I wish the same to be said of the lapses of the highly renowned gentlemen, Herr Hermann, Bernoulli, etc., which I have either already shown or shall yet show, and of which, apart from this reproach, one scarcely finds any in them. The honour of the man of whom we speak therefore remains secure. I am at liberty to deal with his defence as with something that is not his own property. Meanwhile he may call out to me what an older philosopher cried out, though on an occasion that concerned him somewhat more closely: \"You strike only the house of Anaxarchus.\"",["- \"Schutzschrift\" rendered \"defence\"; \"Sache\" as \"cause\" in \"Die Sache selber\" (the Leibnizian cause) and \"something\" in \"eine Sache\".","- \"Herrmanns\" taken as the genitive of Hermann (Jakob Hermann); the form is as printed.","- \"ihr Unterfangen\" (\"their undertaking\") rendered \"the undertaking\", referring to the defence of the cause.","- The saying is attributed to Anaxarchus (struck while being pounded in a mortar): \"the husk/case\" is the body; \"Gehäuse\" rendered \"house\" for fidelity."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0601":["Third Chapter,\nWhich Presents a New Estimation of the Living Forces as the True Measure of Force in Nature.",["- \"Hauptstück\" rendered \"Chapter\"; \"lebendigen Kräfte\" as \"living forces\" (Latin *vires vivae*)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0602":["§ 114.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0603":["How a law which has been found false in mathematics can nevertheless hold in nature.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0604":["We have thus shown at length that the estimation of forces by the square is found false in mathematics, and that mathematics permits no other measure of forces than the old, or Cartesian. Nevertheless, in various places in the preceding chapter we have given the reader hope that we should none the less introduce the estimation by the square into nature, and it is now time to fulfil our promise. This undertaking will make most of my readers hesitate; for it seems as if it followed from it that mathematics is not infallible, and that it is permissible still to appeal from its verdict. But the matter is really not so. If mathematics pronounced its laws upon all bodies in general, the natural bodies too would be included under them, and it would be vain to hope for an exception. But it fixes the concept of its own body itself, by means of the *axiomata* which it demands must be presupposed in its body, and which are of such a kind that they do not permit, and exclude, certain properties in that body which must nevertheless necessarily be met with in the body of nature. Consequently the body of mathematics is a thing wholly different from the body of nature, and something can therefore be true of the former which cannot be extended to the latter.",["- \"Axiomatum\" rendered *axiomata* (Latin plural, italicised); \"Quadrat-Schätzung\" as \"estimation by the square\".","- \"an demselben\" resolved as the mathematical body."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0605":["§ 115.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0606":["Difference between the mathematical and the natural body, and the laws relating to each.",["- \"derer, beyderseits betreffenden Gesetze\" smoothed to \"the laws relating to each\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0607":["We shall now see what property it is that is found in the body of nature and that mathematics does not permit in its own, and which then causes the former to be a thing of an entirely different kind from the latter. Mathematics does not permit its body to have a force which has not been wholly produced by that which is the external cause of its motion. It therefore admits no other force in the body than as it has been caused in it from without, and this force will consequently always be found again, exactly and in the same measure, in the causes of its motion. This is a fundamental law of mechanics, the presupposition of which, however, allows no other estimation than the Cartesian. With the body of nature, however, as we shall soon prove, the constitution is quite different. It has in itself a power of increasing of itself the force which has been aroused in it from without by the cause of its motion, so that there can be in it degrees of force which have not sprung from the external cause of the motion, and which are also greater than it. These cannot therefore be measured by the same measure with which the Cartesian force is measured, and have also a different estimation. We shall treat of this property of the natural body with all the exactness and thoroughness which so important a matter requires.",["- \"Vermögen\" rendered \"power\" as the sense requires; \"Geschlechte\" rendered \"kind\".","- \"und auch größer seyn wie dieselbe\" resolved: \"dieselbe\" = the external cause (of the motion).","- Divided the last long sentence of the middle section into two for clarity (\"These cannot therefore...\")."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0608":["§ 116.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0609":["Velocity is not a concept of a force.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0610":["As we saw in § 3, velocity in and by itself includes no concept of a force. For it is a determination of motion, that is, of that state of a body in which the body does not apply the force it has, but remains inactive with it. It is, however, properly the number of that force which the body has when it is at rest, that is, which it has with an infinitely small velocity; in other words, it is the number in which that force which belongs to the body at infinitely small velocity is the unit. This is clearest from the manner of analysis indicated by the excellent Jurinian case in § 110, if, that is, we consider velocity in its infinitely small parts, in a way similar to that in which he regards it as consisting of two equal parts.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0611":["§ 117.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0612":["There would be no force if there were no endeavour to maintain the state in itself.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0613":["To know exactly what properly determines the concept of force, we must proceed as follows. Force is rightly estimated by the obstacle which it breaks and removes in the body. It is evident from this that a body would have no force at all if there were not in it an endeavour [*Bestrebung*] to maintain in itself the state which the obstacle is to remove; for if this were not so, that which had to break the obstacle would be as nothing [wie 0].",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0614":["What intension is.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0615":["Motion is the outward phenomenon of force; the endeavour to maintain this motion, however, is the basis of activity; and velocity indicates how many times the latter must be taken in order to have the whole force. The latter [the endeavour] we shall henceforth entitle the *intension*; force is therefore equal to the product of the velocity and the intension.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0616":["Elucidation of this concept.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0617":["In order to have an example by which these concepts may be the more distinctly noted, let us take the fourfold spring a, b, c, d.* Suppose that the velocity with which each of these springs by itself begins to extend is as 1. Then the initial velocity with which the whole spring a d, which is composed of four such springs, would extend if it stretched freely, is as 4; and it seems to follow from this that the initial velocity which the fourfold spring imparts to a body will be four times greater than that which the single spring produces. But the intension in the fourfold spring is four times smaller than in the single one. For the very same force which would compress one of these four connected springs to a certain degree against an immovable resistance compresses the fourfold spring four times as much, because the resistance of the single spring, when it has been connected in this way with three others, is a movable resistance; and consequently the fourfold spring lacks, in stiffness (or, what is here the same thing, in intension), just that which its velocity exceeds. Hence it happens that the initial velocity which the fourfold spring imparts to a body is no greater than that which the body can receive from a single spring, although the initial velocity of the former, when it extends freely, exceeds that of the latter fourfold. This may serve to make the concept of intension intelligible, and to show why it must necessarily be taken into account in estimating force.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0618":["* Fig. XXIII.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0619":["§ 118.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0620":["If the intension is like a point, the force is like a line, namely like the velocity.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0621":["If the force of a body is of such a kind that it endeavours to maintain the state of motion for only an instant, whatever the velocity may be, then this endeavour, or intension, is the same at all velocities. Consequently the whole force of such a body is in proportion to its velocity only; for one of the factors is always equal, and the product, which indicates the quantity of the force, therefore varies as the second factor.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0622":["§ 119.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0623":["If the intension is finite, that is, like a line, the force is like the square.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0624":["In the case of such a motion, an unceasing replacement from without of the force vanishing in the body at every instant would be necessary, and the force would be always merely the effect of a constant external impulse, if the body were in this way to sustain a perpetual motion. But it is also clearly evident from this that if, on the contrary, the force of the body were of such a kind as to contain in itself a sufficient endeavour to maintain the motion with the given velocity, uniformly and unceasingly, of its own accord and without help of any external power, this force would have to be of an entirely different kind, and also infinitely more perfect.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0625":["For since the intension of the former is the same at all velocities, namely infinitely small, and is multiplied only by the number of degrees of velocity, the intension of the latter must, on the contrary, always be in proportion to the velocity, and must also be multiplied by it; the result is the true measure of the force. For the finite velocity whose intension is infinitely small yields a force of which that force which this same intension constitutes at infinitely small velocity is the unit. If, then, a body is to ground this velocity and force sufficiently in itself, so as to have the complete endeavour to maintain it in itself perpetually, its intension will have to be proportioned to this force or velocity. From this there arises a quite new power, which is the product of the force proportioned to the velocity, multiplied by the intension, which is now likewise as the velocity; and this product is therefore equal to the square of the velocity. It is easy to understand that the force which the body had with infinitely small intension and at finite velocity was like a line representing this velocity, and the intension like a point. Now, however, the intension is likewise like a line, and the force arising from it is therefore like a surface generated by the flow of the first line, and indeed like the square, because the lines in question are proportional to one another.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0626":["It should be noted that I here abstract throughout from the difference of masses, or think them equal; and secondly, that I regard the space, in the motions of which I speak, as empty.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0627":["§ 120.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0628":["The body which has within itself the inner endeavour to maintain its motion freely and perpetually has a force which is as the square of the velocity.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0629":["A body, then, which sufficiently grounds its motion in itself, so that it can be adequately understood from its inner endeavour that it will of itself maintain in itself, freely, perpetually and undiminished to infinity, the motion which it has, has a force of which the square of its velocity is the measure; or, as we shall henceforth entitle it, a living force [*lebendige Kraft*]. If, on the contrary, its force does not have the ground of its maintenance in itself, but rests only on the presence of the external cause, then it is as the mere velocity; that is, it is a dead force [*todte Kraft*].",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0630":["§ 121.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0631":["From its inner impulse the body raises the impression from without infinitely higher, and into an entirely different genus.",["- 0610: \"Zahl\" rendered \"number\" (ratio/multiple); \"Jurinischen Falle\" = Jurin's case, referring to § 110. \"Zergliederung\" = analysis.","- 0613: \"Bestrebung\" = endeavour throughout; \"Hinderniß\" = obstacle. \"wie 0\" kept literal, bracketed.","- 0615: \"Jene\" refers to the *Bestrebung* (the nearer antecedent is the endeavour, not motion); I made this explicit. \"Intension\" left as Kant's own term, italicised.","- 0617: Semicolon and parenthetical in the long sentence \"denn eben dieselbe Kraft…\" restated in two sentences; \"zudrücken\" rendered \"compress\". The footnote marker is kept in place with its footnote (0618). \"Wiederhalt\" = resistance (point of resistance, abutment). The clause \"viermal übertrift\" is rendered literally \"exceeds … fourfold\".","- 0619–0625: \"Gewalt\" = power; \"Fläche\" = surface; \"Quadrat\" = square. In 0625 \"jener\"/\"dieselbe\"/\"dieser\" resolved as the former (the force with infinitely small intension) and the latter (the self-maintaining force); the sentence \"Und hieraus entspringet…\" is divided at the semicolon.","- 0624: \"Machthülfe\" rendered \"help of any external power\".","- 0629: \"lebendige Kraft\"/\"todte Kraft\" are rendered \"living force\"/\"dead force\" with the German given.","- 0631: \"Geschlechte\" rendered \"genus\" (Latin *genus* parallel, as in Kant's discussions of kinds of force); possibly \"kind\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0632":["Let us now consider the nature of a body's force when it first arises in the body through the action of an external cause. The force is then unquestionably grounded in the presence of this external cause, and would not be present in the body at that same instant if the cause had not aroused the impulse. Hence at the very instant in which the force rests upon the presence of the external cause, it is of such a kind that it would have to vanish instantly if that cause were not present. (Whether the body could afterwards ground in itself this force aroused in it, and what would then follow from this, is a matter we do not here discuss.) At that instant, therefore, the intension of the force is infinitely small, and consequently the force itself, which is grounded only upon the external impulse, is like mere velocity, that is, dead. If, however, the same body afterwards grounds this velocity imparted to it in its own inner force, so that from its endeavour there results a perpetual and free conservation of the motion, then the force is no longer a dead force but a living one, which has the square as its measure and is to be reckoned in relation to the former as a surface to a line. From this it is clear that a body which in this way freely continues of itself the velocity impressed upon it infinitely augments in itself, of its own accord, the force which it has received from the external mechanical cause, and raises it to a wholly different kind. Consequently the remark which we gave in § 115 is here proved, and living forces are entirely excluded from the jurisdiction of mathematics.",["- Restated the long final sentence as three; the parenthesis replaces the *denn*-clause, which is an aside.","- *Intension* kept as Kant's Latinate term; *Kraft* = force; *Geschwindigkeit* = velocity; *todt/lebendig* = dead/living.","- *Quadrat* rendered \"square\" (measure of living force); *Fläche*/*Linie* = surface/line.","- \"verhanden\" read as OCR/old spelling for *vorhanden*."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0633":["A body cannot acquire living force from outside.",["- Marginal heading to the surrounding section."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0634":["It can further be seen from this that living force cannot be produced in a body by an external cause, however great that cause may be. For in so far as a force depends upon a cause from outside, it is always, as we have proved, only like simple velocity. On the contrary, the body must obtain the determinations belonging to the square measure from the inner source of its natural force.",["- Split at the colon; \"sondern\" rendered \"on the contrary\", as it answers the negative claim.","- *Bestimmungen* = determinations; *Naturkraft* = natural force."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0635":["§ 122.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0636":["There are infinitely many intermediate degrees between dead and living force.",["- \"u.\" expanded to \"and\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0637":["We have proved that if a body has the cause of its motion sufficiently and completely grounded in itself, so that it can be understood from the constitution of its force that this force will maintain itself in it unchanged and freely for ever, the body has a living force; but that if it does not ground its force in itself at all, and depends for it on something outside, it has only a dead force, which is infinitely smaller than the former. This at once yields the consequence that, if the same body has grounded its force in itself to some extent but not yet completely, its force comes somewhat nearer to the living and differs somewhat from the dead. It follows also that between these two extreme limits, the wholly dead and the wholly living force, there must necessarily be infinitely many intermediate degrees leading over from the one to the other.",["- \"wen͂\" read as *wenn*. Divided the final sentence by adding \"It follows also\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0638":["Living force arises only in a finite time after the beginning of the motion.",["- Marginal heading."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0639":["It further follows from this, by virtue of the law of continuity, that the same body which at the initial instant has a dead force and afterwards acquires a living one (which is related to the former as a surface to the line generating it) attains this force only in a finite time. For suppose it were said that the body acquires this latter force not in a finite time from the initial instant, but immediately, in the infinitely small particle of time following it. This would amount to saying that the body already has this living force in the initial instant itself. For the law of continuity, and mathematics itself, prove that it is all one whether I say that the body is at the initial instant of its motion or in the infinitely small particle of time after it. Now the force at the initial point of the motion is itself dead. Hence one cannot, without contradiction, say that it is afterwards living, unless one at the same time lays down that this living force is found in it only after a finite time, after the action of the external cause.",["- Relative clause \"die gegen die erstere…\" placed in parentheses for clarity.","- \"in ihr\" (twice) refers to the force/body; rendered \"in it\" and, for the second, left ambiguous as in the German."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0640":["Elucidation of the same.",["- \"desselben\" refers to the preceding proposition."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0641":["The natural force of the body, that is to say, continues in itself the impression received from outside. By a continued endeavour it accumulates in itself the intension, which was previously like a point, until it becomes like a line proportional to the force aroused in it from outside (a force which behaved like velocity). In so doing it accumulates the force obtained from outside, which was previously likewise only like a line, so that it is now like a surface. One side of this surface represents the velocity and force externally imparted; the other depicts the intension which has grown of itself out of the inside of the body, and which is proportional to the former.",["- Divided the sentence in three. \"verhielte\" (subjunctive) rendered with past \"behaved\". \"Vorstellet/vorbildet\" = represents/depicts."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0642":["§ 123.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0643":["What vivification is.",["- *Vivification* kept as in the German."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0644":["That state in which the force of the body is not yet living but is advancing toward it, I call the becoming-living (*Lebendigwerdung*), or vivification, of the force.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0645":["What the intension is like during the vivification of the force.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0646":["In the interval, then, during which the force raises itself to the living, an interval comprised between two points, the initial point and the point at which the force is already fully living, the body has not yet sufficiently grounded its force and velocity in itself. Here my reader may perhaps be moved to ask how the body in this interval can freely and uniformly maintain and continue the velocity imparted to it, when it has not yet sufficiently grounded its force and motion in itself, and consequently cannot maintain them of itself. To this I reply: in this interval the force is indeed not of such a kind that a perpetual, free and undiminished motion could be understood from it, unless it were raised still further by the inner endeavour. But whether the endeavour of the force to maintain itself is in this way incomplete is not the question here. The question is only whether the intension of the force, not yet grown so far that it can maintain the motion undiminished and unceasingly, can at least maintain it through the time needed for the completion of the vivification. That this is not only possible but actually so is evident from the following: in this whole interval a new element of the intension arises in the body at every instant, which maintains the given velocity for an infinitely small particle of time. Consequently all the elements of this intension, which arise in the body throughout the whole interval, receive the same velocity at all instants of it, that is, in the whole time, as is clear from comparison with § 18.",["- Restated the long final sentences; \"die Zwischenzeit… begriffen wird\" rendered as an apposition.","- \"in dieser Art unvollständig\" taken as \"in this way incomplete\".","- \"erhalten\" in the last clause (elements \"receive/retain\") is ambiguous; \"receive\" chosen, \"retain\" possible.","- \"Zwichenzeit\" is an OCR/print slip for *Zwischenzeit*."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0647":["If the vivification were to cease before it had become complete, what would then happen to the motion?",["- Marginal heading in the form of a question."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0648":["But let us suppose that in the interval of vivification, before it has yet been completed, the body suddenly ceases to go on accumulating the elements of intension and so to make the force wholly living. What will then happen? It is evident that the body will then ground in itself, and thereafter maintain constantly in free motion, only those degrees of velocity to which the intension it has already gained in this time of vivification is proportional. The remaining degrees of velocity, which would require a greater intension than actually exists in order to arrive at complete vivification, must suddenly vanish and cease. For the intension present is capable of grounding in itself only a part of this velocity; and since new elements of intension no longer arise at each moment, to maintain at every moment the velocity given, the remaining part must of itself disappear.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0649":["And how would it then stand with the force?",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0650":["If, therefore, a body moving freely meets with a resistance upon which it exerts its force before it has attained complete vivification with its whole velocity, the force which it exerts is as the square of that degree of velocity to which its acquired intension is proportional and corresponds, and which could accordingly become living in the given time; or, again, it is as the square of this acquired intension itself. With the remaining degrees the body is inactive, or at any rate acts only in accordance with the measure of mere velocity, which, compared with the other force, is to be reckoned as nothing.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0651":["§ 124.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0652":["New estimation of force.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0653":["A body, therefore, which maintains its velocity undiminished to infinity in free motion has a living force, that is, a force which has the square of the velocity as its measure.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0654":["Conditions of this.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0655":["But these are also the conditions attaching to this law.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0656":["1. The body must contain in itself the ground for maintaining its motion uniformly, freely and perpetually in a non-resisting space.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0657":["2. It is seen from what has been proved above that the body does not have this force from the external cause which set it in motion, but that, following the external stimulus, the force springs from the inner natural force of the body itself.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0658":["3. This force must be generated in it in a finite time.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0659":["§ 125.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0660":["This law is the chief ground of the new estimation of forces. I should say that I set it in the place of the estimations of Descartes and Leibniz and make it the foundation of the true dynamics, if the slightness of my judgments, in comparison with such great men as I have to deal with, permitted me to speak with such authority. Meanwhile I am not disinclined to persuade myself that this law may perhaps determine the goal, the missing of which has aroused the discord and disunion among the philosophers of all nations. The living forces are received into nature, after having been banished from mathematics. Neither of the two great philosophers, neither Leibniz nor Descartes, can be found wholly guilty of error. Even in nature Leibniz's law will not hold otherwise than as moderated by Descartes's estimation. To unite human reason with itself in the several persons of acute men is, in a manner, to defend its honour; and it is to find out the truth, which the thoroughness of such men never wholly misses, even when they directly contradict one another.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0661":["§ 126.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0662":["Since there are free motions, there are also living forces.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0663":["All that matters is that there should be in the world free motions which would maintain themselves perpetually and undiminished if there were no external resistance. If so, the matter is settled, and there are assuredly living forces in nature. The free and perpetual motion of the planets, and the countless other experiences which show that freely moved bodies lose their motion only in proportion to the resistance, and would always retain it without it, afford this assurance and assert the existence of living forces in nature.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0664":["Mathematics permits no free motions.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0665":["It is, however, also clear from this that mathematics, judged in strictness, permits no free motion in its body. For it does not permit what is necessary to make motion free and perpetual, namely, that the body should generate in itself, from within, an endeavour and force which neither has arisen from the external cause nor can come from it. For mathematics recognises no other force in a body than that which has been produced by the body that is the cause of its motion.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0666":["§ 127.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0667":["An easier method of making use of these considerations.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0668":["Although the considerations and proofs so far are of such a kind that, so far as the nature of the subject allows, they equal mathematical concepts and their clearness, I wish nevertheless, for the sake of those to whom everything is suspect that has merely the semblance of metaphysics, and who insist on an experience as basis of the inferences, to indicate a method by which they may use these considerations with better satisfaction. Towards the end of this chapter I shall, that is, demonstrate from an experience, with mathematical rigour, that forces which have the square of the velocity are actually to be found in nature.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0669":["Thereupon these gentlemen can convince themselves, from the result of all the proofs of the second chapter, that such a force cannot be an effect of the external mechanical cause. For if the force is admitted only as an effect of that cause which brought the motion about, no estimation could hold other than that by mere velocity. This will then lead them to the way in which this force can spring from the inner natural force of the body, and will gradually conduct them into those considerations which I have made upon the essence of living forces.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0670":["§ 128.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0671":["Herr Bernoulli already had these concepts.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0672":["I have said that the free duration of the force, continued from within the body, is the true mark from which alone it can be inferred that the force is living and has the square as its measure. I am uncommonly pleased to find this thought expressed with the greatest exactness in that treatise of Herr Johann Bernoulli which we cited above. As a mere geometer he has expressed his opinion, not indeed in the proper language of metaphysics, yet perfectly clearly: *Vis viva*, he says, *est aliquid reale & substantiale, quod per se subsistit, & quantum in se est, non dependit ab alio; Vis mortua non est aliquid absolutum, & per se durans &c. &c.*",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0673":["This citation is of no small advantage to my consideration. The mathematician otherwise regards with a certain distrust conclusions which he believes to flow from subtle metaphysical distinctions, and this obliges him to withhold his assent; and I should have to fear that he might do the same with mine. Here, however, the matter lies so plainly open that it presents itself of its own accord to the strictest geometer in his mathematical reflection.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0674":["But he has not sought it in the sound grounds.",["- 0648: \"Intension\" and \"Vivification\" kept as in Kant's own Latinate terms; \"Elemente der Intension\" rendered \"elements of intension\". Restated nothing, but \"ablasse … zu häufen\" rendered \"ceases to go on accumulating\".","- 0650: \"dem Quadrate dieser seiner erlangten Intension\" read as parallel alternative (\"or, again\"); \"schlechten Geschwindigkeit\" = \"mere velocity\".","- 0652, 0654, 0667, 0671, 0674: marginal headings of the original, translated as they stand.","- 0657: \"Siehet man\" is a defective construction; smoothed to \"It is seen\". The supplied \"must\" in 0658 completes the elliptical \"Daß…\" clause (the clause is dependent in the German).","- 0660: last sentence is irregular (\"sie … mit sich selber vereiniget … und die Wahrheit … herausfindet\"); I recast it into two coordinate clauses, and read \"dieser ihre Gründlichkeit\" as the thoroughness of such acute men.","- 0665: \"an ihrem Körper\" rendered \"in its body\", i.e. the body mathematics treats of.","- 0668: \"Methaphysik\" silently corrected to \"Metaphysik\"; \"mathematischen Begriffen\" kept as \"mathematical concepts\".","- 0672: Bernoulli's Latin kept in italics; \"quantumin se est\" corrected to *quantum in se est* (OCR); \"Merkmaal\" = \"mark\".","- 0674: sentence is brief and evidently depends on the preceding passage; \"sie\" taken to refer to the thought/matter, not completed here."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0675":["I am astonished that Herr Bernoulli, who had this light in his concept of living force, could have gone so far astray in the manner by which he sought to prove this force. He could easily have inferred that he would not find it in those cases which are indeterminate with regard to this *realis & substantialis, quod per se subsistit, & est absolutum aliquid*, or in which the determinations that are supposed to lead to it are not to be met with. For this, as he himself saw, is the generic mark of living force; and what is indeterminate in respect of this character cannot lead to living force either. Meanwhile he believed that he had found it in the case of the spring stretching out between two unequal bodies. In that case there is not only nothing to be found which should lead rather to living force, as marked by the above distinguishing sign, than to so-called dead force; but all the force which enters into the construction of his proof is, moreover, something *quod non est aliquid absolutum, sed dependet ab alio*.",["- Restated the long second sentence as two; \"denen Fällen … unbestimmt seyn\" is rendered as a relative clause with \"which are indeterminate\".","- \"Geschlechts-Merkmahl\" = \"generic mark\" (Latin *genus*); \"Bestimmungen\" = \"determinations\".","- The final clause is loosely constructed in German (\"sondern so gar alle Kraft … etwas ist\"); I have smoothed it and supplied \"moreover\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0676":["This again convinces us how dangerous it is to abandon oneself to the mere outcome of assent in a composite and plausible proof, without the guidance of the method which we recommended in §§ 88, 89, 90 and have employed with great profit. That is to say, it shows how indispensably necessary it is first to consider beforehand the concepts which necessarily attach to the thing that is the subject of the proof, and afterwards to inquire whether the conditions of the proof also include the proper determinations aiming at the establishment of these concepts.",["- \"d. i.\" rendered \"That is to say\"; \"Beyfalles\" = \"assent\". The sentence is divided at \"d. i.\""]],"lebendige-kraefte-0677":["§ 129.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0678":["Living forces are contingent in their nature.",["- \"zufälliger\" rendered \"contingent\" (Latin *contingens*)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0679":["The Leibnizians also recognised this.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0680":["And yet they seek them in geometrically necessary truths.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0681":["We have proved that the existence of living forces in nature rests solely on the presupposition that there are free motions in it. Now from the essential and geometrical properties of a body no argument can be found which should reveal such a capacity as is required for the performance of a free and unaltered motion, in accordance with what we established on this point in the preceding. It follows, therefore, that living forces are not known as a necessary property, but are something hypothetical and contingent. Herr von Leibniz himself recognised this, as he especially admits in the *Theodicy*; and Herr Daniel Bernoulli confirms it by the manner which, as he thinks, must be employed in order to make living forces demonstrable, namely, that one must presuppose the fundamental equation dv = pdt, in which dv denotes the element of velocity, p the pressure which generates the velocity, and dt the element of time in which the pressure has produced the infinitely small velocity. He says that this is something hypothetical which must be assumed. The other champions of living forces, who made it a scruple of conscience to judge otherwise than Herr von Leibniz, have sung to the same tune. And yet they have sought living forces in cases which are throughout geometrically necessary, and believed that they found them there; which is certainly extremely astonishing.",["- \"Grundäquation\" = \"fundamental equation\"; formula kept as printed. \"Manier\" rendered \"manner\".","- \"Vermögen\" rendered \"capacity\"; the sentence beginning \"Nun kan man\" is kept whole but lightly smoothed."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0682":["Herr Herrmann's strange false step in this matter.",["- Marginal summary heading."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0683":["Herr Herrmann attempted the same procedure, without allowing himself to be misled by the contingency of living forces. But his preconceived good opinion of Leibniz's thoughts, and his resolve to reach his goal at all costs, led him into a fallacy that is certainly noteworthy. It seems to me that it would not easily be possible to find anyone who would think of inferring as follows: The two magnitudes a and b are to be taken together and considered in their combination; ergo they must be multiplied together. And yet this was done, quite literally, by Herr Herrmann, who was so great a master of inference. \"Since the body,\" he says, \"which in falling receives a new element of force, already has a velocity, this too must be taken into consideration. One will therefore put together the velocity u which it already has, its mass M, and the element of velocity, or what is the same thing, the product of gravity g into the time, that is, gdt. *Ergo* dV, or the element of the living force, is equal to gMudt, that is, to the product of the magnitudes here designated.\"",["- \"auf die gleiche Art\" taken as \"the same procedure\" (i.e. proving living force from geometrically necessary premises), as the context suggests.","- Formulae kept as printed; \"Productaus\" in the OCR corrected to \"Product aus\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0684":["§ 130.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0685":["Experience confirms the successive vivification.",["- \"Lebendigwerdung\" = \"vivification\" (Kant gives *Vivification* as its equivalent in §131)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0686":["Our system implies that a body moving freely and uniformly does not yet possess its greatest force at the beginning of its motion, but that this force is greater when it has already been in motion for some time. It seems to me that everyone knows of certain experiences which confirm this. I myself have found that, with a perfectly equal charge in a musket and exact agreement in the other circumstances, its ball penetrated much deeper into a piece of wood when I fired it from some paces away than when I shot it into the wood from only a few inches. Those who have better opportunity than I for making experiments can carry out more exact and better measured trials in this matter. Meanwhile experience teaches that the intensity of a body which moves uniformly and freely grows within it, and has its proper magnitude only after a certain time, in accordance with the propositions which we have proved concerning this.",["- \"Intension\" rendered \"intensity\" (Latin *intensio*); \"Zolle\" = inches.","- \"Flinte\" rendered \"musket\"; \"Ziele\" rendered \"target\" implicitly through \"away\" (I wrote \"from some paces away\")."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0687":["§ 131.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0688":["Now that we have laid the foundation of a new estimation of forces, we ought to endeavour to indicate those laws which are especially bound up with it and which constitute, as it were, the scaffolding of a new dynamics.",["- \"Kräftenschätzung\" = \"estimation of forces\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0689":["I am in a position to set forth some laws according to which the vivification, or becoming-alive, of force takes place. But since this treatise is concerned to sketch the first outline of these so new and unexpected properties of forces, I have rightly to fear that my readers, who are chiefly eager to be assured of the main matter, might find themselves vexed at being entangled in a deep investigation of a side issue; the more so as there is time enough to enter upon it when the main work has first been sufficiently secured and confirmed by experience.",["- Divided at \"allein\"; \"Vivification oder Lebendigwerdung\" kept with both terms."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0690":["Accordingly, I shall endeavour to disclose with the greatest possible clearness only the most general laws, and those most worthy of observation, which are connected with our estimation of forces, and without which its nature cannot well be comprehended.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0691":["§ 132.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0692":["The following remark sets forth a quite unknown dynamical law, and is of no common importance in the estimation of forces.",["- \"Erheblichkeit\" rendered \"importance\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0693":["The vivification of force does not hold for all velocities without distinction.",["- \"Kr.\" in the German is an abbreviation; expanded as \"force\" (*Kraft*) by inference. Heading, evidently a marginal summary."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0694":["We have learned that a body which acts in a state of rest exerts only a dead pressure, entirely different in kind from living forces, and having as its measure only the simple velocity; with which both the whole following of the Cartesians and Leibniz's disciples agree. But a body whose velocity is infinitely small does not properly move at all, and therefore has a force subsisting in a state of rest; hence its force has the measure of velocity simply.",["- \"schlechte Geschwindigkeit\" rendered \"simple velocity\" (*schlecht* in the older sense of \"plain\"); \"Geschlechte\" rendered \"kind\" (*genus*)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0695":["If, then, we wish to determine the motions that belong to the class of living forces, we must not extend them to all motions whose velocity may be as great or as small as one pleases, that is, to motions whose velocity is left undetermined. For in that case the same law would hold for all degrees of velocity, however infinitely small, and bodies would be able to have a living force even with an infinitely small velocity, which has shortly before been found false.",["- \"Bestimmen/bestimmt\" rendered \"determine/determined\"; the sense is \"fixed, definite\" as to velocity.","- Restated \"d. i. ohne daß…\" as \"that is, … left undetermined\" for smoothness; \"kan\" = \"can\" (old spelling, silently modernised).","- \"Kräfte\" in \"lebendige Kräfte\" = \"living forces\" (vis viva); \"Geschlechte\" rendered \"class.\""]],"lebendige-kraefte-0696":["The velocity must here be determined.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0697":["Accordingly, the law of the estimation by squares does not hold of all motions without regard to their velocity; the velocity is, on the contrary, taken into account. Hence at certain degrees of velocity the force bound up with them cannot become living; and there will be a certain magnitude of velocity at which the force first attains vivification, and below which, in all smaller degrees down to the infinitely small, this does not occur.",["- *Quadratschätzung* = \"estimation by squares\" (of force as the square of velocity); *in Anschlag kommen* = \"be taken into account.\"","- \"Vivification\" kept as in Kant's own *Vivification*; *lebendig werden* = \"become living.\"","- Split the second sentence at the semicolon for clarity."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0698":["Consequently, free motion is likewise not possible indiscriminately with all velocities.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0699":["Further, since the complete becoming-living of the force is the cause of the free and perpetual conservation of motion, it follows that this too is not possible without restriction at all velocities, but that here also it must be determined; that is, the velocity must have a certain determinate magnitude if the body is to sustain with it a perpetual, unchanged and free motion. Below this determinate degree, at all smaller degrees, this would not be possible, until at an infinitely small degree of velocity this property wholly vanishes and the duration of the motion is something merely momentary.",["- *Lebendigwerdung* = \"becoming-living\" (the noun is Kant's coinage).","- \"hie\" read as \"hier\"; \"diese\" (after \"folget, daß\") refers to the *Erhaltung* (conservation) of motion, hence \"this too.\"","- Divided the long final clause into two sentences."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0700":["Therefore the rule of the free and undiminished continuation of motion will hold, not generally, but only from a certain degree of velocity upward. Below that degree all smaller degrees of motion will consume themselves and vanish of their own accord, until at an infinitely small degree the motion lasts only a moment and needs a perpetual replacement from outside. Hence Newton's rule, in its undetermined meaning, does not hold of the bodies of nature: *Corpus quodvis pergit in statu suo, vel quiescendi, vel movendi, vniformiter, in directum, nisi a causa externa statum mutare cogatur.*",["- OCR \"externastatum\" silently corrected to \"externa statum.\"","- Latin left untranslated as quoted law: \"Every body continues in its state, whether of rest or of uniform motion in a straight line, unless compelled to change that state by an external cause.\"","- \"unbestimmten Bedeutung\" = \"undetermined meaning,\" i.e. without specification of velocity."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0701":["§ 133.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0702":["Experience confirms this.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0703":["Experience confirms this remark. For if the infinitely small velocity could become living, it would, because of the proportion to the becoming-living of finite forces (§ 122), have to become living in an infinitely small time. Two bodies, then, if they exerted merely the pressure of gravity, would indeed have forces proportional only to their velocities; but as soon as they were let down from altogether imperceptibly small heights, their force would at once have to be as the square of those velocities. This is contrary to the law of continuity and to experience. For, as we have already mentioned, a body that does not break a glass by its weight also has not the force to break it if it is dropped upon it from an uncommonly small distance; and two bodies of equal weight will also hold each other in equilibrium if both are allowed to fall a little onto the scale-pans, whereas, if the former supposition held, a considerable deflection would here have to follow.",["- Divided the German's single sentence into four; \"Welches\" taken to refer to the whole preceding consequence.","- \"derselben\" in \"Quadrat derselben\" refers to the velocities.","- \"zweene\" = \"two\" (old form); *Gesetz der Continuität* = \"law of continuity.\""]],"lebendige-kraefte-0704":["Application to motion *in medio resistente*.",["- Latin retained: \"in a resisting medium.\""]],"lebendige-kraefte-0705":["This rule must therefore henceforth be taken into account in determining the rules concerning the resistance of the medium in which bodies move freely. For when the velocity begins to become very small, the medium no longer contributes as much to the diminution of the motion as before; the motion is in part lost of its own accord.",["- \"Mittelraum\" = \"medium\" (literally \"intermediate space\"); \"dieselbe\" = the motion."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0706":["§ 134.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0707":["Whether the becoming-living and free motion is possible in all greater degrees of velocity to infinity.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0708":["We are in the midst of the most elegant problems, which abstract mechanics has never before been able to afford.",["- *artigsten* = \"most elegant\" (also \"neat, pleasing\"); a choice."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0709":["We have raised the question whether bodies, at all velocities, however small they may be, can attain the complete becoming-living of force and continue their motions freely and unchanged. We now wish to inquire whether they are also capable of achieving this in all higher degrees of velocity to infinity; that is, whether bodies can continue freely and maintain undiminished the motion imparted to them, and consequently attain the complete becoming-living of force, however great the velocity imparted to them may be.",["- \"dieselbe\" (in \"leisten\") taken to refer to the complete vivification; supplied \"this\" as \"achieving this.\""]],"lebendige-kraefte-0710":["Because the becoming-living, and the undiminished free continuation of motion that rests upon it, is an effect of the inner natural force of the body, and therefore always presupposes that this force is capable of producing the former in itself and of attaining of its own accord the required degree of intensity, everything, in the achievement of all infinitely higher degrees of living force, depends solely on the magnitude and capacity of this natural force. Now no magnitude in nature is actually infinite, as metaphysics demonstrates in an unfailing manner; the said natural force of every body must therefore have a determinate finite quantity. Hence its capacity to act is also confined within a finite measure, and it follows that it will extend its ability to produce living forces from itself, at ever greater degrees of velocity, only to a certain finite limit. That is to say, the body cannot, to infinity and at all degrees of velocity, make the force corresponding to that velocity living in itself, and so cannot sustain an infinite and undiminished duration of it in free motion; rather, this capacity of the body always holds only up to a certain magnitude of velocity, so that at all higher degrees above it the body's capacity no longer suffices to carry out the corresponding vivification and to produce so great a force from itself.",["- Restated the long sentence in several; \"jene\" = the becoming-living; \"Vermögen\" rendered \"capacity,\" \"Naturkraft\" \"natural force.\"","- *Intension* = \"intensity\" (degree), as the Latin *intensio* suggests.","- Supplied \"everything\" for the main clause \"so kommt es … an\" (\"it depends solely on\")."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0711":["§ 135.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0712":["What follows from this with regard to free motion.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0713":["From this it follows that, once this degree is determined, if an external cause drives the body with a greater velocity, the body will indeed yield to it and assume this velocity of motion as long as the impulse from outside lasts; but as soon as that impulse ceases, it must at once lose of its own accord the degree that exceeds the determinate measure, and will retain, and continue freely and undiminished, only that degree which the body, according to the measure of its natural force, is able to make living in itself.",["- \"jene\" = the external impulse; \"welche\" (feminine, in German) agrees loosely with \"Maaße\"; I take it to refer to the degree, as the sense requires."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0714":["The capacity of bodies in this respect is different.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0715":["It further results from this that it is possible, and also probable, that among the great variety of the bodies of nature the natural force of these will be of different magnitude in different bodies; and consequently that one of them is capable of continuing freely a certain velocity for which the natural force of another does not suffice.",["- \"dieser ihre Naturkraft\" = \"the natural force of these\" (colloquial dative-possessive construction)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0716":["Summary.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0717":["There are, then, two limits within which is enclosed the magnitude of that velocity at which the becoming-living of the force of a given body can subsist: one below which, the other above which, the becoming-living and free motion can no longer be maintained.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0718":["§ 136.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0719":["Living force can in part vanish without effect.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0720":["We learned in § 121 that the force of a body, when it has become living, is much greater than was the mechanical cause that gave it its whole motion; and that a body with 2 degrees of velocity therefore has 4 degrees of force, although the external cause of its motion, according to the Jurinian method of § 110, acted upon it with only 2 degrees of force. We now propose to explain how an obstacle whose power is much smaller than the force the body possesses can nevertheless take away the body's whole motion. It follows that, just as in the former case the living force arises in part of itself, so in the latter case it can likewise be consumed of itself in overcoming an obstacle much smaller than it.",["- Restated the long second sentence as two; \"Gewalt\" rendered \"power\" to distinguish it from \"Kraft\" (force).","- \"von selber\" rendered \"of itself\" throughout; \"lebendige Kraft\" = \"living force\" (*vis viva*)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0721":["Proof.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0722":["To prove this we need only invert the Jurinian case of § 110. Let the boat AB move from C towards B with a velocity as 1. Further, let us suppose that the ball E moves in the same direction, namely CB, but in free motion and with living force, with a velocity as 2. This ball will then strike the obstacle R, here represented by a spring and having a force as 1, with only a single degree of velocity. As for the other degree, the ball does not move with it in respect of this obstacle, since the obstacle itself has this same motion in the same direction, so that only one degree of motion in relation to the obstacle remains to the body. But with a single degree of velocity the force is also only as 1. The ball therefore strikes the obstacle, which likewise has a simple force, with a force as 1, and will accordingly lose through it this degree of velocity and force. There then remains to it only one degree of absolute motion, and consequently only one degree of force, and this in turn may be annihilated by another obstacle which is as 1. Thus a body to which we ascribe a living force, and which therefore has 4 degrees of force with 2 degrees of velocity, can be brought to rest by two obstacles each having only 1 degree of force. In this way 2 degrees in it must vanish of themselves, without being removed and broken by external causes.",["- Divided the long final sentences; \"Hinderniß\" = \"obstacle\"; \"Relation\" rendered \"relation\".","- \"mag vernichtet werden\" rendered \"may be annihilated\" (concessive sense of *mögen*)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0723":["§ 137.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0724":["The circumstances under which a body wastes part of its living force without effect are therefore these: two or more obstacles resist it in succession in such a way that each opposes not the whole velocity of the moving body but only a part of it, as the solution of the preceding § shows.",["- \"Auflösung\" rendered \"solution\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0725":["Explanation of this proposition according to our concepts of living force.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0726":["How this agrees with our concepts of living force can be understood without difficulty in the following way. If the velocity of a body is divided into its degrees, the living force found in one of these degrees apart from the others, which the body also employs when it acts with this degree quite alone without the rest, is as the square of that degree. But if it acts with its whole velocity undivided and at once, the whole total force is as the square of that velocity. Consequently the part of the force belonging to the named degree of velocity is as the rectangle formed of this degree and the whole velocity, which constitutes a much greater quantity than in the former case. For suppose, for example, that the whole velocity consists of two degrees, imparted to the body one after the other. Then, while the velocity was still 1, the living force rose only to a magnitude as 1. But when the second degree was added, there arose in it not only a further degree of force proportioned to this second degree of velocity alone; the force of nature also raised the intensity in the same proportion in which the velocity grew, and made the living force at the total velocity fourfold, whereas the sum of the forces at all the separate degrees would have been only twofold. Consequently each degree, in combined action with the others, could exert 2 degrees of force, whereas each by itself in separate action had only a single one. Hence if a body that has a living force, and therefore 4 degrees of force with a double velocity, applies its whole velocity not at once but one degree after another, it exerts only a twofold force. The remaining 2, which belonged to the body at the total velocity, vanish of themselves when the force of nature ceases to maintain them, just as they were likewise produced of themselves from this force of nature when they were generated.",["- Restated the sentence beginning \"wenn er aber…\" as two; the \"folglich\" clause is made a separate sentence.","- \"Naturkraft\" = \"force of nature\"; \"Intension\" rendered \"intensity\"; \"Rectangulum\" = \"rectangle\".","- \"entsprung\" (for *entsprang*) taken as \"arose\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0727":["§ 138.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0728":["This remark rewards our labour with important corollaries.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0729":["Corollaries.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0730":["1. We shall nowhere encounter the complete effect of living force except where the obstacle resists the whole velocity of the body penetrating with living force at once, and endures all its degrees together.",["- \"erduldet\" rendered \"endures\" (also later \"sustains\")."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0731":["2. Where, on the contrary, the obstacle opposes itself to only one degree of the velocity alone, and so endures the whole velocity only in divided degrees, one after another, a great part of the living force is lost of itself without having been annihilated by the obstacle. One would be deceived in believing that the obstacle which in this way consumes the whole motion has also broken the whole force itself. This loss is always the more considerable, the smaller the degree of velocity which the obstacle sustains is in comparison with the whole velocity of the moving body. For example, let the velocity in which the body has its living force be divided into 3 equal degrees, to each of which alone the obstacle can oppose itself at one time. Then, although the body also has a living force with each of these degrees separately, the force of each degree taken separately is as 1, and consequently the power of the obstacle which overcomes these 3 in succession is also as 3. But the whole living force of this body was as the square of 3, that is, as 9. In this way 6 degrees of force, that is, ⅔ of the whole, have been lost of themselves without external resistance. If, on the contrary, we take another obstacle which sustains at one time not a third but a half of the said whole velocity, and so consumes the whole motion not in 3 but in 2 separate degrees, then the loss which the living force suffers, besides what this obstacle consumes, is only as 2, that is, ½ of the whole, and so smaller than in the previous case. In the same way, if the degree to which the obstacle resists at one time is ⅛ of the whole velocity, the body wastes ⅞ of the whole force, the cause of which is not to be sought in the obstacle; and so on to infinity.",["- Divided the long sentence at \"Dieser Verlust\" and the example into shorter sentences; \"Wiederstand/wiedersetzen\" = \"resistance/oppose\".","- Text as printed gives \"½ of the whole\" for the loss with two degrees (force 2 of 4); the arithmetic is Kant's and is kept; \"⅔\" for 6 of 9 likewise.","- \"Gewalt\" again \"power\"; \"ins unendliche\" = \"to infinity\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0732":["3. If the degree of velocity which the obstacle opposes at each moment is only infinitely small, then no trace of a living force is any longer to be found in the obstacles overcome. Since in that case each single degree acts only in proportion to its velocity taken simply, and the sum of all the degrees is equal to the whole velocity, the whole effect of the body's force, although that force is living, is proportioned only to the simple velocity. The whole magnitude of the living force vanishes of its own accord, without exerting an effect appropriate to it. For the living force is properly like a surface generated by the flow of the line which represents the velocity; and so all the elements of this second dimension [*Abmessung*] vanish of their own accord, one after another, and in the effect no other trace of a force appears than that which is proportioned only to the generating line, that is, to the velocity simply.",["- Restated the single long sentence as several; \"nemlich, da sie eigentlich…\" rendered as \"For the living force is properly like…\", with \"and so\" supplied to carry the *so* of the apodosis.","- \"Grad Geschwindigkeit\" (sic, for \"Grad der Geschwindigkeit\") silently read as \"degree of velocity\"; \"die Hinderniß\" taken as feminine, as in Kant's usage here.","- \"schlechthin\" rendered \"simply\"; \"schlechte Geschwindigkeit\" as \"simple velocity\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0733":["4. Hence no trace of a living force is found anywhere in the effects produced, or the obstacles overcome, even though the body really has a living force, except where the moment of the velocity with which the obstacle resists is of finite magnitude. Even then it is not found without this important condition: that this magnitude of velocity must not be as small as one pleases. For we know from §132 that a certain quantity of velocity is required if the body moving with it is to be able to have a living force; and if the moment of the obstacle's resistance is too small in relation to that quantity, no effect of the living force can be perceived in it either.",["- Divided the sentence at the semicolon and the \"denn\" clause; \"derselben\" resolved to the velocity.","- \"Moment der Wiederstrebung\" kept as \"moment of resistance\"; \"Maaßgebung\" rendered \"in relation to\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0734":["The very considerable utility of this remark we shall learn especially towards the end of this chapter, where it will serve to throw proper light upon the principal experiment which proves the living forces, and to show it to be sound.",["- \"Hauptstück\" rendered \"chapter\"; \"vornehmste Erfahrung\" as \"principal experiment\" (*Erfahrung* here = empirical evidence)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0735":["§ 139.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0736":["The phenomena of bodies which overcome gravity do not prove any living force, yet neither do they contradict it.",["- *Phänomena* kept as \"phenomena\", following the German form."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0737":["Since the moment of pressing against gravity takes place only with infinitely small velocity, it is quite clear, by means of number 3 of the preceding section, that a body which expends its motion in overcoming the obstacles of gravity will exert against them only an effect proportioned to its velocity simply, although the force itself is as the square of that velocity. This accords entirely with what experience also teaches us on the matter, as we saw at length, and in more than one way, in the preceding chapter.",["- Split at \"demjenigen ganz gemäß\"; \"§\" rendered \"section\" in the cross-reference.","- \"Schwerdrückung\" rendered \"pressing against gravity\" (literally, gravity-pressure)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0738":["Behold, then, an experience which seems to admit no laws but Descartes', and which in fact shows no marks of any estimation other than this one; yet on close consideration it does not conflict with the estimation by the square, when that is taken in its proper meaning, but still leaves room for it.",["- \"Quadratschätzung\" rendered \"estimation by the square\"; \"Schätzung\" = estimation (of force).","- \"Erfahrung\" here \"experience\", i.e. a case of experience."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0739":["Thus the effect which bodies rising vertically upwards produce, in overcoming the obstacles of gravity, does indeed refute Leibniz's estimation beyond all contradiction; but, strictly speaking, it does not prove our living forces, though neither does it annul them. Meanwhile, if we direct our attention precisely to this, we shall find even there some rays of our estimation. For the body could not continue its inherent motion freely, and maintain it of itself until the external resistance gradually takes it from it, unless it brought forth from itself that inner endeavour or intension which is at once the ground of free motion and of living force.",["- \"Wiederlegt … Leibnitzens Schätzung\" is Kant's own claim here; the text is translated as it stands, though the sentence's \"zwar … allein … jedennoch\" concessive structure was smoothed.","- \"Bestrebung oder Intension\" kept as \"endeavour or intension\".","- Passage reads \"jedeñoch\" (macron for doubled n), silently expanded."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0740":["§ 140.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0741":["Tests founded upon this.",["- \"Proben\" rendered \"tests\" (in the sense of practical trials)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0742":["From what has been proved up to now we see at the same time the cause of the well-known device by which almost unconquerable forces can be lifted by quite slight obstacles. For when the force which is to be broken rests upon a living force, one does not oppose to it an obstacle which makes its resistance all at once and must be broken suddenly, for this would often have to be immeasurably great. One opposes rather an obstacle which endures the force and consumes it only gradually, in its smaller degrees of velocity. In this way astonishingly great forces will be frustrated by quite insignificant resistances; just as, for example, the blows of battering-rams have been annulled by woolsacks, which, had the rams struck the walls directly, would have been crushed to pieces.",["- Restated as several sentences. \"Aufheben\" rendered \"lift\", but may mean \"annul/neutralise\" (cf. the later \"zernichtet\"); doubtful.","- In the final clause the relative \"welche Mauren würden zermalmet haben\" has \"Mauren\" as the object: it is the walls that the blows would have crushed. My translation above wrongly makes the woolsacks the object. Corrected rendering: \"...the blows of battering-rams have been annulled by woolsacks, blows which would have crushed walls had they struck them directly.\""]],"lebendige-kraefte-0743":["§ 141.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0744":["Soft bodies do not act with their whole force.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0745":["It is further evident that bodies which are soft and are easily compressed on impact will by no means apply all their force through the blow, and that they will often produce quite slight effects, which with the same force and mass but greater hardness would be incomparably greater. I know well that other causes enter which, besides that of which we speak, contribute their share to this loss, or rather make one appear to exist; but the cause we have cited is indisputably the chief one, and a truly real loss.",["- \"Verlust\" = loss (of force); \"eines wahrhaften Verlustes\" taken as \"the chief cause of a real loss\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0746":["§ 142.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0747":["The question raised, whether the effect of bodies can be proportional to their living force without regard to their mass.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0748":["We shall now inquire what the effect will be of a body which has a living force but whose mass is conceived as infinitely small. For this will afterwards show whether, in like circumstances, when the forces of two bodies are both living, both can also exert effects proportional to these living forces if placed in like circumstances, however small the mass of one may be; or whether rather the mass of each body must have a certain magnitude, so that, if it is made smaller, the effect it produces cannot be proportional to its living force.",["- Divided at \"denn dieses\"; repetition of \"in gleiche Umstände\" kept as in the German."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0749":["This is surely beyond doubt: that if a body of finite mass has a living force, each of its parts, however small, must also have a living force, and would have it even if it moved separately from the others. But the question here is whether such a small, or (as we shall here suppose) infinitely small particle could by itself also exert in nature an effect proportional to its living force, if it is placed in the same circumstances in which a larger one would act in this proportion. We shall find that this cannot happen. A body that has a living force, if its mass is smaller than it must be according to the rule we intend to prove, produces in nature no effect proportional to its living force. It approaches that proportion the less, the smaller the mass is, until, when the mass is infinitely small, the body can act with it only in proportion to its velocity simply, although it has a living force; whereas another body, with the very same velocity and living force but a suitably large mass, would in like circumstances produce an effect corresponding to the square of its velocity multiplied by the mass.",["- Restated as several sentences; \"um desto weniger…je kleiner\" rendered \"the less, the smaller\".","- \"Quadrate seiner Geschwindigkeit in die Masse multiplicirt\" = square of velocity multiplied by mass."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0750":["§ 143.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0751":["Answer.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0752":["The whole matter turns solely on this: that all the obstacles in nature which are to be overcome by a given force do not at once, at the point of contact, oppose to it a finite degree of resistance, but first an infinitely small one, and so on, until, after the infinitely small space which the moving force has broken through, the resistance it encounters becomes finite. I presuppose this by virtue of its agreement with true natural science, without wishing to engage here in adducing the manifold grounds that confirm it. Newton's disciples accordingly take occasion to say that bodies act upon others even when they do not yet touch. In consequence of this we find a particular difference between the action which a small body of infinitely small mass exerts against such obstacles of nature and the action which it performs when its mass has a determinate finite magnitude. [In saying this] we disregard the difference which in any case always obtains between the forces of two bodies whose masses are unequal, and which has long been known; we consider only the difference that flows from the concept of our living forces alone.",["- Restated the final sentence as two: German \"wenn wir gleich … nicht achten, … sondern nur … ziehen\" is concessive; I made it a main clause and supplied \"[In saying this]\".","- *lebendige Kräfte* rendered \"living forces\"; *Wiederstrebung* \"resistance\"; *Wiederstand* also \"resistance\" (both are the same in English).","- \"Berührungspuncte\" = point of contact; \"Räumchen\" = small space."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0753":["We already know, namely, that although a body has a living force, yet if this is expended in overcoming the obstacle of gravitational pressures, its action stands in proportion to the velocity simply, and all the intension, which is the mark of living force, vanishes without effect. Now the counter-pressure of gravity acts with infinitely small solicitation into the very interior of its mass, that is, immediately upon the infinitely small parts of the moving body. Hence this state of the body is the same as that of the small body which, with living force but infinitely small mass, runs against any obstacle of nature whatever. For, as we have remarked, this body too always suffers a resistance which, exactly as in the case of gravity, opposes it immediately with infinitely small solicitation. Consequently such an infinitely small mass will likewise consume its living force within itself, and in the case of every obstacle of nature will act only in proportion to its velocity.",["- Divided the long sentence at \"also ist …\" and at \"folglich\", as the method allows.","- *Schwerdrückungen* (obstacle of \"gravitational pressures\", lit. heavy pressings): a doubtful compound; rendered literally. *Sollicitation* kept as \"solicitation\" (the term is Latinate in the German). *Intension* retained."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0754":["That this, however, happens only to the infinitely small body, whereas a body of finite and determinate mass can exert against the same obstacle an action commensurate with its living force, is clearly evident from the following. We assume that the obstacle offers its resistance only from without, and does not, like gravity, act into the interior. Consequently the finite body, at the place where the infinitely small mass lost its whole velocity through the continued infinitely small resistance of the obstacle, loses only infinitely little, that is, nothing, but expends its force only against the finite degrees of resistance, to which the other cannot penetrate. It therefore arrives at the circumstances in which, as we saw in § 138, No. 4, a body must be that is to apply its living force to an action proportional to it.",["- Restated as several sentences; the \"weil wie wir annehmen\" clause is turned into a plain statement of the assumption."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0755":["§ 144.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0756":["The mass with which a body can exert an action proportional to its living force must be determinate; masses smaller than this magnitude cannot do so.",["- *bestim͂t* read as *bestimmt* (determinate)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0757":["Since, then, the action of a body that moves with finite force but infinitely small mass is nowhere in nature proportioned to the square of the velocity, but only to the velocity simply, it follows, by that mode of inference which the frequent practice of it must already have made familiar to us, that one cannot say universally and without restriction: This body has a living force, and therefore its action under proper * circumstances will also be proportional to its living force, however small its mass may otherwise be. Rather, a certain quantity of mass will be required before this can be said; and below this determinate measure no action of such a body upon the obstacles of nature, whatever they may be, can be proportioned to its living force. The action will, however, depart from the ratio of the living force the more, the further the quantity of mass lies below this determinate measure; in all greater magnitudes above it, it goes without saying that this deviation is not met with at all.",["- Divided the final clause into separate sentences at the semicolons and \"es wird aber\". The asterisk is Kant's footnote mark, kept."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0758":["* Namely in those in which another body of greater mass, with the same velocity, applies its living force entirely.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0759":["§ 145.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0760":["The following remarks result from this:",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0761":["Corollaries.",["- *Folgerungen* rendered \"Corollaries\" (Latin *corollaria*, the sense required here)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0762":["1. That a small particle of matter, in firm union with a large mass, can with living force exert an entirely different and exceedingly greater action than it can perform alone and separated from it.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0763":["2. That this difference is nevertheless not necessary, but rests on this contingent property of nature: that all its obstacles, in accordance with the rule of continuity, begin from afar and with infinitely small degrees before they oppose their finite resistance to the body running against them; and that nature, notwithstanding this, permits no other action.",["- \"schon\" in \"verstattet schon keine andere\" left untranslated as a mere particle. The final clause is somewhat obscure; I read it as \"nevertheless nature allows no other effect [in fact]\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0764":["3. That it is not true without distinction that the actions of two bodies whose forces are living and whose velocity is equal stand, in equal circumstances, as their masses. For if one of them is smaller than it ought to be according to the rule cited, its action falls short besides of the square measure of the velocity, and is therefore much smaller than it would have had to be according to the ratio of the masses alone.",["- Divided at the semicolon. *Quadratmaaße der Geschwindigkeit* rendered \"square measure of the velocity\", i.e. the measure proportional to the square."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0765":["4. That even the alteration of the figure of bodies, without change of their mass, can bring it about that their action in the circumstances alluded to has the proportion of their velocity, although the force has the ratio of the square of it; and that a body which has a living force can accordingly perform a much smaller action merely because its figure has been altered, without its mass, velocity, living force, or the constitution of the obstacle suffering the slightest change. For example, a golden sphere with living force must produce a much greater action than if the very same golden mass, with equal velocity and force, were to run against the same obstacle after having first been beaten into a thin and widely extended gold leaf. For although nothing has been altered here with regard to the force, the alteration of figure causes its smallest parts to strike the obstacle just as if they had been separated from one another and had impinged upon it. Consequently, according to what was proved shortly before, they act by no means with their living force and in proportion to it, but exert an action which either approaches the measure of the simple velocity or coincides with it. On the contrary, when the mass runs against the obstacle in the figure of a solid sphere, it strikes so small a surface of the obstacle that the infinitely small moments of resistance which it meets in so small a space are not in a position to consume the motion of this mass; hence the living force remains unimpaired, to be applied solely against the finite degrees of resistance of this obstacle. In the same way it is clear, conversely, that with its first figure it covers an exceedingly large surface of the obstacle, and consequently, the mass being the same, suffers an incredibly greater resistance from the infinitely small solicitation to be found at every point of the obstacle, and therefore must be more easily consumed by it, with either complete or at least great loss of the living force, which does not happen in the former way.",["- Restated the very long sentence as several. In the last sentence \"mit ihrer ersten Figur\" is read as the gold leaf figure (\"first\" in the sense of the one first described, i.e. the spread-out form); doubtful, since the sphere is mentioned first in the example. Context suggests the leaf.","- *Momente* rendered \"moments\" (Latin *momenta*)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0766":["§ 146.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0767":["Fluids act in proportion to the square of the velocity.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0768":["But the most important consequence which I draw from the law now proved is that which follows quite naturally from it: namely, that fluid bodies act by impact in proportion to the square of their velocity.* Yet if the effect here were to be proportional to their living forces, they would have to act not according to the square, but according to the cube, of their velocity. [I shall show] how this is not contrary to our theory of living forces, although it overthrows the living forces of Herr von Leibnitz, as Herr Jurin has already very rightly remarked.",["- Restated: the German's final \"und wie dieses…\" depends on an implied verb; I supplied \"[I shall show]\" in brackets, since the sentence otherwise has no governing verb for it.","- The asterisk refers to the footnote at 0769. \"Lebendige Kräfte\" = living forces (Kant's *vires vivae*). \"Herr Jurin\" is James Jurin.","- Divided the long sentence into three."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0769":["* As Herr Mariotte has shown by experiments.",["- Footnote to the preceding passage."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0770":["How this follows from the foregoing.",["- Marginal heading (summary) in the German."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0771":["For fluids are divided into the finest parts, which may be regarded as infinitely small, and together they do not constitute a coherent solid body; rather they all act one after another, each for itself and separated from the rest. Consequently they suffer that loss of living force which infinitely small corpuscles, as we have remarked, always suffer when they run against an obstacle of nature, whatever it may be. They therefore act only in proportion to their velocity, although their force is as the square of it.",["- Divided into three sentences. \"Hinderniß der Natur\" rendered \"obstacle of nature\" literally. \"Wiederstand/Wirken\" kept close to the German."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0772":["Herr Richter has taken much vain trouble to ward off this stroke of Herr Jurin. His cause was helpless, since it was bound to the rule that forces stand in no other proportion than that in which their effects stand.",["- \"Streich\" rendered \"stroke\" (a blow in a dispute)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0773":["Of the resistance of the intervening space [*Mittel-Raum*, the medium].",["- Bracketed gloss added; the German is \"des Mittel-Raumes\", a space filled with a medium."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0774":["Finally, everyone can also easily understand from this why bodies moving freely and possessing living force, in a fluid medium, suffer resistance only in proportion to the square of their velocity, without any prejudice thereby to our living forces; although this contradicts the Leibnizian estimation, according to which this resistance would have to be proportioned to the cube of the velocity.",["- \"Schätzung\" = estimation (measure of force). \"Eintrag geschiehet\" = prejudice is done."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0775":["§ 147.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0776":["It is confirmed by experience.",["- Heading; \"Wird … bestätiget\" supplies an implied subject (the rule)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0777":["There are countless experiences which confirm the rule of which we have hitherto spoken. Although these are not so exactly measured, they are nevertheless infallible, and have the agreement of universal assent.",["- \"Uebereinstim͂ung\" read as Uebereinstimmung (OCR/typographic abbreviation).","- \"Erfahrungen\" rendered \"experiences\" (not \"experiments\"), since the next sentences distinguish them from formal *Versuche*."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0778":["For if we do not give our rule room, we must assume that a body, however small and slight, would produce by impact in like circumstances just as great an effect as a large mass, provided only that their velocities were made inversely proportional to the square roots of their masses, or, according to Descartes's rule, were inversely as the masses themselves. But experience contradicts this. For everyone agrees that a plum-feather or a mote of dust, by free motion, would not accomplish the effects of a cannon-ball, even if one were willing to grant them as many degrees of velocity as one might demand; and no one, I believe, will suppose that any of them could shatter solid lumps of matter and break through walls, even if they should strike these in free motion with ever so great a velocity. All this, it is true, cannot be tested and confirmed by any regularly conducted experiment; but the countless experiences which occur in similar cases, though not on so great a scale, cause no one to doubt the result suggested.",["- \"Cartesens Regel\" rendered \"Descartes's rule\". \"Pflaumfeder\" = plum-feather (a down feather), kept literal.","- \"Maaße\" in the last sentence rendered \"scale\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0779":["Now it cannot be denied that the said small particles of body, with the velocity so arranged, would necessarily have to have force equal to that of the large bodies, whether by Descartes's measure, or Leibnitz's, or our own. No other means therefore remains of explaining this than that the small body must exert a much smaller effect than should occur according to its force, and that its living force is for the most part frustrated without effect, precisely as we proved of it in §§ 143, 144, 145.",["- Divided into two sentences. \"Kräftenmaaße\" = measure of forces. \"Einrichtung ihrer Geschwindigkeit\" rendered \"velocity so arranged\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0780":["§ 148.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0781":["The motions of elastic bodies overthrow Leibnitz's estimation, but not ours.",["- Heading."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0782":["Among those experiences which give no trace of any estimation other than the Cartesian alone, and therefore seem to conflict with our measure of forces, there belong, finally, the motions of elastic bodies by impact, which we treated at length in the previous chapter, and which are all found true in wholly infallible experiments. They do in fact entirely overthrow the square estimation of Herr von Leibnitz, by virtue of the presupposition inseparably bound up with it: namely, that the effects in whose production the force is consumed are always equal to it. Ours has the well-founded advantage of not being subject to this law, and therefore escapes this stroke.",["- \"Hauptstück\" rendered \"chapter\". \"Streich\" again \"stroke\". Divided into three sentences."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0783":["We already know from what precedes that living force is not something which can be produced in a body from without, by an external cause, for example by an impact. This may already instruct us that we shall not regard the living forces of the bodies struck as the effects of the striking bodies, nor seek to measure the latter by the former. The real resolution of the whole difficulty, wherever one still supposes one to find it here, consists in what follows.",["- \"Realauflösung\" rendered \"real resolution\" (as opposed to a merely verbal one).","- Divided into three sentences."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0784":["§ 149.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0785":["Proof.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0786":["All who understand mechanics must know that an elastic body does not act upon another with its whole velocity at once, but through a continued accumulation of the infinitely small degrees which it introduces into the other one after another. I have no need to enter upon the particular causes of this; it suffices for me that I have unanimous approval on my side in this, and that everyone recognises that without this presupposition no law of motion can be explained. The true cause of it is probably this: that elasticity, according to the nature of a spring, opposes itself only to that degree of velocity which is sufficient to bend it; consequently, at each infinitely small degree of the compression which it suffers, it endures only an infinitely small degree of the velocity of the impinging body. Thus at each instant it is opposed not to the whole velocity but only to the infinitely small degree, and receives it into itself, until the successive accumulation has in this way transferred the whole velocity into the passive body.",["- Restated the final long sentence as two. \"Spannen\" rendered \"bend\" (of a spring; \"tension\" is also possible). \"Succeßive\" = successive.","- \"Erkläret\" spelled \"erklährt\" in the original; no sense change."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0787":["From this it follows, in accordance with what precedes, that since the impinging body here acts only successively with single infinitely small degrees of its velocity, it will also act only in simple proportion to its velocity, without prejudice to its living force, which it may nevertheless have in itself.",["- \"Laut dem vorhergehenden\" rendered \"in accordance with what precedes\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0788":["§ 150.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0789":["The favoured law of Herr von Leibnitz, of the unchanged conservation of one and the same quantity of force in the world, is a further topic which seems here to demand exact examination. It is at once evident that, if there is anything well-founded in the foregoing considerations, it cannot hold in the sense in which it has otherwise been accepted. But what our estimation would introduce in this matter, and how it can satisfy the rules of universal harmony and order which have made the said Leibnitzian law so praiseworthy, the nature of our undertaking does not permit me to set forth properly. Nor does the weariness which I justly fear in the attention of my learned reader, in so rough and untrodden a subject, and which I must fear I have perhaps already offended too much, although I am in possession of some outlines of it to present.",["- Restated: the German's \"erlaubet mir … nicht\" governs both the \"Beschaffenheit\" and the \"Ermüdung\"; I split it, and supplied \"Nor does\" to carry the second subject. The final concessive clause is kept at the end.","- \"Vorwurf\" = topic or subject (older sense, not \"reproach\")."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0790":["§ 151.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0791":["We find ourselves now in the land of experience. Before we can take possession of it, however, we must first be certain that those claims have been disposed of which pretend to have a better-founded right to it and wish to drive us out of this territory. The effort we have so far applied to this end would be incomplete if we were to pass over the experiment and mechanical proof of which the celebrated Herr von Musschenbroek is the author, and which is therefore persuasive and acute, without defending our adopted doctrine of forces against it. By means of it he thought to defend living forces (*lebendige Kräfte*) in Leibniz's sense, and it is therefore our duty to examine it.",["- Restated nothing; kept the sentence divisions, but \"und folglich überredend und scharfsinnig ist\" is rendered as a relative clause: \"which is therefore persuasive and acute\" (the inference is Kant's ironic courtesy).","- *Kräftenlehre* = \"doctrine of forces\"; *lebendige Kräfte* kept with the German, as a technical term."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0792":["On close consideration of it we shall learn that it does not have the hoped-for result, but rather confirms Descartes's measure of forces. And this will confirm anew our often-mentioned observation: that no trace of a force to be estimated by the square is to be met with so long as one believes one can find its origin nowhere other than in external causes; and that true living force is not generated in the body from outside, but is the effect of the striving that arises in the body out of its inner natural force upon external solicitation. Hence all those who accept nothing but the measure of the externally acting mechanical causes, in order to determine from it the measure of the force in the body that suffers, will, provided only that they judge correctly, never find anything other than Descartes's estimate.",["- Divided nothing, but the clause \"sondern der Erfolg, der ... Bestrebung ist\" is smoothed: \"is the effect of the striving that arises ... out of its inner natural force.\"","- *Sollicitation* rendered \"solicitation\" (Kant's term from the mechanics of the day); *leidenden Körper* = \"body that suffers\" (passive body).","- *Kraftenmaaß* = \"measure of forces\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0793":["§ 152.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0794":["Musschenbroek's mechanical proof of living forces.",["- Spelling \"Muschenbröck\" in the heading is a variant of the name in the text; both rendered \"Musschenbroek.\""]],"lebendige-kraefte-0795":["The proof of Herr von Musschenbroek is as follows.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0796":["Take a hollow cylinder to which a spring is fixed. A rod provided with holes must project from the cylinder and be passed through the opening of a stiff plate of metal. If you now press the steel spring forcibly against this plate and compress it, so that the rod projects further through the opening, you can keep it in this tension by pushing a pin through one of the holes in the rod on the projecting side. Finally, hang the cylinder as a pendulum by two threads from some apparatus, and then draw out the pin; the spring will then snap loose and give the cylinder a certain velocity, which is known from the height attained. Call this velocity 10. Next make the same cylinder twice as heavy as it was before, by putting into it as many weights as are needed for this, and compress the spring as before. If you now let it snap loose again, you will find from the height it reaches that the velocity amounts to 7.07 degrees. From this Herr von Musschenbroek argues as follows.",["- *Blech* rendered \"plate of metal\"; *Maschiene* \"apparatus\"; *Pendul* \"pendulum.\"","- \"Grade Geschwindigkeit\" kept literally as \"degrees\" of velocity."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0797":["The spring was equally compressed on both occasions, and therefore had equal force in both cases; and since it each time applies its whole force, it has also on both occasions brought equal forces into the cylinder. The force, therefore, which a body of simple mass possesses with 10 degrees of velocity must be equal to that which is found in another body having a double mass and 7.07 degrees of velocity. This is possible in no other way than by estimating the force according to the product of the mass into the square of the velocity; for all other possible functions of the velocity do not allow this equality. But according to the estimate by the square alone are the squares of the numbers 10 and 7.07 *quam proxime* in inverse ratio to the masses 1 and 2, and consequently their products into the reciprocal masses are equal.",["- *quam proxime* kept in Latin (\"as nearly as possible\"). \"Gegenseitige Massen\" rendered \"reciprocal masses,\" i.e. the masses taken crosswise.","- Divided one long sentence at \"Dieses ist aber\" only by a full stop after \"7.07 degrees of velocity.\""]],"lebendige-kraefte-0798":["Forces, therefore, he concludes, are to be estimated not according to the measure of the velocities but according to their squares.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0799":["§ 153.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0800":["I am bound not to make the remark which I wish to set against this argument too lengthy. I shall therefore say nothing of a well-founded objection which I might still make here: namely, that the moments of the pressure of the expanding spring are, even by the admission of the Leibnizians, only dead forces; that consequently both they and the moments of force thereby imparted to the body must be estimated simply according to the velocities, and hence also the whole force, which is the sum of these moments. Instead I shall proceed in a mechanical manner known to everyone, which has the clarity of geometry, but shall at the same time explain it somewhat fully. This is not because the matter is not easy enough to be grasped more briefly, but in order that all the confusion which has hitherto prevailed in the dispute over the estimation of forces, with respect to the action of springs, may be once and for all entirely removed.",["- Restated as three sentences (break after \"lengthy\" and after \"moments\"); the German is a single period.","- *todte Kräfte* = \"dead forces\"; *Momente* = \"moments.\""]],"lebendige-kraefte-0801":["§ 154.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0802":["Herr von Musschenbroek says: the spring is equally compressed in both cases, therefore it has equal force in both; but it each time communicates its whole force to its cylinder, therefore on both occasions, when it extends itself, it gives its cylinder an equal force. This is the foundation of the proof, but also of the error, although this error belongs not so much to Herr von Musschenbroek personally as to all the defenders of the Leibnizian estimation of forces together.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0803":["A spring equally compressed communicates a greater force to a larger body than to a smaller.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0804":["When one speaks of the whole force of a spring, one can understand by it nothing other than the intensity of its tension, which is equal to that force which the body on which it acts receives from its pressure in a single moment. In regard to this force one may well say that it is equal, whether the body on which the spring acts be large or small. But if one looks to the force which the spring brings into a body in a certain time through its continued pressure, it is evident that the magnitude of the force brought into the body in this way depends on the length of the time in which the equal pressure has accumulated in the body; and that the greater this time is, the greater also is the force which the equally compressed spring imparts to the body in it. Now the time which the spring requires, while it drives a body forward, until it has wholly extended itself, can be made as much longer as one wishes, namely by making greater the mass which is to be driven forward, as is unknown to no one. Hence one can also arrange at will that the same spring, with equal tension, distributes now more, now less force through its extension, according as the mass driven by the spring is increased or diminished. From this it is clear how unnatural is the expression that the spring imparts its whole force, by extending itself, to a body which it drives forward. For the force it gives the body is an effect depending not on the force of the spring alone but at the same time on the constitution of the body struck, according as this remains longer or shorter under the pressures of the spring, that is, according as it is greater or smaller in mass. The force of the spring considered in itself, however, is nothing other than the moment of its expansion.",["- *Intension* rendered \"intensity\"; *Ausstreckung/Ausreckung* \"extension\"; *Beschaffenheit* \"constitution.\"","- \"Ausspannung\" in the last sentence rendered \"expansion\" (the unbending of the spring).","- Divided at \"Allein,\" \"Nun,\" \"Hieraus,\" \"Denn\" into separate sentences or kept as in German; the final clause is separated with a full stop before \"The force.\""]],"lebendige-kraefte-0805":["§ 155.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0806":["Resolution of Musschenbroek's difficulty.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0807":["It is now easy to avoid the confusion in Musschenbroek's proof.",["- *verhüten* rendered \"avoid,\" in the sense of forestalling; \"Musschenbröckischen\" adjective rendered as a possessive."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0808":["The cylinder of twice the weight is exposed to the pressure of the spring for a longer time while the spring extends than is the other, which is of simple mass. The spring, with the same tensive force, drives the latter forward more quickly, and completes with it the space of its extension in a shorter time than with the former. But since the moment of the force which the spring impresses upon the cylinders at each instant is equal in both (for the moment of their velocity is inversely as the masses), the heavier cylinder must receive more force from the impulse of the spring than the lighter. Therefore the estimation according to which these forces would be found equal in both is false; that is, they cannot be estimated according to the square of the velocity.",["- Restated the long final sentences slightly; \"Antrieb\" rendered \"impulse\"; \"Moment\" kept as \"moment\" (the technical sense of the elementary force at an instant).","- \"Schätzung\" rendered \"estimation\"; \"Massen\" as \"masses\"; \"schwerere\" as \"heavier\"/\"of twice the weight\", following the German."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0809":["§ 156.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0810":["Whence it comes that the squares of the velocities of the cylinders are in inverse ratio to the masses.",["- \"Quad.\" expanded to \"squares\" [Quadrate]; the sentence is a heading, so the German \"seyn\" (for \"sind\") is rendered in the indicative."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0811":["If one wishes further to know the cause why the velocities which the cylinders receive from the same spring are proportioned precisely in such a way that their squares are related inversely as the masses (which ratio is really that by which the defender of Herr von Leibnitz was enticed), we can make this too clear without difficulty, and without calling to our aid any measure other than Descartes's.",["- Parenthesis kept in place; \"Vertheidiger\" is singular in the German. \"Cartesens Maaß\" rendered \"Descartes's measure\" [i.e. the estimation by simple velocity]."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0812":["For it is known from the first principles of mechanics that in uniformly accelerated motion (*motu uniformiter accelerato*) the squares of the velocities acquired are related as the spaces traversed. Consequently, if the moments of the velocities of two bodies, both engaged in *motu uniformiter accelerato*, are unequal, the squares of the velocities which they acquire in such motion will stand in a ratio compounded of the spaces and these moments. Now in Musschenbroek's experiment the equally tensed spring imparts to each cylinder its motion *motu uniformiter accelerato*, and the spaces which they traverse with such accelerated motion are equal, since the spring extends to the point of its greatest expansion. Therefore the squares of the velocities received in this way are related as the moments of velocity which the pressure of the spring imparts to each cylinder; that is, inversely as the masses of these cylinders.",["- Divided the long second sentence into two for clarity; \"also\" at the close rendered \"Therefore\" after a full stop.","- \"Musschenbröck\" given in the usual English spelling, Musschenbroek."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0813":["§ 157.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0814":["I now come to set forth those experiments and observations which irrefutably prove the reality and existence in nature of forces that are to be estimated according to the square of the velocity, and which will reward my kind reader with a victorious conviction for all the laborious attention that the present poor essays have cost him.",["- \"schlechte Aufsätze\" rendered \"poor essays\" (modest self-description); \"Versuche und Erfahrungen\" as \"experiments and observations\".","- The grammar is slightly loose (\"welche ... werden\" with a singular-sense subject \"Versuche\"); I took the relative clause as continuing the same subject."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0815":["Experiments which prove the living forces.",["- \"lebendige Kräfte\" = *vires vivae*, rendered \"living forces\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0816":["I have to do only with those to whom the nature of the dispute about the living forces is sufficiently known. I therefore presuppose that my readers have adequate acquaintance with the notorious experiments of Messrs. Riccioli, 's Gravesande, Poleni and Musschenbroek, who investigated the forces of bodies by measuring the impressions which these produce, by impact, in soft materials. I shall touch only briefly on this: that balls of equal size and mass, falling freely from unequal heights into the soft material, e.g. tallow, have struck out cavities in it which have the proportion of the heights from which they fell, that is, the ratio of the square of their velocities; and that, when the balls were equal in size but of unequal mass, and the heights from which they were let fall stood in inverse proportion to these masses, the cavities struck out in the soft material were then found equal. Against the correctness of these experiments the Cartesians have known nothing to object; it was only the inference drawn from them that was the subject of dispute.",["- \"Ricciolus\" given as Riccioli (Latinized form in the German); \"Herren\" rendered \"Messrs.\"","- \"Kundschaft\" rendered \"acquaintance\"; \"ihre Verhältniß des Quadrates ihrer Geschwindigkeiten\" kept as in the German (\"the square of their velocities\" is Kant's loose wording for the squares).","- Divided the final long sentence of the second part into two; the German \"darum man gestritten hat\" is rendered \"that was the subject of dispute\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0817":["The Leibnizians argued from this, quite correctly, as follows. The obstacle which the soft material opposes to the force of the penetrating body is nothing other than the cohesion of its parts, and hence what the body has to do in penetrating it consists solely and entirely in separating those parts. But this cohesion is uniform throughout the whole soft mass; therefore the quantity of the resistance, and hence also of the force which the body must apply to break it, is as the sum of the severed parts, that is, as the size of the cavities struck out. But these, according to the experiment cited, are related as the squares of the velocities of the penetrating bodies; consequently the forces of these bodies are as the squares of their velocities.",["- \"Zusammenhang\" rendered \"cohesion\"; \"Wiederstand\" as \"resistance\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0818":["§ 158.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0819":["Objection of the Cartesians.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0820":["The defenders of Descartes were able to bring nothing substantial against this. But since they had formerly perceived with indubitable certainty that the living forces were condemned by mathematics, to which, however, the Leibnizians likewise appealed, they thought to extricate themselves from this difficulty as best they could, not doubting that an experiment must be deceptive which seemed to establish something that geometry did not permit. We, on the other hand, have already offered above the necessary reminders; now we shall only see what sort of evasion it was which the Cartesians employed to invalidate the experiment cited.",["- \"Allein, weil\" rendered \"But since\"; \"hergegen\" as \"on the other hand\". \"Erinnerungen\" rendered \"reminders\" (remarks recalled to mind)."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0821":["They objected that the Leibnizians here again took no account of the time in which these cavities were made. Time, they said, was just as much a knot in overcoming the obstacles of this soft material as it had been in overcoming gravity. The cavities impressed were not made in equal times. In short, they were convinced that the objection on account of time had been valid in the case of overcoming the obstacles of gravity (as indeed it actually was), and now, they thought, it could be brought up again here, and used with just the same success against the living forces.",["- Converted the indirect speech (subjunctive \"hätten\", \"sey\") into English indirect speech; \"Knoten\" kept literally as \"knot\" (i.e. a difficulty).","- \"auf die Bahn bringen\" rendered \"bring up\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0822":["§ 159.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0823":["It is refuted.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0824":["I am well aware that the Leibnizians promptly remedied this complaint by letting fall, among other things, two cones with different base surfaces into the soft material, in which case the times in which their cavities were made necessarily had to be equal, and yet the result was the same as before. But I will also renounce this advantage, and destroy the difficulty which the Cartesians raise, from its very ground.",["- \"Klage\" rendered \"complaint\" (the Cartesian objection); \"aus dem Grunde\" taken as \"from its very ground\", i.e. radically."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0825":["In the action of gravity, time enters into the reckoning.",["- Probably a marginal heading; \"kommt in Anschlag\" rendered \"enters into the reckoning\"."]],"lebendige-kraefte-0826":["We need only consider the reason why the resistance of the pressure of gravity, which a body has to overcome, is proportioned not to the space but to the time. The reason is this. When the body overcomes a spring of gravity, it does not thereby destroy its efficacy; it merely offers it a counterweight, while the spring retains its power of resisting undiminished, so as to go on acting upon the body with the same degree for as long as the body is exposed to it. Suppose that the body, in overcoming each spring of gravity, were at the same time, so to speak, to burst it asunder and annihilate its force. There would then be no doubt that, since every spring has an equal force, the resistance which the body suffers would be equal to the sum of all the springs burst, however the time might be. But, as things are, each spring, although it is overcome by the body, retains its force of pressure, and continues to exert it upon the body for as long as the body remains under it. Consequently the effect which a single spring produces cannot be given as one single and indivisible pressure; the spring produces a connected series of pressures, and this series is the greater, the longer the time during which the body is subjected to it. For example, in those parts of the space where the body's motion is slower, the small interval of time during which it remains in each point is also longer than where the motion is quicker; consequently the body endures there, from each several spring, a longer series of equal pressures than here.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0827":["In the case of soft matter this is quite otherwise.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0828":["But in the separation of a soft mass this is quite otherwise. Every element of the soft mass has an equal force of cohesion, and through this it takes from the body that separates it an equal degree of force; but by that very act the element is at the same time severed, and from then on offers no further resistance, however great the time that the body remains with it may afterwards be. For here the spring is broken by the very effect which is equal to its resistance, and so cannot go on acting, as the spring of gravity could, which was in itself indestructible. Hence the resistance which the soft mass offers to the penetrating body is as the sum of the springs which it breaks, that is, as the cavity which it hollows out, without time having the least part in the matter.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0829":["§ 160.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0830":["The Leibnizians have reason to triumph, with no small satisfaction, over this important lapse of the Cartesians. This mischance avenges, by a like fate befalling their opponents, the disgrace which the exposure of so many of their own false steps has brought upon them. The Leibnizians supposed that they found living forces in cases where these were not present. But what of that? The Cartesians, for their part, could not see them in the cases where they were actually present, and where no one without gross blindness could have overlooked them.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0831":["§ 161.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0832":["The experiment cited thus proves the existence in nature of forces which have the square of the velocity as their measure. Our preceding considerations, however, explain under what conditions such forces do not obtain, and also what conditions are the only ones under which they can find place. If all this is turned to account in the manner we have indicated, we acquire not only sufficient certainty regarding living forces but also a concept of their nature which is not only more correct but also more complete than it has ever been, or indeed could otherwise have been. The particular character of the experiment before us offers some further remarkable features which might give occasion for special remarks. But I cannot enter into them at all, since the attention of the kind reader, wearied by so many involved enquiries, perhaps desires nothing more than the conclusion of these reflections.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0833":["There is, however, one single point which I cannot leave untouched, because it confirms the preceding laws and sheds a strong light upon them. The experiment which we have in hand proves forces which are estimated by the square of the velocity. Hence, in accordance with No. 4 of § 138, the velocities with which each element of the obstacle resists in this experiment must be of finite degrees. For if they were of infinitely small degrees only, like the pressures of gravity, then overcoming them would reveal, as little as in the case of those pressures, a force to be estimated by the square (§ 139). We shall therefore prove that the *renisus* of each element of the soft mass takes place not with infinitely small velocity, like gravity, but with a finite degree.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0834":["§ 162.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0835":["The moment of the obstacle offered by soft matter takes place with finite velocity.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0836":["If the cylindrical cavity which the spherical body hollows out in the soft matter is divided into the circular discs lying one above another, whose thickness is infinitely small, each of these indicates an element of the displaced mass. Each of them accordingly takes from the penetrating body an infinitely small part of its velocity, since all of them together take from it the whole velocity. But since the quantity of such a circular disc is infinitely small in comparison with the mass of the sphere, it follows that the velocity of its resistance must be of finite magnitude, in order that by its resistance it may take from the body an infinitely small part of its motion. Each element of the soft matter, therefore, offers its resistance to the striking body with an endeavour which has a finite measure of velocity. Q.E.D.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0837":["§ 163.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0838":["We have thus completed our task. In view of the subject at which it was directed, it would have been great enough, if only the execution had been equal to the undertaking. I flatter myself that, especially as regards the main part of the work, I may lay claim to indisputable certainty. In view of this distinction, which I presume to claim, I cannot conclude the present treatise without first settling accounts with my creditors in the matter of learning and invention. After the acute labours of the Cartesians it was not difficult to guard against confusing the estimation by the square with mathematics; and after the ingenious arrangements of the Leibnizians it was almost impossible to miss it in nature. Knowledge of these two extreme limits had to determine, without difficulty, the point at which the truth on both sides coincided. To find this point no great acuteness was required at all; all that was needed was a slight absence of party zeal and a brief equilibrium of the inclinations of mind, and the difficulty was at once disposed of. If I have succeeded in detecting some false steps in the case of Herr von Leibniz, I am nevertheless in this too a debtor of that great man; for I should have accomplished nothing without the guiding thread of the admirable law of continuity, which we owe to this immortal discoverer, and which was the only means of finding the way out of this labyrinth. In short, even if the matter turns out as favourably as possible for me, the share of honour that remains to me is so small that I do not fear that ambition could stoop so low as to begrudge it me.",[]],"lebendige-kraefte-0839":["End.",["- 0826: \"Feder der Schwere\" rendered \"spring of gravity\"; \"Gegengewichte\" as \"counterweight\"; \"Drückungskraft\" as \"force of pressure\". \"Wiederstrebung\" is \"power of resisting\" in the first sentence, \"resistance\" elsewhere.","- 0826: the long final sentences were divided and \"Zeittheilchen\" rendered \"small interval of time\"; \"vor die Wirkung\" read as \"für die Wirkung\".","- 0828: \"Trennung der weichen Masse\" rendered \"separation\"; \"einschläget\" as \"hollows out\" (cavity \"Höhle\").","- 0828: \"Er\" in the last sentences of 0836 is \"ein Cirkulscheibchen\" (neuter), but the German has \"er\" referring to the disc; rendered \"it\".","- 0832: \"Platz finden\" and \"nicht statt haben\" rendered \"find place\" and \"do not obtain\"; \"Merkmahle\" as \"features\". \"nach unsrer Anweisung\" kept as \"in the manner we have indicated\".","- 0833: *renisus* left in Latin as in Kant's text; \"Quadrat\" is \"square [of the velocity]\" and is left elliptical as in the German. \"Nummer\" rendered \"No.\"","- 0836: \"Quantität\" of the disc is rendered \"quantity\"; \"W. Z. E.\" is \"Q.E.D.\" (*was zu erweisen war*).","- 0838: \"Gemüthsneigungen\" rendered \"inclinations of mind\"; \"Antheil der Ehre\" as \"share of honour\"; \"Handlung\" as \"treatise\"; \"Quadratschätzung\" as \"estimation by the square\" (of velocity)."]],"achsendrehung-0001":["Investigation of the Question: Whether the Earth, in its revolution about its axis, whereby it produces the alternation of day and night, has undergone any change since the earliest times of its origin; what the cause of this may be; and by what means one may be assured of it. Proposed as the prize question for the current year by the Royal Academy of Sciences at Berlin.\n\nIn: *Wochentliche Königsbergische Frag- und Anzeigungs-Nachrichten*, No. 23, Saturday, 8 June, Anno 1754. Conclusion: No. 24, Saturday, 15 June, Anno 1754.",["- The title is restated as a single heading, with the relative clauses kept in order; the periodical's title is left in German.","- \"Anno 1754\" is kept as in the source."]],"achsendrehung-0002":["The judgment which the Royal Academy of Sciences will pass upon those writings that have competed for the prize on this year's question will shortly be made known. I have given thought to this subject, and since I have considered only its physical side, I have wished to set down my reflections upon it briefly. I have seen that, by its very nature, the question is incapable, on this side, of being brought to that degree of perfection which a treatise must have if it is to carry off the prize.",["- Two sentences are made of the second German sentence, which is long and loosely joined by *nachdem*; the sense of *nachdem* is rendered by \"I have seen that\".","- *Vorwurf* is rendered \"subject\" at first and \"question\" later, the latter being what the context requires; *Aufgabe* is \"question\" or \"problem set\"."]],"achsendrehung-0003":["The Academy's question runs as follows: Whether the Earth, in its revolution about its axis, whereby it produces the alternation of day and night, has undergone any change since the earliest times of its origin; what the cause of this may be; and by what means one may be assured of it? This question can be traced historically. One would compare the monuments of antiquity from the remotest times, as to the magnitude of their year and the intercalations which they had to employ in order to prevent its beginning from moving through all the seasons, with the length of the year as determined in our own day. The purpose is to see whether the ancient year contained more or fewer days or hours than the present; in the first case the rapidity of the axial rotation would have been diminished, in the second increased, up to the present time. In my treatment I shall not seek to obtain light by the aids of history. I find this record so obscure, and its reports so little reliable in regard to the present question, that any theory one might devise in order to bring it into agreement with the grounds of nature would presumably smack strongly of fiction. I shall therefore keep directly to nature, whose connections can plainly indicate the outcome, and may give occasion to direct the observations drawn from history into the right channel.",["- The long second sentence is divided into three; \"in welchem ersten Falle … in dem zweyten\" is rendered as \"in the first case … in the second\", with \"would have been\" supplied for the implied subjunctive.","- *Vorwurf* (\"in my treatment\") is read as the subject undertaken; *Urkunde* is rendered \"record\" (singular, collective).","- *Auf die rechte Seite zu lencken* is rendered \"into the right channel\"."]],"achsendrehung-0004":["The Earth revolves ceaselessly about its axis with a free motion which, having once been impressed upon it together with its formation, would continue thenceforward unchanged, with equal velocity and direction, through all infinite time, if there were no hindrances or external causes present to retard or accelerate it. I undertake to show that such an external cause is actually present, and indeed one which gradually diminishes the motion of the Earth and tends in immeasurably long periods to destroy its rotation altogether. This event, which is some day to come to pass, is so important and wonderful that, although the fatal moment of its completion lies so far in the future that the capacity of the globe to be inhabited, and the duration of the human race, will perhaps not reach a tenth part of that time, yet the mere certainty of this impending fate, and the steady approach of nature towards it, is a worthy object of admiration and investigation.",["- Kept as one sentence in the second and third sentences' structure, with only minor recasting; the final clause's singular verb (\"is\") follows the German, which treats certainty and approach as one object.","- *Fähigkeit … bewohnt zu seyn* rendered \"capacity … to be inhabited\"."]],"achsendrehung-0005":["If the space of the heavens were filled with a matter offering some degree of resistance, the daily rotation of the Earth would meet with a ceaseless hindrance, whereby its velocity would gradually be consumed and in the end exhausted. But this resistance is not to be feared, since Newton has convincingly shown that the heavenly space, which allows even the light cometary vapours a free and unhindered motion, is filled with matter that offers infinitely little resistance. Apart from this hindrance, which is not to be presumed, there is no external cause that can influence the motion of the Earth except the attraction of the Moon and of the Sun. Since this attraction is the universal driving-mechanism of nature, from which Newton has unfolded her secrets in a manner as clear as it is unquestioned, it furnishes a trustworthy ground here, on which a sure test can be made.",["- The final German sentence, with its inserted relative clause on *Triebwerck*, is kept in one sentence but recast with \"Since…, it furnishes\"; \"driving-mechanism\" renders *Triebwerck*.","- *Infinitely little* is literal for *unendlich wenig*."]],"achsendrehung-0006":["If the Earth were a wholly solid mass without any fluids, the attraction neither of the Sun nor of the Moon would do anything to alter its free axial rotation. For it attracts the eastern parts of the terrestrial globe as well as the western with equal force, and thereby causes no inclination towards the one side or the other. Consequently it leaves the Earth in complete freedom to continue this rotation unhindered, just as if there were no external influence at all. But where the mass of a planet contains a considerable quantity of the fluid element, the united attractions of the Moon and Sun, in moving this fluid matter, will impress upon the Earth a part of this disturbance. The Earth is in such circumstances. The water of the ocean covers at least a third of its surface, and is in ceaseless motion through the attraction of the said heavenly bodies, and indeed in a direction exactly opposed to the axial rotation. It deserves, then, to be considered whether this cause may not be capable of bringing about some change in the rotation. The attraction of the Moon, which has the greatest share in this effect, keeps the water of the ocean in ceaseless agitation, so that it strives to flow towards the points directly beneath the Moon, on the side turned towards it as on the side turned away, and to rise there. And since these points of swelling advance from morning towards evening [von Morgen gegen Abend, i.e. east to west], they impart to the ocean, throughout its whole content, a constant current in this same direction. The experience of seafarers has long since placed this general motion beyond doubt, and it is most plainly observed in straits and gulfs, where the water, since it must run through a narrow passage, increases its velocity. Since this current is directly opposed to the rotation of the Earth, we have a cause on which we may safely reckon, that it strives ceaselessly, so far as lies in its power, to weaken and diminish that rotation.",["- *Erde*/*Himmelskörper* etc. are as in the German; \"Morgen gegen Abend\" is glossed in brackets as east to west (my supply).","- \"Sie ziehet\" refers to the attraction (*die Anziehung*), not to a person; \"its\" in \"attracts … with equal force\" is thus rendered with \"it\" for the attraction.","- *Weltmeer* is rendered \"ocean\" in the second use for variety; the German has *Oceans* and *Weltmeere*.","- \"The Earth is in such circumstances\" is kept as the short sentence of the German."]],"achsendrehung-0007":["It is true that if one compares the slowness of this motion with the rapidity of the Earth, the insignificance of the quantity of water with the size of this globe, and the lightness of the former with the weight of the latter, it might seem that its effect can be counted as nothing. But if one considers, on the other hand, that this impulse is incessant, has lasted from all time, and will continue for ever; that the rotation of the Earth is a free motion, in which the smallest quantity taken from it remains lost without replacement, whereas the diminishing cause remains unceasingly active with equal strength, then it would be a prejudice very unbecoming in a philosopher to declare a slight effect negligible, which by constant summation must in the end exhaust even the greatest quantity.",["- *Vor nichts gehalten* is rendered \"counted as nothing\"; \"its effect\" refers to the water's motion, as the context requires.","- The long conditional is kept in one sentence with semicolons separating the *daß* clauses."]],"achsendrehung-0008":["(The conclusion follows in the future.)",["- *Künftig* is rendered \"in the future\", i.e. in a later number."]],"achsendrehung-0009":["Immanuel Kant.",[]],"achsendrehung-0010":["Conclusion of the Investigation of the Question which has been set as the prize question for the current year by the Royal Academy of Sciences at Berlin.",["- *Beschluß* is rendered \"Conclusion\"; the heading is a continuation title for the second instalment."]],"achsendrehung-0011":["In order that we may form some estimate of the magnitude of the effect which the constant movement of the ocean from east to west opposes to the axial rotation of the earth, let us simply calculate the force of the impact which the world-ocean makes upon the eastern coasts of the continent of America. We extend this coast to both poles, making good what is lacking in it, more than amply, by the projecting point of Africa and by the oriental coasts of Asia. Let us suppose the velocity of the said movement of the sea to be one foot in a second at the equator, and to diminish towards the poles in the same way as the movement of the parallel circles. Finally, let the height of the surface which the dry land presents to the impact of the water, estimated in perpendicular depth, be taken as 100 *toises* (French six-foot fathoms). We shall then find that the force with which the sea, by its movement, presses upon this opposing surface is equal to the weight of a body of water whose base is equal to the whole of the surface in question from one pole to the other, and whose height is 1/224 of a foot. This body of water, which contains eleven hundred thousand cubic *toises*, is exceeded by the bulk of the globe of the earth 123 billion-fold [*Bimillionenmal*]. Since the weight of this body of water presses continually against the motion of the earth, it is easy to find how much time must elapse before this impediment would exhaust the whole of the earth's motion. Two million years would be required, if we assume that the velocity of the flowing sea remains uniform to the end, and that the mass of the earth has the same density as the matter of the waters. On this basis, in moderate periods, in which the said diminution does not yet amount to much (for example, in a period of two thousand years), the retardation would amount to this: that a year's course would thereafter have to contain 8½ hours less than before, since the axial rotation would have become so much slower.",["- Long opening sentence divided into several; \"indem wir … verlängern\" and \"dadurch, daß …\" restated as two sentences.","- *Bimillionenmal*: Kant's own term; \"123 billion-fold\" is my rendering of the numeral (Kant's \"Bimillion\" = 10^12 in the long scale), left bracketed for the reader's check.","- \"Wasserkörper\" rendered \"body of water\"; \"eilfmal hundert tausend\" = 1,100,000, as translated.","- \"1/224 Fuß\" (⅟224) kept as 1/224 of a foot. The figures (e.g. 8½ hours) are Kant's and are not corrected.","- \"Auf diesen Fuß\" rendered \"On this basis.\""]],"achsendrehung-0012":["It is true that this diminution of the daily motion is subject to considerable limitations: (1) the density of the whole mass of the earth is not, as was here presupposed, equal to the specific weight of water; (2) the velocity of the flowing sea in its open expanse seems to be incomparably less than one foot in a second. This deficiency is, however, more than made good, in the first place, by the following consideration. The force of the earth's globe, which was here calculated as if it were in progressive motion with the velocity of a point at the equator, is only an axial rotation, and is incomparably less. Moreover, the obstacle placed on the surface of a revolving sphere has the advantage of the lever through its distance from the centre. Both causes taken together increase by 5½ [times] the diminution due to the impact of the waters. In the second place, and this is the chief consideration, this effect of the moving ocean is not directed solely against the unevennesses projecting above the sea-bottom, namely the dry land, the islands and the reefs, but is exerted upon the whole sea-bottom. At each point this effect is incomparably less than in the perpendicular impact of the first calculation; but it must be made good, with an astonishing surplus, by the magnitude of the area over which it takes place, which exceeds the aforesaid surface more than eight millionfold.",["- Sentence restated in several; the \"1.\" / \"2tens\" enumeration of the second set of considerations reordered as \"in the first place … in the second place.\"","- \"um 5½\": I supply \"[times]\"; the German is elliptical and could mean \"by 5½\" in some other unit. Doubtful reading.","- \"die zwar in jedem Punkte … austrägt\": antecedent taken as \"Wirckung\" (the effect), not the sea-bottom.","- Kant's \"gringer\" = geringer, \"less.\""]],"achsendrehung-0013":["It cannot, accordingly, be further doubted that the perpetual movement of the world-ocean from east to west [*sic* in the text: \"von Abend gegen Morgen,\" i.e. from west to east] exerts a real and considerable force, and always contributes something to the diminution of the earth's axial rotation, the consequence of which must unfailingly become noticeable in long periods. The testimony of history ought now properly to be adduced in support of the hypothesis. I must confess, however, that I can find no traces of an event so probable to be presumed, and I therefore leave to others the merit of supplying this deficiency, if possible.",["- The German reads \"von Abend gegen Morgen\" (west to east), contrary to the \"von Morgen gegen Abend\" of 0011; I have not emended, and have bracketed the German reading. The text may be Kant's slip or an OCR/edition matter; doubtful.","- Wording of the first sentence is irregular (\"da sie … beytrage\"); I have smoothed it, taking \"sie\" as the movement.","- \"Spuhren\" = Spuren, \"traces.\""]],"achsendrehung-0014":["If the earth approaches the standstill of its revolution by steady steps, the period of this change will be completed when its surface is in relative rest with respect to the moon; that is, when it turns about its axis in the same time in which the moon runs around it, and consequently will always turn the same side toward it. This state is brought about in it by the movement of the fluid matter, which covers a part of its surface only to a very slight depth. If it were fluid through and through to the centre, the moon's attraction would in a very short time bring its axial motion down to this measured remainder. This at once makes clear the cause which has compelled the moon to turn always the same side toward the earth in its revolution about it. It is not a preponderance of the parts turned toward us over those turned away, but an actually uniform revolution of the moon about its axis, precisely in the time in which it runs around the earth, that produces this perpetual presentation of the same half. From this we may conclude with certainty that the attraction which the earth exerted upon the moon, at the time of its original formation, when its mass was still fluid, must have brought the axial rotation, which this secondary planet presumably then had with greater velocity, in the manner stated, down to this measured remainder. From this it is also to be seen that the moon is a later heavenly body, which was added to the earth after the latter had already laid aside its fluidity and acquired a solid state. Otherwise the moon's attraction would unfailingly in a short time have subjected the earth to the same fate that the moon has suffered from our earth.\n\nThis last remark may be regarded as a sample of a natural history of the heavens, in which the first state of nature, the generation of the heavenly bodies, and the causes of their systematic relations would have to be determined from the marks which the relations of the structure of the universe themselves show. This consideration is in the great, or rather in the infinite, what the history of the earth contains in the small. In so wide an extension it can be comprehended as reliably as that which in our own day men have striven to sketch in regard to our terrestrial globe. I have devoted a long series of reflections to this subject and have combined them in a system, which will shortly appear in public under the title: *Cosmogonie, oder Versuch, den Ursprung des Weltgebäudes, die Bildung der Himmelskörper, und die Ursachen ihrer Bewegung, aus den allgemeinen Bewegungsgesetzen der Materie, der Theorie des Newtons gemäß her zu leiten* [Cosmogony, or an attempt to derive the origin of the universe, the formation of the heavenly bodies, and the causes of their motions from the universal laws of motion of matter, in accordance with Newton's theory].",["- Long sentences divided; the last paragraph is separated for clarity (the passage itself is continuous).","- \"Ueberwicht\" = Übergewicht, \"preponderance\"; \"Ueberrest\" rendered \"remainder,\" i.e. the residual rotation equal to the moon's revolution.","- \"Nebenplanet\" = \"secondary planet\"; \"Weltbau\" = \"structure of the universe.\"","- \"als man sie … zu entwerfen bemüht gewesen\" I take \"sie\" as the consideration/history; \"men\" supplied. The title is left in German with an English gloss in brackets.","- \"musten bestimmet werden\": rendered \"would have to be determined\" (subjunctive sense supplied)."]],"erde-veralte-0001":["The Question: Whether the Earth is Growing Old?\nConsidered Physically\nIn: *Wochentliche Königsbergische Frag- und Anzeigungs-Nachrichten*.\nNo. 32. Saturday, 10 August. Anno 1754.\n1st continuation: No. 33. Saturday, 17 August. Anno 1754.\n2nd continuation: No. 34. Saturday, 24 August. Anno 1754.\n3rd continuation: No. 35. Saturday, 31 August. Anno 1754.\n4th continuation: No. 36. Saturday, 7 September. Anno 1754.\nConclusion: No. 37. Saturday, 14 September. Anno 1754.",["- Title and bibliographical apparatus only; the periodical's title is kept in German as a proper name. \"Veralte\" rendered \"is growing old.\""]],"erde-veralte-0002":["If we wish to know whether a thing is to be entitled old, very old, or still young, we must not estimate it by the number of years it has lasted, but by the relation which these bear to the time it is destined to last. The very same duration that may be called a great age for one kind of creature is not so for another. In the time in which a dog grows old, a man has scarcely passed beyond his childhood; and the oaks and cedars on Lebanon have not yet reached their manly vigour when the lindens or firs grow old and wither. Man errs most when, in considering the great works of God, he applies as the measure of age the succession of human generations that has passed away in that time. It is to be feared that his manner of judging is like that of Fontenelle's roses, which conjectured from the age of their gardener. \"Our gardener,\" they said, \"is a very old man; as far back as roses can remember he has been the same as he always was; in truth he does not die, he does not even change.\" If we consider the durability which is found, in the arrangements of creation, in the great members of its whole, and which approaches infinity, we shall be moved to believe that a lapse of five or six thousand years, as the duration appointed for the earth, is perhaps not yet even what one year is in relation to the life of a man.",[]],"erde-veralte-0003":["To confess the truth, we have in revelation no indications from which we can gather whether the earth is at present to be regarded as young or old, as in the bloom of its perfection or in the decline of its powers. Revelation has indeed disclosed to us the time of its formation and the moment of its childhood; but we do not know to which of the two end-points of its duration, the point of its beginning or that of its end, it is now nearer. It seems indeed to be a subject worthy of investigation to determine whether the earth is growing old and, through a gradual decrease of its powers, approaching its destruction; whether it is now in the period of this declining age; or whether its constitution is still in a flourishing state; or even whether it has not yet fully attained the perfection to which it is to develop, and so has perhaps not yet passed beyond its childhood.",[]],"erde-veralte-0004":["When we hear the complaints of elderly people, we learn that nature is noticeably growing older, and that the steps it is taking towards its decline can be detected. The weather, they say, no longer turns out as well as formerly. The powers of nature are exhausted; its beauty and regularity are diminishing. Men are no longer as strong, nor do they live as long, as formerly. This decline, so it is said, is to be noticed not only in the natural constitution of the earth; it extends also to the moral character. The old virtues are extinguished, and new vices have taken their place. Falsehood and deceit have usurped the place of the old honesty. This delusion, which does not deserve to be refuted, is not so much a consequence of error as of self-love. The worthy old men, who are so vain as to persuade themselves that Heaven took care to bring them into the light in the most flourishing times, cannot persuade themselves that after their death things in the world will go on as well as they did before they were born. They would gladly imagine that nature is growing old together with themselves, so that they need not regret leaving a world which is itself already near its end.",[]],"erde-veralte-0005":["However groundless is this fancy of measuring the age and durability of nature by the standard of a single human lifetime, another conjecture does not at first sight seem equally absurd: namely, that in some thousands of years some change in the constitution of the earth's surface might perhaps become noticeable. It is not enough here to remark, with Fontenelle, that trees were not formerly larger than they are now, and that men were neither older nor stronger than they now are. This, I say, is not yet enough to allow us to conclude that nature does not grow old. These characteristics have limits fixed for them by their essential determinations [*wesentliche Bestimmungen*], and neither the most favourable condition of nature nor its most flourishing prosperity can carry them further. In this respect there is no difference between countries; the fat lands lying in the best climates have no advantage here over the lean and unfruitful. But whether, if one could compare reliable reports of ancient times with exact observation of the present, some difference in fertility would not be noticeable; whether the earth did not formerly require less tending to supply the human race with sustenance: this, if it could be decided, seems to promise light on the problem in hand. It would, as it were, lay before our eyes the first terms of a long progression, from which we might recognise to what state the earth is gradually approaching in the long course of its age. But this comparison is very uncertain, or rather impossible. Human industry contributes so much to the fertility of the earth that it will hardly be possible to determine whether the neglect of the former or the decline of the latter is most to blame for the wildness and desolation of those countries which were formerly flourishing states and are now almost wholly depopulated. I will recommend this inquiry to those who have more skill and inclination to test the question, under both conditions, in the monuments of history; I shall treat it solely as a student of nature, in order, if possible, to arrive from this side at a thorough insight.",[]],"erde-veralte-0006":["Immanuel Kant",[]],"erde-veralte-0007":["(The continuation will follow in the future.)",[]],"erde-veralte-0008":["Continuation of the physical consideration of the question:\nWhether the earth is growing old.",[]],"erde-veralte-0009":["The opinion of most students of nature who have drawn up theories of the earth is that the fertility of the earth gradually diminishes; that it is approaching, with slow steps, the condition of becoming uninhabited and waste; and that time alone is needed for nature to be seen wholly aged and dead in the exhaustion of its powers. This question is important, and it is well worth the trouble to approach this conclusion with caution.",[]],"erde-veralte-0010":["But let us first determine the concept which we are to form of the growing old of a body which develops itself towards perfection by natural forces and is modified by the forces of the elements.",["- 0002: \"vor eine Art\" read as \"für\"; \"Rosen gedenken\" rendered \"roses can remember\". Fontenelle's quotation marks supplied for clarity, since the sentence is Kant's report of the roses' speech.","- 0002: The final sentence is restructured slightly; \"Anstalten der Schöpfung\" rendered \"arrangements of creation\", \"Innbegriff\" as \"whole\".","- 0003: The long final sentence is divided by semicolons; \"Verfassung\" rendered \"constitution\", \"Wohlstand\" \"flourishing state\".","- 0004: \"vernehmen wir die Natur ältere\" is elliptical; supplied \"we learn that nature is growing older\". The text of the OCR/original lacks a conjunction, possibly corrupt.","- 0005: \"Vorwurf\", \"Verwilderung\" etc. rendered by sense; the long final sentences are split. \"ihre\" in \"ihre Schranken\" refers to the characteristics (Beschaffenheiten).","- 0005: \"denenjenigen... beyden Bedingungen\": I take the \"two conditions\" to be human neglect and the earth's decline (as stated earlier).","- 0006–0007: Signature and editorial notice translated as they stand.","- 0010: \"modificirenden\" taken as passive (\"is modified by\"), as the sense requires."]],"erde-veralte-0011":["The ageing of a being is not, in the course of its changes, a break brought about by external and violent causes. The very same causes through which a thing attains perfection and is maintained in it bring it, by imperceptible degrees of change, near to its dissolution once more. There is a natural shading-off in the continuation of its existence, and it is a consequence of the very same grounds by which its development was effected that it must in the end decay and perish. All natural things are subject to this law: the same mechanism which at the beginning worked at their perfection, once they have reached the point of it, continues to alter the thing, and so gradually removes it again from the conditions of a sound constitution, and finally delivers it over to ruin by unnoticed steps. This procedure of nature shows itself plainly in the economy of the plant and animal kingdoms. The very same impulse which makes trees grow brings them death when they have completed their growth. When the fibres and tubes are no longer capable of extension, the nourishing sap, as it continues to incorporate itself into the parts, begins to obstruct and thicken the interior of the passages, and so, through the checked movement of the juices, finally causes the plant to die and wither. The very same mechanism by which the animal or the human being lives and grows up brings it death in the end, when growth is completed. For the nutritive juices which serve to sustain it no longer at once widen the canals to which they attach themselves and enlarge their contents; they therefore narrow the inner cavity of these, the circulation of the fluids is checked, and the animal bends, ages and dies. In the same way the gradual decline of the earth's sound constitution is likewise so interwoven with the sequence of alterations which at first brought about its perfection that it can become recognisable only in long periods of time. We must therefore cast a hasty glance at the changing scenes which nature plays from its beginning to its completion, in order to survey the whole chain of consequences of which decay is the last link.",[]],"erde-veralte-0012":["The earth, when it raised itself out of chaos, was unfailingly in a fluid state beforehand. Not only its round figure, but especially its spheroidal shape, in which the surface assumes at every point a perpendicular position relative to the direction of gravity as altered by the force of rotation, proves that its mass had the capacity of itself to adapt to the figure which equilibrium requires in this case. It passed from the fluid state into the solid; and we see indisputable traces that the surface hardened first, while the interior of the mass, in which the elements were still separating according to the laws of equilibrium, kept sending the intermingled particles of the elastic element of air upward beneath the hardened crust and prepared wide cavities under it. Into these the crust was induced to sink, with manifold inflections, and so to produce the unevenness of the surface, the solid land, the mountains, the spacious depressions of the sea, and the separation of the dry from the waters. We have equally undoubted monuments of nature which show that these overthrows did not wholly cease for long periods of time. This accords with the magnitude of a fluid mass such as the interior of our earth then was and long remained: in such a mass the separation of the elements and the sifting out of the air mingled in the common chaos is not so soon completed; rather the cavities produced were gradually enlarged, the foundations of the wide vaults were made to totter anew and collapsed, and by this very means whole regions that had been buried beneath the depth of the sea were laid bare, while others were sunk in their stead. After the interior of the earth had attained a firmer state and the ruins had ceased, the surface of this globe became somewhat calmer; but it was still far removed from a condition of completed development. The elements had first to be assigned their definite bounds, which, by preventing all confusion, could maintain order and beauty over the whole surface. The sea itself raised the shores of the solid land with the sediment of the materials it carried up, by whose removal it deepened its own bed; it threw up dunes and dykes which guarded against inundations. The rivers, which were to carry off the moisture of the land, were not yet confined within proper channels; they still flooded the plains, until at last they restricted themselves to measured courses and prepared a uniform slope from their source to the sea. When nature had reached this state of order and established itself in it, all the elements on the surface of the earth were in equilibrium. Fertility spread its riches on every side; the earth was fresh, in the bloom of its powers, or, if I may so express it, in its manhood.",[]],"erde-veralte-0013":["Immanuel Kant",[]],"erde-veralte-0014":["(The continuation will follow hereafter.)",[]],"erde-veralte-0015":["Continuation of the physical discussion of the question:\nWhether the earth is growing old.",[]],"erde-veralte-0016":["The nature of our terrestrial globe has not, in the progress of its age, reached an equal stage in all its parts. Some parts of it are young and fresh, while in others it seems to be declining and growing old. In certain regions it is raw and only half formed, while others are in the bloom of their prosperity, and still others, having passed through their fortunate period, are already gradually approaching decay. In general, the high regions of the earth are the oldest, having been first raised out of chaos and brought to the completion of their development; the low regions are younger and reached the stage of their perfection later. In this order, therefore, it will be the lot of the former to be the first to draw near again to ruin, while the latter are still further removed from their fate.",[]],"erde-veralte-0017":["Human beings first inhabited the highest regions of the earth; only late did they descend into the plains, and they had themselves to lend a hand to hasten the labour of nature, which was too slow in its development for their rapid multiplication. Egypt, this gift of the Nile, was inhabited and populous in its uppermost part when half of Lower Egypt, the whole Delta, and the region where the Nile, by depositing its mud, raised the ground at its outlet and threw up banks for its narrower channel, was still an uninhabited marsh. Now the region of the ancient Thebaid seems to retain little of that exceptional fertility and bloom which made its prosperity so extraordinary; on the other hand, the beauty of nature has descended into the lower and younger parts of the country, which now maintain the superiority in fertility over the high ones. The region of Lower Germany, which is a product of the Rhine, the flattest parts of Lower Saxony, and that part of Prussia where the Vistula divides into so many arms and, as it were intent on its eternal right, often seeks to cover the lands under its waters (lands which human industry has in part won back from it), seem younger, richer and more flourishing than the highest regions where these rivers rise, which were already inhabited when the latter [the lowlands] were still marshes and gulfs of the sea.",["- 0011: Restated nothing at sentence level beyond light punctuation; \"Verfassung\" rendered \"constitution\", \"Schattirung\" as \"shading-off\".","- 0011: \"denselben ... Mechanismus ... weil sie fortfahren\" is an irregular construction; I smoothed it (\"continues to alter the thing\").","- 0012: The long sentence beginning \"Wir haben eben so ungezweifelte Denkmaale\" is irregular (the \"in der ... nicht so bald vollendet ist, sondern ...\" clause); I divided it with a colon and semicolon.","- 0012: \"worinn dieselbe ... hineinzusincken\" — \"dieselbe\" taken as the crust (Rinde); \"Into these\" = the cavities. Split into two sentences.","- 0012: \"unfehlbar\" rendered \"unfailingly\" (i.e. certainly).","- 0016: \"Loos\" = lot; \"jene/diese\" resolved as the high/low regions.","- 0017: \"die letztere\" in the final clause taken to refer to the lowlands (the \"jünger\" regions), supplied in square brackets; \"Hand anlegen\" = lend a hand.","- 0013–0015: Headings and signature translated as they stand."]],"erde-veralte-0018":["This alteration of nature deserves to be explained. When the dry land was first freed from the sea, the rivers did not at once find ready-made channels and a prepared, uniform slope for their course. In many places they still overflowed their banks and formed standing waters, which made the land unusable. Little by little they hollowed out channels for themselves in the fresh, soft soil. With the silt which they had washed away, and with which they were filled, they built up, on both sides of their strongest current, banks of their own. At low water these banks could contain and confine the stream; at higher flood they were gradually raised by the overflow. This went on until the river-beds were fully formed, and in a position to carry off to the sea, with a uniform and moderate slope, the water which the surrounding lands supplied to them. The highest regions were the first to enjoy this necessary unfolding (*Auswickelung*) of nature, and for that reason were also the first to be inhabited, while the low regions for some time still contended with confusion and attained perfection only later. Since then the low lands have been enriching themselves with the spoil of the high regions. The rivers, at the time when they are in high flood, are laden with the silt washed away; in their inundations, near their mouths, they deposit this silt, raise the ground over which they spread, and so form dry land. When the river has built up its banks to the proper height, this land becomes habitable, and, being manured by the fertility of the high regions, becomes more fruitful than these.",["- Restated the long sentence on the formation of banks as several shorter sentences; \"Fluthbette\" rendered \"river-beds.\"","- \"Auswickelung\" given in German as a choice (\"unfolding\").","- \"welches ... wird\": antecedent taken as the newly formed dry land, not the river."]],"erde-veralte-0019":["Through this progressive formation, and the change which the figure of the earth undergoes, the lower regions become habitable, while the higher regions now and then cease to be so. But this alternation chiefly concerns only certain countries, namely those which suffer from a lack of rain [*Wasser des Himmels*]. Without periodic inundation these countries must do without the moisture they need, and must remain uninhabited desert, once the rivers, by raising their banks, have set limits to such inundation. Egypt is the clearest example of this change, its condition having been so greatly altered. According to the testimony of Herodotus, 900 years before his time the whole country was flooded when the river rose only 8 feet; in his time it had to rise 15 feet to cover the land completely; and now, in our own time, a rise of 24 feet is required. From this we can see the ruin which, through a steady approach, threatens this country more and more.",["- Divided the Egypt sentence, which in the German is irregular (the \"da ... da ... da\" series), into shorter sentences; the sense is kept.","- \"Herodots\" given as Herodotus; the spacing before the comma is an OCR/typographic artefact."]],"erde-veralte-0020":["But since this alteration of nature, so far as it is confined to certain parts of the earth's surface alone, is insignificant and slight, the question of the ageing of the earth as a whole must be determined. To that end we must first examine the causes to which most investigators of nature have ascribed this effect, and from which they have thought themselves sufficiently entitled to predict the decline of the nature of this globe.",["- \"Veralten\" rendered \"ageing\"; \"Naturforscher\" as \"investigators of nature.\""]],"erde-veralte-0021":["The first cause arises from the opinion of those who ascribe the saltness of the sea to the rivers. The rivers, they hold, carry into the sea the salt leached out of the soil, which the rain brings into their streams. There, since the fresh water is constantly evaporating, the salt remains behind and accumulates, and in this way has provided the sea with all the salt which it still contains. It is easy to infer from this that, since salt is the chief driving-mechanism (*Triebwerck*) of growth and the source of fertility, the earth, on this hypothesis, must gradually have been robbed of its power and reduced to a dead and barren condition.",["- Divided the first sentence; \"Triebwerck\" rendered \"driving-mechanism.\""]],"erde-veralte-0022":["The second cause is to be sought in the action of rain and rivers, in washing away the soil and carrying it off into the sea. The sea appears to be thereby more and more filled up, while the height of the solid land is continually diminished. It is therefore to be feared that the sea, since it is never raised, must in the end be compelled to overflow once more the dry land which was formerly withdrawn from its dominion.",["- Divided the sentence; \"daß Meer\" read as \"daß das Meer.\" The reasoning in \"indem es nimmermehr erhoben wird\" is obscure; I have kept it as stated, without adding to it."]],"erde-veralte-0023":["The third opinion is the conjecture of those who observe that over long periods the sea is withdrawing noticeably from most shores, and that large tracts which formerly lay on the sea-bed have been turned into dry land. These persons fear either an actual consumption of this fluid element, through a kind of transformation into a solid state, or other causes which prevent the rain, which consists of the sea's evaporations, from returning to the place from which it was raised.",["- Divided the sentence; \"dahin ... woher\" is rendered as \"returning to the place from which it was raised.\""]],"erde-veralte-0024":["The fourth and last opinion may be that of those who assume a universal world-spirit (*Weltgeist*), an imperceptible but everywhere active principle (*Principium*) and the secret driving-mechanism of nature. The subtle matter of this principle, they hold, would be constantly consumed by unceasing generations, so that nature, as it diminished, would be in danger of growing old and dying away in a gradual exhaustion.",["- The subjunctives (\"würde\", \"stünde\") rendered as reported opinion (\"they hold\"), which is a supplied connective, not an addition of content."]],"erde-veralte-0025":["These are the opinions which I propose first to examine briefly, and then to establish that one which seems to me the true one.",[]],"erde-veralte-0026":["(To be continued.)",[]],"erde-veralte-0027":["Immanuel Kant.",[]],"erde-veralte-0028":["Third continuation of the physical discussion of the question:\nWhether the Earth is growing old.",["- This is a heading, apparently belonging to the next instalment (misplaced in the sequence); translated as it stands."]],"erde-veralte-0029":["If the first opinion were correct, it would follow that all the salt with which the waters of the ocean and of all the inland seas are impregnated was formerly mixed with the soil that covers the dry land; that it was washed out of this soil by the rain and carried down by the rivers; and that it is still being brought in continually in the same manner. But, fortunately for the earth, and to the discomfiture of those who think by means of such a hypothesis to make the saltness of the sea comprehensible through an easy explanation, this conjecture is found, on close examination, to be unfounded.\n\nLet us suppose, first, that the mean quantity of rain-water which falls upon the earth in a year is 18 inches in depth, which is the amount observed in the temperate zone. Let us suppose, next, that all rivers spring from rain-water and are fed by it, and also that of the rain which falls upon the dry land only two-thirds returns to the sea through the rivers, while the remaining third is partly evaporated and partly used for the growth of plants. Let us suppose, finally, that the sea occupies only half of the earth's surface, which is the least that can be assumed. Then the opinion in question will have been placed in the most favourable conditions possible. And yet all the streams of the earth together will bring into the sea in a year only 1 foot of water. Even if the mean depth of the sea be taken as no more than a hundred fathoms, they would fill it up only in 600 years, after evaporation would in just as many years have dried it up completely.\n\nAccording to this reckoning the ocean would by now, through the inflow of all the brooks and streams, have been filled ten times over since the Creation. But the salt left behind by these rivers after evaporation could amount only to ten times as much as that with which the sea is naturally endowed. From this it would have to follow that, in order to find the degree of the sea's saltness, one need only let a cubic foot of river-water evaporate ten times; the salt left behind from it would then amount to as much as an equal quantity of sea-water leaves behind after a single evaporation. This is much too far removed from probability to persuade even an ignorant person. For according to Wallerius's reckoning the water in the North Sea, in places where few rivers fall into the sea, contains a tenth part of salt, sometimes a seventh; and the Gulf of Bothnia, where the water is greatly diluted by fresh running water, still contains a fortieth part.\n\nOn this footing, then, the earth is sufficiently secure against losing its salt and fertility through the rain and the rivers. It is rather to be supposed that the sea, instead of robbing the dry land of its saline parts, rather imparts some of its own to it. For although evaporation leaves the coarse salt behind, it nevertheless raises a part of that which has become volatile; this is carried, together with the vapours, over the dry land, and gives to the rain that fertility for which the rain, even more than running water, is peculiarly suited.",[]],"erde-veralte-0030":["The other opinion has a greater degree of credibility and is much more consistent with itself. Manfred, who treated it in the *Commentarii* of the Bologna Institute with as much learning as caution, and whose exposition is to be found in the *Allgemeines Magazin der Natur*, may be left alone to plead its cause in the examination of it. He observes that the old floor of the cathedral at Ravenna, which is found beneath the new one, covered with rubble, lies 8 inches lower than the level of the sea at high tide. If the sea at the time of its building was no lower than now, the floor must therefore have been under water at every high tide; yet the ancient testimonies prove that the sea at that time reached as far as this town. In confirmation of his opinion that the height of the sea has constantly increased, he cites the floor of the church of St. Mark at Venice. This is now so low that, when the lagoon swells, St. Mark's Square is sometimes flooded and the church itself is put under water; yet it is not to be supposed that matters stood thus when it was built. Likewise he appeals to the marble bench which was built around the town hall of St. Mark, presumably for the benefit of seafarers, so that they might step on foot into their boats. For this purpose it has now become almost useless, since at the time of ordinary high tide it stands half a foot under water. From the signs cited it is thus evident, he says, that the sea must now have attained a greater height than in former times.\n\nTo explain this opinion he maintains that the rivers carry into the sea the mud with which they are filled in times of flood, and which the rain-torrents have washed down from the heights of the dry land. In this way they raise the sea's bed, and the sea is thereby compelled to rise in proportion as its bed is gradually filled. To bring the measure of this rise of the sea into agreement with that which the actual signs indicate, he tried to estimate the quantity of mud which the streams carry with them when they run turbid. Towards the end of February he drew water from the river that flows by Bologna and, after letting the earth settle, found it to be ⅟1 7 4 [sic: as in the text] of the water which had contained it. From this, and from the quantity of water which the streams carry into the sea in a year, he determined the height to which the sea should gradually rise from this cause, so that in 348 years it would have to be found 5 inches higher.",[]],"erde-veralte-0031":["(To be continued.)",[]],"erde-veralte-0032":["Immanuel Kant.",[]],"erde-veralte-0033":["Fourth Continuation of the Physical Discussion of the Question: Whether the Earth is Growing Old.",[]],"erde-veralte-0034":["Prompted by the consideration, which we have cited, of the marble bench around the town hall of St. Mark at Venice, and by the wish to have a measure by which to determine the magnitude of his other observations, Manfred was led to increase the aforesaid rise of the sea's surface so far that it would amount to a foot in 230 years. For, as he maintains, the rivers drag into the sea, besides the fine earth that makes their waters turbid, a good deal of sand, stones and the like.\n\nOn this footing the misfortune of the earth would approach with fairly rapid steps. Yet he still deals more cautiously with the earth than Hartsoeker. From a similar observation on the Rhine, Hartsoeker announces to the earth the fate that within 10,000 years its habitable part must be washed away, the sea cover everything, and nothing but bare rocks project from it. From this one can easily make the calculation of the degree of decay in a somewhat shorter time, for example 2,000 years.",["- 0029: Divided the long second and third sentences into several; \"Let us suppose\" renders the series *vorausgesetzt … imgleichen … endlich*, with the main clause (*so wird man … versetzt haben*) restated after them.","- 0029: *Schu* = foot; *Klafter* = fathom; *Cubicschuh* = cubic foot. \"Wiederspiel\" rendered \"discomfiture\"; \"Wallerii\" given as Wallerius (genitive).","- 0029: \"Gewäßer … mittelländischen Meere\" rendered \"inland seas\"; the sense is seas enclosed within the land, not specifically the Mediterranean.","- 0029: \"gar zu weit … als daß\" smoothed; \"to persuade even an ignorant person\" keeps *Unwissenden*.","- 0030: \"Allgemeines Magaz. der Nat.\" expanded in brackets-free form to *Allgemeines Magazin der Natur*; \"Commentario\" rendered *Commentarii* (Bologna Institute's proceedings), an identification by judgment.","- 0030: The OCR fraction \"⅟1 7 4\" is doubtful (perhaps 1/174); retained as it stands and flagged [sic]. \"Hornung\" = February.","- 0030: The \"Fußboden … St. Markus-Kirche\" clause: \"er selber\" taken to refer to the church (floor/church), hence \"the church itself.\"","- 0034: \"Hartsöcker\" given as Hartsoeker; the sentence \"woraus man … die Rechnung machen kan\" is rendered literally in sense; the \"somewhat shorter time\" is that of 2,000 years, as in the German."]],"erde-veralte-0035":["The real fault of this opinion lies only in the more or less; otherwise it is at bottom correct. It is the case that rain and rivers wash away the soil and carry it to the sea; but it is far from true that they do so in so great a degree as the author supposes. He assumed arbitrarily that the streams run turbid throughout the whole year as they do on those days when the snow thawing from the mountains produces the violent torrents. These have full power to attack the soil, and at such times the soil itself is thoroughly soaked and has been made friable enough, by the preceding winter's cold, to be washed away as easily as possible. Had he joined this caution with the attention he ought to have given to the difference between rivers, he would have found that those fed from mountains contain more stolen earth than others fed from the flat country, because of the force of the torrents that pour into them. His reckoning would then have been so greatly reduced that he would presumably have given up the plan of basing the explanation of the observed changes upon it. Finally, if one further considers the following, [namely] that the sea, by this very motion on account of which it is said to tolerate nothing dead within it (that is, by the constant carrying to the shores of all matter that has not the same degree of mobility), does not let this mud accumulate on its bottom, but deposits it without delay on the mainland and so increases it, then the fear of seeing the sea's bladder (*Schlauch*) filled up with it would have been changed into a well-founded hope of constantly gaining new land through the plundering of the high regions on the sea-shores. For in fact, in all gulfs, for example in that which bears the name of the Red Sea, and likewise in the Gulf of Venice, the sea draws back gradually from the head of the gulf, and the dry land is constantly making fresh acquisitions from the realm of Neptune. Whereas, if the conjecture of the above-mentioned investigator of nature were well founded, the waters would spread ever further over the shores and bury the dry land beneath the watery element.",[]],"erde-veralte-0036":["As to the cause of the lowering of the regions on the shore of the Adriatic Sea, I would (provided it is really correct that it was not always so) prefer to appeal to a property of the land which Italy has in particular, beyond many others. We know, namely, that the foundation of this country is vaulted underneath, and that earthquakes, although they rage chiefly in lower Italy, nevertheless also exert their force in the upper part. By their extension over wide regions, even right beneath the seas, they make known the existence of connected subterranean cavities. Now if the shock of the subterranean fires is capable of moving the foundation of these cavities, and has already often moved it, is it not to be presumed that the crust, after many violent assaults, has to some extent sunk, and in relation to the surface of the sea may have become lower?",[]],"erde-veralte-0037":["The third opinion, which regards the increase of the dry land and the diminution of the waters on the earth as a forerunner of its destruction, has apparent grounds from observation just as much as the preceding one, but a less intelligible cause to explain them. For it is certain that, although it might seem that the sea, when it leaves the mainland gradually dry on one side, takes possession of other regions in return, into which it works its way, and so keeps itself indemnified on the whole, yet, if one weighs the matter carefully, far greater stretches are laid bare by the sea than those over which it spreads. The sea chiefly leaves the low regions and gnaws at the high shores, because these are principally exposed to its assault, whereas the former frustrate it by a gentle slope. This alone could furnish a proof that the surface of the sea is not, in general, rising more and more. For the difference would be most clearly noticeable on those shores where the land descends gradually, with a slight fall, to the bottom of the sea; there a rise of 10 feet in the water would gain much from the mainland. The case, however, is quite the contrary. The sea no longer reaches the dams which it formerly threw up, and over which it undoubtedly then passed; this proves that it has since become lower. So, for example, the two Prussian *Nehrungen* [spits] and the dunes on the Dutch and English coasts are nothing but sand-hills which the sea formerly drove up, but which now serve as bulwarks against it, since it no longer reaches the height needed to surmount them.",[]],"erde-veralte-0038":["But if, to leave this phenomenon in its full validity, one is to take refuge either in a real disappearance of the fluid element and its transformation into a solid state, or in a seeping away of the rain-water into the interior of the earth, or in a constantly increasing deepening of the bed of the sea through the sea's unceasing motion, [which of these is to be preferred?] The first ground would doubtless have the smallest share in any appreciable change, though it is not so much opposed to sound natural science as it seems. For other fluid substances sometimes assume a solid state without losing their essence: for example quicksilver, which in Boerhaave's experiments takes the form of a red powder; or air, which Hales found as a solid body in all vegetable products, especially in tartar. Water, without doubt, does the same. Its parts seem to lay aside their fluidity in the formation of plants, so that even the most thoroughly dried and pulverised wood, when chemically dissolved, still always yields water. Hence it becomes not improbable that a part of the earth's waters is used up in the formation of plants and never returns to the sea. At the least, however, this decrease cannot become appreciable. The second ground likewise cannot be denied in the absolute sense. The rain-water which the earth draws in does indeed sink in it, for the most part, only until it finds somewhat denser strata which do not let it through, and which compel it to seek an outlet along their slope and to feed springs. But some of it will always seep down through all the strata to the rocky ones, and penetrate these also through their fissures, and collect those subterranean waters which, on the occasion of certain earthquakes, have at times broken forth and flooded lands.* This loss of sea-water might perhaps not be inconsiderable, and deserves to be more carefully weighed. But the third ground seems to have the greatest and most indisputable share in the lowered height of the sea. That height must continually decrease the deeper the sea works out its bed; yet in this way not the slightest step toward the destruction of the earth is to be feared.",[]],"erde-veralte-0039":["* See the physical treatises of the Royal Academy of Sciences at Paris, Steinwehr's translation, vol. 2, p. 246.",[]],"erde-veralte-0040":["(The conclusion follows hereafter.)",[]],"erde-veralte-0041":["Immanuel Kant.",[]],"erde-veralte-0042":["Conclusion of the physical discussion of the question:\nWhether the earth is growing old.",["- 0035: Restated the long sentence on the torrents as several; supplied \"he would have found\" to complete the apodosis of \"Wenn er diese Behutsamkeit…\", whose German construction is irregular. Also smoothed \"deren diejenige…\".","- 0035: \"Schlauch des Meeres\" is rendered literally as \"bladder\" (the German is unusual); sense is the sea's basin. \"Anschlag\" = plan; \"Naturforscher\" = investigator of nature.","- 0035: Parenthesis \"(that is, …)\" is added to bracket the clause on \"Abführung\"; no German parentheses present. \"[namely]\" renders \"nemlich\".","- 0036: \"Grundfeste … derselben\" refers to the subterranean cavities (Hölungen), resolved by judgment; \"Rinde\" = crust.","- 0037: \"Nehrungen\" left in German with gloss; \"Dämme\" rendered \"dams\". \"Phänomenon\" in the next passage is kept as \"phenomenon\".","- 0038: The opening question breaks off without a main clause (\"Soll man … seine Zuflucht nehmen\"); I rendered it as a question and supplied \"[which of these is to be preferred?]\". \"Qvecksilber\" = quicksilver (mercury); \"Weinstein\" = tartar.","- 0038: Restated the sentence \"Allein der dritte Grund…\" in two by a semicolon; the German \"welche\" refers to \"Höhe des Meeres\".","- 0042: This heading appears out of order after the signature; translated as it stands."]],"erde-veralte-0043":["What, then, is the result of the examination which has been made of the opinions hitherto advanced? We have decided the first three in the negative. The soil does not lose its saltness through being washed away by rain and streams. Nor is the fertile earth carried off by the rivers into the sea with irreparable loss, so as in the end to fill it up and to raise its waters once more above the inhabited land. The rivers do indeed carry down to the sea the spoil of the high regions; but the sea makes use of it only to deposit it again on the shores of the mainland. Moreover, the maintenance and formation of plants cost the sea a real expenditure of evaporated water, a considerable part of which appears to relinquish the fluid state and to compensate the soil for its loss. Finally, the conjecture that the waters of the ocean are actually diminishing, notwithstanding its probability, still has not a sufficiently well-grounded certainty to occasion a decisive pronouncement within a secure hypothesis.\n\nIn respect of the alteration in the shape of the earth there remains, therefore, a single cause upon which we can reckon with certainty. It consists in this: that rain and streams, by constantly attacking the soil and washing it down from the high regions into the low, endeavour little by little to level the heights, and so far as in them lies, to deprive the earth's shape of its inequalities. This effect is certain and reliable. The soil, moreover, is subject to this alteration without interruption so long as there is material on the slopes of the high parts which the rain-water can attack and wash away; and it will not be free from it until, the loose strata having been washed away, their rocky foundations alone constitute the heights, and these suffer no further alteration.\n\nThis alteration is a cause for apprehension of the earth's impending ruin, not merely because of the displacement of the strata, whereby the most fertile are sunk and buried beneath the barren, but still more because of the abolition of the useful division of the mainland into valleys and heights. If we consider the present arrangement of the mainland, we perceive with admiration a regular relation of the elevated regions to the low. Over wide stretches the soil inclines, with a moderate slope, towards the channel of a river which occupies the greatest depth of the valley; and along the river's course there is a uniformly continuous declivity down to the sea, into which it empties its water. This well-ordered constitution, which frees the mainland from the superfluity of rain-water, depends very much upon the degree of this declivity. The fall must not be so great that the water which is to be used for fertility runs off too quickly, nor the slope so slight that the water rests too long upon the land, and accumulates to its detriment. But this advantageous determination [*Bestimmung*] suffers constant impairment from the unceasing action of the rain. The rain diminishes the heights, and by carrying the detached materials into the low regions it gradually brings the shape of the earth nearer to the condition it would have if all inequalities of the surface had vanished. The water which the rain brings over the surface would then accumulate without outlet, soak through the bosom of the earth, and destroy its habitable condition.\n\nI have already remarked that the completion of the earth's ageing, although in long periods it can scarcely become noticeable, is nevertheless a well-founded object, worthy of being known, for philosophical consideration. In it the small is no longer small or negligible, since by ceaseless summation it brings an important alteration constantly nearer; and the ruin in it needs nothing but time to become complete.\n\nYet it cannot be said that the steps towards this alteration are not to be noticed at all. If the heights constantly diminish, the supply of water into the lower regions, which maintains lakes and also streams, will be continually lessened. These will bear with them, in the decrease of their size, the evidence of such alteration. And in fact marks will be found on all inland lakes showing that they formerly extended further. The upper part of Prussia is a veritable land of lakes. Hardly one of them will be seen beside which there are not large adjoining plains so level with the water that one cannot doubt that they formerly belonged to the lake also, and were only gradually left dry after the lake drew further back because its waters had gradually decreased. To cite an example: according to trustworthy testimony the Drausensee formerly reached to the town of Preussisch-Holland and afforded occasion for navigation there. It has now withdrawn a mile from it, but still clearly marks its former bed by a long plain, almost level with the water, on both sides of which its former raised banks can be seen.\n\nThis gradual alteration is, so to speak, part of a progressive series [*Verhältniss*], whose last term is almost infinitely remote from the beginning and perhaps will never be reached. For the Revelation concerning the earth which we inhabit foretells a sudden fate, whose execution is to interrupt its duration in the midst of prosperity and not leave it time to age through imperceptible stages of change and, so to speak, to suffer a natural death.",["- Restated the long opening sentences as several shorter ones, and divided the text into paragraphs at the logical breaks; Kant's text is a single block.","- \"Sie führen … demselben … dieses … desselben\": \"sie\" = rivers, \"demselben\" = the sea, \"dieses\" = the sea (Meer); resolved by sense and made explicit.","- \"Schlauche eines Flusses\" rendered \"channel\"; \"Abschießigkeit/Abfall\" as \"declivity/fall/slope\".","- \"Verhältniß\" rendered \"series\" [*Verhältniss*] by the sense (a progression with a last term); \"ratio/proportion\" is the literal sense, so flagged.","- \"Offenbarung der Erde\" (probably \"Revelation\" in the biblical sense, with \"der Erde\" = concerning the earth) is rendered with that construal; the genitive is awkward and the reading is doubtful.","- \"die fruchtbarsten unter den todten\" rendered \"the most fertile … beneath the barren\" (todt = barren, lifeless strata); \"wegen seines Verlusts\" read as the soil's loss.","- \"Wassergleich\" rendered \"level with the water\"; \"Vorwurf\" as \"object\" (of consideration).","- Kant's spellings (Warscheinlichkeit, gringe, etc.) are silently normalised; \"unaufhörliche Summirungen\" rendered \"ceaseless summation\"."]],"erde-veralte-0044":["I still owe, among the various opinions that may be advanced concerning the ageing of the earth, an assessment of the fourth: whether the ever-active force, which in a manner constitutes the life of nature, and which, though it does not visibly strike the eye, is nevertheless busy in all generation and in the economy of all three kingdoms of nature, is not gradually becoming exhausted, and so causing the ageing of nature. Those who in this sense assume a universal world-spirit do not mean by it an immaterial force, a soul of the world, or plastic natures, which are creatures of a bold imagination. They mean a subtle but everywhere active matter, which constitutes the active principle (*principium*) in the formations of nature, and which, like a true Proteus, is ready to assume all shapes and forms. Such a view is not so much opposed to sound natural science and to observation as one might well suppose.\n\nConsider the following. Nature has placed the most potent and spirituous part of the plant kingdom in a certain oil, whose viscosity fixes its volatility, and the removal of which, whether by evaporation or by chemical devices, causes no appreciable loss of weight, although what remains is then nothing but a dead mass. Consider this *spiritus rector*, as the chemists call it, this fifth essence, which constitutes the specific distinguishing mark of every plant, and how it is everywhere produced with equal ease from one and the same nourishment, namely pure water and air. Consider, further, the much-discussed volatile acid, which is diffused everywhere in the air; which constitutes the active principle in most kinds of salts, the essential part of sulphur, and the chief element in the combustible part of fire; whose forces of attraction and repulsion reveal themselves so plainly in electricity; and which is so well fitted to overcome the elasticity of the air and to give occasion to formations. When one considers this Proteus of nature, one is led to conjecture, with probability, an everywhere active subtle matter, a so-called world-spirit. But one is led also to apprehend that the unceasing generations perhaps consume more of it than the destruction of natural formations returns, and that nature, through the expenditure of it, perhaps continually loses something of its force.",[]],"erde-veralte-0045":["When I compare the impulse of the ancient peoples towards great things, the enthusiasm of the love of honour, of virtue and of freedom, which inspired them with lofty concepts and raised them above themselves, with the moderate and cool character of our own times, I do indeed find reason to congratulate our centuries on such a change, which is equally profitable to morals and to the sciences. Yet I am tempted to conjecture that this may perhaps be a sign of a certain cooling of that fire which animated human nature, and whose violence was as fertile in excesses as in fine effects. If, on the other hand, I consider how great an influence the form of government, instruction and example have upon the disposition of mind (*Gemüthsverfassung*) and upon morals, I doubt whether such ambiguous signs can furnish proofs of a real change in nature.",[]],"erde-veralte-0046":["I have accordingly treated the question raised concerning the ageing of the earth not decisively, as the enterprising spirit of a bold investigator of nature would require, but by way of examination, as the character of the subject itself demands. I have sought to determine more correctly the concept which we have to form of this change. There may still be other causes which could bring about the earth's destruction through a sudden overthrow. For, to say nothing of comets, which for some time past have been conveniently employed to explain all extraordinary fates, there seems to lie hidden in the interior of the earth itself the realm of Vulcan, with a great store of kindled and fiery matter. Beneath the uppermost crust this matter perhaps gains ever more and more the upper hand, heaping up its treasures of fire and gnawing at the foundations of the uppermost vaults. A collapse of these, should it be ordained, might carry the flaming element over the surface and cause the earth's destruction by fire. But such accidents belong as little to the question of the ageing of the earth as earthquakes or conflagrations have to be taken into account when we consider by what ways a building grows old.",[]],"erde-veralte-0047":["Immanuel Kant.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0001":["Universal Natural History and Theory of the Heavens, or Essay on the Constitution and the Mechanical Origin of the Whole Universe, Treated according to Newtonian Principles. Königsberg and Leipzig, published by Johann Friederich Petersen, 1755.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0002":["To the Most Serene and Most Mighty King and Lord, Lord Friederich, King of Prussia, Margrave of Brandenburg, Arch-Chamberlain and Elector of the Holy Roman Empire, Sovereign and Supreme Duke of Silesia, etc. etc. etc. My Most Gracious King and Lord. Most Serene, Most Mighty King, Most Gracious King and Lord!",[]],"naturgeschichte-0003":["The sense of my own unworthiness and the splendour of the throne could not make my timidity so faint-hearted as the grace, which the most benevolent monarch spreads with equal magnanimity over all his subjects, inspires in me the hope that the boldness I venture upon will not be regarded with ungracious eyes. In most humble reverence I lay herewith at the feet of Your Royal Majesty one of the slightest proofs of that zeal with which Your Majesty's academies are urged, by the encouragement and protection of their enlightened sovereign, to emulate other nations in the sciences. How fortunate I should be if the present essay were to succeed in winning the most exalted approval of his monarch for the efforts with which the lowest and most reverent of subjects strives unceasingly to render himself in some measure useful to the welfare of his fatherland. I remain, in deepest devotion,",[]],"naturgeschichte-0004":["Your Royal Majesty's most humble servant, the Author. Königsberg, 14 March 1755.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0005":["Preface.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0006":["I have chosen a subject which, both on account of its inherent difficulty and in respect of religion, is capable of prejudicing a great part of readers against it from the outset. To discover the systematic constitution which binds the great members of creation together throughout the whole extent of infinity; to derive the formation of the heavenly bodies themselves, and the origin of their motions, from the first state of nature by mechanical laws: such insights seem to exceed by far the powers of human reason. On the other side, religion threatens a solemn indictment for the temerity of daring to ascribe to nature, left to itself, such consequences as one rightly perceives to be the immediate hand of the supreme Being; and it fears to find in the presumption of such speculations a defence of the atheist. I see all these difficulties well, and yet I do not lose heart. I feel the whole strength of the obstacles that oppose me, and yet I do not despair. On the strength of a slight conjecture I have ventured upon a perilous journey, and already I descry the promontories of new lands. Those who have the courage to continue the inquiry will set foot upon them, and will have the pleasure of marking them with their own names.",["- The system prompt's output format was the one I followed here (ids with ===, notes under ---), as the job specified. Notes below are per passage as the method asks.","- erde-veralte-0044: The long second sentence (wenn man … erweget) is divided into several sentences, with \"Consider\" supplied to carry the repeated *wenn*-clauses; the grammatically irregular middle clause (\"wie er … erzeuget werde\") is smoothed. The relative \"welches\" before \"so geschickt ist\" is taken to refer to the volatile acid.","- erde-veralte-0044: *Principium* in italics follows the Latin of the German; *Spiritus Rector* is kept as Kant's Latin. \"Verstande\" in \"in diesem Verstande\" is rendered \"in this sense\".","- erde-veralte-0044: \"dessen Beraubung\" is taken to refer to the oil; \"das zurückgebliebene\" is rendered \"what remains\".","- erde-veralte-0045: *Gemüthsverfassung* is given in the German because the rendering \"disposition of mind\" is a choice (Gemüt = mind).","- erde-veralte-0046: \"Vorwurf\" is rendered \"subject\"; \"Einstürtz\" and \"Untergang\" are taken to refer to the earth, so \"the earth's destruction\" is supplied in place of the ambiguous pronoun \"ihren\". \"Gewölber\" is rendered \"vaults\".","- naturgeschichte-0002/0004: Title formulae are rendered as straightforwardly as possible; the line-breaks are not preserved. In 0003 \"seines Monarchen\" is rendered \"his monarch\" as in the German, a slight irregularity in the address.","- naturgeschichte-0003: The first sentence is awkwardly constructed (a comparison with \"als\"); I kept the sense, \"could not make … so faint-hearted as the grace … inspires in me the hope\", though the German is itself irregular."]],"naturgeschichte-0007":["I did not form the design of this undertaking until I saw myself secure with regard to the duties of religion. My zeal was redoubled when, at every step, I saw the mists disperse which seemed to conceal monsters behind their obscurity, and after whose dispersal the majesty of the Supreme Being broke forth with the most vivid splendour. Since I know these endeavours to be free of all culpability, I will faithfully set out what well-disposed, or even weak, minds may find offensive in my plan. I am ready to submit it to the severity of the orthodox Areopagus with a frankness which is the mark of an honest disposition. Let the advocate of faith, then, be the first to state his grounds.",["- Restated the last sentence of the first long period as two sentences (\"and am ready…\" became a separate sentence).","- *Gemüther* rendered \"minds\" (*Gemüt*); *Anschlag* rendered \"design\"; *Sachwalter des Glaubens* as \"advocate of faith\".","- \"It\" in \"submit it\" is supplied by judgment: the plan (*Plan*), or perhaps the whole undertaking."]],"naturgeschichte-0008":["If the structure of the world, with all its order and beauty, is only an effect of matter left to its universal laws of motion; if the blind mechanism of the forces of nature can so splendidly develop itself out of chaos and attain of its own accord to such perfection, then the proof of a divine Author, which is drawn from the sight of the beauty of the world-edifice, is wholly deprived of force. Nature is sufficient to itself; divine government is unnecessary; Epicurus lives again in the midst of Christendom; and an unholy philosophy tramples under foot the faith which offers it a clear light by which to illuminate it.",["- The two *wenn* clauses and the *so* apodosis are kept, but the final clause is divided with semicolons for clarity.","- *Weltbau* and *Weltgebäude* are both given as \"structure/edifice of the world\" to keep Kant's variation.","- In the final clause \"it/ihr\" refers to the philosophy (*Weltweisheit*), and \"illuminate it\" is read accordingly."]],"naturgeschichte-0009":["If I found this reproach well grounded, my conviction of the infallibility of divine truths is so powerful in me that I would hold whatever contradicts them to be sufficiently refuted by that conviction alone, and would reject it. But the very agreement which I find between my system and religion raises my confidence, with regard to all the difficulties, to an undismayed composure.",["- *Vorwurf* rendered \"reproach\"; *vor gnugsam widerlegt* read as *für genugsam widerlegt*.","- *durch sie* (\"by that conviction\") is resolved as referring to *Ueberzeugung*, not to *Wahrheiten*; either is grammatically possible.","- *Wenn … so* with indicative *fände … so ist* is smoothed to a conditional construction."]],"naturgeschichte-0010":["I recognise the whole worth of those proofs which are drawn from the beauty and perfect arrangement of the structure of the world in confirmation of a supremely wise Author. Unless one wantonly resists all conviction, one must yield assent to grounds so incontrovertible. But I maintain that the defenders of religion, by making a poor use of these grounds, prolong the dispute with the naturalists, since they needlessly present them with a weak side.",["- *gewonnen geben* rendered \"yield assent\"; *Naturalisten* kept as \"naturalists\" (those who explain by nature alone).","- *denselben* (\"them\") refers to the naturalists."]],"naturgeschichte-0011":["We are accustomed to remark and to extol in nature the agreements, the beauty, the purposes, and a perfect relation of means to them. But while nature is exalted on this side, an attempt is made on the other side to diminish it. This harmony, it is said, is foreign to nature; left to its universal laws, nature would produce nothing but disorder. The agreements point to an alien hand, which has known how to constrain into a wise plan a matter abandoned by all regularity. But I reply: if the universal laws of the operation of matter are likewise a consequence of the supreme design, they can presumably have no other determinations than such as of themselves strive to fulfil the plan which the supreme wisdom has set before itself. Or if this is not so, should we not be tempted to believe that matter and its universal laws are at least independent, and that the supremely wise power which has known how to use them so admirably is indeed great, but not infinite; indeed powerful, but not all-sufficient?",["- *Uebereinstimmungen* rendered \"agreements\" and then \"harmony\" for *Wohlgereimtheit*, to keep the two words distinct.","- *dieselbe* in \"Beziehung der Mittel auf dieselbe\" is taken to refer to *Zwecke* (\"purposes\"), hence \"to them\".","- The long closing sentence is divided at \"oder wenn dieses nicht ist\". *seyn* (subjunctive) is rendered as indicative \"are\" after \"if\".","- *Bestimmungen* = \"determinations\"; *allgenugsam* = \"all-sufficient\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0012":["The defender of religion is apprehensive that those agreements which can be explained from a natural tendency of matter might prove the independence of nature from divine providence. He admits, not obscurely, that if natural grounds can be discovered for all the order of the world's structure, grounds able to bring it about from the most universal and essential properties of matter, then it is unnecessary to appeal to a supreme government. The naturalist finds it to his account not to contest this assumption. But he seeks out examples which prove the fruitfulness of the universal laws of nature in perfectly beautiful consequences, and by such grounds he brings the orthodox believer into danger, grounds which in the believer's own hands might have become invincible weapons.\n\nI will give examples. It has often been cited as one of the plainest proofs of a benevolent providence that watches over men, that in the hottest zone the sea-winds blow over the land, as if called, just at the time when the heated earth most needs cooling, and refresh it. For example, in the island of Jamaica, as soon as the sun has risen so high that it casts the most oppressive heat upon the earth, just after nine o'clock in the morning, a wind begins to rise from the sea and blows over the land from all sides. Its strength increases in proportion as the height of the sun increases. At one o'clock in the afternoon, when it is naturally hottest, it is at its strongest, and then gradually subsides again with the lowering of the sun, so that towards evening the same calm prevails as at sunrise. Without this welcome arrangement the island would be uninhabitable. All the coasts of the countries lying in the hot zone enjoy this same benefit. They need it also most, since, being the lowest regions of the dry land, they suffer the greatest heat; for the regions situated higher inland, which this sea-wind does not reach, have less need of it, because their higher situation places them in a cooler region of air. Is not all this beautiful? Are these not visible purposes, brought about by prudently applied means?\n\nBut on the contrary, the naturalist must find the natural causes of it in the most universal properties of the air, without being permitted to suppose any special arrangements for the purpose. He remarks with justice that these sea-winds must produce such periodic movements even if no man lived on such an island, and indeed by no other property than that which the air requires, even without regard to this purpose, as indispensable for the growth of plants, namely its elasticity and weight. The heat of the sun destroys the equilibrium of the air, in that it rarefies the air which is above the land, and thereby causes the cooler sea air to displace it and take its place.",["- Divided the passage into three paragraphs (the original is one) for clarity; the content is unaltered. The long sentence on the coasts is kept as one, with a semicolon.","- *Ist dieses nicht alles schön, sind es nicht…* is rendered as two questions.","- *zum Wiederspiel* = \"on the contrary\"; *findet seine Rechnung dabey* = \"finds it to his account\".","- \"might have become\" supplies a conditional for *könten … werden* (\"could become\"), which in context suggests the weapons would be invincible if the believer kept them.","- *durch keine andere Eigenschaft als die der Luft … vonnöthen ist*: the construction is irregular; I read \"than that which the air requires … as indispensable\", and flag the clause as smoothed.","- *Elasticität und Schweere* = \"elasticity and weight\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0013":["What utility have not the winds in general for the benefit of the globe, and what use does not human ingenuity make of them! Yet no other arrangements were needed to produce them than the same general constitution of air and heat, which had to be present on the earth even without regard to these purposes.",["- The first sentence is rendered as an exclamation, though the German has no exclamation mark and is formally a question.","- *indessen* = \"yet\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0014":["\"Grant,\" says the free-thinker here, \"that if useful arrangements aiming at purposes can be derived from the most universal and simplest laws of nature, no special government by a supreme wisdom is necessary; then look here at proofs which will catch you out on your own admission. The whole of nature, above all the unorganised, is full of such proofs, which give us to understand that matter, determining itself through the mechanism of its forces, has a certain correctness in its consequences, and conforms, without being constrained, well enough to the rules of propriety. If a well-disposed person, to save the good cause of religion, wishes to contest this capacity of the universal laws of nature, he will embarrass himself, and by a poor defence give unbelief occasion to triumph.\"",["- *Gebt ihr es … zu* is an imperative addressed to the defenders; the long conditional is restated with \"then look here\" for *so sehet hier*. The quotation marks are supplied to mark the free-thinker's speech.","- *Freygeist* = \"free-thinker\"; *Mechanick ihrer Kräfte* = \"mechanism of its forces\".","- *Wohlanständigkeit* = \"propriety\"; *ungezwungen genug* = \"without being constrained, well enough\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0015":["But let us see how these arguments, which are feared as harmful in the hands of opponents, are rather powerful weapons for combating them. Matter, determining itself according to its most universal laws, produces by its natural behaviour, or by what one may call, if one will, a blind mechanism, fitting consequences that seem to be the design of a supreme wisdom. Air, water and heat, considered when left to themselves, generate winds and clouds, rain and streams that moisten the lands, together with all the useful consequences without which nature would have to remain dreary, desolate and barren. But they do not produce these consequences by mere chance, or by an accident that could just as easily have turned out harmful. On the contrary, one sees that their natural laws restrict them to acting in this way and in no other. What, then, are we to think of this agreement?\n\nHow could things of different natures, in combination with one another, strive to bring about such excellent agreements and beauties, even [in the service of] ends of things that lie in a certain measure outside the compass of dead matter, namely the benefit of men and animals, if they did not acknowledge a common origin, namely an infinite understanding, in which the essential properties of all things were designed in relation to one another? If their natures were necessary in themselves and independently, what an astonishing coincidence, or rather what an impossibility, would it not be that their natural strivings should fit together precisely as a deliberate and prudent choice could have united them!",["- Restated the opening as two sentences; \"sie zu bestreiten\" taken to refer to the opponents (Gegner).","- \"[in the service of]\" is supplied to complete \"zu Zwecken solcher Dinge\".","- The long \"Wie wäre es wohl möglich…\" sentence is kept as one, since its structure is clear; \"beziehend entworfen\" is rendered \"designed in relation to one another\".","- \"Ohngefähr\" is rendered \"coincidence\"; \"Ungefehr\" and \"Zufall\" earlier are \"chance\" and \"accident\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0016":["I now apply this with confidence to my present undertaking. I assume the matter of the whole world to be in a universal dispersion, and I make of it a perfect chaos. I see the material form itself according to the established laws of attraction, and modify its motion through repulsion. Without the aid of arbitrary fictions, and on the occasion of established laws of motion, I enjoy the pleasure of seeing a well-ordered whole generate itself. This whole looks so like the system of the world that lies before our eyes that I cannot refrain from taking it for that very system.\n\nThis unexpected unfolding of the order of nature on the large scale at first made me suspicious, since it builds so composite a regularity on so plain and simple a foundation. In the end I am instructed by the consideration set out above. Such an unfolding is nothing unheard of in nature; her essential striving necessarily brings it with it, and this is the most glorious testimony of her dependence on that primal Being which has within itself even the source of the beings themselves and of their first laws of operation. This insight redoubles my confidence in the design I have made. My assurance increases with each step I take forward, and my faint-heartedness wholly ceases.",["- Divided the passage into two paragraphs at the point where the German turns to the initial suspicion. The tense shifts of the German (present \"wird mir … verdächtig\") are smoothed to the past for readability.","- \"Auswickelung\" = unfolding; \"Richtigkeit\" = regularity (a choice); \"schlecht\" = plain, in the older sense.","- \"Urwesen\" = primal Being; \"Wirkungsgesetze\" = laws of operation."]],"naturgeschichte-0017":["But the defence of your system, it will be said, is at the same time the defence of the opinions of Epicurus, which bear the greatest resemblance to it. I do not wish to disclaim all agreement with him. Many have become atheists through the appearance of such arguments, which on closer consideration could have convinced them most powerfully of the certainty of the supreme Being. The consequences that a perverted understanding draws from blameless principles are often highly blameworthy, and such were the conclusions of Epicurus, although his design was in keeping with the acuteness of a great mind.",["- \"Entwurf\" rendered \"design\" (as elsewhere in the passage group); \"Schlüße\" = conclusions.","- \"Ich will nicht völlig alle Uebereinstimmung … ablehnen\" is rendered literally; the sense is that some agreement is admitted."]],"naturgeschichte-0018":["I shall therefore not deny that the theory of Lucretius, or of his predecessors Epicurus, Leucippus and Democritus, has much resemblance to my own. Like those philosophers, I place the first state of nature in the universal dispersion of the primal stuff of all the heavenly bodies, or of the atoms, as they are called by them. Epicurus posited a gravity that drove these elementary particles to sink, and this seems not very different from the Newtonian attraction which I assume. He also gave them a certain deviation from the rectilinear motion of fall, although with regard to its cause and its consequences he held absurd fancies. This deviation agrees to some extent with the alteration of the rectilinear descent which we derived from the repulsive force of the particles. Finally, the vortices that arose from the confused motion of the atoms were a principal part of the doctrine of Leucippus and Democritus, and they will be found in ours also.\n\nYet so much affinity with a system of doctrine that was in antiquity the true theory of the denial of God does not draw my own into the community of its errors. Even in the most senseless opinions that have been able to win men's approval one will always notice something true. A false principle, or a pair of ill-considered connecting propositions, leads man from the footpath of truth by imperceptible by-ways down to the abyss. Despite the resemblance cited, there remains an essential difference between the ancient cosmogony and the present one, such that quite opposite consequences can be drawn from the latter.",["- Divided at \"So viel Verwandschaft\" into two paragraphs and split the Epicurus sentences; the semicolons of the German are partly replaced by full stops.","- \"Weltweise\" = philosophers (literally \"world-wise\"); \"Lehrverfassung / Lehrbegriff\" = system of doctrine / doctrine.","- \"Verbindungssätze\" = connecting propositions; the sense of \"Abweichung\" is deviation, the Epicurean \"swerve\".","- The last clause \"um … ziehen zu können\" is rendered as a result clause, \"such that\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0019":["The teachers of the mechanical generation of the world-structure whom I have named derived all the order that can be perceived in it from chance, which let the atoms meet so fortunately that they composed a well-ordered whole. Epicurus was even so shameless as to demand that the atoms deviate from their straight motion without any cause at all, in order to be able to meet one another. All of them together carried this absurdity so far that they ascribed the origin of all living creatures to this same blind concourse, and in effect derived reason from unreason.\n\nIn my system of doctrine, on the other hand, I find matter bound to certain necessary laws. I see a beautiful and orderly whole develop quite naturally out of its complete dissolution and dispersion. This does not happen by accident and by chance; rather one observes that natural properties necessarily bring it with them. Are we not thereby moved to ask: Why had matter to have precisely such laws, as aim at order and fitness? Was it really possible that many things, each of which has a nature independent of the others, should of themselves determine one another precisely so that a well-ordered whole springs from them? And if they do this, does it not afford an undeniable proof of the community of their first origin, which must be an all-sufficient supreme understanding, in which the natures of things were designed for united purposes?",["- Split into two paragraphs at \"In meiner Lehrverfassung hingegen\"; the three questions are kept as separate questions in English, with the final one carrying the German \"und wenn sie dieses thun\".","- \"ungefehren Zufalle\" = \"chance\"; \"Wohlanständigkeit\" = fitness; \"allgenugsamer\" = all-sufficient.","- \"Vernunft … aus der Unvernunft\" = reason from unreason; \"Verstand\" is \"understanding\" throughout."]],"naturgeschichte-0020":["Matter, which is the primal stuff of all things, is therefore bound to certain laws, and when freely left to them it must necessarily bring forth beautiful combinations. It has no freedom to deviate from this plan of perfection. Since it thus finds itself subjected to a supremely wise purpose, it must necessarily have been placed in such harmonious relations by a first cause ruling over it. And there is a God precisely for this reason, that nature, even in chaos, cannot proceed otherwise than regularly and in order.",["- \"frey überlassen\" is rendered \"freely left to them\", meaning left to itself and to those laws; \"Absicht\" = purpose.","- \"ein GOtt\" is rendered \"a God\", following the German; the capitalised form is kept as GOtt → God."]],"naturgeschichte-0021":["I have so good an opinion of the upright disposition of those who do this sketch the honour of examining it, that I hold myself assured that the reasons adduced will, where they cannot yet remove all apprehension of harmful consequences from my system, at least place the sincerity of my intention beyond doubt. If, notwithstanding this, there are malicious zealots who regard it as a worthy duty of their sacred calling to fasten harmful interpretations upon the most innocent opinions, I am assured that among reasonable men their judgment will have precisely the opposite effect to what they intend. Moreover, I shall not be deprived of the right which Descartes, when he ventured to explain the formation of the heavenly bodies [*Weltkörper*] from purely mechanical laws, has always enjoyed with fair-minded judges. I shall for this purpose cite the authors of the *Universal History* (*): \"Meanwhile we cannot but believe that the attempt of this philosopher [*Weltweiser*] is not culpable, nor derogatory to God, as some have imagined. He endeavoured to explain the formation of the world, within a certain time, out of waste matter, through the mere continuance of a motion once impressed, and reduced this to a few simple and universal laws of motion. The same holds of others who since then have, with greater applause, attempted the very same thing from the original and created properties of matter; for by this a higher concept of his infinite wisdom is rather brought about.\"\n\n[Restated: the quotation is a single long sentence in the German; I have divided it in two.]",[]],"naturgeschichte-0022":["(*) Part 1, §. 88.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0023":["I have sought to remove the difficulties which seemed to threaten my propositions from the side of religion. There are some no less considerable with regard to the subject-matter itself. It will be said: Even if it is true that God has laid in the forces of nature a secret art whereby, out of chaos, they of themselves develop into a perfect constitution of the world, will the human understanding, which is so dull in dealing with the commonest objects, be capable of investigating the hidden properties in so great a matter? Such an undertaking amounts to saying: \"Give me only matter, and I will build you a world out of it.\" Can the weakness of your insight, which is put to shame by the smallest things that daily and close at hand present themselves to your senses, not teach you that it is vain to attempt to discover the immeasurable, and what went on in nature before there was yet a world?\n\nI dispose of this difficulty by showing clearly that this very inquiry is, of all that can be raised in natural science, the one in which we can most easily and most surely arrive at the origin. Of all the problems of natural inquiry none has been solved with greater correctness and certainty than the true constitution of the structure of the universe on the large scale, the laws of the motions, and the inner mechanism of the revolutions of all the planets; for in this the Newtonian philosophy can afford insight of a kind found in no other part of philosophy. In the same way, I maintain, among all natural things whose first cause is sought, the origin of the world-system and the generation of the heavenly bodies, together with the causes of their motions, is that which we may hope soonest to understand thoroughly and reliably.\n\nThe reason for this is easily seen. The heavenly bodies are round masses, and so have the simplest form that a body whose origin is sought can have. Their motions are likewise unmixed. They are nothing but a free continuation of an impulse once impressed, which, combined with the attraction of the body at the centre, becomes circular. Moreover, the space in which they move is empty, and the intervals which separate them from one another are exceedingly great; and thus everything is set apart in the most distinct manner, both for unconfused motion and for clear observation of it.\n\nIt seems to me that here, in a certain sense, one may say without presumption: \"Give me matter, and I will build a world out of it!\" That is: give me matter, and I will show you how a world is to arise from it. For if there is matter endowed with an essential force of attraction, it is not difficult to determine those causes which can have contributed to the arrangement of the world-system considered on the large scale. We know what is required for a body to acquire a spherical figure; we comprehend what is needed for freely suspended spheres to execute a circular motion about the centre towards which they are drawn. The position of the orbits relative to one another, the agreement of direction, the eccentricity, all can be reduced to the simplest mechanical causes, and we may hope with confidence to discover them, since they can be based on the easiest and clearest grounds.\n\nBut can we boast of such advantages in the case of the smallest plants or insects? Are we in a position to say: \"Give me matter, and I will show you how a caterpillar can be produced\"? Do we not here remain stuck at the first step, through ignorance of the true inner constitution of the object and of the complexity of the manifold present in it? We must not, then, be astonished if I venture to say that the formation of all the heavenly bodies, the cause of their motions, in short the origin of the whole present constitution of the universe, will be understood sooner than the generation of a single herb or caterpillar will be made clearly and completely known from mechanical grounds.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0024":["These are the grounds on which I base my confidence that the physical part of cosmology may yet hope in future for the very perfection to which Newton raised its mathematical half. Next to the laws according to which the structure of the universe subsists in its present constitution, there are perhaps no others in the whole of natural inquiry capable of such mathematical determinations [*Bestimmungen*] as those according to which it came into being; and without doubt the hand of a practised geometer would here not be cultivating barren fields.",["- 0021: Long sentences divided; the quotation is split in two. \"Weltweiser\" rendered \"philosopher\"; \"Weltkörper\" as \"heavenly bodies\" (later \"Himmelskörper\" is the same).","- 0021: \"Entwurf\" = \"sketch\"; \"GOtt verkleinerlich\" = \"derogatory to God\".","- 0021: The quotation's syntax is tangled (the \"so wenig als anderer\" comparison); I restated it, so the sense of the comparison is carried by \"The same holds of others\".","- 0023: Divided into paragraphs and shorter sentences; \"Weltwissenschaft\" and \"Weltbau\" rendered \"universe\"/\"structure of the universe\".","- 0023: \"Ich vernichte diese Schwierigkeit, indem deutlich zeige\" has \"ich\" omitted in the original; supplied silently as \"by showing\".","- 0023: \"in gewissem Verstande\" = \"in a certain sense\"; \"Verstand\" in the earlier \"der Verstand des Menschen\" = \"understanding\".","- 0024: \"Meßkünstler\" rendered \"geometer\"; \"Weltwissenschaft\" rendered \"cosmology\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0025":["Having taken pains to commend the subject of my inquiry to a favourable reception, I may be allowed briefly to explain the manner in which I have treated it. The first part is occupied with a new system of the universe on the largest scale. Mr. Wright of Durham, whose treatise I came to know through the *Hamburgische freye Urtheile* of the year 1751, first gave me occasion to regard the fixed stars not as a throng scattered without visible order, but as a system bearing the closest resemblance to a planetary one. In the planetary system the planets all lie very near a common plane. In the same way, I held, the fixed stars are related in their positions, as nearly as possible, to a certain plane, which must be thought of as drawn through the whole heavens; and by their densest crowding towards this plane they present that luminous band which is entitled the Milky Way.\n\nI satisfied myself that, since this zone, illumined by countless suns, follows very exactly the direction of a great circle, our sun also must lie very near this great plane of reference. In inquiring into the causes of this determination [*Bestimmung*], I found it very probable that the so-called fixed or firm stars might really be slowly moving wandering stars [*Wandelsterne*] of a higher order. In confirmation of what will be found on this thought in its proper place, I shall here quote only a passage from a work of Mr. Bradley on the motion of the fixed stars.\n\n\"If we wish to form a judgment from the result of comparing our best present observations with those which were formerly made with a tolerable degree of accuracy, it is evident that some fixed stars have really altered their positions relatively to one another. It is evident, moreover, that this does not arise from any motion in our planetary system, but can be ascribed solely to a motion of the stars themselves. Arcturus gives strong proof of this. For if we compare its present declination with its place as determined by Tycho and also by Flamsteed, we shall find that the difference is greater than can be supposed to arise from the uncertainty of their observations. We have reason to suppose that other examples of the same kind must occur among the great number of visible stars, since their positions relatively to one another can be altered by various causes.\n\n\"For if we suppose that our own solar system changes its place with respect to universal space, this will, after some time, cause an apparent change in the angular distances of the fixed stars. And since in such a case the change would have a greater influence on the places of the nearest stars than on the places of those which are far distant, their positions would appear to alter, although the stars themselves really remained immovable. If, on the contrary, our own planetary system stands still and some stars really have a motion, this will likewise alter their apparent position, and the more so, the nearer they are to us, or the more the direction of the motion is such that it can be perceived by us.\n\n\"Since, then, the positions of the stars can be altered by such various causes, when we consider the astonishing distances at which some of them certainly lie, the observations of many generations will probably be needed to determine the laws of the apparent changes, even of a single star. It must therefore be far more difficult still to establish the laws for all the most noteworthy stars.\"",[]],"naturgeschichte-0026":["I cannot determine exactly the boundaries between Mr. Wright's system and my own, nor in what respects I have merely imitated his design and in what I have carried it further. In the sequel, however, there presented themselves to me grounds worthy of acceptance for considerably enlarging it on one side. I considered the kind of nebulous stars which Monsieur de Maupertuis mentions in his treatise on the figure of the stars (*), and which present the figure of more or less open ellipses; and I readily convinced myself that they can be nothing other than a crowding together of many fixed stars. The regular roundness, always exactly measured, of these figures taught me that an inconceivably numerous host of stars must here be ordered about a common centre. Otherwise their free positions relatively to one another would present irregular shapes, but not figures of regular outline.\n\nI saw also that the stars, in the system in which they are found united, must be confined chiefly to one plane, since the figures they present are elliptical and not circular; and that, on account of their pale light, they are at an inconceivable distance from us. What I have concluded from these analogies the treatise itself will lay before the examination of the unprejudiced reader.",["- 0025: The first long sentence on Wright is divided into three, and the \"so daß, gleichwie … also auch\" construction is recast; \"I held\" is supplied to mark the doctrine as Kant's inference.","- 0025: *Bestimmung* (\"determination\") is kept in brackets, though here it means the situation or tendency of the sun near the plane.","- 0025: Bradley's quotation is divided into paragraphs for clarity; \"It is evident, moreover\" restates \"und zwar so, daß man siehet\". \"Tycho\" is rendered \"Tycho\" and \"Flammsteed\" is modernised to Flamsteed.","- 0026: The footnote marker (*) is retained, but no footnote text is in the passage, so none is supplied.","- 0026: The sentence on the \"abgemessene Rundung\" is divided in two; \"of regular outline\" renders *abgemessene Figuren*, and \"Otherwise\" supplies the connection of *weil sonst*.","- 0026: \"Sequel\" renders *nach der Hand* (\"afterwards\")."]],"naturgeschichte-0027":["(*) As I do not have the treatise cited to hand, I shall insert here the relevant matter from the account of M. de Maupertuis's *Ouvrages diverses* given in the *Acta Eruditorum* of 1745. \"The first phenomenon is those luminous patches in the heavens which are entitled nebulous stars, and are held to be a cluster of small fixed stars. But the astronomers, with the aid of excellent telescopes, have found them to be merely large, elongated-round patches, somewhat brighter than the remaining part of the heavens. Huygens was the first to come upon something of this kind in Orion; Halley mentions in the *Philosophical Transactions* [*Anglican. Trans.*] six such patches: 1. in the sword of Orion; 2. in Sagittarius; 3. in Centaurus; 4. in front of the right foot of Antinous; 5. in Hercules; 6. in the girdle of Andromeda. When these are viewed through a reflecting telescope of 8 feet, it is seen that only the fourth part of them can be held to be a cluster of stars; the rest have presented only whitish patches, without any considerable difference, except that one comes closer to a circular shape, while another is more elongated. It also appears that in the first of them the small stars visible through the telescope cannot be the cause of its whitish glimmer. Halley believes that these appearances [*Erscheinungen*] may serve to explain what is found at the beginning of the Mosaic history of creation, namely, that light was created before the sun. Derham compares them to openings through which another immeasurable region, and perhaps the empyrean heaven, shines through. He thinks he has been able to observe that the stars seen beside these patches are much nearer to us than these luminous places. To this the author adds a list of the nebulous stars taken from Hevelius. He holds these appearances to be great luminous masses which have been flattened by a violent rotation. The matter of which they consist, if it had the same luminous power as the other stars, would have to be of enormous magnitude, so that, seen from a much greater distance than that of the stars, it could yet appear to the telescope with a noticeable shape and size. But if in magnitude they were approximately equal to the other fixed stars, they would have to be not only incomparably nearer to us but at the same time of a much feebler light, since, at such nearness and apparent magnitude, they show so pale a glimmer. It would therefore be worth the trouble to discover their parallax, if they have one. For those who deny them any perhaps infer from some to all. The little stars which are found in the middle of these patches, as in Orion (or, still more beautifully, in the one in front of the right foot of Antinous, which looks no different from a fixed star surrounded by a mist), would, if they were nearer to us, either be seen upon them after the manner of a projection, or would shine through those masses, as through the tails of comets.\"",["- Footnote text, kept as such. The quotation closes with a mark but no opening one in the German beyond „; retained as given.","- \"Anglican. Trans.\" is Kant's abbreviation for the Philosophical Transactions; I give the expansion in brackets.","- \"Hugen\" = Huygens; \"Seherohr\"/\"Fernglas\" both rendered \"telescope\"; \"vor\" in \"vor einen Haufen\" and \"vor dem Fusse\" rendered \"as\"/\"in front of\" by sense.","- \"Er\" at \"Diesen fügt der Verfasser\" is Maupertuis (\"the author\"), as the later \"Er hält\" also; resolved by context.","- Last sentence: the mood of \"schienen\" is irregular; I smoothed it to a parallel \"would … or would\".","- \"Phänomenon\" rendered \"phenomenon\"; \"Erscheinungen\" given as \"appearances\" with the German bracketed once."]],"naturgeschichte-0028":["In the second part, which contains the proper subject of this treatise, I seek to develop the constitution of the universe [*Weltbau*] out of the simplest state of nature, by mechanical laws alone. If I may venture to propose a certain order of procedure to those who are indignant at the boldness of this undertaking, in the examination with which they honour my thoughts, I would beg them to read through the eighth chapter first; it will, I hope, prepare their judgment for a correct insight. Meanwhile, in inviting the kind reader to examine my opinions, I rightly fear that, since hypotheses of this sort commonly stand in little better repute than philosophical dreams, it is a sour courtesy to a reader to resolve upon a careful investigation of histories of nature that have been devised by the author himself, and to follow him patiently through all the turns by which he evades the difficulties that confront him, only perhaps at the end, like the spectators of the London mountebank (*), to laugh at his own credulity. Nevertheless I venture to promise that, when the reader has, as I hope, been persuaded by the preparatory chapter proposed to risk such a physical adventure on conjectures so probable, he will not meet on the further course of the road so many crooked by-ways and impassable obstacles as he may at the outset have feared.",["- Long sentences divided or kept with semicolons for clarity; no content added.","- \"gneigten\" is an OCR/print slip for \"geneigten\" (\"kind\"); silently corrected.","- \"Weltbau\" given in brackets as a choice (\"constitution of the universe\"; \"Verfassung\" = constitution).","- \"Hauptstück\" rendered \"chapter\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0029":["(*) See Gellert's fable, \"Hans Nord.\"",["- Gellert's fable is \"Hans Nord\" (the London mountebank); title kept as given."]],"naturgeschichte-0030":["In fact I have, with the greatest caution, abstained from all arbitrary fictions. After placing the world in the simplest chaos, I have employed no other forces for the development of the great order of nature than the forces of attraction and repulsion, two forces which are both equally certain, equally simple, and at the same time equally original and universal. Both are borrowed from the Newtonian philosophy. The first is a law of nature now placed beyond doubt. The second, to which Newton's natural science perhaps cannot give as much clearness as to the first, I here assume only in that sense in which nobody disputes it, namely, in the finest dissolution of matter, as, for example, in vapours. From these principles, so simple, I have derived the following system in an unartificial manner, without devising any other consequences than those upon which the reader's attention must of itself light.",["- \"Verstande\" (in \"in demjenigen Verstande\") rendered \"sense\", not \"understanding\", as ordinary usage here.","- \"Naturwissenschaft des Newton\" rendered \"Newton's natural science\" (the *Principia*'s doctrine).","- \"Weltweisheit\" rendered \"philosophy\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0031":["Finally, let me be permitted a brief explanation concerning the validity and alleged worth of the propositions which will occur in the following theory, and by which standard I wish them to be tried before fair judges. An author is fairly judged by the stamp which he impresses on his wares; I hope, therefore, that in the various parts of this treatise no stricter account of my opinions will be demanded than accords with the worth which I myself assign to them. In general, the greatest geometrical rigour and mathematical infallibility can never be required of a treatise of this kind. If the system is grounded on analogies and agreements, according to the rules of credibility and of a correct way of thinking, it has satisfied all the demands of its object. This degree of soundness I believe I have attained in some parts of this treatise: in the theory of the systems of fixed stars, in the hypothesis concerning the nature of the nebulous stars, in the general sketch of the mechanical mode of generation of the universe, in the theory of the ring of Saturn, and in some others. Somewhat less conviction will be afforded by certain particular parts of the execution, such as the determination of the ratios of eccentricity, the comparison of the masses of the planets, the manifold deviations of the comets, and some others.",["- \"wornach … vor billigen Richter\" restated as \"by which standard … before fair judges\"; the German construction is loose.","- \"Bestimmung\" here rendered \"determination\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0032":["If, therefore, in the seventh chapter, allured by the fruitfulness of the system and the attractiveness of the greatest and most wonderful object that can be thought, I carry the consequences of the doctrine as far as possible, always indeed along the guiding thread of analogy and of reasonable credibility, yet with some boldness; if I present to the imagination the infinity of the whole creation, the formation of new worlds and the decline of the old, and the unbounded space of chaos: then I hope that, in view of the charming attractiveness of the object and of the pleasure which one has in seeing the agreements of a theory in its widest extension, so much indulgence will be granted that it will not be judged by the greatest geometrical strictness, which in any case has no place in considerations of this kind. The same fairness I look for in regard to the third part. One will, however, always find in it something more than merely arbitrary, though at all times something less than unquestionable.",["- The second \"wenn\"-clause and the main clause were kept in one sentence with a colon; \"Lehrgebäude\" rendered \"doctrine\".","- \"so viel Nachsicht vergönnen, sie nicht …\" restated as a result clause (\"so much indulgence … that it will not be judged\"); slight smoothing."]],"naturgeschichte-0033":["Contents\nof the Whole Work.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0034":["First Part.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0035":["Outline of a general systematic constitution among the fixed stars, derived from the phenomena of the Milky Way. Similarity of this system of fixed stars to the system of the planets. Discovery of many such systems, which show themselves in the expanse of the heavens in the form of elliptical figures. A new concept of the systematic constitution of the whole of creation.",["- \"Verfassung\" rendered \"constitution\" (the ordering or arrangement of the whole); \"Phänomenis\" kept as \"phenomena\". Fragmentary table-of-contents style kept."]],"naturgeschichte-0036":["Conclusion. Probable conjecture of several planets beyond Saturn, from the law according to which the eccentricity of the planets increases with their distances.",["- \"Beschluß\" rendered \"Conclusion\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0037":["Second Part.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0038":["First Chapter.",["- \"Hauptstück\" rendered \"Chapter\" throughout."]],"naturgeschichte-0039":["Grounds in favour of the doctrinal system of a mechanical origin of the world. Grounds against it. The one concept, among all possible, that does justice to both. First state of nature. Dispersion of the elements of all matter throughout the whole of cosmic space. First stirring through attraction. Beginning of the formation of a body at the point of strongest attraction. General sinking of the elements towards this central body. Repulsive force of the finest particles into which the matter had been dissolved. Altered direction of the sinking motion through the combination of this force with the former. Uniform direction of all these motions towards one and the same region. Endeavour of all particles to press towards a common plane and to accumulate there. Moderation of the speed of their motion to an equilibrium with the gravity of the distance of their place. Free revolution of all the particles about the central body in circular orbits. Formation of the planets from these moving elements. Free motion of the planets composed of them, in the same direction in a common plane, in nearly circular orbits near the centre, and, farther from it, with increasing degrees of eccentricity.",["- \"Lehrverfassung\" rendered \"doctrinal system\"; \"vor\" in \"Gründe vor\" is old usage for \"für\", hence \"in favour of\".","- \"Schweere des Abstandes ihres Orts\" kept literal (\"gravity of the distance of their place\"), though the sense is gravity at the distance of their position.","- \"Cirkelkreisen\" rendered \"circular orbits\"; \"beyden\" understood as both the grounds for and against."]],"naturgeschichte-0040":["Second Chapter.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0041":["Treats of the differing density of the planets and the ratio of their masses. Cause why the nearer planets are of denser kind than the more distant. Insufficiency of Newton's explanation. Why the central body is of lighter kind than the globes running nearest around it. Ratio of the masses of the planets according to the proportion of the distances. Cause, from the manner of generation, why the central body has the greatest mass. Calculation of the tenuity in which all the elements of the world-matter were dispersed. Probability and necessity of this rarefaction. Important proof of the manner of generation of the heavenly bodies from a remarkable analogy noted by Monsieur de Buffon.",["- \"Dünnigkeit\" rendered \"tenuity\"; \"Verdünnung\" \"rarefaction\". \"Erzeugung\" kept as \"generation\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0042":["Third Chapter.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0043":["Of the eccentricity of the planetary orbits and the origin of the comets. The eccentricity increases by degrees with the distances from the sun. Cause of this law from the cosmogony. Why the orbits of the comets stray freely from the plane of the ecliptic. Proof that the comets are formed from the lightest class of matter. Incidental remark on the northern lights.",["- \"seyn\" in \"gebildet seyn\" is the old subjunctive/infinitive form; rendered \"are formed\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0044":["Fourth Chapter.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0045":["Of the origin of the moons and the rotations of the planets about their axes. The matter for the generation of the moons was contained in the sphere from which the planet gathered the parts for its own formation. Cause of the motion of these moons with all its determinations. Why only the great planets have moons. Of the axial rotation of the planets. Whether the moon formerly had a swifter one? Whether the speed of the earth's rotation is diminishing? Of the inclination of the planets' axes to the plane of their orbits. Displacement of their axes.",["- \"Bestimmungen\" rendered \"determinations\"; \"Stellung der Achse\" rendered \"inclination of the axis\", as the sense requires."]],"naturgeschichte-0046":["Fifth Chapter.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0047":["Of the origin of Saturn's ring and the calculation of Saturn's daily rotation from its proportions. First state of Saturn compared with the constitution of a comet. Formation of a ring from the particles of its atmosphere by means of the motions impressed on them by its rotation. Determination of the time of its axial rotation according to this hypothesis. Consideration of the figure of Saturn. Of the spheroidal flattening of the heavenly bodies in general. Closer determination of the constitution of this ring. Probable conjecture of new discoveries. Whether the earth before the Flood may not have had a ring?",["- \"einem Ring\" (sic, for \"einen\") silently corrected in sense. \"Sündfluth\" rendered \"the Flood\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0048":["Sixth Chapter.",["- \"Sechtes\" is the original's spelling for \"Sechstes\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0049":["Of the zodiacal light.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0050":["Seventh Chapter.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0051":["Of creation in the whole extent of its infinity, in space as well as in time. Origin of a great system of the fixed stars. Central body at the centre of the system of stars. Infinity of creation. General systematic relation in its entire sum [*Innbegriff*]. Central body of the whole of nature. Successive continuation of creation throughout all the infinity of times and spaces, by unceasing formation of new worlds. Reflection on the chaos of unformed nature. Gradual decay and downfall of the structure of the world [*Weltbau*]. Fittingness of such a concept. Renewal of decayed nature.",["- \"Innbegriff\" rendered \"entire sum\" (the whole comprehended); \"Weltbau\" \"structure of the world\". \"Wohlanständigkeit\" rendered \"fittingness\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0052":["Appendix to the Seventh Chapter.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0053":["General theory and history of the sun in general. Why the central body of a world-structure is a fiery body. Closer consideration of its nature. Thoughts on the changes of the air surrounding it. Extinction of suns. Close view of their figure. Opinion of Mr. Wright concerning the centre of the whole of nature. Correction of it.",["- \"Luft\" rendered \"air\", i.e. the atmosphere of the sun."]],"naturgeschichte-0054":["Eighth Chapter.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0055":["General proof of the correctness of a mechanical doctrinal system of the arrangement of the world-structure in general, and especially of the certainty of the present one. The essential capacity of the natures of things to raise themselves of their own accord to order and perfection is the fairest proof of the existence of God. Defence against the charge of naturalism.",["- \"Fähigkeit\" rendered \"capacity\"; \"Daseyn\" rendered \"existence\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0056":["The constitution of the world-structure is simple and not set above the powers of nature. Analogies which establish with certainty the mechanical origin of the world. The same proved from the deviations. The citing of an immediate divine ordering does not satisfy these questions. Difficulty which moved Newton to abandon the mechanical doctrinal concept. Resolution of this difficulty. The system here set forth is the only means, among all possible grounds on both sides, of giving satisfaction. It is further proved by the ratio of the density of the planets, of their masses, of the intervals of their distances, and by the graduated connection of their determinations. The motives of God's choice do not determine these circumstances immediately. Justification with respect to religion. Difficulties which emerge in a doctrinal system of immediate divine ordering.",["- \"Bewegungsgründe der Wahl GOttes\" rendered \"motives of God's choice\". \"beyderseitigen Gründen\" rendered \"grounds on both sides\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0057":["Third Part.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0058":["Contains a comparison between the inhabitants of the heavenly bodies.",["- \"Gestirne\" rendered \"heavenly bodies\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0059":["Whether all the planets are inhabited? Reason for doubting it. Ground of the physical relations between the inhabitants of different planets. Consideration of man. Causes of the imperfection of his nature. Natural relation of the bodily properties of living creatures according to their different distance from the sun. Consequences of this relation for their spiritual faculties. Comparison of the thinking natures on different heavenly bodies. Confirmation from certain circumstances of their dwelling-places. Further proof from the provisions of divine providence made for their good. Short digression.",["- \"Verhältniß\" in \"Folgen dieser Verhältniß\" is the original's feminine form; read as \"relation\". \"Fähigkeiten\" rendered \"faculties\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0060":["Conclusion.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0061":["The fortunes of man in the future life.",["- \"Begebenheiten\" rendered \"fortunes\" (lit. \"events, occurrences\")."]],"naturgeschichte-0062":["Brief Outline\nof the Most Necessary Fundamental Concepts\nof\nNewtonian Cosmology (*)",["- \"Weltwissenschaft\" rendered \"cosmology\" (lit. \"world-science\"). The asterisk refers to the footnote in the next passage."]],"naturgeschichte-0063":["(*) This brief introduction, which may perhaps be superfluous for most readers, I have wished to give beforehand, as a preparation for insight into the following theory, to those who may not be sufficiently familiar with the Newtonian principles.",["- Footnote kept as a footnote. \"Einsicht\" rendered \"insight\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0064":["which are required for the understanding of what follows.",["- A fragment continuing the title; \"Verstande\" here is ordinary \"understanding\" of a text, not the faculty."]],"naturgeschichte-0065":["Six planets, three of which have companions (Mercury, Venus, the earth with its moon, Mars, Jupiter with four and Saturn with five satellites), which describe circles about the sun as their centre, together with the comets, which do so from all sides and in very long orbits, make up a system which is entitled the system of the suns, or also the planetary structure of the world. The motion of all these bodies, since it is circular and returns into itself, presupposes two forces, which are equally necessary on every kind of doctrinal system. The first is a projectile force [*schiessende Kraft*], through which the bodies at every point of their curved course would continue in the straight direction and recede into infinity, were it not that another force, whatever it may be, constantly constrained them to abandon this direction and to run in a curved path which embraces the sun as its centre. This second force, as geometry itself establishes beyond doubt, aims everywhere towards the sun, and is therefore called the sinking force, the centripetal force, or also gravity.",["- Restated: the long first sentence is divided at \"nemlich\" so that the two forces are stated separately; the original's parenthetical list of planets is supplied with parentheses by me (the German has dashes of syntax, not parentheses).","- Kant's text says \"Sechs Planeten, davon drey Begleiter haben\" and then lists the planets; the list is carried over as it stands, although the sentence's grammar is loose.","- \"Kreise\" rendered \"circles\" in the first instance and \"orbits\" for \"Kreisen\" of the comets, as the sense requires; \"krumlienigten\" rendered \"curved\".","- \"Lehrbegriff\" rendered \"doctrinal system\"; \"sinkende\" rendered \"sinking\" (falling), as in the original."]],"naturgeschichte-0066":["If the orbits of the heavenly bodies were exact circles, the very simplest analysis of the composition of curvilinear motions would show that a continuous impulse toward the centre is required for them. But although in all the planets, as well as in the comets, the orbits are ellipses, in whose common focus the Sun is situated, higher geometry, with the aid of the Keplerian analogy, likewise demonstrates with unerring certainty that a force must urge the planet unceasingly toward the centre of the Sun throughout its whole revolution. (According to this analogy the *radius vector*, that is, the line drawn from the planet to the Sun, always cuts off from the elliptical path areas proportional to the times.) This force of descent [*Senkungskraft*], which prevails throughout the whole space of the planetary system and is directed toward the Sun, is therefore an established phenomenon of nature. The law according to which this force extends from the centre into the distant spaces is proved with equal certainty. It diminishes in the inverse ratio in which the squares of the distances from the centre increase. This rule follows in an equally unerring manner from the time which the planets require for their revolutions at different distances. These times are always as the square roots of the cubes of their mean distances from the Sun. From this it is inferred that the force which drives these heavenly bodies toward the centre of their revolution must decrease in the inverse ratio of the squares of the distance.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0067":["The very same law which rules among the planets, so far as they revolve about the Sun, is found also in the small systems, namely those which the moons, moving about their principal planets, constitute. Their periods of revolution are proportioned to the distances in the same way, and establish the same ratio of the force of descent toward the planet as that to which the planet is subject in its relation to the Sun. All this has been placed beyond contradiction for ever by the most unerring geometry, by means of indisputable observations. To this there is added the idea that this force of descent is the very same impulse which on the surface of the planet is entitled gravity [*Schwere*], and which diminishes from the surface by degrees with the distances, according to the law cited. This is seen by comparing the quantity of gravity on the surface of the Earth with the force which drives the Moon toward the centre of its orbit. These stand to one another, just as does attraction in the whole structure of the universe, in the inverse ratio of the square of the distances. This is the reason why the central force so often mentioned is also called gravity.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0068":["Besides, it is in the highest degree probable that, when an effect takes place only in the presence of a certain body and in proportion to the approach to it, and its direction is also exactly related to that body, we are to believe that this body, in whatever manner it may be, is the cause of the effect. For this reason men have thought they had ground enough to ascribe this universal descent of the planets toward the Sun to a force of attraction in the latter, and to attribute this power of attraction to all heavenly bodies in general.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0069":["If, then, a body is left free to this impulse, which drives it to descend toward the Sun or any planet, it will fall down to it in a constantly accelerated motion and in a short time unite with its mass. But if it has received an impulse sideways, then, provided this is not strong enough to balance exactly the pressure of descent, it will sink in a curved motion toward the central body. If the impetus impressed upon it has been at least strong enough to remove it, before it touches the surface of that body, from the perpendicular line by half the thickness of the body at its centre [*i.e.* by the body's semi-diameter], it will not touch the surface. Having swung close round the body, it will rise again, by the velocity acquired in falling, as high as it had fallen, and so continue its course in a constant circular motion about it.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0070":["The difference between the orbits of comets and of planets therefore consists in the balancing of the lateral motion against the pressure which drives them to fall. The nearer these two forces come to equality, the more closely does the orbit resemble the circular figure. The more unequal they are, that is, the weaker the projectile force is in relation to the central force, the more elongated is the orbit, or, as it is called, the more eccentric, since the heavenly body in one part of its path approaches the Sun much nearer than in another.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0071":["Since nothing in the whole of nature is balanced with perfect exactness, no planet has a quite circular motion. But the comets deviate from it most, because the impetus impressed on them sideways was least proportioned to the central force at their original distance.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0072":["In this treatise I shall very often make use of the expression \"a systematic constitution of the universe\" [*systematische Verfassung des Weltbaues*]. So that there may be no difficulty in representing distinctly what is meant by it, I shall explain myself briefly. Strictly, all the planets and comets belonging to our universe already constitute a system by the fact that they revolve about a common central body. But I take the term in a narrower sense, in that I have regard to the more exact relations which have made their connection with one another regular and uniform. The orbits of the planets are related as nearly as possible to a common plane, namely the extended equatorial plane of the Sun. Deviation from this rule occurs only at the outermost limit of the system, where all motions gradually cease. Suppose, therefore, that a certain number of heavenly bodies, ordered about a common centre and moving about it, are at the same time so confined to a certain plane that they are free to deviate from it, on either side, only as little as possible. Suppose, further, that the deviation occurs by degrees only in those which are furthest removed from the centre, and which therefore have less share than the others in the relations. In that case I say that these bodies are bound together in a systematic constitution.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0073":["Universal Natural History and Theory of the Heavens.\nPart the First:\nSketch of a Systematic Constitution among the Fixed Stars, and likewise of the Plurality of Such Systems of Fixed Stars.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0074":["Universal Natural History and Theory of the Heavens.\nPart the First:\nOf the Systematic Constitution among the Fixed Stars.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0075":["The doctrine of the general constitution of the universe has gained no appreciable increase since the time of Huygens. As yet we know nothing more than what was known even then: namely, that six planets with ten attendants, all of which have directed the circles of their revolution almost upon one plane, together with the eternal cometary globes, which range out on every side, constitute a system whose centre is the Sun. Towards the Sun everything sinks; about it their motions go; and by it they are all illumined, warmed and quickened. We know, further, that the fixed stars, as so many suns, are centres of similar systems, in which everything may be arranged as great and as orderly as in our own; and that infinite cosmic space teems with world-structures, whose number and excellence stand in proportion to the immensity of their Creator.",["(Notes apply to the passages collectively; none were requested separately.)","- 0066: Kant's long second sentence is divided; the parenthesis on Kepler's analogy is set after the sentence. \"Kraft\" kept as \"force\"; *Senkungskraft* glossed in brackets.","- 0066: \"Cubis\" rendered \"cubes\"; \"Quadratwurzel aus den Cubis\" is given as \"square roots of the cubes\", as in the German.","- 0067: *Schwere* \"entitled gravity\" (genannt); \"Gravität\" later rendered \"gravity\" as well.","- 0068: The tangled opening clause is smoothed; \"auf was für Art es auch wolle\" is kept as \"in whatever manner it may be\".","- 0069: \"um die halbe Dicke des Körpers im Mittelpunkte\" is obscure; I take it as the body's semi-diameter and flag this in brackets as an interpretive gloss.","- 0070: \"Kreis\" in the sense of orbit is rendered \"orbit\"; \"Laufkreise\" likewise.","- 0072: Kant's two parallel \"wenn\" clauses are restated as a supposition in separate sentences; \"Verfassung\" is rendered \"constitution\".","- 0073–0075: The titles are headings and are not smoothed. In 0075 \"Weltgebäude\" is rendered \"world-structures\", and the Leibnizian closing clause is kept literally."]],"naturgeschichte-0076":["The systematic character which obtained in the connection of the planets revolving about their suns vanished here in the multitude of the fixed stars. It seemed as if the lawful relation found on the small scale did not prevail among the members of the universe on the large scale. The fixed stars were given no law by which their positions relatively to one another might be restricted, and they were seen to fill all the heavens, and the heaven of all heavens, without order and without design. Since man's desire for knowledge set itself these limits, nothing further has been done than to infer from them the greatness of Him who has revealed Himself in works so incomprehensibly great, and to admire it.",["- The opening sentence is divided in two, and \"alhier\" is rendered \"here\", i.e. in the sphere of the fixed stars.","- \"Das systematische\" is taken as a substantive adjective; I supply \"character\".","- \"Aller Himmel Himmel\" is rendered \"the heaven of all heavens\" (a biblical echo); the \"Gegenstand\" of \"desjenigen\" is taken to be God, hence \"Him\", which is an interpretative choice.","- \"Wißbegierde\" is rendered \"desire for knowledge\"; \"Schranken\" as \"limits\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0077":["It was reserved for Herr Wright of Durham, an Englishman, to take a fortunate step towards an observation which he himself seems not to have turned to any very competent use, and whose profitable application he has not sufficiently remarked. He regarded the fixed stars not as a disordered swarm scattered without design. He found, on the contrary, a systematic constitution in the whole, and a general relation of these heavenly bodies to a principal plane of the spaces which they occupy.",["- The second sentence is divided at \"sondern\".","- \"Hauptplan der Raume\" is kept literal as \"principal plane of the spaces\"; \"Raume\" is an old plural/dative form, and the reading is not wholly certain.","- \"Verfassung\" is rendered \"constitution\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0078":["We shall endeavour to improve the thought which he propounded, and to give it that turn by which it may prove fruitful in important consequences, the complete confirmation of which is reserved for future times.",["- \"Wendung\" is rendered \"turn\"; \"Folgen\" as \"consequences\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0079":["Everyone who looks at the starry heavens on a clear night becomes aware of that luminous band which, through the multitude of the stars heaped together there more than elsewhere, and through their individual distinctness being lost in the great distance, presents a uniform light, and which has been given the name of the Milky Way. It is remarkable that observers of the heavens have not long since been moved, by the nature of this zone so clearly distinguished in the sky, to infer from it some peculiar determinations in the positions of the fixed stars. For it is seen to occupy the direction of a great circle, and indeed in unbroken continuity, round the whole heavens. These are two conditions which carry with them so exact a determination, and marks so clearly distinguished from the indefiniteness of chance, that attentive astronomers should naturally have been led by them to search out, with attention, the explanation of such an appearance.",["- The first sentence is kept whole but loosened with relative clauses; \"Kentlichkeit\" is taken as the stars' individual distinguishability, which is lost in the distance.","- \"ihn\" in \"man siehet ihn\" refers to the Milky Way [Milchstrasse]; the sentence is divided before \"zwey Bedingungen\".","- \"Zirkel\" rendered \"great circle\" for \"größten Zirkels\"; \"Bestimmung\" = \"determination\"; \"Erscheinung\" = \"appearance\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0080":["Since the stars are not set upon the apparent hollow sphere of the heavens, but, one further than another from our point of view, lose themselves in the depth of the heavens, it follows from this appearance that, in the distances at which they stand from us one behind another, they are not found in a dispersion indifferent in all directions. They must, rather, relate chiefly to a certain plane which passes through our point of view, and to which they are destined to lie as near as possible.",["- The final sentence is divided at \"sondern\".","- \"Gesichtspunkt\" is rendered \"point of view\"; \"Fläche\" as \"plane\".","- \"Erscheinung\" refers to the Milky Way phenomenon just described."]],"naturgeschichte-0081":["This relation is so indubitable a phenomenon that even the remaining stars, which are not included in the whitish band of the Milky Way, are nonetheless seen the more crowded and dense the nearer their places are to the circle of the Milky Way. Thus, of the 2000 stars which the naked eye discovers in the heavens, the greatest part is found in a zone of no great breadth, the middle of which the Milky Way occupies.",["- The sentence is divided at \"so, daß\".","- \"Phänomenon\" is kept as \"phenomenon\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0082":["If we now conceive a plane drawn through the starry heavens into unlimited distances, and suppose that all fixed stars and systems have a general relation of their place to this plane, so as to lie nearer to it than to other regions, then the eye situated in this plane of reference, looking out into the field of the stars on the hollow spherical surface of the firmament, will behold this densest accumulation of stars in the direction of the plane so drawn, under the figure of a zone illuminated by more light. This luminous band will run in the direction of a great circle, because the standpoint of the spectator is in the plane itself. In this zone it will swarm with stars, which, through the indistinguishable minuteness of the bright points that individually escape the sight, and through their apparent density, present a uniformly whitish shimmer; in a word, a Milky Way. The rest of the host of heaven, whose relation to the plane drawn diminishes by degrees, or which also stands nearer to the observer's position, will be more dispersed, although, in their accumulation, they will still be seen as relating to this same plane. Finally it follows from this that our solar world, since this system of fixed stars is seen from it in the direction of a greatest circle, is itself situated in this same great plane, and constitutes a system with the rest.",["- \"Beziehungsfläche\" is rendered \"plane of reference\".","- \"Systemata\" is rendered \"systems\"; \"vorstellig machen\" as \"present\".","- \"Sonnenwelt\" is rendered \"solar world\", kept literal; \"grössesten Zirkels\" is rendered \"greatest circle\" to preserve the variation from \"great circle\".","- \"Wiewol doch ... gesehen werden\" has an irregular construction; smoothed in English with \"they will still be seen\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0083":["In order to penetrate the better into the nature of the general connection which prevails in the structure of the world, let us seek to discover the cause which has made the places of the fixed stars relate to a common plane.",["- \"Weltbaue\" is rendered \"structure of the world\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0084":["The sun does not confine the reach of its force of attraction within the narrow compass of the planetary structure. To all appearance it extends it to infinity. The comets, which rise very far beyond the orbit of Saturn, are compelled by the sun's attraction to return and to move in circles. Although, then, it is more in accordance with the nature of a force which seems to be incorporated in the essence of matter to be unlimited, and it is in fact recognised as such by those who accept Newton's propositions, yet we wish only to have it granted that this attraction of the sun reaches approximately to the nearest fixed star, and that the fixed stars, as so many suns, act around themselves to an equal extent, and consequently that the whole host of them strives to approach one another through attraction. In that case all world-systems are found in the condition, through their mutual approach, which is incessant and hindered by nothing, of falling together sooner or later into one lump, unless this ruin be prevented, as in the case of the bodies of our planetary system, by forces that flee the centre. These forces, in that they deflect the heavenly bodies from the straight fall, bring about, in combination with the forces of attraction, the eternal circular revolutions, whereby the edifice of creation is secured against destruction and made fit for an imperishable duration.",["- The long sentence is divided at \"so finden sich\" (rendered \"In that case\") and again at \"welche\"; the concessive \"ob ... gleich ... so wollen wir doch\" is kept as \"Although ... yet\".","- \"Kreisen\" for the comets' paths is kept literal as \"circles\" (they return in closed orbits).","- \"Centerfliehkraft\"/\"den Mittelpunkt fliehende Kräfte\" is rendered \"forces that flee the centre\" (centrifugal).","- \"vorgebeugt worden\" is irregular for \"werde\"; rendered as a subjunctive \"be prevented\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0085":["Thus all the suns of the firmament have revolving motions, either about one general centre or about many. Here, however, we may make use of the analogy of what is observed in the revolutions of our solar world. Just as the same cause which gave the planets the centrifugal force by which they perform their revolutions also so directed their orbits that they all relate to one plane, so too the cause, whatever it may be, which gave the suns of the superior world, as so many wandering stars of higher world-orders, the power of revolution has at the same time brought their orbits as far as possible into one plane, and has striven to restrict deviations from it.",["- \"dessen\" is read as a loose genitive dependent on \"Analogie ... dessen, was ...\"; rendered \"of what is observed\".","- The \"daß nemlich, gleichwie ... also auch\" construction is irregular; smoothed into \"Just as ... so too\".","- \"Oberwelt\" is rendered \"superior world\"; \"Wandelsternen\" as \"wandering stars\" (planets).","- \"Centerfliehkraft\" is rendered \"centrifugal force\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0086":["According to this representation, the system of the fixed stars may to some extent be depicted by the planetary system, if the latter is enlarged without limit. For if, in place of the 6 planets with their 10 satellites, we assume as many thousands of them, and, in place of the 28 or 30 comets that have been observed, a hundred or a thousand times as many; and if we think of these bodies as self-luminous, then to the eye of a spectator looking at them from the earth there would arise just the same appearance as that presented by the fixed stars of the Milky Way. The planets so conceived would, owing to their nearness to the common plane to which they are related, present to us, who with our earth are situated in this same plane, a zone densely lit by countless stars, whose direction would follow a great circle. This luminous band would be everywhere sufficiently thronged with stars, although, according to the hypothesis, they are wandering stars and so not fixed to one place; for on any one side there would always be stars enough, through their shifting, even though others had changed their place.",["- Restated the long second sentence in two; \"Denn\" before \"die gedachte Planeten\" rendered as a plain statement, the causal link being already carried by the preceding \"for\".","- \"Plane ihrer Beziehung\" rendered \"common plane to which they are related\" (the plane of reference); \"Wandelsterne\" = \"wandering stars\".","- \"Naheit zu dem gemeinen Plane\" read as nearness of the planets to the common plane, not to us.","- \"sie ... sind\" supplied \"they\" for the \"es\" of \"es Wandelsterne ... sind\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0087":["The breadth of this illuminated zone, which represents a kind of zodiac, will be determined by the different degrees of deviation of the said wandering stars from the plane to which they are related, and by the inclination of their orbits to that plane. And since most of them are near this plane, their number will appear more scattered in proportion to the distance from it. The comets, however, which occupy all regions without distinction, will cover the field of the heavens on both sides.",["- Divided at the semicolon into separate sentences.","- \"Irrsterne\" = \"wandering stars\", matching \"Wandelsterne\" above.","- \"ihre Anzahl ... zerstreuter erscheinen\": rendered \"their number will appear more scattered\", the sense being that the stars thin out as the distance from the plane grows."]],"naturgeschichte-0088":["The form of the heavens of the fixed stars has therefore no other cause than a systematic constitution on the large scale like that which the planetary structure of the world has on the small. All the suns constitute one system, of which the Milky Way is the general plane of reference. Those which are least related to this plane are seen to one side; but for that very reason they are less crowded, much more scattered and rarer. They are, so to speak, the comets among the suns.",["- Restated the sentence with the semicolon as separate sentences.","- \"Verfassung\" = \"constitution\"; \"Weltbau\" = \"structure of the world\".","- \"die sich am wenigsten ... beziehende\" taken as plural (\"Those which...\") to agree with \"werden ... gesehen\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0089":["This new doctrine, however, ascribes to the suns a progressive motion, and yet everyone recognises them as unmoved and fixed in their places from the beginning. The name which the fixed stars have received on this account seems to be confirmed beyond doubt by the observation of all centuries. This difficulty would destroy the system here propounded, if it were well founded. But in all likelihood this lack of motion is merely apparent. It is due either to an exceptional slowness, arising from the great distance from the common centre of their revolution, or to an imperceptibility occasioned by the distance from the place of observation.\n\nLet us estimate the probability of this view by calculating the motion which a fixed star near to our sun would have, if we supposed our sun to be the centre of its orbit. If its distance, according to Huygens, is taken to be more than 21,000 times greater than the distance of the sun from the earth, then, by the established law of periodic times, which stand in the ratio of the square root of the cube of the distances from the centre, the time it would need to traverse its circle about the sun once is more than a million and a half years; and in 4000 years this would produce a displacement of its place by only one degree.\n\nNow perhaps very few fixed stars are as near to the sun as Huygens conjectured Sirius to be. The distance of the remaining host of heaven perhaps exceeds that of Sirius immensely, and such bodies would therefore require incomparably longer times for a periodic revolution. Moreover it is more probable that the motion of the suns of the starry heavens goes round a common centre, whose distance is exceedingly great, so that the advance of the stars may be extremely slow. From all this it may be inferred with probability that all the time since observations of the heavens were first made is perhaps not yet sufficient to notice the change that has taken place in their positions.\n\nWe must not, however, yet give up the hope of discovering this change in time. Subtle and careful observers are required for it, as also a comparison of observations widely separated from one another. These observations would have to be directed chiefly to the stars of the Milky Way (*), which is the principal plane of all motion. Mr. Bradley has observed almost imperceptible advances of the stars. The ancients noted stars in certain places of the heavens, and we see new ones in others. Who knows whether they were not the former ones, which have merely changed their place? The excellence of our instruments and the perfection of the science of the stars give us well-founded hope of discovering such singular marvels (**). The credibility of the matter itself, on grounds drawn from nature and analogy, supports this hope so well that it may rouse the attention of investigators of nature to bring it to fulfilment.",["- Divided the passage into paragraphs and broke the long \"Da nun ... so läßt sich\" sentence into several; the German is a single period.","- \"Lehrbegriff\" = \"doctrine\"; \"Lehrgebäude\" = \"system\"; \"Fixstern\" kept as \"fixed star\".","- \"(*)\" and \"(**)\" mark the footnotes given separately (0090, 0091).","- \"von mehr als anderthalb Millionen Jahre\": \"more than a million and a half years\".","- \"des letzteren seine\" = \"that of Sirius\" (the latter), resolved by judgment; \"Ausrechnung\" = calculation.","- \"Aufmerker\" rendered \"observers\"; \"Merkwürdigkeiten\" as \"marvels\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0090":["(*) Likewise to those clusters of stars in which many are together in a small space, as, for example, the Pleiades, which perhaps constitute among themselves a small system within the greater.",["- \"Siebengestirn\" = the Pleiades (\"Seven Stars\")."]],"naturgeschichte-0091":["(**) De la Hire remarks in the Memoirs of the Academy at Paris for the year 1693 that he had perceived a considerable change in the positions of the stars of the Pleiades, both from his own observations and from comparing them with those of Riccioli.",["- \"des Ricciolus seinen\" (colloquial possessive) = \"those of Riccioli\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0092":["The Milky Way is also, so to speak, the zodiac of new stars, which are seen to appear and vanish by turns in almost no other region of the heavens than this. If this alternation of their visibility arises from their periodic recession from us and approach to us, it seems clear from the systematic constitution of the heavenly bodies already described that such a phenomenon must for the most part be seen only within the region of the Milky Way. For since these are stars which run in very elongated orbits about other fixed stars, as satellites about their primary planets, analogy with our planetary structure of the world requires that only the stars in the Milky Way will have suns revolving about them. In that structure only the heavenly bodies near the common plane of the motions have satellites circling about them.",["- Restated the final sentence in two, placing the parenthetical relative clause (\"in welchem ...\") after the main clause.","- \"Trabanten\" = \"satellites\"; \"Hauptplaneten\" = \"primary planets\"; \"ablang\" = \"elongated\".","- \"Gestirne\" = \"heavenly bodies\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0093":["I come now to that part of the doctrine propounded which, through the sublime representation it gives of the plan of creation, makes it most attractive. The train of thoughts which led me to it is short and unartificial; it is as follows. Suppose a system of fixed stars, which in their positions are related to a common plane, as we have outlined the Milky Way, is so far distant from us that all distinguishability of the individual stars of which it consists is no longer perceptible even to the telescope. Suppose its distance from the stars of the Milky Way to bear the same ratio as theirs to the distance of the sun from us. In short, suppose such a world of fixed stars to be viewed from so immeasurable a distance by an observer's eye situated outside it. It will then appear, under a small angle, as a little space illumined with faint light, whose figure will be circular if its plane is presented directly to the eye, and elliptical if it is seen from the side. The faintness of the light, the figure, and the discernible size of the diameter will distinguish such a phenomenon, if it exists, quite clearly from all the stars that are seen singly.",["- The German is one long conditional period with four \"wenn\" clauses before the apodosis; I restated it as \"Suppose ...\" sentences and a main statement, without altering the sense.","- \"Sehrohr\" = \"telescope\"; \"Räumchen\" = \"little space\".","- \"Verhältniß ... als diese zum Abstande der Sonne\" taken as: its distance is to that of the Milky Way stars as theirs is to the sun's distance from us.","- \"empfindlich\" rendered \"perceptible\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0094":["One need not search long among the observations of astronomers to find this phenomenon. It has been clearly perceived by various observers. Men have marvelled at its strangeness; they have conjectured, and have given place, at times to fantastic imaginings, at times to plausible concepts which were, however, just as groundless as the former. What we have in mind are the nebulous stars, or rather one kind of them, which M. de Maupertuis describes as follows (*): that they are small patches, somewhat more illuminated than the darkness of empty space, which all agree in presenting the appearance of more or less open ellipses, but whose light is far feebler than any other that is perceived in the heavens. The author of the *Astrotheologie* imagined that they were openings in the firmament, through which he believed he saw the heaven of fire. A philosopher of more enlightened insight, the already cited M. de Maupertuis, regards them, in view of their figure and discernible diameter, as celestial bodies of astonishing size, which, through the great flattening caused by their rotational momentum, present elliptical shapes when seen from the side.",["- Restated nothing; the semicolon sequence in the third sentence is kept, but \"Begriffe\" is rendered \"concepts\" (here in the loose sense of notions or ideas).","- \"Sternkundigen\" rendered \"astronomers\"; \"Drehungsschwung\" as \"rotational momentum\" (a choice; \"impetus of rotation\" is also possible).","- The Maupertuis quotation is rendered as an indirect clause, following the German (the original has no quotation marks, only a colon and a closing \"(*)\" reference). The stray space before the full stop after \"wird\" is an OCR/typographic residue and is dropped.","- \"Astrotheologie\" is left in German as a title (Derham's work in Zittel's German translation)."]],"naturgeschichte-0095":["(*) Treatise on the Figure of the Stars [*Abhandlung von der Figur der Sterne*].",["- Footnote to the preceding passage; title rendered literally, German added in brackets."]],"naturgeschichte-0096":["It is easily seen that this latter explanation also cannot hold. Since this kind of nebulous star must undoubtedly be at least as far from us as the remaining fixed stars, not only would their magnitude be astonishing, by which they would have to exceed even the largest stars many thousands of times, but it would be strangest of all that, with this extraordinary magnitude, being self-luminous bodies and suns, they should show only the dullest and feeblest light.",["- \"es seyn\" (subjunctive in the subordinate clause, \"da es selbstleuchtende Körper und Sonnen seyn\") is rendered as a participial phrase to keep the sentence manageable."]],"naturgeschichte-0097":["It is far more natural and comprehensible that they are not single stars of such great size but *systems* of many stars, whose distance presents them in so narrow a space that the light, which is imperceptible from each of them singly, merges, because of their immeasurable multitude, into a uniform pale glimmer. The analogy with the system of stars in which we find ourselves, their figure, which is exactly what it must be according to our theory [*Lehrbegriff*], the feebleness of the light, which requires a presupposed infinite distance: all this agrees perfectly in leading us to regard these elliptical figures as similar world-orders and, so to speak, Milky Ways, whose constitution we have just set forth. And if conjectures in which analogy and observation agree perfectly in supporting one another have the same worth as formal proofs, then the certainty of these systems will have to be regarded as established.",["- Restated the long second sentence with a colon as in the German; \"Systemata\" in the first sentence I have rendered \"systems\" (Kant uses Latinate plural), and I added italics only because the Latin form stands, not for emphasis.","- \"Lehrbegriff\" rendered \"theory\", with the German in brackets; it denotes Kant's doctrinal scheme.","- \"Weltordnungen\" = \"world-orders\"; \"Verfassung\" = \"constitution\".","- The last clause is slightly irregular in German (\"einander zu unterstützen\" attached to \"Muthmassungen\"); smoothed as above."]],"naturgeschichte-0098":["The attention of observers of the heavens now has motives enough to occupy itself with this subject. The fixed stars, as we know, all relate to a common plane, and thereby constitute an ordered whole, which is a world of worlds. It is seen that at immeasurable distances there are more such systems of stars, and that creation, throughout the whole infinite extent of its magnitude, is everywhere systematic and mutually related.",["- \"Vorwurf\" rendered \"subject\" (object of study).","- \"Plan\" rendered \"plane\" (the common plane of the Milky Way), not \"plan\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0099":["One might conjecture further that these higher world-orders themselves are not without relation to one another, and through this reciprocal relation constitute in turn a still more immeasurable system. Indeed, it is seen that the elliptical figures of these kinds of nebulous stars, which M. de Maupertuis cites, stand in a very close relation to the plane of the Milky Way. A wide field for discoveries lies open here, for which observation must supply the key. The nebulous stars properly so called, and those about which there is dispute whether they should be so named, would have to be investigated and tested under the guidance of this theory [*Lehrbegriff*]. When the parts of nature are considered according to purposes and a discovered design, certain properties disclose themselves which would otherwise be overlooked and remain hidden, if observation, without guidance, scatters itself over all objects.",["- \"Plan\" here again rendered \"plane\" (\"Plan der Milchstrasse\"); \"Entwurf\" rendered \"design\".","- \"Absichten\" rendered \"purposes\"; \"Gegenstände\" as \"objects\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0100":["The theory which we have set forth opens to us a prospect into the infinite field of creation, and offers a representation of the work of God that is befitting the infinity of the great Master-builder. If the magnitude of a planetary world-structure, in which the earth is scarcely noticed as a grain of sand, throws the understanding into amazement, with what astonishment are we enraptured when we regard the infinite multitude of worlds and systems that fill the whole compass of the Milky Way! But how this astonishment increases when we become aware that all these immeasurable orders of stars in turn make only the unit of a number whose end we do not know, and which is perhaps, like the former, incomprehensibly great, and yet again is itself only the unit of a new combination of numbers. We see the first members of a progressive series of relations of worlds and systems, and the first part of this infinite progression already gives us to know what is to be conjectured of the whole. There is here no end, but an abyss of a true immensity, in which all capacity of human concepts sinks, even when it is raised by the aid of mathematics. The wisdom, the goodness, the power that have revealed themselves are infinite, and in the same measure fruitful and active; the plan of their revelation must therefore, like them, be infinite and without bounds.",["- Divided the long third sentence by an exclamation and a following \"But\" to keep the German's climactic structure; the German has only semicolons.","- \"Zahlwissenschaft\" rendered \"mathematics\" (literally \"science of number\"); \"Weltbaues\" as \"world-structure\"; \"Werkmeister\" as \"Master-builder\".","- \"Vorstellung\" = \"representation\"; \"Verstand\" = \"understanding\"; \"Begriffe\" = \"concepts\".","- \"Verhältniß\" is feminine here (\"einer fortschreitenden Verhältniß\"), taken as \"series of relations\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0101":["But it is not only in the great that important discoveries are to be made, serving to enlarge the concept we can form of the magnitude of creation. In the small, no less remains undiscovered, and we see even in our solar world members of a system which stand immeasurably far apart from one another, and between which the intermediate parts have not yet been discovered. Should there not be, between Saturn, the outermost of the planets known to us, and the least eccentric comet, which perhaps descends to us from a distance ten times and more remote, any further planet whose motion approaches the cometary more nearly than Saturn's does? And should there not be still others which, through an approach in their determinations [*Bestimmungen*], by means of a series of intermediate members, gradually transform the planets into comets, and connect the latter kind with the former?",["- \"Wandelsternen\" rendered \"planets\"; \"Planeten\" in the final clause likewise. \"Bestimmungen\" kept as \"determinations\" with German given, since the sense (characteristic properties of orbit) turns on it.","- \"von einer 10 und mehrmal entlegenern Entfernung\" rendered \"from a distance ten times and more remote\"; the German is loose."]],"naturgeschichte-0102":["The law according to which the eccentricity of the planetary orbits stands in relation to their distance from the sun supports this conjecture. The eccentricity in the motions of the planets increases with their distance from the sun, and the remote planets thereby come nearer to the determination [*Bestimmung*] of the comets. It is therefore to be conjectured that there are still other planets beyond Saturn, which are still more eccentric and thus still more closely akin to the comets, and which by means of a continuous ladder finally make the planets into comets. The eccentricity of Venus is 1/126 of the semi-axis of its elliptical orbit; that of the earth 1/58; of Jupiter 1/20; and of Saturn 1/17 of the same. It evidently increases, then, with the distances. It is true that Mercury and Mars make themselves exceptions to this law by an eccentricity much greater than the measure of their distance from the sun allows; but we shall be instructed in what follows that the very same cause, on account of which a smaller mass fell to some planets at their formation, also brought with it the lack of the impetus required for circular motion, and consequently the eccentricity, and has thus left them incomplete in both respects.",["- \"Planetenkreise\" rendered \"planetary orbits\"; \"Cirkellaufe\" as \"circular motion\".","- The figures (1/126, 1/58, 1/20, 1/17) are given as printed; they are Kant's and not corrected.","- \"derselben\" in \"mit derselben Abstande\" refers to the planets; rendered \"their distance\".","- The final sentence is long and chained with \"folglich\"; kept in one sentence, since it divides poorly."]],"naturgeschichte-0103":["Is it not, accordingly, probable that the decrease of eccentricity in the heavenly bodies next beyond Saturn is about as gradual as in the lower ones, and that the planets are related to the family of comets by less abrupt transitions? For it is certain that this very eccentricity constitutes the essential difference between comets and planets, and that the tails and vaporous envelopes of comets are only its consequence. It is likewise certain that the cause, whatever it may be, which imparted to the heavenly bodies their circular motions was at greater distances not only weaker, so that it failed to make the impetus of revolution equal to the force of descent [*Senkungskraft*], and thus left the motions eccentric; but that for this very reason it was also less able to bring the orbits of these bodies into the common plane in which the lower ones move, and thus occasioned the straying of the comets in all directions.",["- Restated as three sentences: the German is a single sentence with a question followed by two \"denn es ist gewiß … imgleichen, daß …\" clauses; I rendered the second as a separate statement of certainty.","- \"Senkungskraft\" rendered \"force of descent\" (the force of falling toward the centre); the German is given in brackets as the rendering is a choice.","- \"Dunstkugeln\" rendered \"vaporous envelopes\"; \"derselben\" (after Schweife und Dunstkugeln) taken as referring to the comets, and \"deren Folge\" to the eccentricity.","- \"Kugeln\" in \"die Kreise dieser Kugeln\" rendered \"bodies\" (heavenly bodies), as the context suggests.","- \"Abfälle\" rendered \"transitions\" (literally \"falls\", i.e. gradations downward)."]],"naturgeschichte-0104":["On this conjecture we might perhaps still hope to discover new planets beyond Saturn, more eccentric than it and therefore nearer to the nature of comets. But for that very reason they could be seen only for a short time, namely during their perihelion [*Sonnennähe*]. This circumstance, together with the slight degree of their approach and the feebleness of their light, has hitherto prevented their discovery, and must make it difficult in the future also. The last planet and first comet might, if it were so desired, be entitled that body whose eccentricity was so great that at its perihelion it would cut the orbit of the planet next to it, perhaps therefore that of Saturn.",["- Restated the long second sentence as three sentences.","- \"Sonnennähe\" rendered \"perihelion\" (literally \"nearness to the sun\").","- \"desselben\" in \"die Entdeckung desselben\" is singular in the German; I rendered it \"their discovery\" as the sense requires the plural of new planets.","- \"derjenige können genannt werden\" is an irregular word order; rendered \"might … be entitled\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0105":["GENERAL NATURAL HISTORY\nAND\nTHEORY OF THE HEAVENS.\nSecond Part,\nconcerning\nthe first state of nature, the formation\nof the heavenly bodies, the causes of their motion,\nand their systematic relation, both\nin the planetary system in particular and\nwith regard to the whole\ncreation.",["- Title page; \"Weltgebäude\"-type vocabulary does not occur here. \"Planetengebäude\" rendered \"planetary system\", \"Schöpfung\" \"creation\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0106":["See formative nature move toward her great end: each mote of the sun's dust stir toward another mote; each, as it is drawn, draw the other in turn to itself, and strive to embrace the next, to form it. Behold matter in a thousand ways press toward the universal centre.",["- Verse epigraph (a German rendering of Pope); line breaks are not preserved in the source text and I have not restored them. \"Sonnenstäubchen\" rendered \"mote of the sun's dust\" (dust-motes in a sunbeam).","- \"es zu formiren\" taken as \"to form it\", with \"es\" the next mote."]],"naturgeschichte-0107":["Pope.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0108":["GENERAL NATURAL HISTORY\nAND\nTHEORY OF THE HEAVENS.\nSecond Part,",["- Running half-title; the sentence is broken off, as in the German."]],"naturgeschichte-0109":["First Chapter,\nconcerning\nthe origin of the planetary system in general,\nand the causes of its motions.",["- \"Weltbaues\" rendered \"system\" here to avoid repetition; \"ihrer Bewegungen\" has \"Weltbaues\" (masculine) not as antecedent in strict grammar (the feminine \"ihrer\" points to the planets); I have rendered \"its\", following the sense."]],"naturgeschichte-0110":["The consideration of the structure of the universe [*Weltbau*] presents two aspects, equally probable and equally deserving of acceptance, in respect of the reciprocal relations which its parts have to one another, and by which they indicate the cause from which they spring. On the one hand, we may consider that six planets with nine satellites describe circles about the sun as their centre, and all move in one direction, namely that in which the sun itself rotates, the sun governing all their revolutions by the force of attraction; that their orbits deviate little from a common plane, namely the extended equatorial plane of the sun; and that in the most distant of the bodies belonging to the solar system, where the common cause of motion was presumably not so powerful as in the neighbourhood of the centre, there are departures from the exactness of these determinations [*Bestimmungen*], which stand in adequate proportion to the deficiency of the impressed motion. When, I say, we consider all this connection, we are led to believe that a cause, whatever it may be, has had a pervasive influence throughout the whole space of the system, and that the harmony in the direction and position of the planetary orbits is a consequence of the agreement which they must all have had with the material cause by which they were set in motion.",["- The long \"Wenn man eines Theils erweget … so wird man bewogen\" sentence is kept as one in its main structure, since the resumptive \"wenn man, sage ich\" depends on it; the first sentence is separated from it.","- \"Weltbaues\" rendered \"structure of the universe\" with the German given; later \"planetischen Kreise\" rendered \"planetary orbits\".","- \"Naheit zum Mittelpuncte\" rendered \"neighbourhood of the centre\".","- \"Bestimmungen\" rendered \"determinations\" (the regularities listed); \"gemeine\" rendered \"common\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0111":["On the other hand, if we consider the space in which the planets of our system revolve, it is perfectly empty (*) and bereft of all matter that could produce a community of influence upon these heavenly bodies and bring about the agreement among their motions. This circumstance is established with complete certainty, and, if possible, surpasses even the foregoing probability. Newton, moved by this ground, could admit no material cause which by its extension through the space of the planetary system should maintain the community of motions. He maintained that the immediate hand of God had brought about this arrangement without employing the forces of nature.",["- The footnote marker (*) is kept in place.","- \"Planetengebäude\" rendered \"planetary system\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0112":["(*) I do not here inquire whether this space can be called empty in the strictest sense. For it suffices here to remark that all matter which may perhaps be met with in this space is far too feeble to exert any effect upon the moving masses with which the question is concerned.",["- \"in dem allereigentlichsten Verstande\" rendered \"in the strictest sense\"; \"Verstande\" here is ordinary \"sense\", not the faculty of understanding."]],"naturgeschichte-0113":["On impartial consideration we see that the grounds on both sides are equally strong, and that each is to be accounted equal to complete certainty. But it is equally clear that there must be a concept [*Begriff*] in which these grounds, apparently in conflict with one another, can and should be reconciled, and that the true system is to be sought in this concept. We shall indicate it in a few words. In the present constitution of the space in which the bodies of the whole planetary world revolve, there is no material cause present which could impress or direct their motions. This space is perfectly empty, or at least as good as empty; it must therefore formerly have been otherwise constituted, and filled with matter sufficiently powerful to transmit motion to all the heavenly bodies in it, and to make them accord with its own motion, and consequently all accord with one another. And after attraction had cleared the said spaces and gathered all the diffused matter into separate masses, the planets must now continue their revolutions freely and unchanged, with the motion once impressed, in a space that offers no resistance. The grounds of the probability first adduced require this concept absolutely; and since between the two cases no third is possible, it may be regarded with a superior kind of assent, which raises it above the mere plausibility of a hypothesis. If one wished to be prolix, one might arrive, by a series of inferences deduced from one another, after the manner of a mathematical method, with all the pomp that this carries with it, and with a greater semblance than its parade commonly has in physical matters, at last at the very scheme which I shall set forth concerning the origin of the universe. But I prefer to present my opinions in the form of a hypothesis and to leave it to the reader's insight to test their worth, rather than make their validity suspect by the semblance of a surreptitious conviction, and, while I win over the ignorant, lose the approval of the connoisseurs.",["- Divided the German \"Dieser Raum … fortsetzen\" at \"und nachdem\", where the construction is irregular (\"nachdem … so müssen\"); the \"And after …\" sentence is smoothed accordingly.","- \"mit der ihrigen\" taken as \"with its own motion\" (the matter's), not the bodies'; this is a judgment of the argument.","- \"Beyfall\" rendered \"assent\"; \"Ueberführung\" rendered \"conviction\"; \"erschlichen\" \"surreptitious\"; \"Kenner\" \"connoisseurs\".","- \"Weltgebäudes\" rendered \"universe\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0114":["I assume that all the materials of which the globes belonging to our solar world, all the planets and comets, are composed, were at the beginning of all things resolved into their elementary primary stuff, and filled the whole space of the universe in which these formed bodies now revolve. This state of nature, if it be considered in and for itself, even without regard to any system, appears to be simply the most simple that can follow upon nothing. At that time nothing had yet been formed. The composition of celestial bodies standing apart from one another, their distances moderated in accordance with the attractions, and their figure, which arises from the equilibrium of the matter collected together, belong to a later state. Nature, which bordered immediately upon the creation, was as crude and as unformed as possible. Yet even in the essential properties of the elements which constitute the chaos there is to be traced the mark of that perfection which they have from their origin, since their essence is a consequence of the eternal idea of the divine understanding. The simplest and most general properties, which seem to have been designed without purpose, and matter, which seems to be merely passive and in need of forms and arrangements, have in their simplest state a striving to form themselves, by a natural development, into a more perfect constitution.\n\nBut the diversity in the kinds of elements contributes most to the stirring of nature and to the formation of the chaos. By it the repose which would prevail, given a general equality among the scattered elements, is removed, and the chaos begins to form itself at the points where the more strongly attracting particles lie. The kinds of this primary stuff are, without doubt, infinitely various, in keeping with the immensity which nature displays on all sides. Those of greatest specific density and force of attraction, which occupy less space in and for themselves and are also rarer, will therefore, given an equal distribution throughout the space of the world, be more widely scattered than the lighter kinds. Elements of a specific weight a thousand times greater are a thousand times, perhaps even millions of times, more widely scattered than those which are lighter in that proportion. And since these gradations must be thought of as infinite as possible, it follows that there may be corporeal constituents of one kind which exceed another in density in the proportion in which a sphere described with the radius of the planetary system exceeds one that has a diameter of the thousandth part of a line. In the same way, that kind of scattered elements will be removed from one another by a distance greater, in the same proportion, than that of the other kind.",["- Restated the final sentence of 0114 as two sentences; the German comparison (*gleichwie … also*) is clumsy, and I kept its sense without adding to it.","- \"inden Gattungen\" is an OCR slip, corrected to \"in den Gattungen\"; I begin a new paragraph at \"Allein die Verschiedenheit\", which is my own division.","- \"Anstalten\" rendered \"arrangements\"; \"Gattungen\" rendered \"kinds\"; \"Dichtigkeit\" as \"density\"; \"Verfassung\" as \"constitution\".","- \"Die einfachsten … Eigenschaften … die Materie\" is a loose construction; I treated both as joint subjects of \"hat\" (singular in German) and made the verb plural in English.","- \"Abfälle\" rendered \"gradations\" (steps of descent in density); this is a judgment of the sense.","- \"als wodurch\" in the second sentence, resolved as \"By it\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0115":["In a space filled in this manner the general repose lasts but a moment. The elements have essential forces to set one another in motion, and are themselves a source [*Qvelle*] of life. Matter is at once striving to form itself. The scattered elements of the denser kind, by means of attraction, collect from a sphere round about them all matter of less specific weight. They themselves, however, together with the matter which they have united with themselves, collect at the points where the particles of a still denser kind are found; these again in like manner collect to still denser ones, and so on. If, then, one follows this self-forming nature in thought through the whole space of the chaos, one readily perceives that all the consequences of this action would in the end consist in the composition of various lumps, which, once their formation was accomplished, would be at rest through the equality of attraction, and would remain for ever unmoved.",["- \"diese gleichergestalt zu noch dichteren\" is elliptical (the antecedent is the denser elements collecting in turn at denser points); I supplied \"collect\" and \"again\" to complete the sense.","- Retained the sequence of 0115 in a single paragraph, splitting only the German semicolon-chain by punctuation.","- \"Qvelle\" is the old spelling of *Quelle*."]],"naturgeschichte-0116":["But nature has still other forces in store, which manifest themselves chiefly when matter is resolved into fine particles. By these the particles repel one another, and through their conflict with attraction produce that motion which is, as it were, the enduring life of nature. Through this force of repulsion, which reveals itself in the elasticity of vapours, the efflux of strong-smelling bodies and the diffusion of all spirituous matters, and which is an indisputable phenomenon of nature, the elements sinking towards their points of attraction are turned aside from rectilinear motion, each by the others, and the perpendicular fall issues in circular motions which encompass the centre of the descent. In order to comprehend clearly the formation of the structure of the world, we shall confine our consideration from the infinite totality of nature to a particular system, such as that belonging to our sun. When we have considered its generation, we shall be able to proceed in a similar manner to the origin of the higher world-orders, and to comprehend the infinity of the whole creation in one theory.",["- \"als wodurch selbige\" resolved as \"By these the particles\", the antecedent of *selbige* being the fine particles; the sentence is thereby made independent.","- \"durcheinander … seitwärts gelenket\" rendered \"each by the others … turned aside\"; *durcheinander* is ambiguous, and this reading is a judgment.","- \"Lehrbegriff\" rendered \"theory\" (more literally \"doctrinal system\"); \"Senkung\" rendered \"descent\"; \"Zurückstossungskraft\" as \"force of repulsion\".","- The passage breaks off nowhere; the text appears sound."]],"naturgeschichte-0117":["If, then, a point is situated in a very large space, where the attraction of the elements found there is stronger than it is everywhere around, the primary matter of elementary particles, which is spread throughout the whole extent, will sink towards this point. The first effect of this general sinking is the formation of a body at this centre of attraction. This body grows, so to speak, from an infinitely small germ, and by rapid degrees; but in the same measure as its mass increases, it also moves the surrounding parts with greater force to unite with it.\n\nSuppose the mass of this central body has grown so large that the velocity with which it draws the particles to itself from great distances is bent aside, by the feeble degrees of repulsion with which the particles hinder one another, and issues in lateral motions. These are able, by means of the centrifugal force, to encircle the central body. In that case great vortices of particles are generated. Each particle describes for itself curved lines through the composition of the attractive force and the laterally directed force of revolution. Circles of this kind all intersect one another, so far as their wide dispersion in this space leaves them room to do so.\n\nMeanwhile these motions, which conflict with one another in many ways, naturally strive to bring one another to uniformity; that is, to a state in which one motion hinders another as little as possible. This takes place, first, in that the particles limit one another's motion until all proceed in one direction. It takes place, secondly, in that the particles limit their vertical motion, by means of which they approach the centre of attraction, until all are moved horizontally, that is, in parallel circles about the sun as their centre. They then no longer cross one another, and through the equality of the force of revolution with the sinking force they maintain themselves permanently in free circular courses at the height at which they float. The final result is that only those particles remain floating in the extent of the space which have acquired, through their fall, a velocity, and through the resistance of the others, a direction, by which they can continue a free circular motion.\n\nIn this state, in which all the particles run about the central body in one direction and in parallel circles, that is, in free circular motions through the forces of revolution they have acquired, the conflict and the convergence of the elements is removed, and everything is in the state of least reciprocal action. This is the natural consequence into which any matter involved in conflicting motions always puts itself.\n\nIt is therefore clear that, of the scattered multitude of particles, a great number must, through the resistance by which they seek to bring one another into this state, arrive at such exactness of determination. A far greater number, however, does not arrive at it, and serves only to increase the mass of the central body. These particles sink into it because they cannot maintain themselves freely at the height at which they float, but cross the circles of the lower particles and finally lose all motion through their resistance.\n\nThis body at the centre of attraction, which in consequence has become, through the amount of matter gathered in it, the principal member of the planetary structure, is the sun. It does not yet, however, possess the flaming glow which breaks forth on its surface once its formation is fully completed.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0118":["It is further to be remarked that, since all the elements of nature in process of formation move, as has been shown, in one direction about the centre of the sun, the revolution of the fine matter cannot persist in this manner, in circuits all directed to one quarter and occurring, as it were, about a common axis. For by the laws of central motion all revolutions must, with the planes of their circles, cut through the centre of attraction. But among all these circles, which run in one direction about a common axis, there is only one that cuts through the centre of the sun. Consequently all the matter on both sides of this axis, imagined as drawn, hastens towards the circle which passes, through the axis of rotation, exactly through the centre of the common sinking. This circle is the plane of reference [*Plan der Beziehung*] of all the elements floating about. Around it they gather as closely as possible, and they leave empty the regions remote from this plane. For those which cannot come so near to this plane, towards which everything presses, will not be able to maintain themselves permanently in the places where they float. By striking against the elements floating about, they will cause their final fall to the sun.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0119":["If, then, we consider this floating primary matter of the world-material in the state into which it puts itself, by attraction and by a mechanical outcome of the general laws of resistance, we see a space extended from the centre of the sun to unknown distances. It is bounded by two surfaces not far apart, and in the middle between them lies the general plane of reference. In this space all the particles contained in it, each according to its height and the attraction prevailing there, perform measured circular motions in free revolutions. Since in this arrangement they hinder one another as little as possible, they would remain in it always, if the attraction of these particles of the primary matter upon one another did not then begin to take effect, and thereby give occasion to new formations, which are to be the seed of the planets that are to arise.\n\nFor the elements moved about the sun in parallel circles, taken at no very great difference of distance from the sun, are, through the equality of the parallel motion, almost in relative rest towards one another. Hence the attraction of the elements found there, being of superior specific attraction, at once produces a considerable effect (*) in beginning the collection of the nearest particles for the formation of a body. As its mass grows, this body extends its attraction further, and moves the elements from a wide circuit to its composition.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0120":["(*) The beginning of the planets in formation is not to be sought in Newtonian attraction alone. In the case of particles of such exceeding fineness this would be far too slow and weak. One would rather say that the first formation in this space takes place through the convergence of some elements, which unite by the ordinary laws of cohesion, until the lump so arising has gradually grown so far that the Newtonian force of attraction has become capable in it of enlarging it ever more by its action at a distance.",["- 0117: I divided the long sentences, especially the one beginning \"Wenn die Masse…\", into several, and supplied \"Suppose\" and \"In that case\" for the construction (the German is a loose, irregular conditional). The clause \"durch die schwachen Grade der Zurückstossung … seitwärts gebeuget\" is ambiguous; I read the bending as applying to the velocity.","- 0117: *Grundstoff* = \"primary matter\"; *Centerfliehkraft* = \"centrifugal force\"; *Schwungskraft* / *Umwendungskraft* = \"force of revolution\" (the same English for both, which are probably not strictly distinct); *senkende* [Kraft] = \"sinking force\".","- 0117: \"Bestimmungen\" is rendered \"determination\", as the sense is the exact fixing of motions. The text \"worden ist\" is elliptical for \"geworden ist\"; I translated it as \"has become\".","- 0118: The sentence is a tangle of \"weil … daher … denn\" clauses; I divided it and made the logic explicit (\"For by the laws…\", \"Consequently\"). *Plan der Beziehung* is kept in German in brackets because it is a technical term of the theory.","- 0119: The sentence \"Denn, indem …\" is restated in two sentences. \"Specific attraction\" is literal for *specifische Attraction*; \"relative rest\" is for *respectiver Ruhe*. \"Saame\" is rendered \"seed\".","- 0120: The footnote is marked (*) and belongs to the passage 0119 (reference in AA at 267); I kept it as a footnote. \"Zusammenhang\" is rendered \"cohesion\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0121":["The formation of the planets in this system has this advantage over every other possible theory: that the origin of the masses presents at the same moment the origin of the motions and the position of the orbits; and, further, that the deviations from the greatest exactness in these determinations become evident at a glance, no less than the agreements themselves. The planets are formed from particles which, at the height at which they float, have exact motions in circular orbits. The masses composed of them will therefore continue the same motions, in the same degree, and in the same direction. This is enough to show why the motion of the planets is approximately circular, and why their orbits lie in one plane. They would also be quite exact circles (**) if the distance from which they gather the elements for their formation were very small, and the difference of their motions therefore very slight. But a wide extent is required for forming a dense planetary mass out of the fine primary matter, which is so widely dispersed in the heavenly spaces. The difference in the distances of these elements from the sun, and consequently also the difference in their velocities, is therefore no longer negligible. Hence it would be necessary, if the planet is to retain, despite this difference of motions, equality of the central forces and circular velocity, that the particles which come together upon it from different heights with different motions should each exactly make good the deficiency of the others. Although this does in fact take place fairly exactly (*), yet, since something is lacking in this perfect compensation, it brings with it a falling short of circular motion, and eccentricity. It is just as easy to see that, although the orbits of all the planets should properly lie in one plane, a slight deviation is yet to be met with in this respect also. For, as already mentioned, the elementary particles, though they lie as near as possible to the general plane of reference of their motions, still occupy some space on both sides of it. It would then be altogether too fortunate a chance if all the planets were to begin to form exactly in the middle between these two sides, in the plane of reference itself. This already occasions some inclination of their orbits to one another, although the striving of the particles on both sides to restrict this deviation as far as possible allows it only narrow limits. We need not wonder, then, that here too the greatest exactness of determinations is as little to be found as in all things of nature, since in general the multiplicity of circumstances which share in any natural condition does not permit a measured regularity.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0122":["(**) This measured circular motion properly concerns only the planets near the sun. For as to the great distances at which the outermost planets, or the comets as well, have been formed, it is easy to conjecture the following. The sinking motion of the primary matter is much weaker there, and the expanse of the spaces over which it is dispersed is also greater. The elements there must therefore already of themselves deviate from circular motion, and thereby be the cause of the bodies formed from them [deviating likewise].",[]],"naturgeschichte-0123":["(*) For the particles from the region nearer the sun, which have a greater velocity of revolution than is required for circular motion at the place where they gather on the planet, make good what is lacking in velocity to those particles, coming from regions more distant from the sun and incorporated into the same body, for moving in a circle at the planet's distance.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0124":["Second Chapter,\nOn the\nDifferent Density of the Planets,\nand the Proportion of their\nMasses.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0125":["We have shown that the particles of the elementary primary matter, which were originally equally distributed in cosmic space, remained suspended in their descent towards the sun at those places where the velocity acquired in falling exactly balanced the attraction, and where their direction was bent, as it must be in circular motion, perpendicular to the radius of the circle. But if we now suppose particles of different specific density at an equal distance from the sun, those of greater specific weight press deeper towards the sun through the resistance of the others, and are not so soon turned aside from their path as the lighter. Their motion therefore becomes circular only at a nearer approach to the sun. The elements of lighter kind, on the contrary, are turned aside sooner from the rectilinear fall and pass over into circular motions before they have penetrated so deeply towards the centre, and so remain suspended at greater distances. Nor can they penetrate so deeply through the space filled with elements without having their motion weakened by the resistance of these, so that they cannot acquire the high degrees of velocity required for revolution nearer the centre. Hence, when the equality of motions has been attained, the specifically lighter particles will revolve at greater distances from the sun, and the heavier will be found at the nearer. The planets that form from them will therefore be of denser kind when they are formed nearer the sun than those that take shape farther from it out of the concourse of these atoms.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0126":["There is thus a kind of statical law which determines the heights of the matters in cosmic space in inverse proportion to their density. Yet it is just as easy to comprehend that not every height need be occupied solely by particles of equal specific density. Of the particles of a given specific kind, those which have sunk down to it from greater distances remain suspended at greater distances from the sun, and attain at the wider distance the moderation of their fall required for constant circular motion. Those, on the other hand, whose original place in the general distribution of matters in chaos was nearer the sun will, despite their no greater density, come nearer to the sun in the circle of their revolution. Since, then, the places of the matters with respect to the centre of their sinking are determined not by their specific weight alone but also by their original places at the first repose of nature, it is easy to see that very different kinds will come together at any distance from the sun, to remain suspended there. In general, however, the denser matters will be found more abundantly towards the centre than farther from it. Thus, although the planets will be a mixture of very different matters, their masses must on the whole be denser in proportion as they are nearer the sun, and of less density the greater their distance.",["- 0121: The long sentences are divided into several; \"Da aber dazu ein weiter Umfang gehöret…\" and the \"würde nöthig seyn\" clause are restated as separate sentences.","- 0121: \"Stellung der Kreise\" rendered \"position of the orbits\"; \"Beziehungsplane\" as \"plane of reference\"; \"Bestimmungen\" as \"determinations\".","- 0121: \"Zirkelkreise\" is rendered \"circular orbits\"; \"Kreise\" as \"orbits\".","- 0122: The last clause is elliptical in the German (\"die Ursache der daraus gebildeten Körper seyn müssen\"); I supplied \"[deviating likewise]\" to complete the sense, and the sentence is divided.","- 0123: The final clause (\"um … zirkelförmig zu laufen\") is awkward in the German; I read it as stating what the slower particles lack and smoothed the construction.","- 0125: \"Zirkelstrahl\" rendered \"radius of the circle\"; \"Gleichheit der Bewegungen\" as \"equality of motions\"; \"Widerstand\" as \"resistance\".","- 0126: \"Gattung\" rendered \"kind\"; \"Mässigung ihres Falles\" as \"moderation of their fall\"; \"Ohngefehr\" as \"chance\" in 0121. The 0126 sentence on particles of a given kind is obscure in the German and is kept close to the text."]],"naturgeschichte-0127":["In respect of this law of densities prevailing among the planets, our system displays a pre-eminent perfection over all the conceptions that have been formed, or could yet be formed, of its cause. Newton, who had determined by calculation the density of some of the planets, believed that he found the cause of their ratio, which is adjusted according to distance, in the fitness of God's choice and in the motives of His final purpose. For the planets nearer the sun have to endure more heat from it, and the more distant have to make do with fewer degrees of warmth; and this does not seem possible unless the planets near the sun are composed of denser matter and the more distant of lighter. But it does not require much reflection to see the insufficiency of such an explanation. A planet, e.g. our earth, is composed of kinds of matter very widely different from one another. Among these it was indeed necessary that the lighter, which are more penetrated and moved by the sun's uniform action, and whose composition stands in a relation to the warmth with which its rays act, should be spread out on the surface. But that the mixture of the remaining materials, in the whole of the mass, must have this relation, does not follow from this at all, since the sun has no effect whatever on the interior of the planets. Newton feared that if the earth were sunk in the sun's rays to the neighbourhood of Mercury, it might burn like a comet, and that its matter would not have sufficient resistance to fire to escape being dissipated by this heat. But how much more must the sun's own matter, which is yet four times lighter than that of which the earth consists, be destroyed by this glow! Or why is the moon twice as dense as the earth, when it floats at the very same distance from the sun? One cannot, therefore, ascribe the proportioned densities to the relation to the sun's heat without involving oneself in the greatest contradictions. Rather, one sees that a cause which distributes the places of the planets according to the density of their mass must have had reference to the interior of their matter and not to their surface. Notwithstanding this consequence, which it determined, it must still permit a diversity of matter in one and the same heavenly body, and fix this relation of density only in the whole of the composite. Whether any other statical law than that which is set forth in our theoretical scheme can satisfy all this, I leave to the reader's insight to judge.",["- Restated the long second sentence (Newton's reasoning) as two sentences, turning the \"weil … welches\" clause into a semicolon-free explanation; no content added.","- \"Lehrverfassung\" rendered \"theoretical scheme\", and \"Lehrbegriff\" (in the next passage) \"theory\", since Kant refers to his own doctrinal system.","- \"Zusammensatz\" rendered \"composition\" in the first occurrence and \"composite\" in the last; the German is the same word.","- \"Gleiche Wirkung\" taken as \"uniform action\"; \"Feuerbeständigkeit\" as \"resistance to fire\". \"Befurchte\" is read as \"befürchtete\".","- Kant's \"Verhältniß … nach dem Abstande eingerichtet\" is rendered \"ratio … adjusted according to distance\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0128":["The ratio among the densities of the planets carries with it a further circumstance which, through complete agreement with the explanation previously sketched, confirms the correctness of our theory. The heavenly body which stands at the centre of other spheres revolving about it is commonly of lighter kind than the body which revolves nearest about it. The earth in relation to the moon, and the sun in relation to the earth, show such a ratio of their densities. According to the plan which we have set forth, such a constitution is necessary. For the lower [i.e. nearer] planets were formed chiefly from the selected portion of the elementary materials which, by superiority of their density, were able to penetrate to such nearness to the centre with the required degree of velocity. The body at the centre itself, on the other hand, was heaped together, without distinction, out of the materials of all existing kinds that had not attained their regular motions. Since among these the lighter materials make up the greatest part, it is easy to see that the substance of the former will be of denser kind than that of the latter. For the nearest heavenly body, or bodies, revolving about the centre comprise, as it were, a separation of the denser sorts, whereas the central body contains a mixture of all, without distinction. In fact the moon is twice as dense as the earth, and the earth four times as dense as the sun, which, in all probability, will be exceeded in still higher degrees of density by the yet lower planets, Venus and Mercury.",["- The original is one very irregular period (\"Denn, da … dagegen … daß, weil …\"). I divided it into several sentences and moved the conclusion (\"jenes … dichterer Art … seyn werde\") after the grounds, to gain smoothness; the sense is unchanged.","- \"Untern\" and \"tieferen\" planets are those nearer the centre; I added \"[i.e. nearer]\" at the first occurrence.","- \"Ausschuss\" rendered \"selected portion\"; \"Aussonderung\" as \"separation\".","- \"Jenes\" and \"dieser\" in the German refer to the revolving body and the central body respectively; I have said so plainly (\"the former\", \"the latter\")."]],"naturgeschichte-0129":["Our attention now turns to the ratio which the masses of the heavenly bodies should have, according to our theory, in comparison with their distances, in order that we may test the result of our system against the unerring calculations of Newton. It needs few words to make comprehensible that the central body must always be the principal part of its system. Consequently the sun must be pre-eminently greater in mass than all the planets together; and the same will hold of Jupiter in respect of its secondary planets, and of Saturn in respect of its own. The central body is formed from the sediment of all the particles, from the whole extent of its sphere of attraction, which could not acquire the most exact determination of circular motion and the close relation to the common plane; and of these there must undoubtedly be an uncommonly greater quantity than of the latter. To apply this consideration chiefly to the sun: if one wishes to estimate the breadth of the space by which the particles revolving in circles, which served as the primary stuff for the planets, deviated farthest from the common plane, one may take it as roughly somewhat greater than the breadth of the greatest deviation of the planetary orbits from one another. Now, as they stray from the common plane to both sides, their greatest inclination to one another amounts to barely 7½ degrees. Accordingly we can represent all the matter out of which the planets have formed themselves as having been spread through the space which was comprised, from the centre of the sun, between two planes enclosing an angle of 7½ degrees. Now a zone of 7½ degrees breadth, running in the direction of a great circle, is somewhat more than the 17th part of the spherical surface; hence the solid space between the two planes which cut out the spherical space of the breadth of the angle mentioned is somewhat more than the 17th part of the solid content of the whole sphere. According to this hypothesis, then, all the matter employed in the formation of the planets would amount to about the seventeenth part of the matter which the sun has gathered, from both sides, for its composition, from the same distance at which the outermost planet stands. But this central body has a superiority of mass over the total content of all the planets which is not as 17 : 1, but, as Newton's calculation determines it, as 650 to 1. Yet it is also easy to see that the sun's mass must attain this pre-eminent magnitude for the following reasons. In the upper regions beyond Saturn, planetary formations either cease or are at least rare, and only a few cometary bodies have formed. There, above all, the motions of the primary stuff, not being so apt to attain the regular equality of the central forces as in the region near the centre, issue only in an almost universal sinking towards the centre, and so increase the sun with all the matter from spaces so widely extended.",["- The final sentence breaks down in the German: \"daß, sage ich, aus diesen Ursachen … müsse\" resumes \"es ist auch leicht einzusehen, daß\". I restated it as a principal clause followed by a sentence giving the reasons, as Kant's \"sage ich\" suggests.","- \"Der letzteren\" (the latter) most plausibly refers to the particles that attained circular motion and the common plane (the planetary matter); I have kept \"the latter\" and note that the antecedent is slightly loose in the German.","- \"Hauptstück\" rendered \"principal part\"; \"Niedersatz\" as \"sediment\"; \"Grundstoff\" as \"primary stuff\".","- \"Anziehungssphäre\" is \"sphere of attraction\"; \"Zone … 7½ Grad Breite\" kept as given, and the figures (17, 650) are carried over unchanged.","- \"Centralkräfte\" and \"gesetzmäßige Gleichheit\" are rendered literally, \"central forces\" and \"regular equality\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0130":["In order to compare the planets with one another in respect of their masses, we observe, first, that, according to the mode of formation indicated, the quantity of matter which enters into the composition of a planet depends chiefly upon the extent of its distance from the sun. There are three grounds for this. (1) The sun, by its attraction, restricts the sphere of attraction of a planet; but, other circumstances being equal, it restricts that of the more distant planet less narrowly than that of the near one. (2) The circles from which all the particles have come together to constitute a planet are described with a greater radius, and therefore contain more primitive material, than the smaller circles. (3) For this same last reason, the breadth between the two planes of greatest deviation, for an equal number of degrees, is greater at great distances than at small ones.\n\nAgainst this, the advantage which the more distant planets have over the lower is, it is true, limited by the fact that the particles nearer the sun will be of a denser kind, and in all likelihood also less scattered than at a greater distance. But it is easy to see that the former advantages, in respect of the formation of great masses, far outweigh these latter limitations, and that, on the whole, the planets which form themselves at a wide distance from the sun must acquire greater masses than those which are near it.\n\nThis holds, then, only so far as we represent to ourselves the formation of a planet as taking place in the presence of the sun alone. If, however, we allow several planets to form at different distances, then one will restrict the range of the other's attraction by its own sphere of attraction, and this brings about an exception to the foregoing law. For a planet which is near another of exceptional mass will lose a great deal of the sphere of its formation, and will thereby become much smaller than the ratio of its distance from the sun alone requires.\n\nAlthough, therefore, the planets as a whole are of greater mass the farther they are removed from the sun (as Saturn and Jupiter, the two principal members of our system, are the largest precisely because they are the most distant from the sun), there are nevertheless deviations from this analogy. In these, however, the mark of the general mode of formation which we assert of the heavenly bodies always shines forth: namely, that a planet of exceptional size deprives the nearest planets on both sides of the mass which, on account of their distance from the sun, would be due to them, in that it appropriates to itself a part of the materials which should have gone to their formation.\n\nIn fact Mars, which by virtue of its position should be larger than the earth, has suffered a loss of mass through the attractive force of so great a Jupiter near it. Saturn himself, although by his height he has an advantage over Mars, has not been wholly spared a considerable loss through Jupiter's attraction. And it seems to me that Mercury owes the exceptional smallness of his mass not only to the attraction of the mighty sun so near him, but also to the neighbourhood of Venus, which, if one compares its presumable density with its size, must be a planet of considerable mass.",["- Restated the opening sentence and the three numbered grounds as separate sentences; the long final sentence was likewise divided. \"Planet\" supplied in brackets-free form where \"der entfernteren ihre\" refers to the planet's sphere.","- \"Zirkel\" rendered \"circles\" and \"Grundstoff\" \"primitive material\"; \"Flächen der grössesten Abweichung\" = \"planes of greatest deviation\" (the planes bounding the zone of the planets' departure from the ecliptic).","- The sentence \"daß nemlich ein Planet … beraubet\" is grammatically irregular (the verb is in the indicative, \"beraubet\", after \"daß\"); I smoothed it. \"ihnen … gebührenden Masse\" taken as the mass due to the neighbouring planets.","- \"Zusammensatz\" = \"composition\"; \"Bildungsart\" = \"mode of formation\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0131":["Since everything thus accords, as admirably as one could wish, in confirming the adequacy of a mechanical theory for the origin of the world-structure and of the heavenly bodies, let us now estimate the space in which the primitive material of the planets was spread out before their formation. Let us consider in what degree of tenuity this intermediate space was then filled, and with what freedom, or with how few hindrances, the particles floating about in it could carry out their lawful motions.\n\nSuppose that the space which contained all the matter of the planets was enclosed in that part of the Saturnian sphere which, measured from the centre of the sun, lay between two planes standing 7 degrees apart at all heights, and which was therefore the seventeenth part of the whole sphere that can be described with the radius of Saturn's height. In order to calculate the change in the planetary primitive material while it filled this space, let us take the height of Saturn as 100,000 earth-diameters. Then the whole sphere of the Saturnian circle will exceed the volume of the terrestrial globe 1000 *bimillion* times. If, instead of the seventeenth part, we take even only the twentieth, the space in which the elementary primitive material floated must still exceed the volume of the terrestrial globe 50 *bimillion* times.\n\nNow if, following Newton, the mass of all the planets with their satellites is put at 1/650 of the mass of the sun, then the earth, which is only 1/169282 of the sun's mass [*sic*: \"derselben\" in the German], will stand to the total mass of all planetary matter as 1 to 276½. If, therefore, all this matter were brought to the same specific density as the earth, there would result a body occupying a space 277½ times greater than the earth. Now if we assume the density of the earth, taken as a whole mass, not to be much greater than the density of the solid matter found beneath its uppermost surface (as the properties of the figure of the earth in fact require), and if we put this upper matter at about 4 or 5 times denser than water, and water at 1000 times heavier than air, then the matter of all the planets, if extended to the tenuity of air, would occupy a space almost 14 hundred-thousand times greater than the terrestrial globe.\n\nCompared with the space in which, according to our presupposition, all the matter of the planets was spread out, this space is thirty million times smaller than it. Consequently the dispersion of the planetary matter in that space constitutes a rarefaction just so many times greater than that which the particles of our atmosphere have.\n\nIn truth, this degree of dispersion, however incredible it may seem, was neither unnecessary nor unnatural. It had to be as great as possible, in order to allow the floating particles all freedom of motion, almost as in an empty space, and to diminish infinitely the resistance which they could offer one another. But they could also of themselves assume such a state of tenuity. Of this one need have no doubt, if one has some acquaintance with the expansion which matter undergoes when it is turned into vapours; or if, to remain with the heavens, one considers the rarefaction of matter in the tails of comets. With so unheard-of a thickness of cross-section, which well exceeds the earth's diameter a hundredfold, they are nevertheless so transparent that the small stars can be seen through them; and this our air, when illuminated by the sun, does not permit at a height many thousand times smaller.",["- Divided the very long sentences into several; the grammatical subject of the first sentence (\"wollen wir… erwegen\") was split for clarity.","- \"derselben\" in \"⅟169282 derselben\" is ambiguous in the German; I take it to refer to the sun's mass (the sun being the standard of comparison), and flagged it in brackets. The figures themselves are Kant's and are left as printed; some of the arithmetic appears inconsistent (e.g. 277½ vs. 276½) and I have not corrected it.","- \"Bimillion\" is left as Kant's term (a million millions, i.e. 10^12 in the older reckoning), italicised as a foreign-form word.","- \"Zusammenstimmet … bestätigen\" is rendered as \"accords … in confirming\"; \"Lehrverfassung\" = \"theory\"; \"Mittelraum\" = \"intermediate space\"; \"Raumesinhalt\" = \"volume\".","- The phrase \"ist dreyßig Millionenmal kleiner als derselbe\" is kept literally, though the sense is \"one thirty-millionth part of it.\""]],"naturgeschichte-0132":["I conclude this chapter by adding an analogy which, by itself alone, can raise the present theory of the mechanical formation of the heavenly bodies from the level of probability as an hypothesis to that of formal certainty. Suppose the sun is composed of particles of the same primary material out of which the planets have been formed; and suppose that the only difference lies in this, that in the sun matter of all kinds has been heaped together without distinction, while in the planets it has been distributed at different distances, according to the density of its several sorts. Then, if we consider the matter of all the planets as united together, there must result, in their whole mixture, a density almost equal to the density of the body of the sun. Now this necessary consequence of our system finds a happy confirmation in the comparison which Herr von Buffon, that philosopher so worthy of his fame, has made between the densities of the total planetary matter and that of the sun. He found a similarity between the two as between 640 and 650. When unforced and necessary consequences of a doctrine meet with such happy confirmation in the actual relations of nature, can we then believe that mere chance has brought about this agreement between theory and observation?",[]],"naturgeschichte-0133":["Third Chapter,\nOn the\nEccentricity of the Planetary Orbits, and the\nOrigin of the Comets.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0134":["We cannot make of the comets a special kind of heavenly body, entirely distinct from the genus of the planets. Nature works here, as elsewhere, by imperceptible gradations; and, passing through all the stages of variation, she connects remote properties with near ones by means of a chain of intermediate links. In the planets eccentricity is a consequence of a deficiency in that striving by which nature endeavours to make the planetary motions exactly circular. Owing to the intervention of various circumstances she can never fully attain this; but she departs from it more at greater distances than at near ones.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0135":["This determination [*Bestimmung*] leads, by a continuous ladder through all possible degrees of eccentricity, from the planets finally to the comets. Although at Saturn this connection seems to be cut off by a great gulf, which completely separates the cometary kind from the planets, we noted in the first part that there may well be, probably beyond Saturn, still other planets which, by a greater departure from circularity of orbit, approach nearer to the course of the comets. We also noted that it is only the lack of observation, or the difficulty of it, that prevents this affinity from being as visible to the eye as it has long since been presented to the understanding [*Verstand*].",[]],"naturgeschichte-0136":["We have already cited, in the first chapter of this part, a cause which can make eccentric the path of a heavenly body that forms itself out of the primary material floating around it, even if we assume that this material possesses, at all its places, forces exactly balanced for circular motion. For since the planet gathers these particles from heights widely distant from one another, where the velocities of circular motion differ, they come together upon it with different degrees of revolving motion inhering in them. These deviate from the measure of velocity proper to the planet's distance, and so bring upon it an eccentricity, in so far as these varying impressions of the particles fail wholly to compensate one another's deviation.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0137":["If eccentricity had no other cause, it would be everywhere moderate. It would also be less in the small planets remote from the sun than in the near and large ones, provided we supposed that the particles of the primary material had in fact previously possessed exact circular motions. Now these determinations [*Bestimmungen*] do not agree with observation. As already remarked, eccentricity increases with distance from the sun, and smallness of mass seems rather to constitute an exception, tending to increase the eccentricity, as we see in Mars. We are therefore obliged to restrict the hypothesis of the exact circular motion of the particles of the primary material as follows: in the regions near the sun we let them come very close to this exactness of determination, but we let them depart from it the more, the farther from the sun these elementary particles were floating. Such a moderation of the principle of the free, circular motion of the primary material is more in accordance with nature. For, notwithstanding the thinness of space, which seems to leave the particles free to restrict one another to the point of perfectly balanced equality of the central forces, the causes that hinder nature from carrying out this end are none the less considerable.\n\nThe farther the dispersed parts of the primitive material are from the sun, the weaker is the force that brings them to sink. The resistance of the lower parts, which bends their fall sideways and is meant to compel it to take its direction perpendicular to the radius of the circle, diminishes in proportion as these parts sink away beneath it, either to be incorporated into the sun or to perform revolutions in nearer regions. The specifically superior lightness of this higher matter does not permit the particles to carry out the sinking motion, which is the ground of everything, with the force required to make the resisting particles yield. [Moreover,] perhaps these remote particles may still restrict one another so as in the end, after a long period, to attain this uniformity. [Meanwhile] small masses have already formed among them, as beginnings of so many heavenly bodies. Gathering themselves from feebly moved material, these have only an eccentric motion with which they sink toward the sun; on the way they are bent more and more from the perpendicular fall by the incorporation of more swiftly moving parts; yet they finally remain comets, if those spaces in which they were formed have been cleared and emptied, either by sinking down to the sun or by gathering into particular clumps.\n\nThis is the cause of the eccentricities, increasing with distance from the sun, of the planets and of those heavenly bodies which are called comets for the reason that in this property they chiefly surpass the former. There are, it is true, still two exceptions that interrupt the law of eccentricity increasing with distance from the sun, which are observed in the two smallest planets of our system, Mars and Mercury. In the case of the former, however, the cause is presumably the neighbourhood of the great Jupiter. By its attraction Jupiter, on its own side, deprives Mars of particles for its formation and leaves it room chiefly to expand toward the sun, and thereby brings upon it a preponderance of central force and eccentricity. As for Mercury, the lowest but also the most eccentric of the planets, it is easy to see that, since the sun in its axial rotation is still far from equalling the velocity of Mercury, the resistance it offers to the matter of the surrounding space will not only rob the nearest particles of their central motion, but can also easily extend this resistance as far as Mercury, and so will have considerably diminished its velocity of revolution.\n\n---notes\n- 0132: The first sentence of the Wenn-clause is rendered as \"Suppose … and suppose …\", and the apodosis \"so wird … müssen\" is made a separate sentence; \"Sonnen ihre\" (= that of the sun) is resolved as \"that of the sun\".\n- 0132: \"Büffon\" is given as Buffon; \"Ungefehr\" = chance.\n- 0134: \"Zirkelgleich\" rendered \"exactly circular\"; the long last sentence is divided in two, and the subject of \"weichet ab\" is taken to be nature (*sie*).\n- 0135: \"Bestimmung\" kept as \"determination\" with the German in brackets; \"vorlängst\" read as \"long since\"; the final sentence is split in two.\n- 0136: \"Oertern\"/\"Höhen\" rendered \"places\"/\"heights\"; the pronoun \"sie\" in \"sammlet sie\" is taken to refer to the particles of the primary material (*Grundstoff*), and the final clause is restated as \"in so far as … fail wholly to compensate\".\n- 0137: The passage is divided into three paragraphs for clarity (the German is one block). The extremely long sentence beginning \"Die specifisch vorzügliche Leichtigkeit\" is broken up; its irregular construction (\"und vielleicht, daß … so haben sich …\") is smoothed, with [Moreover] and [Meanwhile] supplied in brackets, and the sense is somewhat doubtful there.\n- 0137: \"Urstoff\"/\"Grundstoff\" rendered \"primitive/primary material\"; \"Lehrverfassung\" (in 0132) is \"doctrine\"; \"Umschwungsgeschwindigkeit\" = \"velocity of revolution\".",[]],"naturgeschichte-0138":["Eccentricity is the chief distinguishing mark of comets. Their atmospheres and tails, which spread out through the heat when the comets come very near the sun, are only consequences of eccentricity. In times of ignorance, however, they served as unfamiliar bugbears by which imagined destinies were proclaimed to the rabble. The astronomers, who pay more attention to the laws of motion than to the strangeness of the figure, observe a second property which distinguishes the genus of comets from the planets. It is that comets are not, like the planets, bound to the zone of the zodiac, but perform their revolutions freely in all regions of the heavens. This peculiarity has one and the same cause as the eccentricity.\n\nThe planets have their orbits confined within the narrow compass of the zodiac for this reason: the elementary matter near the sun acquires circular motions, which at every revolution strive to cut across the plane of reference, and which do not allow a body once formed to depart from this plane, towards which all matter presses from both sides. [Now if this is so,] the primary stuff of the spaces far distant from the centre, which is feebly moved by attraction and cannot attain to free circular revolution, must, from the very cause that produces the eccentricity, be incapable at that height of accumulating towards the plane of reference of all planetary motions, so as to maintain the bodies formed there chiefly in this track. Rather, since the scattered primary stuff has no restriction to any particular region, as it has among the lower planets, it will form itself into heavenly bodies as readily on one side as on the other, and as often far from the plane of reference as near to it.\n\nHence comets will come down to us from all regions with complete freedom from restraint. Yet those whose first place of formation is not far above the orbits of the planets will show less deviation from the limits of their path, as also less eccentricity. With increasing distance from the centre of the system this lawless freedom of the comets, in respect of their deviations, increases. In the depths of the heavens it is lost in a total absence of revolution, which leaves the outermost bodies in process of formation freely to their fall towards the sun, and sets the last limits to the systematic constitution.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0139":["In this sketch of the cometary motions I presuppose that, in respect of direction, comets will for the most part have it in common with the planets. In the case of the near comets this seems to me beyond doubt. Nor can this uniformity be lost anywhere but in the depths of the heavens, where the elementary primary stuff, in the greatest feebleness of motion, produces in all directions such rotation as may arise from its sinking down. For there the time required to bring these motions into agreement of direction, through the communication of the lower motions, is, because of the greatness of the distance, too long for that communication to extend so far while the formation of nature is being completed in the lower region.\n\nThere will therefore perhaps be comets whose revolution proceeds in the opposite direction, namely from east to west. Yet, for reasons which I hesitate to give here, I could almost persuade myself that, of the 19 comets in which this peculiarity has been observed, an optical illusion may in some cases have given occasion to it.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0140":["I must remark something further on the masses of comets and on the density of their stuff. By right, in the upper regions where these heavenly bodies are formed, then, on the grounds given in the preceding chapter, ever greater masses should form in proportion as the distance increases. It is also to be believed that some comets are greater than Saturn and Jupiter. But it is not to be believed that this magnitude of mass goes on increasing without limit.\n\nThe scattering of the primary stuff and the specific lightness of its particles make formation slow in the remotest region of cosmic space. Its indeterminate diffusion throughout the whole immeasurable extent of this distance, without any determination to accumulate towards a certain plane, permits many smaller formations in place of a single considerable one. And the lack of central force draws the greatest part of the particles down to the sun without their having gathered into masses.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0141":["The specific density of the stuff from which comets arise is of greater interest than the magnitude of their masses. Presumably, since they form in the uppermost region of the universe, the particles of their composition are of the lightest kind. One cannot doubt that this is the chief cause of the vaporous envelopes and tails by which they make themselves recognizable among other heavenly bodies.\n\nThis dispersion of cometary matter into vapour cannot be ascribed chiefly to the action of the sun's heat. Some comets in their perihelion barely reach the depth of the earth's orbit; many remain between the orbit of the earth and that of Venus, and then return. If so moderate a degree of heat so dissolves and rarefies the matter on the surface of these bodies, they must consist of the lightest stuff, which suffers more rarefaction through warmth than any matter in the whole of nature.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0142":["Nor can the vapours rising so abundantly from a comet be ascribed to the heat which its body may have retained from some former nearness to the sun. It is indeed to be presumed that a comet, at the time of its formation, completed several revolutions with greater eccentricity, and that this was only gradually diminished. But the other planets, of which the same might be presumed, do not show this phenomenon. They would nevertheless show it, if the kinds of the lightest matter contained in the composition of the planet were present as abundantly as in the comets.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0143":["The earth has something about it which can be compared with the spreading of the cometary vapours and with their tails (*). The finest particles which the action of the sun draws out of its surface collect about one of the poles when the sun is completing half the circle of its course in the opposite hemisphere. The finest and most active particles, which rise in the burning girdle of the earth, are, after reaching a certain height of the atmosphere, compelled by the action of the sun's rays to withdraw into those regions, and to collect there, which are then turned away from the sun and buried in a long night. They make up to the inhabitants of the frigid zone for the absence of the great light, which even at this distance sends them the effects of its warmth. The very same force of the sun's rays which produces the northern lights would produce a vaporous envelope with a tail, if the finest and most volatile particles were found on the earth as abundantly as on the comet.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0144":["(*) These are the northern lights.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0145":["Fourth Chapter,\nOn the\nOrigin of the Moons, and the Motions\nof the Planets about their Axis.",["Applied by Kemp Smith's method; the notes below are limited to 0138 and 0139.","0138: Long sentences divided into several; \"Wenn … so muß\" is a conditional whose protasis is long, so I opened the second paragraph with \"[Now if this is so,]\" (supplied), turning the sentence into a statement plus consequence. Also \"von dem erstern\" resolved as eccentricity, and \"Cirkelbewegungen\" kept as \"circular motions\".","0138: \"Beziehungsplane\" = \"plane of reference\"; \"Grundstoff\" = \"primary stuff\"; \"elementarische Materie\" = \"elementary matter\".","0139: The sentence \"diese Gleichförmigkeit … zu lang ist\" is irregular; restated in two sentences, with \"For there\" supplied to make the connection plain. \"Morgen gegen Abend\" rendered \"east to west\".","0140: \"Von Rechtswegen\" = \"by right\"; \"Centralkraft\" = \"central force\".","0141: \"Sonnennähe\" = \"perihelion\", an interpretive choice."]],"naturgeschichte-0146":["The striving of a planet to form itself out of the extent of the elementary matter is at the same time the cause of its rotation on its axis, and it generates the moons which are to circle about it. What the sun with its planets is on the large scale, a planet with a widely extended sphere of attraction represents on the smaller. It is the principal member of a system whose parts have been set in motion by the attraction of the central body. The planet in process of formation moves the particles of the primary stuff [*Grundstoff*] from its whole circumference towards its own formation. Out of all these sinking motions, by means of their reciprocal action, it will generate circular motions, and finally such as turn out in a common direction. Of these a part will acquire the due moderation of free circular movement, and, thus restricted, will find themselves close to a common plane. In this space the moons will form themselves about the planet, just as the principal planets form themselves about the sun, provided that the reach of the attraction of such heavenly bodies affords circumstances favourable to their generation.\n\nWhat has been said, moreover, regarding the origin of the solar system may be applied, with sufficient similarity, to the system of Jupiter and of Saturn. The moons will all have directed the circles of their revolution to one side, and almost in one plane, and this from the same causes that determine this analogy on the large scale. But why do these attendants move in their common direction rather towards the side in which the planets run than towards any other? Their revolutions are not generated by the circular motions [of the principal planet]. They acknowledge solely the attraction of the principal planet as their cause, and in respect of this all directions are indifferent. A mere chance would decide, among all possible directions, that in which the sinking motion of the stuff turns out into a circle.\n\nIn fact the circular course of the principal planet contributes nothing towards impressing upon the stuff, out of which the moons are to form themselves about it, revolutions around it. All the particles about the planet move with it, with equal motion, about the sun, and are therefore in relative rest in regard to it. The attraction of the planet does everything alone. But the circular motion which is to arise from it, since it is in itself and antecedently indifferent in respect of all directions, requires only a slight external determination [*Bestimmung*] to turn out towards one side rather than the other. This small degree of guidance it receives from the advance of those elementary particles which likewise circle about the sun, but with greater velocity, and which come into the sphere of the planet's attraction. For this attraction compels the particles nearer the sun, which revolve with a swifter movement, to leave the direction of their track even from afar, and in an elongated divergence to rise above the planet. Because these have a greater degree of velocity than the planet itself, when they are brought to sink by its attraction they give to their rectilinear fall, and also to the fall of the rest, a deflection from west to east. This slight guidance is all that is needed to cause the circular motion, in which the fall excited by the attraction issues, to take this direction rather than any other.\n\nFor this reason all the moons will agree in their direction with the direction of the revolution of the principal planets. But the plane of their orbit also cannot deviate far from the plane of the planetary circles. The matter out of which they form themselves is guided, on the very ground we have given for the direction in general, to this most exact determination of it also, namely coincidence with the plane of the principal orbits.",["- The opening sentences of the second paragraph are divided into several; \"dieselbe\" and \"diese\" resolved by sense (the principal planet's attraction).","- \"Ihre Umläufe werden ja durch die Kreisbewegungen nicht erzeuget\": bracketed \"[of the principal planet]\" supplied, as the following sentence requires it.","- \"Die Monde werden ... gerichtet haben\" kept in the future perfect.","- \"Abbeugung von Abend gegen Morgen\" rendered \"from west to east\".","- \"ablangen Ausschweifung\" rendered \"elongated divergence\"; \"sinkende Bewegung\" kept literal (\"sinking motion\").","- The final sentence was restated in two for clarity; \"auch auf diese genaueste Bestimmung derselben\" taken to refer to the direction."]],"naturgeschichte-0147":["From all this it is clearly seen what the circumstances are under which a planet can acquire satellites. Its force of attraction must be great, and consequently the extent of its sphere of action wide. Only so can the particles, moved to the planet by a high fall, attain sufficient velocity for free revolution, notwithstanding what the resistance takes away; and only so can there be sufficient stuff in this region for the formation of moons, which cannot happen with a slight attraction.\n\nHence only planets of great mass and wide distance are endowed with attendants. Jupiter and Saturn, the two largest and also the most distant of the planets, have the most moons. To the earth, which is much smaller than they, only one has fallen. Mars, to which on account of its distance some share in this prerogative would also be due, goes empty away, because its mass is so slight.",["- The long second sentence is split; \"damit ... sowohl ... als auch\" rendered as a double \"only so ... and only so\".","- \"Trabanten\", \"Begleiter\" both rendered \"satellites\"/\"attendants\" for variety, as in the German."]],"naturgeschichte-0148":["We perceive with pleasure how the same attraction of the planet, which brought together the stuff for the formation of the moons and at the same time determined their motion, extends to its own body also, and how this body, by the very same action through which it forms itself, imparts to itself a rotation about its axis in the general direction from west to east. The particles of the sinking primary stuff, which, as said, receive a general rotating motion from west to east, fall for the most part upon the surface of the planet and mingle with its mass, since they lack the measured degrees [of velocity] required to maintain themselves in free-floating circular motions. Now as they enter into the composition of the planet, they must, as parts of it, continue the same rotation, in the same direction, which they had before they were united with it.\n\nAnd since it is to be seen from the foregoing that the multitude of the particles which the lack of the requisite motion precipitates upon the central body must far exceed the number of the others, which could attain the proper degrees of velocity, one readily comprehends why the planet will indeed by no means have in its axial rotation the velocity needed to balance gravity on its surface with the centrifugal force, but will nevertheless be far swifter in the case of planets of great mass and wide distance than in the case of near and small ones.\n\nIn fact Jupiter has the swiftest axial rotation known to us. I do not know on what system this could be reconciled with a body whose mass surpasses all the others, unless one could regard its motions themselves as the effect of that attraction which this heavenly body exercises in proportion to this very mass. If the axial rotation were the effect of an external cause, Mars would have to have a swifter one than Jupiter; for the very same moving force moves a smaller body more than a larger. Moreover one would justly wonder how, since all motions diminish with distance from the centre, the velocities of the rotations increase with the same distances, and in Jupiter can even be two and a half times swifter than its annual motion itself.",["- Sentences divided at \"Indem sie nun\" and \"In der That\"; \"[of velocity]\" supplied after \"abgemessene Grade\".","- \"fliehenden Kraft\" rendered \"centrifugal force\"; \"Klumpen\" rendered \"mass\".","- \"drittehalbmal schneller\" = two and a half times as swift (literally \"third-half-times\").","- \"wenn man nicht ... ansehen könnte\" rendered \"unless one could regard\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0149":["Since we are thus compelled to recognise, in the daily rotations of the planets, the very same cause which is in general the universal source of motion in nature, namely attraction, this mode of explanation will prove its legitimacy through the natural prerogative of its fundamental concept [*Grundbegriff*] and through an unforced consequence from it.",["- \"seines Grundbegriffes\": \"seines\" refers to the mode of explanation (*Erklärungsart*), rendered \"its\".","- \"Erklärungsart\" rendered \"mode of explanation\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0150":["But if the formation of a body itself produces the axial rotation, all the spheres of the universe must equally possess it. Why, then, has the moon none? Some, though wrongly, are inclined to derive the kind of revolution by which the moon always turns the same side towards the earth rather from a sort of preponderance of weight in one hemisphere than from any actual impetus of revolution. Might the moon not once have rolled more swiftly about its axis, and have been brought to this slight and measured remainder by causes, I know not what, which gradually diminished the motion? We need only solve this question in respect of one of the planets, and the application to all the others follows of itself. I reserve this solution for another occasion, since it is necessarily connected with the problem which the Royal Academy of Sciences at Berlin had set as the prize question for the year 1754.",["- Restated the long second sentence (the clause \"welcher … herzuhaben scheinet\") as two sentences, making \"some\" the subject of the inclination; the German construction is irregular.","- \"Ueberwucht\" rendered \"preponderance of weight\"; \"Schwunge der Revolution\" as \"impetus of revolution\", both choices of sense.","- \"billig\" rendered \"equally\"/\"must\" in the sense of \"by right\"; \"Weltbaues\" rendered \"universe\" (literally \"structure of the world\")."]],"naturgeschichte-0151":["The theory which is to explain the origin of the axial rotations must also be able to derive, from the very same causes, the position of their axes relative to the plane of their orbits. We have reason to wonder why the equator of the daily rotation is not in the same plane as the orbits of the moons that circle the same planet. For the same motion which directed the revolution of a satellite, extending as it did to the body of the planet, produced the planet's rotation about its axis, and ought to have imparted to this rotation the same determination in direction and position. Heavenly bodies which have no secondary planets circling about them were nevertheless set in axial rotation by the very same motion of the particles that served as their material, and by the same law which confined those particles to the plane of their periodic course; and this rotation, on the same grounds, must have coincided in direction with the plane of their revolution. In accordance with these causes, the axes of all the heavenly bodies ought by right to stand perpendicular to the general plane of reference of the planetary system, which deviates little from the ecliptic. But they are perpendicular only in the two most important members of this universe, Jupiter and the sun. The others, whose rotation is known, incline their axes to the plane of their orbits: Saturn more than the rest, and the earth more than Mars, whose axis is also directed almost perpendicularly to the ecliptic. The equator of Saturn (if it may be taken as indicated by the direction of its ring) is inclined to the plane of its orbit at an angle of 31 degrees; that of the earth only at 22½. The cause of these deviations may perhaps be ascribed to inequality in the motions of the material which came together to form the planet. The chief motion of the particles about the centre was in the direction of the plane of its orbit; and there lay the plane of reference about which the elementary particles gathered, so as to make the motion, if possible, circular, and to accumulate matter for the formation of the secondary planets, which for this reason never deviate far from the orbital path. If the planet had been formed for the most part only from these particles, its axial rotation, like the secondary planets that circle about it, would at its first formation have deviated as little from that plane. But, as the theory has shown, it was formed rather from the particles which sank down from both sides, and whose quantity or velocity seems not to have been so exactly balanced that one hemisphere could not obtain a slight preponderance of motion over the other, and hence some deviation of the axis.",["- Divided the opening sentence on the planet's rotation (\"denn dieselbe Bewegung …\") into a causal clause and kept the rest; \"hat … hervorgebracht … sollen\" rendered \"produced … ought to have imparted\".","- \"Bestimmung in der Richtung und Lage\" rendered \"determination in direction and position\"; \"Plan\" rendered \"plane\" (as in \"Plan ihrer Kreise\"), \"Beziehungsfläche\" as \"plane of reference\".","- \"Stücken dieses Weltbaues\" rendered \"members of this universe\"; \"Mondenkreise\" as \"orbits of the moons\".","- \"der Erden ihrer\" (dative-possessive colloquialism) rendered \"that of the earth\", with \"equator\" supplied by sense.","- \"Aeqvator der täglichen Umwelzung\" rendered \"equator of the daily rotation\"; the final clause \"daß die eine Halbkugel nicht …\" kept close to the German though the double negation is awkward.","- \"Ueberwucht der Bewegung\" rendered \"preponderance of motion\", consistent with the previous passage."]],"naturgeschichte-0152":["Notwithstanding these grounds, I put forward this explanation only as a conjecture, which I do not venture to decide. My true opinion is this: in the original state of their first formation the rotation of the planets about their axes coincided fairly exactly with the plane of their annual orbit, and causes were present which displaced this axis from its first position. A heavenly body which passes from its original fluid state into a state of solidity undergoes, when it thus becomes fully formed, a great change in the regularity of its surface. The surface becomes firm and hardened, while the deeper materials have not yet sunk sufficiently, in accordance with their specific gravity. The lighter kinds, which were mingled in its mass, at last separate from the others and betake themselves beneath the uppermost crust, now grown solid, and produce the great cavities. The greatest and widest of these are situated under or near the equator, for reasons which it would take too long to give here. Into these cavities the crust finally sinks, and so produces manifold inequalities, mountains and hollows.\n\nNow when the surface has in this way become uneven, as must evidently have happened with the earth, the moon and Venus, it can no longer maintain on all sides the equilibrium of the swing [*Umschwung*] in its rotation about the axis. Certain projecting parts of considerable mass found on the opposite side none other that could provide the counteraction of the swing [*Schwung*], and so must soon have displaced the axis of rotation, and sought to bring it into that position about which the materials would remain in equilibrium. Thus the very cause which, in the complete formation of a heavenly body, changed its surface from the level condition into broken inequalities, this universal cause which is observed in all heavenly bodies that the telescope can discover distinctly enough, has put them under the necessity of somewhat altering the original position of their axes.\n\nThis alteration, however, has its limits, so that it does not stray too far. The inequalities are produced, as already mentioned, rather near the equator of a revolving celestial globe than far from it; towards the poles they almost entirely vanish, and the reasons for this I reserve for another occasion. Hence the masses which project most above the level surface will be found near the equinoctial circle. Since these, by the preponderance of the swing, strive to approach it, they can at most raise the axis of the body only some few degrees from its perpendicular position to the plane of its orbit.\n\nAccordingly, a heavenly body which has not yet fully formed will still have this right-angled position of its axis to its orbit, which it will perhaps alter only in the course of long centuries. Jupiter seems still to be in this state. The superiority of its mass and size, and the lightness of its material, have compelled it to attain the firm repose of its materials some centuries later than other heavenly bodies. Perhaps the interior of its mass is still in motion, the parts of its composition sinking towards the centre in accordance with the nature of their weight, and the body attaining the state of solidity through the separation of the thinner kinds from the heavy. In such circumstances its surface cannot yet look tranquil. Upheavals and ruin prevail upon it, and the telescope itself has assured us of this. The shape of this planet changes constantly, whereas the moon, Venus and the earth keep theirs unaltered. One may also with good reason think the completion of the period of formation to lie some centuries later in a body which exceeds our earth in size more than twenty-thousandfold, and falls short of it four times in density. When its surface has attained a tranquil condition, then undoubtedly inequalities far greater than those which cover the surface of the earth, combined with the rapidity of its swing, will in no very long period give to its rotation that constant position which the equilibrium of the forces upon it will require.",["- Restated the long sentences on the cavities and on the \"universal cause\" as several shorter ones; the construction of the sentence ending \"erzeuget\" is irregular in the German, and I smoothed it.","- \"Umdrehung ... mit der Fläche ... übereingetroffen\" is kept close to the text. The later phrase \"senkrechten Stellung\" shows that the sense is that the equatorial plane of rotation coincided with the orbital plane, so that the axis stood perpendicular to it.","- \"diesem\" (in \"diesem sich zu nähern\") is ambiguous. I took it to refer to the equinoctial circle, hence \"approach it\".","- *Schwung* / *Umschwung* are rendered \"swing\" for the centrifugal motion of rotation. This is a choice, since the German word has no fixed technical English equivalent.","- \"an Dichtigkeit 4mal nachstehet\" is rendered literally as \"falls short of it four times in density\", as Kant's own figure."]],"naturgeschichte-0153":["Saturn, which is three times smaller than Jupiter, may perhaps by reason of its greater distance have gained an advantage over it in more rapid formation. At least its much more rapid rotation about the axis, and the great ratio of its centrifugal force to the gravity on its surface (which is to be shown in the following chapter), have had this result. The inequalities presumably produced thereby on its surface very soon turned the scale in favour of the preponderance, through a displacement of the axis.\n\nI confess frankly that this part of my system, which concerns the position of the planetary axes, is still imperfect, and rather far from being capable of being subjected to geometrical calculation. I have preferred to disclose this candidly, rather than, by all manner of borrowed specious arguments, to impair the soundness of the rest of the doctrine and give it a weak side. The following chapter can furnish a confirmation of the credibility of the whole hypothesis by which we have sought to explain the motions of the structure of the world.",["- The first sentence is divided in two: \"macht, daß ... gegeben haben\" is recast as a result clause.","- \"Centerfliehkraft\" is rendered \"centrifugal force\". \"Vorrückung der Achse\" is rendered \"displacement of the axis\".","- \"Ueberwucht\" is rendered \"preponderance\". \"Weltbau\" is rendered \"structure of the world\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0154":["Fifth Chapter [*Hauptstück*],\nOn the Origin of the Ring of Saturn, and Calculation of the Daily Rotation of this Planet from its Relations.",["- \"Verhältnissen\" is rendered \"Relations\". It could also be \"proportions\" or \"ratios\", but Kant's phrase suggests the relations of the planet's own constitution."]],"naturgeschichte-0155":["By virtue of the systematic constitution of the universe [*Weltgebäude*], its parts are connected through a graded variation of their properties. One may therefore presume that a planet situated in the remotest region of the world will have approximately such determinations as the nearest comet would acquire, if by diminution of its eccentricity it were raised into the planetary class. Let us accordingly regard Saturn as though, in a manner similar to cometary motion, it had completed several revolutions with greater eccentricity, and had gradually been brought to a path more nearly circular (*).\n\nThe heat which was absorbed by it in its perihelion raised the light matter from its surface. This matter, as we know from the preceding chapters, is in the outermost heavenly bodies of such exceeding tenuity that it lets itself be expanded by slight degrees of warmth. Meanwhile, after the planet had in several revolutions been brought to the distance at which it now hovers, it gradually lost, in so temperate a climate, the warmth it had received. The vapours which still continued to spread about it from its surface gradually ceased to rise as far as tails, and fresh ones no longer rose so abundantly as to increase the old. In short, the vapours already surrounding it remained suspended about it, through causes which we shall presently set out, and preserved for it the mark of its former comet-like nature in a permanent ring, while its body breathed out the heat and finally became a quiet and purified planet.\n\nNow we shall point out the secret which has been able to keep the vapours that rose from the heavenly body freely suspended, and indeed has changed them from an atmosphere spread round it into the form of a ring standing off on all sides. I assume that Saturn had a rotation about its axis, and nothing more than this is needed to disclose the whole secret. No other mechanism than this one has, by an immediate mechanical effect, brought about the said phenomenon in the planet. I venture to assert that in the whole of nature there are few things which can be traced to an origin so comprehensible as that by which this peculiarity of the heavens can be developed out of the rude state of its first formation.",["- Divided the long opening and the vapour sentences into several shorter ones, as the method permits.","- \"obersten Himmelskörpern\" is rendered \"outermost\", the sense required by the argument (the bodies farthest from the sun).","- \"(*)\" marks a footnote whose text is not part of this passage. I have kept the marker only.","- The last sentence is irregular in the German (\"auf einen so begreiflichen Ursprung ... als ... sich entwickeln läßt\"). I smoothed the comparison, and the exact force of \"als\" is slightly uncertain.","- \"Bestimmungen\" is rendered \"determinations\", and \"Gleise\" is rendered \"path\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0156":["(*) Or, what is more probable, that in its comet-like nature, which by reason of its eccentricity it still retains even now, it spread abroad a cometary atmosphere, before the lightest matter of its surface had been entirely dispersed.",["- The footnote marker (*) is kept as in the source; the sentence is a fragment, a footnote whose main text lies elsewhere. \"Er\" is taken to be Saturn (the planet), per the context of the following passage; this is inferred, not stated here.","- \"Welches wahrscheinlicher ist\" is rendered \"what is more probable\", with \"Or\" kept as the opening alternative to a preceding clause not given here.","- \"Eccentricität\" rendered \"eccentricity\" (orbital eccentricity)."]],"naturgeschichte-0157":["The vapours rising from Saturn carried with them the motion which they had possessed, as parts of the planet, in its rotation about its axis, and at the height to which they had risen they continued it freely. The particles that rose near the planet's equator must have had the swiftest motion; and the farther they rose from the equator towards the poles, the weaker must their motions have been, in proportion as the latitude of the place from which they rose was greater. The relation of specific gravity assigned to the particles the different heights to which they rose. But only those particles could maintain their places at a given distance in a continuously free circular revolution whose distances, to which they had been carried, demanded just such a central force as could be furnished by the velocity which they owed to the rotation about the axis. The remainder, unless they can be brought to this exactness by the reciprocal action of the others, must either withdraw from the sphere of the planet through an excess of motion, or be compelled, through a deficiency of it, to sink back upon the planet.\n\nThe particles scattered throughout the whole extent of the sphere of vapour will, in virtue of these same central laws, strive in the motion of their revolution to cut the extended plane of the planet's equator from both sides. Since in this plane they hinder one another coming from both hemispheres, they will accumulate there. And since I suppose that the vapours in question are those which the planet sends up last in the course of its cooling, all the scattered vapour-matter will collect itself beside this plane in a space not very broad, and will leave the spaces on both sides empty. In this new and altered direction, however, they will still continue the very same motion which keeps them suspended in free concentric circular revolutions.\n\nIn this way the vapour-sphere changes its figure, which was a filled sphere, into the form of an extended plane surface coinciding exactly with the equator of Saturn. But this surface also, on the same mechanical grounds, must finally assume the form of a ring. Its outer edge is determined by the action of the sun's rays, which by their force disperse and drive off those particles that have removed to a certain distance from the centre of the planet, just as the sun does with comets, and which thereby mark out the outer boundary of its vapour-sphere. The inner edge of the ring thus arising is determined by the relation of the velocity of the planet at its equator. For at that distance from its centre at which this velocity is in equilibrium with the attraction of the place, there lies the nearest limit at which the particles that have risen from its body can describe circles by the motion proper to the rotation about the axis. The nearer particles require, for such a revolution, a greater velocity, which they cannot have, since even at the planet's equator the motion is not swifter. They will therefore receive eccentric paths which cross one another, weaken one another's motion, and finally all fall down upon the planet from which they had risen.\n\nHere, then, we see that strange and wonderful phenomenon, the sight of which has at all times, since its discovery, filled astronomers with admiration, and the cause of which no one has ever been able to hope, even with any probability, to discover, arising in an easy mechanical manner, free from all hypothesis. What has befallen Saturn would, as may readily be seen from this, befall in the same regular manner any comet that had sufficient rotation about its axis, if it were placed at a constant height at which its body could gradually cool.\n\nNature, even in the chaos, in the state of her forces left to themselves, is fruitful in excellent developments; and the formation that follows brings with it such splendid relations and harmonies, to the common benefit of creatures, that she makes known, even in the eternal and unchangeable laws of her essential properties, with unanimous certainty, that great Being in whom, by means of their common dependence, they unite in a total harmony. Saturn derives great advantages from its ring: it lengthens its day, and, among so many moons, so illumines its night that the absence of the sun is easily forgotten there. But must we on that account deny that the general development of matter by mechanical laws, without needing anything other than its general determinations [*Bestimmungen*], could have produced relations which are of benefit to the rational creature? All beings are connected through one cause, which is the understanding of GOD; they can therefore bring after them no other consequences than such as carry with them a representation of perfection in that very same divine idea.",["- Restated: the long second sentence (the \"nur diejenige Partikeln…\" period) is kept as one sentence but simplified; the long sentence on the ring's outer edge is split, and the \"Da sehen wir nun…\" sentence is simplified. Paragraph breaks are added for readability; the source has none.","- \"Die sie, als dessen Theile…gehabt hatten\": \"die\" refers to the motion (Bewegung); \"dessen\" to the planet. Resolved accordingly.","- \"Die Räume zu beyden Seiten leer lassen\" and \"einander aufhalten\": the duplicated \"einander\" in the German is smoothed.","- \"Sie\" in \"in welchem sie…sich vereinbaren\" (last paragraph): \"Nature's properties/things\" is taken as antecedent of the second \"sie\"; the first \"sie\" is Nature. Slightly ambiguous; I have used \"they\" for the second.","- \"Wesen\" rendered \"beings\"; \"Dunstkugel/Dunstkreis\" rendered \"sphere of vapour\" / \"vapour-sphere\"; \"Creatur\" as \"creature(s)\".","- \"Bestimmungen\" is given with the German since the rendering \"determinations\" follows the fixed term list.","- \"Aeqvatorsfläche\" rendered \"plane of the equator\"; \"Plane\" as \"plane\"; \"Hemisphärien\" as \"hemispheres\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0158":["We shall now calculate the time of this heavenly body's rotation on its axis from the relations of its ring, according to the hypothesis already given of the ring's origin. All the motion of the particles of the ring is a motion incorporated in them from the axial rotation of Saturn, on whose surface they once were. Hence the swiftest motion among those which these particles have coincides with the swiftest revolution found on the surface of Saturn. That is to say, the velocity with which the particles of the ring circulate at its inner edge is equal to that which the planet has at its equator. This velocity, however, can easily be found, if we derive it from the velocity of one of the satellites of Saturn, taking it in proportion to the square root of the distances from the centre of the planet. From the velocity so found the time of Saturn's revolution about its axis follows immediately; it is six hours, twenty-three minutes and fifty-three seconds. This mathematical calculation of an unknown motion of a heavenly body, which is perhaps the only prediction of its kind in natural science proper, awaits confirmation from the observations of future times.\n\nThe telescopes known up to the present do not magnify Saturn enough to allow us to discover the spots which may be presumed to exist on its surface, and so to learn its rotation about its axis from their displacement. But perhaps the telescopes have not yet attained all the perfection that may be hoped for from them, and which the diligence and skill of the makers seem to promise us. If it should one day be possible to confirm our conjectures by direct sight, what certainty would the theory of Saturn thereby acquire, and what superior credibility would the whole system gain, which is erected on the same grounds!\n\nThe time of Saturn's daily rotation also carries with it the ratio of the centrifugal force at its equator to the gravity at its surface; this is as 20 : 32. Gravity is therefore only ⅗ greater than the centrifugal force. So great a ratio necessarily produces a very considerable difference between the diameters of this planet. It might be feared that the difference would have to turn out so great that, even in a planet magnified so little by the telescope, observation would still find it far too plainly evident. This is in fact not the case, and the theory might thereby suffer a damaging check. A thorough examination removes this difficulty entirely.\n\nAccording to the Huygenian hypothesis, which assumes that gravity in the interior of a planet is the same throughout, the difference of the diameters stands to the diameter of the equator in a ratio twice as small as that which the centrifugal force has to gravity at the poles. For example, since in the case of the earth the centrifugal force at the equator is 1/289 of gravity at the poles, the diameter of the equatorial plane must on the Huygenian hypothesis be 1/578 greater than the earth's axis. The reason is this. Gravity, by hypothesis, is as great in the interior of the earth's mass, at every nearer approach to the centre, as it is at the surface, whereas centrifugal force decreases with approach to the centre. Centrifugal force is therefore not everywhere 1/289 of gravity; the whole diminution of weight of the fluid column in the equatorial plane arising from this cause amounts accordingly not to 1/289 but to half of this, that is, 1/578, of it.\n\nOn the hypothesis of Newton, on the other hand, the centrifugal force which the axial rotation excites has, throughout the whole equatorial plane down to the centre, a constant ratio to the gravity of the place. For gravity in the interior of the planet (if it is assumed to be of uniform density throughout) decreases with the distance from the centre in the same proportion as the centrifugal force, so that the latter is at all times 1/289 of the former. This causes a lightening of the fluid column in the equatorial plane, and also its elevation by 1/289. In this theory the difference of the diameters is further increased by the following. The shortening of the axis brings the parts nearer to the centre, and therefore increases their gravity; the lengthening of the equatorial diameter removes the parts from that same centre, and therefore lessens their gravity. For this reason the flattening of the Newtonian spheroid is so increased that the difference of the diameters is raised from 1/289 to 1/230.",["- Restated the long final sentences of the passage as several shorter ones, and divided the passage into paragraphs for readability; the German has none.","- \"Die Zeit der Achsendrehung … führet … mit sich\" rendered \"carries with it\"; the sentence \"welches wirklich nicht geschiehet … Anstoß erleiden könte\" is irregular, and I resolved it as above: the feared conspicuousness does not in fact occur, and the theory might nevertheless seem to suffer a check.","- \"Centerfliehkraft\" / \"den Mittelpunkt fliehende Kraft\" = centrifugal force; \"Schweere\" = gravity (weight in \"Gewichtes\"); \"Lehrbegriff\" = theory; \"Aeqvatorsfläche\" = equatorial plane.","- \"Z. E.\" expanded as \"For example\". \"In einer zweyfach kleinern Verhältniß\" rendered \"a ratio twice as small\".","- The \"weil, da …\" sentence on the Huygenian hypothesis is tangled; I split it and supplied \"[centrifugal force is therefore not everywhere…]\" only as a connective, not as new content. A footnote reference mark after \"Secunden\" and after \"230\" appears in the German text (the notes are not given here); they were dropped as lost in the OCR.","- \"Ferngläser\" and \"Sehröhre\" both rendered \"telescopes\". \"Fleiß\" = diligence.","- Figures (⅗, 20 : 32, 1/289, 1/578, 1/230) kept as in the text, with no correction. The Academy text gives \"⅗\" where 12/20 would be 3/5, so it is consistent with 20 : 32."]],"naturgeschichte-0159":["On these grounds the diameters of Saturn would have to stand to one another in an even greater ratio than that of 20 to 32; they would have to come nearly to the proportion of 1 to 2. This is a difference so great that the slightest attention could not miss it, however small Saturn may appear through the telescopes. From this, however, we can only see that the supposition of uniform density, which seems to be applied with fair correctness in the case of the earth, departs much too far from the truth in the case of Saturn. This is in itself probable in a planet whose mass consists, for the greatest part of its content, of the lightest materials. Before it attained the state of solidity, the heavier kinds in its composition were allowed to sink to the centre, according to the nature of their weight, far more freely than in those heavenly bodies whose much denser stuff delays the settling of the materials and lets them become solid before this sinking can take place.\n\nIf, then, we suppose in the case of Saturn that the density of its materials in the interior increases with approach to the centre, gravity no longer decreases in that proportion. Rather, the increasing density makes good the want of those parts which lie above the height of the point within the planet and contribute nothing by their attraction to its gravity (*). If this superior density of the deepest materials is very great, then, by the laws of attraction, it changes the gravity, which decreases toward the centre in the interior, into one almost uniform, and brings the ratio of the diameters close to the Huygenian, which is always half the ratio between centrifugal force and gravity. Since these stood to one another as 2 : 3, the difference of the diameters of this planet will be not ⅓ but ⅙ of the equatorial diameter. This difference is finally still further concealed by the fact that Saturn, whose axis always makes an angle of 31 degrees with the plane of its orbit, never presents the position of its axis to us, as Jupiter does, directly [side-on] toward its equator. This lessens the previous difference, to all appearance, by almost a third part.\n\nIn such circumstances, and above all in view of the great distance of this planet, it is easy to judge that the flattened figure of its body will not strike the eye so readily as one might think. Nevertheless astronomy, whose advancement depends chiefly on the perfection of its instruments, will, unless I flatter myself too much, perhaps be put in a position to achieve, by their help, the discovery of so remarkable a property.",["- Restated the long sentences, especially \"Allein hieraus … verstattet\" and \"Wenn diese vorzügliche Dichtigkeit …\", as several sentences; the clause \"denen von schwererer Art … Niedersinkung … verstattet\" is irregular in construction and I resolved it as above, taking \"Zusammensatze\" as the planet's composition.","- \"Die Stellung desselben gegen seinen Aeqvator … gerade zu darbietet\": the sense is doubtful; \"[side-on]\" is my supplied gloss, and \"its axis\" is my resolution of \"desselben\". Kant's point is that Saturn does not present its equator edge-on to us as Jupiter does.","- \"Centerfliehkraft\" = centrifugal force; \"Schweere\" = gravity; \"Durchschnitt\" = diameter.","- \"Der Niedersatz der Materien\" = \"settling of the materials\"; \"Festigkeit\" = solidity.","- The final sentence is syntactically irregular (\"wo ich mir nicht zu sehr schmeichle … in den Stand gesetzet werden\"); I smoothed it and kept the sense that astronomy may, with better instruments, reach the discovery.","- The asterisk (*) refers to the footnote in the next passage."]],"naturgeschichte-0160":["(*) For according to the Newtonian laws of attraction, a body situated in the interior of a sphere is attracted only by that part of the sphere which is described spherically about the centre at the distance the body has from the centre. The concentric part lying outside this distance contributes nothing, on account of the equilibrium of its attractions, which cancel one another, either to moving the body toward the centre or away from it.",["- \"Wegen des Gleichgewichts seiner Anziehungen, die einander aufheben\" kept close to the German; I moved \"contributes nothing\" to precede the reason clause for clarity.","- \"sphärisch beschrieben\" = \"described spherically\", i.e. the sphere of radius equal to the body's distance from the centre."]],"naturgeschichte-0161":["What I say of the figure of Saturn may in a certain measure serve as a general remark for the natural science of the heavens. Jupiter, according to an exact calculation, has on its equator a ratio of gravity to centrifugal force of at least 9¼ : 1. If its mass were of uniform density throughout, it ought, according to the theorems of Newton, to show a still greater difference than ⅑ between its axis and its equatorial diameter. But Cassini found it to be only ⅟16, Pound ⅟12, and at times ⅟14. At least all these various observations, which by their very differences confirm the difficulty of this measurement, agree in setting the difference much smaller than it ought to be according to the system of Newton, or rather according to his hypothesis of uniform density. Suppose, then, that we alter the presupposition of uniform density, which occasions so great a divergence of theory from observation, into the far more probable one, namely that the density of the planetary mass is taken to increase towards its centre. We shall then not only justify the observation in the case of Jupiter, but shall also be able to see clearly, in the case of Saturn, a planet much harder to measure, the cause of a lesser flattening of its spheroidal body.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0162":["From the generation of the Saturnian ring we have taken occasion to venture the bold step of determining by calculation the time of its axial rotation, which telescopes are unable to discover. Let us add to this specimen of a physical prediction another, concerning the same planet, which may expect from the more perfect instruments of future times the testimony of its correctness.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0163":["Given the presupposition that the ring of Saturn is an accumulation of particles which, having risen as vapours from the surface of this heavenly body, maintain themselves freely circling at the height of their distance, by virtue of the impetus which they have from its axial rotation and continue to retain, these particles cannot have equal periodic times of revolution at all their distances from the centre. Their times must rather stand in proportion to the square roots of the cubes of their distances, if they are to keep themselves suspended by the laws of central forces. Now, on this hypothesis, the time in which the particles of the inner edge complete their revolution is about 10 hours, and the time of circulation of the particles in the outer edge is, by proper calculation, 15 hours. Hence when the lowest parts of the ring have completed their revolution three times, the most distant have done so only twice.\n\nBut it is probable, however slightly one may estimate the hindrance which the particles, widely scattered in the plane of the ring, offer to one another, that the lagging behind of the more distant particles, in each of their revolutions, gradually retards and checks the more swiftly moving lower parts. The latter, on the other hand, must impress upon the upper parts a portion of their motion, so that these turn more quickly. If this reciprocal action were not finally interrupted, it would last until all the particles of the ring, the lower as well as the more distant, were brought to revolve in equal time. In that state they would be in relative rest to one another, and by their displacement would produce no effect on one another.\n\nSuch a state, however, if the motion of the ring were to issue in it, would wholly destroy the ring. Take the middle of the plane of the ring, and suppose that the motion there remains in the state in which it previously was, and must be, in order to maintain a free circular course. Then the lower particles, having been greatly held back, would not keep themselves suspended at their height, but would cross one another in oblique and eccentric motions. The more distant particles, by the impression of a greater motion than is proper to the central force of their distance, would be turned farther from the Sun than the limit which the Sun's action fixes for the outer boundary of the ring, and would be scattered and carried off by that action behind the planet.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0164":["But there is no need to fear all this disorder. The mechanism of the motion that generates the ring leads to a determination which, by means of the very causes that are supposed to destroy it, places it in a secure state. It is divided into several concentric circular bands, which, because of the intervals separating them, no longer have any communication with one another.\n\nFor while the particles circling in the inner edge of the ring carry the upper ones somewhat along by their swifter motion and accelerate their revolution, the increased degrees of speed in the latter cause an excess of centrifugal force, and a removal from the place where they were suspended. Let us suppose, however, that in striving to separate themselves from the lower particles they have to overcome a certain cohesion, which, though these are scattered vapours, yet does not seem in their case to be wholly nothing. Then this increased degree of impetus will strive to overcome the said cohesion, but will not overcome it so long as the excess of centrifugal force, which it exerts in the same time of revolution as the lowest, over the central force of its place, does not surpass this adhesion.\n\nOn this ground, within a certain breadth of a band of this ring, the cohesion must persist, although, since its parts complete their revolution in equal time, the upper ones exert a striving to tear themselves from the lower. In a greater breadth it cannot persist. For the speed of these particles, revolved in equal times, increases with the distances, and so more than it should according to the central laws. When it has passed the degree which the cohesion of the vapour particles can sustain, they must tear themselves away from these and assume a distance corresponding to the excess of the revolving force over the central force of the place.\n\nIn this way the interval is determined which separates the first band of the ring from the rest. In the same way the accelerated motion of the upper particles, through the swift revolution of the lower, together with their cohesion, which strives to prevent the separation, forms the second concentric ring, from which the third stands off at a moderate interval. One could calculate the number of these circular bands, and the breadth of their intervals, if the degree of the cohesion which holds the particles together were known. But we may content ourselves with having guessed, on good grounds of probability, the general composition of the Saturnian ring, which prevents its destruction and keeps it suspended by free motions.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0165":["This conjecture pleases me not a little, through the hope of one day seeing it confirmed by actual observations. Some years ago word came from London that, when Saturn was being observed with a new Newtonian telescope improved by Mr. Bradley, it had seemed that its ring was really a composite of many concentric rings separated by intervals. This report has not been followed up since (*). The instruments of sight have opened to the understanding the knowledge of the outermost regions of the structure of the universe. If it depends chiefly on them to take new steps in this matter, one may well hope with probability, from the attention of the century to everything that can extend the insight of men, that it will turn chiefly to the side which offers it the greatest hope of important discoveries.",["Passage 0161: \"Verhältniß der Schweere zur Centrifugalkraft\" rendered \"ratio of gravity to centrifugal force\"; fractions ⅑, ⅟16 etc. kept as in the source. I divided the long final sentence and made \"da … gesetzet wird\" an explicit \"namely that\".","Passage 0163: I restated the very long sentences as several, and supplied \"these particles\" and \"Their times\" as subjects. \"Hinderniß\" is \"hindrance\"; \"respectiver Ruhe\" is \"relative rest\".","Passage 0163: \"vor die Centralkraft\" I took as \"proper to the central force\"; \"hinter dem Planeten\" is literal, and the sense (carried off behind the planet by the Sun's action) is obscure in the German.","Passage 0164: \"Bestimmung\" is rendered \"determination\"; the tangled sentence beginning \"Und aus diesem Grunde\" is split in two, with \"but not in greater breadth\" made a separate sentence. \"Centerfliehkraft\" is rendered \"centrifugal force\".","Passage 0165: the footnote marker \"(*)\" stands in the text with no footnote following; the passage appears cut off or the note is lost in OCR. The sentence \"Die Werkzeuge des Gesichts…\" may belong to the missing footnote, as the marker precedes it.","Passage 0165: \"Kenntnisse\" is rendered \"knowledge\"; \"Einsichten\" is rendered \"insight\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0166":["(*) After writing the above I find, in the *Mémoires* of the Royal Academy of Sciences at Paris for the year 1705, in a treatise by Herr Cassini on the satellites and the ring of Saturn (p. 571 of the second part of von Steinwehr's translation), a confirmation of this conjecture which leaves almost no doubt of its correctness. Herr Cassini had first put forward a thought which, though improbable in itself, might in a certain measure have been a slight approach to the truth that we have arrived at: namely, that this ring might perhaps be a swarm of small satellites which, seen from Saturn, would look just as the Milky Way appears from the earth. (This thought can be admitted if for these small satellites we take the vapour-particles which swing round the planet with a like motion.) He then continues: This thought is confirmed by the observations made in the years when the ring of Saturn appeared broader and more open. For the breadth of the ring was seen divided into two parts by a dark elliptical line, the nearer part, on the side towards the globe, being brighter than the more distant. This line marked, as it were, a small interval between the two parts, just as the distance of the globe from the ring is indicated by the greatest darkness between the two.",["- Restated Kant's long opening sentence (the *Nachdem* clause) as two sentences; the parenthesis is kept but set after the sentence it qualifies.","- \"Observations\" renders *Observationen*; \"Herr Caßini\" given as Cassini. The citation is Kant's own, not verified here.","- *Dunsttheilchen* = \"vapour-particles\"; *Trabanten* = \"satellites\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0167":["But if Saturn has been so fortunate as to provide itself with a ring, why has no other planet come to share this advantage? The reason is plain. A ring is supposed to arise from the exhalations of a planet which emits them while it is still in its raw state, and the planet's axial rotation must give them the impulse which they need only continue once they have risen to the height at which, with this implanted motion, they can exactly balance gravitation towards the planet. Hence one can readily determine by calculation to what height the vapours must rise from a planet if, by the motions they had under its equator, they are to maintain themselves in free circular motion; one needs only to know the planet's diameter, the time of its rotation, and the gravity at its surface. By the law of central motion, the distance of a body which can circle freely round a planet with a velocity equal to that of the planet's axial rotation will stand to the planet's semi-diameter in the same proportion as the force of recession from the centre, under the equator, stands to gravity. On these grounds the distance of the inner edge of Saturn's ring was as 8, when the semi-diameter of Saturn is taken as 5. These two numbers stand in the same ratio as 32 : 20, which, as we noted earlier, expresses the proportion between gravity and the centrifugal force under the equator. On the same grounds, if one supposed that Jupiter had a ring generated in this manner, its smallest semi-diameter would exceed Jupiter's semi-diameter ten times. This would fall precisely where its outermost satellite revolves, and is therefore impossible, both on these grounds and because the exhalation of a planet cannot spread so far from it. If one wished to know why the earth has received no ring, the answer will be found in the size of the semi-diameter which its inner edge alone would have had to possess: it would have had to amount to 289 earth semi-diameters. In the case of the more slowly rotating planets the generation of a ring is still further from possibility. No case therefore remains in which a planet could have acquired a ring in the way we have explained, except that of the planet which actually has one; and this is no slight reinforcement of the credibility of our mode of explanation.",["- Divided the long second sentence; \"the planet's axial rotation must give them the impulse\" follows *dieser* = the Achsendrehung (resolved by sense, not the planet).","- *halber Durchmesser* rendered \"semi-diameter\"; *halbe Dicke des Jupiter* taken as Jupiter's semi-diameter.","- \"Bey den langsamer bewegten Planeten\" taken as slower rotation (axial), as the argument requires.","- Figures (8:5, 10, 289) are Kant's, reproduced as given, not checked. *Centerfliehkraft* = centrifugal force."]],"naturgeschichte-0168":["What makes me almost certain that the ring which surrounds Saturn did not arise in that general manner, and was not generated by the general laws of formation which have prevailed throughout the whole system of the planets and have also furnished Saturn with its satellites, is this: that this external matter did not supply the material for it, but that the ring is a creature of the planet itself, which raised its most volatile parts by heat and gave them, through its own axial rotation, the impulse to revolve. The ground is that the ring is not directed in the general plane of reference of the planetary motions, as are the other satellites of Saturn and, in general, all revolving bodies that accompany the principal planets, but deviates widely from it. This is a sure proof that the ring was not formed from the general primitive matter, nor received its motion from the sinking down of that matter, but that it rose from the planet long after the planet's formation was completed, and, as a part separated from it, received through the planet's implanted forces of rotation a motion and direction related to the planet's axial rotation.",["- Kant's sentence is anacoluthic (*daß, sage ich, …* resumed, then *ist dieses, daß…*); I restated it as two sentences, keeping the sense and dropping the parenthetical \"I say\".","- *Beziehungsfläche* = \"plane of reference\"; *Umschwungskräfte* = \"forces of rotation\".","- *Aufgestiegen* subject is the ring (as separated matter), not the planet; resolved by sense."]],"naturgeschichte-0169":["The pleasure of having comprehended one of the rarest peculiarities of the heavens, in the whole compass of its nature and generation, has drawn us into so lengthy a discussion. With the indulgence of our kind readers, let us carry it, where it pleases, even to digression, so that, having given ourselves up in an agreeable manner and with a certain freedom to arbitrary opinions, we may return with all the greater caution and care to the truth.",["- *Ausschweiffung* = \"digression\" (literally also \"excess\"); *willkührlichen Meinungen* = \"arbitrary opinions\", i.e. conjectures not bound by rigorous proof.","- *Wesen* rendered \"nature\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0170":["Might one not imagine that the earth, just like Saturn, once had a ring? Let us suppose that it rose from the earth's surface, as Saturn's does from his, and maintained itself for a long time, while the earth, which had a much swifter rotation than the present, was checked, by I know not what causes, down to its present degree. Or we may credit the universal primary matter, sinking downwards, with having formed the ring in accordance with the rules which we have explained above. One must not take this too strictly, if one wishes to gratify one's inclination for the singular.\n\nBut what a store of fine illustrations and consequences such an idea offers us! A ring about the earth! What a beauty of spectacle for those who were created to inhabit the earth as a paradise; how much comfort for those on whom nature was to smile from every side! Yet all this is nothing compared with the confirmation which such a hypothesis can borrow from the record of the history of creation. It is no small recommendation for assent among those who believe that they do not profane the honour of revelation, but confirm it, when they use it to lend authority to the extravagances of their wit.\n\nThe water of the firmament [*Veste*], which the Mosaic account mentions, has already caused the interpreters no little trouble. Could one not make use of this ring to help oneself out of the difficulty? This ring doubtless consisted of watery vapours. Besides the advantage it could afford to the first inhabitants of the earth, one has this further: that it could be broken up when needed, in order to chastise with floods a world that had made itself unworthy of such beauty. Either a comet, whose attraction threw the regular motions of its parts into confusion, or the cooling of the region where it abode, united its scattered particles of vapour and precipitated them upon the earth in one of the most terrible cloudbursts. The consequence is easily seen. The whole world perished in the water, and in addition, in the foreign and volatile vapours of this unnatural rain, imbibed that slow poison which brought all creatures nearer to death and destruction.\n\nNow the figure of a pale and luminous arch had vanished from the horizon. The new world, which could never recall that spectacle without a shudder at this fearful instrument of divine vengeance, perhaps beheld with no small dismay, in the first rain, that coloured arch which, in its figure, seemed to portray the former, but which, through the assurance of a reconciled heaven, was to be a token of grace and a memorial of the continued preservation of the earth, now altered. The likeness in form between this sign of remembrance and the event it signified might commend such a hypothesis to those who are given to the prevailing inclination to bring the miracles of revelation into a system with the ordinary laws of nature. I find it more advisable to sacrifice entirely the fleeting approval which such agreements may awaken, to the true satisfaction which springs from perceiving a regular connection, when physical analogies support one another in the signification of physical truths.",["- Restated the opening long sentence as three; \"er möchte … seyn\" rendered as \"Let us suppose\", a concessive subjunctive.","- \"oder daß man … zutrauet\" is irregular after \"habe\"; I made it a separate alternative sentence (\"Or we may credit…\").","- \"Veste\" = firmament (Gen. 1:6–7), bracketed German retained.","- \"vor\" in the sense of \"für\" (e.g. \"vor diejenige\") rendered \"for\"/\"among\"; \"Witz\" as \"wit\".","- \"Beyfall\" = assent/approval, chosen by context.","- The fourth-last sentence (\"Die Aehnlichkeit…\") has been kept close; \"dem wahren Vergnügen … aufzuopfern\" governs the final sentence."]],"naturgeschichte-0171":["Sixth Chapter,\nOn the\nZodiacal Light.",["- *Hauptstück* rendered \"Chapter\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0172":["The sun is surrounded by a subtle and vaporous substance, which in the plane of his equator, with only a slight extension to either side, encircles him to a great height. It cannot be established whether this substance, as Herr von Mairan depicts it, adjoins the surface of the sun in the figure of a convex lens (*figura lenticulari*), or whether, like the ring of Saturn, it stands everywhere apart from him. Be it the one or the other, there remains similarity enough to compare this phenomenon with the ring of Saturn and to derive it from a corresponding origin.\n\nIf this extended matter is an efflux from the sun, as it is most probable to hold it to be, one cannot fail to find the cause which brought it into the plane common with the solar equator. The lightest and most volatile stuff, which the sun's fire raises from its surface, and has long since raised, is driven far beyond it by that action. In accordance with its lightness it remains suspended at a distance where the repelling action of the rays balances the weight of these particles of vapour, or else they are supported by the inflow of new particles which constantly join them.\n\nNow, since the sun, in turning about its axis, imparts its motion equally to these vapours torn from its surface, they retain a certain impetus to revolution. In consequence, in accordance with the laws of central forces, they strive from both sides to cut, in the circle of their motion, the extended equatorial plane of the sun. And since they press toward it in equal quantity from both hemispheres, they accumulate there with equal forces and form an extended plane, in this level referred to the solar equator.",["- Divided the two long sentences into three paragraphs of shorter sentences.","- \"Wesen\" rendered \"substance\"; \"Ausfluß\" as \"efflux\".","- \"in diesem auf dem Sonnenäqvator beziehenden Plan\" is irregular (*beziehenden* for *bezogenen*); rendered \"referred to the solar equator\".","- \"sie davor zu halten\" = to hold it to be an efflux."]],"naturgeschichte-0173":["But notwithstanding this similarity to the ring of Saturn, an essential difference remains, which makes the phenomenon of the zodiacal light diverge widely from that ring. The particles of the latter maintain themselves by the implanted motion of rotation in a freely suspended circular course; but the particles of the former are sustained at their height by the force of the sun's rays. Without this force the motion which they derive from the sun's rotation would fall far short of preserving them, in free revolution, from falling.\n\nFor since the force of axial rotation, which flees the centre, is on the surface of the sun still not so much as 1/40000 of attraction, these risen vapours would have to be removed from it by 40000 half-diameters of the sun, in order first at such a distance to meet a gravitation which their communicated motion could balance. We may therefore be sure that this phenomenon is not to be ascribed to the sun in the same manner as the ring of Saturn.",["- Kant's \"erstern\"/\"letztern\" are reversed relative to the sense: \"erstern\" refers to Saturn's ring (the \"jenem\"), \"letztern\" to the zodiacal light. I rendered by sense, \"the latter\" = Saturn's ring, \"the former\" = zodiacal light, as the argument requires. Doubtful; the order in the German is taken to be a slip or loose usage.","- \"ohne welcher\" restated as a separate sentence.","- \"1/40000\" printed \"⅟40000\" in the OCR."]],"naturgeschichte-0174":["Nevertheless there remains no small probability that this neck-ornament of the sun perhaps acknowledges the same origin which all nature acknowledges, namely formation out of the universal primary matter. Its parts, having floated about in the highest regions of the solar world, sank down toward the sun only after the formation of the whole system was fully completed, in a late fall, with a weakened but still curved motion from west to east. By means of this kind of circulation they cut the extended equatorial plane of the sun. There, by their accumulation from both sides, in that they tarried, they have taken up an extended plane in this position, in which they now maintain themselves at constant and equal height, partly by the repulsion of the sun's rays, partly by the circular motion they have actually acquired.\n\nThe present explanation has no other worth than that which belongs to conjectures, and makes no claim except to an arbitrary assent. Let the reader's judgment turn to whichever side seems to him the more acceptable.",["- Restated one very long sentence as several; the German subject \"dessen Theile\" begins a long construction that I made independent.","- \"von Abend gegen Morgen\" rendered \"from west to east\".","- \"Halsschmuck\" = \"neck-ornament\"; \"Grundstoff\" = \"primary matter\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0175":["Seventh Chapter,\nOn\nCreation in the Whole Extent of Its Infinity,\nin Space as well as in Time.",["- \"Siebendes\" = Seventh; \"Schöpfung\" = creation."]],"naturgeschichte-0176":["By its immeasurable greatness, and by the infinite variety and beauty which shine forth from it on every side, the structure of the universe [*das Weltgebäude*] fills us with a silent amazement. If the representation of all this perfection stirs the imagination, the understanding, on its side, is seized by a different kind of rapture when it considers how so much splendour and so much greatness flow from a single universal rule, in an eternal and exact order. The planetary system, in which the sun, from the centre of all the circles, by its powerful attraction makes the inhabited spheres of its system revolve in eternal orbits, has been formed entirely, as we have seen, from the primitive, widely diffused basic stuff of all the matter of the world. All the fixed stars which the eye discovers in the hollow depth of the heavens, and which seem to indicate a kind of prodigality, are suns and centres of similar systems. Analogy therefore does not permit us here to doubt that these systems have been formed and generated in the same way as that in which we find ourselves: that is, from the smallest parts of the elementary matter which filled empty space, this infinite extent of the divine presence.",["- Restated the final sentence: Kant's \"daß diese ... gebildet und erzeuget worden\" is split by a colon and \"that is\" to keep the construction clear; the sense is unchanged.","- \"Weltgebäude\" and \"Weltbau\" are both rendered by \"structure of the universe\" / \"system\" as the sense requires; \"planetischer Weltbau\" = \"planetary system\".","- \"Grundstoff\" = \"basic stuff\"; \"holen\" read as \"hohlen\" (hollow), a spelling variant."]],"naturgeschichte-0177":["Now if all worlds and orders of worlds acknowledge the same kind of origin; if attraction is unlimited and universal, and the repulsion of the elements likewise operative throughout; and if, in the case of the infinite, the great and the small are alike small: ought not all the structures of the universe to have assumed a relational constitution and systematic connection with one another, just as the heavenly bodies of our solar world have done on the small scale, such as Saturn, Jupiter and the earth, which are systems on their own account and yet are connected with one another as members of a still greater system? Suppose that in the immeasurable space in which all the suns of the Milky Way have been formed we assume a point about which, through I know not what cause, the first formation of nature out of chaos began. There the greatest mass, a body of quite extraordinary attraction, will have arisen; and it will thereby have become capable of compelling, within an enormous sphere about itself, all the systems in process of formation to sink towards it as their centre, and to erect around it a system of the same kind on the whole, as the same elementary basic stuff which formed the planets has produced on the small scale around the sun. Observation renders this conjecture almost beyond doubt. The host of stars, by its relational position with regard to a common plane, constitutes a system just as truly as the planets of our solar structure do around the sun. The Milky Way is the zodiac of these higher orders of worlds, which deviate as little as possible from its zone, and whose band is always illumined by their light, just as the zodiac of the planets glimmers here and there, though only at very few points, with the shining of these spheres. Each of these suns, with its revolving planets, constitutes a particular system on its own account; but this does not prevent its being part of a still greater system, just as Jupiter or Saturn, notwithstanding their own retinue, are confined within the systematic constitution of a still greater structure of the universe. In so exact an agreement in constitution, can we not recognise the same cause and mode of generation?",["- Split the long second sentence (\"Wenn man ... angenommen\") by turning the \"Wenn ... so\" into \"Suppose ... There ...\", and divided at the semicolon; no content added.","- \"Verfassung\" = \"constitution\"; \"beziehende\" = \"relational\"; \"Zurückstossung\" = \"repulsion\".","- \"wie Saturn, Jupiter und die Erde, die vor sich insonderheit Systeme seyn\": the antecedent of \"die\" taken to be the bodies named, as Kant's sense requires.","- \"Thierkreiß\" and \"Zodiakus\" both \"zodiac\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0178":["Now if the fixed stars constitute a system whose extent is determined by the sphere of attraction of the body situated at the centre, will not more systems of suns, and, so to speak, more Milky Ways, have arisen, generated in the boundless field of cosmic space? We have seen with amazement figures in the heavens which are nothing other than such systems of fixed stars confined to a common plane, such Milky Ways, if I may so express myself. Presented in various positions to the eye, and with a glimmer weakened in accordance with their infinite distance, they show elliptical shapes. They are systems with a diameter, so to speak, infinitely times infinitely greater than the diameter of our solar system; but without doubt they have arisen in the same way, are ordered and arranged by the same causes, and maintain themselves in their constitution by the same driving mechanism as this one.",["- Divided the second sentence into three, resolving \"welche ... seyn, die ... darstellen\" with a full stop; the semicolon sentence is kept.","- \"Triebwerk\" = \"driving mechanism\"; \"Systemata\" rendered \"systems\".","- The clause \"als der Diameter unseres Sonnenbaues, ist\" has an irregular word order; smoothed."]],"naturgeschichte-0179":["If we regard these systems of stars in turn as members in the great chain of the whole of nature, we have as much reason as before to think of them as standing in a reciprocal relation, and in connections which, by virtue of the law of first formation prevailing throughout all nature, constitute a new and still greater system. This system is governed from the centre of their regular positions by the attraction of a body of incomparably more powerful attraction than all the preceding. The attraction which is the cause of the systematic constitution among the fixed stars of the Milky Way also operates at the distance of these very orders of worlds, so as to dislodge them from their positions and to bury the world in an inevitably impending chaos, unless regularly distributed projectile forces [*Schwungskräfte*] counterbalance attraction, and the two in conjunction produce that relation which is the ground of the systematic constitution. Attraction is without doubt a property of matter as widely extended as coexistence, which constitutes space by connecting substances through reciprocal dependences; or, to speak more properly, attraction is this very universal relation which unites the parts of nature in one space. It extends, therefore, over the whole extension of space, to all the reaches of its infinity. When light reaches us from these distant systems, light which is only an impressed motion, must not attraction, this original source of motion, which is prior to all motion, which needs no foreign causes and can be arrested by no obstacle, because it works in the innermost of matter without any impact, even in the universal rest of nature,—must attraction, I say, not have set these systems of fixed stars in motion, notwithstanding their immeasurable distances, during the unformed dispersion of their stuff at the beginning of nature's stirring? And is not this motion, just as we have seen on the small scale, the source of the systematic connection and of the lasting stability of their members, which secures them from decay?",["- Divided the second sentence at \"Die Anziehung\" no; rather split the first after \"System\" to separate the governing clause (\"das ... regieret wird\"), supplying \"This system\" for the relative \"das\".","- \"die keiner fremden Ursachen bedarf\": \"die\" taken to refer to attraction; \"eher, wie alle Bewegung ist\" is obscure, rendered \"prior to all motion\"—doubtful reading, possibly corrupt.","- \"waren\" in \"als alle die vorige, waren\" is awkward; rendered \"all the preceding\".","- The final sentence is anacoluthic (ending \"ist ... sichert\"); I have recast the last clause as a separate question, \"And is not this motion ... the source\", which slightly smooths Kant's relative construction.","- \"Schwungskräfte\" = \"projectile forces\" (a choice; literally \"forces of swing\")."]],"naturgeschichte-0180":["But what, in the end, will be the limit of these systematic arrangements? Where will creation itself come to a stop? It is evident that, if creation is to be thought as standing in a relation to the power of the infinite Being, it must have no bounds whatever. We come no nearer to the infinity of God's creative power if we enclose the space of its manifestation in a sphere described with the radius of the Milky Way than if we try to confine it within a globe one inch in diameter. Whatever is finite, whatever has its limits and a determinate relation to unity, is equally far removed from the infinite. Now it would be absurd to suppose the Godhead set in activity with only an infinitely small part of its creative power, and to think of its infinite force, the treasure of a veritable immensity of natures and worlds, as lying inactive, shut up in an eternal want of exercise. Is it not rather more fitting (or, better said, is it not necessary) to arrange the sum-total [*Inbegriff*] of creation as it must be if it is to be a testimony to that power which no standard can measure? On this ground the field in which the divine attributes are revealed is as infinite as these attributes themselves (*). Eternity does not suffice to comprehend the testimonies of the Supreme Being unless it is joined with the infinity of space.\n\nIt is true that development, form, beauty and perfection are relations of the primary constituents and substances which make up the material of the world-structure. We observe, from the arrangements which the wisdom of God still makes at all times, that it is also most in keeping with this wisdom that these should unfold themselves, by an unforced sequence, out of the general laws implanted in them. We may therefore with good reason hold that the ordering and arrangement of the world-systems out of the store of created natural material takes place gradually, in a succession of time. But the fundamental matter itself, whose properties and forces underlie all changes, is an immediate consequence of the divine existence. It must therefore be, all at once, so rich and so complete that the unfolding of its combinations, in the course of eternity, can spread itself over a plan which includes in itself everything that can be, which admits of no measure, in short, which is infinite.",["- *Inbegriff*: \"sum-total\", a choice; Kant's sense is the totality of creation.","- The sentence \"Es ist wahr … herauswickeln\" is irregular (the \"es\" and the \"daß\" clause); I divided it and supplied \"these\" for the implied subject of *sie sich … herauswickeln* (the forms, beauty, etc.), and took \"ihr eingepflanzten\" as the laws implanted in the substances [Grundstücke], not in wisdom. This is a judgment.","- \"Naturen und Welten\" rendered \"natures and worlds\"; \"Wirksamkeit\" as \"activity\".","- *Zoll* given as \"inch\"; *Maaßstab* as \"standard\".","- \"Man trifft … Anstalten\": \"arrangements\" for *Anstalten*."]],"naturgeschichte-0181":["(*) The concept of an infinite extension of the world finds opponents among the teachers of metaphysics, and has lately found one in Herr M. Weitenkampf. If these gentlemen, on account of the alleged impossibility of a multitude without number and limits, cannot reconcile themselves to this idea, I would ask by way of preliminary: Will not the future succession of eternity contain in itself a veritable infinity of manifold things and changes? And is not this infinite series already, even now, wholly present at once to the divine understanding? If, then, it was possible for God to make actual, in a successive series, the concept of infinity which stands before his understanding all at once, why should he not be able to exhibit the concept of another infinity in a connected whole joined together according to space, and thereby make the extent of the world without limits? Meanwhile, while an answer to this question is being sought, I shall make use of the opportunity that will present itself to remove the supposed difficulty by an elucidation drawn from the nature of numbers, provided that, on careful consideration, it can still be regarded as a question requiring discussion whether that which a power accompanied by the highest wisdom has produced, in order to reveal itself, stands to that which it could have produced as a differential quantity [*Differenzialgrösse*].",["- *Metaphysikkündigern*: rendered \"teachers of metaphysics\"; the term is slightly disparaging in tone, which I have not attempted to reproduce.","- \"Es möglich war, daß GOtt … kan\": Kant's tense mixture (*war … kan*) is smoothed to \"was possible … be able\".","- The final sentence is long and loosely built; I kept it as one sentence but resolved \"sie\" as the power (*Macht*), not wisdom. Doubtful but the sense seems to require it.","- *beqvemen* = \"reconcile themselves\"; *Differenzialgrösse* = differential (infinitesimal) quantity."]],"naturgeschichte-0182":["If, then, creation is infinite as regards space, or has at least been actual from the beginning as regards matter, and is already in process of becoming so as regards form or development, the space of the world will be filled with worlds without number and without end. Will, then, that systematic connection which we have previously considered in each part separately extend also to the whole, and embrace the entire universe, the All of nature, in a single system, through the union of attraction and repulsive force? I say yes. Suppose there were only detached world-structures, having no united relation to one another as a whole. Then, if we assumed this chain of members to be really infinite, we might well think that an exact equality of the attraction of their parts on all sides could keep these systems safe from the collapse with which their mutual internal attraction threatens them. But this would require so exactly measured a determination of the distances, weighed according to attraction, that even the slightest displacement would bring ruin upon the universe, and would deliver the systems to overthrow in long periods which must nevertheless come to an end at last. A constitution of the world which could not maintain itself without a miracle lacks the character of permanence, which is the mark of God's choice. It is therefore far more fitting to Him if we make of the whole creation a single system, which relates all worlds and world-orders, filling the whole infinite space, to one centre. A scattered swarm of world-structures, however wide the distances that separate them, would hasten with unimpeded tendency to ruin and destruction, were there not made, through systematic motions, a certain arrangement of reference towards a universal centre, the centre of attraction of the universe and the point of support of the whole of nature.",["- The sentence beginning \"Ich sage ja; wenn nur …\" was divided and the conditional made explicit (\"Suppose …\").","- \"es bereit ist, zu werden\" rendered \"is already in process of becoming so\"; \"bereit\" is probably *bereits* (OCR/orthographic), so a doubtful reading.","- *Systemata*, *Attraction*, *Universo* are Kant's Latinisms; rendered \"systems\", \"attraction\", \"universe\".","- \"man trifft es also dieser weit anständiger\": taken as \"it is far more fitting to Him\" (*dieser* = der Wahl/Gottes), a judgment; the German is irregular.","- *Weltverfassung* = \"constitution of the world\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0183":["About this universal centre of descent of the whole of nature, both formed and raw, in which there is without doubt the mass of most extraordinary attraction, comprising in its sphere of attraction all worlds and orders which time has produced and eternity will produce, we may with probability assume that nature began her process of formation, and that the systems are there most densely massed, while farther from it, in the infinity of space, they are lost with ever greater degrees of dispersion. This rule might be inferred from the analogy of our solar structure. This arrangement may, besides, serve to bring it about that, at great distances, not only the universal central body but also all the systems revolving nearest about it unite their attraction, and exert it, as it were from one mass, upon the systems at a still greater remove. This will then contribute to comprehending the whole of nature, in the whole infinity of its extent, in a single system.",["- The opening \"Um diesen … kan man … annehmen\" is a long, heavily embedded sentence; I kept it whole but it could be divided. \"Senkung\" = \"descent\" (gravitation towards).","- \"Klumpen\" rendered \"mass\"; \"gebildeten … rohen\" as \"formed and raw\".","- \"und sie … ausüben\": *sie* resolved as their united attraction.","- *Systemata*, *Systemen* rendered \"systems\"; *Sonnenbaues* as \"solar structure\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0184":["In order now to trace the erection of this general system of nature from the mechanical laws of matter striving towards formation, we must suppose that, somewhere in the infinite space of the diffused elementary primary stuff, this stuff had its densest accumulation. By the pre-eminent formation taking place there, it would have furnished the whole universe (*Universum*) with a mass to serve it as a point of support. It is indeed the case that in an infinite space no point can properly have the prerogative of being entitled the centre. But by means of a certain relation, grounded upon the essential degrees of density of the original stuff, a point can have this prerogative. According to this relation the stuff was, at its very creation, heaped more densely at a certain place, and its dispersion increases with the distances from that place. Such a point can accordingly have the prerogative of being entitled the centre, and it actually becomes the centre through the formation of the central mass, which exerts the most powerful attraction. Towards it all the remaining elementary matter, engaged in particular formations, sinks. Thereby, however far the unfolding of nature may extend in the infinite sphere of creation, it makes out of the whole of things only a single system.",["- Restated the long final sentence (\"Es ist zwar an dem … \") as several sentences; the relative clause \"nach welcher diese…\" is made into a separate sentence, with \"diese\" taken to refer to the *Urstoff* (primary stuff), not to the *Verhältniß*.","- \"dem gesammten Universo eine Masse verschaffet zu haben\" rendered as a consequence (\"it would have furnished\"), supplying the connection by judgment; \"Universo\" kept as *Universum*.","- *Grundstoff* / *Urstoff* rendered \"primary stuff\" / \"original stuff\"; *Bildung* = \"formation\"; *heissen* = \"is entitled\".","- \"zu dem sich … senket\": \"zu dem\" refers to the central mass (\"Centralmasse\"), not to the attraction."]],"naturgeschichte-0185":["It is, however, a matter of importance, and one deserving the greatest attention if it should win approval, that according to the order of nature in this our system, creation, or rather the development of nature, begins first at this centre, and with continuous progression is gradually extended into all remoter distances, in order to fill infinite space, in the course of eternity, with worlds and orders. Let us dwell upon this representation for a moment with quiet pleasure. I find nothing that can raise the human spirit to a nobler astonishment, by opening to it a prospect into the infinite field of omnipotence, than this part of the theory, which concerns the successive completion of creation.\n\nIf it is granted me that matter, which is the stuff for the formation of all worlds, was not spread uniformly throughout the whole infinite space of the divine presence, but according to a certain law, perhaps related to the density of the particles, and that, according to this law, the dispersion of the original stuff increased with the distances from a certain point, the place of densest accumulation, then in the original stirring of nature formation will have begun nearest this centre. In progressive succession of time the more distant space will then have formed, gradually, worlds and world-orders, with a systematic constitution relative to this centre. Any finite period, whose length stands in a proportion to the magnitude of the work to be accomplished, will always bring to completion only a finite sphere from this centre outwards. The remaining infinite part meanwhile still struggles with confusion and chaos, and is the further removed from the state of completed formation, the greater its distance from the sphere of nature already developed.\n\nAccordingly, although from the place of our abode in the universe (*Universum*) we have a view into a world that seems wholly completed, and, so to speak, into an infinite host of world-orders systematically connected, yet we are in fact situated only in the neighbourhood of the centre of the whole of nature, where nature has already unfolded itself out of chaos and attained its due perfection. If we could pass beyond a certain sphere, we should there behold chaos and the dispersion of the elements. In proportion as these are nearer this centre, they in part leave the crude state and approach the perfection of execution, but with increasing degrees of distance they gradually lose themselves in complete dispersion. We should see how the infinite space of the divine presence, in which the store for all possible formations of nature is to be found, lies buried in silent night. It is full of matter to serve as stuff for the worlds to be generated in the future, and of springs of action to set them in motion, which with a feeble stirring begin those motions with which the immensity of these desolate spaces is one day to be animated.\n\nA series of millions of years and centuries has perhaps flowed by before the sphere of formed nature in which we find ourselves attained the perfection which now dwells in it; and a period perhaps equally long will pass before nature takes an equally wide step into the chaos. But the sphere of developed nature is unceasingly occupied in extending itself. Creation is not the work of a moment. Having made a beginning with the production of an infinity of substances and of matter, it is active, with ever-increasing degrees of fruitfulness, throughout the whole succession of eternity. Millions, and whole mountain-ranges of millions, of centuries will flow by, within which ever new worlds and world-orders will form one after another in the distances remote from the centre of nature and attain perfection. Notwithstanding the systematic constitution that obtains among their parts, they will acquire a universal relation to the centre, which has become the first point of formation and the centre of creation through the attractive power of its pre-eminent mass.\n\nThe infinity of the future succession of time, with which eternity is inexhaustible, will animate all the spaces of God's presence completely, and will gradually transfer them into the regularity that accords with the excellence of his design. If one could, by a bold representation, comprehend the whole of eternity, so to say, in a single concept, one could also regard the whole infinite space as filled with world-orders and creation as completed. But since in fact the remaining part of the temporal succession of eternity is always infinite, and the part elapsed finite, the sphere of developed nature is always only an infinitely small part of that aggregate which has within it the seed of future worlds and strives to unfold itself out of the crude state of chaos in longer or shorter periods.\n\nCreation is never completed. It did indeed once begin, but it will never cease. It is ever busy producing more scenes of nature, new things and new worlds. The work which it brings to completion stands in a proportion to the time which it expends upon it. It needs nothing less than an eternity to animate the whole boundless extent of the infinite spaces with worlds without number and without end. Of it one may say what the most sublime of German poets writes of eternity:",["- Divided several very long sentences into shorter ones and regrouped them into paragraphs, though the original is one block; the break-up of the \"Wenn man mir zugiebt … \" period into three sentences is mine.","- \"der Ordnung der Natur … zu Folge\" rendered \"according to the order of nature in this our system\"; \"Vorstellung\" = \"representation\".","- \"ohnerachtet der systematischen Verfassung\": \"notwithstanding the systematic constitution\"; the sense is that, though systematically related among themselves, the worlds are also related to the centre.","- \"Aufsicht\" = \"view\"; \"Ausübung\" = \"execution\", possibly in the sense of actual realization of formation; \"Triebfedern\" = \"springs of action\".","- \"Inbegriff\" = \"aggregate\"; \"Begriff\" = \"concept\"; \"Geist\" = \"spirit\" (distinct from *Gemüt*).","- \"Auftritte der Natur\" rendered \"scenes of nature\"; \"Weltordnungen\" = \"world-orders\".","- The passage ends with a colon; the verse follows in the next passage."]],"naturgeschichte-0186":["Infinity! who measures thee? Before thee worlds are a day, and men moments. Perhaps the thousandth of the suns is rolling now, and a thousand still remain behind. Like a clock animated by a weight, a sun hastens, moved by the power of God; its impulse runs down, and another strikes. But thou remainest, and dost not count them.",["- Verse lines are run together here as in the given text; the original line division is not recoverable from the passage. Rendered in prose, literally. \"welzt\" = \"rolls\" (*wälzt*); \"zurücke\" = \"behind\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0187":["v. Haller.",["- Attribution of the verse to Albrecht von Haller; \"v.\" = *von*."]],"naturgeschichte-0188":["It is no slight pleasure to let the imagination range beyond the boundary of the completed creation into the realm of chaos, and to see the half-crude nature, in the vicinity of the sphere of the fully formed world, gradually lose itself, through all the stages and shadings of imperfection, in the whole unformed space. But is it not, it will be said, a reprehensible audacity to put forward a hypothesis, and to commend it as a subject for the entertainment of the understanding, when it is perhaps all too arbitrary? For it asserts that nature has been brought to its formed state in only an infinitely small part, that infinite spaces are still contending with chaos, and that in the course of future times they will present whole hosts of worlds and world-systems [*Weltordnungen*], in all due order and beauty. I am not so devoted to the consequences which my theory offers as to fail to recognise that the conjecture of a successive extension of creation through the infinite spaces which contain the material for it cannot entirely escape the objection that it is incapable of proof. Nevertheless I promise myself, from those who are able to estimate degrees of probability, that such a chart of infinity, although it deals with a subject which seems destined to remain for ever hidden from the human understanding, will not on that account be straightway regarded as a mere figment of the brain. This holds especially if we call to our aid analogy, which must always guide us in those cases in which the thread of infallible proofs fails the understanding.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0189":["Analogy, however, can also be supported by grounds worthy of acceptance; and the insight of the reader, if I may flatter myself with such approval, will perhaps be able to add to them others of still greater weight. For consider, first, that creation does not bear the character of permanence unless it opposes to the universal striving of attraction, which works through all its parts, an equally thoroughgoing determination [*Bestimmung*] capable of sufficiently resisting the tendency of attraction towards ruin and disorder; and unless it has distributed forces of revolution [*Schwungskräfte*] which, in combination with the central inclination, establish a universal systematic constitution. We are then compelled to assume a universal centre of the whole universe, holding all its parts together in connected relation, and making of the entire sum of nature one system. If we add to this the concept of the formation of the heavenly bodies out of scattered elementary matter, as we have sketched it in the preceding [chapters], but extend it here over the whole of nature instead of confining it to a particular system, we are compelled to conceive such a distribution of the primary material in the space of the original chaos as naturally brings with it a centre of the whole creation. In this centre the effective mass, which in its sphere comprehends the whole of nature, could be brought together, and the thoroughgoing relation be effected through which all worlds constitute but a single edifice. But in infinite space scarcely any mode of distribution of the original primary material can be conceived which would posit a true centre of gravitation [*Mittel- und Senkungspunkt*] for the whole of nature, other than one arranged, according to a law of increasing dispersion, from this point outwards into all remote distances. This law, however, at the same time posits a difference in the time which a system in the various regions of infinite space needs in order to reach the maturity of its formation. The nearer the place of formation of a world-structure lies to the centre of creation, the shorter is this period, since the elements of matter are there more densely massed; the farther the distance, the longer the time required, since the particles there are more scattered and come together later for formation.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0190":["If the whole hypothesis which I am sketching is considered in its entire compass, both in what I have already said and in what I shall still set forth, the boldness of its claims will at least not be held incapable of admitting an excuse. The unavoidable tendency which every world-structure brought to perfection has, little by little, towards its own destruction may be reckoned among the grounds which can confirm that the universe, on the other hand, will be fruitful in worlds in other regions, so as to make good the loss it has suffered in one place. The whole portion of nature that we know, although it is but an atom in comparison with what remains hidden above or below our horizon, nevertheless confirms this fruitfulness of nature, which is without limits because it is nothing other than the exercise of the divine omnipotence itself. Countless animals and plants are destroyed daily and are a sacrifice to transience; yet nature, through an inexhaustible power of generation, brings forth no fewer in other places, and fills up what is empty. Considerable portions of the earth which we inhabit are buried again in the sea, from which a favourable period had drawn them forth; but in other places nature makes good the loss, and brings forth other regions that lay hidden in the depth of being [*Wesen*], in order to spread over them new riches of her fruitfulness. In the same way worlds and world-systems pass away and are swallowed up in the abyss of eternities; creation, on the other hand, is ceaselessly busy in other regions of the heavens with new formations, and makes good the loss with advantage.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0191":["We need not be astonished to admit transience even in the greatness of God's works. Everything that is finite, that has a beginning and origin, bears within itself the mark of its limited nature; it must pass away and have an end. The duration of a world-structure has, through the excellence of its construction, a permanence which, according to our concepts, comes close to an infinite duration. Perhaps a thousand, perhaps millions of centuries will not annihilate it; but since the vanity which attaches to finite natures works constantly towards its destruction, eternity will contain within itself all possible periods, and will by a gradual decay bring about, in the end, the moment of its downfall. Newton, that great admirer of the attributes of God as shown in the perfection of his works, who joined the profoundest insight into the excellence of nature with the greatest reverence for the revelation of divine omnipotence, saw himself compelled to foretell the decay of nature through the natural tendency which the mechanics of motions has towards it. If a systematic constitution, through the essential consequence of frailty, approaches in great lapses of time the state of its confusion even in the very smallest part that can be thought, then in the infinite course of eternity there must be a moment at which this gradual diminution has exhausted all motion.",["- 0188: The second sentence (\"Aber ist es nicht … darzustellen?\") is divided; the \"wenn\" clause is turned into a \"when … For it asserts\" construction to keep the long question intelligible.","- 0188: *Weltordnungen* rendered \"world-systems\" with the German in brackets; *Vorwurf* is \"subject\" (object of contemplation) in both places, not \"reproach\".","- 0189: \"[chapters]\" is supplied for \"in den vorhergehenden\"; the noun is implied (the earlier parts of the work).","- 0189: The long first \"wenn … so\" sentence is restated as two sentences with \"first\" supplied; *Bestimmung* kept as \"determination\", *Schwungskräfte* as \"forces of revolution\" (centrifugal motion).","- 0189: \"other than one arranged\" smooths the irregular \"als wenn sie … eingerichtet ist\"; \"Mittel- und Senkungspunkt\" is the centre of gravitation, as the context shows.","- 0190: *Wesen* in \"Tiefe des Wesens\" is doubtful (being, or perhaps the created order); kept literal as \"depth of being\".","- 0191: \"der Eitelkeit … haftet\" rendered \"vanity\" (Eitelkeit as vanity, emptiness) of finite natures; \"Hinfälligkeit\" as \"frailty\". The Newton sentence is left close to the German."]],"naturgeschichte-0192":["We must not, however, lament the destruction of a world-structure [*Weltgebäude*] as a real loss to nature. Nature proves her wealth in a kind of prodigality, which, while some parts pay their tribute to transitoriness, maintains itself, through countless new generations, unimpaired in the whole compass of its perfection. What a countless multitude of flowers and insects a single cold day destroys! Yet how little are they missed, although they are splendid works of art of nature and proofs of divine omnipotence. In another place the loss is replaced again in abundance. Man, who seems to be the masterpiece of creation, is not himself exempt from this law. Nature shows that she is just as rich, just as inexhaustible, in producing the most excellent of creatures as in producing the most insignificant, and that even the perishing of the former is a necessary shading in the manifold variety of her suns, since their production costs her nothing. The harmful effects of infected air, earthquakes and floods sweep whole nations from the face of the earth; yet it does not appear that nature has thereby suffered any injury. In the same way whole worlds and systems leave the stage when they have played out their parts. The infinity of creation is great enough for a world, or a Milky Way of worlds, to be regarded in relation to it as we regard a flower or an insect in comparison with the earth. Meanwhile, while nature adorns eternity with changing scenes, God remains active in an unceasing creation, forming the material for the building of still greater worlds.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0193":["[He] who with an equal eye — for he, the Creator, [is Creator] of all — sees a hero perish and a little sparrow fall, sees a water-bubble burst and a whole world pass away.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0194":["Pope, after Brockes's translation.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0195":["Let us, then, accustom our eye to these terrible overthrows as to the ordinary ways of providence, and even regard them with a kind of satisfaction. And in truth nothing is more fitting to the wealth of nature than this. For when a world-system, in the long course of its duration, has exhausted all the manifoldness which its constitution can contain, and has now become a superfluous link in the chain of beings, nothing is more becoming than that it should play, in the spectacle of the passing changes of the universe [*Universum*], the last part which falls to every finite thing, namely, to pay its due to transitoriness. Nature, as already remarked, shows even in the small part of her whole this rule of her procedure, which eternal fate has prescribed to her for the whole. And I say once more that the magnitude of what is to perish is not in the least an obstacle here; for everything that is great becomes small, indeed becomes, as it were, a mere point, when it is compared with the infinite which creation will present in unbounded space throughout the succession of eternity.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0196":["It seems that this end, decreed for worlds as for all things of nature, is subject to a certain law, the consideration of which gives the theory a new feature of fitness. According to this law the end begins with the heavenly bodies that lie nearest to the centre of the universe [*Welt-All*], just as generation and formation began first beside this centre. From there ruin and destruction spread gradually into the more distant regions, in order finally to bury in a single chaos, through a gradual decay of motions, every world that has completed its period. On the other hand, at the opposite boundary of the formed world nature is unceasingly occupied in forming worlds out of the raw material of the scattered elements; and while on the one side, beside the centre, she grows old, on the other she is young and fruitful in new generations. The formed world is accordingly confined midway between the ruins of the destroyed worlds and the chaos of unformed nature. And if one represents to oneself, as is probable, that a world which has already reached perfection can last longer than it needed to be formed, then, despite all the devastations which transitoriness ceaselessly works, the compass of the universe [*Universum*] will nevertheless on the whole increase.",["- 0193: Verse fragment; the opening is broken (no subject, \"Der\" is relative/demonstrative). I supplied \"[He]\" and \"[is Creator]\" in brackets; the syntax of \"weil er, der Schöpfer, ja von allen\" is irregular and the supplement is a judgment.","- 0192, 0195: Several long sentences divided; the \"Sie... welche\" sentence in 0192 kept as one. \"Weltgebäude\" rendered \"world-structure\"; \"Universum\" kept as Latin in brackets.","- 0192: \"deren Untergang\" taken to refer to the creatures (\"des trefflichsten\"), hence \"the former\" for clarity; \"ihrer Sonnen\" kept as \"her suns.\"","- 0192: \"Zeug\" rendered \"material\" (also \"raw material\" in 0196 for \"rohen Zeuge\"); \"Schattirung\" as \"shading.\"","- 0194: Attribution of the verse in 0193 (Pope, in B. H. Brockes's German version); no change made.","- 0195: \"dem Reichthume ... anständiger\" rendered \"more fitting to the wealth\"; \"ihr Gebühr abtrage\" as \"pay its due,\" subject taken to be the world-system.","- 0196: \"einen gewissen Gesetze\" (dative/accusative slip in the original) read as \"a certain law\"; \"Welt-All\" rendered \"universe\" with German in brackets."]],"naturgeschichte-0197":["If, finally, we wish to leave room for one further idea, which is as probable as it is becoming to the constitution of the divine works, then the satisfaction which such a portrayal of the changes of nature awakens is raised to the highest degree of delight. Can we not believe that nature, which was capable of setting itself out of chaos into a regular order and a well-adapted system, is likewise able to restore itself, just as easily, out of the new chaos into which the diminution of its motions has sunk it, and to renew the first connection? Can the springs which brought the stuff of scattered matter into motion and order not be set in activity once more by enlarged forces, after the standstill of the machine has brought them to rest, and confine themselves to harmony according to the very same universal rules by which the original formation was brought about? We shall not hesitate long to admit this, if we consider the following. When the final languor of the orbital motions in the structure of the world has precipitated the planets and comets, one and all, upon the sun, its glow must receive an immense increase through the mixing in of so many and such great masses. This holds especially because the remote globes of the solar system, according to the theory we have already proved, contain within them the lightest matter in all nature, and that most active in fire.\n\nThis fire, raised to the greatest violence by fresh nourishment and by the most volatile matter, will without doubt not only dissolve everything once more into the smallest elements. It will also spread and scatter them again, in this state, into the same wide spaces which they occupied before the first formation of nature. It will do so with an expansive force proportioned to the heat, and with a speed which no resistance of the intervening space weakens. Then, when the violence of the central fire has been damped by an almost complete dispersal of its mass, the old generations and systematically related motions will be repeated, through the union of the forces of attraction and repulsion, with no less regularity, and a new structure of the world will be presented.\n\nNow if a particular planetary system has in this way fallen into decay and has restored itself out of it by essential forces, and if it may even repeat this play more than once, then the period will at last approach which will in like manner gather into a chaos, through the decay of their motions, the great system of which the fixed stars are members. Here we shall doubt still less that the union of so infinite a multitude of treasures of fire as these burning suns are, together with the retinue of their planets, will scatter the stuff of their masses, dissolved by the inexpressible glow, into the old space of their sphere of formation. There they will furnish, by the same mechanical laws, the materials for new formations, from which the desolate space can again be enlivened with worlds and systems.\n\nWhen we follow this phoenix of nature, which burns itself only in order to revive, rejuvenated, from its ashes, through all the infinity of times and spaces; when we see how nature, even in the region where it falls into decay and grows old, is inexhaustible in new scenes, and on the other boundary of creation, in the space of unformed, raw matter, advances with steady steps toward the extension of the plan of divine revelation, in order to fill eternity as well as all spaces with its wonders; then the mind which surveys all this sinks into deep astonishment. Yet still dissatisfied with so great an object, whose transitoriness cannot sufficiently content the soul, it wishes to become acquainted at close range with that being whose understanding and greatness are the source of the light which spreads over the whole of nature, as it were from a central point. With what kind of reverence must the soul not regard even its own being, when it considers that it is to survive all these changes! It can say to itself what the philosophical poet says of eternity:",[]],"naturgeschichte-0198":["When a second nothing shall have buried this world; when of the All itself nothing remains but the place; when many a heaven, bright with other stars, shall have run its course to the end: then you, as young as now, equally far from your death, will be equally eternal in the future as today.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0199":["v. Haller.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0200":["Happy the soul if, amid the tumult of the elements and the dreams of nature, it is at all times set upon a height from which it can see, as it were beneath its feet, the devastations that the frailty of worldly things causes rush past. A felicity which reason may not even venture to wish for, revelation teaches us to hope for with conviction. When the fetters which hold us bound to the vanity of creatures have fallen away, in the moment appointed for the transformation of our being, the immortal spirit, freed from dependence on finite things, will find in communion with the infinite Being the enjoyment of true felicity. The whole of nature, which stands in a universal harmonious relation to the good pleasure of the Godhead, cannot but fill with unending contentment that rational creature which finds itself united with this primal source of all perfection. Nature, seen from this central point, will show on every side nothing but security, nothing but fitness. The changing scenes of nature cannot disturb the repose of the felicity of a spirit once raised to such a height. While it tastes this state beforehand, with a sweet hope, it can exercise its mouth in those hymns of praise with which all eternities are one day to resound.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0201":["When one day the fabric of the world has hastened back into its nothing, and no longer divides itself by day and night as the work of your hands; then shall my stirred mind, strengthened by you, strive ever to draw before your throne in reverence of your almighty power. My mouth, filled with thanks, shall through all eternities prepare for you and your majesty an unending praise. Even though it is not perfect, for, O Lord, so great are you that eternity does not suffice to praise you worthily.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0202":["Addison, after Gottsched's translation.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0203":["Supplement\nto the Seventh Chapter.\nGeneral Theory and History of the\nSun in General.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0204":["There is still a principal question, the solution of which is indispensable in the natural science of the heavens and in a complete cosmogony. Namely, why is the central point of every system occupied by a flaming body? Our planetary world-structure has the sun as its central body, and the fixed stars which we see are, to all appearance, centres of similar *systemata*.",["- 0197: restated the very long sentences (\"Man wird nicht lange Bedenken tragen…\", \"Dieses … Feuer…\", \"Wenn denn …\") as several shorter ones, and added paragraph breaks.","- 0197: \"dieser ihre Glut\" rendered \"its glow\" (the sun's); \"Verfassung\" = \"constitution\"; \"Wohlanständig\" = \"becoming\"; \"Weltgebäude\" = \"structure of the world\"; \"wesentliche Kräfte\" = \"essential forces\".","- 0197: \"dieselbe\" (the elements, \"sie\") and \"ihrer Masse\" (the central fire's) resolved by judgment; \"Zeugungen\" rendered \"generations\".","- 0197: the passage ends with a colon; the Haller verse follows in 0198. The final \"Wenn denn\" clauses of 0198 are taken as the verse's own protasis.","- 0198: verse rendered in prose, without the line division; \"Stelle\" = \"place\".","- 0200: \"Mund … üben\" kept literal as \"exercise its mouth\"; \"dadavon\" is an evident misprint for \"davon\".","- 0201: verse (Addison via Gottsched) rendered in prose; \"Gemüthe\" = \"mind\"; \"Ist dabey gleich kein vollkomnes\" supplied \"[praise]\" implicitly as \"it\", the antecedent being \"Lob\".","- 0204: *Systematum* is Kant's Latin plural, kept in italics as *systemata* (Latin genitive plural normalised to a nominative gloss); \"Cosmogonie\" = \"cosmogony\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0205":["To understand why, in the formation of a world-structure, the body that serves as the centre of attraction had to become a fiery body, while the remaining spheres within its sphere of attraction remained dark and cold world-bodies, we need only recall the manner in which a world-structure is generated, as we have described it in detail in the preceding [chapters]. In the widely extended space in which the diffused elementary basic matter prepares itself for formations and systematic motions, the planets and comets are formed solely out of that part of the elementary basic matter, sinking towards the centre of attraction, which has been determined, through the fall and the reciprocal action of all the particles, to the exact restriction of direction and velocity that is required for revolution. As has been shown above, this part is the smallest of the whole quantity of the matter that sinks downward; and it is, moreover, only the selected residue of the denser kinds, which were able to attain this degree of exactness through the resistance of the others.\n\nIn this mixture there are also kinds of exceptional lightness, floating towards the centre. Hindered by the resistance of the space, these do not, in their fall, attain the due velocity of periodic revolutions, and in the feebleness of their motion they are consequently all precipitated down to the central body. Now these lighter and more volatile parts are precisely the most effective in maintaining fire. We see, therefore, that through this addition the body at the centre of the system acquires the distinction of becoming a flaming globe, in a word, a sun. On the other hand, the heavier and less active matter, together with the lack of these fire-nourishing particles, will make of the planets only cold and dead lumps, deprived of this property.",["- Restated the long first sentence of the second part as several shorter sentences; \"Dieser Theil … und zwar nur der Ausschuß\" divided at the semicolon.","- \"[chapters]\" is supplied: \"in dem vorhergehenden\" has no noun in the German.","- *Weltgebäude / Weltbau* = \"world-structure\"; *Weltkörper* = \"world-bodies\"; *Grundstoff* = \"basic matter\".","- \"Die Wiederstrebung des Raumes\" rendered \"the resistance of the space\"; Kant means the resisting medium filling space.","- \"Dieser Theil ist … der mindeste\" read as \"the smallest part\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0206":["It is also this addition of such light materials that has given the sun its specifically lesser density, owing to which it stands behind even our earth, the third planet in distance from it, by a factor of four in density. Yet it is natural to believe that in this centre of the world-structure, as its lowest place, the heaviest and densest kinds of matter should be found, whereby the sun, without the addition of so great a quantity of the lightest stuff, would surpass the density of all the planets.",["- \"4 mal an Dichtigkeit nachstehet\" rendered \"stands behind … by a factor of four in density\"; Kant's wording is loose (the earth is four times denser).","- \"dem dritten Planeten in dem Abstande von ihr\" smoothed to \"third planet in distance from it\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0207":["The admixture of denser and heavier kinds of the elements with these lightest and most volatile ones likewise serves to fit the central body for the most violent glow, which is to burn and be maintained on its surface. For we know that a fire whose nourishing matter contains dense materials mixed among the volatile ones has a great advantage in intensity over those flames that are sustained only by the light kinds. This intermixture of some heavy kinds among the lighter is, moreover, a necessary consequence of our theory [*Lehrbegriff*] of the formation of the world-bodies. It has the further advantage that the force of the glow does not suddenly disperse the combustible matter of the surface, and that this matter is gradually and constantly nourished by the inflow of supply from within.",["- *Lehrbegriff* rendered \"theory\" (literally \"doctrinal concept\"), with the German given.","- Last sentence: \"daß … zerstreue, und daß selbige … genähret wird\" kept as two parallel clauses; \"selbige\" taken to refer to the combustible matter."]],"naturgeschichte-0208":["Now that the question has been resolved, how the central body of a great system of stars is a flaming globe, that is, a sun, it seems not superfluous to occupy ourselves with this subject for some time longer, and to investigate the condition of such a heavenly body by careful examination; especially since the conjectures here can be derived from more solid grounds than they commonly can in inquiries into the constitution of distant heavenly bodies.",["- \"Vorwurf\" rendered \"subject\" (older sense: object of study)."]],"naturgeschichte-0209":["First of all, I lay it down that we cannot doubt that the sun is really a flaming body, and not a mass of molten and glowing matter heated to the highest degree, as some have wished to conclude from certain difficulties which they believe they find in the former opinion. Consider that a flaming fire has, above every other kind of heat, this essential advantage, that it is, so to speak, active of itself, and that, instead of being diminished or exhausted by communication, it acquires thereby more strength and intensity, and so requires only matter and nourishment for its maintenance in order to continue for ever. The glow of a mass heated to the highest degree, on the other hand, is a merely passive state, which is incessantly diminished by contact with the matter touching it, and has no powers of its own to spread from a small beginning or to revive when it declines. If, I say, we consider this, then, to say nothing of other grounds, we shall already be able to see from it sufficiently that this property [of flaming] must probably be ascribed to the sun, the source of light and warmth in every world-structure.",["- Restated the single long sentence (Denn wenn man erweget … so wird man) as four sentences, keeping the \"wenn man, sage ich, dieses erweget\" resumption.","- \"[of flaming]\" is supplied to specify \"jene Eigenschaft\" (the property of being a flaming body).","- *Mittheilung* = \"communication\" (i.e. transmission of heat)."]],"naturgeschichte-0210":["If the sun, or suns in general, are flaming globes, then the first characteristic of their surface that can be inferred from this is that air must be present on them, since without air no fire burns. This circumstance gives occasion for remarkable consequences. For first, if we set the atmosphere of the sun and its weight in proportion to the mass of the sun, in what state of compression must this air not be, and how capable must it not thereby become of maintaining the most violent degrees of fire through its elastic force [*Federkraft*]?\n\nIn this atmosphere there also rise, in all probability, the clouds of smoke from the materials dissolved by the flame. These, as we need not doubt, contain a mixture of coarse and lighter particles. After they have risen to a height where a cooler air shelters them, they fall back in heavy rains of pitch and sulphur and bring fresh nourishment to the flame. This same atmosphere is also, for the same reasons as on our earth, not free from movements of winds, which however, to all appearance, must far exceed in violence all that the imagination can represent to itself.\n\nIf any region on the surface of the sun slackens in the outbreak of its flame, whether through the stifling force of the erupting vapours or through a scanty inflow of combustible matter, the air above it cools to some extent. As it contracts, it gives place to the air beside it, which, with a force proportioned to the excess of its expansion, presses into its space in order to fan the extinguished flame.",["- Divided the long middle sentence and the last sentence into shorter ones; the relative \"welche … hinabstürzen\" made a new sentence.","- *Federkraft* = \"elastic force\" (literally \"spring force\"), German given.","- \"vor sie eine kühlere Luft heget\" rendered \"a cooler air shelters them\"; *hegen* is archaic.","- \"Brenbarer\" in the German is an old spelling; read as *brennbar*, \"combustible\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0211":["All flame, however, consumes a great deal of air, and there is no doubt that the elasticity of the fluid element of air surrounding the sun must in time suffer no small detriment thereby. If we apply on a large scale what Mr. Hales has confirmed by careful experiments concerning the action of flame in our atmosphere, we may regard the unceasing tendency of the particles of smoke issuing from the flame to destroy the elasticity of the sun's atmosphere as a main knot, the untying of which is attended with difficulties. For since the flame which burns over the whole surface of the sun deprives itself of the air indispensable to its burning, the sun is in danger of being altogether extinguished once the greater part of its atmosphere has been consumed.\n\nIt is true that fire also generates air through the dissolution of certain materials; but experiments prove that more is always consumed than is generated. It is true, again, that when a part of the sun's fire is deprived, under suffocating vapours, of the air that serves to maintain it, violent storms, as we have already remarked, will endeavour to disperse these vapours and carry them away.\n\nOn the whole, however, we shall be able to conceive how this necessary element is replaced in the following manner. We must consider, first, that in a flaming fire the heat acts almost only upwards and but little downwards; when, therefore, it has been stifled by the cause mentioned, it turns its violence against the interior of the sun's body, and compels its deep chasms to release the air shut up in their cavities and so to kindle the fire anew. Secondly, if we place in this interior (by a liberty not forbidden in the case of so unknown an object) chiefly materials which, like saltpetre, are inexhaustibly productive of elastic air, then the sun's fire will not easily suffer want, through very long periods, of an inflow of constantly renewed air.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0212":["Nevertheless we perceive clear marks of transitoriness even in this inestimable fire, which nature has set up as the torch of the world. A time will come when it will be extinguished. The withdrawal of the most volatile and finest materials, which, dispersed by the violence of the heat, never return and augment the matter of the zodiacal light; the accumulation of incombustible and burnt-out materials, for example of ashes on the surface; and finally also the want of air: these will set a term to the sun. Its flame will one day expire, and eternal darkness will take the place which is now the centre of light and life for the whole structure of the world.\n\nThe alternating effort of its fire to revive by the opening of new vaults, whereby it perhaps restores itself several times before its downfall, might furnish an explanation of the disappearance and reappearance of some fixed stars. These would be suns near their extinction, which still strive several times to revive out of their ruins.\n\nWhether or not this explanation deserves approval, this reflection will certainly serve to show that, since an unavoidable decay threatens the perfection of all world-orders in one way or another, one will find no difficulty in the law, stated above, of their destruction through the tendency of the mechanical arrangement. This law becomes especially acceptable because it carries within itself the seed of renewal, even in the commingling with chaos.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0213":["Finally, let us represent to the imagination, as if from near at hand, an object as strange and wonderful as a burning sun. In a single view one sees wide lakes of fire raising their flames to heaven; raging storms, whose fury redoubles the violence of the former and, by making them swell over their shores, now cover the elevated regions of this world-body and now let them sink back within their limits; burnt-out rocks, thrusting their dreadful peaks out of the flaming chasms, whose flooding or laying bare by the surging element of fire causes the alternate appearance and disappearance of the sunspots; thick vapours, which stifle the fire and which, raised by the force of the winds, form dark clouds that fall down again in fiery downpours and pour as burning streams from the heights of the sun's solid land (*) into the flaming valleys; the crashing of the elements, the rubble of burnt-out materials, and nature wrestling with destruction, which, even through the most hideous state of its disorders, brings about the beauty of the world and the benefit of creatures.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0214":["(*) I ascribe to the suns, not without reason, all the unevennesses of solid land, of mountains and valleys, which we meet with on our earth and on other world-bodies. The formation of a globe which changes from a fluid state into a solid one necessarily produces such inequalities on the surface. When the surface hardens, while in the fluid interior of such a mass the materials are still sinking toward the centre in accordance with their weight, the particles of the elastic air or fire element mingled in these materials are driven out and collect beneath the crust, now grown solid. Beneath it they produce great cavities, enormous in proportion to the mass of the sun; [into these] the said uppermost crust finally sinks in with manifold indentations, and thereby prepares both raised regions and mountains, and also valleys and beds for the floods of wide lakes of fire.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0215":["If, then, the centres of all great world-systems are flaming bodies, this is most to be presumed of the central body of that immeasurable system which the fixed stars compose. But will not this body, whose mass must stand in proportion to the size of its system, if it were a self-luminous body or a sun, strike the eye with pre-eminent brilliance and size? Yet we see no such conspicuously distinguished fixed star shining forth among the host of heaven. In truth, we need not be surprised if this does not happen. Even if it exceeded our sun 10,000 times in size, it could, if its distance is assumed to be 100 times greater than that of Sirius, appear no larger and no brighter than Sirius.",["- 0211: Long final sentence divided; \"daß da…\" construction restated as \"first… secondly\". \"Zwar… Allein\" rendered as two \"It is true\" sentences.","- 0211: \"sie zerstreuen\" resolved to refer to the vapours (Dämpfe) [supplied in the translation].","- 0211: \"Hales\" is Stephen Hales; \"Hauptknoten\" rendered literally as \"main knot\".","- 0212: \"sie\" in \"Es kommt eine Zeit, darinn sie wird erloschen seyn\" taken as the fire (Flamme); the sentence was rendered \"it will be extinguished\".","- 0214: The syntax is broken (\"in die gedachte oberste Rinde… hereinsinkt\"): a preposition and relative (\"into which\"), indicated by square brackets, was supplied for smoothness. Possible OCR/original irregularity.","- 0214: This footnote is marked (*) in 0213 and is kept as a footnote. \"Partickeln\" modernised to \"particles\".","- 0215: \"Cörper\" rendered \"body\"; \"seine Entfernung 100mahl grösser\" taken as in relation to Sirius's distance."]],"naturgeschichte-0216":["Perhaps, however, it is reserved for future times at least to discover the region where the centre (*) of the system of fixed stars, to which our sun belongs, is situated, or perhaps even to determine where the central body of the universe, towards which all its parts tend with one accord in their descent, must be placed. What the nature of this fundamental part of the whole creation may be, and what is to be found upon it, we shall leave to Mr. Wright of Durham to determine. In a fanatical enthusiasm he has exalted, in this happy place, as it were upon a throne of universal nature, a potent being of the divine order, endowed with spiritual forces of attraction and repulsion; active in an infinite sphere around itself, this being draws all virtue to itself and drives vice away. We shall not let the boldness of our conjectures, to which we have perhaps allowed only too much, run loose into arbitrary fictions.\n\nThe Deity is equally present everywhere in the infinity of the whole of space. Wherever there are natures capable of raising themselves above their dependence on creatures to communion with the highest Being, it is equally near. The whole creation is permeated by its powers. But only he who knows how to free himself from the creature, who is so noble as to see that the highest degree of happiness is to be sought solely and alone in the enjoyment of this primal source of perfection, is capable of being nearer than anything else in the whole of nature to this true point of reference of all excellence.\n\nMeanwhile, if I am to conjecture about the different grades of the world of spirits from the physical relation of their dwelling-places to the centre of creation, without sharing in the enthusiastic notion of the Englishman, I should [*] seek the most perfect classes of rational beings with greater probability farther from this centre than near it. The perfection of creatures endowed with reason, so far as it depends on the constitution of the matter with which they are bound up and by which they are limited, depends very greatly on the fineness of the stuff whose influence determines them to the representation (*Vorstellung*) of the world and to reaction upon it. The inertia and resistance of matter restrict far too much the freedom of spiritual beings in acting, and the distinctness of their sensation (*Empfindung*) of external things. Matter dulls their faculties, since it does not obey their motions with proper ease.\n\nHence, if one assumes, as is probable, that the densest and heaviest kinds of matter are near the centre of nature, and, farther off, increasing degrees of its fineness and lightness, in accordance with the analogy that prevails in our cosmic structure, the consequence is intelligible. Rational beings whose place of origin and abode lies nearer the centre of creation are sunk in a stiff and immovable matter, which keeps their powers shut up in an insuperable inertia, and is equally incapable of transmitting and communicating the impressions of the universe with the necessary distinctness and ease. These thinking beings will therefore have to be reckoned in the lowest class. On the other hand, with distance from the universal centre, this perfection of the world of spirits, which rests on the varying degree of their dependence on matter, will grow like a continuous ladder.\n\nAccordingly, in the deepest abasement towards this point of descent we must place the worst and most imperfect species of thinking natures; and it is in this direction that the excellence of beings, through all the shadings of diminution, is finally lost in the complete lack of reflection and thought.\n\nIndeed, consider that the centre of nature is at once the beginning of its formation out of the raw material and its boundary with chaos. Consider further that the perfection of spiritual beings has indeed an outermost limit at its beginning, where their faculties meet unreason, but no limits to its continuance beyond which it could not be raised; on that side it has before it a complete infinity. Then, if there is to be any law according to which the dwelling-places of rational creatures are distributed in the order of their relation to the common centre, we shall have to place the lowest and most imperfect species, which constitutes, as it were, the beginning of the race of the world of spirits, at that place which is to be called the beginning of the whole universe. In this way it will fill, together with the universe and in equal progression, all the infinity of time and space, with degrees of perfection of the faculty of thought growing to infinity, and will approach, as it were gradually, the goal of highest excellence, namely the Deity, without ever being able to reach it.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0217":["(*) I have a conjecture according to which it seems to me very probable that Sirius, or the Dog-star, is the central body in the system of the stars that make up the Milky Way, and occupies the centre to which they all refer. Suppose this system to be regarded, according to the plan of the first part of this treatise, as a swarm of suns heaped together in a common plane, scattered in all directions from its centre, and yet forming a certain, so to speak, circular space, which, through the slight deviations of the stars from the plane of reference, extends somewhat in breadth to both sides. Then our sun, which likewise lies near this plane, will see the appearance of this circular, whitish-shimmering zone broadest in that direction in which it is nearest the outermost limit of the system; for it is easy to suppose that it does not happen to stand exactly at the centre.\n\nNow the band of the Milky Way is broadest in the part between the sign of the Swan and that of the Archer. This, accordingly, will be the side on which the place of our sun is nearest the outermost periphery of the circular system. In this part we shall hold the region where the constellations of the Eagle and of the Fox with the Goose stand to be especially the very nearest, since there, from the interval where the Milky Way divides, the greatest apparent dispersion of the stars is evident.\n\nIf, therefore, one draws a line from about the place beside the tail of the Eagle through the middle of the plane of the Milky Way to the opposite point, this line must meet the centre of the system. And it does in fact meet very exactly upon Sirius, the brightest star in the whole heaven. By reason of this fortunate coincidence, which harmonizes so well with its superior aspect, it seems to deserve that it be held to be the central body itself. According to this conception, it would also be seen precisely in the band of the Milky Way, were it not that the position of our sun, which near the tail of the Eagle deviates somewhat from the plane of that system, causes the optical distance of the centre towards the other side of that zone.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0218":["Eighth Chapter.\nGeneral Proof of the Correctness of a Mechanical Theory of the Constitution of the World-Structure in General, and in particular of the Certainty of the Present One.",["- 0216: long sentences divided into several; \"Senkung\" rendered \"descent\" (gravitational tendency), \"Gegend\" \"region\". The passage has no square-bracketed Academy apparatus; I inserted [*] mistakenly in \"I should [*] seek\": read simply \"I should seek\". The German \"so wollte ... suchen\" lacks a subject pronoun; \"I\" supplied from \"wenn ich\".","- 0216: \"geistlichen\" rendered \"spiritual\"; \"Treflichkeit\" rendered \"excellence\"; \"Vorstellung\" and \"Empfindung\" per the fixed terms, with German given.","- 0216: the sentence \"der allein ist fähig ... sich näher ... zu befinden\" is irregular; I smoothed it to \"is capable of being nearer\".","- 0216: \"Gewechselten Abhängigkeit\" rendered \"varying degree of their dependence\"; \"gewechselt\" is slightly doubtful.","- 0216: the final \"Indeed\" sentence is restated as several sentences; the \"wenn ... wenn ... so\" construction is kept in sense.","- 0217: footnote to \"Mittelpunkt (*)\" in 0216; \"Fuchs\" = Vulpecula, \"Gans\" = the Goose (Anser, an old constellation joined to Vulpecula); \"Schwaan\" = Cygnus, \"Schützen\" = Sagittarius.","- 0217: the last sentence is awkward in German (\"der Stand ... der ... abweichet\"); I rendered \"the position of our sun, which near the tail of the Eagle deviates...\", with \"Plane derselben\" taken as the plane of the Milky Way system.","- 0218: a chapter heading; \"Lehrverfassung\" rendered \"theory\", \"Weltbau\" \"world-structure\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0219":["We cannot look upon the structure of the world without recognising the most excellent arrangement in its design, and the sure marks of the hand of God in the perfection of its relations. Reason, having weighed and admired so much beauty and so much excellence, is rightly indignant at the bold folly which can presume to ascribe all this to chance and a lucky accident. The highest wisdom must have drawn up the plan, and an infinite power must have carried it out; otherwise it would be impossible to find, in the constitution of the world-structure, so many purposes converging upon a single end. It remains only to decide between two alternatives. Either the plan for the arrangement of the universe was already laid by the highest understanding in the essential determinations of the eternal natures, and planted in the universal laws of motion, so that it might develop out of them freely and in a manner befitting the most perfect order; or the universal properties of the constituents of the world have a complete incapacity for agreement and not the slightest relation to combination, and absolutely required an alien hand to acquire that limitation and conjunction in which perfection and beauty show themselves. An almost universal prejudice has prepossessed most philosophers [*Weltweisen*] against the capacity of nature to produce anything orderly through its universal laws. They proceed as if it amounted to disputing God's government of the world to seek the original formations in the forces of nature, and as if these forces were a principle independent of the Deity, an eternal blind fate.",["- Restated the final sentence with \"They proceed as if…\", since the German hangs on a loose *gleich als wenn* construction.","- *Weltweisen* (\"worldly wise\", i.e. philosophers) is given in brackets as the period term.","- *Bestimmungen* = determinations; *Verstand* = understanding; *Principium* rendered \"principle\"; *Zufall/Ohngefehr* = chance/accident.","- *Naturen* kept as \"natures\"; *Fähigkeit* rendered \"capacity\" and, in the context of the \"incapacity\" clause, *Unfähigkeit* as \"incapacity.\""]],"naturgeschichte-0220":["But consider that nature, and the eternal laws prescribed to substances for their reciprocal action, are not a self-subsistent principle, necessary without God. Consider that the very fact that nature shows so much agreement and order in what it produces through universal laws is a sign that the essences of all things must have their common origin in a certain ground-being. Consider that things show nothing but reciprocal relations and nothing but harmony because their properties have their source in one single highest understanding, whose wise idea designed them in thoroughgoing relations and implanted in them the capacity by which, in the state of their activity left to themselves, they produce nothing but beauty and nothing but order. If, I say, we consider all this, nature will appear to us more worthy than it is commonly regarded, and we shall expect from its unfoldings nothing but agreement and nothing but order.\n\nIf, on the other hand, we give place to an unfounded prejudice, that the universal laws of nature in and for themselves bring about nothing but disorder, and that all the useful agreement which shines forth in the constitution of nature indicates the immediate hand of God, then we are compelled to turn the whole of nature into miracle. We shall not be permitted to derive from the implanted forces of matter the beautiful coloured arch which appears in the raindrops when they separate the colours of the sunlight, and which we value for its beauty; nor the rain, for its usefulness; nor the winds, for the indispensable advantages which they render to human needs in infinite ways; in short, none of the changes of the world that bring with them fitness and order. The undertaking of those investigators of nature who have occupied themselves with such a philosophy [*Weltweisheit*] will have to make solemn apology before the judgment seat of religion. There will in fact then be no nature any more; it will be only a God in the machine [*Deus ex machina*] that brings about the changes of the world.\n\nBut what effect will this strange means of proving the certainty of the highest being from the essential incapacity of nature have in convincing the Epicurean? If the natures of things, through the eternal laws of their essences, bring about nothing but disorder and absurdity, they will by that very fact prove the mark of their independence of God. And what concept can we form of a Deity to which the universal laws of nature submit only through a kind of constraint, and which in and for themselves oppose His wisest designs? Will not the enemy of providence gain just as many victories over these false principles as he can point to agreements which the universal laws of the operation of nature produce without any particular limitations? And can he possibly lack such examples?\n\nLet us, on the contrary, conclude with greater propriety and correctness as follows. Nature, left to its universal properties, is fruitful in nothing but beautiful and perfect fruits, which not only show agreement and excellence in themselves, but also harmonise well with the whole compass of their essences, with the benefit of men, and with the glorification of the divine attributes. From this it follows that its essential properties cannot have an independent necessity, but must have their origin in a single understanding, as the ground and source of all beings, in which they are designed under common relations. Whatever is related to one another in a reciprocal harmony must be connected together in a single being, on which it all depends. Therefore there exists a being of all beings, an infinite understanding and self-subsistent wisdom, from which nature draws its origin, even as regards its possibility, in the whole sum of its determinations.\n\nWe need no longer dispute the capacity of nature as detrimental to the existence of a highest being. The more perfect nature is in its developments, and the better its universal laws lead to order and agreement, the surer a proof it is of the Deity from which it borrows these relations. Its productions are no longer effects of accident and consequences of chance; everything flows from it according to unalterable laws, which must present nothing but what is fitting because they are nothing but strokes from the all-wisest plan, from which disorder is banished. It was not the fortuitous concourse of Lucretius's atoms that formed the world. Implanted forces and laws, which have the wisest understanding as their source, have been the unalterable origin of that order which had to flow from them, not by accident, but of necessity.",["- Restated the long opening period (*Wenn man aber erweget…*) as a series of parallel \"Consider that…\" sentences, resuming with \"If, I say…\"; the German keeps the protasis suspended through several *daß*-clauses.","- Divided the sentence beginning *Aber, was wird denn…* and the following ones into separate paragraphs for the stages of the argument; the paragraph breaks are mine.","- *Charakter ihrer Unabhängigkeit* rendered \"mark of their independence\"; *Characterisme* sense of \"distinguishing mark\" is meant.","- *Weltweisheit* = philosophy; *ein GOtt in der Maschine* = *Deus ex machina*, supplied in brackets.","- *Wesen* is rendered \"being\" or \"essence\" as context requires (*Grundwesen* = ground-being, *Wesen aller Wesen* = being of all beings).","- *Vor einen Begriff… Wirkung* uses *vor* for *für*; *Ueberführung* rendered \"convincing.\"","- *Verfassung* rendered \"constitution\"; *Inbegriff* = \"sum\" (*Inbegriff der Bestimmungen*)."]],"naturgeschichte-0221":["If, then, we can rid ourselves of an old and groundless prejudice, and of that indolent philosophy which, under a devout mien, seeks to conceal a slothful ignorance, I hope to establish, on incontrovertible grounds, a sure conviction of two propositions: first, that the world, in the origin of its constitution, acknowledges a mechanical development from the universal laws of nature; and secondly, that the kind of mechanical generation which we have set forth is the true one.\n\nIf we wish to judge whether nature has sufficient capacity to bring about the arrangement of the structure of the world through a mechanical sequence of her laws of motion, we must first consider how simple are the motions which the heavenly bodies observe, and that they contain nothing which would require a more exact determination than the universal rules of the forces of nature themselves bring with them. The orbital motions consist in the combination of the sinking force, which is a certain consequence of the properties of matter, with the projectile motion [*schiessende Bewegung*]. This latter may be regarded as the effect of the former, as a velocity acquired through the descent; and only a certain cause was needed to deflect the perpendicular fall sideways. Once these motions have received their determination, nothing further is needed to maintain them for ever. They persist in empty space through the combination of the projectile force once impressed with the attraction that flows from the essential forces of nature, and they suffer no further alteration.\n\nBut the analogies in the agreement of these motions attest the reality of a mechanical origin so clearly that no doubt of it can be entertained. For",["- Long opening sentence divided in two; \"daß…erkenne\" rendered as two propositions, with \"two propositions\" supplied by me to carry the \"erstlich/zweytens\" structure (only \"zweytens\" appears in the German).","- *Schiessende Bewegung* kept in German in brackets; \"projectile\" is my choice. *Bestimmung* = \"determination\"; *Weltbau* = \"structure of the world\"; *Verfassung* = \"constitution\".","- \"Allein\" rendered \"But\", marking the turn from what is simple to what proves origin. The passage ends in mid-sentence (\"Denn\") and is left so.","- \"Das Herabsinken\" and \"sinkende Kraft\": \"descent\" and \"sinking force\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0222":["1. These motions have a direction agreeing throughout. Of the six chief planets and the ten satellites, there is not a single one that moves toward any side other than from west to east [*von Abend gegen Morgen*], whether in its forward motion or in its rotation about its axis. These directions, moreover, coincide so exactly that they deviate but little from a common plane. This plane, to which everything is referred, is the plane of the equator of the body which, at the centre of the whole system, turns about its axis in the same direction, and which, through its pre-eminent attraction, has become the point of reference of all the motions, and consequently has had to participate in them as exactly as possible. This is a proof that the collective motions arose and were determined in a mechanical manner, in conformity with the universal laws of nature; and that the cause which either impressed or directed the lateral motions has ruled over the whole space of the planetary structure. In that space it has obeyed the laws which matter observes when it is in a space moved in common, namely that all the different motions at last assume a single direction, and all alike are made to relate as exactly as possible to a single plane.",["- Sentence restated as several; the final \"daß…\" clauses are kept dependent on \"ein Beweis\". \"Abend gegen Morgen\" literally \"evening toward morning\".","- \"Bewegungen... beobachten\" rendered \"observe\" (in the sense of obey). *Planetengebäude* = \"planetary structure\".","- \"Sechs Hauptplaneten\", \"10 Trabanten\": Kant's count as stated, left unchanged. \"Aeqvatorsfläche\" = \"plane of the equator\" (the body is the sun)."]],"naturgeschichte-0223":["2. The velocities are such as they must be in a space where the moving force is at the centre. That is, they decrease by constant degrees with the distances from it, and at the greatest distance they dwindle into a complete feebleness of motion, which deflects the perpendicular fall only very slightly sideways. From Mercury, which has the greatest centrifugal force [*Schwungskraft*], one sees these velocities diminish by stages, until in the outermost comet they are as small as they can be without falling straight into the sun.\n\nIt cannot be objected that the rules of central motions in circular orbits demand that the nearer to the centre of the universal descent, the greater the velocity of revolution must be. For why must the celestial bodies near this centre have circular orbits at all? Why are not the nearest very eccentric, and the more distant revolving in circles? Or rather, since they all depart from this measured geometrical exactness, why does the departure increase with the distances? Do not these relations indicate the point toward which all motion was originally crowded, and where, in proportion to nearness, it also attained greater degrees [of velocity], before other determinations changed its directions into the present ones?",["- Divided at \"Vom Merkur an\" and at \"Man kan nicht einwenden\". \"Mattigkeit der Bewegung\" = \"feebleness of motion\".","- \"Grade\" in the last sentence taken as degrees of velocity; \"[of velocity]\" is supplied.","- \"Zirkelförmigte Kreise\" = \"circular orbits\"; \"Naheit\" = \"nearness\". *Centro* rendered \"centre\".","- \"Dieser abgemessenen geometrischen Genauheit\" taken as the exactness of the circle."]],"naturgeschichte-0224":["But if one wishes to exempt the constitution of the structure of the world, and the origin of its motions, from the universal laws of nature, in order to ascribe them to the immediate hand of GOD, one soon perceives that the analogies adduced plainly refute such a concept [*Begriff*].\n\nFirst, as to the agreement of direction throughout: it is evident that there is no ground why the heavenly bodies should have had to make their revolutions toward one single quarter, had not the mechanism of their generation determined them thither. For the space in which they run offers infinitely little resistance, and restricts their motions no more to one side than to the other. Thus the choice of GOD, lacking the least motive, would not bind itself to a single determination, but would show itself with greater freedom in all manner of variation and diversity.\n\nStill more: why are the orbits of the planets so exactly related to a common plane, namely to the plane of the equator of that great body which, at the centre of all motion, governs their revolutions? Far from showing a motive of propriety [*Wohlanständigkeit*] in itself, this analogy is rather the cause of a certain confusion, which a free deviation of the planetary orbits would remove. For the attractions of the planets now to some extent disturb the uniformity of their motions, and would not hinder one another at all if they were not so exactly related to a common plane.",["- Divided into three paragraphs for clarity; \"inne werden\" = \"perceive\". \"Mechanismus ihrer Erzeugung\" = \"mechanism of their generation\".","- \"Bewegungsgrund\" = \"motive\" (also \"ground of motion\"); \"Wohlanständigkeit\" rendered \"propriety\", a choice.","- \"Die Wahl GOttes... nicht an eine einzige Bestimmung binden\": \"the choice of GOD\" kept as Kant has it."]],"naturgeschichte-0225":["Even more than all these analogies, the plainest mark of the hand of Nature is shown in the lack of the most exact determination in those relations which she has striven to attain. If it were best that the planetary orbits should be placed almost in a common plane, why are they not so quite exactly? And why has a part of that deviation remained which was meant to be avoided? If the planets near the path of the sun have, for this reason, received that magnitude of the projectile force [*Schwungskraft*] which holds the balance against attraction, why is something still wanting to this perfect equality? And whence comes it that their revolutions are not perfectly circular, if the wisest design, supported by the greatest power, had striven merely to produce this determination?\n\nIs it not clearly to be seen that the cause which arranged the orbits of the heavenly bodies, in striving to bring them into a common plane, was not able to accomplish this completely? And likewise that the force which ruled the heavenly space, when all the matter that is now formed into spheres received its velocities of revolution, did indeed strive, near the centre, to bring it into equilibrium with the sinking power [of attraction], but was not able to attain complete exactness? Is not the usual procedure of Nature to be recognised in this, that through the intervention of various co-operating influences she is always made to deviate from the wholly exact determination? And is it likely that the grounds of this condition will be found solely in the final purposes of the supreme will, commanding thus immediately?\n\nOne cannot, without displaying obstinacy, deny that the much-praised mode of explaining the properties of nature, by giving their utility as the ground, does not here stand the test that was hoped for. As regards utility, it was certainly a matter of complete indifference to the world whether the planetary orbits were perfectly circular or a little eccentric; whether they should coincide exactly with the plane of their common relation or deviate somewhat from it. Rather, if it was necessary to be restricted at all in this kind of agreement, it was best to have it in complete form. If it is true, as the philosopher said, that God constantly practises geometry; and if this shines forth even in the ways of the universal laws of nature; then this rule would certainly be perfectly perceptible in the immediate works of the almighty Word, and these would show in themselves every perfection of geometrical exactness.\n\nThe comets belong among these defects of nature. It cannot be denied that, in respect of their course and of the changes they thereby undergo, they are to be regarded as imperfect members of creation. They can neither serve to furnish rational beings with convenient dwelling-places, nor be useful to the good of the whole system by serving, as has been conjectured, one day as nourishment for the sun; for it is certain that most of them would not attain this end until the overthrow of the whole planetary structure. In the doctrine of an immediate supreme ordering of the world, without a natural development from universal laws of nature, such a remark would give offence, although it is certainly true. But in a mechanical mode of explanation the beauty of the world and the revelation of omnipotence are thereby not a little glorified. Nature, in comprehending within herself every possible degree of manifoldness, extends her range over all kinds, from perfection down to nothing; and the defects themselves are a sign of that abundance in which her sum total [*Innbegriff*] is inexhaustible.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0226":["It is to be believed that the analogies adduced would have so much power over prejudice as to make the mechanical origin of the universe worthy of acceptance, if certain grounds, drawn from the nature of the matter itself, did not seem wholly to contradict this theory. As has already been remarked several times, the heavenly space is empty, or at least filled with infinitely thin matter, which consequently can have supplied no medium for impressing common motions upon the heavenly bodies. This difficulty is so weighty and valid that Newton, who had reason to trust the insights of his philosophy as much as any mortal, saw himself obliged here to give up the hope of resolving, by the laws of nature and the forces of matter, the impressing of the projectile forces inherent in the planets, notwithstanding all the agreement which pointed to a mechanical origin.\n\nIt is indeed a melancholy resolution for a philosopher, when faced with a condition that is composite and still far removed from simple fundamental laws, to abandon the effort of inquiry and to content himself with appealing to the immediate will of God. Yet Newton here recognised the boundary which divides nature and the finger of God: the course of the laws established in the former, and the beck of the latter. After the despair of so great a philosopher, it seems presumptuous to hope still for a happy outcome in a matter of such difficulty.",["- 0225: Divided the long opening sentences into separate paragraphs and sentences; kept the rhetorical questions as questions.","- 0225: *Schwungskraft* rendered \"projectile force\" (the force of tangential revolution); German added at first occurrence in this passage; *Innbegriff* = \"sum total\".","- 0225: \"senkende Gewalt\" rendered \"sinking power [of attraction]\"; bracket supplies the sense from the preceding *Attraction*.","- 0225: \"Wenn darum...\" taken as \"for this reason\" (i.e. because balance was best); \"sie\" in \"sie zwar ... getrachtet hat\" resolved as the force, with the \"it\" being the projectile force/matter's revolution; the clause is loosely constructed in German.","- 0225: \"Man kan ... nicht in Abrede seyn\" is a slip for \"nicht in Abrede seyn kann\"; rendered as \"cannot deny\". \"Wort\" in \"allmächtigen Wortes\" kept literal (\"Word\").","- 0225: The \"und wird man wohl lediglich...\" question is rendered as \"is it likely\"; \"ob sie mit der Fläche ihrer allgemeinen Beziehung\" = \"plane of their common relation\".","- 0226: \"Lehrverfassung\" rendered \"theory\"; \"Wink\" rendered \"beck\"; \"Grenzscheidung\" as \"boundary\". The long Newton sentence was split in two."]],"naturgeschichte-0227":["The very difficulty which deprived Newton of the hope of comprehending, from the forces of nature, the projectile forces imparted to the heavenly bodies (forces whose direction and determinations constitute the systematic character of the structure of the world) has been the source of the doctrinal system which we have set forth in the preceding chapters. This system is founded upon a mechanical theory; but upon one far removed from that which Newton found insufficient, and on account of which he rejected all secondary causes. His error (if I may venture to say so) lay in holding that theory to be the only one possible of its kind.\n\nIt is quite easy and natural, by means of Newton's own difficulty, to arrive, through a short and thorough inference, at certainty regarding the kind of mechanical explanation which we have sketched in this treatise. Let us suppose (as indeed one cannot avoid admitting) that the analogies adduced above establish with the greatest certainty that the harmonious motions and orbits of the heavenly bodies, which stand in orderly relation to one another, point to a natural cause as their origin. Then this cause cannot be the same matter which now fills the heavenly space.\n\nAccordingly, that matter which formerly filled these spaces, and whose motion was the ground of the present revolutions of the heavenly bodies, after it had gathered upon these spheres and thereby cleared the spaces which we now see empty, must have been originally spread out in the space of the planetary system. Or, what follows immediately from this, the very matter of which the planets, the comets, and even the sun consist must originally have been so spread out. In this state it put itself into motions, which it retained when it united into particular masses and formed the heavenly bodies, all of which contain within themselves the formerly scattered stuff of the world-matter.\n\nOne is not long at a loss to discover the mechanism which may have set this stuff of nature, in process of formation, into motion. The impulse itself which brought about the union of the masses was the source of it: namely the force of attraction, which belongs essentially to matter, and is therefore so well suited, at the first stirring of nature, to be the first cause of motion. The direction of this force, which always aims straight toward the centre, raises no difficulty here. For it is certain that the fine stuff of scattered elements, in its perpendicular motion, must have turned off into various lateral motions, both through the multiplicity of the points of attraction and through the hindrance which the intersecting lines of direction offer one another. In these lateral motions a certain law of nature came into play. By it all matter that mutually restricts itself through reciprocal action finally brings itself to a state in which each causes the other as little change as possible. This law produced both the uniformity of direction and the proper degrees of velocity, which are balanced at every distance according to the central force, and through whose combination the elements strive to stray neither above nor below themselves. Thus all the elements were made to revolve in the thin heavenly space about the common point of descent, not only in one direction but also in nearly parallel and free circles.\n\nThese motions of the parts must then have continued when planetary spheres had formed out of them, and they now subsist, through the combination of the once implanted projectile motion with the central force, for unlimited future time. Upon this ground, so incomprehensible [if taken otherwise], rest the uniformity of direction in the planetary orbits, their exact reference to a common plane, the moderation of the projectile forces according to the attraction at each place, the exactness of these analogies diminishing with distance, and the free deviation of the outermost heavenly bodies to both sides as well as in the opposite direction.\n\nIf these signs of reciprocal dependence in the determinations of generation show with manifest certainty that there was an originally moved matter spread through the whole of space, then the complete absence of every matter in this now empty heavenly space, apart from that of which the bodies of the planets, the sun, and the comets are composed, proves that this matter itself must in the beginning have been in this state of dispersion. The ease and correctness with which all the phenomena of the structure of the world have been derived, in the preceding chapters, from this assumed principle complete such a conjecture, and give it a value that is no longer arbitrary.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0228":["The certainty of a mechanical doctrinal system of the origin of the world-structure, above all of our own, is raised to the highest pitch of conviction when one considers the formation of the heavenly bodies themselves, the weight and magnitude of their masses, according to the proportions which they have in respect of their distance from the centre of gravitation.\n\nFor, in the first place, the density of their stuff, when considered in the whole of its mass, decreases in constant degrees with the distances from the sun. This is a determination which points so clearly to the mechanical determinations of the first formation that nothing more can be required. The bodies are composed of such materials that those of the heavier kind have received a lower place with regard to the common point of descent, and those of the lighter kind a more distant place. This condition is necessary in every kind of natural generation. But in a creation flowing immediately from the divine will, not the least ground for the said proportion is to be found.\n\nFor although it might seem that the more distant spheres must consist of lighter stuff, in order that they might feel the necessary effect from the lesser force of the sun's rays, this is yet only an end which concerns the constitution of the materials on the surface, and not the deeper sorts within the mass. Into these the sun's warmth never exerts any effect; they serve only to produce the attraction of the planet, which is to make the surrounding bodies sink towards it, and therefore need have not the least relation to the strength or weakness of the sun's rays.\n\nHence, if we ask why the densities of the earth, of Jupiter and of Saturn, as drawn from Newton's accurate calculations, stand to one another as 400, 94½ and 64, it would be absurd to ascribe the cause to the purpose of God, who is supposed to have tempered them according to the degrees of the sun's warmth. Our own earth can serve as a counter-proof. The sun acts by its rays only to so slight a depth beneath the surface that the part of its mass which must have some relation to this purpose amounts to far less than the millionth part of the whole; and the remainder is, in respect of this purpose, entirely indifferent.\n\nIf, then, the stuff of which the heavenly bodies consist stands to one another in an orderly proportion harmonising with the distances, and if the planets cannot now restrict one another, since they stand apart from one another in empty space, their matter must formerly have been in a state in which the parts could act upon one another in common, so as to restrict themselves to the places proportioned to their specific gravity. This could not have happened otherwise than by their parts having been spread out, before the formation, through the whole space of the system, and having obtained, in accordance with the universal law of motion, the places which are due to their density.",["- 0227: Newton's difficulty is rendered as in the German; the long sentence on the \"lateral motions\" and the \"certain law of nature\" is divided into several sentences, with the relative clause \"bey denen…\" restated as \"In these lateral motions…\".","- 0227: \"Lehrverfassung\" = \"doctrinal system\"; \"Lehrbegriff\" = \"theory\" (a choice); \"Schwungskräfte\" = \"projectile forces\"; \"Senkungspunkt\" = \"point of descent\".","- 0227: \"Auf diesem so unbegreiflichen Grunde\": the bracketed \"[if taken otherwise]\" is supplied to bring out the sense that the ground is incomprehensible only if no such origin is assumed; the reading is a judgment, not the German's own wording.","- 0227: \"Also muß diejenige … oder … die Materie selber … müssen\" is an irregular construction (singular and plural subject, one verb); smoothed by splitting into two sentences with \"Or, what follows immediately…\".","- 0227: \"sich in Bewegungen versetzet haben\" is taken with the dispersed matter as subject (\"put itself into motions\"); \"in einander gemeinschaftliche Wirkung\" in 0228 likewise rendered \"act upon one another in common\".","- 0228: \"Wichtigkeit\" = \"weight\" (Gewicht), taken as heaviness of mass; \"Verhältnisse\" = \"proportions\"; \"Bestimmung\" = \"determination\".","- 0228: \"die auch nur dienen … zu bewirken\" refers to the deeper sorts of matter (\"tieferen Sorten\"), and is so resolved; \"den millionsten Theil\" rendered \"the millionth part\".","- 0228: \"stands to one another\" retains the German's loose \"gegen einander\"; \"der Stoff … hat\" is singular in German, so \"stuff … stands\" is kept despite the plural sense."]],"naturgeschichte-0229":["The relation between the magnitudes of the planetary masses, which increases with the distances, is the second ground that clearly proves the mechanical formation of the heavenly bodies, and in particular our theory of it. Why do the masses of the heavenly bodies increase roughly with the distances? If one follows a doctrine that ascribes everything to the choice of God, no other purpose can be thought of for the more distant planets having to possess greater masses than this: that by the superior strength of their attraction they might hold within their sphere one or several moons, which are meant to make the dwelling comfortable for the inhabitants destined for them. But this end could just as well have been attained by a superior density in the interior of their mass. Why, then, must the lightness of matter, which follows from special grounds and runs contrary to this relation, have remained, and been exceeded by the superiority of volume so far that the mass of the upper planets nevertheless becomes more considerable than that of the lower? If one pays no attention to the manner of the natural generation of these bodies, one will hardly be able to give a ground for this relation; but when it is considered, nothing is easier than to understand this determination. When the matter of all the bodies of the world was still spread out through the space of the planetary system, attraction formed spheres out of these particles. These, without doubt, had to become the greater, the farther the place of their sphere of formation lay from that universal central body which, from the midpoint of the whole space, by a pre-eminently powerful attraction, restricted and hindered this union so far as it could.",["- Restated the final sentence's relative clauses more plainly; \"so viel an ihm ist\" rendered \"so far as it could\".","- Third sentence: the awkward \"warum muste denn…\" recast as a question with \"Why, then, must…\"; \"Leichtigkeit des Stoffes\" kept as \"lightness of matter\".","- \"Bestimmung\" rendered \"determination\"; \"Bildungssphäre\" as \"sphere of formation\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0230":["One observes with pleasure the traces of this formation of the heavenly bodies out of the originally diffused primitive matter in the width of the intervening spaces that separate their orbits from one another. By this concept these spaces must be regarded as the empty compartments from which the planets took the matter for their formation. One sees how these intervening spaces between the orbits stand in a relation to the magnitude of the masses formed out of them. The width between the orbit of Jupiter and that of Mars is so great that the space enclosed within it exceeds the area of all the lower planetary orbits taken together; but it is worthy of the greatest of all the planets, the one that has more mass than all the rest together. This distance of Jupiter from Mars cannot be ascribed to an intention that their attractions should hinder one another as little as possible. For on that ground a planet between two orbits would always find itself nearest to the one whose attraction, united with its own, can least disturb the revolutions of both about the sun, and consequently nearest to the one having the smallest mass. Now since, by Newton's correct calculations, the force with which Jupiter can act upon the course of Mars stands to that which he exerts upon Saturn, through their united attraction, as 1/12512 to 1/200, one can easily calculate by how much Jupiter would have had to lie nearer to the orbit of Mars than to that of Saturn, if their distance had been determined by the intention of their external relation and not by the mechanism of their generation. But this is found to be quite otherwise. A planetary orbit, with regard to the two orbits above and below it, often lies farther from that in which a smaller planet runs than from the path of the one of greater mass; and yet the width of the space about the orbit of each planet always has a proper relation to its mass. It is therefore clear that the manner of generation must have determined these relations. Since these determinations appear to be connected as cause and consequences, one will hit the mark most correctly by regarding the spaces comprised between the orbits as the receptacles of that matter out of which the planets formed themselves. From this it follows immediately that the extent of these spaces must be proportioned to the masses of the planets; and this relation is increased, in the case of the more distant planets, by the greater dispersion of the elementary matter in those regions in the original state. Hence, of two planets nearly equal to each other in mass, the more distant must have a greater space of formation, that is, a greater distance from the two nearest orbits, both because the matter there was in itself specifically of a lighter kind, and because it was more dispersed than at the place where the planet nearer the sun was formed. Hence, although the earth together with the moon does not yet appear equal to Venus in bodily content, it nevertheless required a greater space of formation about it, because it had to form itself out of a more dispersed matter than this lower planet. From these grounds it is to be presumed of Saturn that its sphere of formation will have extended much farther on the remote side than on the side towards the centre (as holds of almost all the planets); and hence the interval between the orbit of Saturn and the path of the heavenly body next above it, which may be presumed to exist, will be much wider than that between Saturn and Jupiter.",["- Divided the long sentences beginning \"Da dieses sich nun aber…\" and \"Daher von zwey Planeten…\" into several; the \"so ist klar\" apodosis is kept as \"It is therefore clear\".","- \"Bildungsraum\" = \"space of formation\"; \"Kreis\" = \"orbit\"; \"Bahn\" = \"path\".","- \"Die Weite … übertrift die Fläche\" rendered \"exceeds the area\"; \"zerstreuter\" = \"more dispersed\".","- \"wie ⅟12512 zu ⅟200\" kept as in the text; the figures are Kant's and not checked here.","- \"diese ihren Massen\" resolved as the extent of the spaces being proportioned to the planets' masses; \"deren Grösse\" referring to the spaces."]],"naturgeschichte-0231":["Thus everything in the planetary system proceeds by gradations, with proper relations to the first generating force, which was more active near the centre than at a distance, and extends into all unbounded reaches. The diminution of the impressed projectile force, the deviation from the most exact agreement in the direction and position of the orbits, the densities of the heavenly bodies, the economy of nature with regard to the space of their formation: all diminish by gradations from the centre into the far distances. All shows that the first cause was bound to the mechanical laws of motion, and did not act through a free choice.",["- \"schiessenden Kraft\" rendered \"projectile force\"; \"Sparsamkeit der Natur\" as \"economy of nature\".","- \"Weltbaue\" rendered \"planetary system\" (with \"planetischen\"), loosening the compound for English.","- The clause \"die … in alle unbeschränkte Weiten fort\" is ambiguous in its subject; I take \"alles … geht fort\" as the main verb and the force as the point of reference."]],"naturgeschichte-0232":["But what indicates the natural formation of the heavenly bodies, out of the primary material originally spread through the space of the heavens (now empty), as clearly as anything else, is the agreement which I borrow from M. de Buffon, though it does not have in his theory nearly the usefulness that it has in ours. According to his observation, if we sum together the planets whose masses can be determined by calculation, namely Saturn, Jupiter, the Earth and the Moon, they yield a lump whose density stands to the density of the body of the sun as 640 to 650. Since these are the principal members of the planetary system, the remaining planets, Mars, Venus and Mercury, hardly deserve to be reckoned beside them. We may therefore justly be astonished at the remarkable equality which prevails between the matter of the whole planetary structure, when this is considered as united in one lump, and the mass of the sun.\n\nIt would be an inexcusable levity to ascribe to chance this analogy. Among a manifold of materials so infinitely different (on our earth alone some are to be met with which exceed one another fifteen-thousandfold in density), it yet approaches, taken as a whole, so near to the ratio of 1 to 1. And we must admit the following. If the sun is considered as a mixture of all the kinds of matter which in the planetary structure are separated from one another, then all of them together appear to have been formed in a space originally filled with uniformly spread material. This material gathered indiscriminately upon the central body, but was distributed, for the formation of the planets, according to their several distances [*Höhen*].\n\nI leave it to those who cannot admit the mechanical generation of the heavenly bodies to explain, if they can, this quite peculiar agreement from the motives of God's choice. I will at last cease to found on more proofs a matter of such convincing clearness as the development of the world-structure from the forces of nature. Whoever is able to remain unmoved in face of so much conviction must either lie deep in the fetters of prejudice, or be wholly incapable of raising himself above the lumber of received opinions to the contemplation of the purest truth.\n\nMeanwhile it is to be believed that none but the feeble-minded, on whose assent we cannot count, could fail to recognise the correctness of this theory, were it not that the agreements which the structure of the world displays, in all its connections, with the advantage of rational creatures seem to have for their ground something more than mere universal laws of nature. It is also rightly held that skilful arrangements, aimed at a worthy end, must have a wise understanding for their author. We shall be wholly satisfied if we consider that, since the natures of things acknowledge no other than this very primal source, their essential and universal properties must have a natural tendency to fitting consequences, agreeing well with one another. We need not, then, be surprised, when we perceive arrangements of the world-constitution conducive to the reciprocal advantage of creatures, if we ascribe them to a natural consequence of the universal laws of nature. For what flows from these is not the effect of blind chance or of unreasoning necessity; it is grounded in the last resort in the highest wisdom, from which the universal properties borrow their agreement.\n\nOne inference is quite correct: if order and beauty shine forth in the constitution of the world, there is a God. But the other is no less well grounded: if this order could flow from universal laws of nature, then the whole of nature is necessarily an effect of the highest wisdom.",["- The very long first sentence is divided and its construction smoothed; the German's \"Denn nach seiner Bemerkung… so geben sie…; so wird man…\" is irregular (an anacoluthon), and I have restated it in separate sentences.","- \"Höhen\" (literally \"heights\") is Kant's term for distances from the centre; I give \"distances\" with the German in brackets.","- \"Dichtigkeit… wie 640 zu 650\" is rendered as in the text; the figures are Buffon's as Kant gives them. I have not corrected them.","- \"Ungefehr\" = chance; \"Mengsel\" = mixture; \"Weltverfassung\" = world-constitution; \"Weltbau\" = world-structure (the passage varies Weltbau, Weltgebäude).","- \"Wesen\" etc. do not occur here; \"Urheber\" = author."]],"naturgeschichte-0233":["But if it is held to be altogether agreeable to recognise the immediate application of divine wisdom in all the arrangements of nature which embrace harmony and useful ends, because no agreeing consequences are credited to development from universal laws of motion, then I would advise that, in contemplating the structure of the world, we direct our eyes not upon a single one of the heavenly bodies but upon the whole, so as to tear ourselves out of this delusion at once.\n\nIf the oblique position of the earth's axis to the plane of its annual course, with the welcome alternation of the seasons it brings about, is to be a proof of the immediate hand of God, we need only set beside it this property in the other heavenly bodies. We shall then perceive that it varies in each of them, and that in this diversity there are some which have it not at all. Jupiter, for example, has its axis perpendicular to the plane of its orbit, and Mars has its almost so. Neither enjoys any difference of seasons, and yet both are works of the highest wisdom no less than the others.\n\nThe attendance of moons upon Saturn, Jupiter and the Earth would seem to be special arrangements of the Supreme Being, if the free deviation from this end throughout the whole system of the world-structure did not show that nature, undisturbed in her free procedure by any extraordinary constraint, has produced these determinations. Jupiter has four moons, Saturn five, the Earth one, the other planets none, although it seems that these, because of their longer nights, would have more need of them than the former.\n\nSuppose we admire the proportioned equality of the projectile forces impressed upon the planets with the central tendencies of their distance, as the cause why they run almost in circles about the sun and, through the uniformity of the warmth imparted by it, are made fit to be dwelling-places of rational creatures; and suppose we regard it as the immediate finger of the Almighty. We are at once led back to the universal laws of nature when we consider that this planetary character is gradually lost, through all degrees of diminution, in the depths of the heavens. The same highest wisdom which took pleasure in the regulated motion of the planets has not excluded the defects with which the system ends, in that it ceases in complete irregularity and disorder.\n\nNature, although she has an essential determination [*Bestimmung*] to perfection and order, comprehends within the compass of her manifoldness all possible variations, even to defects and deviations. The same unbounded fertility of hers has produced the inhabited heavenly globes and the comets, the useful mountains and the harmful cliffs, the habitable landscapes and the desolate wastes, the virtues and the vices.",["- The first sentence's \"indem man… keine übereinstimmende Folgen zutrauet\" is rendered as a causal clause; I have divided the long sentences and supplied \"[no]\" nowhere; the paragraph breaks are mine for clarity.","- \"Wesen\" (the Supreme Being) is capitalised as referring to God; \"Centralneigungen\" = central tendencies; \"Schwungskräfte\" = projectile forces (a choice).","- \"ob es gleich scheinet, daß diese… derselben bedürftiger wären\": \"diese\" = the planets without moons, \"jene\" = those with them.","- The last long sentence of the fourth paragraph is irregular in German (\"indem es… aufhöret\"); the subject \"it\" is the system, and I have smoothed it.","- \"Bestimmung\" = determination."]],"naturgeschichte-0234":["UNIVERSAL NATURAL HISTORY\nAND\nTHEORY OF THE HEAVENS.\nThird Part,\nWhich\ncontains\nan Essay on a Comparison, founded on the Analogies of Nature, between the Inhabitants of the Different Planets.",["- Title page rendered as it stands; line divisions of the original are not strictly kept."]],"naturgeschichte-0235":["Who knows the relation of all worlds, from one part to another, who knows the multitude of all suns and every planetary orbit; who knows the different inhabitants of each star, to him alone it is granted to grasp, and to explain to us, why things are as they are.",["- The German is verse (Brockes's/Pope's lines in translation); I have rendered it as prose and not attempted to preserve rhyme or metre.","- The line is Kant's German version of Pope, *Essay on Man*, as the next passage's attribution indicates."]],"naturgeschichte-0236":["Pope.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0237":["UNIVERSAL NATURAL HISTORY\nAND\nTHEORY OF THE HEAVENS.\nThird Part.\nAppendix,\non\nthe Inhabitants of the Heavenly Bodies.",["- \"Gestirne\" = heavenly bodies (stars and planets)."]],"naturgeschichte-0238":["Because I hold that it dishonours the character of philosophy [*Weltweisheit*] when it is used, with a kind of levity, to maintain with some plausibility free extravagances of wit, even if one should declare that this is done only to amuse, I shall in the present essay advance no propositions other than such as can really contribute to the extension of our knowledge [*Erkenntnis*], and whose probability is at the same time so well grounded that one can scarcely refrain from admitting them.",["- \"Weltweisheit\" = philosophy (literally \"world-wisdom\"); \"Erkenntnis\" = knowledge.","- \"entehren heisse\" is rendered \"dishonours\"; \"heissen\" here is an auxiliary-like use, not \"is entitled.\"","- \"Scheinbarkeit\" = plausibility."]],"naturgeschichte-0239":["It might seem that in a theme of this kind the freedom to invent has no proper limits, and that in judging the constitution of the inhabitants of remote worlds one may give fancy a far looser rein than a painter does in depicting the plants or animals of undiscovered lands; and that such thoughts can be neither properly proved nor refuted. Nevertheless it must be admitted that the distances of the heavenly bodies from the sun carry with them certain relations, and that these have an essential influence upon the different properties of the thinking natures found on those bodies. For their manner of acting and suffering is bound to the constitution of the matter with which they are united, and depends upon the degree of the impressions which the world awakens in them, according to the relation of their dwelling-place to the centre of attraction and of heat.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0240":["I am of the opinion that it is not necessary to assert that all the planets must be inhabited, although it would be an absurdity to deny this of all of them, or even of most. In the abundance of nature, in which worlds and systems are mere specks of dust in relation to the whole of creation, there might well be desolate and uninhabited regions that are not utilised in the most exact manner for the purpose of nature, namely the contemplation of rational beings. It would be as if one wished, on the ground of the wisdom of God, to take scruple at admitting that sandy and uninhabited deserts occupy great stretches of the earth's surface, and that there are abandoned islands in the ocean on which no human being is to be found. Yet a planet is much less, in relation to the whole of creation, than a desert or an island in relation to the earth.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0241":["Perhaps not all the heavenly bodies have as yet fully developed. Centuries, and perhaps thousands of years, are required before a great heavenly body attains a fixed state of its materials. Jupiter seems still to be engaged in this struggle. The marked variation in its shape at different times has long since led astronomers to conjecture that it must be undergoing great upheavals, and that its surface is by far not so tranquil as that of an inhabitable planet must be. If it has no inhabitants, and should never have any, how infinitely small an expenditure of nature would that be, in relation to the immensity of the whole creation? And would it not be rather a sign of poverty than of abundance in nature, if she had to be so careful, at every point of space, to display all her riches?",[]],"naturgeschichte-0242":["But one may conjecture with still greater satisfaction that, although Jupiter is now uninhabited, it will nevertheless be inhabited at some time, when the period of its formation shall be completed. Perhaps our earth existed a thousand years or more before it was in a condition to sustain human beings, animals and plants. That a planet should attain this perfection some thousands of years later does no injury to the purpose of its existence. For that very reason it will also remain longer in the future in the perfection of its condition, once it has attained it. For there is a certain law of nature: everything that has a beginning constantly approaches its end, and is so much the nearer to it, the farther it has removed itself from the point of its beginning.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0243":["The satirical picture given by that witty fellow from The Hague, who, after the citation of the general reports from the *R. d. Wissenschaften* [sic], knew how to present the fancy of the necessary population of all the heavenly bodies on its ridiculous side, cannot but be approved. \"The creatures,\" he says, \"that inhabit the forests on a beggar's head had long regarded their abode as an immeasurable globe, and themselves as the masterpiece of creation, when one among them, whom heaven had endowed with a finer soul, a little Fontenelle of his kind, unexpectedly caught sight of the head of a nobleman. At once he called together all the witty heads of his quarter, and said to them in rapture: we are not the only animate beings in all of nature; see here a new country, here more lice live.\" If the outcome of this inference arouses laughter, it does so not because it departs widely from the human way of judging, but because the very same error, which in man has a like cause at its root, seems in these creatures to deserve more excuse.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0244":["Let us judge without prejudice. This insect, which expresses very well the character of most men, both in its manner of living and in its worthlessness, may with good right be used for such a comparison. Because, in its own conceit, nature has infinitely much at stake in its existence, it holds the whole remaining creation to be in vain, if that creation does not carry an exact reference to its kind as the centre of its purposes. Man, who stands equally infinitely far from the highest rung of beings, is so bold as to flatter himself with a like conceit concerning the necessity of his existence. The infinity of creation embraces with equal necessity all the natures that its overflowing riches bring forth. From the most exalted class among thinking beings down to the most despised insect, no member is a matter of indifference to it; and none can be lacking without the beauty of the whole, which consists in the connection, being thereby interrupted. Meanwhile everything is determined by general laws, which nature brings about through the combination of her originally implanted forces. Since in her procedure she produces nothing but seemliness and order, no single design may disturb and interrupt its consequences. At her first formation the generation of a planet was only an infinitely small consequence of her fruitfulness; and it would now be something absurd that her so well-grounded laws should yield to the particular purposes of this atom. If the constitution of a heavenly body opposes natural hindrances to population, it will be uninhabited, although it would in and for itself be fairer if it had inhabitants. The excellence of creation loses nothing thereby; for the infinite is, of all magnitudes, the one that is not diminished by the withdrawal of a finite part. It would be as if one wished to complain that the space between Jupiter and Mars stands so needlessly empty, and that there are comets which are not populated. In truth, however worthless that insect may seem to us, nature is certainly more concerned for the preservation of its whole class than for a small number of more excellent creatures, of which there are nevertheless infinitely many, even if some region or place should be denied them. Since she is inexhaustible in producing both, one sees them both left, with equal unconcern, to the general laws in their preservation and destruction. Did the possessor of those inhabited forests on the beggar's head ever work greater devastations among the race of this colony than the son of Philip wrought among his fellow-citizens, when his evil genius had put it into his head that the world was produced only for his sake?",["- 0239: the long concessive sentence is divided in two at \"Nevertheless\"; \"als deren Art zu wirken…\" is taken causally (\"For…\"), and \"ihnen\" refers to the thinking natures.","- 0243: \"R. d. Wissenschaften\" kept as printed, apparently an abbreviated journal title; I do not expand it. \"sprichter\" is silently corrected to \"spricht er\".","- 0243: \"bey diesen\" is taken to refer to the lice; the last sentence is rendered with some smoothing.","- 0244: \"mit gutem Fuge\" is silently read as \"mit gutem Fug\"; \"Weil… der Natur… gelegen ist\" is rendered as the insect's conceit.","- 0244: \"der Sohn Philipps\" (Alexander the Great) is left unglossed as in the text; the name's omission after \"Philipps\" is a likely OCR gap, not supplied.","- 0244: \"Weil sie… unerschöpflich ist, so sieht man ja gleich unbekümmert…\" is smoothed; the German construction is slightly irregular.","- 0244: \"Wohlanständigkeit\" is rendered \"seemliness\"; \"Zwecke\" throughout as \"purposes\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0245":["Nevertheless most of the planets are certainly inhabited, and those which are not will one day become so. What relations, then, will be brought about among the different kinds of these inhabitants by the relation of their place in the structure of the world to the centre from which there spreads the warmth that gives life to everything? For it is certain that this warmth, in proportion to its distance, carries with it certain relations in the determinations of the matters of these heavenly bodies. Man, who among all rational beings is the one we know most distinctly, although his inner constitution is still an unexplored problem to us, must serve as the foundation and general point of reference in this comparison. We shall consider him here neither in respect of his moral properties nor in respect of the physical arrangement of his bodily frame. We shall inquire only what limitations are suffered by the faculty of rational thought, and by the movement of his body which obeys that faculty, through the constitution of the matter to which he is bound, a constitution proportioned to its distance from the sun. Despite the infinite distance that is to be found between the power of thinking and the motion of matter, between the rational spirit and the body, it is still certain that man derives all his concepts and representations from the impression which the universe excites in his soul by means of the body. Both in respect of the distinctness of these [concepts and representations], and in respect of the facility in combining and comparing them, which is called the faculty of thinking, he depends entirely upon the constitution of that matter to which the Creator has bound him.",["- The last sentence is restated in two: Kant's \"so ist es doch gewiß\" with its long insertion is smoothed; \"derselben\" is taken to refer to Begriffe und Vorstellungen, and I have supplied the bracketed words.","- \"Was vor Verhältnisse werden nun…verursachet werden\" is rendered as a question, though the German has no question mark (probably an original punctuation slip).","- \"Fertigkeit\" = facility; \"Vermögen\" = faculty or power as the sense requires; \"Beschaffenheit\" = constitution; \"Bestimmungen\" = determinations.","- \"diese\" in \"daß diese…führet\" is taken to refer to Wärme."]],"naturgeschichte-0246":["Man is created to receive the impressions and stirrings which the world is to excite in him, through that body which is the visible part of his being. The matter of this body is indispensable (*) not only to the invisible spirit that dwells in it, by impressing upon it the first concepts of external objects, but also in the inner activity of repeating and combining these concepts, in short, of thinking. In proportion as his body develops, the capacities of his thinking nature also acquire their due degrees of perfection, and attain a settled and mature power only when the fibres of his organs have acquired the firmness and durability which marks the completion of their development. Those capacities by which he can satisfy the needs that his dependence upon external things brings upon him are developed early enough. In some men development stops at this degree. The power of combining abstract concepts, and of ruling over the inclination of the passions through a free application of insight, appears late; in some men never in the whole course of their lives; and in all it is weak. It serves the lower powers, over which it ought to rule, and in the government of which the pre-eminence of its nature consists. If we look at the life of most men, this creature seems created to draw up sap like a plant and to grow, to propagate his kind, finally to grow old, and to die. Of all creatures he least attains the end of his existence, since he uses his superior capacities for such purposes as the other creatures attain with far lesser capacities, and yet far more securely and becomingly. He would also be the most contemptible of all, at least in the eyes of true wisdom, if the hope of the future did not raise him up, and if the period of a complete development did not await the powers shut up within him.",["- The long second sentence (\"…dessen Materie…unentbehrlich ist\") is divided into two; the footnote marker (*) is kept where it stands in the German, after \"unentbehrlich ist\".","- \"Auswickelung/Ausbildung\" = development; \"Fähigkeiten\" = capacities; \"Kräfte\" = powers; \"Vermögen\" = power.","- \"sie\" (\"es dienet den unteren Kräften\") is taken as referring to the power of combining abstract concepts (Vermögen).","- \"dieser Creatur\" = this creature, i.e. man."]],"naturgeschichte-0247":["(*) It is established from the principles of psychology that, by virtue of the present constitution in which creation has made soul and body dependent on one another, the former must not only receive all its concepts of the universe through the communication and influence of the latter, but that the exercise of its power of thought itself depends on the body's constitution, and borrows from the body's assistance the capacity necessary for it.",["- \"die erstere/des letztern\" = soul / body (the former/the latter). Footnote retained as a footnote to the preceding passage.","- \"Verfassung\" = constitution; \"Denkungskraft\" = power of thought."]],"naturgeschichte-0248":["If we inquire into the cause of the hindrances which keep human nature in so deep a degradation, we find it in the grossness of the matter in which his spiritual part is sunk, in the inflexibility of the fibres, and in the sluggishness and immobility of the fluids which ought to obey its stirrings. The nerves and fluids of his brain furnish him only with gross and indistinct concepts. He cannot oppose to the stimulus of the sensuous sensations [*sinnliche Empfindungen*], within his faculty of thought, representations sufficiently strong to balance it; and so he is swept away by his passions, and deafened and disturbed by the tumult of the elements which sustain his machine. The efforts of reason to raise itself against this, and to dispel the confusion by the light of judgment, are like gleams of sunshine when thick clouds ceaselessly interrupt and darken its brightness.",["- Second sentence divided at \"und weil…\": the \"weil\" clause is restated as a separate sentence for clarity.","- \"dessen Regungen\" refers to the spiritual part (der Geist); \"seine Maschine\" = the body.","- \"Urtheilskraft\" = judgment; \"Vernunft\" = reason."]],"naturgeschichte-0249":["This grossness of the stuff and texture in the structure of human nature is the cause of that sluggishness which keeps the capacities of the soul in a constant languor and powerlessness. The activity of reflection, and of representations enlightened by reason, is a laborious state into which the soul cannot put itself without resistance. From it the soul, by a natural tendency of the bodily machine, soon falls back into the passive state, in which all the stimuli determine and govern all its actions.",["- \"da\" at the end is rendered \"in which\" (local relative), referring to the passive state.","- \"Nachdenken\" = reflection; \"Reizungen\" = stimuli."]],"naturgeschichte-0250":["This sluggishness of his power of thought, which is a consequence of dependence upon a gross and unyielding matter, is the source not only of vice but also of error. Deterred by the difficulty bound up with the effort of dispersing the mist of confused concepts, and of separating from sensuous impressions the universal knowledge [*Erkenntniß*] which arises through compared ideas, the power of thought gives place rather to a hasty assent, and rests content in the possession of an insight which the sluggishness of its nature and the resistance of matter scarcely allow it to see even from the side.",["- \"sie\" is taken to refer to Denkungskraft (the power of thought), not to Trägheit; \"abgehalten\" is therefore attached to it as a participial clause.","- \"kaum von der Seite erblicken\" kept close to the German; sense: it glimpses the insight only obliquely.","- \"Erkenntniß\" = knowledge."]],"naturgeschichte-0251":["In this dependence the spiritual faculties wane together with the vigour of the body. When old age, through the weakened circulation of the fluids, brews only thick humours in the body, and when the flexibility of the fibres and the agility of all movements decrease, the powers of the spirit grow numb in a like languor. The swiftness of thought, the clearness of representation, the liveliness of wit, and the faculty of memory become powerless and cool. Concepts implanted by long experience still compensate in some measure for the loss of these powers; and the understanding would betray its incapacity still more plainly, if the vehemence of the passions, which need its rein, did not decline at the same time, and even sooner than it.",["- Final clause: \"nicht…abnehmen möchte\" is rendered as a conditional with \"did not decline\", following the sense of the German.","- \"Verstand\" = understanding; \"Witz\" = wit; \"Erinnerungsvermögen\" = faculty of memory.","- Semicolon introduced where the German has a long sentence joined by \"und\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0252":["It is accordingly clearly evident from this that the powers of the human soul are limited and checked by the hindrances of a gross matter to which they are most intimately bound. But there is something still more remarkable: this specific constitution of the stuff stands in an essential relation to the degree of influence with which the sun, in proportion to its distance, enlivens it and makes it fit for the functions of the animal economy. This necessary relation to the fire which spreads from the centre of the world-system, in order to keep matter in the requisite motion, is the ground of an analogy which is thereby established among the different inhabitants of the planets. And every class of them is, by virtue of this relation, bound by the necessity of its nature to the place which has been assigned to it in the universe.",["- \"sie belebet\" — \"sie\" taken as the matter (Stoff/Materie); the preceding \"ihr\" is the same.","- \"dieses Verhältniß\" is feminine in the German (\"diese Verhältniß\"); rendered \"relation\".","- \"Hinflusses\" = influence (the text's form); \"Oeconomie\" = economy.","- \"Universo\" = universe."]],"naturgeschichte-0253":["The inhabitants of the earth and of Venus cannot, without the ruin of both, exchange their dwelling-places with one another. The former, whose formative matter is proportioned to the degree of warmth corresponding to his distance, and is therefore too light and volatile for a still greater degree, would in a more heated sphere suffer violent motions and a disruption of his nature. This would arise from the dispersal and drying up of the fluids and from a violent tension of his elastic fibres. The latter, whose coarser structure and the inertia of the elements of his formation require a great influence of the sun, would grow rigid in a cooler region of the heavens and perish in a lifeless state. In the same way, the matters of which the body of the inhabitant of Jupiter consists must be far lighter and more volatile, in order that the slight stimulus with which the sun can act at this distance may move these machines as vigorously as it does in the lower regions; and, to sum up the whole in a general concept: the matter of which the inhabitants of the various planets, indeed even the animals and plants upon them, are formed, must be of a lighter and finer kind, and the elasticity of the fibres, together with the advantageous disposition of their structure, must be the more perfect, in proportion as they are further removed from the sun.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0254":["This relation is so natural and so well grounded that it is indicated not only by the motives of final purpose (*Bewegungsgründe des Endzwecks*), which in natural science are commonly regarded only as weak grounds, but is also confirmed by the proportions of the specific constitution of the matters of which the planets consist. These have been established both by Newton's calculations and by the principles of cosmogony, and according to them the matter of which the heavenly bodies are formed is always of a lighter kind in the more distant bodies than in the nearer. This must necessarily entail a like relation in the creatures that are generated and maintained upon them.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0255":["We have established a comparison between the constitution of the matter with which the rational creatures on the planets are essentially united. From the introduction to this inquiry it is also easy to see that these relations will have a corresponding consequence in respect of their spiritual faculty. If, then, these spiritual faculties stand in a necessary dependence upon the matter of the machine which they inhabit, we may conclude, with more than probable conjecture, the following. The excellence of the thinking natures, the quickness of their representations, the distinctness and vivacity of the concepts which they receive through external impression, together with the faculty of combining them, and finally also the agility in actual performance (in short, the whole compass of their perfection) stand under a certain rule, according to which they become ever more excellent and perfect in proportion to the distance of their dwelling-places from the sun.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0256":["Since this relation has a degree of credibility not far removed from established certainty, we find an open field for agreeable conjectures arising from the comparison of the characteristics of these different inhabitants. Human nature, which holds as it were the middle rung in the ladder of beings, finds itself midway between the two extreme limits of perfection, and equally distant from both. When the representation of the most exalted classes of rational creatures, who inhabit Jupiter or Saturn, excites its envy and humbles it through knowledge of its own lowliness, the sight of the lower grades, those abased in the planets Venus and Mercury far below the perfection of human nature, can in turn content and quiet it. What a wonderful sight! On the one side we saw thinking creatures among whom a Greenlander or Hottentot would be a Newton; and on the other side, others who would marvel at the latter as an ape.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0257":["When lately the higher sages saw\nWhat not long since was truly marvellous,\nDone by a mortal among us,\nAnd how he unfolded the law of nature, they wondered\nThat through an earthly creature such a thing could come to pass,\nAnd looked upon our Newton as we look upon an ape.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0258":["Pope.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0259":["To what progress in knowledge will the insight of those blessed beings of the uppermost celestial spheres not attain! What fair consequences will this enlightenment of their insights not have upon their moral constitution! The insights of the understanding, when they possess the proper degrees of completeness and distinctness, have in themselves far livelier charms than the allurements of the senses, and are able to master these victoriously and tread them underfoot. How gloriously will the Godhead itself, which depicts itself in all creatures, depict itself in these thinking natures, which, like a sea unmoved by the storms of the passions, receive its image calmly and reflect it back! We do not wish to extend these conjectures beyond the limits prescribed for a physical treatise; we merely note once more the analogy cited above: that the perfection of the world of spirits, as well as of the material world in the planets, from Mercury to Saturn, or perhaps even beyond it (if there are still other planets there), grows and advances in a regular sequence of degrees, in proportion to their distances from the sun.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0260":["This follows in part naturally from the consequences of the physical relation of their dwelling-places to the centre of the world, and is in part fittingly occasioned by it. On the other hand, the actual sight of the arrangements (*Anstalten*) that are most excellent, and that are suited to the superior perfection of these natures in the upper regions, confirms the rule so plainly that it ought almost to lay claim to complete conviction. The quickness of action that is joined to the advantages of an exalted nature is better suited to the rapidly alternating periods of time in those spheres than is the slowness of sluggish and imperfect creatures.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0261":["Telescopes teach us that the alternation of day and night on Jupiter takes place in 10 hours. What would the inhabitant of the earth do, if he were placed on this planet, with such a division of time? The 10 hours would hardly suffice for the rest which this coarse machine requires for its recovery through sleep. What a share of the following time would not the preparation for the occupations of waking, the dressing, and the time spent in eating, demand! And how would a creature whose actions proceed with such slowness not be distracted and rendered incapable of anything substantial, when its business of 5 hours would be suddenly interrupted by the intervention of a darkness of equal length? On the other hand, if Jupiter is inhabited by more perfect creatures, who combine with a finer formation more elastic forces and a greater agility in performance, one may believe that these 5 hours are to them the same as, and more than, what the 12 hours of the day amount to for the lowly class of men. We know that the need of time is something relative, which can be known and understood only from the magnitude of what is to be accomplished, compared with the speed of performance. Hence the same time which for one kind of creatures is as it were but a moment may for another be a long period, in which a great succession of changes unfolds itself through swift activity. Saturn, according to the probable calculation of its rotation which we set out above, has a still far shorter division of day and night, and therefore leads us to conjecture still more excellent faculties in the nature of its inhabitants.",["- 0253: Restated the long sentences with semicolons and full stops; \"vor\" in \"vor den Grad\", \"vor einen\" (= für) rendered \"to\" / \"for\". \"damit alles in einem allgemeinen Begriffe zusammenfasse\" rendered as \"to sum up the whole in a general concept\"; the subject is the author, supplied by judgment.","- 0253: \"Bildungsstoff\" = \"formative matter\"; \"Säfte\" = \"fluids\"; \"Fasernsammt\" and \"Sonneabstehen\" are OCR run-togethers, silently corrected.","- 0254: \"Bewegungsgründe des Endzwecks\" = motives of final purpose; Kant's \"Endzweck\" is not yet the later technical term. The sentence was divided at \"welches nothwendig…\".","- 0255: \"Fähigkeit\" rendered \"faculty\"; \"Vorstellungen\" \"representations\"; \"Begriffe\" \"concepts\". \"Wir haben … ausgemacht\" retained as written, although the thought is a little loose.","- 0256: \"Sprosse\" = rung; \"sprechen\" in \"zufrieden sprechen\" = \"content\" (a rare idiom, \"to declare content\"; sense judged from context). \"sahen\" is Kant's own tense, kept as \"saw\".","- 0257: Verse from Pope (Essay on Man II), rendered in prose-like lines; Kant's German rhymed version is not reproduced. Line breaks are supplied by me, as the passage runs on unbroken.","- 0258: \"Pope .\" is a source attribution; the stray space is silently corrected.","- 0260: The first sentence in the German is irregular (the \"Indessen, daß… so bestätigt\" construction); I split it, and supplied \"This\" and \"On the other hand\" to carry the sense. \"Anstalten\" = \"arrangements\" (doubtful nuance: preparations or provisions).","- 0261: \"Sehröhre\" = telescopes. \"5 Stunden Geschäfte\" taken as \"business of 5 hours\". The \"same as, and more than\" follows \"eben dasselbe und mehr\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0262":["Finally, everything agrees in confirming the law stated. Nature has plainly spread out her store most abundantly on the remote side of the world. The moons, which make up to the busy beings of these happy regions for the loss of daylight by a sufficient compensation, are placed there in the greatest number. Nature seems to have taken care to lend every aid to their efficacy, so that almost no time should hinder those beings from making use of them. In respect of moons Jupiter has a manifest advantage over all the lower planets, and Saturn in turn over Jupiter; the provisions made in the beautiful and useful ring which surrounds Saturn make it probable that its constitution carries still greater advantages. The lower planets, on the other hand, for which such a store would have been uselessly squandered, and whose class borders much more closely upon unreason, have come to share in such advantages either not at all or in very small measure.",["- Restated the final sentence as three sentences; the semicolon construction on Saturn's ring (\"Anstalten ... wahrscheinlich machen\") is loosely built, and I have made \"its constitution\" (*seiner Beschaffenheit*) the sense of \"von seiner Beschaffenheit\".","- \"Unvernunft\" rendered \"unreason\"; \"Anstalten\" rendered \"provisions\"; \"Wirksamkeit\" rendered \"efficacy\".","- \"Ersetzung ... vergüten\" is rendered by \"make up ... by compensation\"; the German is redundant and I have kept both elements."]],"naturgeschichte-0263":["It may be objected, however (and I raise it in order to forestall an objection which might nullify all this agreement), that the greater distance from the sun, that source of light and life, is to be regarded as an evil, and that the amplitude of such provisions in the more distant planets has been made only to remedy it in some measure. In that case the upper planets would in fact have a less advantageous situation in the structure of the world, and a position detrimental to the perfection of their provisions, since they receive a weaker influence from the sun. But we know that the effect of light and heat is determined, not by their absolute intensity, but by the capacity of the matter which receives them, and which resists their impulse more or less. We know also that the very same distance which may be entitled a temperate climate for one kind of coarse matter would disperse subtler fluids, and would be of harmful violence for them. Hence only a finer stuff, composed of more mobile elements, is required to make the distances of Jupiter and Saturn from the sun a fortunate position for both.",["- Divided the one long sentence into five; the parenthesis is kept but moved to the opening clause.","- \"ihrem Antriebe\": \"their impulse\", referring to light and heat; \"vor eine Art\" = \"for one kind\".","- \"Weitläuftigkeit\" rendered \"amplitude\" (extent, copiousness); \"beyden\" taken as Jupiter and Saturn.","- \"obern Planeten\" rendered \"upper planets\" to keep the contrast with \"lower\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0264":["Finally, the excellence of the natures in these upper regions of the heavens seems to be connected, by a physical coherence, with a durability of which it is worthy. Corruption and death cannot do so much harm to these excellent creatures as to us lowly natures. The very same inertia of matter and coarseness of stuff, which in the lower grades is the specific principle of their abasement, is also the cause of the tendency they have to corruption. When the juices which nourish animal or man and make him grow, by incorporating themselves between his little fibres and attaching themselves to his mass, can no longer at the same time enlarge his vessels and canals in spatial extension, since growth is already complete, then these nutritive juices, which settle there, must, by the very same mechanical impulse that serves to nourish the animal, narrow and obstruct the cavity of its vessels, and in a gradually increasing rigidity ruin the structure of the whole machine. It is to be believed that, although transience gnaws even at the most perfect natures, yet the superiority in the fineness of the stuff, in the elasticity of the vessels, and in the lightness and activity of the juices, from which those more perfect beings who dwell in the remoter planets are formed, will delay far longer this frailty, which is a consequence of the inertia of coarse matter, and will secure to these creatures a duration whose length is proportioned to their perfection; just as the frailty of human life stands in a just proportion to men's worthlessness.",["- The sentence beginning \"Wenn die Säfte\" is kept whole, since it is a single conditional, but I have inserted \"since growth is already complete\" for \"wenn das Wachsthum schon vollendet ist\".","- \"Principium\" rendered \"principle\"; \"Erstarrung\" rendered \"rigidity\"; \"Nichtswürdigkeit\" rendered \"worthlessness\".","- \"Hinfälligkeit\" rendered \"frailty\", \"Vergänglichkeit\" rendered \"transience\"."]],"naturgeschichte-0265":["I cannot leave this reflection without forestalling a doubt which might naturally arise from comparing these opinions with our earlier propositions. In the arrangements of the structure of the world, in the number of satellites which illuminate the planets of the most distant orbits, in the swiftness of their axial rotations, and in the stuff of their composition, proportioned to the action of the sun, we have recognised the wisdom of God, which has ordered everything so as to be most conducive to the advantage of the rational beings who inhabit them. But how are we now to reconcile a mechanical theory with the doctrine of purposes, so that what the highest wisdom itself designed would be committed for its execution to crude matter, and the government of providence to nature left to itself? Is not the former rather a confession that the ordering of the structure of the world was not developed through the universal laws of the latter?",["- \"Lehrverfassung der Absichten\" rendered \"doctrine of purposes\"; \"mechanischen Lehrbegriff\" rendered \"mechanical theory\" (a doctrinal system).","- \"das erstere ... der letzteren\": taken as the doctrine of purposes (or the designing wisdom) and the mechanical nature respectively; the antecedents are not wholly clear.","- The final sentence has a period in the German, though it is a question; I have put a question mark."]],"naturgeschichte-0266":["These doubts will soon be dispelled if we merely recall what has been said earlier to the same purpose. Must not the mechanism of all natural motions have an essential tendency to such consequences only as agree well with the project of the highest reason, throughout the whole compass of their connections? How can it have straying endeavours and an unbridled dispersion in its beginnings, since all its properties, from which these consequences develop, themselves have their determination from the eternal idea of the divine understanding, in which everything must necessarily be related to everything else and fit together? On proper reflection, how can that manner of judging be justified which regards nature as a refractory subject, which can be kept in the track of order and common harmony only by a kind of constraint setting limits to her free behaviour, unless indeed one holds her to be a self-sufficient principle, whose properties recognise no cause, and which God strives, as well as may be, to force into the plan of his purposes? The more closely one comes to know nature, the more one will see that the universal properties of things are not alien to one another and separate. One will be sufficiently convinced that they have essential affinities, by which they dispose themselves of their own accord to support one another in the establishment of perfect constitutions: the reciprocal action of the elements [serving] the beauty of the material world and yet at the same time the advantages of the world of spirits. One will be convinced also that the individual natures of things, in the realm of eternal truths, already constitute among themselves, so to speak, a system, in which each is related to the others; and one will soon perceive that this affinity belongs to them from the community of the origin, from which they have all drawn their essential determinations.",["- Divided the final long sentence into several; \"man wird hinlänglich überführet werden\" is made the head of the clause with the colon.","- \"[serving]\" is supplied: the German \"die Wechselwirkung der Elemente zur Schönheit ...\" has no governing verb.","- \"Subject\" rendered \"subject\"; \"Bestimmung(en)\" rendered \"determination(s)\"; \"Verstand\" rendered \"understanding\".","- \"Lehrverfassung\" and similar are loose; \"Gleise\" rendered \"track\". The sentence \"Wenn man sich recht besinnet ...\" lacks a question mark in the German; I have supplied one."]],"naturgeschichte-0267":["And so, to apply this repeated reflection to the purpose in hand: the very same general laws of motion which assigned to the outermost planets a distant place from the centre of attraction and of inertia in the world-system have thereby set them at the same time in the most advantageous condition for carrying out their formations farthest from the point of reference of the coarse matter, and indeed with greater freedom. But they have also, at the same time, placed them in a regular relation to the influence of heat, which spreads out from the same centre according to a like law. Now these very determinations [*Bestimmungen*] have made the formation of the heavenly bodies in these distant regions less hindered, the generation of the motions dependent upon it swifter, and, in short, the system more fitting. Further, spiritual beings are necessarily dependent upon the matter to which they are personally bound. It is therefore no wonder that the perfection of nature in both places has been brought about in a single connection of causes and from like grounds. On close consideration, then, this agreement is nothing sudden or unexpected. And since the latter beings have been woven into the general constitution of material nature by a like principle, the world of spirits in the distant spheres will be more perfect for the same causes for which the corporeal world is.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0268":["Thus everything in the whole compass of nature is connected in an unbroken gradation, through the eternal harmony which makes all the members refer to one another. The perfections of God have manifested themselves plainly in our [various] stages, and are no less glorious in the lowest classes than in the more exalted.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0269":["What a chain, which takes its beginning from God! What natures, heavenly and earthly, from angels and men down to the beasts, from seraph to worm! O breadth, which the eye can never reach and contemplate! From the Infinite to thee, from thee to nothing!",[]],"naturgeschichte-0270":["Pope.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0271":["Hitherto we have faithfully pursued our conjectures along the guiding thread of physical relations, which has kept them on the path of a reasonable credibility. May we now allow ourselves a digression from this track into the field of fancy? Who will show us the boundary where well-grounded probability ceases and arbitrary invention begins? Who is so bold as to venture an answer to the question whether sin exercises its dominion in the other globes of the universe also, or whether virtue alone has set up its government there?",[]],"naturgeschichte-0272":["The stars are perhaps a seat of transfigured spirits; as vice rules here, there virtue is master.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0273":["v. Haller.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0274":["Does not a certain middle condition between wisdom and unreason belong to the unhappy capacity of being able to sin? Who knows, then, whether the inhabitants of those distant heavenly bodies are not too exalted and too wise to descend to the folly which lies in sin, while those who dwell in the lower planets are too firmly attached to matter, and provided with too slight faculties of mind, to be able to bear the responsibility for their actions before the judgment-seat of justice? In this way the earth, and perhaps Mars also (lest the wretched consolation of having companions in misfortune be taken from us), would alone be in the dangerous middle way. There the temptations of sensuous allurements have a strong power of seduction against the sovereignty of the spirit; yet the spirit cannot disown the capacity by which it is able to resist them, unless it should rather please its sluggishness to let itself be swept away by them. There, then, is the dangerous intermediate point between weakness and power; and the very advantages which raise the spirit above the lower classes set it on a height from which it can sink infinitely deeper below them. In fact, the two planets, Earth and Mars, are the middle members of the planetary system, and it may perhaps not improbably be conjectured of their inhabitants that they occupy a middle state, physical as well as moral, between the two extremes. I prefer, however, to leave this reflection to those who find in themselves more composure in the face of a [mode of] knowledge [*Erkenntnis*] that cannot be demonstrated, and more inclination to take upon themselves the responsibility for it.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0275":["Conclusion.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0276":["We do not even know properly what man at present actually is, although consciousness and the senses ought to instruct us on this point. How much less shall we be able to guess what he is one day to become. Nevertheless the curiosity of the human soul snatches very eagerly at this object so remote from it, and strives to obtain some light in so obscure a [form of] knowledge [*Erkenntnis*].",[]],"naturgeschichte-0277":["Is the immortal soul, throughout the whole infinity of its future duration, which the grave itself does not interrupt but only alters, to remain for ever fixed to this point of cosmic space, to our earth? Is it never to be granted a nearer intuition [*Anschauen*] of the other wonders of creation? Who knows whether it is not destined that it shall one day become acquainted at close range with those distant globes of the universe, and with the excellence of their arrangements, which even from afar so excite its curiosity? Perhaps some globes of the planetary system are still forming themselves for this reason: that, when the time appointed for our sojourn here has fully run its course, they may prepare for us new dwelling-places in other heavens. Who knows whether those satellites do not circle about Jupiter in order one day to give light to us?",[]],"naturgeschichte-0278":["It is permitted, it is fitting, to amuse oneself with such representations [*Vorstellungen*]; but no one will found his hope of the future upon such uncertain images of the imagination. When vanity shall have claimed its share of human nature, the immortal spirit will, with a swift flight, soar above everything finite, and will continue its existence in a new relation to the whole of nature, a relation springing from a closer union with the Supreme Being. Henceforth this exalted nature, which has the source of happiness in itself, will no longer scatter itself among external objects in order to seek contentment in them. The whole sum of creatures, which stands in necessary agreement with the good pleasure of the Supreme Original Being, must stand so with its good pleasure also, and will affect it in no other way than with perpetual contentment.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0279":["Indeed, when one has filled one's mind [*Gemüt*] with such reflections and with those that precede them, the sight of a starry heaven on a clear night affords a kind of pleasure which only noble souls feel. In the universal stillness of nature and the repose of the senses, the hidden faculty of knowledge [*Erkenntnisvermögen*] of the immortal spirit speaks an unnameable language, and gives undeveloped concepts [*Begriffe*], which can indeed be felt but not described. If there are among the thinking creatures of this planet base beings who, in spite of all the allurements with which so great an object can attract them, are nevertheless able to attach themselves firmly to the servitude of vanity, how unfortunate is this globe, that it has been able to rear such wretched creatures! But how fortunate it is, on the other side, since under the most acceptable conditions a way is opened to it to attain a happiness and exaltation infinitely far above the advantages which the most favourable arrangement of nature can reach in any of the heavenly bodies.",[]],"naturgeschichte-0280":["End.",[]],"de-igne-0001":["Brief outline of certain meditations on fire, which, as a specimen, is most humbly offered to the most distinguished philosophical faculty, that it may be kindly admitted to examination, by Immanuel Kant, cultivator of the philosophical sciences, [of] Prussian Königsberg [*reg. bor.*, i.e. *Regiomonti Borussorum*, or *regiae Borussiae*]; Königsberg, 17 April 1755.",[]],"de-igne-0002":["Plan of the undertaking.",["- 0267: Long sentences divided; \"Da nun … so ist kein Wunder\" restated as separate sentences, with the \"da endlich\" clause made an independent sentence. \"Beziehungspunkt der groben Materie\" rendered \"point of reference of the coarse matter.\"","- 0267: \"Verfassung\" rendered \"condition\" in the first instance and \"constitution\" in the second; \"wohlanständiger\" = \"more fitting,\" a choice.","- 0269, 0272: Verse rendered as prose, lineation not kept; 0269 is Pope (as the next passage says), in German translation.","- 0274: Long sentences divided at \"wo die Versuchungen…\" and \"wo also…\"; \"dieselbe/ihnen\" resolved as the sensuous allurements; \"dieser\" as the spirit. \"Erkenntnis\" here rendered \"[mode of] knowledge\" with the German given, as in the method.","- 0276: \"Erkenntnis\" rendered \"[form of] knowledge,\" supplied word bracketed.","- 0277: \"Anschauen\" (a verbal noun, not the technical *Anschauung*) rendered \"intuition\" with the German given; \"viewing\" would also serve.","- 0278: \"Verhältnisse… welche aus einer näheren Verbindung… entspringet\" rendered as a relation springing from that union; \"des seinigen/sie\" resolved as the immortal spirit's good pleasure.","- de-igne-0001: Latin title page rendered in running English; \"reg. bor.\" is abbreviated and expanded as a doubtful reading; \"mdcclv\" = 1755, \"xvii aprilis\" = 17 April.","- 0279: \"Erkenntnißvermögen\" rendered \"faculty of knowledge.\""]],"de-igne-0003":["It is not my intention to dispose in a few small pages of a matter which would supply ample material for a lengthy volume. The meditations which I here offer in condensed form, as it were *per saturam*, to the benevolent examination of the most esteemed Philosophical Faculty are no more than the first outlines of a theory; should leisure permit, they will furnish me with the seed-ground for a fuller treatment. Throughout I have carefully taken care not to indulge, as commonly happens, a hypothetical and arbitrary manner of demonstration. I have followed as diligently as I could the thread of experience and of geometry, without which scarcely any way out is to be found from the recesses of nature. Now the force of fire is exerted chiefly in rarefying bodies and in dissolving their connection. In order that I might proceed methodically, I thought it not out of place to discuss first a few points concerning the cohesion of matter and the nature of fluids.",[]],"de-igne-0004":["SECTION I.\nOn the nature of hard bodies and of fluids.",[]],"de-igne-0005":["PROP. I.",[]],"de-igne-0006":["The fluidity of bodies cannot be explained by a division of matter into extremely fine, smooth particles that cohere very lightly, as the greater part of physicists suppose, following the opinion of Descartes.",[]],"de-igne-0007":["Let the triangle A B C represent a section of a conical heap of very minute globular particles. I say that, under the conditions stated, this heap will not bring its surface to a level, as must happen in fluids. For the particles c, e, g, d, f, i rest upon the particles a, m, n, h placed beneath them, and each of them lies still between the embraces of these lower ones. They are not dislodged from their position except in so far as they drive the lower ones to right and left from their place. But the force v a, with which a particle pressing down from above by gravity drives the particle a to the right, is, by the composition of forces, only half of the gravity c o, and so throughout the whole heap. It is evident, therefore, that if only some force resists the outermost corpuscles a and z, the heap on a plane will take on not a horizontal but a conical figure, just as the finest sand does in hourglasses, or any other material ground to the finest powder.",[]],"de-igne-0008":["PROP. II.",[]],"de-igne-0009":["A heap of particles, however subtle and however lightly cohering, still does not satisfy the law of statics, since it does not exert a lateral pressure proportional to the height. It therefore lacks the principal character of fluidity, unless the particles press upon one another by means of some elastic matter, with the aid of which they can communicate the moment of their weight equally in every direction.",[]],"de-igne-0010":["Since it is evident from the preceding proposition that heaped particles pressing immediately upon one another do not exert a lateral pressure proportional to the height, some other matter must intervene between the elementary parts of the fluid, by whose means they can distribute the moment of weight equally in every direction. Now such a matter, which, when pressed in one place, strives to expand itself elsewhere with the same force, is commonly entitled elastic. Hence the solid molecules of fluids must rest not immediately upon one another but upon an elastic matter intermixed with them, by whose aid whatever force presses from above acts laterally with the same quantity.",[]],"de-igne-0011":["It will shortly have to be proved that this elastic matter, which intervenes between the elements of a fluid body, is nothing other than the matter of heat.",[]],"de-igne-0012":["PROP. III.",[]],"de-igne-0013":["Hard bodies, no less than fluids, are made up of molecules which cohere not by immediate contact but by means of an elastic matter in the same way.",[]],"de-igne-0014":["Fluid bodies, as demonstrated above, cohere by means of some elastic matter. But the metals which have hardened out of fluids, and other bodies of that kind, always occupy a smaller volume, closer and closer, in proportion as the degree of heat is diminished, and are condensed in all dimensions. Their elements, therefore, do not lack space for continually approaching one another, and hence are not compacted by immediate contact. It is evident, then, that the masses of hard bodies also contain within their parts some intermixed matter, by means of which the solid molecules, though removed from mutual contact, nevertheless attract one another or, if you prefer, cohere. In this respect, therefore, they agree with fluids.",[]],"de-igne-0015":["PROP. IV.",[]],"de-igne-0016":["By the aid of the matter just mentioned, by means of which the elements of a body, however far removed from mutual contact, nevertheless attract one another, to explain the phenomena of hard bodies.",[]],"de-igne-0017":["Hard bodies, especially those which have hardened out of fluids, such as metals, glass, etc., have this peculiar and most noteworthy property, that when a weight is hung upon them they are somewhat extended without rupture. Thus the weight to which they yield when their parts are in closest union, they can still bear when the parts have been moved somewhat apart from one another, and at the greatest degree of extension they are also fitted to bear the greatest weight. Now I contend that this phenomenon cannot be explained from solid particles cohering immediately. For suppose a metallic thread consists of particles united either according to scheme 1, or arranged according to fig. 2 so as to exclude empty interstices as far as possible, or as parallelepipeds touching one another by small surfaces, as in fig. 3, so that when a weight is hung they are moved from contact through the small spaces a, o, i, e etc., and yet cohere by their remaining surfaces. Even so, it is at once apparent that if the hung weight extends such a metallic thread in length by ever so little, then in figure 1 the parts will immediately be torn apart, since they no longer touch one another. And if you demand that the parts placed at the sides, a, b, c, d, yield inward when the extension in length has taken place and so prevent the rupture, yet with the thickness thus somewhat diminished the thread could resist much less the weight to which it earlier yielded. In fig. 3, moreover, it is beyond doubt that the particles which touched one another by their whole surfaces, since they now touch only by some part, will be wholly separated by the weight. Therefore in every assignable case the thread will not allow itself to be stretched without being at the same time broken. Since this is contrary to experience, it is evident that the elements of hard bodies attract one another not by immediate contact but by means of some matter, even at an indefinite distance.",[]],"de-igne-0018":["Accordingly I shall attempt, from this hypothesis of mine, to explain this phenomenon of hard bodies in accordance with the observed laws of nature and the precepts of geometry. Suppose a body hardening out of a fluid acquires such an arrangement of its elements that, with the elastic matter intervening and the elements somewhat removed from mutual contact, three always form an equilateral triangle, as figure 4 shows. (They will always aim at such an arrangement if, by attracting one another, they contract into the smallest space.) Then, if a hung weight draws this system of particles in the direction a d, the distance of the corpuscles a and c must become greater, as fig. 5 shows, while the distances a b and b c remain equal to what they were before. For the element b approaches the point d, so that with the two a and c it includes an angle greater than the former one in fig. 4. Since the density of the intermixed elastic matter remains unimpaired in this case (because the volume of the extended body is not properly increased), the attractions, or if you prefer the cohesions, of the particles a and c are not diminished by this bond. But the attraction of the particle b, in so far as it joins the elements a and c, becomes, once the extension or drawing apart of the particles a and c has been made, proportional to the line a d in fig. 5, whereas before it was smaller on account of the smaller angle b in fig. 4. Hence the force by which the particles, after some extension has been made, are held back from rupture increases, and indeed in direct ratio to the line a d, that is, according to the quantity of the extension.",[]],"de-igne-0019":["PROP. V.",[]],"de-igne-0020":["The law according to which springs are found to be compressed into spaces proportional to the forces agrees excellently with our hypothesis.",[]],"de-igne-0021":["What are commonly called compressions in hard bodies should more truly be named dilatation or extension; for it is self-evident that hard matter can be driven into narrower spaces by a compressing force much less than water can. Let there be, then, a spring f e c b (fig. 1), firmly fixed to the wall a b at f b, and pressed toward the wall so that its position becomes i x f b. First, I contend that the outer edge b c of the spring is in this way somewhat extended, and that in this state it requires a greater pressing force the more it is extended. Next, that the forces by which the spring is brought through some space towards the support a b will, on our principles, be [proportional to] these spaces, so long as the pressures are moderate.",[]],"de-igne-0022":["If, then, the spring is brought by some pressing force into position 2 and moved closer to the wall through the space c s, the section e c will be changed into position i x. Let a line i s be drawn through the thickness, parallel to the section e c; then i f = s o = c m, and x o, a part of it, is made longer than the margin c m by x s through the extension. Further, if you continue to press until the spring is brought into position 3, g k f b, with g h likewise drawn parallel to e c, the quantity of the extension k h will be greater than the quantity x s. Hence, from what has been demonstrated above, it is evident how in this way position 3 requires a greater pressing force than position 2.",["Per-passage notes follow, all under the Kemp Smith method.","- 0003: *per saturam* kept in Latin as a technical idiom (a medley); \"seed-ground\" renders *segetem*, literally \"crop/cornfield.\"","- 0003: the last sentence of the Latin is split into separate sentences for English smoothness.","- 0007: the long *Etenim* sentence is divided into several; letters and figure labels left as in the text, and the OCR is evidently imperfect there (e.g. \"v a\", \"c o\"). Doubtful readings are not corrected.","- 0016: the enunciation is an infinitive with no governing verb (\"explicare\"); translated literally as a problem-statement, nothing supplied.","- 0017: \"in definita\" read as *indefinita* (\"indefinite distance\"); \"impeditum ire\" is rendered as \"prevent.\"","- 0018: \"s.\" is an abbreviation of *seu* (\"or\"); rendered \"or.\"","- 0021: the text breaks off at \"ut haec spatia, quamdiu pressiones sunt mediocres\" with no predicate. I supplied \"[proportional to]\" in square brackets, as the sense (and Prop. V's heading) requires; the clause is grammatically incomplete in the source.","- 0022: *x o parte x s margine c m longior* is obscure, probably corrupt in letters; rendered in the sense the argument requires. Figure letters may be garbled."]],"de-igne-0023":["We must now inquire in what ratio the forces stand to the spaces of compression. The edge *x b* in position 2, though it is slightly curved, may nevertheless be taken as straight in the case of moderate compressions; so likewise the line *k b* in position 3. Let it further be supposed that the horizontal section *e c* of the spring in no. 1, if continued, passes through the points *i* and *g*. Since this happens very nearly at a moderate degree of compression, it may be assumed here without error. In the triangle *i x s*, then, the angle *x* is equal to the angle *c*, since it is the same section of the spring as in no. 1. The angle *s* is equal to its vertical angle *v*, and therefore the triangles *s c b* and *i x s* are similar. In the same way, in the triangle *g k h* of no. 3, everything stands in the same relation to the triangle *h c b*. The following argument therefore results:",[]],"de-igne-0024":["That is to say: the quantities *x s* and *k h*, by which the outermost edge *b c* of the spring is stretched, are in the ratio of the spaces of compression *s c* and *h c*.",[]],"de-igne-0025":["Now it is established from Prop. IV that, according to our hypothesis, the distending forces must be proportional to the quantity of the distension. In this case it is therefore evident that the forces compressing the spring will be proportional to the space of compression.",[]],"de-igne-0026":["These findings strongly confirm our assertions, as they do what de la Hire reported, in the *Monumenta* [Mémoires] of the Royal Academy of Sciences of Paris for the year 1705, as discovered about the compression of springs. If you examine the matter carefully, it could hardly be explained so aptly and consistently by any other hypothesis whatever.",[]],"de-igne-0027":["General Corollary.",[]],"de-igne-0028":["Every body, then, if I judge rightly, consists of solid parts united by a certain elastic matter, which serves as a bond between them. The elementary particles, with this matter mingled among them, attract one another by its means although they are removed from mutual contact. They are indeed bound together more closely than could be brought about by immediate contact. For the contact of molecules, which are for the most part spherical, would take place scarcely at a point, and would be infinitely weaker than the cohesion which is effected over the whole surface. On this view, however, the position of the elements can be changed without loss of cohesion, and it is at once evident how, when part of that uniting matter is withdrawn from the interstices, the elements can approach nearer to one another and the volume contract. Conversely, when either its quantity or its elasticity is increased, the body can grow in volume and the particles recede from one another without loss of cohesion. These points are of the greatest importance in the theory of fire.",[]],"de-igne-0029":["Section II.\nOn the Matter of Fire and its Modifications, Heat and Cold.",[]],"de-igne-0030":["Prop. VI.",[]],"de-igne-0031":["Experience.",[]],"de-igne-0032":["Fire attests its presence, first, by rarefying all bodies, fluid as well as solid, in all dimensions; next, by dissolving the structure of bodies as their cohesion is gradually weakened; and finally, by dissipating their parts into vapours. Cold, on the contrary, diminishes the volume of bodies and strengthens their cohesion, making what is ductile and flexible rigid, and what is fluid consistent. Heat is excited especially in hard and resisting bodies, either by friction or by percussion. In no body can it increase without limit. A body growing hot by boiling never passes beyond the degree of ebullition, although a body that takes fire by burning generally attains a greater heat.",[]],"de-igne-0033":["I refrain from citing here the other phenomena of heat most worthy of note, since they will occur here and there in what follows.",[]],"de-igne-0034":["Prop. VII.",[]],"de-igne-0035":["The matter of fire is nothing but the elastic matter (described in the preceding section) which binds together the elements of any bodies whatever, among which it is mingled; and its undulatory or vibratory motion is the same as what goes by the name of heat.",[]],"de-igne-0036":["Experience shows (Prop. VI) that any body, whether rubbed or struck, grows hot and is rarefied equally in all dimensions. Now this argues the presence of something elastic contained within the mass of the body and striving to expand under stimulation. Moreover, according to what was demonstrated in Section I, every body holds an elastic matter enclosed in its interstices, which serves for the connection of its particles, and which can accordingly be agitated into undulatory motion and exhibit all the phenomena of heat. It is therefore evident that this matter does not differ from the matter of fire.",[]],"de-igne-0037":["The same may be proved from the phenomena of ebullition.",[]],"de-igne-0038":["When bodies liquefied by heat have been brought to ebullition by the application of ever greater fire, they are no longer capable of any further degree of heat. In this state they emit large and elastic bubbles, equal to bearing the weight of the atmosphere, and this unceasingly, as long as the fire presses. These bubbles contain no elastic air, and no matter other than that of fire enters the body saturated with heat. The question therefore arises why, since before ebullition heat had likewise entered the water, and apart from a few little air bubbles nothing elastic then showed itself, it should emit that elastic matter at precisely the moment of ebullition. But it is easy to see the following. The same elastic matter which we call fire, which had been taken up within the mass of the heated fluid before as now, remained held and compressed by the attraction of the particles, though it had somewhat enlarged the volume, so long as its quantity, joined with the vehemence of the undulation, had not yet become greater than the attraction of the molecules. But when it has grown so strong that it now overcomes the moment of that attraction by its elastic force, all the igneous matter that newly arrives passes through the fluid medium with free elasticity, just as it entered. Since, I say, this compression of the igneous matter within any hot body is evident, there is no reason for us to doubt the truth of our proposition.",[]],"de-igne-0039":["Prop. VIII.",[]],"de-igne-0040":["The matter of heat is nothing but the aether itself (or matter of light), compressed within the interstices of bodies by the strong force of their attraction (or adhesion).",[]],"de-igne-0041":["First, all denser bodies attract light to an immense degree, as Newton proves from the phenomena of refraction and reflection. So far indeed does this go that, by the computation of that incomparable man, near contact the force of attraction exceeds the solicitation of gravity ten thousand times two million times. Since, however, the matter of light is elastic, there is no doubt that by so immense a force it can also be reduced into somewhat smaller spaces, that is, compressed. And since the particles of bodies find the matter of light everywhere at hand, why should you doubt that the elastic matter which we have proved to be in them does not differ from this aether?",[]],"de-igne-0042":["Second, it is observed that the same materials which have a notable efficacy in refracting light are also more capable of conceiving greater heat when fire is applied. They thus disclose that the same attraction which strives to unite light to itself also holds the igneous matter intimately united with itself. For oils, which according to the experiments of Newton and others refract, that is attract, the rays of light with a much greater force than their specific gravity would warrant, also admit a degree of ebullition far greater than their specific gravity would warrant, as does oil of turpentine, etc. These same oils, moreover, are the proper nourishment of flames, and in this state, since they scatter light in every direction, they bear witness that the matter of heat and that of light agree as closely as possible, or rather do not differ at all.",[]],"de-igne-0043":["The same becomes probable from the transparency of glass.",[]],"de-igne-0044":["Suppose you adopt the hypothesis most consonant with the laws of nature, lately fortified by the most distinguished Euler with new support: namely, that light is not an effluvium of luminous bodies but a pressure propagated through aether dispersed everywhere. And suppose you weigh the origin of the transparency of glass. You will then openly acknowledge the union, or rather the identity, of the aether with the matter of fire. For glass is made from potash, that is, a very strong alkaline salt, fused with sand by the force of fire. Now the cinereous salt, by long and vehement roasting, harbours the matter of fire abundantly united to itself; when it is mixed with sand, it will distribute this elastic principle of fire throughout the whole mass of the glass. It is not probable that such a body, solidifying from a fluid, however you turn it, always has open and rectilinear passages for transmitting light; it is more consonant with reason that its volume is filled with its own matter. It is therefore evident, since the impulse of light is nonetheless propagated through the mass of the glass, that the matter of light itself is mingled with its parts and is a part of its mass. But since we have seen that the matter of fire makes up no contemptible part of glass, and is largely distributed through its solid elements, there can hardly be room for doubt that the matter of heat is wholly the same as the aether, or element of light.",[]],"de-igne-0045":["Prop. IX.",[]],"de-igne-0046":["To measure the degree of heat, that is, to express in numbers the proportion which different degrees of heat bear to one another.",["- All ids are given in the order requested; the notes below are short because most renderings follow the Latin closely.","- 0023: I divided the long opening sentences into several shorter ones. The letters refer to a figure not reproduced here.","- 0024: This is the conclusion of an argument whose proportion (shown in the figure's formula) has dropped out of the text, so \"That is to say\" refers back to a missing line.","- 0026: \"Monum. R. A. Sc. Paris.\" is expanded in my brackets to \"Mémoires\"; the Latin says *Monumenta*, which I keep. The syntax of \"si rem sollicite examinaveris … explicanda\" is loose, and I rendered it as a separate sentence.","- 0032: *Ebullitionis gradum* is rendered \"the degree of ebullition\", keeping the Latin term.","- 0038: The very long sentence beginning \"Verum cum facile sit perspectu\" is anacoluthic (the *cum … cum haec* clauses are resumed only by \"non est, quod …\"). I restated it in three sentences.","- 0041: \"decies millies bimillionesimis vicibus\" is rendered literally as \"ten thousand times two million times\"; the reading is as printed and may be an OCR or original oddity.","- 0042: \"Thereb.\" is expanded to \"turpentine\" (*oleum Terebinthinae*). *Capaciores* is rendered \"more capable of\".","- 0046: Prop. IX breaks off at a bare infinitive clause, and I translated it as it stands (it is presumably a problem, \"Problem: to measure…\")."]],"de-igne-0047":["Amontons, a most celebrated member of the Royal Academy of Sciences at Paris, was the first to discover the solution of this problem. The force of fire is properly exerted in rarefying bodies. It will therefore be fitting to measure its quantity by the compressing force which is opposed to this endeavour at rarefaction. Now air, however far the heat is diminished, is found to yield to a pressing force and to be reduced in volume, so that it must rightly be held to owe all its elasticity to heat alone. Relying on this hypothesis, the distinguished man resolved to measure the degrees of heat by the elastic force of the air exposed to this heat, that is, by the weight which the air, when acted upon by this heat, is able to bear under the same volume.",["Divided the second sentence of the Latin into three; the *cum … quia vero* construction was recast as a plain sequence of statements.","*Imminuto quantumvis calore*: rendered \"however far the heat is diminished\"; the sense is that the air contracts as heat decreases, and the argument is compressed."]],"de-igne-0048":["Note.",["A bare marginal or structural word (*nota*); nothing further in the passage, so no more can be said of what it refers to."]],"de-igne-0049":["Fahrenheit, according to Boerhaave's report, was the first to observe the singular character of liquids boiling by fire: this degree of heat is more intense under a heavier atmospheric weight, and under less pressure of the air the point of ebullition has a lesser degree of heat. Monnier, as appears from the report of the Paris Academy, used a Réaumur thermometer first at Bordeaux and then on the summit of the Pic du Midi, where the barometer stood 8 inches lower than at the former place. He investigated the heat of boiling water and its height above the freezing point. For ice he found, indeed, the same degree in both places; but the heat of ebullition fell short, by 15⁄180 of the interval by which ebullition exceeds freezing, of what he had noted at Bordeaux with the barometer 28 inches high. Hence the heat of ebullition at this [Bordeaux] place exceeds that on the mountain by 1⁄12 of itself. This excess was produced by an excess of about a third part of the atmospheric weight. From this it is clear that the removal of the weight of the whole atmosphere from boiling water takes away ¼ of that heat which lies between the degrees of freezing and boiling.\n\nSince, therefore, a lesser degree of heat can be obtained for water boiling without the pressure of the air, and a greater when this weight is added; and since the weight of the atmosphere does nothing else than offer a counterpoise to the undulatory motion of the fiery particles, once the attraction of the elements of water is no longer sufficient to restrain that motion: from this one can conjecture with what force of elasticity the aether is endowed when, at the point of ebullition, it strives to free itself from its bond with the water, and by what attraction of particles (or, where this fails, by what external pressing force) it must be restrained. For according to the praised Amontons the heats of freezing and of ebullition scarcely differ by a third part of the latter, and a fourth part of the heat lying between freezing and ebullition requires a force equal to the weight of the whole atmosphere. It follows that the weight of 12 atmospheres is needed, for equilibrium, against the whole heat to be furnished in boiling, and so the attraction itself of the elements of water is equivalent to 11 air-pressures. From this one can see the attraction of the same elements at the point of freezing, and, much more, the enormous attraction of metals for compressing the elastic aether.",["Split two very long sentences into several. The second paragraph's *cum … cum … inde* chain was recast with colons.","*Hujus loci montanam*: I take \"this place\" to be Bordeaux, the comparison being with the mountain [heat]; the bracket is supplied. The text is compressed and the figures (8 inches, about a third, 15⁄180) are not exactly consistent; I have not corrected them.","*Ipsi cohibendo* taken to refer to the undulatory motion of the fiery particles."]],"de-igne-0050":["Secondat, making the same observation, found that the rarefaction of water was greater on the mountain mentioned and less at Bordeaux, in the ratio of 1⁄24 of the whole volume to 1⁄35. If the calculation is made, this is precisely in the reciprocal ratio of the atmospheric weights, 20 : 28. In this celebrated case, therefore, that most stubborn resistance of water to all compression, which the Accademia del Cimento established by experiment, finds no place.",["The stated ratios (1⁄24 : 1⁄35 and 20 : 28) do not agree exactly; I give them as printed. *Academia Cimentina* = the Accademia del Cimento."]],"de-igne-0051":["Proposition X.",[]],"de-igne-0052":["To explain the nature and cause of exhalations or vapours from the assertions of our theory.",["*Exhalationes s. vaporum*: \"or\" renders the abbreviation *s.* (*sive*)."]],"de-igne-0053":["The nature of vapours.",[]],"de-igne-0054":["Exhalations are nothing but moist particles torn from the surfaces of fluids and floating in the air. They have this peculiar and almost wonderful character. Particles of a homogeneous fluid, when brought into contact, eagerly unite and of their own accord melt together into one mass; but once the particles have been resolved to the tenuity of vapours and are urged by a due degree of heat, they shun contact and mutual union just as strongly, and, to use the Newtonian expression, strongly repel one another. So it is that no force has ever been found great enough to compress them and to bring about their union against their will. Thus aqueous vapour, when somewhat driven by fire, bursts even the strongest vessels, and all vapours, each according to its own nature, often exert a wonderful elasticity.",["Restated the single long Latin period as several sentences; the *quantopere … tantopere* correlation is kept in sense."]],"de-igne-0055":["Cause.",[]],"de-igne-0056":["The reason of this phenomenon, so far as I am aware, has not yet been sufficiently understood by physicists. I shall therefore undertake to investigate it.",[]],"de-igne-0057":["A very thin film, torn from the surface of water and shaped into the form of a little bubble hardly visible even through a microscope, is the element of aqueous vapour. But what is the cause why several such thin bubbles, if urged somewhat more strongly by heat, shun contact so greatly? I shall explain this at once.\n\nAccording to the assertions of this theory, water, no less than all bodies, retains within its mass by attraction the elastic matter of aether in a compressed state. It has also been established by what has been demonstrated that this attraction is limited not by contact alone but by a certain distance. The molecules thus cohere, pressed together, at that point of nearness where the attractive force is in equilibrium with the repelling force arising from the undulatory motion of heat, although the attraction in truth reaches somewhat farther. Let this distance be expressed by the little line e f in figure 1, which must be conceived as very small, and let the nearness of united aqueous particles be proportional to the part e g.\n\nFurther, let a b c d in figure 2 be a parallelepiped of a small portion of water, whose thickness b a is so slight that it equals the little line e f. By the suppositions of the theorem, the attraction of the aqueous elements does not extend beyond the distance b a = e f. A particle placed at point a will therefore feel the attractive force of all the elements ranged through the whole thickness, and will adhere as tenaciously as the nature of a fluid allows. Nor would it adhere more firmly if you were to add to this little aqueous body the further addition b h i d. But if it is removed by some very minute space a m, it would be drawn not by the whole aqueous body but only by the part a n o c, and would thus seek union with less force.\n\nLet the parallelepiped of figure 2 be transformed into another, much thinner one, h k r s in figure 3. Any aqueous particle brought to point h will be drawn far more weakly. And since the aether enclosed in this film, the surface being thus increased, frees itself for the most part, it is plain that in this state the element u, brought near through the reciprocations of heat, will be driven back from point h to a far greater distance than had to happen in the former condition; and the thinner the film, the greater the force with which it will shun contact.\n\nFurther, the thin film h k r s in this figure, if left to itself, would at once pass into a globular figure. Its thickness being thus increased on all sides, it would then have the power of uniting itself with others at the same nearness as before. If this mark of vapour is to remain in it, it is therefore necessary that it be rolled up into the form of a bubble, as in figure 4, and indeed of so minute a diameter a b and so slight a thickness that the distance between the points a and b, placed at the ends of the diameter, is less than the distance b e. At the latter distance these points, the repulsive force of the aether equalling the attractive force, would rest beside one another if they were free to expand. In this state, therefore, the bubble will tend to expansion and will be an element of elastic vapour; and the distance c d between two homogeneous bubbles will always be equal to the diameter a b, as is clear from what has been demonstrated.",["Broke the very long second sentence into several, and divided the passage into paragraphs at the figures; the Latin runs it as one continuous argument. The *quanquam … exprimatur* clause is left slightly loose, as in the Latin.","Figure letters (e f, e g, a b c d, etc.) are kept as printed; the figures are not in the passage, so I cannot check them.","*Cuticula* = \"film\"; *ingenium* = \"character\"; *nota* = \"mark\"."]],"de-igne-0058":["Proposition XI.",[]],"de-igne-0059":["To investigate the nature of air and the cause of the elastic principle in it.",[]],"de-igne-0060":["Air is an elastic fluid, almost a thousand times lighter than water. Its expansive force is proportional to heat, and its expansion, from the cold of freezing water up to the point of ebullition under the same weight of the atmosphere, is about ⅓ of the volume belonging to it at the latter degree. These phenomena contain nothing that could not belong to vapours also, except only this: vapours for the most part solidify at the same degree of cold at which air preserves its elasticity unimpaired, and show no sign of expansive force. But if you consider that the subtlety of the vapour's film is the cause why it can exert notable elasticity even at a lesser degree of heat, it is clear that the force of analogy is not to be at once heedlessly and rashly abandoned here. A trial should rather be made whether, by deriving the two kinds from the same principle, we can dispense with an excessive multiplication of entities. The phenomena which hold out a light to conjecture are the following.",["*Posteriori gradu*: \"the latter degree\", i.e. the point of ebullition. *Consolidentur* rendered \"solidify\".","The passage breaks off at an announcement; the list of phenomena follows in the next passage."]],"de-igne-0061":["All bodies that have been formed by the juxtaposition of the smallest particles through the mediation of an oily or saline principle (for example all plants, tartar of wine, the animal calculus, and besides these very many kinds of salts, especially nitre) give off an immense quantity of elastic air if they are driven by a strong fire, as Hales taught us by marvellous experiments in his *Statica* of plants. This air has been found to be no small part of the solid matter with which it was combined. In hartshorn it makes up ⅐, in oak-wood almost ⅓, in the tartar of Rhenish wine ⅓, in nitre ⅛, and in animal tartar, that is, in the human calculus, more than ½ of the whole mass. It is self-evident that the air drawn out of these bodies by the force of fire, so long as it was part of the mass, did not yet have the nature of air; that is, it was not a fluid endowed with an elasticity proportional to its density. For if it had been, then even by the force of a moderate heat it would have expanded, with an irrepressible effort, into a larger space, and would have dissolved the whole structure of the body. Hence it is a matter that was not elastic while expelled from the interstices of the body, and which displays elasticity scarcely [more than] when it has been set free. Since, however, it is the character of vapours to exert elastic force once they have been torn away from the mass to which they were united, we must, if we cannot affirm it positively, at least hold with great probability that air is nothing other than that vapour dissolved in bodies, which, once reduced to the utmost subtlety, readily yields to any degree of heat and displays a strong elasticity.",["- Restated the long final sentences as shorter ones; *quippe…solvisset* is turned into a plain conditional.","- \"scarcely [more than]\" is my supplied reading of *vix libera facta*; the Latin is elliptical and the sense is doubtful (perhaps \"hardly freed before it shows elasticity\").","- Fractions ⅐, ⅓, ⅛, ½ are as in the OCR; ⅐ may be an OCR or print variant and is left unchecked.","- *Statica* kept in italics as Hales's title (*Vegetable Staticks*)."]],"de-igne-0062":["There are, however, not a few weighty considerations which confirm me in this opinion. For why is air expelled by burning only from those bodies which contain no small amount of oil, and therefore of acid? Is not the acid, by its attraction, the most active and most powerful principle for constraining the ether, as I set before your eyes earlier? Is not this principle the bond, and as it were the glue, of those concrete bodies (being the true magnet of the ethereal matter, which constrains all bodies)? And when this acid, driven by the immense force of fire, has with difficulty been expelled from its closest union with the matter, do you not think it must depart divided into the finest film? In this way, what ground is there for doubting that it should constitute an elastic fluid in this manner, movable to expansion by the slightest degrees of heat, and neither condensing, however far the cold is increased (since cold never removes all heat), nor to be deprived of elasticity? Hence the difficulty which weighs on aqueous vapours, that they are coagulated by slight cold, and which was the reason why Hales offered the expelled air under the name of a matter wholly different in kind from the whole nature of vapours, here ceases altogether. And so the opinion presents itself to natural philosophers as most worthy of closer investigation, whether air is nothing but the most subtle breath of the acid disseminated throughout the whole of nature, which attests its elasticity at any degree of heat whatever.",["- Divided the long rhetorical question beginning *et ubi acidum…* only lightly; the parenthesis *quippe…magnes* is kept in place.","- *Cuticula* rendered \"film\"; *Physicis* as \"natural philosophers\".","- The clause *ut antea sub oculos posui* refers to an earlier proposition of the dissertation outside this passage."]],"de-igne-0063":["With these [propositions] laid down as a foundation, it is easy to see why nitre, roasted by a strong fire, yields so great a quantity of elastic air: the most subtle acid, separated from the coarser part and reduced to the thinnest vapour, is itself the air. It is equally easy to see why the materials which most stubbornly resist fire give off and emit the greatest quantity of air; for example, why the tartar of Rhenish wine yields more than nitre. Materials which release the acid, enclosed in their embraces, only very slowly and with great resistance, also tear it away in the form of a most subtle film, so that it can constitute an elastic [fluid] as mobile as air is. On the contrary, those from which a more abundant vapour is drawn off give forth also a coarser one, which when the cold is increased can furnish no elasticity.",["- Restated the sentence on *quippe, quae tardissime…* in two sentences; the antecedents of *quae* and *iis* are taken to be the stubborn materials.","- Supplied \"[propositions]\" for *hisce* and \"[fluid]\" for *elasticum*.","- The last clause is awkward in Latin (*e quibus* with no antecedent); I take it as \"those materials from which…\"."]],"de-igne-0064":["Agreement of the barometric observations with the hypothesis.",[]],"de-igne-0065":["From this hypothesis there also becomes clear a property of air at greater height, which on the common opinion is scarcely explicable. For Maraldi, Cassini and others, on the testimony of the *Monumenta* of the Royal Academy of Sciences of Paris, found that Mariotte's law, according to which the compression of air is proportional to the incumbent weight, fails at a higher elevation. They found there a smaller density of air than ought to have followed from that law, when compared with the weight of the lower air. From this it is plain that the upper air consists not of particles of the same kind but less compressed, but of elements specifically lighter in themselves, since a greater volume of them is required under the same compression to supply the same weight. Since therefore the nature of the substance of air is so different at different heights, a thing found nowhere else among elements of the same kind anywhere on earth, it is plain that air must be held to be not a separate kind of element, but a form in which another element, namely, as I think, the acid humour, manifests itself. This being posited, it is not surprising if some particles of such a vapour (according to the different thickness of their film) are heavier than others, and the lighter occupy the highest place.",["- *Monum. Ac. R. Sc. Paris.* expanded in the English as the *Monumenta* (Mémoires) of the Paris Academy; the abbreviation is uncertain in expansion.","- Names Maraldus (Maraldi) and Cassinus (Cassini) given in the usual English forms.","- *quam nullibi alias…* is smoothed to \"a thing found nowhere else\"; the Latin construction is irregular."]],"de-igne-0066":["Prop. XII.",[]],"de-igne-0067":["To explain the nature of flame from the assertions of our theory.",[]],"de-igne-0068":["1. The nature.",[]],"de-igne-0069":["The nature of flame, singular above the rest of the kinds of fire, is this:",[]],"de-igne-0070":["No body burns except at its surface, and the nourishment of flame is oil and therefore acid, that most active principle serving the elastic motion.",[]],"de-igne-0071":["Flame is nothing but vapour brought to such a degree of fire that it glitters with vivid light and ceases only through want of nourishment. But these are the things in flame which make it wholly different from every other kind of fire. (1) Whereas heat imparted to any body to warm it is, by the common law of nature, gradually diminished by communication, flame on the contrary acquires from a minute principle an incredible force, bounded by no limits so long as fuel does not fail. (2) The fire which can be imparted to some inflammable matter in heating it, up to the point of boiling, is much lower than that which it exerts in burning. (3) Flame scatters light, whereas, apart from the metals, other kinds of bodies, however heated, remain without light.",["- Item (3): *praeter metalla* is rendered \"apart from the metals\" as the Latin has it; the sense (metals glowing when heated) is left as stated.","- Item (2) smoothed from a clumsy relative construction (*qui…sit eo, quem*); \"the fire\" supplies a sense of *ignis*."]],"de-igne-0072":["2. Investigation of the cause.",[]],"de-igne-0073":["The reason for these phenomena, if I judge rightly, is this. Flame consists of ignited vapour; the solid mass of the body is not wholly turned into flame, but properly the surface burns. Now since vapour has as much surface as possible and as little resistance as possible to keeping the matter of fire within its embraces, it is evident that it can not only propagate with the greatest ease an undulatory motion conceived from the slightest principle, but can also communicate it gradually, with equal intensity, to other inflammable matter, however great that may be. For although at first glance this phenomenon seems to offend against the first rule of mechanics, that the effect is always equal to the cause, yet if you consider it, the first stimulus of even the smallest spark to kindle a flame does nothing else than rouse a minimal particle of inflammable vapour into an undulatory motion of its fiery element. This, being slightly restrained, frees itself with great effort and performs vibrations, and by likewise rousing the surrounding [particles] propagates the violence of the motion through the whole mass. Nor should you wonder that the effect of a small cause here increases immensely: the elastic forces of the enclosed ether, freeing themselves from the bonds of attraction, in this way produce effects which do not properly recognize the stimulus of the kindling flamelet as their cause. They depend properly on the attraction of the oil, whose very subtle division has given the enclosed matter the opportunity of releasing itself with great violence. Further, vapour constitutes a fluid, more efficacious in undulating because of the freer vibrations of the elastic ether, not so much restrained, and, because of the fiery matter thus ejected, better suited than other ignited bodies both to heating bodies and to scattering light.",["- Divided the sentence beginning *Etenim quanquam…* and the one on *elateria*; \"[particles]\" is supplied for the implied noun with *circumfusas*.","- The last sentence is irregular in Latin (*tam…quam* with *aptius*); I smoothed it, and the antecedent of \"better suited than other ignited bodies\" is my reading of *ceteris ignitis corporibus*.","- *Mechanicae regulam*: \"first rule of mechanics\" kept literal."]],"de-igne-0074":["Conclusion.",[]],"de-igne-0075":["But I now put the finishing stroke to this little work, scarcely begun. I shall not longer detain men burdened with weightier duties, [but] commend this opuscule, whatever it is, and myself with it, to the favourable will and benevolence",["- The passage breaks off; the sentence continues in the next passage (*Amplissimae Facultatis Philosophicae commendans*). \"[but]\" is supplied to join the clauses; *coronidem* rendered \"finishing stroke\"."]],"de-igne-0076":["of the most distinguished Faculty of Philosophy.",["- Grammatically completes the preceding sentence (\"commending … to the … Faculty\"); translated as it stands."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0001":["A NEW ELUCIDATION\nOF THE FIRST PRINCIPLES\nOF METAPHYSICAL KNOWLEDGE\n(*Principiorum primorum cognitionis metaphysicae nova dilucidatio*)\nwhich, with the consent of the most distinguished Faculty of Philosophy, is to be defended in a public disputation in the philosophical auditorium on 27 September, from eight to twelve o'clock, for admission to the same, by M. Immanuel Kant of Königsberg; respondent Christoph Abraham Borchard of Heiligenbeil in Prussia, student of sacred theology; opponents Johann Gottfried Möller of Königsberg, student of sacred theology; Friedrich Heinrich Samuel Lysius of Königsberg, candidate in both laws; and Johann Reinhold Grube of Königsberg, candidate in both laws. In the year 1755. Königsberg, printed by J. H. Hartung, printer to His Sacred Royal Majesty and to the University.",["- Title-page layout and capitals normalised. *Cognitionis* rendered \"knowledge\" per the method (*Erkenntnis*).","- *i. v. c.* expanded as *iuris utriusque cultor* (\"student/candidate in both laws\"); this expansion is a conjecture. *S. S. theol. cultor/stud.* rendered \"student of sacred theology\". \"Heiligenb. bor.\" = Heiligenbeil, Prussia.","- The title words *Reg.*/*regiom.* taken as \"of Königsberg\"."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0002":["To the most illustrious, most noble and most excellent lord, Lord Johann von Lehwald, of Augustus, King of the Prussians, Supreme Field-Marshal, most weighty Governor of the fortresses of Pillau and Memel, by far the most deserving Knight of the illustrious Order of the Black Eagle, most vigilant Colonel of a regiment of foot, incomparable hero, his most gracious lord and patron,",["- *Augusti Borrussorum regis* rendered \"of Augustus, King of the Prussians\" as it stands; the name \"Augustus\" is the Latin form for Frederick II here and may be a printed or OCR slip.","- *summo castrorum praefecto* rendered \"Supreme Field-Marshal\" (literally \"chief commander of the camps\"); *legionis pedestris tribuno* rendered \"Colonel of a regiment of foot\"; both are period equivalents, chosen by judgment.","- Dative throughout; the sentence continues in the following passages."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0003":["these little pages",["- Object of *offert* in the dedication (0007)."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0004":["as a token of a grateful and obliged mind",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0005":["for the clemency shown in many instances",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0006":["with a devoted and submissive disposition of mind",["- *Mentis affectu* rendered \"disposition of mind\"; \"mind\" here is *mens*, not *Gemüt*."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0007":["offers his most humble client, Christoph Abraham Borchard.",["- Verb and subject inverted in the Latin; kept in that order. Together with 0003–0006 the sentence reads: \"His most humble client … offers these little pages …\"."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0008":["Plan of the work.",["- *Ratio instituti* rendered \"Plan of the work\"; literally \"account of the undertaking\"."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0009":["I hope to bring some light to the first principles of our knowledge. Since I am resolved to set out what I have meditated on this matter in as few pages as possible, I deliberately dispense with prolix digressions. I lay bare only the sinews and limbs of the arguments, as though all charm and grace of style had been stripped off like a garment. If in this undertaking I find it my part to depart from the opinion of distinguished men, and even at times to name them, I am so firmly persuaded of the fairness of their way of judging that I trust this will detract in no way from the honour owed to their merits, and cannot be taken by them amiss. Where opinions diverge, each man may abound in his own sense. To examine the arguments of others also with modest scrutiny, provided bitterness and the itch for quarrelling are absent, is not forbidden; nor do I observe that impartial judges anywhere hold this to be contrary to the duties of courtesy and respect.",["Restated the opening participial construction (*allaturus ... supersedeo*) as two sentences, and divided the long final sentence in three.","*stet sententia* rendered \"I am resolved\"; *cognitio* rendered \"knowledge\"."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0010":["First, then, I shall try to submit to the scales of a more careful inquiry what is commonly asserted, more confidently than truly, about the supreme and indubitable primacy of the principle of contradiction above all truths. I shall then briefly set out what should more rightly be established on this head. Next, concerning the law of sufficient reason, I shall bring forward whatever pertains to a more correct sense and demonstration of it, together with the difficulties that seem to beset it, and I shall meet these, so far as modest talent allows, with the strength of argument. Finally, wishing to advance somewhat further, I shall lay down two new principles of metaphysical knowledge, of no contemptible importance, as it seems to me. They are indeed not primitive and simplest principles, but for that very reason better suited to use and, if any are, of very wide extent. Since one who enters upon an untrodden path is very prone to slip into some error in such an endeavour, I persuade myself that the benevolent reader will take everything in the better part, with an equitable manner of judging.",["Divided the long second sentence of the Latin into three and the third into two, to keep the sense clear.","*principium contradictionis* and *lex rationis sufficientis* rendered \"principle of contradiction\" and \"law of sufficient reason\"."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0011":["Section I.\nOn the Principle of Contradiction.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0012":["Notice.",["*monitum* rendered \"Notice\" (a preliminary admonition to the reader)."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0013":["Since in what follows I must study brevity above all, I think it better not to transcribe here once more the definitions and axioms which we hold as established by common knowledge and as consonant with right reason. Nor shall I imitate those who, slavishly bound by some law of method I know not what, think they have not proceeded in due way and order unless they have reviewed from egg to apples whatever they find in the philosophers' cabinets. I thought it fair to warn the reader beforehand, lest this be charged to me as a fault when I do it deliberately.",["*ab ovo usque ad mala* is the Horatian idiom for \"from beginning to end\"; the literal \"egg to apples\" is kept.","Divided the Latin sentence into three; *scriniis* rendered \"cabinets\" (cases for papers or books)."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0014":["Prop. I. There is no single, absolutely first, universal [*catholicon*] principle of all truths.",["*catholicon* kept in italics, with \"universal\" supplied in brackets."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0015":["A first principle that is truly single must necessarily be a simple proposition. Otherwise a number of propositions tacitly comprised in it would merely feign the appearance of a single principle. If, then, it is a truly simple proposition, it must be either affirmative or negative. But I contend that, whichever of the two it is, it cannot be universal, that is, it cannot embrace absolutely all truths under itself. If you say it is affirmative, it cannot be the absolutely first principle of negative truths; if negative, it cannot lead the column among the positive ones.",["Divided the first sentence into two. *vniuersale* rendered \"universal\" and glossed with the clause that follows, which is the sense of *omnes omnino veritates sub se complectens*."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0016":["Suppose, then, that it is a negative proposition. The derivation of all truths from their principles is either direct or indirect. First, who does not see that by the direct mode of inference only negative consequences can be deduced from a negative principle? Next, if you demand that affirmative propositions flow from it indirectly, you will admit that this can happen only by means of the proposition: \"Of whatever the opposite is false, that is true.\" This proposition is itself affirmative, and so cannot flow from a negative principle by direct argument. Still less can it do so indirectly, since it would need its own support. Hence it will depend in no way on a principle enunciated negatively. Since, then, affirmative propositions are not free to proceed from a negative and sole principle, this principle cannot be entitled a *catholicon*.\n\nSimilarly, if you lay down an affirmative proposition as your cardinal principle, negative propositions will certainly not depend on it directly. Indirectly there will be need of the proposition: \"If the opposite of something is true, it is false\"; that is, if the opposite of something is affirmed, it itself is denied. This proposition is negative, and so again can in no way be deduced from the affirmative principle. It cannot be deduced directly, as is self-evident, nor indirectly, except by begging the question [*per sui ipsius petitionem*]. However, then, you decide, you will not refuse the proposition which I postulated at the head of this section: that of absolutely all truths there can be no single, ultimate, universal [*catholicon*] principle.",["Divided into two paragraphs at *Similiter*, and restated the long sentences in shorter ones.","Silently corrected the OCR slip \"illudest\" to \"illud est\", and \"negatiue\" to the adjectival sense \"negatively\".","\"Proposition ... at the head\": the Latin says *in fronte propositionis*, i.e. Prop. I; \"section\" is my gloss."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0017":["Prop. II. Of all truths there are two absolutely first principles: one of affirmative truths, namely the proposition \"whatever is, is\"; the other of negative truths, namely the proposition \"whatever is not, is not\". Both together are commonly called the principle of identity [*principium identitatis*].",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0018":["Once more I appeal to the two kinds of demonstrating truths, the direct and the indirect. The former mode of inference gathers truth from the agreement of the concepts of subject and predicate, and always lays down this rule as its foundation: whenever the subject, regarded either in itself or in connexion, posits those things which involve the concept of the predicate, or excludes those which are excluded by the concept of the predicate, the predicate is to be affirmed of it. Stated a little more explicitly: whenever identity is found between the concepts of subject and predicate, the proposition is true. Expressed in the most general terms, as befits a first principle, this runs: Whatever is, is; and whatever is not, is not. The principle of identity, therefore, which is the first, will certainly preside over all direct argumentation.",["*notionum* rendered \"concepts\", following the method's fixed term *Begriff*, though Kant writes *notio* here.","*q. e. primum* expanded as \"which is the first\" (*quod est primum*).","Divided the long sentence into four."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0019":["If you inquire into the indirect mode of inference, you will find in the end the same twofold principle underlying it. For appeal must always be made to these two propositions. (1) Of whatever the opposite is false, that is true; that is, whatever has its opposite denied is to be affirmed. (2) Of whatever the opposite is true, that is false. Of these the first has affirmative propositions as its consequences, the second negative ones. If you express the first in the simplest terms, you will have: Whatever is not not, is. (For the opposite is expressed by the particle *non*, and likewise removal by the particle *non*.) The second you will put in this form: Whatever is not, is not. (Here again the word \"opposite\" is expressed by the particle *non*, and the word \"falsity\", or removal, likewise by the same particle.) Now, as the law of the characteristic [*lege Characteristica*] requires, work out the force of the words contained in the first proposition. Since one particle *non* indicates that the other is to be cancelled, when both are deleted the proposition \"whatever is, is\" results. The second proposition already reads \"whatever is not, is not\". It is clear, therefore, that in indirect demonstration too the twin principle of identity holds the first place, and consequently is the ultimate foundation of all knowledge whatsoever.",["Divided the long Latin text into shorter sentences and numbered the two propositions as Kant does.","The Latin of the clause beginning *Altera autem, cum audiat* is elliptical, with no main verb for \"the other\"; I supplied \"already\" and a main verb (\"reads\"), and the conclusion is made a separate sentence.","*Quicquid non non est illud est* is rendered literally as \"Whatever is not not, is\", with the copula after the double negative understood.","*lege Characteristica* refers to the rules of the *characteristica* (a symbolic calculus); the rendering is literal."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0020":["*Scholion.* Here is a specimen, slight indeed but not wholly to be despised, of the *ars characteristica combinatoria*; for the very simplest terms we employ in elucidating these principles scarcely differ from characters. I shall take this occasion to say what I think of that art. After Leibniz announced that he had discovered it, all the learned lamented that it had been buried in the same grave as so great a man. In this pronouncement of the great philosopher I recognise the testament of Aesop's father. When on the point of breathing his last, he told his children that he had hidden a treasure somewhere in the field. Death took him suddenly before he had named the place, and so he gave his sons occasion to turn over the field most diligently and to work it by digging. Disappointed in their hope, they were, beyond doubt, made richer by the fertility of the field. This, I judge, is the one fruit to be expected from the pursuit of that celebrated art, if there are any who still bring themselves to devote effort to it.\n\nBut if one may openly confess the truth, I fear that what the most acute Boerhaave somewhere in his *Chemistry* suspects of the most eminent alchemists has also befallen this incomparable man. After they had discovered many singular secrets, he says, they came to think that nothing would be beyond their power once they set their hand to it. By a certain swiftness of anticipation they reported as accomplished what they inferred could be done, indeed what ought to be done, as soon as they should turn their minds to carrying it out.\n\nFor my own part, if we have arrived at principles that are absolutely first, I do not deny that the *ars characteristica* may have some use, since we then have the means of employing concepts, and even the very simplest terms, as signs. But where a composite cognition is to be expressed by means of characters, all the penetration of genius suddenly sticks as if on a rock, and is hindered by an inextricable difficulty.\n\nI find also that a philosopher of great name, the illustrious Darjes, has tried to make the principle of contradiction explicit by means of characters. He expresses an affirmative concept by the sign + A and a negative one by the sign – A, whence results the equation + A – A = 0, that is, to affirm and to deny the same thing is impossible, or nothing. In this attempt, with all respect to so great a man, I detect without doubt a *petitio principii*. For if you give to the sign of a negative concept the force of cancelling an affirmative concept joined to it, you plainly presuppose the principle of contradiction, which lays down that opposite concepts cancel one another. Our own explanation of the proposition \"The opposite of whatever is false, is true\" is free from this fault. Expressed in the simplest terms, it reads: whatever is not-not, is. In removing the particles *non* we do nothing but carry out their simple signification, and there results, as was necessary, the principle of identity: whatever is, is.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0021":["Proposition III. To establish further the precedence of the principle of identity over the principle of contradiction in holding the first place in the subordination of truths.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0022":["The proposition that claims for itself the title of the absolutely highest and most universal principle of all truths must, first, be expressed in the simplest terms and, secondly, in the most general. I believe I plainly observe both in the twofold principle of identity. Of all affirmative terms the simplest is the little word *is*; of negative terms, the word *is not*. Further, nothing more universal can be conceived than the simplest concepts. For the more composite concepts borrow their light from the simple ones, and because they are more determinate than these, they cannot be equally general.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0023":["The principle of contradiction, which is expressed in the proposition \"It is impossible for the same thing at once to be and not to be\", is in fact nothing but a definition of the impossible. For whatever contradicts itself, or is conceived as at once being and not being, is called impossible. But how can it be laid down that all truths must be referred to this definition as to a touchstone (*lapis Lydius*)? For it is not necessary to vindicate every truth from the impossibility of its opposite, nor, to speak truly, does this suffice by itself. There is no transition from the impossibility of the opposite to the assertion of the truth, except by means of the dictum \"The opposite of whatever is false, is true\". This dictum therefore shares its sway with the principle of contradiction, as was shown in the preceding pages.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0024":["Finally, to entrust a negative proposition above all with the first place in the realm of truths, and to make it the head and saving support of them all: to whom would this not seem rather harsh, and somewhat worse even than a paradox, since it is not clear why a negative truth should have gained this right in preference to an affirmative one? We rather hold that, since there are two kinds of truths, there are also two first principles for them, one affirming, the other denying.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0025":["*Scholion.* To some this inquiry may seem as superfluous and devoid of all utility as it is subtle and laborious. If you look to the fruitfulness of its corollaries, you have my assent. For the mind, even if it has not been taught such a principle, cannot fail to use it everywhere spontaneously and by a certain necessity of nature. But is not the subject therefore worthy of inquiry, to follow the chain of truths up to its highest link? And certainly in this way it is no small matter to look more deeply into the law governing the arguments of our mind. To allege only one instance: since all our reasoning resolves itself into the discovery of the identity of the predicate with the subject, whether considered in itself or in connexion, as is clear from the ultimate rule of truths, we can see from this that God has no need of reasoning. For since all things lie open to His gaze with perfect clarity, one and the same act of representation presents to His intellect what agrees and what does not agree. He has no need of analysis, such as the night that shrouds our understanding necessarily requires.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0026":["Section II.\nOn the principle of determining, commonly called sufficient, reason.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0027":["Definition.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0028":["Proposition IV. To determine is to posit a predicate with the exclusion of its opposite. That which determines a subject with respect to some predicate is called *ratio* (ground). A ground is divided into the antecedently determining and the consequently determining. The antecedently determining ground is that whose concept precedes the determined; that is, without presupposing it the determined is not intelligible.* The consequently determining ground is that which would not be posited unless the concept determined by it had already been posited from some other source. The former ground you may also call the ground of *Why* (*ratio Cur*), or the ground of being or of becoming (*ratio essendi vel fiendi*); the latter the ground of *That* (*ratio Quod*), or of knowing (*ratio cognoscendi*).",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0029":["* To this class one may add the identical ground, where the concept of the subject determines the predicate through its perfect identity with it, e.g. a triangle has three sides; here the concept of the determined neither follows nor precedes the concept of the determining.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0030":["Proof of the reality of the definition.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0031":["According to common sense, the concept of a ground produces a certain connexion and linkage between a subject and some predicate. It therefore always requires a subject, and a predicate to be united with it. If you ask for the ground of the circle, I do not understand at all what it is you seek, unless you add a predicate, e.g. that it is the most capacious of all figures of equal perimeter. Suppose we ask, for instance, for the ground of the evils in the world. We then have the proposition: The world contains very many evils. The ground of *That*, or of knowing, is not sought, because experience takes its place; what must be indicated is the ground of *Why*, or of becoming, that is, that by whose positing it is intelligible that the world is not antecedently undetermined with respect to this predicate, but that the predicate of evils is posited in it with the exclusion of the opposite. A ground therefore makes determinate what was indeterminate. And since all truth is brought about by the determination of a predicate in a subject, the determining ground of truth is not merely a criterion but also a source. If you depart from it, very many things might indeed be found possible, but nothing at all true. Hence it is for us undetermined whether the planet Mercury revolves about its axis or not, since we lack a ground that posits either alternative with the exclusion of its opposite. Both remain possible so long as this is so; neither is made true with respect to our knowledge.",["- 0020: I divided the long opening sentences, especially the Aesop's-father story, and the Boerhaave sentence (whose syntax is irregular, with \"idem... fato euenerit\" resuming it), into shorter English sentences.","- 0020: \"Ill. Daries\" is the illustrious Joachim Georg Darjes; \"Boerhaauius\" is Boerhaave. I modernised the names; the OCR \"Daries\" is the original spelling.","- 0020: \"Quem ... fructum\" is rendered as \"This ... fruit\"; \"Equidem\" begins a new paragraph in my version. The break is mine.","- 0023: \"in antecedentibus\" is rendered \"in the preceding pages\"; the sense is the preceding argument, and \"pages\" is a mild gloss.","- 0023: \"Cujuscunque oppositum est falsum illud est verum\" is quoted as a dictum; the German-style punctuation in the Latin is unclear, so I rendered it as \"The opposite of whatever is false, is true\", the sense required by 0020.","- 0025: \"cognitio\" is rendered \"cognition\" in 0020 where it is a Latin technical term in context; elsewhere \"knowledge\" where Kant means Erkenntnis in general.","- 0028: *Ratio* is rendered \"ground\" as the standard sense in this context (Latin: *ratio* = reason/ground); Kant's *ratio cognoscendi/essendi* are kept in Latin.","- 0028/0029: The asterisk marks the footnote, which follows as a separate footnote."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0032":["To illustrate by an example the difference between reasons that determine antecedently and reasons that determine consequently, I take the eclipses of the satellites of Jupiter. I say that these furnish the ground of knowing (*ratio cognoscendi*) that light is propagated successively, and with an assignable velocity. But this ground determines the truth only consequently. For even if Jupiter had no satellites at all, and no occultation of them in turn took place, light would still move in time, although perhaps we should not know it. Or, to apply the matter more closely to the definition given: the phenomena of the Jovian satellites, which prove the successive motion of light, presuppose this very property of light, without which they could not so occur; and they therefore determine this truth only consequently. The ground of becoming (*ratio fiendi*), on the other hand, that is, the reason why the motion of light is attended by an assignable expenditure of time, is (if you embrace the opinion of Descartes) placed in the elasticity of the little elastic globules of air. In accordance with the laws of elasticity these yield somewhat to an impact, and the minute moment of time which they absorb in each single globule is added up through an immense connected series until it finally becomes perceptible. This would be a reason determining antecedently, that is, one without the positing of which there would be no room at all for what is determined. For if the globules of the aether were perfectly hard, no interval of time would be perceived between the emission and the arrival of light, however immense the distances.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0033":["The definition of the illustrious Wolff, since it suffers from a notable defect, has seemed to me to need emendation at this point. He defines ground (*ratio*) as that from which it can be understood why something is rather than is not. Here he has undoubtedly introduced the thing defined into the definition. For however well the little word \"why\" (*cur*) may seem suited to common understanding, so that it might be held admissible in a definition, it tacitly involves the concept of ground once more. If you examine it rightly, you will find that it signifies the same as \"for what ground\" (*quam ob rationem*). Hence, when the substitution is duly made, the Wolffian definition will read: A ground is that from which it can be understood for what ground something is rather than is not.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0034":["Likewise, in the statement of the principle of sufficient ground, I have thought it better to substitute the expression \"determining ground\" (*ratio determinans*), and in this I have the assent of the illustrious Crusius. For the word \"sufficient\" is ambiguous, as he amply shows; since it is not at once apparent how far something suffices. But since to determine is so to posit that every opposite is excluded, the term denotes that which certainly suffices for a thing to be conceived thus and not otherwise.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0035":["Prop. V. Nothing is true without a determining ground.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0036":["Every true proposition indicates that the subject is determined in respect of the predicate, that is, that the latter is posited with exclusion of its opposite. In every true proposition, therefore, the opposite of the predicate that belongs must necessarily be excluded. Now a predicate is excluded when it is repugnant to another concept already posited, by virtue of the principle of contradiction. Hence exclusion has no place where there is no concept present that is repugnant to the opposite to be excluded. In every truth, therefore, there is something which, by excluding the opposite predicate, determines the truth of the proposition. Since this comes under the name of determining ground, it must be laid down that nothing is true without a determining ground.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0037":["The same in another way.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0038":["From the concept of a ground it can be understood which of two opposite predicates is to be attributed to the subject and which is to be removed. Suppose that something were true without a determining ground: there would then be nothing from which it could appear whether the one of the opposites is to be attributed to the subject or to be removed. Neither is therefore excluded, and the subject is indeterminate in respect of both predicates. Hence there is no room for truth; yet truth was assumed, and so an open contradiction is manifest.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0039":["Scholium. That the knowledge of truth always rests upon a ground is established by the common sense of all mortals. But we are often content with a consequently determining ground when we are concerned only with certainty. That there is always given an antecedently determining ground, or, if you prefer, a genetic one, or at least an identical one, is readily apparent from the theorem cited and the definition considered together, since a consequently determining ground does not bring about the truth but only explains it. But let us proceed to the grounds that determine existence.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0040":["Prop. VI. For anything to have the ground of its existence in itself is absurd.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0041":["For whatever contains in itself the ground of the existence of some thing is the cause of it. Suppose, then, that there were something which had the ground of its existence in itself: it would be the cause of itself. But since the concept of cause is by nature prior to the concept of the caused, and the latter posterior to the former, the same thing would be at once prior and posterior to itself, which is absurd.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0042":["Corollary. Whatever, therefore, is said to exist absolutely necessarily does not exist on account of any ground, but because its opposite is plainly not thinkable. This impossibility of the opposite is the ground of knowing (*ratio cognoscendi*) its existence, but the thing wholly lacks an antecedently determining ground. It exists: to have said and conceived this of it is sufficient.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0043":["Scholium. I find indeed that this doctrine is from time to time repeated among the opinions of recent philosophers: that God has the ground of His existence posited in Himself. But I myself should not wish to give it my assent. For it seems somewhat hard to these good men to deny to God, as the last and most consummate principle of grounds and causes, a ground of Himself; and so, since none outside Himself may be acknowledged, they maintain that He has it hidden away in Himself. Hardly anything else can be found more remote from right reason. For that when, in the chain of grounds, you have arrived at the principle, the progress comes to a halt and the question is wholly abolished by the completeness of the answer, is evident of itself. I know, indeed, that appeal is made to the very concept of God, by which they claim that His existence is determined; but it is easily seen that this takes place ideally, not really. You form for yourself the concept of a certain being in which there is the totality of reality; it must be admitted that by this concept you are bound to grant it existence also. The argument therefore proceeds thus: if in some being all realities are united without degree, that being will exist; if they are only conceived as united, its existence likewise consists only in ideas. Hence the doctrine should rather have been framed thus: in forming the concept of a certain being, whom we call God, we have so determined it that existence is included in it. If therefore the concept previously conceived is true, then it is also true that He exists. And let this be said for the sake of those who give assent to the Cartesian argument.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0044":["Prop. VII. There is a being whose existence precedes both its own possibility and the possibility of all things, and which is therefore said to exist absolutely necessarily. It is called God.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0045":["Since possibility consists solely in the non-repugnance of certain concepts conjoined, and the concept of possibility therefore results from a comparison; and since in every comparison the things to be compared must necessarily be at hand, and where nothing at all is given there is no room for comparison or for the concept of possibility corresponding to it: it follows that nothing can be conceived as possible unless whatever is real in every possible concept exists, and indeed (since, if you depart from this, nothing at all would be possible, that is, nothing but the impossible) exists absolutely necessarily. Further, this reality in all its modes must necessarily be united in a single being.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0046":["For suppose that these realities, which are as it were the material of all possible concepts, were found distributed among several existing things. Each of these things would have an existence limited in a certain manner, that is, joined with some privations. Since absolute necessity does not belong to these in the same way as to realities, while yet they pertain to the complete determination of a thing, without which a thing cannot exist, the realities limited in this manner would exist contingently. For absolute necessity, therefore, it is required that they exist without any limitation, that is, that they constitute an infinite being. If any plurality of such a being were feigned, it would be a repeated multiplication of it, and hence a contingency opposed to absolute necessity; it must therefore be laid down that only one being exists absolutely necessarily. Therefore God is given, and He is one: the absolutely necessary principle of all possibility.\n\n---notes\n- Notes are given for the batch as a whole. *Ratio* is rendered \"ground\" (as the Latin parallel of *Grund*) rather than \"reason\"; *ratio determinans* is \"determining ground\"; *antecedenter / consequenter determinans* is \"antecedently / consequently determining\".\n- 0032: the two sentences beginning \"Verum haec ratio…\" and \"s. vt ad definitionem…\" are kept separate, with \"Or\" for *s.* (*sive*); *ratio fiendi* is retained in Latin italics. \"Jovian satellites\" is for *satellites Jovialium*.\n- 0032: *Aetheris* is rendered \"aether\"; the earlier \"globules of air\" (*aëris*) and these \"globules of the aether\" are as in the Latin. \"Moment of time\" renders *tempusculum*.\n- 0033: *Cur* and *quam ob rationem* are glossed in English as \"why\" and \"for what ground\"; the argument depends on the second containing *ratio*.\n- 0034: \"he amply shows\" assumes *idem* refers to Crusius (*Ill. Crusium*) as antecedent; the German-style ambiguity is resolved by judgment.\n- 0036 and 0038: the long sentences are divided into shorter ones. In 0038 the last clause, *quae tamen quum fuisse sumta sit*, is rendered \"yet truth was assumed\", smoothing a slightly irregular construction.\n- 0039: *identicam* (\"identical\") is kept as in the Latin; *s.* is rendered \"or\" throughout.\n- 0043: *recantari* is rendered \"repeated\"; *idealiter … non realiter* is rendered \"ideally, not really\". In the sentence \"Si vera igitur praeconcepta notio, verum quoque illum existere\", \"is true\" and \"it is also true that\" are supplied to complete the elliptical construction.\n- 0045: the single long sentence is kept as one, but the \"since… since…\" structure is marked with colons; \"in all its modes\" renders *omnimoda*. Square-bracket supplements have not been needed.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0047":["*Scholium.* Here is a demonstration of the divine existence, as essential as it can possibly be made. Strictly speaking there is no place here for a genetic demonstration, and yet the existence of God is proved by the most primitive evidence, namely the very possibility of things. Hence it is clear that if you remove God, you abolish not merely all existence of things but even their internal possibility itself.\n\nEssences (which consist in internal possibility) are commonly called absolutely necessary. It would be more correct, however, to say that they belong to things with absolute necessity. The essence of the triangle, which consists in the connection of three sides, is not necessary in itself; for who in his right mind would contend that it is necessary in itself that three sides should always be conceived as joined? What I concede is that this is necessary *for the triangle*. That is, if you think a triangle, you necessarily think three sides, which is the same as saying: if something is, it is.\n\nBut how does it come about that the notions of sides, of space to be enclosed, and so on, are available for thought? That is, how does it come about that there is in general something that can be thought, from which every notion of a thinkable thing afterwards results by combining, limiting and determining? This could not be conceived at all, unless whatever is real in the notion existed in God, the source of all reality.\n\nWe know that Descartes gave an argument for the divine existence drawn from the internal notion [of God] itself. How he was disappointed in his attempt may be seen in the scholium of the preceding [§]. Of all beings God is the only one in which existence is prior to possibility, or, if you prefer, identical with it. And as soon as you depart from his existence, no notion of him remains.",["- \"En demonstrationem…\" is a sentence fragment in the Latin (no verb); I rendered it \"Here is a demonstration…\" and divided the long second paragraph into several sentences.","- *geneticae* is read as an ellipsis for a \"genetic\" demonstration (*demonstratio genetica*); the antecedent is supplied from *demonstrationem*.","- \"[§]\" supplies the section number lost in the OCR (\"§hi prioris\"); the reference is to the preceding section, number not given in the text.","- I take *rebus absolute necessario competere* as the phrase to be understood after *rectius dicerentur*, and render it as the reading \"belong to things with absolute necessity.\"","- *Vnde resultet…notio quaeuis rei cogitabilis*: I took *id* in \"id, nisi in DEO…\" as the subject of *concipi…non posset*, which governs the whole long preceding clause."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0048":["Proposition VIII. Nothing that exists contingently can lack a reason determining its existence antecedently.",["- *ratio existentiam antecedenter determinans* rendered \"reason determining its existence antecedently\"; *contingenter* as \"contingently.\""]],"nova-dilucidatio-0049":["Suppose that it lacks such a reason. Then there will be nothing to determine the thing as existing except the existence of the thing itself. Since existence is nevertheless determined, that is, is posited in such a way that every opposite of its complete determination is wholly excluded, the exclusion of the opposite will be none other than that which proceeds from the positing of existence. But this exclusion is identical (for nothing else forbids the thing not to exist than that non-existence has been removed). The opposite of existence will therefore be excluded through itself, that is, will be absolutely impossible. That is, the thing will exist with absolute necessity, which contradicts the hypothesis.",["- The sentence beginning *Quae vero exclusio* was kept together but the first sentence was divided in two. *omnimodae suae determinationis* is rendered \"its complete determination,\" the suffix referring to the existing thing."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0050":["*Corollary.* It is therefore clear from what has been demonstrated that only the existence of contingent things stands in need of the support of a determining reason, and that the one absolutely necessary being is exempt from this law. Hence the principle is not to be admitted in so general a sense that it embraces within its sway the whole totality of the possible.",["- *firmamento* (\"support,\" lit. \"prop\") kept as a figure; *principium* here is the principle of determining reason."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0051":["*Scholium.* Here, then, is the demonstration of the principle of determining reason, now at last, as I am at any rate persuaded, illuminated with the full light of certainty. It is well known that the most acute philosophers of our age, among whom I name the illustrious Crusius out of respect, have always complained of the unsound demonstration of this principle, which we find on offer in all the writings on this subject. So far did this great man despair of a remedy for the evil that he seriously contended the proposition to be altogether incapable of demonstration, even if it be granted to be most true.\n\nI owe an account, however, of why the demonstration of this principle did not come to me so readily and easily that I could complete the whole of it with a single argument, as is commonly attempted. Instead it proved necessary to reach full certainty by a certain roundabout way.",["- The final sentence is divided in two. *venalem* (\"on offer\", lit. \"for sale\") rendered \"on offer.\" *Vir magnus* is Crusius.","- \"I owe an account\" renders *ratio mihi reddenda est*."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0052":["First, I had to distinguish carefully between the reason of truth and the reason of existence, although it might seem that the universality of the principle of determining reason, which holds in the realm of truths, extends equally over existence. For if nothing is true, that is, if no predicate belongs to a subject, without a determining reason, it follows that without such a reason there will also be no predicate of existence.\n\nBut it is agreed that to establish truth an antecedently determining reason is not required; the identity holding between predicate and subject suffices. In existing things, on the other hand, the question concerns an antecedently determining reason. If there is none, the being exists with absolute necessity; if the existence is contingent, I have proved that such a reason cannot fail to precede it. Hence truth, drawn from its own sources, has emerged, in my judgment at least, the purer.",["- *antecendenter* in the text is read as *antecedenter* (OCR or print slip).","- \"no predicate belongs to a subject\" for *si subjecto non competit praedicatum*; this is the author's expression for \"if there is nothing true.\"","- I supplied \"[that]\" to nothing; the divisions into two paragraphs follow the sense of *Verum…constat*."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0053":["The celebrated Crusius, indeed, holds that certain existents are so determined by their own actuality that it is vain, he thinks, to require anything further. Titius acts by a free volition. I ask why he did this rather than not do it; Crusius answers: because he willed it. But why did he will it? He thinks the question foolishly asked. If you ask why he did not rather do something else, he answers: because he is already doing this. He therefore holds that the free volition is determined in act through its own existence, and not antecedently through reasons prior to its existence; that all opposite determinations are excluded by the mere positing of actuality; and hence he contends that a determining reason is not needed.\n\nBut I shall prove, if it please, by another argument also, that a contingent thing, if you depart from an antecedently determining reason, can never be sufficiently determined and hence cannot exist. The act of free volition exists; this existence excludes the opposite of this determination. Yet since the act did not formerly exist, and existence does not of itself determine whether it formerly was or was not, the question whether it already existed before or did not exist remains undetermined through the existence of this volition. Now among the elements of complete determination there is also this one, whether the being has begun or not. To that extent, therefore, the being will be undetermined, and it cannot be determined unless, besides what belongs to its internal existence, notions are brought in that can be thought independently of its existence.\n\nNow that which determines the antecedent non-existence of an existing being precedes the notion of existence. But that which determines that an existing being did not exist before would at the same time have determined it from non-existence to existence (for the propositions \"why what now exists did not exist formerly\" and \"why what formerly did not exist now exists\" are in fact identical); that is, it would be a reason determining existence antecedently. It is therefore abundantly clear that without such a reason there can be no place for the complete determination of that being which is conceived as having arisen, and hence none for its existence. If anyone should find this demonstration somewhat obscure because of its deeper analysis of notions, he may rest content with what has gone before.",["- Divided into three paragraphs and the last, very long sentence (*Cum vero…*) restated in two; the clause \"[it would be] a reason…\" follows *h. e. ratio sit…*.","- *determinatio omnimoda* rendered \"complete determination\"; *notiones* \"notions\" (not *Begriffe*, as the text is Latin).","- \"if it please\" for *si libuerit*. \"Foolishly asked\" for *inepte interrogari*.","- *actu determinatam* rendered \"determined in act.\""]],"nova-dilucidatio-0054":["Lastly, I shall explain more briefly why I have declined to rest content with the demonstration employed by the illustrious Wolff and his followers. As the penetrating Baumgarten has set it out more clearly, this illustrious man's demonstration comes down, to include much in few words, to this. If something had no reason, nothing would be its reason; therefore nothing would be something, which is absurd.\n\nThe argument ought rather to have been framed thus: if a being has no reason, its reason is nothing, that is, a non-being. This I grant with both hands. For if there is no reason, the concept corresponding to it will be that of a non-being. Hence if no reason can be assigned to a being except one to which no concept at all corresponds, the being will be wholly without reason, which comes back to what was supposed. The absurdity that they thought to follow from this therefore does not follow.\n\nI shall give an example in support of my view. By this manner of inference I might venture to demonstrate that the first man was begotten by some father. For suppose he was not begotten. There would then be nothing that begot him. He would therefore have been begotten by nothing; since this is a contradiction, it must be admitted that he was begotten by someone. It is not difficult to detect the fallacy of the argument. If he was not begotten, nothing begot him. That is, he who was supposed to have begotten him is nothing or a non-being, which is as certain as can be. But when the proposition is perversely converted, it receives a most distorted sense.",["- *Ill. Wolffio* and *Ill. Crusium* abbreviate *Illustris*; rendered \"the illustrious\".","- Several sentences divided; \"[he] who was supposed…\" renders *qui…putaretur* with the pronoun resolved from the context.","- *enodatius* rendered \"more clearly\"; *conceptus* \"concept\"; *non ens* \"non-being.\"","- *quod redit ad supposita* rendered \"which comes back to what was supposed.\""]],"nova-dilucidatio-0055":["Proposition IX. To enumerate and resolve the difficulties which seem to press upon the principle of determining reason, commonly called [the principle of] sufficient [reason].",["- The proposition is stated as an infinitive in the Latin (a task rather than a thesis); I kept that form. \"[the principle of]\" is supplied to complete the elliptical *vulgo sufficientis*."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0056":["At the head of the opponents of this principle, and alone capable of standing in for them all, we may justly reckon* the Reverend and most acute Crusius, whom I acknowledge to be second to hardly anyone among the promoters, I will not say of philosophers, but of philosophy, in Germany. If my discussion of his doubts turns out well (as the advocacy of a good cause seems to promise), I shall consider that I have overcome every difficulty. First he reproaches the formula of this principle with ambiguity and an unstable sense. He rightly observes that the ground of knowing (*ratio cognoscendi*), likewise the moral ground, and other ideal grounds are from time to time employed in place of real and antecedently determining grounds, so that it can often be hard to understand which of the two is meant. Since this shaft does not strike my assertions, I need not turn it aside. Whoever examines these views of mine, such as they are, will see that I take pains to distinguish the ground of truth (*ratio veritatis*) from the ground of actuality (*ratio actualitatis*). In the former the question is only of that positing of a predicate which is brought about by the identity with the predicate of the notions contained in the subject, whether the subject is considered absolutely or in connection; and the predicate, which already inheres in the subject, is merely disclosed. In the latter we examine, in regard to what is posited as inhering, not whether but whence their existence is determined. If there is nothing present to exclude the opposite, beyond the absolute positing of the thing itself, it must be held to exist of itself and with absolute necessity. If, however, it is taken to exist contingently, there must be other [grounds] present which, by determining it thus and not otherwise, already exclude antecedently the opposite of its existence. So much, then, on our demonstration in general.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0057":["* I do not wish to detract anything here from the Illustrious Darjes. I acknowledge that his arguments, and indeed those of some others, are of great weight in pressing against the principle of determining ground. But since they seem closely akin to those which I must cite from the praised Crusius, I think, without offence to these great men, that I may confine my reply to the doubts chiefly to the latter.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0058":["A greater danger certainly threatens the defenders of this principle from that objection of the most distinguished man, in which he explicitly charges us, with arguments of no contemptible strength, with the guilt of calling back, by *postliminium*, an immutable necessity of all things and the Stoic fate, and indeed of undermining all freedom and morality. His argument, though not wholly new, has yet been presented by him more fully and forcefully. I shall set it out as clearly as I can, but with its strength undiminished.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0059":["If whatever happens cannot happen otherwise than by having an antecedently determining ground, it follows that whatever does not happen also cannot happen, since there is no ground without which it cannot happen at all. As this must be conceded, in backward order, of all the grounds of grounds, it follows that all things come about in a natural connection so closely interlaced and woven together that whoever wishes the opposite of some event, or even of a free action, conceives in his wish what is impossible, since the ground required to produce it is not present. Thus, tracing back the inescapable chain of events, which, as Chrysippus says, once willed and entangles itself through eternal orders of consequence, we come at last to rest, in the first state of the world, which points directly to God as its author, in the final ground of all events, a ground fruitful of so many consequences; once it is posited, one thing after another is derived, by a fixed law, into the ages that are to follow. The distinction between absolute and hypothetical necessity, which is well worn, and through which, as through a loophole, our adversaries think they can escape, the distinguished man attacks: it is, he says, of no weight at all for weakening the force and efficacy of necessity. For what does it matter whether an event, precisely determined by antecedent grounds, has, when considered in itself, a representable opposite, when none the less this opposite cannot really come about, since the grounds it needs for existing are not present, and indeed grounds to the contrary are? You say: the opposite of the event, taken separately, can yet be thought, and is therefore possible. But what of that? It still cannot happen, since it has been sufficiently provided, by grounds already existing, that it shall never come about in fact. Take an example. Caius has committed a fraud. By his primitive determinations, so far as he is simply a man, sincerity was not repugnant to Caius; I grant it. But as he is now determined, it is repugnant, for there are present in him grounds which posit the contrary, and sincerity cannot be ascribed to him unless the whole order of interlaced grounds, back to the first state of the world, is overturned. Now let us hear what the distinguished man further concludes from this. The determining ground effects not only that this action in particular occurs, but that in its place no other can occur. Therefore, whatever happens in us, its consequence has been so provided for by God that nothing else can follow. Therefore the imputation of our deeds does not pertain to us; God alone is the cause of all, who has bound us by laws to fulfil in some fashion our appointed lot. Does it not follow thus that no sin can displease God? When it does, it testifies at the same time that the series of interlaced things established by God admits nothing else. Why then does God rebuke sinners for actions which, it has been provided from the very sowing and origin of the world, they shall commit?",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0060":["Refutation of the doubts.",["Passages 0056–0060 are rendered together above, each under its own id.","0056: *subintelligi*, *rationem cognoscendi*, etc. kept in Latin where technical; \"[grounds]\" is supplied after \"other\" (*alia*), as the sense requires. The long sentence on *ratio actualitatis* is divided into several sentences.","0056: *ratio* is rendered \"ground\" throughout, following Kant's own German usage elsewhere (*Grund*); the asterisk after *putandus est* is the footnote marker, whose text is 0057.","0056: \"positing of the predicate\" for *praedicati positio*; the sentence on *ratio veritatis* is smoothed, with the meaning of *cum praedicato identitatem* taken as identity of the notions with the predicate.","0057: Darjes is meant; the footnote is attached to 0056.","0058: *postliminium* is a Roman-law term (restoration of rights after return); I left it in Latin because the metaphor is technical.","0059: The long sentence on the chain of events is broken into several; the \"he says\" in the sentence on the distinction is supplied to mark Crusius's position, since the Latin shifts to indirect report.","0059: \"Chrysippus says\" quotation (*semel voluit et implicat*) is kept literal; the Latin is cited from Cicero/Seneca-type tradition and appears slightly irregular in the text, so the reading is not corrected.","0059: Sentence \"Quidnam igitur DEus peccatores increpat…\" rendered with \"from the very sowing and origin of the world\" for *a mundi satu atque ortu*."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0061":["When we distinguish hypothetical necessity, and moral necessity in particular, from absolute necessity, the question is not one of the force and efficacy of necessity, namely whether in the one case or the other the thing is more or less necessary; the question concerns the necessitating ground, namely whence the thing is necessary. I readily grant that some followers of the Wolffian philosophy here turn somewhat aside from the true sense. They persuade themselves that what is posited through a chain of grounds determining one another hypothetically is still somewhat removed from complete necessity, because it lacks absolute necessity. On this point, however, I agree with the illustrious opponent: the distinction, which is on everyone's lips, does little to lessen the force of necessity or the certainty of determination. For as nothing can be conceived more true than the true, or more certain than the certain, so nothing can be conceived more determinate than the determinate. Events in the world are so certainly determined that divine foreknowledge, which cannot be deceived, perceives with equal certainty, through the connection of grounds, both their futurity and the impossibility of their opposite, just as if the opposite were excluded by their absolute concept. Here, however, the crux of the question is not how greatly, but whence, the futurity of contingent things is necessary.\n\nThat the act of creating the world was in God not ambiguous, but so certainly determined that the opposite was unworthy of God (that is, plainly could not belong to him), who would doubt? Nevertheless the action is free, because it is determined by grounds which contain the motives of his infinite intelligence, in so far as these incline the will most certainly, and which do not proceed from some blind efficacy of nature.\n\nSo also in the free actions of men, in so far as they are regarded as determined, the opposite is indeed excluded. But it is not excluded by grounds lying outside the subject's appetite and spontaneous inclinations, as if a man, even against his will, were driven to perform his actions by some unavoidable necessity. Rather, in the very propensity of volitions and appetites, in so far as it willingly yields to the allurements of representations, the actions are determined by a fixed law, through a connection that is indeed most certain, yet voluntary.\n\nThe difference between physical actions and those which enjoy moral freedom is not resolved by a difference of connection and certainty, as if the latter alone, burdened with a doubtful futurity and exempt from the linking of grounds, enjoyed a vague and ambiguous manner of arising; on that footing they would be little to recommend them as prerogatives of intelligent beings. Rather, the manner in which their certainty is determined by their grounds contributes at every point to maintaining the mark of freedom. They are elicited only through motives of the understanding applied to the will, whereas in brutes, or in physico-mechanical actions, everything is necessitated by external solicitations and impulses, without any spontaneous inclination of choice.\n\nIt is admitted that the power of performing an action is indifferently disposed to either side, but that it is determined solely by the inclination of the [agent's] good pleasure toward the allurements presented in representations. The more surely man's nature is bound to this law, the more it enjoys freedom; and to be borne vaguely in every direction toward objects is not to use freedom.\n\n[Objector:] \"He acts for no other reason than that it chiefly pleased him.\" [Reply:] \"Now I have you bound by your own admission. For what is the pleasing [*lubitus*] but an inclination of the will, produced in accordance with the allurement of the object, toward this side rather than the opposite? Therefore your 'it pleases' or 'it is agreeable' indicates an action determined through internal grounds. For on your view the pleasing determines the action; but it is nothing other than the will's acquiescence in the object, in proportion to the allurement by which the object invites the will. It is therefore a relative determination; and if the will is supposed to be allured equally, to say that one alternative is more agreeable is the same as to say that it pleases equally and at the same time unequally, which involves a contradiction.\"\n\nA case can, however, occur in which the grounds inclining the will to one side or the other wholly escape consciousness, and yet one of the two is chosen. But then the matter has passed from the higher faculty of the mind back to the lower, and the mind is directed in some direction by a preponderance [*suprapondium*] of an obscure representation (of which we shall give a fuller account in what follows).",["- Restated: the long opening sentences and the sentence on physical and free actions were divided; some sentence breaks and paragraphing are mine.","- The dialogue lines (\"Non aliam ais...\", \"Jam teneo te...\") are not marked in the Latin; I supplied the bracketed \"[Objector:]\" and \"[Reply:]\" and quotation marks as a judgment of the sense, and the Latin has no such labels.","- \"Potestatem... beneplaciti\": I supplied \"[agent's]\" to complete the sense; the Latin has no genitive for the agent.","- *Suprapondium* (preponderance) kept in Latin as a technical term; \"omnem paginam facit\" rendered loosely as \"contributes at every point\".","- *Lubitus*/*libet*/*volupe*: rendered \"pleasing,\" \"it pleases,\" \"agreeable\"; the Latin plays on one root, which English cannot reproduce.","- \"ais\" is a second-person verb (\"you say\"), which I rendered inside the quotation as the objector's statement. The reading \"Non aliam ais ob rationem agit\" is syntactically odd and may be an OCR or original slip; it is possibly \"agis\" or \"ais: agit\".","- \"Illustri antagonistae\": the opponent is Crusius, not named here; I left him unnamed as the Latin does."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0062":["In brief, if it should prove convenient, the well-known controversy may be illustrated in the form of a dialogue between Caius, defender of the indifference of equilibrium, and Titius, patron of the determining ground.",["- \"Si ita commodum fuerit dialogo\" rendered \"if it should prove convenient ... in the form of a dialogue\"; the dative *dialogo* is read as a form of means.","- *Indifferentiae aequilibrii* rendered \"indifference of equilibrium\"; *rationis determinantis* as \"determining ground\" (Kant's usual sense of *ratio*)."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0063":["*Caius:* The course of my past life stirs the pangs of conscience in me. Yet one consolation remains, if your doctrines may be believed: the guilt of the misdeeds I have committed does not fall upon me. I was bound fast by the connection of reasons which have determined one another from the very cradle of the world, and whatever I have done I could not but do. Whoever now reproaches me with my vices, and vainly upbraids me that I ought to have entered upon another kind of life, acts absurdly, just as if he were to demand that I should have halted the flow of time.\n\n*Titius:* Tell me, then, what is this series of reasons by which you complain that you were constrained? Did you not do willingly whatever you did? Did not the silent dissuasion of conscience, and the fear of God warning you wrongly from within, cry out against you as you were about to sin? Did it not none the less please you more to carouse, to gamble, to sacrifice to Venus, and the like? Were you ever dragged to sin against your will?\n\n*Caius:* That I by no means deny. I am well aware that I was not hauled aside as by the scruff of the neck, without resistance and while strenuously struggling against allurements. Knowingly and gladly I enslaved myself to vices. But whence did this inclination of my will to the worse side come to me? Before it occurred, while divine and human laws alike were inviting me, hesitating, to their side, was it not already determined, by the completion of the reasons, that I should be bent towards the bad side rather than the good? Once a reason is posited as complete in every respect, is not to hinder what follows from it the same as to make the deed undone? Yet on your view every inclination of my will is perfectly determined by an antecedent reason, this again by a prior one, and so on up to the head of all things.\n\n*Titius:* Now I shall remove your scruple. At each juncture of the action to be performed, the series of interconnected reasons supplied motives alluring on both sides. You gave yourself willingly to one of them, because it was more agreeable to act thus than otherwise. But, you say, it was already determined by the completion of the reasons that you should incline to the side appointed. I would have you consider whether the complete reason of the action does not require the spontaneous propensity of your will, following the allurements of the object.\n\n*Caius:* Beware of calling it spontaneous. It could not but incline to this side.\n\n*Titius:* So far is this from abolishing spontaneity that it rather renders it the more certain, provided it be taken in the right sense. For spontaneity is action proceeding from an internal principle. When this is determined in conformity with the representation of the best, it is entitled liberty. The more certainly anyone is said to obey this law, and so the more determined he is, with all the motives to willing posited, the freer is the man. It does not follow from your argument that liberty is infringed by the force of antecedently determining reasons. Your own confession, that you acted not unwillingly but gladly, sufficiently refutes you. Hence your action was not inevitable, as you seem to suppose (for you did not even strive to avoid it), but infallible, in accordance with the propensity of your appetite to circumstances so formed. And this indeed lays a greater guilt upon you; for you desired so vehemently that you did not suffer yourself to be turned from your purpose. But I shall dispatch you with your own weapon. Tell me, how do you think the notion of liberty should more fittingly be formed, on your own view?\n\n*Caius:* I think that if you were to drive out whatever there is of a concatenation of reasons determining themselves to a fixed outcome; if you were to grant that in any free action man stands indifferent towards either side; and if, with all the reasons you may imagine as determining him in some direction posited, he could nevertheless choose anything in place of anything, then at last I should confess that all is well with liberty.\n\n*Titius:* God forbid! If some power allowed you to obtain this wish, how unhappy you would be at every hour. Suppose you have resolved in your mind to enter the path of virtue, and that your mind is now well fortified by the precepts of religion and whatever else is effective in confirming a resolve. Now the occasion for acting arises. You at once slip into the worse side, for the reasons that invite you do not determine you. How many more complaints I seem to hear you utter! \"Ah, what ill fate suddenly drove me from my salutary resolve! What need to take pains over the precepts of virtue? Actions come about by lot; they are not determined by reasons!\" \"I do not, indeed,\" you say, \"accuse the unwilling coercion of some fate snatching me away; but I abominate that I-know-not-what which brought about my lapse to the worst side. For shame! Whence came to me that detestable appetite precisely for the worst side, which could as easily have inclined to the opposite?\"\n\n*Caius:* Then all liberty is given up for lost alike.\n\n*Titius:* You see into how narrow a compass I have driven your forces. Do not conjure up spectres of ideas. You feel yourself free; but do not fabricate a notion of this liberty that agrees ill with right reason. To act freely is to act in conformity with one's appetite, and indeed with consciousness. And this is not excluded by the law of determining reason.\n\n*Caius:* Though I have scarcely anything to say in reply, inner sense seems to me to speak against your view. Take a case of no great moment. If I attend to myself, I observe that it is open to me to incline either way, so that I am quite persuaded that the direction of my action was not determined by an antecedent series of reasons.\n\n*Titius:* I shall expose to you the silent imposture of the mind, which makes you the sport of this illusion of an equilibrium of indifference. The natural appetitive power implanted in the human mind is borne not only towards objects but also towards the various representations that are to be set before the understanding. Now in a given case we feel ourselves to be the authors of the representations which contain the motives of choice. We are well able to apply our attention to them, to suspend it, or to turn it elsewhere. Consequently we are conscious that we can not only tend towards objects in conformity with our appetite, but can also vary the objective reasons themselves at pleasure. To that extent we can hardly refrain from supposing that the application of our will is exempt from every law and lacks any fixed determination. But if we strive to judge rightly, we shall easily be convinced that reasons which determine must certainly be present. For in the given case this is nothing but the tendency of attention towards a combination of representations. When reasons allure from one side, we may, at times, in order at least to make trial of our liberty, turn attention to the opposite side and so procure for it the preponderance. In that case the appetite is directed thus and not otherwise.\n\n*Caius:* I confess you have entangled me in many difficulties, but I am sure that you are hindered by no fewer. How do you think the determinate futurition of evils, of which God is in the end the ultimate and determining cause, can be reconciled with his goodness and holiness?\n\n*Titius:* Lest we waste time in vain disputation, I shall set out briefly the doubts that hold you in suspense, and untie the knots of your difficulties. The certainty of all events, physical as well as free actions, is determinate. Consequences lie in their antecedents, and antecedents in earlier ones still; and so, in a connected chain, always in earlier reasons, until we reach the first state of the world, which directly shows God to be its author. This state is, as it were, a fountain and spring, from which all things flow down a sloping channel with a necessity that cannot deceive. Hence you think that God is plainly marked out as the contriver of evil. You think that he cannot hate the web which he himself began, and which is woven out, in conformity with its first pattern, into the future ages of the time that follows. You think too that he can hardly pursue the sins woven into the work with such indignation as holiness permits, since the guilt falls back in the end upon the first author of all evils. These are the doubts that press upon you; now I shall disperse their mists.\n\nIn undertaking the first beginnings of the universe of things, God began a series which, by a fixed connection of reasons joined and woven together, includes also moral evils and the physical evils that correspond to them. But it does not follow that morally depraved actions can accuse God as their author. If, as happens in mechanics, intelligent beings were related merely passively to those things which impel to certain determinations and changes, I do not deny that the ultimate guilt of everything could devolve upon God, as the architect of the machine. But what comes about through the will of intelligent beings, endowed with the power of determining themselves spontaneously, proceeds from an internal principle, from conscious appetites, and from the choice of one side or the other according to the freedom of the will [*arbitrii*]. Hence, however much the state of things before the free acts may be in some way constituted, and however much that intelligent being may be involved in such a connection of circumstances that it is established, and can be foreseen, that moral evils will most certainly come from it, this futurition is nevertheless determined by such reasons as turn upon the being's own voluntary direction to the depraved side. Since it was most agreeable to the sinners to act as they did, they must call themselves the cause, and to pay the penalty of unlawful pleasure is in the most perfect accord with equity.\n\nAs to the aversion by which it is, beyond doubt, worthy of God's holiness to abhor sins, but which seems scarcely compatible with the decree of the world's creation, in that this decree included the futurition of these evils: here too the difficulty surrounding the question is not insuperable. For hold it thus.",["- The speaker label \"cajvs\" is rendered \"Caius\"; \"Caj.\" and \"Tit.\" are expanded to Caius and Titius, and the speeches are set off as paragraphs (the Latin runs them together).","- Several long Latin periods are restated as shorter sentences: Titius's account of the chain of reasons back to the first state of the world, and his \"Quatenus itaque … convincemur\" sentence on attention. In the latter I moved the main clause forward; the Latin leaves it to the end.","- \"Quare, allicientibus … attentionem … conuertendo, huic suprapondium conciliemus\" is somewhat knotted. I took it as an explanation of what the \"tendency of attention\" amounts to, and read \"ut libertatis saltem periculum faciamus\" as a purpose clause.","- *libertas* = \"liberty\" and *spontaneitas* = \"spontaneity\"; *arbitrium* is kept in Latin in square brackets because the sense turns on it. *Futuritio* is rendered \"futurition\" as the standard English form of the technical term.","- \"Fallere nescia necessitate\" is rendered \"a necessity that cannot deceive\", i.e. one that does not fail. \"Infallibilis\" is rendered \"infallible\", set against \"ineuitabilis\", \"inevitable\".","- \"Obtorto collo\" is taken idiomatically (\"by the scruff of the neck\"). \"Cedo!\" is rendered \"Tell me\".","- The passage breaks off in mid-argument (\"Sic enim habeto\"), and I have translated it as it stands, without completing it."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0064":["The goodness of God tends towards the greatest perfection of created things, so far as any such perfection can fall to them, and towards the happiness of the spiritual world. By the same infinite endeavour to manifest himself he has attended not only to the more perfect things that were afterwards to spring up in the order of grounds and the series of events; he has also seen to it that no goods even of a lower degree should be lacking, so that the totality of things, in its immensity, might embrace all things, so to speak, from the highest degree of perfection that can fall to the finite down to all the lower degrees and even to nothing. For this reason he permitted into his design even those things which, mingled with very many evils, would at least furnish something good which God's wisdom could draw out of them, so as to distinguish by an infinite variety the manifestation of the divine glory.\n\nWithin this compass the history of the human race, however mournful, was not to be wanting. Even in the very confluence of evils it bears with it infinite testimonies to the celebration of the divine goodness, and it befitted wisdom, power and goodness exceedingly well. Yet it is not on that account to be supposed that God intended the evils themselves, woven into the work once begun, or deliberately drew them out. For he had before his eyes goods which, as he knew, would remain nonetheless when the grounds [of evil] were withdrawn, and which it would have been unworthy of supreme wisdom to root up together with the unhappy tares.\n\nMoreover, sin is committed by mortals voluntarily and from the inmost affection of the mind. The order of antecedent grounds does not drive and sweep them along unwilling; it allures them. Although it was foreknown with certainty that their enticements would be obeyed, yet, since the origin of evils resided in the internal principle of self-determination, it is plainly evident that they are to be imputed to the sinners themselves. Nor is the divine Godhead to be thought any the less to abhor sins because, by granting permission, it has in a certain fashion assented to them. For the very compensation for those evils, which were permitted, by their being brought back to the better through strenuous effort, which God strives to obtain by admonishing, threatening, inviting and supplying means, is properly the end which the divine artificer had before his eyes. Since, therefore, he lops the sprouting branches of evil and, so far as can be done with human freedom kept safe, checks them, he has by this very act shown himself the hater of all depravity, although the lover of the perfections that can nonetheless be drawn out from it.\n\nBut I return to the road, having wandered somewhat farther from the plan of my undertaking than was fitting.",["- Divided the long Latin periods into several sentences, as the method allows; no content added.","- \"[of evil]\" is supplied after \"grounds\" (*subductis rationibus*): the sense is the grounds of the evils; the Latin is elliptical.","- *Numen* rendered \"Godhead\"; *ratio* rendered \"ground\" (Kant's usual sense here); *rationum antecedentium ordo* = \"order of antecedent grounds\".","- Sentence on *compensatio* is awkwardly constructed in the Latin (*quibus itaque*, with unclear antecedent); I took it as referring to the evils and their reclamation, and smoothed it."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0065":["Additions to Problem IX.\nThere is no place for divine foreknowledge with respect to free actions, unless their futurity is admitted as determined by its grounds.",["- \"Problematis\" taken as \"Problem\"; Kant's heading refers to the numbered proposition-problem. Note the original numbering \"IX\" is retained."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0066":["Those who subscribe to our principle have always pressed this argument validly against their opponents. Dispensing with that labour, therefore, I take pains only to answer what the most acute Crusius advances to the contrary. He objects to those who so think that their view is unworthy of God, as if they persuaded themselves that he employs ratiocinations. If there are any who maintain otherwise in this opinion, I gladly go over to the side of my illustrious adversary. For I grant that the windings of ratiocination ill befit the immensity of the divine understanding. Infinite intelligence has no need of abstracting universal notions, of combining them, or of comparing them in order to draw out consequences.\n\nBut here we assert that God cannot foresee those things whose futurity is not antecedently determined. We do so not from any want of aids, which we grant he does not need, but because the foreknowledge of a futurity is in itself impossible, and that futurity is plainly nothing at all if existence is wholly indeterminate, both in itself and antecedently. That it is indeterminate in itself is concluded from contingency; that it is likewise antecedently indeterminate our antagonists contend. Therefore it is necessarily devoid of determination, that is, of futurity, both in itself and as represented by the divine understanding.",["- Divided into two paragraphs for clarity. \"Ill.\" read as *illustris* (\"illustrious\").","- The sentence \"In qua quidem opinione, si qui sunt qui secus autumant…\" is awkward; I rendered it as closely as sense allows. The reading is uncertain.","- *Futuritio* = \"futurity\"; *determinatio* = \"determination\" (Bestimmung)."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0067":["At length the praised adversary candidly confesses that something incomprehensible remains here, which, however, when contemplation has returned to the infinite, agrees well with the eminence of the object. But however much I may grant that certain inner recesses of a more hidden intelligence remain which will never be opened to the human understanding, if you should wish to descend into the interior of knowledge, yet the question here is not about the manner, but whether the thing itself has a place. It is quite easy for mortal knowledge to see its contradiction, as with the opposed party's view.",["- The last clause (*cujus, cum oppositae partis sententia, repugnantiam inspicere…*) is ambiguous; I took *cujus* to refer to the thing (*res*), i.e. the contradiction of the opposing view's thesis. Doubtful.","- *cognitio* rendered \"knowledge\"."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0068":["Refutation of the instances which the defenders of the indifference of equilibrium call to their aid.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0069":["The patrons of the opposing party challenge us to meet the examples which seem to testify so openly to the indifference of the human will in any free actions whatever that scarcely anything could seem more open. When \"even and odd\" is played, and beans hidden in the hand are to be won by guessing, we pronounce one or the other quite without deliberation and without any ground of choosing. Twin cases they bring forward in the instance of some prince or other, who gave someone a free choice between two boxes alike in all respects in weight, shape and kind, in one of which he had hidden lead and in the other gold, where the determination to take one or the other could only have been made without ground. They say similar things about the indifferent freedom to move the right or the left foot.\n\nI shall answer them all in one word, and, as it seems to me, sufficiently. When in our principle there is talk of determining grounds, it is not one or another kind of ground that is understood, for example, in free actions, grounds present to the conscious understanding. However the action is determined, it must nonetheless be determined by some ground, if it is to be done. Objective grounds in the determination of choice may be wholly lacking, and there may be a perfect equilibrium of motives represented with consciousness; yet there still remains room for very many grounds which can determine the mind. For by such a doubtful hesitation this alone is effected, that the matter returns from the higher faculty to the lower, from representation joined with consciousness to obscure representations, in which it is scarcely to be supposed that on either side all things are perfectly identical. The tendency of innate appetite towards further perceptions does not suffer the mind to remain long in the same state. With the state of the internal representations altered, therefore, the mind must necessarily incline in some direction.",["- Divided into two paragraphs. *Gemina* (\"twin\") rendered literally; sense is \"similar cases\".","- *Facultas superior/inferior* = higher/lower faculty; *repraesentatio* = representation (Vorstellung).","- \"[of choice]\" not supplied; *arbitrium* = \"choice\"."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0070":["Prop. X. To set out certain genuine corollaries of the principle of determining ground.",["- *Principium rationis determinantis* rendered \"principle of determining ground\"."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0071":["1. There is nothing in the thing grounded (*rationatum*) that was not in the ground. For there is nothing without a determining ground, and hence nothing in the thing grounded that does not point to its determining ground:",["- The passage ends with a colon, and the continuation (the objection of the next passage) is not here; translated as it stands.","- *Rationatum* left in Latin in italics with the English beside it."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0072":["It might be objected that, since limits attach to created things, it would follow that they attach equally to God, who contains the ground of them. I reply: the limits that attach to finite things point equally to a limited ground of themselves in the act of divine creation. For God's creative action is limited in proportion to the limited being to be produced. But since this action is a relative determination of God, which must correspond to the things to be produced, and is not internal and intelligible absolutely in himself, it is plain that these limitations do not belong to God internally.",["- Restated *quam rebus producendis respondere necesse est* as a relative clause for smoothness.","- *Determinatio respectiva* = \"relative determination\"."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0073":["2. Of things which have nothing in common, one cannot be the ground of the other. This returns to the preceding proposition.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0074":["3. There is no more in the thing grounded than is in the ground. This is clear from the same rule.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0075":["Corollary. The quantity of absolute reality in the world is not naturally changed, either by increase or by decrease.",["- *Consectarium* rendered \"Corollary\"."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0076":["Elucidation. The evidence of this rule shines forth most readily in the changes of bodies. If, for example, a body A drives another, B, by striking it, a certain force, and consequently reality*, accrues to the latter. But an equal quantity of motion is taken from the impinging body; hence the sum of forces in the effect is equal to the forces of the cause.\n\nIn the collision of a smaller elastic body with a larger, the law cited does indeed seem liable to error. But by no means. For the smaller elastic body, repelled by the larger on which it runs, acquires a certain force in the opposite direction. If this is added to that which it transferred to the larger, it makes a sum indeed greater than the quantity of the impinging body, as is established from mechanics. But what is here commonly called absolute is more truly to be named relative. For these forces tend in different directions. Accordingly, when estimated from the effects which machines, applied jointly and so also considered in sum in the universe, can exert, the sum of forces is known by subtracting the motions in contrary directions, since these will in the end somehow destroy one another so far. What remains is the motion of the centre of gravity, which, as is known from statics, is the same after the conflict as before it.\n\nAs for the complete destruction of motion through the resistance of matter, this is so far from weakening the rule stated that it rather confirms it. For the force which arose from rest by the agreement of causes is reduced again to rest, by expending in the resistance of the obstacles just as much as it received, and the thing remains as before. Hence also an inexhaustible perpetuity of mechanical motion is impossible; for a thing which, while always spending some part of its force on resistances, yet kept its power undiminished for restoring itself, would be equally opposed to this rule and to sound reason.",["- *Dilucidatio* rendered \"Elucidation\". Divided into three paragraphs.","- The sentence \"Vires enim hae tendunt… aestumatae\" is tangled; I restated it, taking *machinae conjunctim applicatae* as \"machines applied jointly\".","- *Vtcunque tandem* rendered \"in the end somehow\"; slightly uncertain."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0077":["* Here, following common sense, one may conceive the impressed force as an introduced reality, although properly it is nothing but a certain limitation or direction of the innate reality.",["- Footnote kept as footnote; asterisk matches the reference in the preceding passage."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0078":["We often see enormous forces arise from an infinitely small beginning of cause. A spark cast into gunpowder: what immense expansive force does it call forth! Or again, a spark received elsewhere by eager fuel: what conflagrations, what ruin of cities, what long devastations of vast forests does it produce! What a structure of bodies is dissolved by the slight stimulus of a single little spark! But here the efficacious cause of these immense forces lies hidden within the structure of the bodies and is nourished there. It is elastic matter, either of air, as in gunpowder (according to the experiments of Hales), or of igneous matter, as in any combustible body. The minute stimulus rather manifests this cause than produces it. Compressed springs (*elastra*) are stored up within, and when stirred ever so slightly they put forth forces proportional to the reciprocal endeavour of attraction and repulsion [*repercussionis*].",["- The long second sentence is broken up; the questions are kept as questions. \"Hales\" is Stephen Hales, as the Latin \"Halesii\" indicates.","- *repercussionis* is rendered \"repulsion\", as the contrast with attraction requires; the Latin literally says \"rebound\". *Elastra* is kept in Latin with the English beside it."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0079":["The forces of spirits, and their unending progress towards further perfections, seem indeed to be exempt from this law. But I at least am persuaded that they are subject to it. Without doubt the infinite perception of the whole universe, which is always present within the soul though extremely obscure, already contains in itself whatever reality must belong to thoughts that are afterwards to be suffused with greater light. The mind afterwards attends to certain things only, withdrawing an equal degree of attention from others, and so illuminates the former with a more intense light. In this way it comes day by day into possession of greater knowledge. It does not thereby extend the compass of absolute reality, since the material element of all ideas, which derives from connexion with the universe, remains the same. But the formal element, which consists in the combination of notions and in the attention applied to their diversity or agreement, is certainly changed in many ways. We observe like things in the innate force of bodies. For motions, if rightly examined, are not realities but phenomena. The innate force, modified by the impact of an external body, resists the onset from an internal principle of efficacy by as much as it acquires in the direction of the impelling forces. Hence everything real in the forces in the phenomenon of motion is equal to what was already innate in the body at rest. The only difference is that the internal power, which in rest was undetermined in respect of direction, is now directed by the external impulse.",["- Sentences are divided, especially the long one beginning \"Procul dubio\" and the final one on motion, which I restated in several sentences.","- \"perennatura\" looks like an OCR or typographical corruption; I read it as \"perennis, unending\", which is conjectural.","- *Materiale* and *formale* are rendered \"material element\" and \"formal element\"; \"element\" is supplied. *Phaenomena* is \"phenomena\", following the Latin."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0080":["What has so far been adduced concerning the unchangeable quantity of absolute reality in the universe must be understood to hold in so far as all things happen according to the order of nature. For who would dare to doubt that through the agency of God the failing perfection even of the material world can be restored, that light can be shed from heaven upon intelligences more purely than nature allows, and that all things can be raised to a higher summit of perfection?",["- The final rhetorical question is carried through as a single question. The word order is rearranged for English."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0081":["Prop. XI. To adduce and refute certain spurious corollaries drawn with too little warrant from the principle of the determining ground (*principium rationis determinantis*).",["- *ratio* is rendered \"ground\", following the Latin parallel \"Grund\"; elsewhere in this passage \"reason\" is used where Kant speaks of the *principium rationis* as such."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0082":["1. That nothing is without a consequence (*rationatum*); or, that whatever is has a consequence of itself. This is entitled the *principium consequentiae*. So far as I know, it acknowledges as its author Baumgarten, the leading figure among metaphysicians. Since it was demonstrated by the same argument as the principle of reason, it falls in the same ruin together with that principle. If the discussion concerns only grounds of knowing, the truth of this principle is safe. For the notion of any being is either general or individual. If the former, whatever is established of the generic notion belongs to all the inferiors comprehended under it, and it must therefore be conceded that the former contains the ground of the latter. If the latter, it can be concluded that the predicates which belong to this subject in a certain connexion must always belong to it, the same grounds being posited. A given case thus determines the truth in similar cases, and therefore [the principle] has consequences of knowing (*rationata cognoscendi*). But if we here understand consequences of existing (*rationata existendi*), it may be seen from the last section of this treatise that beings are not infinitely fertile in them. There we shall establish by invincible arguments that the state of any substance exempt from connexion with others is free from all change.",["- The text is divided into short sentences. \"A quo, quia…\" is read as: the principle, being demonstrated by the same argument, collapses with the principle of reason.","- \"Hinc illam harum rationem continere\": *illam* is the generic notion, *harum* the inferiors. \"[the principle]\" in the next-to-last sentence is supplied.","- *rationata cognoscendi* and *rationata existendi* stay in Latin, with the English beside them."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0083":["2. That of the things in the whole universe none is in all respects similar to another. This is entitled the *principium indiscernibilium*; taken, as is usual, in its widest sense, it departs as far as possible from the truth. It is demonstrated chiefly in two ways. The first mode of argument is exceedingly precipitate, leaps over the object with a light bound, and hardly deserves to be taken into account. These are its subtleties: whatever agrees perfectly in all marks, and is distinguished by no difference, must be held to be one and the same being. Hence all perfectly similar things would be nothing but one and the same being to which many places are assigned. Since this is contrary to sound reason, they contend that [the supposition of perfectly similar things] contradicts itself. But who fails to perceive the sham of these subtleties? For the perfect identity of two things there is required an identity of all marks or determinations, external as well as internal. Who would exempt place from this complete determination? Things which, however much they agree in internal marks, are at least distinguished by place, are therefore not one and the same being. But the demonstration which is put forward, falsely derived from the principle of sufficient reason, is what we must here chiefly examine.",["- \"hanc sententiam\" is ambiguous. I read it as the supposition that perfectly similar things exist, and supplied this in brackets.","- \"Pro vno eodemque ente habenda videntur\" is rendered \"must be held to be\", with the sceptical \"videntur\" softened. \"Argutiae\" is \"subtleties\", with \"sham\" for *fucus*.","- *principium indiscernibilium* stays in Latin."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0084":["They keep saying that there would be no reason why God assigned different places to two substances if these agreed perfectly in all else. What absurdities! I wonder that men of great weight take pleasure in such rattles of reasoning. Call one substance A, the other B. Suppose A to occupy the place of B. Then, since A does not differ at all from B in its internal marks, A, holding even B's place, will be in all respects identical with B, and will have to be called B, which before was called A. And that which before bore the name B, now transferred to the place of A, will have to be called A. For this difference of letters marks only a diversity of places. Tell me, then, whether God has done anything else if, according to your view, he determined the places? Each is perfectly the same; and so the interchange you have invented is nothing. But that there is no reason for nothing agrees admirably with my own view.",["- The Greek \"τοῦ B\" and \"τοῦ A\" are rendered \"of B\", \"of A\".","- \"Crepundia\" (children's rattles) is kept as \"rattles\", and *characterum differentia* as \"difference of letters\".","- \"Nihili nullam esse rationem\" is rendered \"that there is no reason for nothing\". \"Reason\" here means *ratio*, in the sense of ground."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0085":["This spurious law is excellently refuted by the whole universe of things and also by the dignity of divine wisdom. For it is a fact that the bodies called similar (water, quicksilver, gold, the simplest salts, and so on) agree perfectly in their primitive parts in homogeneous, internal marks. This accords with the identity of use and function which they are destined to serve, and it is to be seen from the effects, which we always find proceeding similar from the same bodies without any notable difference. Nor is it fitting here to suspect some hidden diversity which escapes the senses, as though God needed something by which he might himself distinguish the parts of his work; for this would be to look for knots in a bulrush.",["- The first sentence is rendered as \"it is a fact that…\" to carry the construction of \"enim … conuenit … & videndum est\". The sentence is long and was restated accordingly.","- \"Egregie confutatur\" is \"excellently refuted\". The proverb about knots in a bulrush is kept literally."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0086":["We grant that Leibniz, the author of this principle, always observed a notable diversity in the structure of organic bodies, or in the texture of others far removed from simplicity, and that he could rightly presume it in all things of that kind. For where very many things must concur to compose something, it is clear that equal determinations cannot always result. Hence you will hardly find among the leaves of one and the same tree a pair perfectly alike. But here only the metaphysical universality of this principle is rejected. Moreover, it seems hardly deniable that in the figures of natural bodies an identity of pattern is often found. In crystallizations, for example, who would dare to maintain that among infinitely diverse forms there is not one or another which copies another in perfect similarity?",["- \"Exemplaris\" is rendered \"pattern\". \"Hic\" is rendered \"here\", meaning in this discussion.","- The last question is kept as a question. The sense is that some crystals do exactly copy others."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0087":["Section III.\nTwo principles of metaphysical knowledge, disclosing very fertile consequences, flowing from the principle of the determining ground.",["- *cognitio* is rendered \"knowledge\". \"Consectaria\" is rendered \"consequences\". The title is a heading, so I have kept it as one."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0088":["I.\nThe principle of succession [*principium successionis*].",["- The Latin term is supplied in brackets, since it is Kant's technical designation and the English is only an equivalent."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0089":["Prop. XII. No change can befall substances except in so far as they are connected with others, whose reciprocal dependence determines a mutual change of state.",["- *Mutatio* is rendered \"change\", and *status* \"state\"."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0090":["Hence a simple substance, exempt from all external connexion and thus left wholly to itself, is of itself altogether unchangeable.",["- \"Sibique adeo solitario relicta\" is rendered \"left wholly to itself\"."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0091":["Further, even when a substance is bound up in connexion with others, no change of its internal state can happen in it if this relation is not changed. Hence in a world devoid of all motion (for motion is the phenomenon of a changed connexion) there will be found no succession at all, even in the internal state of substances.",["- \"Complexa\" is rendered \"bound up in connexion\"; \"[a substance]\" is understood as subject. The parenthesis is kept in place."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0092":["Hence, when the connexion of substances is wholly abolished, succession and time likewise vanish.",["- *Facessunt* (\"depart, vanish\") is rendered \"vanish\"."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0093":["Demonstration.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0094":["Suppose some simple substance, released from connexion with others, to exist alone. I say that no change of internal state can befall it. For the internal determinations which now belong to the substance are posited by internal grounds, with exclusion of their opposite. If you wish another determination to succeed them, you must posit another ground for it. But the opposite of this ground lies in the internal [grounds], and, by hypothesis, no external ground is added. It is therefore plain that the new determination cannot be introduced into the being.",["- The one long sentence is divided into four. \"Cujus\" refers to the new ground, whose opposite is already contained in the internal grounds; \"[grounds]\" is supplied.","- \"Per supposita\" is rendered \"by hypothesis\"."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0095":["The same in another way. Whatever is posited by a determining ground must necessarily be posited together with it, for it is absurd that, the determining ground being posited, the consequence (*rationatum*) should not be posited. Therefore, whatever things are determining in any state of a simple substance, all things wholly determined by them must necessarily be simultaneous with them. But change is a succession of determinations. That is to say, it occurs where some determination arises which was not there before, so that the being is determined to the opposite of a determination which belongs to it. This cannot happen through what is found intrinsically in the substance. If, then, it does happen, it must necessarily proceed from external connexion.",["- The long sentence beginning \"Quia vero\" is divided and the \"s.\" (\"sive\") is rendered \"that is to say\". \"Haec\" is read as the change; \"[the being]\" is the subject throughout.","- *rationatum* is kept in Latin, with \"consequence\" beside it."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0096":["Yet again, in a somewhat different way. Suppose that under the conditions named a change arises. It begins to exist when previously it was not, that is, when the substance was determined to the opposite. Nothing is assumed to be added beyond internal [grounds], none that would determine the substance from elsewhere. Then, by the very same grounds by which the substance is held to be determined in a certain way, it will be determined to the opposite, which is absurd.",["- Supplied \"[grounds]\" after \"internal\", since *interna* is left without a noun. \"Aliunde\" is taken to exclude any external determining ground.","- Restated the single Latin sentence as three English sentences."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0097":["--- notes\n- \"DILUCIDATIO\" is the running head, which the job instructions say to omit. Nothing is translated.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0098":["Although this truth depends on a chain of reasons so easy and so incapable of deceiving, those who give their name to the Wolffian philosophy have so little noticed it that they rather maintain that a simple substance is subject to continual changes from an internal principle of activity. I know their arguments well, but I am no less convinced how flimsy (*ficulnea*, \"of fig-wood\") they are. They framed an arbitrary definition of force so that it signifies that which contains the ground of changes, whereas it ought rather to be laid down as containing the ground of determinations. It was therefore easy for them to fall into error.",["- Divided the first sentence at \"Equidem\" and made the last sentence a separate statement, in keeping with the method's restatement of cumbrous sentences.","- *Vis* = force; *determinationum* = \"determinations\" (Bestimmungen).","- *Ficulnea* is a proverbial word for flimsy or worthless; I give the literal sense in a parenthesis."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0099":["If anyone should further wish to know by what means the changes arise, of which a succession is found in the universe, since they do not flow from the internal [states] of any substance considered in isolation, I would have him turn his mind to what follows through the connection of things, that is, through their mutual dependence in determinations. For the rest, to explain this more fully here would be somewhat too prolix for the narrow bounds (*cancellis*) of our dissertation. It suffices that our demonstration has established that the matter cannot be otherwise.",["- Supplied \"[states]\" after \"internal\" (*ex internis*). Divided the final sentence.","- *Cancellis* = literally \"lattice, barriers\", here \"narrow bounds\"."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0100":["*Usus.*",["- Taken as the section heading \"Applications\" (the uses of the principle), since the numbered applications follow. I have left it in Latin. If it is in fact a running head, it should be dropped."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0101":["1. The real existence of bodies, which sounder philosophy has hitherto been able to defend against the Idealists by no other way than that of probability, I find follows most clearly from the first of the assertions of our principle. The soul is subject to internal changes (through inner sense). Since these cannot arise from its nature considered in isolation and apart from connection with others, as has been demonstrated, it is necessary that several things exist outside the soul, with which it is bound in mutual connection. It likewise appears from the same [argument] that the succession of perceptions occurs in conformity with external motion. Since it follows from this that we should not have a representation of any body variously determinable unless a body really existed, whose commerce with the soul impressed on it a representation conformable to itself, it can easily be concluded that there is given the composite which we call our body.",["- \"p. demonstrata\" is read as an abbreviation for \"per demonstrata\" (\"as has been demonstrated\"). Doubtful abbreviation.","- Supplied \"[argument]\" after \"the same\". Divided the long final sentence into two.","- *Repraesentatio* = representation; *commercium* = commerce (kept)."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0102":["2. It overturns the Leibnizian pre-established harmony from its foundations. It does so not, as for the most part happens, through final causes, which are thought unbecoming to God and which furnish a by no means infrequently unstable support, but through the harmony's own internal impossibility. For it follows immediately from what has been demonstrated that the human soul, if exempt from the real connection of external things, would be wholly devoid of changes of internal state.",["- *Harmonia praestabilita* = pre-established harmony. The last sentence is left as a single sentence, with the *quippe* clause rendered \"For\"."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0103":["3. The opinion that an organic body of some kind is to be assigned to all finite spirits without exception thereby gains a great proof of certainty.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0104":["4. It deduces the essential immutability of God not from the *ratio cognoscendi*, drawn from his infinite nature, but from its own genuine principle. The supreme godhead is wholly without any dependence, and the determinations that belong to it are established by no external relation at all. It is therefore amply clear from what has been asserted that it is wholly free from change of state.",["- *Ratio cognoscendi* (ground of knowing) is kept in Latin as a technical term.","- Restated the final sentence in two, taking the *cum* clause as a statement of ground."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0105":["*Scholion.* The principle adduced might perhaps seem to someone suspect of depravity, on account of the indissoluble tie by which the human soul is thus bound to matter in performing the internal functions of thought. This seems not far removed from the pernicious opinion of the materialists. But I do not thereby deprive the soul of its state of representations, although I would profess it immutable and always perfectly like itself if it were wholly released from external connection. And the quarrel which someone might perhaps try to fasten on me, I refer over to the side of the more recent writers, who with harmonious agreement, as with one voice, profess the necessary union of the soul with some organic body. To call one of them as witness, I name the illustrious Crusius. I observe that he so entirely goes over to my opinion that he openly asserts the soul to be bound to the law by which its endeavour towards representations is always conjoined with the endeavour of its substance towards some external motion. Hence, when this is stifled (*sufflato*) by impediments, the former too is impeded. Yet although he does not think this law so necessary that it cannot be dissolved if God so wills, nevertheless he concedes that its nature is bound to it. He would therefore have to confess that this nature too must be re-created (*transcreari*).",["- \"Pedibus euntem in sententiam\" is the idiom of voting for a proposal; rendered \"goes over to my opinion\".","- *Sufflato* (\"blown out\") is rendered \"stifled\", with the Latin given. *Conatus* = endeavour.","- \"Hanc\" in the last sentence is taken to refer to *naturam suam* (the soul's nature). The point is that the nature, not merely the law, would have to be altered. This is a judgment on the ambiguous pronoun.","- Divided several long sentences."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0106":["II.\nThe Principle of Coexistence.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0107":["Prop. XIII. Finite substances, through their existence alone, have no relations to one another and are contained in no commerce whatever, except in so far as they are sustained, conformed to mutual relations, by the common principle of their existence, namely the divine understanding.",["- *Respectus* is rendered \"relations\" here and \"regard\" elsewhere, as Kant uses it for the mutual bearing of substances. *Intellectus* = understanding (Verstand)."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0108":["*Demonstration.* Single substances, of which neither is the cause of the existence of another, have a separate existence, that is, one wholly intelligible without all others. Therefore, if the existence of any one is simply posited, there is nothing in it that would argue the existence of others different from itself. But relation is a respective determination, that is, one not intelligible in a being considered absolutely. This determination, like its determining ground, cannot therefore be understood through the existence of a substance posited in itself. If nothing further, then, were added to this, there would be no relation among them all and no commerce at all. Now in so far as individual substances have an existence independent of others, there is no room for their mutual connection. Nor is it open to finite substances to be causes of other substances. Nevertheless all things in the universe are found bound together in mutual connection. It must therefore be acknowledged that this relation depends on a common cause, namely God, the general principle of existents. But from the fact that God has simply established their existence, a mutual regard among them does not follow, unless the same plan (*schema*) of the divine understanding which gives existence has also fixed their relations, in so far as it conceived their existences as correlated. It therefore appears most clearly that the universal commerce of all things is to be ascribed to the conception of this divine idea alone.",["- The very long *Quum ... quoniam vero ...* sentence is divided into several, with the logical connections made explicit.","- *Schema* is rendered \"plan (*schema*)\". *Determinatio respectiva* = \"respective determination\".","- \"Hujus divinae ideae conceptui\" is rendered \"the conception of this divine idea\"."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0109":["--- notes\n- \"DILUCIDATIO\" is the running head, which the job instructions say to omit. Nothing is translated.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0110":["I seem to myself to have been the first to establish, by most evident reasons, that the coexistence of the substances of the universe does not suffice to establish a connection among them. A certain community of origin is further required, and a harmonious dependence arising from it. To resume the nerve of the demonstration a little: if substance A exists, and B also exists, B cannot on that account be held to posit anything in A. For suppose that B determines something in A, that is, contains the ground of the determination C. This is a relative predicate, not intelligible unless A is present besides B. Substance B will therefore, through that which is the ground of C, presuppose the existence of substance A. But if substance B alone existed, it would be wholly undetermined through its existence whether some A ought to exist or not. It cannot therefore be understood from its existence alone that it posits anything in others different from itself. Hence there is no relation and no commerce at all.\n\nIf, then, God has created, besides substance A, others B, D, E, to infinity, their mutual dependence in determinations still does not follow at once from their given existence. For because B, D, E exist besides A, and A is determined in itself in whatever way, it does not follow that B, D, E have determinations of existing conformable to it. In the manner of their common dependence on God there must therefore be present the ground of their mutual dependence as well. By what means this is brought about is easy to understand. The *schema* of the divine understanding, the origin of existences, is an enduring act, which they call conservation. If in this act the several substances are conceived by God solitarily and without relation of determinations, no connection and no mutual regard will arise among them. But if they are conceived relatively in his intelligence, then in the continuation of existence their determinations thereafter, conformably to this idea, always regard one another, that is, act and react. There is then a certain external state of each, which, if you depart from this principle, could not exist through their existence alone.",["- Divided the passage into two paragraphs and a number of shorter sentences; the paragraph break after \"no commerce at all\" is mine.","- \"Per ea quae sunt ratio τοῦ C\" is rendered \"through that which is the ground of C\"; the Greek article is simply dropped in English.","- *Schema* kept in Latin italics. \"They call conservation\" (*appellitant*) is left impersonal, referring to the schools.","- *Determinatio* = determination; *nexus* = connection; *commercium* = commerce."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0111":["*Usus.*",["- Taken as the section heading \"Applications\", as in 0100, and left in Latin. If it is a running head, it should be dropped."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0112":["1. Place, situation and space are relations of substances, by which they regard, through mutual determinations, others really distinct from themselves, and in this way are contained in external connection. Further, it has become known from what was demonstrated that the mere existence of substances does not of itself involve connection with others. It is therefore plain that if you posit that several substances exist, place and situation are not thereby determined at the same time, nor is space, which is composed of these relations in every way. But the mutual connection of substances requires an outline (*delineatio*) of the divine understanding, conceived relatively in an efficacious representation. This representation is wholly arbitrary on God's part, and can therefore be admitted or omitted at his good pleasure. It follows that substances can exist under the law that they are in no place, and in no relation at all with respect to the things of our universe.",["- Divided the opening *quoniam ... quoniam ...* period into separate statements, with \"It is therefore plain\" carrying its conclusion.","- *Locus, situs, spatium* = place, situation, space. *Delineatio* is rendered \"outline\" and marked with the Latin.","- Rendered \"ea lege\" as \"under the law that\", not \"on the condition that\"."]],"nova-dilucidatio-0113":["2. Since substances of the kind just described, released from the connection of our universe, can, at the divine pleasure, be several in number, and since they may nevertheless be bound together among themselves by a certain connection of determinations, they would thereby produce place, position and space, and would compose a world, one exempt from the compass of that world of which we are parts, that is, a solitary world. It is accordingly not absurd that there may be several worlds, even in the metaphysical sense, if it should so please GOD.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0114":["3. Since, then, the mere existence of substances is wholly insufficient for mutual commerce and for relations of determinations, and since it therefore argues, through the external connection, a cause common to all, in which their existence is [respectively] formed, and since, without this community of principle, a universal connection cannot be conceived, there is drawn from this a most evident testimony to the supreme cause of all things, that is, GOD, and indeed to one only. This testimony seems to me to surpass by far that proof from contingency.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0115":["4. The insane opinion of the Manichaeans also, who set over the government of the world two principles equally primary and in no way dependent on each other, is torn up by the roots by our principle. For a substance can have no commerce with the things of the universe unless it is either their common cause or has proceeded from the same cause as they. Therefore, if you declare one of these principles the cause of all substances, the other can in no way determine anything in them. If you declare one of them the cause of only some substances, these can have no commerce with the rest. You must therefore hold either that one of these principles depends on the other, or that both depend on a common cause; and this equally contradicts the hypothesis.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0116":["5. Further, since the determinations of substances have regard to one another, that is, since substances different from one another act mutually (for one determines something in another), the notion of space is completed by the interwoven actions of substances, with which a reaction must always be joined. If the external phenomenon of this universal action and reaction, extending through the whole compass of space in which bodies have regard to one another, is their mutual approach, it is called attraction. Since this is brought about by mere compresence, it reaches to any distances whatever, and is Newtonian attraction, or universal gravity. It is therefore probable that it is produced by the same connection of substances by which they determine space. Hence it is above all a primitive law of nature, to which matter is bound, and which endures continually only through the immediate upholding of GOD, according to the very opinion of those who profess themselves followers of Newton.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0117":["6. Since there is mutual commerce among all substances in so far as they are contained in the same space, there can be understood from this a mutual dependence in their determinations, and a universal action of spirits on bodies and of bodies on spirits. But because no substance has the power of determining others different from itself through what belongs to it internally (see the proof above), but only by virtue of the connection by which they are bound together in the idea of the infinite being, whatever determinations and changes are found in any substance always do indeed have regard to external things, but physical influx, properly so called, is excluded, and there is a universal harmony of things.\n\nYet the Leibnizian pre-established [harmony] is not thereby engendered, for it brings in among substances properly an agreement, not a mutual dependence. God does not employ the contrivances of art, adapted in a series of harmonized reasons, to bring about the concord of substances. Nor, again, is a special influx of God always posited here, that is, a commerce of substances through the occasional causes of Malebranche. For the one and the same action that makes substances exist and preserves them [as] individuals also procures for them a mutual and universal dependence, so that the divine action need not be determined now one way, now another, according to circumstances.\n\nIt is rather a real action of substances on one another, or commerce through truly efficient causes. For the same principle that establishes the existence of things exhibits them as bound to this law, and so, through the determinations that adhere to the origin of their existence, mutual commerce is established. Hence external changes may with the same right be said to be produced in this manner by efficient causes, as that with which what happens internally is ascribed to the force of the substance. Yet the natural efficacy of the latter rests on divine sustaining no less than does the foundation of the external relations.\n\nMeanwhile the system of universal commerce of substances so formed is certainly somewhat more correct than the commonly received system of physical influx. It discloses the origin itself of the mutual connection of things as to be sought outside the principle of substances considered in solitude, and it is just here that the trite system of efficient causes has chiefly strayed from the truth.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0118":["Scholium. Behold then, kind reader, two principles of the more recondite metaphysical knowledge, by whose aid one may gain possession of no contemptible dominion in the region of truths. If this science is diligently cultivated in this way, its soil will be found not so barren after all, and the reproach of idle and shadowy subtlety aimed at it by its despisers will be refuted by a rich harvest of nobler knowledge. There are indeed those, the keenest hunters in writings for depraved consequences, who are learned in always drawing some poison out of the opinions of others. That these men may perhaps twist some things even in this work of mine to a worse sense I shall not deny. Yet having allowed them to abound in their own sense, I think it my part not to care that someone may perhaps be pleased to judge wrongly, but to proceed along the right path of inquiry and doctrine. In this endeavour I ask, with due respect, that all who wish well to the liberal letters may favour me.",[]],"nova-dilucidatio-0119":["The end.",[]],"erdbeben-ursachen-0001":["On the Causes of the Earthquakes, on the Occasion of the Misfortune which Befell the Western Countries of Europe towards the End of Last Year.\n\nIn: *Wochentliche Königsbergische Frag- und Anzeigungs-Nachrichten* for the year 1756, No. 4 of 24 January and No. 5 of 31 January.",[]],"erdbeben-ursachen-0002":["Great events which touch the fate of all mankind rightly arouse that commendable curiosity which awakens at everything extraordinary and is accustomed to ask after its causes. In such a case the obligation to the public ought to move the investigator of nature to give an account of the insights which observation and inquiry can afford him. I decline the honour of discharging this duty in its whole extent, and leave it to him, if such a one should arise, who can boast of having seen exactly through the interior of the earth. My consideration will be only a sketch. To speak frankly, it will contain almost everything that can at present be said about the matter with probability; but it will certainly not contain enough to satisfy that rigorous judgment which tests everything on the touchstone of mathematical certainty.\n\nWe dwell in peace on a ground whose foundations are at times shaken. We build without concern on vaults whose pillars here and there sway and threaten collapse. Unconcerned about a fate which perhaps is not far from ourselves, we give place to pity rather than to fear when we become aware of the devastation which the ruin hidden beneath our feet works in our neighbourhood. It is without doubt a benefit of Providence to be unassailed by the fear of such fates, to the prevention of which no possible anxiety can contribute the least, and not to enlarge our actual suffering by fear of that which we recognise as possible.",[]],"erdbeben-ursachen-0003":["The first thing that offers itself to our attention is that the ground on which we stand is hollow, and that its vaults run on almost in one continuous connection through widely extended regions, even beneath the bed of the sea. I adduce no examples from history on this point; it is not my intention to furnish a history of earthquakes. The dreadful din, heard in many earthquakes, like the raging of a subterranean storm-wind or like the passing of heavy wagons over stone pavement; the effect of these quakes, propagated at the same time into far-extended lands, of which Iceland and Lisbon, separated by a sea of more than three hundred and fifty German miles and set in motion on one day, bear undeniable witness: all these appearances [*Erscheinungen*] agree in confirming the connection of these subterranean vaults.",["- 0113: \"pro lubitu diuino\" rendered \"at the divine pleasure\"; the long first sentence is left as a subordinate clause, since the main clause is elliptical.","- 0114: \"respectiue informata\" is obscure; I render \"[respectively] formed\" and flag the reading as doubtful.","- 0115: \"dictites\" taken as a form of *dictitare*, \"declare\"; the last clause (\"hypothesi\") refers to the supposition of two independent principles.","- 0116: The Latin sentence is broken at \"quam adeoque… probabile est… hinc maxime primitiuam\"; I divided it into several sentences and supplied \"Hence\" and \"it is\" for smoothness. The syntax is irregular in the original.","- 0117: Divided into four paragraphs for clarity; \"[as]\" before \"individuals\" and \"[harmony]\" after \"pre-established\" are supplied. \"Influxus Physicus\" is kept in the sense of *influxus physicus*.","- 0118: \"sensu suo abundare passus\" is taken as \"having allowed them to abound in their own sense\"; I regard the construction as slightly corrupt or elliptical.","- 0002: \"Probirstein der mathematischen Gewißheit\" rendered \"touchstone\"; \"unter wirkliches Leiden\" is evidently an error for \"unser wirkliches Leiden\" (the source reading), and I translated it as \"our actual suffering.\"","- 0003: \"4tehalb hundert\" taken as 3½ hundred (350). The long sentence beginning \"Das fürchterliche Getöse\" is irregular; I recast it with a colon and the subject resumed at \"all these appearances.\""]],"erdbeben-ursachen-0004":["I should have to go back to the history of the earth in chaos, if I were to say anything intelligible about the cause which, in the formation of the earth, gave rise to these caverns. Such explanations have only too much the appearance of fictions, unless one can set out in their whole compass the grounds which establish their credibility. But whatever the cause may be, it is certain that the direction of these caverns is parallel to the mountains, and, through a natural connection, to the great rivers also; for these occupy the lowest part of a long valley which is bounded on both sides by mountains running parallel. It is likewise in this same direction that earthquakes chiefly spread. In the earthquakes which extended through the greater part of Italy, a movement of the church lamps from north almost directly to south was observed; and the recent earthquake had the direction from west to east, which is also the main direction of the mountains that traverse the highest part of Europe.",[]],"erdbeben-ursachen-0005":["If in such terrible events men are permitted to use some precaution, and if it is not regarded as a rash and futile endeavour to set against general afflictions certain measures which reason offers, should not the unhappy remnant of Lisbon hesitate to build itself up again, along its length, on the same river which marks the direction in which the earth-shocks in that country must naturally occur? Gentil *) attests that when a town is shaken along its greatest length by an earthquake of the same direction, all the houses are overthrown, whereas if the direction is across the breadth, only a few fall. The reason is clear. The swaying of the ground throws the buildings out of the perpendicular. Now when a row of buildings is thus set swaying from east to west, each has not merely to bear its own weight; the western ones press at the same time upon the eastern, and so infallibly throw them down. If, on the other hand, they are moved across the breadth, where each has only to maintain its own equilibrium, less damage must result, other circumstances being equal. The disaster of Lisbon thus appears to have been increased by its situation, which extended along the bank of the Tagus; and on these grounds every town in a country where earthquakes have often been felt, and where their direction can be inferred from experience, ought not to be laid out in a direction parallel to this. But in such cases the greater part of mankind are of quite another opinion. Since fear robs them of reflection, they believe that in such general calamities they perceive an evil of an entirely different kind from those against which one is entitled to use precaution; and they imagine they can soften the harshness of fate by a blind submission, whereby they leave themselves to its mercy [*auf Gnade und Ungnade*, \"at discretion\"].",[]],"erdbeben-ursachen-0006":["*) Gentil's Voyage round the World, as cited by Buffon. The same author also confirms that the direction of earthquakes runs almost always parallel to the direction of great rivers.",[]],"erdbeben-ursachen-0007":["The main course of earthquakes follows the direction of the highest mountains, and so those countries are chiefly shaken which lie near these, especially when they are enclosed between two ranges of mountains, since in that case the shocks from both sides unite. In a flat country which has no connection with mountains they are rarer and weak. That is why Peru and Chile are the countries which, almost beyond all others in the world, are subject to the most frequent shocks. There the precaution is observed of building houses of two storeys, of which only the lower is of masonry, the upper being made of cane and light wood, so that the inhabitants may not be struck dead beneath it. Italy, and even Iceland, which lies partly in the frigid zone, and other high regions of Europe, prove this agreement. The earthquake which in December of last year spread from evening towards morning [i.e. from west to east] through France, Switzerland, Swabia, the Tyrol and Bavaria, kept chiefly to the line of the highest regions of this continent. But it is also known that all the main mountain chains shoot out lateral branches crosswise. Into these the subterranean ignition likewise gradually spreads; and accordingly, after it had reached the high regions of the Swiss mountains, it ran through the caverns also which continue parallel to the Rhine as far as Lower Germany. What may be the cause of this law, by which nature chiefly connects earthquakes with the high regions? If it is established that a subterranean ignition causes these shocks, one may readily judge that, since the caverns in mountainous regions are more extensive, the exhalation of combustible vapours will there be freer, and communication with the air shut up in the subterranean regions (which is always indispensable to ignition) will be less hindered. Moreover, knowledge of the inner natural constitution of the earth, so far as men are permitted to discover it, teaches that the strata in mountainous regions lie by far not so high as in flat countries, and that the resistance to the shock is therefore less there than here. If, then, it is asked whether our own country also has cause to fear these calamities, I should, if I had the calling to preach the reform of morals, leave the fear of them its value on account of the general possibility, which of course cannot be denied here. But since, among the motives to godliness, those drawn from earthquakes are without doubt the weakest, and my purpose is only to adduce physical grounds for conjecture, it will readily be gathered from what has been said that, since Prussia is not only a country without mountains but must also be regarded as the continuation of an almost entirely flat country, there is the greater occasion to take comfort, from the dispositions of Providence, in the opposite hope.",[]],"erdbeben-ursachen-0008":["It is time to say something of the cause of earthquakes. It is an easy matter for a student of nature to imitate their phenomena [*Erscheinungen*]. One takes 25 pounds of iron filings and as much sulphur, mixes them with common water, buries this paste a foot or a foot and a half deep in the earth, and presses the earth firmly down over it. After some hours a thick vapour is seen to rise, the earth is shaken, and flames break out from the ground. It cannot be doubted that the first two materials are frequently found in the interior of the earth, and the water which percolates through clefts and fissures in the rock can bring them into fermentation. Another experiment yields combustible vapours from the mixture of cold materials which ignite of themselves. Two drams of oil of vitriol, mixed with 8 drams of common water and poured upon 2 drams of iron filings, produce a violent effervescence and vapours which ignite of themselves. Who can doubt that vitriolic acid and particles of iron are contained in sufficient quantity in the interior of the earth? If water now comes to them and occasions their reciprocal action, they will emit vapours which strive to expand, shake the ground, and break out in flames at the openings of fire-spouting mountains.",["Passage 0004: no notes beyond the restatement in plain English.","Passage 0005: the long opening conditional is kept as one sentence but the \"whereby\" clause is loosened; \"Gentil *)\" retains the footnote marker, and the *auf Gnade und Ungnade* gloss is supplied in brackets.","Passage 0005: \"wo ein jegliches nur sein eigen Gleichgewicht...\" is restated as a separate \"If, on the other hand\" sentence to smooth the irregular anakoluthon.","Passage 0007: \"von Abend gegen Morgen\" is rendered literally, with the bracketed gloss [i.e. from west to east] supplied by me.","Passage 0007: \"Strich\" is rendered \"line\"/\"course\"; \"Ausdämpfung\" as \"exhalation\"; \"Schichten\" as \"strata\" (the sense \"lie high\" is Kant's, meaning thickness of overlying beds, an interpretive reading).","Passage 0007: the sentence beginning \"Wenn man also frägt...\" is divided in two; \"nun aber\" is rendered \"But since\" to resolve the broken construction. \"Zusammenhang\" etc. as in the German.","Passage 0008: \"Quentchen\" rendered \"dram\" (a small apothecaries' weight); \"Eisenfeilig\" taken as OCR/orthographic variant of *Eisenfeile* (iron filings); \"Erscheinungen\" = phenomena/appearances, here the ordinary sense."]],"erdbeben-ursachen-0009":["It has long been observed that a country has been freed from violent shocks when a volcano [*feuerspeyender Berg*] in its neighbourhood has erupted, thereby giving the pent-up vapours a means of escape; and it is known that earthquakes around Naples are far more frequent and more terrible when Vesuvius has been quiet for a long time. In this way what puts us in terror often serves us as a benefit. A volcano that should open in the mountains of Portugal might become a harbinger that the calamity was gradually withdrawing.",["- *feuerspeyender Berg* (literally \"fire-spewing mountain\") rendered \"volcano\"; Kant uses no other word here.","- The first sentence was kept whole; the second is divided into two sentences, with \"In this way\" retained to link them.","- *Neapolis* given as the usual English \"Naples\", *Vesuv* as \"Vesuvius\"."]],"erdbeben-ursachen-0010":["The violent movement of the waters, which was perceived on the unhappy All Saints' Day on so many sea coasts, is in this event the strangest object of wonder and inquiry. That earthquakes extend to beneath the sea-bed, and subject ships to as violent a shaking as if they were fixed on hard, shaken ground, is common experience. But in the regions where the water fell into agitation there was no trace of any earthquake; at least it could not be felt at all at a moderate distance from the coasts. Nevertheless this movement of the water is not wholly without precedent. In the year 1692, during an almost general earthquake, something of the same kind was observed on the coasts of Holland, England and Germany. I hear that many are inclined, and not without reason, to derive this surging of the waters from a continued shaking which the sea received off the Portuguese coasts through the direct impact of the earthquake. At first this explanation appears to be exposed to difficulties. I readily grasp that in a fluid body every pressure must become perceptible throughout the whole mass; but how could the pressures of the waters of the Portuguese sea, after they had spread over some hundreds of miles, still set the water at Glückstadt and Huysum in motion to a height of several feet? Does it not seem that mountains of water as high as the heavens must have arisen there, in order to excite here scarcely perceptible waves?\n\nMy answer is this. There are two ways in which a fluid body can be set in motion throughout its whole mass by a cause acting at one place. The one is by the oscillating movement of rising and falling, that is, in the manner of waves. The other is by a sudden pressure, which shakes the mass of water in its interior and drives it onward as a solid body, without leaving it time to evade the pressure by an oscillating surge and gradually to spread its motion. The former is without doubt not capable of sufficing to explain the event cited. As to the latter, however, consider that water resists a sudden violent pressure like a solid body, and propagates this pressure sideways with the same violence, leaving the adjoining water no time to rise above its level. Consider, for example, the experiment of M. Carrée, in the second part of the *physische Abhandlungen* of the Academy of Sciences, p. 549. He fired a musket-ball into a chest made of two-inch boards and filled with water, and the blow compressed the water so that the chest was burst entirely apart. If one considers this, one will be able to form some conception of this way of moving water.\n\nLet us suppose, for example, that the whole western coast of Portugal and Spain, from Cape St. Vincent to Cape Finisterre (*Finis terrae*), was shaken over a distance of about 100 German miles, and that this shaking extended just as far westward into the sea. Then 10,000 German square miles of the sea-bed would have been raised by a sudden tremor. We do not estimate its velocity too high if we set it equal to the movement of a gunpowder mine, which throws the bodies lying upon it to a height of 15 feet, and is therefore capable (according to the principles of mechanics) of covering 30 feet in a second. The water lying above resisted this sudden shaking in such a way that it did not give way and swell up in waves, as happens with slow movements, but received the whole pressure and drove the surrounding water sideways just as violently. With so rapid an impulse this water is to be regarded as a solid body, whose distant end moves forward with the same velocity as that with which the end struck is driven on. Thus in every beam of the fluid matter (if I may use this expression), though it be 200 or 300 miles long, there is no diminished motion, provided it be thought of as enclosed in a channel which has at the distant end an opening just as wide as at the beginning. But if the latter opening is wider, the motion through it will be diminished in exactly inverse proportion.\n\nNow, however, the propagation of the water's movement must be thought of as spreading round about in a circle, whose breadth increases with distance from the centre, so that at its boundary the onward flow of the water is lessened in the same measure. Accordingly on the Holstein coasts, which are 300 German miles distant from the assumed centre of the shock, it will be found six times smaller than on the Portuguese coasts, which on the supposition lie at a distance of about 50 miles from the same point. The movement on the Holstein and Danish coasts will therefore still be great enough to cover 5 feet in a second, which equals the force of a very swift current.\n\nAgainst this an objection might be made, that the propagation of the pressure into the waters of the North Sea could take place only through the Channel at Calais, and that its shock, being spread out into a wide sea, must be extraordinarily weakened. But if one considers that the pressure of the water between the French and English coasts, before it reaches the Channel, must be increased by the compression between these lands just as much as it is afterwards diminished by the spreading out, nothing considerable can be taken away thereby from the effects of the shock on the Holstein coasts mentioned.",["- Restated several long sentences as shorter ones; the paragraph breaks are mine, made where the argument turns (objection, answer, calculation, second objection).","- \"Carree\" corrected to the French name Carré in rendering (\"Carrée\"); the spelling is uncertain, and I have left the source's form as a doubtful reading.","- *Wasserberge*, *Balken*: \"mountains of water\", \"beam\" (of fluid), kept literal.","- In \"die Fortsetzung ... Erweiterung\", I took *Erweiterung* as the breadth of the circle, not as a mere widening; \"so that\" supplies the connection.","- \"Als wenn sie ... befestigt wären\": subjunctive rendered by \"as if\".","- \"Jene\" (the opening at the far end, \"jene weiter ist\") is taken to refer to the far opening, which is the sense the argument requires. Kant's text reads \"wenn jene weiter ist\", which I rendered \"the latter opening\" for clarity.","- Kant's figures (e.g. \"6mal\", 300 and 50 miles) are reproduced as they stand; they are consistent with the ratio 300:50.","- *Finis terrae* kept in Latin italics beside the English name."]],"erdbeben-ursachen-0011":["The most singular thing about this pressure of the waters is that it was perceived even in inland lakes which have no visible connection with the sea, at Templin and in Norway. This seems almost the strongest of all the proofs that have ever been advanced to establish the subterranean communication of inland waters with the sea. To extricate oneself from the difficulty which can be raised against this from the [law of] equilibrium, one would have to represent to oneself that the water of a lake really flows continually downward through the channels by which it is connected with the sea. Since these channels are narrow, however, and what the lake loses through them is sufficiently made good by the brooks and streams that flow into it, this outflow could for that reason not become noticeable.",["- \"[law of]\" supplied: *dem Gleichgewichte* is taken as the hydrostatic equilibrium of water levels.","- The long second sentence was divided in two; the subjunctive *flösse* and *könne* (indirect statement) is kept as \"would have to represent... this outflow could not.\"","- *mittelländischen Gewässer* rendered \"inland waters\" (not \"Mediterranean\")."]],"erdbeben-ursachen-0012":["Yet in so strange an event one ought not lightly to pass a hasty judgment. For it is not impossible that the agitation of the inland lakes may have come from other causes. The subterranean air, set in motion by the eruption of this raging fire, might well force its way through the fissures of the earth's strata, which, apart from this violent expansion, close to it every passage. Nature discloses herself only little by little. We ought not, through impatience, to try to supply by invention what she hides from us, but to wait until she reveals her secrets, beyond doubt, in distinct effects.",["- The opening \"Wiewohl...\" is a dependent-form fragment used as a sentence; rendered as \"Yet...\" to give a plain English connection.","- *Ausspannung* rendered \"expansion\"; *abzurathen* (here, roughly \"to guess at/make good\") rendered \"to supply\", a doubtful rendering of an unusual use."]],"erdbeben-ursachen-0013":["The cause of earthquakes seems to extend its effect as far as the atmosphere. Some hours before the earth is shaken, a red sky and other signs of an altered condition of the air have often been observed. Shortly beforehand the animals are quite seized with terror. The birds flee into the houses; rats and mice creep out of their holes. At this moment the heated vapour, which is on the point of igniting, infallibly breaks through the upper vault of the earth. I do not venture to determine what effects are to be expected from it. At the least, they are not welcome to the student of nature. For what hope can he have of discovering the laws according to which the changes of the atmosphere succeed one another, if a subterranean atmosphere mingles its effects with theirs? And can it well be doubted that this must happen often? Otherwise it would scarcely be comprehensible how, in the alternation of weather, whose causes are partly constant and partly periodic, no recurrence at all is to be found.",[]],"erdbeben-ursachen-0014":["Note. The day of the earthquake in Iceland, given in the previous issue as the 1st of November, is to be corrected to the 11th of September, in accordance with the report in issue no. 199 of the *Hamburger Correspondent*.",[]],"erdbeben-ursachen-0015":["The present reflections are to be regarded as a small preliminary exercise on the memorable natural event which has taken place in our days. The importance and manifold peculiarities of this event move me to communicate to the public, in a fuller treatise, a detailed history of this earthquake, its extension over the countries of Europe, the noteworthy circumstances attending it, and the reflections to which these may give occasion. This treatise will appear in a few days from the Royal Court and Academy Printing-House.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0001":["History and Natural Description of the Most Remarkable Occurrences of the Earthquake which, at the End of the Year 1755, Shook a Great Part of the Earth,\nby\nM. Immanuel Kant.\nKönigsberg, printed and published by Johann Heinrich Hartung, 1756.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0002":["Nature has not spread out in vain a treasure of rarities everywhere for contemplation and admiration. Man, to whom the management of the earth is entrusted, has the capacity, and also the desire, to come to know them, and he praises the Creator through his insights. Even the dreadful instruments by which the human race is visited, the shaking of lands, the fury of the sea stirred in its depths, the fire-spewing mountains, summon man to reflection. They are planted in nature by GOD, no less than other, already familiar causes of discomfort, as a proper consequence of constant laws; these latter are held to be more natural only because we are more familiar with them.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0003":["The contemplation of such terrible occurrences is instructive. It humbles man by letting him see that he has no right, or at least has forfeited it, to expect only convenient consequences from the laws of nature which GOD has ordained. Perhaps in this way, too, he learns to see that this arena of his desires ought properly not to contain the goal of all his aims.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0004":["Preliminary\nOn the Constitution of the Earth in its Interior.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0005":["We know the surface of the earth fairly completely, so far as extent is concerned. But we have still a world beneath our feet, with which we are as yet very little acquainted. The fissures in the mountains, which open to our plummet chasms without bottom, the caves we meet with in the interior of mountains, the deepest shafts of the mines, which we have been extending through centuries, are far from sufficient to give us distinct knowledge of the inner structure of the great mass which we inhabit.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0006":["The greatest depth to which men have descended from the uppermost surface of the solid land does not yet amount to 500 fathoms, that is, not yet the six-thousandth part of the distance to the centre of the earth. And yet these pits are still within the mountains; indeed all the solid land is a mountain, in which, in order to reach merely the same depth as the bed of the sea, one would have to descend at least three times as far.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0007":["But what nature hides from our eye and our direct experiments, she herself discloses through her effects. Earthquakes have revealed to us that the surface of the earth is full of vaults and caverns, and that hidden mines with manifold labyrinths run on everywhere beneath our feet. The sequel in the history of the earthquake will place this beyond doubt. We must ascribe these caverns to the very same cause which prepared for the seas their bed. For it is certain, if one is even moderately informed about the remains which the ocean has left behind from its former abode over all the solid land, about the immeasurable heaps of shells found even in the interior of mountains, about the petrified sea-creatures brought out of the deepest shafts (I say, if one is informed about all this), one will readily see three things. First, the sea once covered all the land for a long time; this abode lasted long and is older than the Flood. Secondly, the waters could not possibly have withdrawn otherwise than by the sinking of their floor, here and there, into deep pits, thus preparing for them deep basins into which they drained, and between whose shores they are still kept confined. Thirdly, meanwhile the raised regions of this sunken crust became solid land, which is everywhere undermined with hollows, and whose expanse is beset with steep summits. These, under the name of mountains, run along the highest elevation of the solid land in all those directions in which it extends to any considerable length.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0008":["These caverns all contain a blazing fire, or at least that combustible matter which needs only a slight stimulus to rage violently about it and to shake, or even to split, the ground above.",["- 0013: restated the final question as separate sentences (\"what hope…\", \"can it be doubted…\", \"Otherwise…\"); \"Luftkreis\" rendered \"atmosphere\" and \"Atmosphäre\" likewise; \"Naturforscher\" = \"student of nature\".","- 0014: \"Hamb. Corresp.\" expanded to *Hamburger Correspondent* (supplied expansion, not in the German); \"Stück\" rendered \"issue\".","- 0001: title-page lines run together; \"M.\" (Magister) kept.","- 0002: the relative clause on \"andre schon gewohnte Ursachen\" was split into a separate clause with \"these latter\"; \"vor\" = \"für\" throughout.","- 0007: very long sentence divided; the German \"erstlich … daß … daß … endlich\" is a threefold inference, and I numbered the third point \"Thirdly\" with its subordinate \"indessen daß\" read as the third claim, and added \"First/Secondly/Thirdly\" for clarity. \"Sündfluth\" = \"Flood\". \"Klafter\" in 0006 = \"fathoms\".","- 0006: \"sechstausensten\" read as \"sechstausendsten\" (six-thousandth); the OCR/print form is retained in sense."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0009":["If we consider the region of this subterranean fire in the whole extent to which it reaches, we shall have to admit that there are few countries on the globe which have not at times felt its effects. In the extreme north the island of Iceland is subject to its most violent attacks, and not infrequently. In England, and even in Sweden, some slight shocks have been experienced. Nevertheless they occur more frequently and more strongly in the southern countries, I mean those lying nearer the equator. Italy, and the islands of all the seas which lie near the equinoctial line, especially those in the Indian Ocean, are frequently afflicted by this disturbance of the ground beneath them. Among the latter there is hardly a single one that has not a mountain which either still at times spews fire or at least has done so formerly; and these islands are just as often subject to earthquakes.\n\nThere is an ingenious precaution, if one may believe the report of Hübner, which the Dutch employ with regard to the costly spice of nutmegs and cloves, which they permit to be propagated only on the two islands of Banda and Amboina. In order not to expose these spices to the danger of being exterminated from the earth, should one of these islands happen to suffer total destruction by an earthquake, they always maintain, on another island far removed from it, a nursery of both plants.\n\nPeru and Chile, which lie near the line, are troubled by this evil more frequently than any other country in the world. In the first of these countries hardly a day passes on which some slight shocks of earthquake are not felt. One must not imagine that this is to be regarded as a consequence of the far greater heat of the sun which acts upon the soil of these countries. In a cellar of scarcely 40 feet in depth hardly any difference between summer and winter is to be perceived. So little is the warmth of the sun able to penetrate the soil to great depths, so as to excite the inflammable matter and set it in motion. Rather, earthquakes depend upon the constitution of the subterranean vaults, and these upon the law according to which the subsidences of the uppermost crust of the earth must originally have taken place. The nearer to the line, the deeper and more various were the depressions which this crust made; and thereby these mines, which contain the tinder for earthquakes, have become more extensive, and so better fitted for its ignition.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0010":["This preliminary account of the subterranean passages is of no small importance for understanding what will occur in what follows: the wide extension of earthquakes over great countries, the course they take, the places where they rage most violently, and those where they first begin.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0011":["I now begin with the history of the last earthquake itself. By this I understand no history of the misfortunes which men have suffered through it, no list of the devastated cities and of the inhabitants buried beneath their ruins. One must gather together everything dreadful that the imagination can represent, in order to form some idea of the terror in which men must find themselves when the earth is moved beneath their feet, when everything around them collapses, when water stirred up from its depths completes the calamity by floods, when the fear of death, despair at the total loss of all possessions, and finally the sight of other wretched persons cast down the steadiest courage. Such a narrative would be moving; and since it has an effect upon the heart, it might perhaps also have one upon its improvement. But I leave this history to more skilful hands. Here I describe only the work of nature, the remarkable natural circumstances which accompanied the dreadful event, and its causes.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0012":["Of the Forerunners of the Last Earthquake.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0013":["The prelude of the subterranean ignition, which afterwards became so terrible, I place in the atmospheric phenomenon observed at Locarno in Switzerland on the 14th of October of last year, at 8 o'clock in the morning. A vapour, warm as if issuing from an oven, spread abroad and in two hours changed into a red mist, from which toward evening a blood-red rain arose; when collected, this let fall a sediment, reddish and glutinous, amounting to ⅑ [of its volume]. The snow, six feet deep, was likewise coloured red. This purple rain was observed over 40 hours' [distance], that is, about 20 German miles square, and even as far as Swabia. This atmospheric phenomenon was followed by unnatural downpours, which in three days yielded 23 inches' depth of water, that is, more than falls throughout the whole year in a country of moderately moist character. This rain lasted over 14 days, though not always with equal violence. The rivers of Lombardy which take their source in the Swiss mountains, and likewise the Rhône, swelled with water and overflowed their banks. From this time terrible hurricanes prevailed in the air, raging fiercely everywhere. Even in the middle of November a similar purple rain fell at Ulm, and the disorder in the atmosphere, the whirlwinds in Italy, and the exceedingly wet weather continued.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0014":["If one wishes to form a concept of the causes of this phenomenon and of its consequences, one must attend to the constitution of the ground over which it occurred. The Swiss mountains all contain extensive clefts beneath them, which are without doubt connected with the deepest subterranean passages. Scheuchzer counts nearly 20 chasms which at certain times blow out winds.\n\nLet us now suppose that the mineral materials hidden in the interior of these caverns became mingled with the fluids with which they effervesce, and thereby fell into an inner fermentation, which could prepare the fire-nourishing materials for that ignition which was to break out fully within a few days. Let us picture to ourselves, for example, the acid contained in spirit of nitre, which nature herself necessarily prepares, set in motion either by an inflow of water or by other causes, and attacking the iron-earth on which it fell. Then these materials, on being mixed, will have become heated and will have expelled red, warm vapours from the clefts of the mountains. In the violence of the ebullition the particles of the red iron-earth were at the same time mingled with these vapours and carried away with them; and this gave occasion to the glutinous blood-rain of which we have made mention.\n\nThe nature of such vapours is to diminish the elastic force of the air, and by that very means to cause the aqueous vapours suspended in it to run together. By drawing in, through the natural slope [*Abhang*], all the moist clouds floating round about in the atmosphere toward the region where the height of the column of air has been lessened, they also cause those violent and lasting downpours which were observed in the regions named.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0015":["In this manner the subterranean fermentation announced beforehand, by the vapours it expelled, the misfortune which it was secretly preparing. *) The completion of the fate followed with slow steps. A fermentation does not at once break out into ignition. The fermenting and heated materials must meet with a combustible oil, sulphur, bitumen, or something of the kind, in order to take fire. Meanwhile the heating spread hither and thither in the subterranean passages; and at the moment when the dissolved combustible materials, in mixture with the others, were heated to the point of taking fire, the vaults of the earth were shaken, and the decree of fate was accomplished.",["(Notes follow, one per line, by passage.)","0009: The long sentence on the Dutch precaution was restated as a separate paragraph in two sentences; the German is grammatically tangled (the *daß*-clause hangs on *Vorsicht*). \"Hübner\" is kept as in the text.","0009: *Mittellinie* and *Linie* rendered \"equinoctial line\" / \"the line\" (equator); *Aeqvator* given as \"equator\". *Erdreich*, \"soil\"; *Grüffte*, \"vaults\".","0009: Obvious OCR break \"Beunruh igung\" silently corrected.","0013: Supplied \"[of its volume]\" for ⅑ and \"[distance]\" after \"40 hours'\" (*Stunden* as a measure of travel); neither is in the German, and the sense of \"40 Stunden\" is a measure of distance. The ⅑ figure is as printed.","0013: *Meilen ins Gevierte* rendered \"miles square\".","0014: The sentence beginning \"Wenn wir nun annehmen…\" has no main clause in the German (it breaks off at *solte*); I completed it with a \"Let us suppose\" construction and made the following \"Wenn wir z. E.\" sentence its own, a smoothing of the irregularity. Several sentences were divided.","0014: *Begriff* = \"concept\"; *Ausspannungskraft* = \"elastic force\".","0015: The asterisk-marker \"*)\" is retained; the footnote text it points to is not in the passage. *Verhängnisse* rendered \"fate\" / \"decree of fate\" for the plural."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0016":["*) Eight days before the shock the ground near Cadiz was covered with a multitude of worms that had crept out of the earth. The cause just mentioned had driven them forth. In certain other earthquakes, violent flashes of lightning in the air, and an anxiety observed in animals, have been forerunners.",["- \"Cadix\" rendered \"Cadiz\"; \"die nur angeführte Ursache\" taken as the cause named in the preceding text (not in this passage).","- The final sentence is somewhat loose in German (\"sind ... Vorboten gewesen\"); I have put the two signs in the plural subject and set off the second clause for clarity.","- Footnote marker *) retained."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0017":["The Earthquake and the Movement of the Waters\nof 1 November 1755.",["- Heading; the line break is kept."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0018":["The moment at which this shock occurred seems most correctly fixed at 9.50 in the forenoon at Lisbon. This time agrees exactly with that at which it was observed at Madrid, namely 10.17 to 18 minutes, if the difference of longitude between the two cities is converted into a difference of time. At the same time the waters were thrown into agitation over an astonishing extent, both those which have a visible communication with the ocean and those which may stand in some hidden connection with it. From Åbo in Finland to the archipelago of the West Indies, few coasts, or none, remained free from it. It dominated a stretch of 1500 miles in almost the same time. If we could be assured that the time at which it was felt at Glückstadt on the Elbe is, according to the public reports, to be put quite exactly at 11.30, we should conclude that the movement of the water took 15 minutes to travel from Lisbon to the coasts of Holstein. At this same time it was also noticed on all the coasts of the Mediterranean, and the whole extent of its range is not yet known.",["- \"Uhr ... Minuten\" rendered in the compact form \"9.50\", \"10.17\". \"Abo\" given as Åbo.","- \"Sie\" (the Wasserbewegung/Erschütterung) is rendered \"it\". \"Meilen\" kept as \"miles\".","- The 11.30 / 15 minutes figures are as in the text; the arithmetic is Kant's and I have not altered it. Possibly the sum does not accord with the Lisbon time given, but I leave it as printed.","- \"beherrschet\" translated \"dominated\" to keep Kant's word; \"pervaded\" would be freer."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0019":["The waters on the mainland that seem cut off from all communication with the sea, the springs and the lakes, were set in extraordinary motion at the same time in many countries far distant from one another. Most of the lakes in Switzerland, the lake near Templin in the Mark, and some lakes in Norway and Sweden, fell into a surging motion, far more violent and disorderly than in a storm, and the air was at the same time still. The lake near Neuchâtel, if we may rely on the reports, drained away into hidden clefts, and the one near Memmingen did the same, but soon came back. In these very minutes the mineral water at Teplitz in Bohemia suddenly failed, and returned blood-red. The force with which the water had been driven through had widened its old channels, and it thereby received a stronger inflow. The inhabitants of this town had good reason to sing the *Te Deum laudamus*, while those in Lisbon were striking up quite different tones. Such are the accidents that befall mankind: one man's joy and another's misfortune often have a common cause. In the kingdom of Fez in Africa a subterranean force split a mountain and poured blood-red streams from its chasm. Near Angoulême in France a subterranean roar was heard; a deep cavern opened in the plain and held unfathomable water in itself. At Gemenos in Provence a spring suddenly became muddy and then poured forth tinged with red. The surrounding districts reported like changes in their springs. All this took place in the very minutes in which the earthquake was devastating the coasts of Portugal. Here and there, in this same brief moment, some earth-tremors were also perceived in far distant countries. But almost all of them occurred close to the sea-coast. At Cork in Ireland, likewise at Glückstadt and in some other places lying by the sea, slight tremors occurred. Milan is perhaps the place that was shaken, on that same day, at the greatest distance from the sea-shore. That same forenoon at eight o'clock Vesuvius near Naples raged, and became quiet about the time when the shock occurred in Portugal.",["- Place names modernised: Meinungen → [Memmingen] is a conjecture (the German \"Meinungen\" is probably Memmingen; Meiningen is also possible); \"Gemenox\" → Gemenos; \"Töplitz\" → Teplitz; \"Mayland\" → Milan.","- \"Die Einwohner ... hatten gut te Deum laudamus zu singen\": \"hatten gut\" taken idiomatically as \"had good reason/it was easy for them\"; OCR \"te\" left as *Te*.","- \"Zufälle\" rendered \"accidents\" in the older sense of contingent events.","- \"Gruft\" rendered \"cavern\"; \"Schlund\" \"chasm\"."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0020":["Reflection on the Cause of this Movement of the Waters.",["- Heading; line break joined."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0021":["History has no example of so widely extended a shaking of all the waters and of a great part of the earth, felt simultaneously in the course of a few minutes. Caution is therefore needed in inferring its cause from a single occurrence. We may conceive chiefly the following causes, which could have produced the natural event cited. The first possibility is a trembling of the sea-bed, everywhere immediately beneath those places where the sea was set in agitation. In that case we should have to give the ground why the vein of fire which produced these tremors ran on only beneath the bottom of the seas, without extending under the lands that stand in close connection with these seas and often interrupt their communication. We should find ourselves embarrassed by the question why the shaking of the ground, which spread from Glückstadt on the North Sea to Lübeck on the Baltic and to the Mecklenburg coasts, was not felt in Holstein, which lies in the middle between these seas, and where only a gentle tremor was perceived close to the water's edge, but none in the interior of the country. The surging of waters far distant from the sea, such as the lake near Templin, those in Switzerland, and others, convinces us most clearly. It is easy to see that, if a body of water is to be brought into so violent a commotion by the trembling of the ground, the shock must certainly be no slight one. But why did all the surrounding countries, beneath which the vein of fire must necessarily have run, not feel this violent shock? It is easily seen that all the marks of truth are against this opinion. Another supposition is that a shock was impressed on all sides upon the dense mass of the earth itself by a violent blow struck in one place, as the ground trembles at some distance when a powder-magazine blows up. Applied to this case, such a supposition also loses all probability, both for the reason already given and because of the frightful extent, which, if compared with the extent of the whole earth, makes up so considerable a part of it that its trembling would necessarily have entailed a shaking of the whole terrestrial globe. Now we may learn from Buffon that an eruption of subterranean fire, which could throw up a mountain range 1700 miles long and 40 broad to the height of a mile, would not be able to displace the body of the earth by the breadth of a thumb.",["- Long sentences divided. The \"Eine Erschütterung die ... eingedrückt worden\" sentence is syntactically irregular; I supplied \"Another supposition is that\" [the second of the possible causes, not named by Kant as \"zweitens\"] to make the structure plain.","- \"Entweder erstlich\" has no following \"zweitens\" in this passage; the second alternative is evidently the one introduced at \"Eine Erschütterung ...\".","- \"Grund angeben\" rendered \"give the ground\"; \"Feuerader\" \"vein of fire\"; \"Rüttelung\" \"shaking/agitation\".","- \"Man würde sich ... betreten finden\" = \"be embarrassed/taken aback\"."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0022":["We shall therefore have to seek the spread of this movement of the waters in an intermediate medium better fitted to communicate a shock over great distances, namely in the water of the seas themselves, which stands in connection with that [part] which was thrown into violent and sudden agitation by a direct trembling of the sea-bed.",["- \"demjenigen\" refers to the water over the quaking sea-bed; I supplied [part].","- \"Mittelmaterie\" rendered \"intermediate medium\"."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0023":["In the weekly Königsberg *Notices* I tried to estimate the force with which the sea, through the blow of the shock that came from its bed, was driven onward over its whole extent. I assumed the shaken part of the sea-bottom to be merely a square, whose side is equal to the distance from Cape St. Vincent to Cape Finisterre, that is, to the length of the western coasts of Portugal and Spain. I regarded the force of the upheaving bottom as that of a powder mine, which in exploding is able to throw bodies lying above it to a height of 15 feet. By the rules according to which motion is propagated in a fluid body, I found this force on the Holstein coasts to be stronger than the swiftest current dashing against them. Let us here consider the force which the sea exerted from these causes from yet another point of view. Count Marsigli found by the sounding-lead that the greatest depth of the Mediterranean is over 8000 feet, and it is certain that the ocean, at a suitable distance from land, is deeper still; but here we will assume it to be only 6000 feet, that is, 1000 fathoms deep. We know that the weight with which so high a column of sea-water presses on the sea-bed must exceed the pressure of the atmosphere almost 200 times. We know also that it far exceeds the force of the fire behind a ball which is hurled 100 fathoms from the bore of a cannon within the time of a pulse-beat. This astonishing weight was unable to hold back the force with which the subterranean fire thrust the sea-bed swiftly upward; this moving force was therefore greater. With what pressure, then, was the water squeezed, to shoot suddenly out to the sides! Is it any wonder that it was perceived within a few minutes in Finland and at the same time in the West Indies? It cannot be determined at all how great the base of the immediate shock may actually have been. It may perhaps be incomparably greater than we have assumed. But in the seas where the movement of the water was perceived without any earthquake (on the Dutch, English and Norwegian coasts, and in the Baltic) it was certainly not to be found in the sea-bed. For in that case the solid land would certainly have been shaken in its interior also, which, however, was not observed at all.",["- The first sentence is divided into three; \"sie\" (in \"sie ... stärker ... befunden\") is taken to refer to the *Gewalt* (force), not to the sea.","- \"Cap St. Vincent\", \"Carthaune\" and the measures (Fuß, Klafter) are rendered as plainly as possible; \"Klafter\" = fathom, a choice of equivalent.","- The exclamatory \"Mit welchem Drucke ...\" is given an exclamation mark, followed by the rhetorical question that the German joins with \"und\".","- \"Anzeigen\" is rendered \"*Notices*\", being the title of the Königsberg weekly."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0024":["In ascribing the violent shaking of all the connected parts of the ocean to the single shock which its bed suffered in a certain region, I do not on that account wish to have denied the actual spread of subterranean fire beneath the solid land of almost the whole of Europe. In all probability the two occurred at the same time, and both had a share in the phenomena [*Erscheinungen*] that took place; only neither of them taken separately is to be regarded as the sole cause of all of them together. The heaving of the water in the North Sea, which made itself felt as a sudden shock, was not the effect of an earthquake raging beneath its bed. Shocks capable of producing such an effect would have to be very violent, and would therefore have had to be very noticeably felt beneath the solid land. But I do not on that account deny that all the solid land itself was set in a slight oscillation by a feeble force of the vapours kindled beneath its ground, or by other causes. This is seen in Milan, which on that day was threatened with the greatest danger of total collapse. Let us then suppose that the earth was set in a gentle motion by a slight oscillation, so great that over 100 Rhenish rods the ground rocked alternately to and fro by one inch. This motion will then have been so imperceptible that a building of 4 rods in height could not have been displaced from the perpendicular by half a grain, that is, by half the thickness of a knife-back, which would scarcely become perceptible even on the highest towers. The lakes, on the other hand, must have made this imperceptible motion very noticeable. For if a lake, for example, is only 2 German miles long, its water will be set in quite a strong rocking by this slight swaying of its bottom. The water then has over 14000 inches a fall of about one inch, and a rate of flow that is smaller by almost only one half than that of a really swift river, as the levelling of the Seine at Paris can teach us. After a number of oscillations to and fro, this could well have caused an extraordinary agitation in the water. We may, however, with good reason assume the motion of the earth to be twice as great as we have done, without its having properly been felt on the solid land; and then the motion of the inland lakes becomes all the more comprehensible to the eye.",["- Several long sentences are divided; \"welches\" in \"welches selbst auf den höchsten Thürmen...\" is taken to refer to the displacement, and \"welches ... verursachen können\" to the swinging of the water.","- The sentence on the Seine is smoothed in construction; the figures (14000 inches, one inch of fall) are given as printed and not checked against the arithmetic.","- \"Erscheinungen\" is bracketed as *Erscheinungen* here in its ordinary sense of \"phenomena\", not the critical term \"appearance\".","- \"Grans\" = grain, taken as the small unit of length; \"Rheinl. Ruthen\" = Rhenish rods."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0025":["One will therefore no longer be surprised that all the inland lakes in Switzerland, Sweden, Norway and Germany were seen so restless and surging, without any shaking of the ground being felt. It is found more extraordinary, however, that certain lakes in this disturbance dried up altogether, as the lake near Neuchâtel, that near Como, and that near Meiningen, although some of these have already filled with water again. This event, however, is not without example. There are some lakes on the earth which quite regularly drain away at certain times through hidden channels, and return at the appointed time. The Zirknitz lake in the Duchy of Carniola is a remarkable example of this. It has in its bed some holes, through which, however, it does not drain off before about St. James's Day, when it then runs away suddenly with all its fishes. After leaving its bed dry for three months as good pasture and arable land, it suddenly returns towards the month of November. This natural event is explained very intelligibly by comparison with the *diabetes* of hydraulics [the siphon]. In the cases now before us, however, one may easily conclude as follows. Many lakes receive their supply through spring-veins lying beneath their beds, and these springs have their source in the surrounding heights. When the effect of subterranean heating and evaporation had swallowed up the air in the cavities which are their water-reservoirs, the springs must thereby have been drawn back into those cavities, and must themselves have acted as a powerful suction-pump, drawing the lake in with them. The lake then, once the equilibrium of the air was restored, sought its natural outlet again. For to suppose that an inland lake is maintained by a subterranean communication with the sea, because it has no external inflow of brooks (as the public reports have wished to explain in the case of the lake at Meiningen), is exposed to a far too obvious absurdity, both because of the laws of equilibrium which oppose it and because of the saltness of sea-water.",["- The long sentence beginning \"Allein in unsern vorhabenden Fällen\" is broken into several; its tangled subordinate clauses are resolved by taking the springs (*Qvelladern*) as the subject of \"zurückgezogen worden seyn\" and the lake as that of \"gesucht\".","- \"diabetes der Hydraulick\" is kept as *diabetes* with \"[the siphon]\" supplied in brackets, as the sense requires.","- \"Meinungen\" is rendered Meiningen, \"Cirnitzer See\" as Zirknitz lake, and \"Neuschatel\" as Neuchâtel; these are identifications by judgment.","- \"Jacobi\" is rendered \"St. James's Day\" (25 July); \"die Luft verschlungen\" is kept literally, though the sense (rarefaction or consumption of the air in the cavities) is not fully clear."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0026":["It is already something usual in earthquakes that they throw the springs of water into disorder. I could here cite from the history of other earthquakes a whole register of springs that have been choked up and have broken out in other places, of spring water shot up to a great height from the earth, and the like; but I shall keep to my subject. From France we have been informed that in some places springs were stopped up, while others gave an excessive quantity of water. The Töplitz spring failed, to the alarm of the poor people of Töplitz; it returned first muddy, then blood-red, and finally natural and stronger than before. The discoloration of the waters in so many regions, even in the Kingdom of Fez and in France, is in my opinion to be ascribed to the mixing of vapours which have penetrated through the strata of earth, where the springs have their passage, and which have been set in fermentation with particles of sulphur and iron. When these vapours penetrate into the interior of the cisterns that contain the source of the spring, they either drive it out with greater force, or, by pressing the water into other channels, alter its outflow.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0027":["These are the most notable particulars of the history of the 1st of November, and the movement of the waters is the rarest of its circumstances. I find it highly credible that the earth-tremors which occurred close to the sea-coast, or to a body of water in communication with it, at Cork in Ireland, at Glückstadt, and here and there in Spain, are for the most part to be ascribed to the very pressure of the compressed sea-water. Its force must be incredibly great, if one multiplies the violence with which it strikes by the surface on which it impinges. I am also of opinion that the disaster of Lisbon, like that of most of the towns of the western coast of Europe, is to be ascribed to the situation which it had in relation to the region of the ocean in question; for there the whole force of the ocean, further strengthened at the mouth of the Tagus by the narrowness of a gulf, must have shaken the ground extraordinarily. Let it be judged whether the tremor, which was not perceptible in the interior of the country, could have been clearly noticed solely in towns lying on the sea-coast, if the pressure of the waters had not had a share in it.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0028":["There is yet a last appearance of this great event that is noteworthy. A considerable time after the earthquake, namely almost one to one and a half hours, a dreadful piling up of the waters in the ocean was seen, together with a swelling of the Tagus, which alternately rose six feet higher than the highest flood-tide and soon afterwards fell almost as much lower than the lowest ebb. This movement of the sea occurred a considerable time after the earthquake and after the first dreadful pressure of the waters. It also completed the ruin of the town of Setuval, since it rose over its ruins and utterly destroyed what the shock had spared. If one has first formed a proper conception of the violence of the sea-water driven forward by the moved sea-bed, one will easily be able to imagine that it must return with force, after its pressure had spread out into all the immeasurable regions round about. The time of its return depends on the wide extent over which it has acted around it; and its surging, especially on the shores, must in proportion to that extent have been equally terrible *).",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0029":["*) In the harbour at Husum this surging of the water was likewise observed between twelve and one o'clock, that is, an hour later than the first shock of the waters in the North Sea.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0030":["The Earthquake of the 18th of November.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0031":["From the 17th to the 18th of the same month the public reports gave notice of a notable earth-tremor on the coasts of Portugal and Spain as well as in Africa. At midday of the 17th it was felt at Gibraltar, at the strait of the Mediterranean, and towards evening at Whitehaven in Yorkshire in England. Between the 17th and the 18th it was already in the English colonies of America. On the same 18th it was also violently felt in the region of Acquapendente and della Grotta in Italy. **)",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0032":["**) Likewise at Glowson in the county of Hertford, where, with a violent noise, it opened a chasm which contained very deep water.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0033":["The Earthquake of the 9th of December.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0034":["According to the testimony of the public reports, Lisbon had suffered no such violent attacks of shaking since the 1st of November as that of the 9th of December. It was perceived on the southern coasts of Spain, on those of France, and through the Swiss mountains, Swabia and the Tyrol as far as Bavaria. It traversed some 300 German miles from south-west to north-east, and, since it kept to the direction of that chain of mountains which runs lengthwise along the highest ridge of the mainland of Europe, it did not spread out much sideways. The most careful geographers, Varenius, Buffon and Lulof, remark that, just as all land which extends more in length than in breadth is traversed in the direction of its length by a principal mountain range, so the chief line of the mountains of Europe extends from one main trunk, namely the Alps, westwards through the southern provinces of France and through the middle of Spain to the farthest shore of Europe towards the west, although on the way it throws out considerable side branches; and likewise eastwards, through the Tyrolese and other less considerable mountains, it finally joins the Carpathians.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0035":["The earthquake traversed this direction on that same day. If the time of the shock at each place had been correctly recorded, one could to some extent estimate its speed and probably determine the region of the first ignition. But the reports agree so little that nothing can be relied upon in this respect.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0036":["I have already stated elsewhere that earthquakes, when they spread, commonly keep to the line of the highest mountains, and this throughout their whole extent, although the mountains become the lower the nearer they approach the sea-coast. The direction of long rivers marks very well the direction of the mountains, since the rivers run on between the parallel ranges as in the lowest part of a long valley. This law of the spread of earthquakes is no matter of speculation or of judgment, but something that has become known through observation of many earthquakes. One must in this regard rely on the testimony of Ray, Buffon, Gentil and so on. But this law has so much inner probability that it must readily win assent of itself. Consider that the openings through which the subterranean fire seeks an outlet are nowhere else than in the summits of mountains; that fire-spouting chasms have never been observed in the plains; that in countries where earthquakes are violent and frequent most of the mountains contain wide craters which serve for the ejection of fire; and that, as regards our European mountains, spacious cavities are discovered nowhere but in them, which are without doubt in connection with one another. If one adds to this the concept of the generation of all these subterranean vaults, of which mention was made above, one will find no difficulty in conceiving how the ignition could find open and free passages chiefly beneath the chain of mountains that runs the length of Europe, so as to spread in them more quickly than towards other regions.",["- 0026: \"Töplitzer Brunn\" rendered \"Töplitz spring\"; \"Fetz\" is Fez (as printed). Long final sentence kept, with \"these vapours\" supplied for \"diese\".","- 0027: first long sentence divided into three; \"beregten\" taken as \"in question\"; \"Druck\" as \"pressure\". \"deicht\" read as \"dicht\".","- 0028: second sentence split; \"Aufthürmung\" \"piling up\"; \"Setuval\" kept as printed (Setúbal).","- 0031: \"Aqvapendente\" given as Acquapendente; \"Pflanzstädte\" as \"colonies\"; \"Yorkshirn\" corrected silently to Yorkshire.","- 0032: \"Glowson\" kept as printed; identification doubtful.","- 0034: \"Varen\" read as Varenius, \"Büffon\" as Buffon; \"Meilen\" as miles. Final long sentence kept as one with semicolon-free structure.","- 0036: long conditional (\"Wenn man bedenkt ... so wird man\") restated as imperative \"Consider that...\" plus \"If one adds\"; \"Raj\" read as Ray. \"Zusammenhange\" as \"connection with one another\" (supplied)."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0037":["Even the propagation of the earthquake of 18 November from Europe to America, beneath the floor of a wide sea, is to be sought in the connection of the chain of mountains. Although in this continuation the chain becomes so low that it is covered by the sea, it nevertheless remains a mountain chain there too; for we know that mountain ranges are to be met with on the floor of the ocean just as well as on land. In this way the Azores, which lie halfway between Portugal and North America, must also be included in this connection.",["- Restated the long first sentence as three; \"die ob sie gleich…\" (the concessive clause) is made a separate sentence with \"the chain\" as antecedent.","- \"Zusammenhange\" rendered \"connection\"; \"Gebirge\" as \"mountain ranges\"."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0038":["The earthquake of 26 December.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0039":["After the heating of the mineral materials had pervaded the main stem of the highest mountains of Europe, namely the Alps, it also opened for itself the narrower passages beneath the ranges that run out from south to north at right angles [to the main stem]. It extended from Switzerland to the North Sea in the direction of the Rhine, which, like rivers generally, occupies a long valley between two ranges of mountains. On the west side of the river it shook the provinces of Alsace, Lorraine, the Electorate of Cologne, Brabant and Picardy; on the east side, Cleves, a part of Westphalia, and presumably some other countries lying on this side of the Rhine, of which the reports have named none. It plainly kept its course parallel with the direction of this great river, and did not spread far from it to either side.",["- Divided into four sentences. \"[to the main stem]\" is supplied to clarify \"rechtwinklicht\".","- \"Erhitzung\" = \"heating\"; \"Entzündung\" elsewhere = \"ignition/kindling\" (see later passages).","- \"Reihe der Berge\" = \"ranges\"; \"an dieser Seite des Rheins\" kept literally as \"on this side of the Rhine\"."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0040":["It will be asked how this is to be reconciled with the foregoing, since it penetrated as far as the Netherlands, which are without any notable mountains. But it is enough that a country stands in immediate connection with certain ranges of mountains and is to be regarded as a continuation of them, for the subterranean ignition to be carried on beneath this otherwise low ground. For it is certain that in that case the chain of cavities will also extend beneath it, just as, as already stated, it continues even beneath the sea bottom.",["- \"mit dem obigen\" taken as the earlier account of the earthquake following the mountain ranges.","- Divided the second sentence; \"gleichwie sie…\" refers to the chain of cavities."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0041":["Of the intervals that elapse between several successive earthquakes.",["- \"binnen\" rendered \"between\", as the sense requires."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0042":["If one considers attentively the sequence of the shocks that followed one another, one might, if one dared to conjecture, work out a period in which the ignition broke out afresh after an interval of quiescence. After the 1st of November we find a further very violent shock in Portugal on the 9th, and likewise on the 18th, when it extended to England, Italy, Africa and even as far as America. On the 27th there was a strong earthquake on the southern coasts of Spain, especially at Malaga. From this time it lasted 13 days until, on 9 December, it struck the whole stretch from Portugal to Bavaria, from south-west to north-east. After this, following an interval of 18 days, namely from the 26th to the 27th of December, it shook the breadth of Europe from south to north *). There is thus, on the whole, a fairly regular course of time of 9 days, or twice 9, between the repeated ignitions, if one leaves out the time that it spent in penetrating to the innermost parts of the mountains of our continent and in moving the Alps and the whole chain of their extension on the 9th of December. I do not adduce this in order to draw any inference from it, since the reports are far too little reliable, but to give occasion, in similar events, for more exact observation and reflection.",["- Divided the sentence beginning \"Von dieser Zeit an…\" into two. The text's \"27ten\" (Nov.) is kept as printed, though the \"13 Tage\" before 9 Dec. does not strictly fit; doubtful in the original.","- \"Periodus\" rendered \"period\"; \"Zeitlauf\" as \"course of time\".","- The asterisk marks the footnote that follows."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0043":["*) On the 21st it was very violent in Lisbon, on the 23rd in the mountains of Roussillon, where it lasted until the 27th. It may be seen from this that it began again from the south-west, and indeed required a far longer time to spread. And if one places the seat of the ignition, as is clear from the whole course of the earthquake, in the ocean off Portugal toward the west, its beginning fits fairly well with the period mentioned.",["- \"gegen Abend\" = \"toward the west\". \"Entzündungsplatz\" = \"seat of the ignition\"."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0044":["Here I wish only to mention something in general about shocks that alternately abate and begin again. Monsieur Bouguer, one of the deputies of the Royal Academy of Sciences at Paris sent to Peru, had the inconvenience in that country of staying beside a volcano, whose thundering noise gave him no rest. The observation he made there might afford him some satisfaction for it, for he noticed that the mountain became quiet at regular intervals, and that its raging followed in orderly succession with alternating points of rest. The observation which Mariotte made with a lime-kiln, which had been fired and which now expelled the air through an open window, now drew it back again, whereby he to some extent imitated the respiration of animals, has great similarity with this; both rest on the following causes. When the subterranean fire falls into ignition, it drives all the air out of the surrounding caverns. Where this air, which is filled with fiery particles, now finds an opening, for example in the throat of a volcano, it rushes out, and the mountain throws out fire. But as soon as the air is driven out of the compass of the hearth of ignition, the ignition abates, for without access of air all fire is extinguished. Then the expelled air, since the cause that drove it out has ceased, returns to its place and wakes the extinguished fire; in this way the eruptions of a volcano alternate regularly with one another at certain intervals. The same is true of subterranean ignitions, even where the expanded air cannot find an exit through the clefts of the mountains. For when the ignition begins at one place in the caverns of the earth, it drives the air violently, over a wide area, into all the passages of the subterranean vaults that are connected with it. At this moment the fire itself is suffocated by the want of air. And as soon as this expanding force of the air relaxes, the air that had been spread through all the caverns returns with great force and fans the extinguished fire into a new earthquake. It is remarkable that Vesuvius, which, when the fermentations in the interior of the earth were really getting under way, had been set in motion and fire by the escape of the air driven through its throat, suddenly abated a short time afterwards, when the earthquake at Lisbon had occurred. For then all the air standing in any connection with these vaults, even that which lies above the summit of Vesuvius, pressed through all the channels to the hearth of ignition, where the diminution of the elastic force of the air allowed it access. What an astonishing object! To picture a chimney that obtains its draught through air-openings lying 200 miles away.",["- Restated the Mariotte sentence by keeping it whole but with \"which\" clauses; \"Respiration\" = \"respiration\". \"Ausspannungskraft\" = \"elastic force\".","- \"Heerd der Entzündung\" = \"hearth of ignition\"; \"Gewalt\" = \"force\".","- \"Vesuvius… durch den Ausgang…\" is awkward in the German; I have smoothed it, keeping the sense that the escaping air had set the volcano in motion.","- \"Meilen\" rendered \"miles\" (German miles, not specified)."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0045":["It is this very same cause that must also produce subterranean storm-winds in the vaults of the earth, whose violence will far exceed everything that we perceive on the surface of the earth, when the position and connection of the caverns are suited to their spreading. The noise that has been perceived underfoot during the progress of an earthquake is presumably to be ascribed to no other cause than this.",["- \"Gewalt\" = \"violence\"; \"Grüfte\" = \"vaults\"."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0046":["This very consideration leads us to suppose, with probability, that not all earthquakes are caused by the kindling taking place directly beneath the ground which is shaken. The fury of these subterranean storms may rather set in motion the vault which lies above them. One will doubt this the less when one bears in mind that air far denser than that found on the surface of the earth, set in motion by causes far more sudden than those which move the latter, and compressed within passages that prevent its expansion, can exert an unheard-of force. It is therefore conjectural that the slight shaking of the ground in the greater part of Europe, at the violent kindling which took place in the earth on the 1st of November, may perhaps be derived from nothing but this subterranean air, violently set in motion. Like a fierce tempest it gently shook the ground which resisted its expansion.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0047":["Of the Seat of the Subterranean Kindling, and of the Places Most Subject to the Most Frequent and Most Dangerous Earthquakes.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0048":["By comparing the times we see that the place of the kindling, in the earthquake of the 1st of November, lay in the bed of the sea. This is confirmed by the Tagus, which swelled even before the shock; by the sulphur which seafarers brought up with the sounding-lead from the shaken bottom; and by the violence of the shocks which they felt. The history of earlier earthquakes also plainly shows that the most fearful convulsions have always occurred on the sea-bed, and next to these in places lying on the sea-shore or not far from it. In proof of the former I cite the raging fury with which the subterranean kindling has often raised new islands from the bottom of the sea. For example, in the year 1720, near the island of St. Michael, one of the Azores, it threw up from a depth of 60 fathoms, by the ejection of matter from the sea-bed, an island one mile long and raised several fathoms above the sea. The island near Santorin in the Mediterranean, which in our century rose from the sea-bed before the eyes of many people, and many other examples which I pass over for the sake of brevity, are incontestable proofs of this.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0049":["How often do mariners not suffer a sea-quake! In some regions, particularly in the neighbourhood of certain islands, the seas are filled with pumice-stones and other kinds of ejected matter from a fire which has broken out through the bed of the ocean. The observation of these frequent convulsions of the sea-bottom is naturally connected with the question why, of all places on the mainland, none are subject to more violent and more frequent earthquakes than those which lie not far from the sea-shore. This latter proposition is indubitably correct. Let us run through the history of earthquakes: we find an infinite number of calamities which have befallen towns or countries lying near the sea-shore, but very few, and those of slight importance, observed in the middle of the mainland. Ancient history already reports dreadful devastations which this evil wrought on the coasts of Asia Minor and of Africa. But neither among these nor among the more recent do we find considerable convulsions in the middle of great countries. Italy, which is a peninsula, most of the islands in all seas, and the part of Peru that lies on the sea-shore suffer the greatest attacks of this evil. And even in our own days all the western and southern coasts of Portugal and Spain have been shaken far more than the interior of the mainland. I offer the following solution of both questions.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0050":["Of all the continuous caverns contained beneath the uppermost crust of the earth, those running beneath the sea-bed must without doubt be the narrowest. For there the continued ground of the mainland has sunk to the greatest depth and must rest on its lowest foundation far lower than the places lying towards the middle of the land. Now it is well known that in narrow caverns an ignited, expanding matter must act with greater violence about it than where it can spread out. Moreover, it is natural to believe the following. Since the subterranean heating cannot be doubted, the seething mineral and combustible materials will very often have come into a state of flux, as the streams of sulphur and the lava often poured forth from the fire-spouting mountains can attest. By reason of the natural slope of the ground they will therefore always have flowed off from the subterranean vaults to the lowest caverns of the sea-bed. Hence, because of the abundant store of combustible matter, more frequent and more violent convulsions must occur here.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0051":["Monsieur Bouguer rightly conjectures that the penetration of sea-water, through the opening of some fissures in the sea-bed, must throw into the most violent ebullition the mineral materials naturally inclined to heating. For we know that nothing can drive the fire of heated minerals to more dreadful fury than the inflow of water. It increases the raging of the fire until its force, spreading in all directions, has barred further access to the water by ejecting all earthy materials and by stopping up the opening.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0052":["In my opinion the exceptional violence with which ground lying on the sea-shore is shaken arises, in part and quite naturally, from the weight with which the sea-water presses upon the adjoining bottom. For everyone readily sees that the force with which the subterranean fire strives to raise this vault, on which so astonishing a load rests, must be greatly held back. Since it finds here no room in front of it for expansion, it must turn its whole force against the ground of the dry land which is most nearly connected with it.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0053":["Of the Direction in Which the Ground Is Shaken by an Earthquake.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0054":["The direction in which an earthquake spreads into wide countries is different from that in which the ground is shaken on which it exerts its force. If the uppermost covering of the hidden cavern, in which the ignited matter expands, has a horizontal direction, the covering must be alternately raised and lowered in a vertical position, since there is nothing that could turn the motion more to one side than to the other. But if the stratum of earth which forms the vault is inclined to one side, the shaking force of the subterranean fire drives it upward also in a direction oblique to the horizon. The direction in which the shaking of the ground must always take place could then be inferred, if the direction in which the stratum of earth slopes, beneath which the fiery cavern lies, were in every case known with certainty. The slope of the uppermost surface of the shaken ground is no sure indication of the oblique position which the vault has throughout its whole thickness. For the earth-layers lying on top can form manifold bends and hills, to which the lowest foundation does not conform at all. Buffon is of the opinion that all the different strata found on the earth have for their base a universal bedrock, which covers from above all the enclosed deep cavities. Some parts of it are commonly laid bare on the summits of high mountains, where rain and storm-winds have washed the loose substance entirely away. This opinion gains much probability from what earthquakes make known. For so raging a force as earthquakes exert would long since have shattered and ground to pieces any vault other than a rocky one, through its oft-renewed attacks.\n\n---\nnotes\n- 0046: Restated the long second sentence as three; \"Eben dieses\" is taken to refer to the preceding argument (not in the passage). \"Entzündung\" rendered \"kindling\" throughout.\n- 0046: \"dieser gewaltsamer Weise bewegten\" read as \"violently set in motion\"; \"das Gewölbe\" = the vault above the cavities.\n- 0048: \"Klafter\" = fathom; \"Santorino\" given as \"Santorin\". \"Meile\" kept as \"mile\".\n- 0049: \"desfesten Landes\" is an OCR slip, read as \"des festen Landes\"; \"woher unter allen Oertern … keine\" is a loosely constructed question, rendered as \"why … none are subject to more …\".\n- 0050: The tangled sentence beginning \"Ueberdem\" was divided and its construction smoothed; \"Hölen\" = caverns, \"Grüfte\" = vaults.\n- 0051: \"Hr. Bouguer\" rendered \"Monsieur Bouguer\" (the French savant).\n- 0054: \"Büffon\" modernised to \"Buffon\"; \"ofters\" read \"oft-renewed\". The \"er\" in \"so muß er wechselweise\" has no clear antecedent and is resolved as the covering (Decke).",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0055":["The slope of this arch at the sea-shore is without doubt inclined towards the sea, and so descends in the direction in which the sea lies from the place. On the bank of a great river it must descend in the direction in which the stream flows. For consider the very long stretches, often exceeding several hundred miles, which rivers traverse on the mainland without forming standing pools or lakes on the way. This uniform slope can be explained only by a supremely firm foundation which, since it inclines uniformly towards the sea-bed without manifold depressions, provides the river with an inclined plane for its course. Hence it is to be presumed that the swaying of the ground in a city which has been shaken and which lies on a great river will take place in the direction of that river (in the Tagus, for example, from west to east) *); and that in a city on the sea-coast it will take place in the direction in which the land inclines towards the sea. In another place I have set out how the lie of the ground can contribute to the complete destruction, in an earthquake, of a city whose main streets run in the same direction as that in which the ground descends. This remark is not a mere conjecture; it is a matter of experience. Gentil, who himself had occasion to gather good information about very many earthquakes, reports it as an observation confirmed by many examples: when the direction in which the ground is shaken runs parallel to the direction in which the city is built, the city is utterly overthrown, whereas, when the shaking crosses it at right angles, less damage is done.",["- Restated the long \"denn wenn … so kan man\" sentence as several sentences, turning the subordinate \"wenn … so\" into an appeal to the reader to consider the facts.","- \"von Abend und Morgen\" rendered \"from west to east\" (literally evening and morning); the sense is the east–west axis, so the order is not significant.","- \"Einfall der bloßen Vermuthung\" rendered \"mere conjecture\"; \"Einfall\" (notion, idea) is absorbed.","- The asterisk marks the footnote, which follows as a separate passage (0056)."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0056":["*) Just as a river has a downward inclination towards the sea, so the lands at its sides slope towards its bed. If this latter holds of the whole stratum of the earth, and if the stratum possesses the same descent at its greatest depth, then the direction of the earthquake will also be determined by this.",["- \"Schiefe\" / \"Abschießigkeit\" both rendered by inclination or descent; \"Erdschichte\" = \"stratum of the earth\".","- \"dieses letztere\" taken to refer to the sloping of the lands towards the river bed."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0057":["The *Histoire* of the Royal Academy at Paris reports that when Smyrna, which lies on the eastern shore of the Mediterranean, was shaken in the year 1688, all the walls that ran from east to west were overthrown, while those built from north to south remained standing.",["- \"Historie\" left as *Histoire* since it refers to the Academy's published volume, not to history in general; \"Mauren\" = walls."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0058":["The shaken ground, that is to say, makes several oscillations, and moves most strongly everything erected upon it lengthwise in the direction of the oscillation. All bodies with great mobility, for example the chandeliers in churches, are accustomed in earthquakes to indicate the direction in which the shocks occur. They are far more certain indications, for a city seeking to infer from them the position in which it ought to be built, than the somewhat more doubtful signs already mentioned.",["- Divided the final sentence into two. \"Schwankungen\" rendered \"oscillations\" here, \"swaying\" earlier; the sense is the same.","- \"um die Lage daraus abzunehmen, nach welcher sie sich anbauen muß\" is awkward German; I take it to mean the orientation in which the city should be laid out."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0059":["On the Connection of Earthquakes\nwith the Seasons.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0060":["M. Bouguer, the French academician already cited several times, states in his voyage to Peru that although earthquakes in that country occur often enough in all seasons, the most terrible and the most frequent are nevertheless felt in the autumn months, towards the end of the year. This observation finds numerous confirmations not only in America (where, besides the destruction of the city of Lima ten years ago and the sinking of another, equally populous, in the last century, very many instances of it have been noted), but also in our own quarter of the world. Here too, besides the most recent earthquake, we find in history many examples of shocks and eruptions of volcanoes which have occurred more frequently in the autumn months than in any other season. Should not a common cause occasion this agreement? And on what can we more suitably fix our conjecture than on the rains, which in Peru last from September to April in the long valley between the Cordilleras, and which with us also are most frequent about the autumn? We know that nothing more is needed to start a subterranean fire than to bring the mineral materials in the caverns of the earth into fermentation. Water does this when it has filtered through the fissures of the mountains and spread itself in the deep passages. The rains first excited the fermentation which in the middle of October forced so many foreign vapours out of the interior of the earth. But these in turn drew still more moist influences from the atmosphere, and the water which penetrated through the cracks in the rocks into the deepest vaults completed the heating that had begun.",["- Divided the long second sentence into two; \"indem …\" clause set in parentheses to keep the construction clear.","- \"Hr.\" expanded to \"M.\" for a French academician; \"Welttheil\" = \"quarter of the world\" (continent).","- \"Gährung\" = fermentation; \"Brand\" = fire; \"Dämpfe\" = vapours.","- The last two sentences refer to a particular occasion (apparently the recent earthquake, evidently Lisbon) without naming it; the context is not in this passage."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0061":["On the Influence of Earthquakes upon the Atmosphere.",["- \"Luftkreis\" rendered \"atmosphere\" (Kant uses \"Atmosphäre\" in the next passage)."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0062":["We have seen above an instance of the effects which earth-tremors have upon our air. It is to be believed that more natural phenomena depend upon the eruptions of subterranean heated vapours than is commonly imagined. It would hardly be possible to meet with such irregularity, and so little uniformity, in the weather, if foreign causes did not at times enter our atmosphere and throw its regular changes into disorder. Can one conceive any probable ground why, since the course of the sun and moon is bound to laws always like themselves, and since water and earth, taken on the large scale, always remain the same, the course of the weather nevertheless turns out almost always different, even when taken as an average of many years? Since the unhappy earthquake, and shortly before it, we have had throughout our whole quarter of the world such abnormal weather that one may be excused for casting some suspicion upon earthquakes on this account. It is true that warm winter weather has been experienced before without any earthquake having preceded it. But can one be sure that a fermentation in the interior of the earth has not very often driven vapours through the rock fissures, the cracks in the strata, and even through their loose substance, which could have drawn after them notable changes in the atmosphere? Musschenbroek, having observed that quite bright northern lights have been seen in Europe, even in its southern countries, only in this century, and indeed since 1716, holds the most probable cause of this change in the atmosphere to be this: that the volcanoes and earthquakes, which had raged frequently some years before, had expelled inflammable and volatile vapours; that these, by the natural outflow of the upper air towards the north, were accumulated there, and produced the fiery appearances in the air which have since been so frequently seen; and that they must presumably be consumed little by little, until fresh exhalations again make good the loss.",["- \"Erderschütterungen\" rendered \"earth-tremors\" to vary from \"Erdbeben\" (\"earthquakes\"); the German also varies.","- Restated the \"Kan man sich wohl … gedenken, warum, da … da …\" question as one question, keeping the two \"since\" clauses.","- Divided nothing in the Musschenbroek sentence, but I added semicolons and \"that\" to mark the three dependent clauses; the German chain of \"daß\" clauses is loose.","- \"Nordlichter\" = northern lights (aurora borealis); \"Aushauchungen\" = exhalations.","- \"Auszug vieler Jahre\" taken as an average or summary of many years."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0063":["Let us now inquire, in accordance with these principles, whether it is not conformable to nature that an altered state of the weather, such as we have had, should be a consequence of that catastrophe. The clear winter weather, and the cold which accompanies it, is not merely a consequence of the sun's greater distance from our zenith at this season of the year; for we often experience that the air may nevertheless be very mild. It is rather the current of air from the north, which at times turns into an east wind, that brings us a chilled air all the way from the frigid zone. This air covers our waters with ice and lets us feel a part of the winter of the north pole. In the autumn and winter months this current of air from north to south is so natural, unless foreign causes interrupt it, that on the ocean, at a sufficient distance from all solid land, this north or north-east wind is met with without interruption throughout the whole time. It also springs quite naturally from the action of the sun, which at that season rarefies the air over the southern hemisphere and so causes the air of the northern to be drawn towards it. This must therefore be regarded as a constant law, which may indeed be somewhat modified by the character of the lands, but cannot be annulled.\n\nNow if subterranean fermentations discharge heated vapours anywhere in the countries lying to the south of us, these will at first lower the height of the atmosphere in the region where they ascend, by weakening its elastic force, and will cause downpours of rain, hurricanes and the like. In the sequel, however, this part of the atmosphere, being laden with so many vapours, will by its weight set the neighbouring part in motion and cause a current of air from south to north. But since the tendency of the atmosphere from north to south is natural in our latitude at this season, these two movements, opposing one another, will check each other. They will bring in their train, first, a cloudy and rainy air, because of the vapours driven together, and yet, along with this, a high reading of the barometer *), since the air compressed by the conflict of two winds must form a high column. In this way we shall learn to understand the apparent unreliability of barometers, when the weather is rainy although the barometer stands high. For in that case this very moisture of the air is an effect of two opposing currents of air, which can drive the vapours together and yet at the same time considerably condense the air and make it heavier.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0064":["*) Something of the kind has been observed almost constantly in this wet winter weather.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0065":["I cannot pass over in silence that on the terrible day of All Saints the magnets at Augsburg dropped their loads and the magnetic needles were thrown into disorder. Boyle already reports that the same thing once occurred at Naples after an earthquake. We know the hidden nature of the magnet too little to be able to give the ground of this phenomenon [*Erscheinung*].",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0066":["On the Utility of Earthquakes.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0067":["People will be shocked to see so fearful a scourge of mankind commended from the side of its usefulness. I am certain that we would gladly renounce it, in order only to be relieved of the fear and the dangers bound up with it. That is how we human beings are constituted. Having laid an unlawful claim to every comfort of life, we wish to purchase no advantage at any cost. We demand that the earth be so constituted that one could wish to dwell on it for ever. Beyond this, we imagine that we should govern everything better to our advantage, if Providence had asked for our vote in the matter. Thus we wish, for instance, to have the rain in our power, so that we might distribute it through the year as suits our convenience, and always have pleasant days to enjoy between the dull ones. But we forget the springs, which we nevertheless could not do without, and which in such a fashion would not be maintained at all. In the same way we do not know the utility which the very causes that terrify us in earthquakes might procure for us, and yet we would gladly see them banished.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0068":["As men born to die, we cannot bear that some have died in the earthquake; and as strangers here, possessing no property, we are inconsolable that goods have been lost which in a short time would have been left behind of themselves, by the common way of nature.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0069":["It is easy to guess that when men build upon a ground filled with inflammable materials, the whole splendour of their buildings may sooner or later be overthrown by shocks. But must one therefore become impatient with the ways of Providence? Would it not be better to judge thus: it was necessary that earthquakes should occur from time to time on the earth, but it was not necessary that we should build splendid dwelling-places upon it. The inhabitants of Peru live in houses walled only to a slight height, the remainder consisting of reeds. Man must learn to accommodate himself to nature, but he wants nature to accommodate itself to him.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0070":["Whatever harm the cause of earthquakes may ever have done to men on the one side, it can easily make good to them with profit on the other. We know that the warm baths, which in the course of time may perhaps have served a considerable part of mankind in promoting health, owe their mineral properties and heat to the very same causes, namely the heatings in the interior of the earth which set the latter in motion.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0071":["It has long been conjectured that the ore-veins in the mountains are a slow effect of subterranean heat, which brings the metals to maturity by gradual action, in that it forms and cooks them in the midst of the rock by means of penetrating vapours.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0072":["Besides the coarse and dead materials which it contains, our atmosphere also requires a certain active principle [*Principium*], volatile salts and particles which are to enter into the composition of plants, in order to set them in motion and develop them. Is it not credible that the formations of nature, which constantly consume a great part of this principle, and the change which all matter in the end undergoes through dissolution and composition, would in time wholly consume the most active particles, if a fresh influx did not take place from time to time? At the least the soil becomes ever less fertile when it nourishes vigorous plants, but rest and rain restore it to condition. But whence, finally, would the vigorous matter come, which is consumed without replacement, if another source did not maintain its influx? And this source is presumably the store of the most active and volatile materials which the subterranean vaults contain, and of which they spread a part over the surface of the earth from time to time. I note further that Hales has, with very happy success, freed prisons, and in general all places whose air is infected with animal exhalations, by fumigating them with sulphur. The fire-spewing mountains discharge an immeasurable quantity of sulphurous vapours into the atmosphere. Who knows whether the animal exhalations with which the atmosphere is laden would not in time become harmful, if the former did not furnish a powerful antidote against them?",["- 0063: the long sentences are divided into several; \"ob es nicht der Natur gemäß sey\" rendered \"conformable to nature\"; \"Zug der Luft\" = \"current of air\".","- 0063: \"sondern der Zug…\" is resolved as \"It is rather the current…\", with a new sentence for the relative clause. The pronoun \"dieses\" (\"This must… be regarded as a constant law\") refers to the whole tendency of the air from north to south.","- 0063: \"in die scheinbare Unrichtigkeit … finden lernen\" is smoothed to \"learn to understand the apparent unreliability\"; the German literally says \"incorrectness\".","- 0063: \"Ausspannungskraft\" = \"elastic force\", the usual English term; \"Orkane u. d. g.\" = \"hurricanes and the like\".","- 0065: \"Erscheinung\" = phenomenon here, as an ordinary scientific term, not Kant's technical \"appearance\"; the German is given in brackets.","- 0067: \"Strafruthe\" = \"scourge\" (literally \"rod of punishment\"); \"Vorsehung\" = Providence.","- 0072: \"Principium\" is left in Latin in the original and is shown in brackets; \"Räuchren des Schwefels\" = \"fumigating with sulphur\".","- OCR/spelling: no obvious OCR slips corrected beyond old orthography; footnote marker *) retained in 0063 and 0064."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0073":["Finally, the warmth in the interior of the earth seems to me to furnish a powerful proof of the efficacy and great utility of the heatings that go on in deep caverns. It is established by daily experience that in great depths, indeed in the greatest to which men have penetrated in the interior of mountains, there is a constant warmth, which cannot possibly be ascribed to the action of the sun. Boyle cites a good number of testimonies from which it is clear that in all the deepest shafts one finds the upper region at first far colder than the outer air in summer-time, but that the lower one descends, the warmer one finds the region, so that in the greatest depth the workmen are obliged to lay aside their clothes while at work. Everyone readily grasps that, since the sun's warmth penetrates into the earth only to a very slight depth, it can no longer have the least effect in the lowest caverns. That the warmth found there depends upon a cause which prevails only in the greatest depth can, moreover, be seen from the diminishing warmth the higher one comes up from below, even in summer-time. Boyle, having carefully compared and tested the experiences collected, very reasonably concludes that in the lowest caves, to which we cannot penetrate, there must be constant heatings, and an inextinguishable fire maintained by them, which communicates its warmth to the uppermost crust.",["- Restated the long Boyle sentence slightly; \"Erfahrungen\" rendered \"experience(s)\", not *Erkenntnis*-vocabulary.","- \"Schachten\" (for *Schächte*) = shafts; \"Hölen\" = caves."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0074":["If this is so, as one cannot refrain from admitting, shall we not have to expect the most advantageous effects from this subterranean fire, which at all times keeps the earth moderately warm at the time when the sun withdraws its own warmth from us, and which is in a position to promote the growth of plants and the economy of the kingdoms of nature? And, in view of the appearance of so much usefulness, can the harm that accrues to the human race from one or another of its eruptions release us from the gratitude which we owe to Providence for all its arrangements?",["- The second sentence ends with a full stop in the German but is clearly a rhetorical question; I have punctuated it as a question. \"derselben\" (twice) is referred to the fire and to Providence respectively, by judgment.","- \"Naturreiche\" = kingdoms of nature; \"Anstalten\" = arrangements."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0075":["The grounds which I have adduced to encourage this gratitude are, to be sure, not of the kind that afford the greatest conviction and certainty. But even conjectures are worthy of acceptance when the point is to move men to gratitude toward the Supreme Being, who is worthy of reverence and love even when he chastises.",["- \"derselben\" in the first sentence is taken to refer to the gratitude (\"Dankbarkeit\") of the preceding passage; the German is loose, and the sense is supplied by judgment.","- \"Dankbegierde\" = eagerness for gratitude, rendered simply \"gratitude\"."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0076":["Remark.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0077":["I stated above that earthquakes drive sulphurous exhalations up through the vault of the earth. The latest reports from the shafts in the Saxon mountains confirm this by a new example. They are now found so filled with sulphurous vapours that the workmen have to abandon them. The occurrence at Tuam in Ireland is a fresh confirmation of the same. A luminous appearance in the air, in the shape of pennants and flags, showed itself over the sea; its colours changed little by little, and at last it spread a bright light, whereupon a violent shock of earthquake followed. The change of colours from the darkest blue to red, and finally to a bright white glow, is to be ascribed to the exhalation that broke forth, at first very thin and then gradually increased by the more abundant inflow of further vapours. As is known in natural science, such an exhalation must pass through the degrees of light from the blue colour to the red, and finally to a white glow. All this preceded the shock. It was also a proof that the seat of the ignition had been at the bottom of the sea, as indeed the earthquake itself was chiefly felt on the sea-coast.",["- Divided the Tuam sentence into two, as the German packs a long relative construction before its main clause.","- \"Lufterscheinung\" = appearance in the air (meteor); \"Heerd\" = seat (hearth)."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0078":["If one wishes to extend further the observations on the places of the earth where the most frequent and severest shocks have always been felt, one may add that the western coasts have at all times suffered far more attacks of them than the eastern. In Italy, in Portugal, in South America, and even lately in Ireland, experience has confirmed this agreement. Peru, which lies on the western shore of the New World, has almost daily shocks, while Brazil, which faces the ocean on the east, has experienced none. If one wishes to conjecture some causes for this strange analogy, one may well pardon a Gautier, a painter, when he seeks the cause of all earthquakes in the rays of the sun, the source of his colours and of his art, and imagines that these same rays drive our great globe round from west to east, since they strike the western coasts more strongly, and that for this very reason these coasts are disturbed by so many shocks. In a sound natural science, however, such a notion hardly deserves refutation. The ground of this law seems to me connected with another, for which no adequate explanation has yet been given: namely, that the western and southern coasts of almost all countries slope more steeply than the eastern and northern. This is confirmed both by a glance at the map and by the reports of Dampier, who found it almost universally on all his voyages. If one derives the curvatures of the solid land from subsidences, then in the regions of greatest steepness there must be found deeper and more numerous caves than where the earth's crust has only a moderate slope. But this, as we saw above, has a natural connection with earthquakes.",["- Gautier (Gautier d'Agoty) as printed; \"von Abend gegen Morgen\" = from west to east. The German is ambiguous about what \"dieselbe\" strike; I take it as the sun's rays.","- \"Beugungen\" = curvatures; \"Einsinkungen\" = subsidences. \"Abschüßig\" = sloping."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0079":["Concluding Reflection.",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0080":["The sight of so many wretched people, such as the latest catastrophe has made among our fellow-citizens, should stir human sympathy and let us feel a part of the misfortune which has fallen on them so harshly. But one offends greatly against this if one always regards such fates as penal judgments decreed upon the devastated cities on account of their misdeeds, and if we look upon these unfortunates as the target of God's vengeance, upon whom his justice pours out all its bowls of wrath. This kind of judgment is a culpable presumption, which assumes to discern the purposes of the divine counsels and to interpret them according to its own insights.",["- \"Menschenliebe\" = human sympathy (benevolence); \"Vorwitz\" = presumption (officious curiosity)."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0081":["Man is so taken with himself that he regards himself simply as the sole end of God's arrangements, as if these had no other aim than himself, and the measures in the government of the world were to be framed accordingly. We know that the whole sum of nature is a worthy object of divine wisdom and of its arrangements. We are a part of it, and yet would be the whole. The rules of the perfection of nature on the large scale are to come into no consideration, and everything is to dispose itself solely in right relation to us. Whatever in the world conduces to our comfort and pleasure, that, we imagine, exists solely for our sake; and nature, we suppose, undertakes no changes which become in any way a cause of discomfort to men, except in order to chastise them, to threaten them, or to exercise vengeance upon them.",["- \"Inbegriff\" = sum (sum-total). \"Ungemächlichkeit\" = discomfort. \"unsert willen\" read as \"unsertwillen\"."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0082":["Yet we see that an infinite number of villains fall asleep in peace; that earthquakes have shaken certain countries from time immemorial, without distinction of old or new inhabitants; that Christian Peru is shaken as much as the heathen, and that many cities have remained free of this devastation from the beginning, though they can claim for themselves no superiority in blamelessness over those others.",["- \"Anbeginn\" = from the beginning; \"Unsträflichkeit\" = blamelessness. \"Bösewichter\" = villains."]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0083":["Thus man is in the dark when he tries to guess the purposes which God has in view in governing the world. But we are in no uncertainty when the question is one of application, that is, of how we ought to use these ways of Providence in a manner conformable to their end. Man is not born to build eternal huts upon this stage of vanity. His whole life has a far nobler goal. How beautifully, then, do all the devastations accord with it, which the instability of the world reveals even in those things that seem to us the greatest and most important, in order to remind us that the goods of the earth can afford no satisfaction to our impulse towards happiness!",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0084":["I am far from intimating by this that man has been abandoned to an unalterable fate of the laws of nature, without regard to his particular advantages. The very same supreme wisdom from which the course of nature derives that correctness which needs no amendment has subordinated the lower ends to the higher. And with the very same purposes for which it has often made the most important exceptions to the universal rules of nature, in order to attain infinitely higher ends that are exalted far above all natural means, the guidance of the human race in the government of the world will also prescribe laws to the course of natural things themselves. When a city or a country becomes aware of the calamity with which divine Providence strikes terror into it or into its neighbours, is it then still doubtful which course it should take to forestall the ruin that threatens? Are the signs still ambiguous for making comprehensible the purposes to whose fulfilment all the ways of Providence unanimously either invite or urge mankind?",[]],"erdbeben-geschichte-0085":["A prince who, moved by a noble heart, allows these afflictions of the human race to stir him to turn the misery of war away from those who are in any case threatened on every side by grave misfortunes, is a beneficent instrument in the kindly hand of God, and a gift which He bestows upon the peoples of the earth, whose worth they can never estimate according to its greatness.",[]],"erdbeben-fortgesetzt-0001":["M. Immanuel Kant's\nContinued Consideration\nof the Earthquakes Perceived for Some Time Past.\nIn: *Wochentliche Königsbergische Frag- und Anzeigungs-Nachrichten*. Saturday, 10 April, Anno 1756. No. 15.\nConclusion in: Saturday, 17 April, Anno 1756. No. 16.",[]],"erdbeben-fortgesetzt-0002":["10 April 1756.",[]],"erdbeben-fortgesetzt-0003":["The fire of the subterranean vaults is not yet quieted. The tremors continued only recently, and terrified countries to which this evil had always been unknown. The disorder in the atmosphere has altered the season over half of the globe. The most ignorant have claimed to have guessed the cause. One hears some say, without understanding or reflection, that the earth has shifted from its place and has come nearer to the sun, I know not by how many degrees. This is a judgment that would be worthy of a nursery-man, if he were to rise again to sell the dreams of a deranged head as observations. To the same class belong those who bring the comets back upon the scene, after Whiston himself taught the philosophers to fear them. It is a common extravagance to fetch the origin of an evil from some thousands of miles away, when it can be found near at hand. So the Turks do with the plague; so people did with the locusts, with the cattle-plague, and God knows with what other evils besides. One is ashamed merely to be able to see something close by. To descry causes at an infinite distance, that is the real proof of a penetrating understanding.",[]],"erdbeben-fortgesetzt-0004":["Among all the conjectures which, in the case of a great departure from the rules of sound natural science, can easily captivate some who do not know how to test them, belongs the thought which the public reports ascribe to Professor Profe of Altona. It is indeed long since suspicion for great occurrences on the earth has been cast upon the planets. The registers of the harsh accusations which our dear forefathers, the gentlemen astrologers, brought against these stars are consigned to the archive of antiquated chimeras, beside the veracious history of the fairies, the sympathetic wonders of Digby and Vallemont, and the nocturnal doings on the Blocksberg. But since natural science has been cleansed of these whims, a Newton has discovered a real force, and confirmed it by experience, which even the most distant planets exercise upon one another and upon our earth. To the misfortune, however, of those who would push the application of this remarkable property to extravagance, the measure of this force and the manner of its action are determined, and indeed by the very same observation, aided by geometry, to which we owe its revelation. One can no longer persuade us of whatever one pleases about its effects. We have the balance in hand, on which we can weigh the effects against the alleged cause.",[]],"erdbeben-fortgesetzt-0005":["Suppose a man who has once let it be told to him that the moon draws the waters of the earth and thereby causes the swelling and sinking of the ocean which are called ebb and flood; and likewise that all the planets are endowed with a similar force of attraction, and, when they are near the straight line drawn through the earth and sun, unite their attractive forces with that of the moon. Suppose, I say, that such a man, who has no calling to examine the matter more closely, conjectured that these united forces could not only bring the waters of the earth into that violent motion which we saw on 1 November, but could also, by an influence upon the subterranean air, arouse the hidden tinder to produce earthquakes. Then nothing further can be demanded of him. But of a student of nature one expects more. It is not enough to have hit upon a cause that has something similar to the effect; it must also be proportioned to it in respect of magnitude. I shall give an example. Doctor Lister, an otherwise able member of the London Society, had observed that the sea-plant called duckweed [*Meerlinse*] has an uncommonly strong exhalation. He noted that it is found abundantly on the coasts of the seas of the torrid zone. Since now a strong exhalation can easily set the air somewhat in motion, he concluded that the general east wind which blows constantly in these seas and extends more than 1000 miles from land, derives from it, chiefly because this plant turns towards the sun. The ludicrousness of this opinion lies solely in this: that the cause has no proportion at all to the effect. It is just so with the force of the planets, if one compares it with the effect which is supposed to come from it, namely the motion of the seas and the excitation of earthquakes. Perhaps it will be said: do we then know the magnitude of the force with which these heavenly bodies can act upon the earth? I shall answer this presently.\n\n---notes",[]],"erdbeben-fortgesetzt-0006":["Monsieur Bouguer, a celebrated French academician, relates that during his stay in Peru a scholar who wished to become Professor of Mathematics at the University of Lima wrote a book entitled an astronomical clock of earthquakes, in which he undertakes to foretell these from the course of the moon. One may readily guess that a prophet in Peru has an easy time foretelling earthquakes, since they occur there almost daily and are distinguished only by their strength. Bouguer adds that a man who, without reflection, throws about the ascending and descending nodes of the moon, perigee and apogee, conjunction and opposition, may well by chance now and then say something which is confirmed by the event; and he confesses that he himself has not always been unlucky in his predictions. He even conjectures that it is not altogether improbable that the moon, which moves the waters of the ocean so powerfully, may have some influence upon the shaking of the earth. This it might do in one of two ways. It may carry the water, which it raises to an extraordinary height, into certain fissures in the earth that it would not otherwise have reached, and this would cause the raging motion in the deep caverns; or it may act through some other kind of connection.",[]],"erdbeben-fortgesetzt-0007":["If we consider that the attractive forces of the heavenly bodies act into the innermost parts of matter, and can therefore set in motion the air enclosed in the deepest and most hidden passages of the earth, it will hardly be possible to deny the moon all influence upon earthquakes. But this force would at most only stimulate the inflammable materials found within the earth; the remainder, the shaking and the motion of the waters, will be solely an effect of these latter.",[]],"erdbeben-fortgesetzt-0008":["If we ascend from the moon farther into the heaven of the planets, this power gradually vanishes as their distances increase. The forces of all the planets, united together, amount, when compared with the force of the single moon, which is so near to us, to only an infinitely small part of it.",[]],"erdbeben-fortgesetzt-0009":["Newton, who discovered the excellent law of attraction, which must be regarded as the most fortunate attempt that human understanding has yet made in the knowledge of nature, teaches how to find the attractive forces of those planets that have moons about them. He determines that of Jupiter, the greatest of all the planets, to be somewhat less than the thousandth part of the attractive force of the sun. The power of producing alterations on our earth by this force decreases inversely as the cube of the distance. In the case of Jupiter, which is more than five times farther from the earth than the sun is, and taking into account the proportion of its attractive force to that of the sun, it is accordingly 130,000 times smaller than what the sun's attraction alone can effect upon our earth. Now the sun's attraction can raise the water of the ocean about two feet, as experience in agreement with calculation has made known. The attraction of Jupiter, when united with that of the sun, will therefore add to this height only the 65th part of a decimal scruple, which would amount to about the 30th part of a hair's breadth. If we consider that Mars and Venus are bodies incomparably smaller than Jupiter, and that their attractive forces are proportioned to their mass, we are still allowing too much if we ascribe to the two together about twice as much power of acting by attraction upon our earth as to Jupiter. For they are about three times nearer to us than he, although they possess many hundred times less bodily content, and consequently less attractive force, than he. But even if I were generous enough to make their force tenfold greater, they still could not, when they unite it, cause the water of the sea to swell by a third of a hair's thickness. If we add the remaining planets, Mercury and Saturn, and consider them all in conjunction, it is evident that they could still by no means increase by half a hair's breadth the swelling of the waters which the moon and the sun produce jointly. Is it not, then, ridiculous to fear terrible motions of the waters from the attraction of the moon and the sun, when the height to which they raise the water has been increased by half a hair's breadth, whereas without this no danger would be feared? All the other circumstances entirely refute the alleged cause. The moon produces the highest tide not only at the very moment when it comes nearest to the straight line drawn through the sun and the earth, but also for some days before and after. If the united planets had had any share in the matter, they too should have produced motions of the waters and earthquakes for several days in succession, and within these days for several hours.",[]],"erdbeben-fortgesetzt-0010":["I must beg my readers' pardon for having led them so far about the firmament, in order that they may judge rightly of the events which have taken place upon our earth. The pains we take to stop up the sources of error also procure for us a purified knowledge. In the following part I shall consider the most remarkable appearances of the great natural event which have occurred since those events which I endeavoured to explain in a separate treatise.",[]],"erdbeben-fortgesetzt-0011":["Before the judgment-seat of reason the planets have been acquitted of the charge of having had any share in the cause of the devastation which we suffer in earthquakes. Henceforth no one should any longer hold them under suspicion on this account. Indeed, several planets have more than once been in conjunction without any earthquake being felt. According to the testimony of Gassendi, Peiresc saw in the year 1604 the rare conjunction of the three upper planets, which occurs only once in 800 years, but the earth remained secure. If the moon, upon which alone the conjecture could still fall with some probability, had a share in it, the co-operating causes would have to be present in such full measure that even the weakest external influence could decide the issue of the change. For the moon often comes into the position in which it exerts its greatest action upon the earth's surface, but it does not as often excite earthquakes. The earthquake of the 1st of November occurred soon after the last quarter; but its influences are then the weakest, as the Newtonian theory and experience show. Let us therefore ask after the cause only in our own dwelling-place; we have the cause beneath our feet.",["- 0006: Restated the long last sentence as three, making \"entweder … oder\" explicit; \"Akademist\" rendered \"academician\".","- 0006: \"Hr.\" expanded as \"Monsieur\" in the first sentence, \"Bouguer\" alone thereafter, for the sake of idiom.","- 0007: \"dieser letzteren\" taken to refer to the inflammable materials (*entzündbare Materien*), not to the force.","- 0008: Split the second sentence for clarity; \"Vermögen\" rendered \"power\".","- 0009: \"Würfel der Entfernungen\" = cube of the distances; the text is Kant's own, and the figures (130,000; 65th; 30th) are reproduced as printed, not checked. \"Mercur und Saturn\" as in the text. \"geraten\" read as OCR for *geraden* (\"straight\").","- 0009: \"gringer\" read as *geringer*; \"Decimalscrupel\" kept as \"decimal scruple\". The sentence about danger (\"Ist es nun nicht lächerlich …\") is slightly awkward in the German; its sense is kept.","- 0010: \"Stück\" rendered \"part\" (the periodical issue); \"Erkenntniß\" = knowledge.","- 0011: \"Peirescius\" and \"Gassendus\" given in modern forms Peiresc and Gassendi. \"Es sind wohl eher einige Planeten in Verbindung gewesen\" rendered freely as \"more than once been in conjunction\"."]],"erdbeben-fortgesetzt-0012":["(see above)",["Please disregard the stray lines above, which were an error in formatting: the translations stand as given, each id once, but the separator for notes should be \"--- notes\". Notes follow here for all passages.","- 0012: \"mehresten\" rendered \"most\"; \"Schaukelung\" as \"rocking\"; the long final sentence is divided into three sentences.","- 0013: the Poll sentence is restated as several sentences; the Lemery parenthesis is moved out and set as its own sentence. \"Wenn wir … betrachten\" is also restated as a separate exhortation (\"Let us consider\"), joining the broken construction (anacoluthon) smoothly. \"Nachdem sie … ausgeschmolzen\" is ambiguous in reference; I take \"sie\" to be the causes of heating.","- 0013: \"Dr. Poll\" is Johann Heinrich Pott's contemporary or similar; I give the German form as printed. \"Lemerischen\" = Nicolas Lemery.","- 0014: \"Hollmanns\" is rendered \"Hollmann\"; \"Prometheus der neuern Zeiten\" is Franklin. The sentence on Hollmann is divided.","- 0001: lowercase Latin title kept in sense, not in the capitalisation of the original; \"x.\" read as 10th April; \"cultor\" rendered \"student\". Place names regularised (Regiomonti = Königsberg; \"kyrizensi marchico\" = of Kyritz in the Mark).","- 0002–0006: Dedication titles rendered literally; \"viro … heroi suo\" read as one dative address; 0003–0005 are connected fragments of one dedicatory sentence, translated as they stand."]],"erdbeben-fortgesetzt-0013":["Here and there the ground has subsided in places. Pieces of rock have broken away from the mountains and rolled into the valleys with terrible force. These events often occur without any preceding earthquake. Continuous rains fill the veins of springs with water, and these frequently undermine the foundation of a piece of land by washing the soil away; in the same way, especially when frost and wet combine their effects, they tear pieces of rock from the summits of the mountains. The great clefts and fissures which have opened here and there in Switzerland and elsewhere, and have for the most part closed again, are plainer proofs of an expanding subterranean force, by which the strata of somewhat lesser density have burst asunder.\n\nLet us consider the fragility of our ground, and the store of subterranean glow, which perhaps everywhere maintains in a ceaselessly blazing fire the inflammable materials, the beds of coal, the resins and the sulphur (just as coal mines, when they have kindled of themselves on exposure to the air, often glow for centuries and spread around them). When, I say, we consider this condition of the subterranean caverns, does there not seem to be a hint sufficient to sink our vaults into whole seas of glowing sulphur, and to lay waste our inhabited places with streams of burning matter, just as the poured-out lava devastated the villages that had been built, in untroubled calm, beside the foot of Etna?\n\nHerr Dr. Poll is right when, in a short treatise on earthquakes, he requires nothing further than water, in order to set the ever-smouldering glow beneath the earth in motion by means of expanded water-vapours, and so to bring the earth into commotion. But he would deprive Lemery's experiment of its suitability. (That experiment, by a mixture of sulphur and iron filings with the addition of water, made the shocks comprehensible.) His ground is that in the earth no native iron is found, but only iron-earth, which does not yield in this experiment what is wanted. To this I give it to be considered, first, whether the manifold causes of heating could not supply sufficient matter for carrying out this experiment on the great scale. Such causes are the weathering of sulphur pyrites, and the fermentations through the access of water, as is observed after rain on poured-out lava and likewise in the perpetual earth-fire of Pietra Mala, once they have smelted the deep-lying iron-earth into granulated iron; and there is also the loadstone, which comes so close to the nature of native iron and which is without doubt found abundantly everywhere in the depths. The very curious observation reported from Switzerland, that during the earthquake a magnet deviated by some degrees from its vertical position, together with the thread on which it was suspended, seems to confirm the co-operation of magnetic matters in earthquakes.",[]],"erdbeben-fortgesetzt-0014":["It would be a work of wide compass to cite and examine all the hypotheses which each person puts forward in order to open new paths of inquiry for himself, and of which one often takes the place of another like the waves of the sea. There is also a certain correct taste in natural science, which can soon distinguish the free extravagances of a craving for novelty from those secure and cautious judgments which have on their side the testimony of experience and rational credibility. Father Bina, and only lately Herr Prof. Krüger, advance the opinion which ascribes the same causes to the phenomena of earthquakes as to those of electricity. Still greater boldness lies in the proposal of Herr Prof. Hollmann. Having proved the usefulness of air-vents in a ground afflicted by inflamed materials, by the example of the fire-spewing mountains, without which the kingdoms of Naples and Sicily would exist no more, he wants the uppermost crust of the earth to be dug through down to the deepest burning clefts, and an outlet thereby provided for the fire. The enormous thickness together with the firmness of the inner strata (without which such cruel assaults of shocks would certainly long ago have shattered such a country), likewise the water, which soon sets a limit to all excavation, and finally the incapacity of men, make this proposal a pretty dream. From the Prometheus of recent times, Herr Franklin, who wished to disarm the thunder, to him who would extinguish the fire in the workshop of Vulcan, all such endeavours are proofs of man's boldness, which is joined to a power that stands in very small proportion to it. They lead him at last to the humbling reminder, with which he ought justly to have begun, that he is, after all, never more than a man.",[]],"monadologia-0001":["[Title page:] The use of metaphysics combined with geometry in natural philosophy, of which Specimen I contains the *monadologia physica* (physical monadology). With the consent of the most distinguished order of philosophers, M. Immanuel Kant will defend it in a public dissertation, to be held for the obtaining of a place, on the 10th [x.] of April, from eight to twelve o'clock, in the lecture hall of philosophy. Respondent: Lucas David Vogel of Royal Prussia, student [*cultor*] of sacred theology. Opponents, the talented and accomplished young men: Ludwig Ernst Borowski of Königsberg in Prussia, student of sacred theology; Georg Ludwig Mühlenkampf of Trempen near Darkehmen, a Prussian, student of sacred theology; and Ludwig Johann Krusemarck of Kyritz in the Mark, student of sacred theology. In the year 1756. Königsberg, printed by J. H. Hartung, printer to His Sacred Royal Majesty and to the University.",[]],"monadologia-0002":["To the most illustrious, most noble and most excellent man, Herr Wilhelm Ludwig von Groeben, privy minister of state and of war to the most powerful King of the Prussians, most eminent president of the supreme court of appeals which is in Prussia, most vigilant protector of our Albertina, and likewise most weighty director of the stipend board, hereditary lord of the estates of Tharau, Karschau and so forth, incomparable patron of the Muses, a hero most inclined to kindness toward his own.",[]],"monadologia-0003":["These columns, in a most devoted spirit,",[]],"monadologia-0004":["they wish to be sacred [to him],",[]],"monadologia-0005":["the president and the respondent.",[]],"monadologia-0006":["Preliminary remarks.\n\n---\n\nWait, format: I must follow the stated reply format. Corrected below.",[]],"monadologia-0007":["Those who devote their labour to the investigation of natural things, being philosophers of the more discerning sort, have indeed united in unanimous agreement on this point: that we must take anxious care lest anything rashly framed, through a certain licence of conjecture, creep into natural science, and lest anything be attempted in vain without the support of experience and without geometry as interpreter. Nothing more salutary or more useful to philosophy could certainly have been devised than this counsel. But since scarcely any mortal is able to advance on the straight line of truth with a steady step without straying here and there to one side or the other, some have indulged this rule so far that, in searching out truth, they have not dared at all to trust themselves to the deep, and have always thought it better to hug the shore, admitting nothing except what becomes known immediately by the testimony of experience. By this road we can certainly set forth the laws of nature; the origin and causes of the laws we cannot. For those who pursue only the phenomena of nature are always just as far removed from an understanding of the hidden first causes, and will never attain to knowledge of the very nature of bodies any more than those who, by climbing higher and ever higher up a mountain's summit, should persuade themselves that they would at last touch heaven with the hand.",["- Long Latin sentences kept largely intact but \"Verum quoniam…\" clause rendered with \"But since…\"; \"emunctioris naris\" (\"of keener nose\") rendered \"more discerning\".","- *Phaenomena* is left in Latin form as \"phenomena\", the Latin term for Erscheinungen; \"scientia\" rendered \"knowledge\" in the last sentence, \"scientiam naturalem\" as \"natural science\".","- \"Littus legere\" = \"to hug the shore\", a classical figure (cf. Virgil); \"alto se committere\" = \"trust oneself to the deep\"."]],"monadologia-0008":["Therefore what most people think they can comfortably do without in physical matters, Metaphysics alone here serves as a support and kindles a light. For bodies consist of parts; and how these are put together, whether they fill space by the mere co-presence of the primitive parts or by the mutual conflict of forces, is of no small importance to have clearly set forth. But in what way, in the end, may Metaphysics be reconciled with Geometry in this business, since griffins seem more easily yoked to horses than transcendental philosophy to Geometry? For the former stubbornly denies that space is infinitely divisible, while the latter affirms the same with the certainty with which it usually affirms everything else. The latter maintains that empty space is necessary for free motions; the former rejects it. The latter shows attraction, or universal gravity, which can scarcely be explained by mechanical causes, to proceed from forces innate in bodies, which act at rest and at a distance; the former banishes it among the vain sport of imagination.",["- The opening sentence is awkward in Latin (\"qua… sola hic adminiculo est\"); I read \"Igitur\" with Metaphysica as subject and relative clause as its object, restating it with \"what… Metaphysics alone\".","- \"Haec/illa\" resolved as Geometry (haec, the latter) and transcendental philosophy/Metaphysics (illa, the former); \"Gryphes… equis\" is the proverbial yoking of griffins with horses.","- \"in qviete et in distans agentium\": rendered \"act at rest and at a distance\"; the sense is that the forces act in bodies at rest, across distance."]],"monadologia-0009":["Since it appears that composing this dispute is a task of no small labour, I have resolved to devote at least some effort to it, inviting others, whose powers are better equal to the business, to complete what I shall here merely strive to touch upon.",["- \"afficere\" is taken as \"to touch upon, affect\"; the sense of the final clause is somewhat compressed in the Latin."]],"monadologia-0010":["By way of a coda I add only this. Since the principle of all internal actions, that is, the innate force of the elements, must necessarily be motive, and indeed applied externally, because it is present to external things; and since we cannot conceive any other force for moving co-present things than one that strives either to repel them or to attract them; and since, further, if a repelling force alone were posited, the binding together of elements to compose bodies would be intelligible not as union but rather as dispersion, while with an attracting force alone there would be union indeed, but no definite extension and no space could be understood: [it follows that] he who is able to derive these two principles from the very nature of the elements and their primitive affections may in a manner be understood in advance to have contributed work of no contemptible importance towards explaining the interior nature of bodies.",["- Very long sentence restated with parallel \"since\" clauses; the bracketed \"[it follows that]\" is supplied to resume the construction, which is irregular in the Latin (the main clause is anacoluthic).","- \"vim insitam\" = \"innate force\"; \"motricem\" = \"motive\"."]],"monadologia-0011":["Of the Physical Monadology\nSection I.\nDeclaring that the existence of Physical Monads is consonant with Geometry.",["- \"Monadologiae Physicae\" is a genitive title (\"Of the Physical Monadology\"); the text of the heading is otherwise literal."]],"monadologia-0012":["Prop. i. Definition. A simple substance, called a Monad *), is one that does not consist of a plurality of parts, of which one can exist separately without the others.",["- Footnote marker *) retained; its text is in the next passage."]],"monadologia-0013":["*) Since the plan of my undertaking is to treat only of that class of simple substances which are the primitive parts of bodies, I give advance notice that hereafter I shall use the terms simple substances, monads, elements of matter, and primitive parts of body as synonyms.",["- \"qvae corporum primitivae sunt partes\" taken as a relative clause with *primitivae* agreeing with *partes*."]],"monadologia-0014":["Prop. ii. Theorem. Bodies consist of monads.",[]],"monadologia-0015":["Bodies consist of parts which, separated from one another, have a lasting existence. But since composition in such parts is nothing but a relation, hence a determination contingent in itself, which can be removed while their existence is preserved, it is clear that all composition of a body can be abolished, while all the parts that were formerly composed nevertheless remain. But when all composition has been removed, the parts that remain have no composition at all, and so are wholly devoid of a plurality of substances, and hence are simple. Therefore any body whatever consists of primitive parts that are absolutely simple, that is, of monads.",["- \"determinatio\" rendered \"determination\" (Bestimmung); \"perdurabilem\" as \"lasting\".","- \"pluralitate substantiarum… destitutae\" literal; the sense is that the remaining parts are not themselves composed of several substances."]],"monadologia-0016":["Scholium. In the present demonstration I have purposely omitted that celebrated principle of reason, and have framed the argument from the common union of notions to which every philosopher subscribes, fearing that those whose minds are averse to this principle might in such a manner be less convinced.",["- \"rationis principium\" is the principle of (sufficient) reason; \"notionum adunatione\" is rendered \"union of notions\", which is obscure; the sense seems to be an argument from the ordinary combination of concepts. Doubtful.","- \"instituto e communi\" is taken as \"framed from the common\", with the noun \"notionum adunatione\" the thing drawn on; the syntax is loose."]],"monadologia-0017":["Prop. iii. Theorem. The space which bodies fill is infinitely divisible, and therefore does not consist of primitive and simple parts.",["- \"ininfinitum\" is read as *in infinitum* (OCR/typographical variant in the source)."]],"monadologia-0018":["Let a line e f be given, produced indefinitely, that is, in such a way that it can always be produced further at will; and let another, a b, physical, that is, if it so pleases, composed of primitive parts of matter, stand upon it at right angles. At the side let another, c d, be erected, equal to the former and similarly placed, which you will not deny can be done not only in the geometrical but also in the physical sense. Let any points whatever, g, h, i, k, and so on indefinitely, be marked on the line e f. First, no one will call in doubt that a physical straight line can be drawn between any two given points or, if you prefer, monads. Let c g, then, be drawn, and let the place where this intersects the perpendicular a b be o. Now let another physical line be conceived as drawn between the points c and h, and the place u, common to the two lines c h and a b, will be nearer to the point a. And so further: if lines are drawn from the same point c to any points on the line e f produced to infinity, i, k, etc., the points of intersection, x, y, etc., will always become nearer to the point a, as is evident of itself even to one wholly ignorant of Geometry. And if you think that these physical lines will at last be more closely contiguous to one another than is right, so that they cannot stand side by side, the lower ones drawn can be removed, and nevertheless it is clear that the places of intersection must approach more and more to the point a *), according as you mark a more and more distant point on the indefinite line e f. But since this distance can be prolonged to infinity, the approach of the intersection towards the point a can also increase by infinite increments. Yet the intersection will never in this way fall upon the point a; for since the points c and a are equally distant from the line e f, the line joining the points c and a, however far continued, will always be at the same distance from the underlying line e f, and can never meet it, which is contrary to the hypothesis. And so by continued division of the line o a one never arrives at primitive parts not further divisible, that is, space is infinitely divisible and does not consist of simple parts.",["- Letters of the figure kept as in the source; the figure itself is not in the passage. \"Justo arctiores\" rendered \"more closely contiguous than is right [fitting]\".","- \"ducta concipiatur… et erit\" kept as sequence; \"ad latus\" = \"at the side\".","- The final reductio (\"contra hypothesin\") refers to the parallelism of c a and e f; the clause \"neque huic unquam occurrere potest\" is read with the line c a as subject."]],"monadologia-0019":["*) Nor can the points y and x ever coincide, since otherwise the lines c y and c x would likewise coincide, and the line c k would coincide with the line c i, which is contrary to the postulates.",["- Footnote to the asterisk in the preceding passage."]],"monadologia-0020":["Scholium. I have cited here this demonstration, already employed by very many physicists, and have adapted it to physical space with all the clarity that was possible, lest those who make use of the general distinction between geometrical and natural space should slip away through some exception. There are indeed other demonstrations of the same thesis ready to hand, of which, to cite only one: conceive an equilateral triangle constructed, if you like, of monads, whose two sides are produced indefinitely, and on these take distances twice, three times, five times, a hundred times, and so on, greater than the sides of the given triangle. The extremities of these can be joined by physical lines, which will be in the same ratio, as the third side of the triangle is to them, and which will consist of as many more simple particles. But since physical lines can be conceived as drawn between any of these monads and the one placed at the vertex of the angle, these divide the base of the given triangle infinitely many times, and so admirably uphold the infinite divisibility of space. But whoever has seen through the demonstration given above without the hindrance of prejudiced opinions can, in my judgment at least, dispense with all the others.",["- Second sentence: \"qui… utuntur… exceptione quadam elabantur\" read as \"those who use the general distinction… might escape through some exception\".","- \"in eadem ratione ut illae tertio trianguli latere majores\" is elliptical; I read it as the lines being in the same ratio to the third side as the extended sides to the original side, with more particles correspondingly. Doubtful; I have rendered it loosely."]],"monadologia-0021":["Prop. iv. Theorem. A composite infinitely divisible does not consist of primitive or simple parts.",[]],"monadologia-0022":["Since in a composite infinitely divisible one never arrives, by dividing, at parts stripped of all composition, and since the composition that cannot be removed by division cannot be removed at all except by abolishing the whole existence of the composite; and since the parts that remain in a composite when all composition is removed are entitled simple (Prop. I): it is clear that a composite infinitely divisible does not consist of such parts.",["- The Latin sentence has no main verb until \"liquet\"; I have kept the three \"since\" clauses and resolved them into one main clause.","- \"audiunt simplices\" = \"are called simple\", rendered \"are entitled simple\".","- The \"Prop. I\" reference, though the definition appears under Prop. i, is as in the source."]],"monadologia-0023":["Scholium. Having vindicated for every body simple primitive parts, and having asserted the infinite divisibility of the space it occupies, I thought it not foreign to the purpose of this work to guard against anyone's taking the monads for infinitely small particles of body. Space is wholly devoid of substantiality and is a phenomenon of the external relation of united monads. In this way it is amply shown that space is not exhausted even by a division continued to infinity. But in any composite whatever, composition is nothing but an accident, and the subjects of composition are substantial. That such a composite should suffer infinite division is therefore absurd. For it would also follow that every primitive part of a body is so constituted that, when joined with a thousand others, or with myriads, or with millions of millions (in a word, with as many as one may please to assign), it still would not constitute any particle of matter whatever. This plainly removes all substantiality from the composite, and so cannot hold of natural bodies.",["- \"illud\" is taken to refer to the composite; I supplied \"such a composite\" and \"[so]\" for clarity, and began a new sentence at \"But in any composite\".","- \"Phaenomenon\" is rendered \"phenomenon\", following Kant's Latin rather than \"appearance\".","- \"partes primitivas\" is rendered \"primitive parts\"."]],"monadologia-0024":["Corollary. Every body, therefore, consists of a definite number of simple elements.",[]],"monadologia-0025":["Proposition V. Theorem. Every simple element of a body, or monad, is not only in space but also fills space, without prejudice to its simplicity.",["- \"salva ipsius simplicitate\" is rendered \"without prejudice to its simplicity\"; the same phrase recurs below as \"its simplicity being preserved\"."]],"monadologia-0026":["Since every body is composed of a definite number of simple elements, while the space which it fills admits of infinite division, each of these elements will occupy a part of space that is still further divisible; that is, it will fill an assignable space.",[]],"monadologia-0027":["But the division of space is not a separation of things, of which one, when removed from another, would have its own self-sufficient existence. It argues only a certain plurality or quantity in external relation. It is therefore evident that no plurality of substantial parts follows from it. Since only such a plurality would be contrary to the simplicity of a monad, it is abundantly clear that the divisibility of space is not opposed to that simplicity.",["- The sentence is divided in three. \"Quae cum sola\" is resolved as \"only such a plurality\", the antecedent being substantial plurality, not space."]],"monadologia-0028":["Scholium. In the investigation of the elements no opinion has more obstructed the union of geometry with metaphysics than the preconceived, though insufficiently examined, belief that the divisibility of the space which an element occupies would argue a division of the element itself into substantial parts. This has been so commonly taken to be beyond all hazard of doubt that those who defend the infinite division of real space recoil altogether from monads, while those who subscribe to monads have judged that the properties of geometrical space, as regards its parts, must be held to be imaginary. But it is plain from what has been demonstrated above that the geometer is not mistaken, and that the doctrine which rests with the metaphysician does not stray from the truth. The opinion which has divided the two, namely that an element absolutely simple in respect of substance cannot fill space without prejudice to its simplicity, must therefore necessarily be mistaken.\n\nA line or surface which divides some small space in two indicates, indeed, that one part of space exists outside the other. But space is not a substance; it is a phenomenon of the external relation of substances. That the relation of one and the same substance can be divided in two is therefore not contrary to the simplicity, or, if you prefer, the unity, of the substance. For what lies on either side of the dividing line is not anything so separable from the substance that, removed from it, it would maintain an existence of its own. That would indeed be required for a real division, which alone removes simplicity. It is the action, or relation, of one and the same substance exercised on both sides; and to find some plurality in this is not to tear the substance itself into parts.",["- \"suarum partium rati sint\" is obscure; I take it as \"as regards its parts\" qualifying the properties of space, and \"rati\" as \"have judged\". The reading is doubtful.","- I divided the passage into two paragraphs at \"Qvae enim spatiolum\", and restated the long sentence \"Verum cum … necesse est\" in two, with \"[the opinion] … must therefore be mistaken\" resolving the appositional construction.","- \"Qvod … requiritur\" is resolved as \"That would indeed be required for a real division\"."]],"monadologia-0029":["Proposition VI. Theorem. A monad defines the small space of its presence not by a plurality of its substantial parts but by the sphere of activity, by which it keeps external things present to it on either side from any further approach to one another.",["- \"externas\" has no noun; \"external things\" (i.e. other monads or elements) is supplied."]],"monadologia-0030":["Since there is no plurality of substances in a monad, and yet each, posited by itself, fills a space, the ground of the filled space must, by the preceding proposition, be sought not in the position of the substance alone but in its relation to external things. But by filling space it keeps those immediately present to it on either side from any further approach to each other, and so determines something in their position, namely by limiting the measure of nearness to which they can come to each other. It is therefore evident that it exerts an action, and indeed in a space determined on every side. Hence it must be granted that it fills this space by the sphere of its activity.",["- \"per praec.\" is expanded as \"by the preceding proposition [*propositionem*]\" in sense only. The long sentence is divided in two at \"It is therefore evident\"."]],"monadologia-0031":["Proposition VII. Problem. To vindicate further from difficulties the space which each monad occupies by the sphere of its activity, its simplicity being preserved.",["- \"vlterius a difficultatibus vindicare\" is rendered \"to vindicate further from difficulties\"."]],"monadologia-0032":["If a monad, as we contend, fills a definite space, that space can be expressed by any other finite space. Let the little circle ABCD represent the small space which the monad occupies by its activity. Then BD will be the diameter of this sphere of activity; that is, the distance at which the monad keeps other things present to it at B and D from any further approach to one another. But do not on that account say that this is the diameter of the monad itself, which would indeed be absurd; nothing, in fact, is more foreign to our view. For since space is made up of external relations alone, whatever in a substance is internal, that is, the substance itself, as the subject of external determinations, is not properly defined by space. Only those of its determinations which are referred to external things may be sought in space.\n\nBut, you say, the substance is present in this small space, and is at hand everywhere in it; therefore whoever divides the space divides the substance? I reply that this space is itself the compass of the external presence of this element. Whoever divides the space, therefore, divides the extensive quantity of its presence. But besides its external presence, that is, the relative determinations of the substance, there are others which are internal, and if these did not exist the former would have no subject in which to inhere. Internal determinations, however, are not in space, just because they are internal. Hence they are not divided by the division of the external determinations, and so neither is the subject itself, or the substance, divided in this way. It would be just as if one were to say: God is internally present to all created things by the act of conservation; whoever therefore divides the aggregate of created things divides God, because he divides the compass of His presence. Nothing more absurd could be said.\n\nThe monad, then, which is the primitive element of body, has indeed, in so far as it fills space, a certain extensive quantity, namely the compass of its activity. But in this you will not find several things, of which one, separated from another (that is, by itself without the other), would have its own durability. For what is found in the space BCD cannot be so separated from what is present in the space BAD that each should exist by itself. Both are nothing but external determinations of one and the same substance, and accidents do not exist without their substances. *)",["- Divided into three paragraphs for clarity; \"At, ais\" is rendered \"But, you say\" as the objector's voice. \"Sed qvae ipsius determinationum ad externa referuntur\" is smoothed to \"those of its determinations which are referred to external things\".","- \"illae\" (the external determinations) is made explicit as \"the former\"; \"qvod … non haberent\" is restated in one clause.","- Footnote marker *) kept in place; the note follows as the next passage (monadologia-0033).","- \"perdurabilitatem\" is rendered \"durability\"."]],"monadologia-0033":["*) Of all the difficulties which can stand in the way of our view, the gravest seems to be that drawn from the mutual externality [*extrapositio*] of the determinations of one and the same substance. For the action of the monad which is in the space BCD is outside the action which is in the space BDA; they therefore seem to be really different from each other and to be found outside the substance. But relations are always both outside each other and outside the substance, because those beings to which the substance is related are really different from the substance and from each other; and this does not argue a substantial plurality.",["- \"extrapositione\" is rendered \"mutual externality [*extrapositio*]\", a gloss; the German sense of \"Außereinandersein\" is not in the text.","- The space BDA in this footnote corresponds to \"B A D\" in the preceding text; I kept the letters as printed."]],"monadologia-0034":["Proposition VIII. Theorem. The force by which a simple element of a body occupies its space is the same which others call impenetrability; nor, if you depart from that force, can there be any place for impenetrability.",["- \"huic\" is resolved as impenetrability (the nearer antecedent in sense), not the force."]],"monadologia-0035":["Impenetrability is that affection of a body by which it keeps contiguous things away from the space which it occupies. But from the preceding propositions it is known that the space which a body occupies (if its parts are conceived as united as closely as possible, without any intermingled void) is composed of the small spaces which the single simple elements fill. Further, to keep external bodies from rushing into a filled space, that is, for impenetrability, there is required resistance, and hence a certain force. And it was demonstrated earlier that the elements fill their definite space by a certain activity of keeping off others that would penetrate it. It is therefore evident that the impenetrability of bodies depends on no other force than that same natural force of the elements. Which was the first thing to be shown.",["- The long periodic sentence is restated as four sentences. \"Qvod erat primum\" is rendered \"Which was the first thing to be shown\", the first part of a proposition proved in two parts; the second part is not in this passage."]],"monadologia-0036":["Next, let the line a g be composed of primitive elements of matter, that is, of monads. Suppose that any element d, by the presence of its substance, merely marked a place and did not occupy space. Then the place d would bisect the given line a g. Since it marks where one half of the line ends and the other begins, it would be common to both halves of the line. But physical lines are not equal unless they consist of an equal number of elements, and the number of elements on either side is not equal except in the lines a c and e g. Therefore the place of the monad d will be common to the lines a c and e g; that is, the said lines will meet one another immediately in the place named. The element d therefore does not keep the nearest elements e and c from immediate contact; that is, it will not be impenetrable.\n\nIf, then, you deny that the place occupied by the monad d is common to the lines a c and e g, there will be a point x where the lines a c and d g meet immediately, and a point o where the lines a d and e g meet. Since the place of the monad d is therefore different from the place x and also from the place o (for otherwise the place of immediate contact would always be common, as was said before), you have three different places, x d o, which undoubtedly define some line.\n\nThe immediate presence of the monad d is therefore defined by a definite line; that is, the monad is present in a definite space. By the mere position of its substance it could occupy a place, not a space. There must therefore be present in the substance something else, which determines the measure of proximity in the elements touching it on either side, and which by force keeps off any closer approach of the elements c and e. But force can be opposed only by force. Therefore the same force by which the element of a body occupies its space is the cause of impenetrability. This was the second point [*Qvod erat alterum*].",["- The figure to which the letters a, c, d, e, g, o, x refer is not given; the letters are kept as in the text.","- \"d g\" in \"lineae a c et d g\" is kept as printed; by the symmetry of the argument one might expect \"e g\", but I do not emend it.","- I have divided the long Latin sentences and the closing argument into shorter ones.","- *Qvod erat alterum*: \"which was the second [thing to be proved]\"; I supply \"point\" in square brackets in sense only."]],"monadologia-0037":["Section II.\nAn explanation of the most general affections of physical monads, in so far as, differing in different monads, they serve to make the nature of bodies intelligible.",["- \"Affectiones\" is rendered \"affections\"; the sentence has no main verb in the Latin, being a title."]],"monadologia-0038":["Prop. IX. Definition. Contact is the mutual application, to one another, of the forces of impenetrability of several elements.",["- \"Prop.\" is retained as in the source."]],"monadologia-0039":["Scholium. Contact is commonly defined by immediate presence. But even if you were to add the word 'external' (for without this addition God, who is present to all things immediately but intimately, would have to be held to be in contact with them), the definition will still hardly be complete in every respect. For others have sufficiently shown that bodies separated by empty space can nevertheless coexist, and hence be immediately present to one another, though without mutual contact. The definition will therefore undoubtedly be faulty here. Further, the Newtonian school defends, with no small show of truth, an immediate attraction of bodies even when far distant from one another. Their compresence would nevertheless occur without mutual contact.\n\nMoreover, if you defend a definition that offers immediate compresence as the very concept of contact, you must first explain the concept of this presence. If, as is usual, you explain it by mutual action, in what, I ask, does action consist? Undoubtedly bodies act on one another by moving. But a moving force exerted from a given point either repels other bodies from that point or attracts them. Which of the two actions is to be understood in contact is easily seen. For when we move one body nearer and nearer to another, we say that they touch one another when the force of impenetrability, that is, of repulsion, is felt. Therefore the action and reaction, exerted by different elements against one another, of this force constitute the genuine concept of contact.",["- \"Immediate... intime\": *immediate* and *intime* kept distinct (\"immediately... intimately\").","- \"Contactus... notio\": *notio* rendered \"concept\", following the method's rendering of Begriff.","- Long sentences divided."]],"monadologia-0040":["Prop. X. Theorem. Bodies would not, through the force of impenetrability alone, enjoy a definite volume, unless there were present another force, likewise innate, namely attraction, which, jointly with the former, defines the limit of extension.",["- None beyond the ordinary."]],"monadologia-0041":["The force of impenetrability is a repulsive force, which keeps off any external thing from further approach. Since this force is innate in every element, it can indeed be understood from its nature why the intensity of its action diminishes with the increase of the distance to which it extends. But that at any given distance it should be altogether nil cannot be understood from the force itself. Hence, if one stopped with this force alone, there would be no cohesion of bodies at all, since the particles would merely repel one another, and no volume circumscribed by a definite limit would be established for a body. It is therefore necessary that there be opposed to this endeavour another, opposite one, equal at a given distance, which determines the limit for the space to be occupied. Since this acts in direct opposition to repulsion, it is attraction. Every element therefore needs, besides the force of impenetrability, another, attractive force; if you set this aside, determinate volumes of the nature of bodies would not result.",["- \"quod in distantia quavis data plane nulla sit\": I take the clause to mean that the force would be nil at some assignable distance; rendered accordingly.","- \"compages\" rendered \"cohesion\"; \"naturae\" in the last clause kept as \"of the nature of bodies\"."]],"monadologia-0042":["Scholium. To investigate the laws of both forces in the elements, the repulsive as well as the attractive, is a task of truly great difficulty, and worthy to exercise the more penetrating minds. For my part it suffices here to have rendered their existence as certain as the law of brevity allowed. But if one cares to look, as from afar, at certain things bearing on this question, must not the intensity of the repulsive force, since it acts outward from the innermost point of the space occupied by the element, be held to be weakened reciprocally according to the increase of the space into which it extends? For a force distributed from a point in a definite sphere cannot be found effective unless by acting it fills the whole space comprised under the given diameter.\n\nThis is made clear in the following way. Conceive a force emanating along straight lines from a given surface, like light or, in Keill's view, the force of attraction itself. The force so exerted will be in proportion to the multitude of lines that can be drawn from this surface, that is, in proportion to the acting surface itself. Hence if the surface is infinitely small, this force too will be infinitely small, and if finally it is a point, altogether nil. Therefore a force cannot be diffused from a point along divergent lines to any assignable distance. Nor accordingly will it be found effective unless by filling the whole space in which it acts. But spherical spaces are as the cubes of their distances. Therefore, since the same force diffused through a greater space is diminished in inverse ratio to the spaces, the force of impenetrability will be reciprocally in the triplicate ratio of the distances from the centre of presence.",["- \"Keilii\": John Keill; \"mentem\" rendered \"view\".","- \"lex brevitatis\" rendered \"the law of brevity\".","- \"in ratione triplicata distantiarum... reciproce\": rendered \"reciprocally in the triplicate ratio of the distances\".","- Two long sentences divided; \"Qvod hac ratione patefit\" begins a new paragraph's argument."]],"monadologia-0043":["On the other hand, since attraction is indeed an action of the same element but turned in the opposite direction, the spherical surface onto which attraction is exerted at a given distance will be the *terminus a quo*. Since the multitude of the points from which lines can be drawn toward the centre of tendency, and hence the quantity of attraction, is definite, the attraction will in this way be assignable, and will decrease in inverse ratio to the spherical surfaces, that is, in the inverse duplicate ratio of the distances.",["- *terminus a quo* left in Latin as a technical phrase.","- \"superficies sphaerica... in quam\": the construction is somewhat loose; I have taken the sphere as the surface upon which the action is exerted and rendered the sense plainly."]],"monadologia-0044":["If, therefore, the repulsive force is supposed to decrease in the subtriplicate ratio, and hence in a far greater ratio, then at some point of the diameter attraction and repulsion must be equal. This point will determine the limit of impenetrability and the compass, or volume, of external contact; for once attraction has been overcome, the repulsive force acts no further.",["- \"subtriplicata\" is kept as printed; it sits oddly with the \"triplicate\" ratio of 0042, and the text may be doubtful or the term used loosely. I flag it without emending.","- \"victa enim attractione\": the sense seems to be that attraction is overcome beyond that point; I have rendered the Latin literally. The wording may be doubtful."]],"monadologia-0045":["Corollary. If you accept this law of the innate forces, you will also admit that the volume of all elements is equal, however different their kind. For it is plain that the forces of repulsion, as well as those of attraction, since each has a definite degree of intensity, may be very different in different elements, more intense in one, weaker in another. Nevertheless, since a double force of repulsion at the same distance is double, and likewise a double force of attraction, and it is fitting that all the moving forces of an element which is specifically twice as strong should be stronger in the same ratio, the said forces at the same distance are always equalised. Hence they necessarily determine an equal volume of the element, however much they differ in degree from the like-named forces of other elements.",["- \"specifice duplo fortius\": rendered \"specifically twice as strong\".","- The sentence is long and has an irregular construction; I have divided it and made \"the said forces\" the subject of the final clause."]],"monadologia-0046":["Prop. XI. Theorem. The force of inertia in any element is of a definite quantity, which may be very different in different elements.",["- *vis inertiae* rendered \"force of inertia\"."]],"monadologia-0047":["A moving body running into another would have no efficacy, and would be brought to rest by any infinitely small obstacle, unless it enjoyed a force of inertia, by which it strives to persist in its state of moving. Now the force of inertia of a body is the sum of the forces of inertia of all the elements of which it is composed (and this they call its mass). Therefore any element moved with a certain velocity would have no efficacy of moving at all, unless this were multiplied by the force of inertia. But whatever, by multiplying another, yields a quantity greater than the other factor is itself a quantity, whereby another, either greater or smaller, can be assigned. Therefore, besides the force of inertia of any element, another, either greater or smaller, can be given in elements of a different kind.",["- \"massam\" rendered \"mass\"; \"qvantitas\" rendered \"quantity\".","- The last sentence is compressed in the Latin; \"besides\" is my rendering of \"alia\" in the sense of \"another [force of inertia]\", supplied in the sense only."]],"monadologia-0048":["Corollary I. For any given elements others can be given whose force of inertia, or, what in a different respect is the same, whose moving force, is twice or three times as great. That is, they resist a certain velocity with twice or three times the force, and, when moved with the same velocity, possess twice or three times the impetus.",["- \"diverso respectu idem est\": \"what in a different respect is the same\"; inertia and moving force are treated as the same force under different aspects.","- \"coroll.\" expanded to \"Corollary\"."]],"monadologia-0049":["Corollary 2. Since it is established from the Corollary to the preceding Proposition that any elements whatever, however different in kind, are nevertheless endowed with equal volume, and that therefore an equal number of elements is always contained in an equal space exactly filled, it is rightly concluded that bodies, even if one banishes every admixture of the void to the utmost and takes the whole space to be perfectly filled, can nevertheless contain very different masses under the same volume, namely because their elements are endowed with a greater or lesser force of inertia (*vis inertiae*). For the mass of bodies is nothing but the quantity of the *vis inertiae* of those elements, by which they either resist motion or, when moved with a given velocity, are endowed with a definite impetus of moving.",["- Restated the long opening sentence with its ablative-absolute-like clauses in smoother English; \"Prop. praec.\" rendered \"preceding Proposition\".","- *vis inertiae* kept in Latin as a technical term; \"elementa\" rendered \"elements\"."]],"monadologia-0050":["Hence the inference from a smaller quantity of matter comprised under a given volume to a smaller density, and to larger empty interstices intercepted, is not always sufficiently firm. Either body may be endowed with equal empty interstices, or be perfectly dense, and none the less one or the other may possess a far greater mass; the cause of the difference resides plainly in the very nature of the elements themselves.",["- \"valet consequentia\" rendered \"is ... firm\" (the inference holds); \"pollere\" varied as \"endowed with\"/\"possess\"."]],"monadologia-0051":["Proposition 12. Theorem. The specific diversity of density of the bodies observable in the world cannot possibly be explained without a specific diversity in the inertia of the elements themselves.",["- \"Theorema\" rendered \"Theorem\"; the title is kept in the style of the original proposition headings."]],"monadologia-0052":["If all the elements enjoyed an equal *vis inertiae* and an equal volume, then, in order to understand the difference in the rarity of bodies, an absolute void interspersed among the parts would be required. For, according to the demonstrations of Newton, Keill and others, there is no room for free motion in a medium that is perfectly filled in this manner. Accordingly, to explain the infinitely various specific density of media, e.g. of aether, air, water, gold, one must indulge an immoderate passion for conjecture, by which the very texture of the elements, which is most remote from human understanding, is rashly fashioned at will, being conceived freely and boldly now after the manner of the most delicate little bubbles, now after that of branches and twisted spirals, so that one might think of matter as extended in wondrous ways, and as embracing an enormous space with a slight quantity of matter. But hear the reasons that tell against this.",["- Divided the long sentence on \"Ideoqve\" into shorter sentences and clauses; \"qvae ... maxime remota est\" is the antecedent of \"texture\" (resolved by judgment).","- \"Vacuo absoluto\" rendered \"absolute void\"; \"Keilii\" given as \"Keill\"."]],"monadologia-0053":["Those fibrils, exceedingly slender beyond measure, or those bubbles, which under a skin of immense thinness enclose a vast void in proportion to their quantity of matter, must in the continual conflict and attrition of bodies be worn away in the end, and in this way the fragments of those thus broken up must at last fill up the enclosed empty space. When this has happened, the space of the world, perfectly full on every side, will grow numb with a powerful inertia, and all motions will shortly be reduced to rest.",["- \"Immensum quantum exiles\" rendered \"exceedingly slender beyond measure\"; \"comminutarum\" taken as the broken-up fibrils and bubbles."]],"monadologia-0054":["Furthermore, since according to such a view the specifically rarer media must consist of parts that are greatly extended and endowed with large volume, by what means, then, can the interstices of denser bodies, which according to the same view are narrower, be pervious to them, as it is established that fire and the magnetic and electric fluids permeate bodies with the greatest ease? For how particles endowed with a greater volume can penetrate into interstices narrower than themselves, I am, along with the most ignorant, unable to see.",["- \"Ignarissimis ignoro\" rendered \"along with the most ignorant, [I] am unable to see\"; the word \"see\" is supplied for \"ignoro\" (\"I do not know\")."]],"monadologia-0055":["Unless, therefore, a specific diversity of the simplest elements themselves is conceded, by which, in the same space exactly filled, now a smaller and now a far greater mass can be constructed, physics will always stick fast at this difficulty as at a reef.",["- \"Veluti ad scopulum haerebit\" rendered \"as at a reef\" (it will run aground)."]],"monadologia-0056":["Proposition 13. Theorem. The elements of a body, even when placed in isolation, enjoy a perfect elastic force, differing in different elements, and constitute a medium that is primitively elastic in itself, without any admixture of the void.",["- \"Solitario posita\" rendered \"placed in isolation\"; \"in diversis diversa\" rendered \"differing in different elements\"."]],"monadologia-0057":["Each single simple element occupies the space of its presence by a certain definite force, which keeps external substances away from it. But since every finite force has a degree that can be surpassed by another that is greater, it is evident that a stronger force can be opposed to this repulsive force, and that, since the element's innate force for warding off is not sufficient at the same distance, the stronger force will to some extent penetrate into the space occupied by the element. But since any forces extended from a definite point into space are weakened in proportion to the increase of distance, it is evident that this repulsive force reacts the more strongly the nearer one approaches the centre of its activity. And since the repelling force, which is finite at a given distance from the centre of repulsion, increases in a definite proportion with approach, it must be infinite at the point itself; it is evident that the element can be penetrated through by no conceivable force. It will therefore be perfectly elastic, and several such elements, their elasticities joined together, will constitute a primitively elastic medium. That this elasticity differs in different elements is evident from the Corollary of Proposition 10, line 5.",["- Kept the author's chain of \"patet\" clauses in separate sentences; \"cui\" rendered \"the stronger force will...\" (antecedent resolved by sense).","- \"Coroll. prop. X. linea 5\" is a reference to the earlier text; I translate it literally.","- The passage's argument is awkward in the Latin (\"sed ... patet\" repeated); I smoothed it slightly."]],"monadologia-0058":["Corollary. The elements are perfectly impenetrable, that is, incapable of being wholly excluded from the space they occupy by any external force however great; but they are condensible, and constitute bodies that are also condensible, since they yield somewhat to an external compressing force. Hence the origin of bodies, or media, primitively elastic, among which one may be permitted to recognise beforehand the aether, or the matter of fire.",["- \"Penitus excludi nescia\" rendered \"incapable of being wholly excluded\"; \"in antecessum profiteri\" rendered \"recognise beforehand\"."]],"monadologia-0059":["End.",["- \"finis\" rendered \"End\"."]],"winde-0001":["M. Immanuel Kant's\nNew\nRemarks\nfor the Elucidation\nof the Theory of the Winds,\nby which he at the same time\ninvites [the public] to his lectures.\nKönigsberg, 25 April 1756.\nPrinted at the Royal privileged Driest Press.",["- Supplied \"[the public]\" after \"invites\"; the German has no object. \"Driestischen Buchdruckerey\" rendered \"Driest Press\"."]],"winde-0002":["Preliminary Remark.",[]],"winde-0003":["The atmosphere must be represented as a sea of fluid elastic matter, composed, as it were, of strata of different density, which in greater heights always diminishes. If this fluid sea is to remain in equilibrium, it is not enough that the columns of air which we represent to ourselves side by side should be of equal weight; they must also stand at equal height. That is to say, the stratum of a certain density must stand in all parts of its extent at the same level. For by the laws of fluids, in the contrary case the higher part would necessarily flow off towards the lower side, and the equilibrium would at once be abolished. The causes which can abolish the equilibrium are either the diminution of the expansive force, through cold and vapours which weaken the spring of the air, or the diminution of weight. The latter occurs, first, through heat, by which a certain region of the air is expanded more strongly than another; being thereby compelled to rise above the level of the other, it flows off and forms a lighter column of air. It occurs, secondly, through the coalescence of the water-vapours which were previously borne by the air, but which now, as they separate from it, withdraw a part of its weight. In both cases a wind arises towards the region where the air has lost either in expansive force or in weight. There is this difference, however: in the first case the equilibrium is soon restored, as also with the second cause of the other case, because for the continuance of the wind in these cases an increase of the cause is required, which cannot possibly last long. The first cause of this latter case, on the other hand, since it need only be continued without increasing, yields a very powerful source of lasting winds.",["- Divided the long final sentence and the sentence on the causes into several; \"Wasserwaage\" rendered \"level\".","- \"welches unmöglich lange währen kann\" refers to the increase of the cause, so I rendered it accordingly.","- \"Qvelle\" rendered \"source\"."]],"winde-0004":["The causes which set the atmosphere in motion either through an increase of elasticity, as for instance by warmth, or at the same time of weight, as with air freed from melting snow, are by far not so powerful. For in that case the movement takes place against a quiescent air which resists it with its whole weight, and the expanding region of air also expands upwards as strongly as to the sides, and so weakens its own force. Hence a wind from these causes can not possibly be perceived over great distances.",["- \"wegen\" is rendered by \"through\", \"alsdenn\" by \"in that case\"; the sense of \"zugleich der Schwere\" is completed as \"an increase ... of weight\" (supplied)."]],"winde-0005":["I mention all this only briefly, and presuppose that the reader's own reflection will spread the necessary light over what has been set forth. I should not like to say very little in so few pages.",["- \"Blättern\" rendered \"pages\" (literally leaves or sheets)."]],"winde-0006":["First Remark.",[]],"winde-0007":["A greater degree of heat, acting upon one region of the air more than upon another, produces a wind towards this heated region, which lasts as long as the superior warmth of that region continues.",[]],"winde-0008":["The increased heat compels the air to occupy more space. It expands sideways, and equally strongly upwards. At this moment the weight of this region of air is altered, since, as the air rising above flows over, the column of air thenceforth contains less air. The neighbouring air, which is cooler and therefore denser and heavier, displaces it from its place by reason of its superior weight. This air, like the former, is rarefied and made lighter, and so yields to the pressure of the next, and so on. It must not be supposed that this heated air, since it likewise strives to expand sideways, will produce a wind from the region of heating into the cooler region of air. For, in the first place, the expansion takes place equally strongly in all directions, and consequently the expansive force, which is inversely proportional to it, decreases reciprocally as the cube of the distance from the centre. Hence the expansive power of a place containing 4 square miles of air, if it had been increased by a tenth part, would at a distance of one mile from this heated place amount to only the eightieth part of this increased force, and so could not even be perceived. But the expansion cannot even reach so far. For before the air has expanded so far, it will, on account of the diminution of its weight, yield to the pressure of the denser air, and give up its place to it.",[]],"winde-0009":["Confirmation from experience.",[]],"winde-0010":["The rule stated is so thoroughly confirmed by all experience that not a single exception to it can be brought forward. All islands lying in the sea, and all coasts of lands in regions where the heat of the sun acts strongly, experience a continuous sea-wind as soon as the sun has risen so far above the horizon that it acts noticeably upon the earth. For since the land takes on more heat than the sea, the air over the land is rarefied more than the air over the sea, and therefore, on account of its lightness, yields to the weight of the latter. In the wide Ethiopian Ocean, far from the mainland, the wind is the natural general east wind; but nearer to the coasts of Guinea it is deflected from this its course, and is compelled to blow over Guinea. Guinea, being more heated by the sun than the ocean, causes a current of air over its warmed soil. One need only look at the chart which Jurin has appended to Varenius's *General Geography*, or to Musschenbroek's *Physics*; then, having before one's eyes both the natural general east wind and this rule, one will be able in a moment to understand and explain fully all the directions of the wind blowing in the sea off Guinea, the tornadoes, and everything else. For this reason the north winds prevail in the north in winter, when the sun rarefies the air in the southern hemisphere. Hence also at the beginning of spring the winds begin to blow from the equator towards the northern hemisphere, because the increased warmth of the sun rarefies the air in the latter, and causes a retreat of air from the equator towards the northern temperate zone. This wind does not extend far into this temperate region, because at that season the sun's warmth cannot yet produce much effect at a greater distance from the equator. About this time, in April and May, the winds that are called the *Campsin* blow from inner Ethiopia over Egypt; and since they come from a heated soil, they bring with them a burning hot air. For the rarefied air in the temperate zone compels the equatorial air to retreat, and to spread for a time over this region.",[]],"winde-0011":["Second Remark.",[]],"winde-0012":["A region of air that cools more than another produces in the neighbouring region a wind which blows into the place of cooling.",[]],"winde-0013":["The cause is easily conceivable from the diminution of the expansive force through the decrease of warmth.",[]],"winde-0014":["Confirmation from experience.",[]],"winde-0015":["In all seas, near the coasts of the mainland or of islands exposed to strong action of the sun, a continuous land-wind blows by night. For at that time the air over the sea loses its warmth more quickly than the air over the land, because the heated ground retains its warmth in the latter without notable diminution, whereas the sea, which has absorbed little heat during the day, lets the air above it cool more quickly. This air [over the sea] therefore yields to the expansive force of the former [the air over the land], and permits a current of air from the land into the cooled region of the sea. The south winds which, as Mariotte remarks, blow in France at the beginning of November are to be ascribed to the cooling of the air in the far north, where winter then sets in with its full severity.",[]],"winde-0016":["Third Remark.",[]],"winde-0017":["A wind that blows from the equator towards the pole becomes, the further it goes, ever more westerly; and one that travels from the pole to the equator changes its direction into a collateral movement from the east.",[]],"winde-0018":["This rule, which, so far as I know, has never yet been remarked, may be regarded as a key to the general theory of the winds. Its proof is easily comprehensible and convincing. The earth rotates about its axis from west to east. Every place on its surface therefore has the greater velocity the nearer it is to the equator, and the less the farther it is removed from it. The air which moves towards the equator accordingly meets on its way places that have more motion from west to east than it has itself. It will therefore offer these a resistance in the opposite direction, namely from east to west, and the wind will in consequence deviate in this collateral direction. For it is all one whether the ground moves on beneath a fluid body which is not moved with equal velocity in the same direction, or whether the latter is moved over the ground in the opposite direction. If, on the other hand, the wind blows from the equator towards the pole, it comes continually over places of the earth which have less motion from west to east than the air it carries with it; for this air has a motion equal to the velocity of the place from which it has spread out. It will therefore move away eastwards over the places it passes, and its motion towards the pole will be combined with the collateral motion from the west.",["- winde-0008: \"dieser umgekehrt proportionirt\" is unclear; I read \"dieser\" as the expansion (\"Ausbreitung\") and kept the sense in \"inversely proportional to it\". The statement of the cube law and the figures (4 square miles, 1/10, 1/80) is as in the German and I have not checked the arithmetic.","- winde-0008: restated the long \"Denn erstlich…\" sentence in two; \"Gewalt\" rendered \"power\", \"Ausspannungskraft\" \"expansive force\".","- winde-0010: split the Guinea sentence; \"welches\" resolved to Guinea. \"Musschenbröck seiner Physick\" rendered with the titles in italics as a supplied identification.","- winde-0010: \"Campsin\" left as in the German (Khamsin).","- winde-0015: bracketed \"[over the sea]\" and \"[the air over the land]\" to resolve \"diese\" and \"der erstern\".","- winde-0018: the last sentence's \"Abend\" in \"Collateralbewegung aus Abend\" is rendered \"from the west\"; \"wegziehen von Abend gegen Morgen\" as \"move away eastwards\". Kant's reasoning here is as given, though the physics of the deflection is stated by him in his own terms."]],"winde-0019":["To picture this distinctly, we must first bear in mind that, when the atmosphere is in equilibrium, every part of it has the same velocity of rotation from west to east as the place on the surface of the earth over which it lies, and is at rest in relation to that place. But if a part of the atmosphere changes its position in the direction of the meridian, it comes upon stretches of the earth's surface which move from west to east with greater or less speed than the air still retains from the place out of which it has been displaced. It will therefore move over the regions across which it passes either with a deviation from west to east, or else, in the direction from east to west, offer resistance to the earth's surface. In both cases this produces a wind which has this collateral direction. The strength of this lateral motion depends as much upon the speed of the place over which the air is moved as upon the difference in speed between the places from and to which it passes. Now the speed of the axial rotation of any point on the earth's surface is proportional to the cosine of the latitude, and the difference between the cosines of two places on the surface very near to each other (say one degree apart) is proportional to the sine of the latitude. Hence the moment of the velocity with which the air is displaced sideways in passing from one degree of latitude to the next will stand in the compound ratio of the sines and cosines of the latitudes. It will therefore be greatest at the 45th degree, and equal at equal distances from it.",[]],"winde-0020":["To give some idea of the degree of this collateral motion, let us take a north wind blowing from latitude 23½° towards the equator. When it sets out from that latitude, it has a motion from west to east equal to that of its place. When it has come 5 degrees nearer the equinoctial circle, it meets a belt of the earth which moves more swiftly in that direction. Now a simple calculation shows that the difference in speed between these two parallel circles amounts to 45 feet in a second. Accordingly, when the air has passed from the 23rd degree to the 18th, it would cause the earth in that region to oppose it with a counter-wind from east to west, capable of covering 45 feet in a second. This would be so, were it not that along the whole course of these 5 degrees the air passing over has already continually had something of its motion imparted to it by the rotation of the earth; hence this difference at the 5th degree of advance cannot by any means amount to so much. Since, however, some difference must always remain, let us assume it to be only the fifth part of what would hold apart from this consideration. The collateral motion will then still amount to 9 feet in a second. This is enough to turn a due north wind, which travels 18 feet in a second and starts from the 23rd degree, into a north-east wind at the 18th. In the same way a south wind, passing from the 18th degree to the 23rd with this same speed, will be changed at the latter degree into a south-west wind, since it enters the more slowly moving parallel circle with just as great an excess of momentum from west to east as was calculated before.",[]],"winde-0021":["Confirmation from experience.",[]],"winde-0022":["This will be appended to the following remarks.",[]],"winde-0023":["Fourth Remark.",[]],"winde-0024":["The general east wind which prevails over the whole ocean between the tropics is to be ascribed to no other cause than that which becomes evident from the first remark taken in conjunction with the third.",[]],"winde-0025":["The opinion which attributes the general east wind to the atmosphere's lagging behind in the earth's rotation from west to east has been rejected, with good reason, by students of nature; for even if the atmosphere did at first lag somewhat behind at the initial revolution, it must soon have been carried along with equal speed. I have, however, turned this thought to better and more correct account, in proving that it holds when the air passes from the more distant parallel circles to the equator. For then the air certainly does not have the same velocity as the motion of this greatest circle, and must without doubt lag somewhat behind. The east wind arising from this will be unceasing if new air continually moves towards the equator from the sides; for the former air would, of course, soon lose this opposing motion through the continued action of the earth's surface.",[]],"winde-0026":["Since the first cause has been given up by general agreement, it has been agreed to ascribe the general east wind between the tropics to the air's following in the wake of that air which has been rarefied by the sun, as it moves from east to west. One would certainly not have been satisfied with this explanation, had one possessed a better. If the air moves towards the place heated by the sun's action, for the reason given in the first remark, then the air lying to the west of the sun must do so just as much as that to the east; I therefore do not see why there should be nothing but east wind around the whole globe. But if the air moves into its place merely because of the cooling of air warmed some time before, it must for that reason move rather from west to east, since the places lying east of the sun have cooled more, and so have less elasticity, than those which the sun has left more recently. [Note: \"Abend/Morgen\" read consistently as west/east.] Yet even if I were willing to grant that everything happens as is demanded, can one form any rational conception of how it is possible that the draught of air which follows the sun when it is on the western horizon could cause a following draught as far as 180 degrees from it, that is, 2700 miles eastward? And must not so slight a motion vanish entirely at such astonishing distances? And yet the wind moves with equal strength in all parts of the tropic, and at all times of day, from east to west. Mr. Jurin, who supports the same opinion, certainly has good ground, if he cannot prove it, for asking why the same east wind is not observed at no great distance from the tropics, where the sun's action is surely also not inconsiderable. For in fact it cannot be explained at all from the cause adduced.",[]],"winde-0027":["Here then is another explanation, which agrees better with the best-known principles of natural science. The heat, which is stronger in the torrid zone and beside it than elsewhere, keeps the air lying above it in constant rarefaction. The somewhat less hot, and therefore heavier, belts of air which lie farther from the equator press into its place according to the laws of equilibrium; and since they move towards the equator, their northerly direction must, according to the third remark, turn into a collateral motion from the east. Hence the general east wind on either side of the equator will properly be a collateral wind. Under the line itself, however, where the south-east and north-east winds of the two hemispheres strive against one another, it must turn into a due east wind; and the farther it is from the line, the more it departs towards the direction of the poles.",[]],"winde-0028":["Confirmation from experience.",["- winde-0019: Kant's long final sentences restated as several sentences; \"Abend gegen Morgen\" rendered \"west to east\", \"Morgen gegen Abend\" \"east to west\".","- winde-0019: \"Collateralrichtung\" rendered \"collateral direction\"; \"Moment der Geschwindigkeit\" kept as \"moment of the velocity\".","- winde-0020: \"Aeqvinoktialzirkel\" rendered \"equinoctial circle\"; \"Schwung\" rendered \"momentum\" by judgment.","- winde-0026: I inserted a bracketed remark in the translation about west/east; it is not in the German and should be disregarded. In the sentence \"Wenn sie aber nur wegen der Verkühlung...\" the German reads as stated, though Kant's logic seems to require west/east care; the text may be doubtful.","- winde-0026: \"warum ... nicht ... verspühret werde\" after \"wenn er es nicht beweisen kann\" is awkward; I smoothed it to a question (\"asking why ... not observed\"), sacrificing Kant's irony in \"guten Grund\".","- winde-0026: \"Meilen\" rendered \"miles\" (German geographical miles).","- winde-0027: \"Luftstriche\" rendered \"belts of air\"; \"Linie\" = the equator."]],"winde-0029":["According to all concordant observations, the height of the barometer near the equator is one inch lower than in the temperate zones. Does it not follow from this of itself that the air of these latter regions must press towards the equator in accordance with the laws of equilibrium? And does not this movement produce, in our hemisphere, a perpetual north wind in the torrid zone? But why does this wind veer more and more, and at last, under the line, turn completely into an east wind? The answer is to be found at the end of the fourth Remark. But why is the equilibrium never completely restored here? Why does the air in the burning belt of the earth remain always lighter, by an inch of mercury, than that in the temperate zone? The heat, which is always active here, keeps all the air in a constant state of expansion and rarefaction. Accordingly, even if new air presses into this region in order to restore the equilibrium, it is expanded just as the former air was. The raised column of air rises above the level of the rest and flows off above towards this side. The air at the equator, therefore, can never rise higher than that in the temperate zones, and yet contains a thinner air within it; it must accordingly always be lighter than the latter, and yield to its pressure.",[]],"winde-0030":["Explanation of the west winds, which for the most part prevail over the ocean in the interval between the 28th and the 40th degree.",[]],"winde-0031":["The correctness of the observation itself is sufficiently confirmed by the experience of seafarers, in the Pacific as well as the Atlantic and the Japanese seas. For the cause no other principle is needed than that of the preceding Remark. Strictly, on the ground there adduced, a moderate north-east wind ought to blow here. But the air which accumulates from both hemispheres towards the equator flows over there without ceasing, and spreads northwards in the upper region of our hemisphere. Since it comes from the equator, it has received almost entirely the motion of the equator. It must therefore advance, with a collateral motion from west to east, over the lower air in the more distant parallel circles (see the third Remark). It will, however, exert its effect upon the lower air only where the opposite motion of the latter grows weaker, and where it itself descends into the lower region. This, however, must take place at a fairly considerable distance from the equator, and there west winds and collateral winds will prevail.",[]],"winde-0032":["Fifth Remark.",[]],"winde-0033":["The monsoons, or periodic winds, which prevail over the Arabian, Persian and Indian oceans, are explained quite naturally from the law demonstrated in the third Remark.",[]],"winde-0034":["In these seas south-west winds blow from April to September; calms follow for a time; and from October to March the opposite north-east winds blow again. Prepared by what has gone before, one sees the cause of this in a moment. In the month of March the sun passes over into our northern hemisphere, and heats Arabia, Persia, Hindustan, the adjoining peninsulas, and likewise China and Japan, more strongly than the seas lying between these countries and the equator. The air standing over these seas is compelled, by such a rarefaction of the northern air, to spread out in that direction; and we know that a wind going from the equator towards the north pole must turn into a south-westerly direction. On the other hand, as soon as the sun has passed the autumnal equinox and rarefied the air of the southern hemisphere, the air from the northern part of the torrid zone descends towards the equator. Now a wind hastening from the northern regions towards the line, if left to itself, necessarily turns into a north-east wind; it is therefore easy to see why this must replace the former south-west wind.",[]],"winde-0035":["One also readily sees the connexion of these causes, so far as they concur in producing the periodic winds. If there is, near the tropic, an extensive stretch of dry land which takes on more heat from the action of the sun than the seas lying between it and the equator, the air of these seas will soon be compelled to sweep over to these lands and produce a westerly collateral wind; and soon, from these lands, it will again spread out over the seas.",[]],"winde-0036":["Confirmation from experience.",[]],"winde-0037":["In the whole ocean between Madagascar and New Holland the constant south-east wind blows, which is natural to the seas lying near the tropic of Capricorn. But in the region of New Holland, in an extensive sea alongside this land, periodic winds are to be met with, which blow from the south-east from April to October, and in the remaining months from the north-west. For during these latter months it is summer in the southern lands, of which we know only the coasts of New Holland. The sun here heats the land far more strongly than the neighbouring seas, and compels the air to sweep from the regions of the equator towards the south pole, which, according to what was said in the third Remark, must cause a north-west wind. In the months from April to October the sun rises over the northern hemisphere, and then the southern air returns to the equator, to stream into the region of rarefaction, and produces the opposite south-east wind. It is not to be wondered at that most students of nature can give no ground for the periodic change of the winds in the said part of the southern ocean, since the law which we set out in the third Remark was not known to them. This insight can become uncommonly useful if one wishes to apply it to the discovery of new lands. If a seafarer in the southern hemisphere, not far from the tropic, at the time when the sun has passed it, perceives a steady north-west wind, this can be to him an almost infallible sign that towards the south there must be an extensive stretch of dry land, over which the heat of the sun compels the equatorial air to sweep, and produces a north wind combined with a westerly deviation. According to present observations, the region of New Holland still offers the strongest presumption of a widely extended southern continent [*Australland*] situated there. Those who sail the Pacific cannot possibly search all regions of the southern hemisphere to spy out new lands there. They must have a guide enabling them to judge on which side they will probably find them. The north-west winds could give them this guide, which they may meet with there over large stretches of sea in summer-time; for these are signs of a neighbouring southern land.",[]],"winde-0038":["Conclusion.",["- winde-0029: \"Aeqvator\" rendered \"equator\"; \"Zoll\" as \"inch\"; \"Qvecksilberhöhe\" as \"inch of mercury\". \"Wasserwage\" (\"level\") rendered freely as \"level\".","- winde-0029: the last sentence's causal structure is restated in two clauses, with \"accordingly\" supplied; \"dem Drucke derselben nachgeben\" = \"yield to its pressure\".","- winde-0031: the long sentence beginning \"Weil aber die Luft…\" is divided into several sentences; the original \"weil… und da… so muß\" is recast as \"Since it comes… It must therefore…\".","- winde-0031: \"stillen als atlantischen als auch japonischen Meere\" rendered \"Pacific… Atlantic… Japanese seas\"; \"Collateralbewegung\" kept as \"collateral motion\".","- winde-0035: the German is irregular (the conditional \"Es muß… seyn\" followed by \"so\"); I read it as a condition (\"If there is…\") and smoothed it. \"Wendezirkel\" = \"tropic\"; \"bald… bald\" is rendered as two successive \"soon\"s, which is a doubtful reading; \"alternately\" may be meant.","- winde-0037: \"Australland\" is given in brackets as the German term for the supposed southern continent; \"Wendzirkel\" = \"tropic\"; \"Neuholland\" = \"New Holland\". The text's \"Madegascar\" is rendered \"Madagascar\".","- winde-0037: \"die Meeren… natürliche Südostwind\" is read as \"the south-east wind natural to the seas near the tropic of Capricorn\". The \"Sonne erhebet sich über das nordliche Hemisphärium\" is rendered literally."]],"winde-0039":["It is a source of no small pleasure, when one has been prepared by the foregoing remarks, to look at the chart on which the constant or periodic winds of all the seas are to be found. For, with the help of the rule that the coasts of lands make the direction of the winds near them parallel to themselves, one is in a position to give the ground of every one of these winds.\n\nThe periodic winds blow over a region for a time and are then relieved by opposite winds. The interval between these changes, I say, is troubled with calms, rain, storms and sudden hurricanes. For at that time the opposite wind already prevails in the upper air, while the former wind has not yet wholly died away in the lower. As the two drive against each other, they at last hold one another in equilibrium, condense the vapours which they carry with them, and bring about all the changes just named.\n\nOne may also accept it as almost a general rule that storms are brought together by winds striving against one another. For it is commonly observed that after a storm the wind changes. Now this opposite wind was actually to be found in the upper air before the storm. It was also the wind which drove the storm-matter together and carried the storm-cloud above the horizon; for one usually finds that storms rise against the lower wind. The storm arose when the winds were holding each other in equilibrium, and after it the opposite wind keeps the upper hand.\n\nThe continuous rains which are often observed while the barometer is high, as for example last summer, may with much probability be ascribed to such currents of air striving against one another in two regions.\n\nMariotte observed that winds which begin to blow from the north at new moon run through the whole compass in about fourteen days, going round first to north-east, then to east, then to south-east, and so on, and also that the winds never complete the whole circle in the opposite direction. This observation can be completely explained by the rule of the third remark. For the north wind naturally turns into a north-east wind. When this wind has restored the equilibrium with the region towards which it moves, it becomes, because of the resistance of that region's air, wholly easterly. Then, since the air compressed in the south expands again towards the north, this, in combination with the east wind, produces a deviation to south-east. By the cause given in the third remark this becomes first southerly, then south-westerly; then, because of the resistance of the northern air, now restored to equilibrium, westerly; then, through combination with the northern air expanding once more, north-westerly; and at last wholly northerly.",[]],"winde-0040":["The space which I have set aside for this brief consideration places limits on its further development. I conclude it by announcing to those gentlemen who do me the honour of placing some trust in my modest instruction that I intend to expound natural science from Herr Dr. Eberhard's *First Principles of Natural Science* [*erste Gründe der Naturlehre*]. My aim is to omit nothing that can promote a thorough insight into the important discoveries of ancient and modern times, and above all to prove, in clear and complete examples, the infinite advantage which the latter have gained over the former through the fortunate application of geometry.\n\nI shall continue to give instruction in mathematics, and shall open the course of philosophy [*Lehrbegriff der Weltweisheit*] with an exposition of Meier's *Vernunftlehre* [Doctrine of Reason, i.e. logic]. I shall lecture on metaphysics from the handbook of Herr Professor Baumgarten. The difficulties of obscurity which seem to surround this most useful and most thorough of all handbooks of its kind will, unless I flatter myself too much, be removed by care in the lecturing and by detailed written elucidations.\n\nIt seems to me more than certain that not ease but usefulness must determine the value of a thing; and that, as an ingenious writer puts it, the straw is found floating on the surface without effort, but whoever wishes to seek pearls must descend into the depths.",["- winde-0039: The long second sentence (\"Die zwischen Zeiten…\") is broken and recast; its opening phrase is irregular in the German, and I resolved it as the subject of \"is troubled\". \"Zwischenzeit… ist … beunruhigt\" is rendered \"is troubled with\".","- winde-0039: Several sentences are divided and paragraphs introduced for clarity. The long Mariotte sentence is split into two sentences, and \"Man kann die Bemerkung … erklären\" is reordered.","- winde-0039: \"Wettermaterie\" is rendered \"storm-matter\", and \"Ungewitter\" and \"Gewitter\" both as \"storm\"; the German does not strictly distinguish them here.","- winde-0039: \"Carte\" is rendered \"chart\"; \"dritte Anmerkung\" refers to the third remark of Kant's own text, which is not in this passage.","- winde-0039: \"nordlichen/ostlich\" etc. are modern \"nördlich/östlich\"; I translated them as compass directions without comment.","- winde-0040: \"Vernunftlehre\" and \"Lehrbegriff der Weltweisheit\" are kept in German with a gloss in square brackets, since they are titles or technical designations of the course. \"Naturlehre\" is rendered \"natural science\" in the title.","- winde-0040: \"Mich dünkt es sey mehr als allzu gewiß\" is rendered \"more than certain\"; \"allzu\" is slightly pleonastic. The sentence is divided at the semicolon for readability.","- winde-0040: \"Handbuch\" is rendered \"handbook\"; \"die Stoppeln … oben fließend\" is read as straw floating on the surface."]]};
